/* v2.9.0 lens pages: researched and checked 24 October 2026 */
const LP={}
LP["blackness"]={name:"What it means to be Black",pfx:"bk",kpis:[{s:"global",h:"United Kingdom: 2021 numbers",n:"2.4 million",l:"Black people in England and Wales in 2021 (4.0%)",ic:"chart"},{s:"ukafrica",h:"Country of birth",n:"1.59 million",l:"residents of England and Wales born in Africa (2021)",ic:"globe"},{s:"ukafrica",h:"Growth since 2011",n:"+50.4%",l:"growth in the Black African group, 2011 to 2021",ic:"chart"},{s:"lived",h:"Black British Voices Project: belonging",n:"81%",l:"of respondents call themselves British; 49% are proud to be (2023)",ic:"heart"}],sections:[
 {k:"identity",h:"What 'Black' means as an identity",intro:"Being Black is not one fixed thing. It is an identity people choose for themselves, shaped by history, place, politics and law rather than by biology. For why the words we use matter, see the hub's Words matter page; for what DNA can and cannot tell us, see the DNA page.",points:[
  {h:"Self-identification",text:"Official statistics in the UK and the USA count ethnicity or race by asking people how they identify. The ONS describes ethnicity as multi-dimensional and subjective; the US Census Bureau says its race categories reflect a social definition, not a biological one.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Self-identification means the same person can answer differently over time or on different forms.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS, 29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Ethnicity is multi-dimensional and subjective, with various ways in which a person may choose to define their ethnic group."),SRCQ("About the topic of race (US Census Bureau)","https://www.census.gov/topics/population/race/about.html","Supports the What it means to be Black page","generally reflect a social definition of race recognized in this country and not an attempt to define race biologically, anthropologically, or genetically.")]},
  {h:"A political and cultural identity",text:"'Black' has been used as a shared identity for people of African descent across many countries, and as a political banner. It is not a biological category (see the hub's DNA page). Some researchers study how African migrants take on, or resist, a Black identity after moving to Europe.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Being and becoming black (Max Planck Institute for the Study of Religious and Ethnic Diversity)","https://www.mmg.mpg.de/834157/being-and-becoming-black","Supports the What it means to be Black page","examine the processes and circumstances that lead to them taking on, or possibly resisting, an identity as Black"),SRCQ("The decision to capitalize Black (Associated Press)","https://blog.ap.org/announcements/the-decision-to-capitalize-black","Supports the What it means to be Black page","conveying an essential and shared sense of history, identity and community among people who identify as Black")]},
  {h:"'Political blackness' in Britain",text:"From the 1960s to the 1980s, many activists of African Caribbean, South Asian and African heritage organised together under the word 'Black' to fight racism in housing, work, policing and schools. Groups such as Southall Black Sisters (mainly South Asian women, founded 1979) used the 'Black' banner.",figure:"",year:"1960s–1980s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("South Asian political Blackness in 1970s Britain (History Workshop, Saffron East)","https://historyworkshop.org.uk/black-history/south-asian-political-blackness-in-1970s-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","In the 1960s, 1970s and 1980s, political Blackness became a powerful force for many racialised activists"),SRCQ("South Asian political Blackness in 1970s Britain (History Workshop, Saffron East)","https://historyworkshop.org.uk/black-history/south-asian-political-blackness-in-1970s-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Although SBS was mostly made up of South Asian women, the movement always operated under the ‘Black’ political banner.")]},
  {h:"Why 'political blackness' fell out of use",text:"Historians give several reasons. Some South Asian writers say claiming Blackness can feel like appropriating histories, such as enslavement, that are not shared. Others argue the umbrella could hide the specific experiences of Black women and people of African descent. Some trade unions still use 'Black' in the political sense.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Views differ: some see political blackness as a lasting model of solidarity; others think it has run its course. Both views are reported here.",src:[SRCQ("South Asian political Blackness in 1970s Britain (History Workshop, Saffron East)","https://historyworkshop.org.uk/black-history/south-asian-political-blackness-in-1970s-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","adopting aspects of Black identities can sometimes feel like an appropriation of struggles that are not universally shared"),SRCQ("To Be (Politically) Black and British (AAIHS Black Perspectives, Nicole M. Jackson)","https://www.aaihs.org/?p=19865","Supports the What it means to be Black page","the idea could just as easily obscure certain kinds of oppression as illuminate them"),SRCQ("South Asian political Blackness in 1970s Britain (History Workshop, Saffron East)","https://historyworkshop.org.uk/black-history/south-asian-political-blackness-in-1970s-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Many unions still use Black in its political sense to group racialised members")]},
  {h:"Négritude",text:"Négritude was a literary and cultural movement that began in Paris in the 1930s among French-speaking African and Caribbean writers, led by Black students from French colonies including Aimé Césaire (Martinique) and Léon-Gontran Damas (French Guiana). It affirmed Black culture against French colonial assimilation.",figure:"",year:"late 1920s–1950s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Négritude (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/negritude/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","three black students coming from different French colonies: Aimé Césaire (1913–2008) from Martinique, Léon Gontran Damas (1912–1978) from Guiana"),SRCQ("Negritude (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/art/Negritude","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Negritude, literary movement of the 1930s, '40s, and '50s that began among French-speaking African and Caribbean writers")]},
  {h:"Pan-Africanism",text:"Pan-Africanism is the idea that people of African descent share common interests and should unite. The Fifth Pan-African Congress met in Manchester from 15 to 21 October 1945, with some 200 people attending and 87 delegates representing about fifty organisations.",figure:"",year:"1945",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Pan-Africanism (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Pan-Africanism","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Pan-Africanism, the idea that peoples of African descent have common interests and should be unified."),SRCQ("Remembering the Fifth Pan-African Congress (LUCAS, University of Leeds, Christian Høgsbjerg)","https://lucas.leeds.ac.uk/article/remembering-the-fifth-pan-african-congress-christian-hogsbjerg/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","the Fifth Pan-African Congress was held there from the 15th-21st October 1945"),SRCQ("Remembering the Fifth Pan-African Congress (LUCAS, University of Leeds, Christian Høgsbjerg)","https://lucas.leeds.ac.uk/article/remembering-the-fifth-pan-african-congress-christian-hogsbjerg/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","It saw some two hundred people in attendance, with 87 delegates representing some fifty organisations.")]},
  {h:"Black Consciousness (South Africa)",text:"Steve Biko founded the Black Consciousness Movement in apartheid South Africa. It encouraged Black people to recognise their own dignity and self-worth. Biko died from injuries suffered in police custody in 1977.",figure:"",year:"1970s",disputed:false,note:"Whether Black Consciousness included Coloured and Indian South Africans as 'Black' is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Steve Biko (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Steve-Biko","Supports the What it means to be Black page","was the founder of the Black Consciousness Movement in South Africa."),SRCQ("Steve Biko (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Steve-Biko","Supports the What it means to be Black page","SASO was based on the philosophy of Black consciousness, which encouraged Blacks to recognize their inherent dignity and self-worth.")]},
  {h:"Capitalising 'Black'",text:"Many publishers now write 'Black' with a capital B when it refers to people, to signal a shared identity rather than a colour. The Associated Press changed its style in 2020. UK government statistics guidance uses 'Black people' and advises against 'Blacks'.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:true,note:"Practice varies between publishers and countries, and whether to capitalise 'white' is still debated.",src:[SRCQ("The decision to capitalize Black (Associated Press)","https://blog.ap.org/announcements/the-decision-to-capitalize-black","Supports the What it means to be Black page","The lowercase black is a color, not a person."),SRCQ("PBS, other media companies, capitalize Black (PBS Standards)","https://www.pbs.org/standards/blogs/standards-articles/capitalizing-b-in-black/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","recently decided to start capitalizing “Black” when referring to people in a racial, ethnic, or cultural context."),SRCQ("Writing about ethnicity (Ethnicity facts and figures, GOV.UK)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","‘the Black ethnic group’ and ‘Black people’ were all acceptable phrases. ‘Blacks’ was not.")]}]},
 {k:"global",h:"Around the globe",intro:"Who counts as Black, and what it means, changes from country to country. Law, census design and history all play a part.",points:[
  {h:"United Kingdom: census categories",text:"In England and Wales, people first choose one of five high-level ethnic groups, including 'Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African', then a more detailed option such as African, Caribbean or Other Black. Mixed groups include 'White and Black Caribbean' and 'White and Black African'. Scotland's 2022 census used separate headings for 'African' and 'Caribbean or Black'.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Northern Ireland's census figures are not included here: they are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS, 29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Firstly, a person identifies through one of the following five high-level ethnic groups:"),SRCQ("Scotland's Census 2022: ethnic group, national identity, language and religion (NRS)","https://www.scotlandscensus.gov.uk/2022-reports/scotland-s-census-2022-ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","‘African, African Scottish or African British’, ‘Caribbean or Black’")]},
  {h:"United Kingdom: 2021 numbers",text:"In 2021, 2.4 million people in England and Wales (4.0%) identified as Black: 1.49 million Black African (2.5%), 623,000 Black Caribbean (1.0%) and 298,000 Other Black (0.5%). A further 513,000 identified as Mixed White and Black Caribbean (0.9%) and 250,000 as Mixed White and Black African (0.4%).",figure:"2.4 million (4.0%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Totals differ by a handful of people between census tables because ONS adds small random changes to protect confidentiality.",src:[SRCQ("Population of England and Wales (Ethnicity facts and figures, GOV.UK)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","2.4 million people (4.0%) were from black ethnic groups"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_20_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African: African\",1488381"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_20_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African: Caribbean\",623119"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_20_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups: White and Black Caribbean\",513042")]},
  {h:"USA: the one-drop rule",text:"For generations in the USA, a custom and later laws treated anyone with any African ancestry as Black (the 'one-drop rule', or hypodescent). Scholars say this limited people's ability to define themselves.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The exact dates of particular state laws are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("The Devil and the One Drop Rule (Michigan Law Review, Christine B. Hickman, 1997)","https://michiganlawreview.org/journal/the-devil-and-the-one-drop-rule-racial-categories-african-americans-and-the-u-s-census/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","provides that one drop of Black blood makes a person Black."),SRCQ("Hypodescent (EBSCO Research Starters)","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/anthropology/hypodescent","Supports the What it means to be Black page","classifies anyone with any African American ancestry as Black, effectively limiting self-identification")]},
  {h:"USA: census categories today",text:"The US census asks about race and Hispanic origin separately. 'Black or African American' is one of five minimum race categories, and since 2000 people can choose more than one race.",figure:"",year:"2000",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("About the topic of race (US Census Bureau)","https://www.census.gov/topics/population/race/about.html","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Black or African American – A person having origins in any of the Black racial groups of Africa."),SRCQ("About the topic of race (US Census Bureau)","https://www.census.gov/topics/population/race/about.html","Supports the What it means to be Black page","For the first time in Census 2000, individuals were presented with the option to self-identify with more than one race")]},
  {h:"USA: a diverse Black population",text:"Pew Research Center estimates 48.3 million people in the USA identified as Black in 2023 (14.4%). More than 5 million (about 11%) were born abroad. The total includes 5.6 million multiracial Black people and 3.0 million Black Hispanic people.",figure:"48.3 million (14.4%)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Pew warns that immigrants are slightly undercounted in the survey these figures use.",src:[SRCQ("Facts about the U.S. Black population (Pew Research Center)","https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/fact-sheet/facts-about-the-us-black-population/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","In 2023, an estimated 48.3 million people self-identified as Black, making up 14.4% of the country’s population."),SRCQ("Facts about the U.S. Black population (Pew Research Center)","https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/fact-sheet/facts-about-the-us-black-population/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","In 2023, more than 5 million Black Americans were foreign born, about 11% of the U.S. Black population."),SRCQ("Facts about the U.S. Black population (Pew Research Center)","https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/fact-sheet/facts-about-the-us-black-population/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","There were 3.0 million Black Hispanic people in the U.S. in 2023")]},
  {h:"Brazil: preto and pardo",text:"Brazil's census asks people to choose a 'cor ou raça' (colour or race): branca, preta, parda, amarela or indígena. In the 2022 census, pardo (brown, often of mixed heritage) became the largest group for the first time since the current series began in 1991, at 45.3% (92.1 million). 10.2% (20.6 million) identified as preto (Black). The Black movement in Brazil has long grouped pretos and pardos together as negros.",figure:"Pardo 45.3%; preto 10.2%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"These figures are as reported by Brazilian news media from the national statistics office (IBGE) release; they are not confirmed directly from IBGE's own release.",src:[SRCQ("Número de brasileiros que se declaram pardos supera pela 1ª vez o de brancos (IstoÉ Dinheiro, reporting IBGE, 2023)","https://istoedinheiro.com.br/numero-de-brasileiros-que-se-declaram-pardos-supera-pela-1a-vez-o-de-brancos","Supports the What it means to be Black page","cerca de 92,1 milhões de brasileiros (45,3% da população) se declararam pardos."),SRCQ("Número de brasileiros que se declaram pardos supera pela 1ª vez o de brancos (IstoÉ Dinheiro, reporting IBGE, 2023)","https://istoedinheiro.com.br/numero-de-brasileiros-que-se-declaram-pardos-supera-pela-1a-vez-o-de-brancos","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Já outros 20,6 milhões (10,2%) se identificam como pretos."),SRCQ("Número de brasileiros que se declaram pardos supera pela 1ª vez o de brancos (IstoÉ Dinheiro, reporting IBGE, 2023)","https://istoedinheiro.com.br/numero-de-brasileiros-que-se-declaram-pardos-supera-pela-1a-vez-o-de-brancos","Supports the What it means to be Black page","a separação entre pretos e pardos desconsidera o esforço histórico de agrupamento desses dois segmentos sob a denominação “negros”")]},
  {h:"The Caribbean and Canada",text:"Caribbean migration shaped Black communities across the Atlantic. In Canada in 2021, Jamaica and Haiti were the top birthplaces of Caribbean-born Black people, and Statistics Canada stresses there is 'not one, but many' Black populations.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Detailed census definitions for individual Caribbean countries are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Black History Month: celebrating diversity in Canada (Statistics Canada)","https://www.statcan.gc.ca/o1/en/plus/9022-black-history-month-celebrating-diversity-canada","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Jamaica and Haiti were the top countries of birth for Caribbean-born Black populations"),SRCQ("The Diversity of the Black Populations in Canada, 2021 (Statistics Canada)","https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/en/catalogue/89-657-X2024005","Supports the What it means to be Black page","there is not one, but many diverse Black populations in Canada.")]},
  {h:"France: no ethnic statistics",text:"France follows a 'colour-blind' model. A 1978 law restricted collecting race-based data, and France does not collect census data on race or ethnicity.",figure:"",year:"1978",disputed:true,note:"This source dates from 2001; debate in France about ethnic data has continued since, and current rules are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Race policy in France (Brookings, Erik Bleich, 1 May 2001)","https://www.brookings.edu/articles/race-policy-in-france","Supports the What it means to be Black page","France therefore collects no census or other data on the race (or ethnicity) of its citizens."),SRCQ("Race policy in France (Brookings, Erik Bleich, 1 May 2001)","https://www.brookings.edu/articles/race-policy-in-france","Supports the What it means to be Black page","a 1978 law specifically banned the collection and computerized storage of race-based data")]},
  {h:"South Africa: apartheid categories and their legacy",text:"Under apartheid, the Population Registration Act of 1950 classified every South African by race. Statistics South Africa still reports 'population groups': in its 2022 census, Black African people were the largest group, followed by people classified as ‘Coloured’ at 8.2%. Some MPs have questioned continuing to use these terms.",figure:"",year:"1950; 2022",disputed:true,note:"The year the Act was repealed is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Population Registration Act (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Population-Registration-Act","Supports the What it means to be Black page","promulgated in 1950 during the apartheid era"),SRCQ("Briefing by Statistician-General on the Census 2022 results (Parliamentary Monitoring Group, South Africa)","https://pmg.org.za/committee-meeting/37818/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","the Black African population group constituted the largest proportion of South Africa’s population, followed by Coloured at 8.2%"),SRCQ("Briefing by Statistician-General on the Census 2022 results (Parliamentary Monitoring Group, South Africa)","https://pmg.org.za/committee-meeting/37818/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","A Member argued that continuing to use the term “Black Africans” undermined the South African law.")]},
  {h:"Afro-Latin Americans",text:"About 130 million people of African descent live in Latin America, roughly a quarter of the population, according to Princeton's PERLA project as reported by Pew. Many Latin American countries only recently began counting race in their censuses.",figure:"c.130 million",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Country figures for Colombia, Cuba and the Dominican Republic are not confirmed by the sources cited; estimates vary widely with how questions are asked.",src:[SRCQ("Afro-Latino: a deeply rooted identity among U.S. Hispanics (Pew Research Center, 2016)","https://www.pewresearch.org/?p=13072","Supports the What it means to be Black page","about 130 million people of African descent live in Latin America, making up roughly a quarter of the total population"),SRCQ("Afro-Latino: a deeply rooted identity among U.S. Hispanics (Pew Research Center, 2016)","https://www.pewresearch.org/?p=13072","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Until recently, most Latin American countries did not collect official statistics on ethnicity or race")]},
  {h:"Afro-descendants in Asia and the Middle East",text:"Communities of African descent live in India (Siddis, in Gujarat, Maharashtra, Telangana and Karnataka), Türkiye (Afro-Turks, descendants of enslaved Africans in the Ottoman Empire) and Iraq (Afro-Iraqis, mainly around Basra).",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Population estimates for these communities are very uncertain; the Basra range is wide.",src:[SRCQ("Who are the Siddis? (Sahapedia)","https://www.sahapedia.org/who-are-siddis-multiple-pasts-and-presents-african-community-india","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Siddis and Habshis are Indian citizens of African descent who live in the states of Gujarat, Maharashtra, Telangana and Karnataka in India."),SRCQ("The Making and Unmaking of the African Community of Ottoman Izmir, 1870-1922 (University of Arizona)","https://sbs.arizona.edu/node/192","Supports the What it means to be Black page","interviews with the descendants of the enslaved known as ‘Afro-Turks,’"),SRCQ("Afro-Iraqis, climate change and environmental injustice in Basra (Carnegie Endowment, 2026)","https://carnegieendowment.org/middle-east/research/2026/03/afro-iraqis-climate-change-and-environmental-injustice-in-basra","Supports the What it means to be Black page","the Afro-Iraqi community in Basra Governorate—estimated at between 400,000 and 2 million people")]},
  {h:"Within Africa",text:"In Africa, most people are part of the majority, so 'Black' is rarely the main way they describe themselves. Afrobarometer surveys in 2021–2023 found 41% felt more connected to their national than their ethnic identity, 45% felt equally attached to both, and 14% put ethnic identity first.",figure:"41% / 45% / 14%",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The survey asked about national and ethnic identity, not about 'Black' identity; that Africans rarely use 'Black' at home is an interpretation, not a survey finding. North African experiences differ again.",src:[SRCQ("Africans struggle with interethnic trust but embrace coexistence (Afrobarometer, 2025)","https://www.afrobarometer.org/articles/africans-struggle-with-interethnic-trust-but-embrace-coexistence-new-afrobarometer-pan-africa-profile-shows/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Four in 10 Africans (41%) feel more connected to their national than their ethnic identity"),SRCQ("Being and becoming black (Max Planck Institute for the Study of Religious and Ethnic Diversity)","https://www.mmg.mpg.de/834157/being-and-becoming-black","Supports the What it means to be Black page","taking on, or possibly resisting, an identity as Black")]}]},
 {k:"ukafrica",h:"The wider African context of people in the UK",intro:"Black people in the UK have roots across Africa and the Caribbean, and a history in Britain that goes back nearly 2,000 years.",points:[
  {h:"Growth since 2011",text:"Between 2011 and 2021 in England and Wales, the Black African group grew from 990,000 to 1.49 million (about +50%). The Black Caribbean group grew from 595,000 to 623,000 (about +5%). The Black groups together grew from 1.86 million (3.3%) to 2.41 million (4.0%).",figure:"Black African +50.4%; Black Caribbean +4.8%",year:"2011–2021",disputed:false,note:"Percentage changes are calculated from the census counts. ONS says 2021's new write-in option for Black African means the two years are not perfectly comparable.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS, 29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African: African\" (2.5%, 1.5 million in 2021, up from 1.8%, 990,000 in 2011)."),SRCQ("Census 2011 KS201EW Ethnic group, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_608_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&rural_urban=0&measures=20100&select=cell_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black/African/Caribbean/Black British: Caribbean\",594825"),SRCQ("Census 2011 KS201EW Ethnic group, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_608_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&rural_urban=0&measures=20100&select=cell_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black/African/Caribbean/Black British\",1864890")]},
  {h:"African backgrounds people wrote in",text:"The new write-in option showed the largest specific African backgrounds were Nigerian (271,000), Somali (151,000) and Ghanaian (113,000).",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS, 29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the What it means to be Black page","The largest ethnic groups specified within this write-in included \"Nigerian\" (271,000, 0.5%), \"Somali\" (151,000, 0.3%) and \"Ghanaian\" (113,000, 0.2%).")]},
  {h:"Country of birth",text:"1.59 million residents of England and Wales were born in Africa (2.7%). The largest countries of birth were Nigeria (270,768), South Africa (217,180), Kenya (134,944), Ghana (132,577), Zimbabwe (124,146) and Somalia (108,921).",figure:"1.59 million Africa-born",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Country of birth is not ethnicity. Not everyone born in an African country identifies as Black, and most Black people in the UK were born here. A breakdown of Africa-born people by ethnic group is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Census 2021 TS012 Country of birth (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2032_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=geography_name,c2021_cob_58_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"England and Wales\",\"Africa\",1586958"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS012 Country of birth (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2032_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=geography_name,c2021_cob_58_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"England and Wales\",\"Africa: Central and Western Africa: Nigeria\",270768"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS012 Country of birth (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2032_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=geography_name,c2021_cob_58_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"England and Wales\",\"Africa: South and Eastern Africa: South Africa\",217180"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS012 Country of birth (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2032_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=geography_name,c2021_cob_58_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"England and Wales\",\"Africa: South and Eastern Africa: Somalia\",108921")]},
  {h:"Languages",text:"Most people speak English as their main language. Among people whose main language is an African language, the largest were Somali (62,240), Tigrinya (22,825), Akan (19,757), Igbo (11,074), Amharic (10,834), Shona (10,773), Swahili (10,154) and Yoruba (9,942).",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The census asked only for a person's main language, so it undercounts people who speak an African language alongside English.",src:[SRCQ("Census 2021 TS024 Main language (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2043_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_mlang_94_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"African language: Somali\",62240"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS024 Main language (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2043_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_mlang_94_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"African language: Tigrinya\",22825"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS024 Main language (detailed), England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2043_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_mlang_94_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"African language: Akan\",19757")]},
  {h:"Religion",text:"Of people in the Black groups in England and Wales, about 67% said they were Christian, 17% Muslim and 8.5% no religion; 6.4% did not answer.",figure:"Christian 67.0%; Muslim 17.3%",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Percentages are calculated from census counts. The religion question was voluntary.",src:[SRCQ("Census 2021 RM031 Ethnic group by religion, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2131_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_8_name,c2021_religion_10_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Christian\",1613753"),SRCQ("Census 2021 RM031 Ethnic group by religion, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2131_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_8_name,c2021_religion_10_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Muslim\",416327"),SRCQ("Census 2021 RM031 Ethnic group by religion, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2131_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_8_name,c2021_religion_10_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Total\",2409280")]},
  {h:"Where people live",text:"About half (49.3%) of people in the Black groups in England and Wales live in London, where they make up 13.5% of residents. Outside London, the largest shares are in Manchester (11.9%), Birmingham (11.0%) and Nottingham (10.0%). In Lewisham the share is 26.8%.",figure:"London 1.19 million (13.5%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The London share of 49.3% is calculated from the census counts.",src:[SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group by region (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=TYPE480&measures=20100,20301&select=geography_name,c2021_eth_20_name,measures_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"London\",\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Percent\",13.5"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group by local authority (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=TYPE154&measures=20100,20301&select=geography_name,c2021_eth_20_name,measures_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Birmingham\",\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Value\",125760"),SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group by local authority (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=TYPE154&measures=20100,20301&select=geography_name,c2021_eth_20_name,measures_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Manchester\",\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Percent\",11.9")]},
  {h:"Roman Britain: the Ivory Bangle Lady",text:"A wealthy young woman buried in Roman York in the later 4th century AD had ancestral links to North Africa, according to scientific study of her remains.",figure:"",year:"4th century AD",disputed:true,note:"Her ancestry was assessed from skull measurements; such methods have limits, and the finding is about ancestry, not how she saw herself.",src:[SRCQ("Celebrating Ivory Bangle Lady (York Museums Trust)","https://yorkmuseumstrust.org.uk/?p=42893","Supports the What it means to be Black page","an important woman with North African ancestry who lived and died in York more than 1,600 years ago."),SRCQ("Celebrating Ivory Bangle Lady (York Museums Trust)","https://yorkmuseumstrust.org.uk/?p=42893","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Ivory Bangle Lady had mixed racial heritage and ancestral links to North Africa.")]},
  {h:"Tudor England: John Blanke",text:"John Blanke was a Black African trumpeter at the courts of Henry VII and Henry VIII, recorded in 1507 and pictured in the Westminster Tournament Roll of 1511.",figure:"",year:"1507–1512",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("John Blanke: investigating a life (The National Archives)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/john-blanke/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","John Blanke was a trumpeter at the courts of Henry VII and Henry VIII."),SRCQ("John Blanke: investigating a life (The National Archives)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/john-blanke/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","November 1507 shows ‘John Blanke the blacke Trumpet’ receiving 8d per day.")]},
  {h:"The Windrush generation",text:"The Empire Windrush docked at Tilbury on 22 June 1948, bringing Caribbean passengers who, as citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies, had the right to live and work in Britain.",figure:"",year:"1948",disputed:true,note:"The National Archives page gives two different passenger figures (1,072 total and 492); the number of Caribbean passengers is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Empire Windrush: Caribbean migration (The National Archives)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/the-empire-windrush/empire-windrush-caribbean-migration/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Ena Clare Sullivan was a passenger on the 'Empire Windrush' which docked at Tilbury harbour in Essex on 22 June 1948."),SRCQ("Empire Windrush: Caribbean migration (The National Archives)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/the-empire-windrush/empire-windrush-caribbean-migration/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","They were citizens of the United Kingdom and Colonies.")]},
  {h:"African migration and refugees",text:"African migration to the UK grew after many African countries became independent, and again from the 1990s. Somalia's civil war, from 1988, produced a large flow of refugees, many of whom came to Britain.",figure:"",year:"1988 onwards",disputed:false,note:"Dates and numbers for post-1960s African migration, and for Eritrean and Sudanese refugees, are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Resilient women: what we can learn from Somali refugees in Britain (LSE, Joanna Lewis, 2021)","https://lse.ac.uk/research/research-for-the-world/race-equity/resilient-women-what-we-can-learn-from-somali-refugees-in-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","The failures and excesses of the Said Bare regime had led to civil war by 1988."),SRCQ("Resilient women: what we can learn from Somali refugees in Britain (LSE, Joanna Lewis, 2021)","https://lse.ac.uk/research/research-for-the-world/race-equity/resilient-women-what-we-can-learn-from-somali-refugees-in-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","This conflict had generated a sudden and massive flow of refugees.")]},
  {h:"UN Decades for People of African Descent",text:"The UN's International Decade for People of African Descent ran from 2015 to 2024. A Second International Decade runs from 1 January 2025 to 31 December 2034, with the theme 'Recognition, Justice and Development'. The first decade led to the UN Permanent Forum on People of African Descent.",figure:"",year:"2015–2024; 2025–2034",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Background: International Decade for People of African Descent (United Nations)","https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/background","Supports the What it means to be Black page","The Second International Decade for People of African Descent is an observance that runs from 1 January 2025 to 31 December 2034."),SRCQ("Background: International Decade for People of African Descent (United Nations)","https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/background","Supports the What it means to be Black page","led to initiatives such as the establishment of the Permanent Forum on People of African Descent.")]},
  {h:"The African Union and the diaspora as a 'sixth region'",text:"The African Union defines the African diaspora as people of African origin living outside the continent, whatever their citizenship, who are willing to contribute to Africa's development. The AU has recognised the diaspora as the 'sixth region' of Africa, alongside the North, South, East, West and Central regions.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The date of the AU decision is not confirmed by the sources cited; the 'sixth region' source is a 2016 Ghanaian news report.",src:[SRCQ("The Diaspora Division (African Union)","https://au.int/en/diaspora-division","Supports the What it means to be Black page","people of African origin living outside the continent, irrespective of their citizenship and nationality"),SRCQ("African Diaspora acknowledged as 6th region in Africa by AU (MyJoyOnline, 2016)","https://www.myjoyonline.com/african-diaspora-acknowledged-as-6th-region-in-africa-by-au/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","has acknowledged the African Diaspora as the sixth Region of the Continent")]}]},
 {k:"lived",h:"Lived experience: what research reports",intro:"These are findings published by researchers. They report what people said in surveys and interviews; they are not claims made on anyone's behalf.",points:[
  {h:"Black British Voices Project: belonging",text:"The Black British Voices Project (University of Cambridge with The Voice and I-Cubed, 2023) surveyed over 10,000 Black Britons. 81% described themselves as British, but pride was split: 49% were proud to be British and 45% were not.",figure:"81% British; 49% proud",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Over 10,000 Black Britons from across the UK completed an extensive survey"),SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","A majority (81%) describe themselves as British")]},
  {h:"Black British Voices Project: work, health, education, policing",text:"88% reported racial discrimination at work. 95% said the school curriculum neglects Black lives. 87% expected worse healthcare because of their race, and 87% had little or no trust in the criminal justice system. 74% were uncomfortable with the label 'BAME'.",figure:"88% workplace discrimination",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Participants volunteered to take part, so results may not represent all Black Britons exactly.",src:[SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","a high percentage of respondents (88%) saying they have experienced racial discrimination at work."),SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","95% of respondents believe the UK’s curriculum neglects Black lives and experiences"),SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Three-quarters (74%) of respondents felt uncomfortable with this label.")]},
  {h:"Faith and identity",text:"84% of Black British Voices participants described themselves as religious or spiritual. This fits census findings that most people in the Black groups report a religion.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","84% of participants self-described as religious or spiritual")]},
  {h:"EVENS survey (2023)",text:"The Evidence for Equality National Survey, led by the Centre on the Dynamics of Ethnicity, found 40–50% of Black Caribbean, Any other Black, and White and Black Caribbean respondents reported racist abuse in public places. Over a third of Black Caribbean and Any other Black respondents reported discrimination from the police.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Over a third of people from minority groups have experienced racist assaults, survey finds (University of Manchester, April 2023)","https://www.manchester.ac.uk/discover/news/racist-assaults-survey-finds/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","Between 40-50% of people from Black Caribbean, Any other Black, and White and Black Caribbean groups reported facing racist abuse"),SRCQ("Over a third of people from minority groups have experienced racist assaults, survey finds (University of Manchester, April 2023)","https://www.manchester.ac.uk/discover/news/racist-assaults-survey-finds/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","over a third of people from the Black Caribbean, Any Other Black, Roma and Gypsy/Traveller groups.")]},
  {h:"Census 2021 on identity",text:"The census lets people describe themselves. A new write-in option for Black African and an online search-as-you-type tool made it easier to self-define, which ONS says may partly explain some of the increases.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"ONS or EHRC surveys on belonging and discrimination specific to Black groups are not confirmed by the sources cited here.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS, 29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the What it means to be Black page","making it easier for people to self-define when completing the census online.")]}]},
 {k:"limits",h:"Limits, debates and where figures differ",intro:"The strongest case against any single picture of 'being Black' is that categories simplify real lives. These notes set out the limits openly.",points:[
  {h:"Categories are imperfect",text:"Census categories mix race, nationality and culture, and change over time. People with the same family history may tick different boxes. The US Census Bureau says its categories include national origin and sociocultural groups.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("About the topic of race (US Census Bureau)","https://www.census.gov/topics/population/race/about.html","Supports the What it means to be Black page","the categories of the race item include racial and national origin or sociocultural groups."),SRCQ("Facts about the U.S. Black population (Pew Research Center)","https://www.pewresearch.org/social-trends/fact-sheet/facts-about-the-us-black-population/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","individuals may change how they identify their race and ethnicity in their lifetime.")]},
  {h:"Grouping African and Caribbean people",text:"Combining Black African and Black Caribbean people into one 'Black' figure can hide big differences. For example, between 2011 and 2021 the Black African group grew by about 50% while the Black Caribbean group grew by about 5%. Government guidance prefers to refer to groups individually where possible.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Others argue a shared 'Black' figure is still useful for tracking racism that affects both groups. Both views are reported here.",src:[SRCQ("Writing about ethnicity (Ethnicity facts and figures, GOV.UK)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","The terms can also mask disparities between different ethnic groups and create misleading interpretations of data."),SRCQ("Population of England and Wales (Ethnicity facts and figures, GOV.UK)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","figures for the white other and black African and black other ethnic groups may not be directly comparable for 2011 and 2021")]},
  {h:"Critiques of 'BAME'",text:"The UK government no longer uses 'BAME' or 'BME' because they emphasise some groups and leave out others. The Commission on Race and Ethnic Disparities recommended stopping its use in March 2021. See the hub's Words matter page.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Writing about ethnicity (Ethnicity facts and figures, GOV.UK)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","We do not use the terms BAME (Black, Asian and minority ethnic) and BME (Black and minority ethnic)"),SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","was considered “unhelpfully homogenising” when classifying Britain’s diverse non-White population")]},
  {h:"Where figures differ",text:"Figures for the same group can differ slightly between tables, because of rounding and ONS's confidentiality adjustments. For example, the total Black population is 2,409,278 in one 2021 table and 2,409,280 in another. In 2011, the Black share was 3.3% when calculated from the total but 3.4% when rounded subgroup shares are added.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Differences are small and do not change the overall picture.",src:[SRCQ("Census 2021 TS021 Ethnic group, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2041_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_20_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",2409278"),SRCQ("Census 2021 RM031 Ethnic group by religion, England and Wales (ONS via Nomis)","https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/api/v01/dataset/NM_2131_1.data.csv?geography=2092957703&measures=20100&select=c2021_eth_8_name,c2021_religion_10_name,obs_value","Supports the What it means to be Black page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\",\"Total\",2409280")]},
  {h:"Survey limits",text:"Large community surveys such as the Black British Voices Project give voice to many people, but they rely on volunteers. The researchers note that Black communities have historically been wary of reports on race.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Black British Voices: the findings (University of Cambridge, 28 September 2023)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","We are mindful that historically black communities have been wary of reports conducted on race")]}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Your identity is yours to define. Official forms ask how you see yourself, and people can answer differently over time.",refs:["bk-identity-self-identification", "bk-lived-census-2021-on-identity"]},{t:"Most respondents in the largest survey of Black Britons call themselves British, while pride in being British is split.",refs:["bk-lived-black-british-voices-project-belonging"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Do not assume a Black colleague is African, or that an African colleague sees themselves as Black. Most Black people in the UK were born here.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-country-of-birth", "bk-global-within-africa"]},{t:"Ask, and use the words people use for themselves. The government no longer uses 'BAME'.",refs:["bk-limits-critiques-of-bame", "bk-identity-capitalising-black"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Expect reports of discrimination at work to be common, and act on them.",refs:["bk-lived-black-british-voices-project-work-health", "bk-lived-evens-survey-2023"]},{t:"Black communities differ in faith, language and heritage; one network or one event cannot speak for all.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-religion", "bk-ukafrica-languages", "bk-ukafrica-african-backgrounds-people-wrote-in"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Plan for the African languages your users speak, such as Somali, Tigrinya and Akan, alongside English.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-languages"]},{t:"Half of Black residents of England and Wales live in London, but several cities outside London have large Black communities.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-where-people-live"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Report Black African, Black Caribbean and Other Black separately where numbers allow: they grew at very different rates.",refs:["bk-limits-grouping-african-and-caribbean-people", "bk-ukafrica-growth-since-2011"]},{t:"Country of birth is not ethnicity; do not use one in place of the other.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-country-of-birth"]},{t:"Census categories are built differently in each country; compare them with care.",refs:["bk-global-brazil-preto-and-pardo", "bk-global-usa-census-categories-today", "bk-limits-categories-are-imperfect"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Being Black is an identity people choose for themselves. It is shaped by history, place and law, not by biology.",refs:["bk-identity-self-identification", "bk-identity-a-political-and-cultural-identity"]},{t:"Each country counts Black people in its own way. Brazil, the USA and the UK all use different categories.",refs:["bk-global-brazil-preto-and-pardo", "bk-global-usa-census-categories-today", "bk-global-united-kingdom-census-categories"]},{t:"In 2021, 2.4 million people in England and Wales were Black (4.0%).",refs:["bk-global-united-kingdom-2021-numbers"]},{t:"1.59 million residents of England and Wales were born in Africa. Nigeria is the most common country of birth.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-country-of-birth"]},{t:"Black people have lived in Britain since Roman times.",refs:["bk-ukafrica-roman-britain-the-ivory-bangle-lady", "bk-ukafrica-tudor-england-john-blanke"]}]}],time:[{y:"4th century AD",t:"The Ivory Bangle Lady, a woman with North African ancestry, is buried in Roman York",src:[SRCQ("yorkmuseumstrust.org.uk","https://yorkmuseumstrust.org.uk/?p=42893","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1507",t:"John Blanke, 'the blacke Trumpet', recorded at the court of Henry VII",src:[SRCQ("nationalarchives.gov.uk","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/john-blanke/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1900",t:"First Pan-African conference held in London, organised by Henry Sylvester Williams",src:[SRCQ("lucas.leeds.ac.uk","https://lucas.leeds.ac.uk/article/remembering-the-fifth-pan-african-congress-christian-hogsbjerg/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"Late 1920s–1930s",t:"Césaire, Damas and Senghor meet in Paris; Négritude begins",src:[SRCQ("plato.stanford.edu","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/negritude/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1945",t:"Fifth Pan-African Congress, Manchester (15–21 October)",src:[SRCQ("lucas.leeds.ac.uk","https://lucas.leeds.ac.uk/article/remembering-the-fifth-pan-african-congress-christian-hogsbjerg/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1948",t:"Empire Windrush docks at Tilbury (22 June)",src:[SRCQ("nationalarchives.gov.uk","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/the-empire-windrush/empire-windrush-caribbean-migration/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1950",t:"Population Registration Act passed in apartheid South Africa",src:[SRCQ("britannica.com","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Population-Registration-Act","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1977",t:"Steve Biko dies from injuries suffered in police custody",src:[SRCQ("britannica.com","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Steve-Biko","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1978",t:"French law restricts collection of race-based data",src:[SRCQ("brookings.edu","https://www.brookings.edu/articles/race-policy-in-france","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1979",t:"Southall Black Sisters formed, using 'Black' as a political banner",src:[SRCQ("historyworkshop.org.uk","https://historyworkshop.org.uk/black-history/south-asian-political-blackness-in-1970s-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1988",t:"Civil war in Somalia leads to refugee flows, including to Britain",src:[SRCQ("lse.ac.uk","https://lse.ac.uk/research/research-for-the-world/race-equity/resilient-women-what-we-can-learn-from-somali-refugees-in-britain","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"1991",t:"Ethnic group question first included in the census for England and Wales",src:[SRCQ("ons.gov.uk","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2000",t:"US census lets people choose more than one race for the first time",src:[SRCQ("census.gov","https://www.census.gov/topics/population/race/about.html","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2015–2024",t:"UN International Decade for People of African Descent",src:[SRCQ("un.org","https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/background","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2020",t:"Associated Press adopts a capital 'B' for Black",src:[SRCQ("blog.ap.org","https://blog.ap.org/announcements/the-decision-to-capitalize-black","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2021",t:"Commission on Race and Ethnic Disparities recommends government stops using 'BAME' (March); Census 2021 in England and Wales",src:[SRCQ("ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity/","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2022",t:"Brazil's census: pardo becomes the largest group for the first time since 1991",src:[SRCQ("istoedinheiro.com.br","https://istoedinheiro.com.br/numero-de-brasileiros-que-se-declaram-pardos-supera-pela-1a-vez-o-de-brancos","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2023",t:"Black British Voices Project report and EVENS survey findings published",src:[SRCQ("cam.ac.uk","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/black-british-voices-report","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]},{y:"2025–2034",t:"UN Second International Decade for People of African Descent",src:[SRCQ("un.org","https://www.un.org/en/observances/decade-people-african-descent/background","Supports the What it means to be Black page","")]}]};
LP["enterprise"]={name:"Black enterprise: starting and growing a business",pfx:"en",kpis:[{s:"picture",h:"£25 billion a year from around 250,000 firms",n:"£25 billion",l:"a year added to the economy by about 250,000 ethnic minority-led firms (2018)",ic:"chart"},{s:"barriers",h:"Lower median turnover",n:"£25,000",l:"median turnover of Black-owned businesses, against £35,000 for white-owned (2020 study)",ic:"scale"},{s:"barriers",h:"Higher rejection and discouragement",n:"52%",l:"of ethnic minority-led firms that applied for finance were turned down, against 22% (2018 to 2020)",ic:"info"},{s:"support",h:"One in five Start Up Loans to ethnic minority founders",n:"20%",l:"of the first £1 billion of Start Up Loans went to ethnic minority founders",ic:"hands"}],sections:[
 {k:"picture",h:"The picture: Black and ethnic minority enterprise in the UK",intro:"Ethnic minority-led firms are a substantial, innovative and outward-looking part of the UK economy. Most published data groups all ethnic minority groups together, and figures for Black-led firms alone are rare.",points:[
  {h:"£25 billion a year from around 250,000 firms",text:"The FSB estimated that in 2018 around 250,000 firms were led by ethnic minority entrepreneurs, generating £25 billion of gross value added.",figure:"£25 billion GVA; about 250,000 firms",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"Based on the government Small Business Survey. The figure covers all ethnic minority groups, not Black-led firms alone.",src:[SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","In 2018, these businesses generated £25 billion of GVA")]},
  {h:"The fastest-growing self-employed group",text:"Across ethnic groups, Black and Black British self-employment grew fastest between 2011 and 2018.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"Growth rate, not overall level: Pakistani and Bangladeshi workers had the highest self-employment shares.",src:[SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","the Black/Black British self-employed community had grown the fastest since 2011")]},
  {h:"Higher entrepreneurial activity",text:"Since 2002, ethnic minority entrepreneurs have typically reported higher rates of early-stage entrepreneurial activity than other people.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"A pattern across survey years, reported by the FSB.",src:[SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Since 2002 typically ethnic minority entrepreneurs have reported a higher Total Entrepreneurial Activity rate than non-ethnic minority entrepreneurs")]},
  {h:"Innovative and exporting",text:"Ethnic minority businesses were more likely than other firms to have innovated recently and, in every UK region, more likely to export.",figure:"30% engaged in recent product or service innovation",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","30% of ethnic minority businesses engaged in recent product or service innovation"),SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","EMB owners in every UK region were more likely to export than non EMB owners")]},
  {h:"About 6% of SME employers are ethnic minority-led",text:"In the government's 2024 survey, about 6% of small and medium employers were led by a team at least half from minority ethnic groups. That is little changed since 2015.",figure:"about 6%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"2023 was 7%. The survey does not give a separate figure for Black-led businesses.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Business and Trade: Longitudinal Small Business Survey 2024: SME employers (published 25 September 2025)","https://gov.uk/government/statistics/small-business-survey-2024-businesses-with-employees/longitudinal-small-business-survey-2024-sme-employers-businesses-with-1-to-249-employees","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Around 6% of SME employers were led by a management team with at least half of its members from minority ethnic groups")]},
  {h:"Firms with no employees",text:"In 2021, 4.9% of SMEs with no employees and 6.1% of SME employers were majority-led by people from an ethnic minority.",figure:"4.9% (no employees); 6.1% (employers)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Groups all ethnic minority groups together as \"All Other Ethnic Groups Combined\". No Black-only figure is given.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures: Leadership of small and medium enterprises (2021 data, updated 8 August 2023)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/business/leadership-of-small-and-medium-enterprises/latest","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","in 2021, 4.9% of SMEs with no employees were majority led by those from an ethnic minority")]},
  {h:"Black founders start up as successfully as white founders",text:"The British Business Bank found that Black and white entrepreneurs succeed in starting a business at similar rates. The gaps appear after launch.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Quoted from the full report PDF, a copy hosted by a third party because the Bank's own page blocks automated reading. The report notes small sample sizes for some groups.",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Black and White entrepreneurs have greater levels of success, which are similar to each other."),SRCQ("Oliver Wyman press release on the British Business Bank report, 21 October 2020","https://www.oliverwyman.com/media-center/2020/oct/entrepreneurs-from-ethnic-minority-backgrounds-experience-substantially-worse-business-outcomes-than-white-entrepreneurs.html","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","The success rate for starting up a business for Black entrepreneurs is in line with the UK average")]},
  {h:"More preparation, and spotting unmet demand",text:"Black, Asian and other ethnic minority entrepreneurs invest more time and money developing their ideas than white entrepreneurs. One in six Black entrepreneurs starts a business after spotting a gap in products suited to them.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Black, and Asian and Other Ethnic Minority entrepreneurs are investing more time and money developing their ideas than White entrepreneurs."),SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","One in six Black entrepreneurs decide to start a business after identifying a gap in the availability of products suited to them.")]}]},
 {k:"barriers",h:"Barriers: what the evidence shows after launch",intro:"The gaps are mostly in outcomes after launch and in access to finance. The British Business Bank found that some gaps remain even after controlling for more than 30 factors, and pointed to systemic disadvantage.",points:[
  {h:"Lower median turnover",text:"Black business owners reported median turnover of £25,000, against £35,000 for white owners. For owners from a Black African background the figure was £17,000.",figure:"£25,000 against £35,000; £17,000 Black African",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"From the Bank's 2020 entrepreneur survey, taken during the first year of COVID-19. Median turnover only, not profit or growth.",src:[SRCQ("British Business Bank: Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK (2020)","https://british-business-bank.co.uk/research-alone-together","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Black business owners have median turnover of just £25,000"),SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Among those from a Black African background, it is just £17,000.")]},
  {h:"Profit and aims",text:"72% of Black business owners reported making a profit the previous year, against 84% of white owners. About half of Black entrepreneurs met their non-financial aims, against nearly 70% of white entrepreneurs.",figure:"72% against 84%",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The Bank's later factsheet puts the non-financial aims figure at 49%.",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","72% of Black and 62% of Asian and Other Ethnic Minority business owners report making a profit last year"),SRCQ("British Business Bank: Unlocking the potential of diverse entrepreneurs, factsheet","https://www.british-business-bank.co.uk/unlocking-the-potential-of-diverse-entrepreneurs-factsheet","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","49% of Black entrepreneurs meet their non-financial aims, compared to nearly 70% of White entrepreneurs")]},
  {h:"Gender and ethnicity compound",text:"37% of Black female entrepreneurs did not make a profit the previous year, against 16% of white male entrepreneurs.",figure:"37% against 16%",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","37% of Black female entrepreneurs did not make a profit last year, compared to just 16% of White male entrepreneurs.")]},
  {h:"Finance stops ideas",text:"Difficulty getting finance was the reason 39% of Black aspiring entrepreneurs, and 49% of Asian and other ethnic minority ones, gave for stopping work on a business idea.",figure:"39% (Black)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("British Business Bank: Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK (2020)","https://british-business-bank.co.uk/research-alone-together","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","the reason why 39% and 49%, respectively, stop working on their business idea")]},
  {h:"Higher rejection and discouragement",text:"Between 2018 and mid-2020, 52% of ethnic minority-led businesses that applied for finance were turned down, against 22% of white-led businesses. In 2021, 20% wanted to apply but were put off, against 8%.",figure:"52% against 22% turned down; 20% against 8% discouraged",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Covers all ethnic minority-led businesses, not Black-led alone. The white-led comparison figures were read on the factsheet but are not in the quoted text.",src:[SRCQ("British Business Bank: Small Business Finance Markets Report 2021/22, factsheet","https://british-business-bank.co.uk/about/research-and-publications/small-business-finance-markets-report-2021/factsheet","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Between 2018 and mid-2020, 52% of Ethnic Minority-led businesses that applied for finance were turned down"),SRCQ("British Business Bank: Small Business Finance Markets Report 2021/22, factsheet","https://british-business-bank.co.uk/about/research-and-publications/small-business-finance-markets-report-2021/factsheet","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","In 2021, 20% of Ethnic Minority-led businesses wanted to apply for finance but were stopped by something")]},
  {h:"Systemic disadvantage, not only circumstance",text:"After controlling for sector, money, human and social capital and access to finance, some disparities remain. The British Business Bank attributes this to systemic disadvantage.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","After controlling for more than 30 explanatory factors"),SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Poorer access to finance, particularly among Black African and Indian entrepreneurs")]},
  {h:"Discrimination and distance from formal support",text:"The FSB reported that ethnic minority entrepreneurs sometimes face discrimination, and are often detached from formal support networks. They rely more on informal networks, especially in Black-led and mixed-led businesses.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Reliance on informal networks is also a strength: it is community infrastructure.",src:[SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Ethnic minority entrepreneurs sometimes face discrimination at different stages of their business life."),SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","EMBs more commonly relied on informal networks to do this when compared with the rest of the small business sector")]},
  {h:"Household wealth matters for everyone",text:"Regardless of ethnicity, entrepreneurs with household income of £75,000 or more were more likely to make a profit than those under £20,000. Lower household income is more common among Black Caribbean and Black African entrepreneurs.",figure:"87% against 76%",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","87% of those with household income of £75,000 or more made a profit last year")]}]},
 {k:"support",h:"Public schemes and what they offer",intro:"No UK-wide public scheme is reserved for Black founders. The main public route, Start Up Loans, reaches ethnic minority founders well above their share of businesses. Some regional and London programmes target under-represented founders. Listing a scheme here is not an endorsement.",points:[
  {h:"Start Up Loans: up to £25,000 with mentoring",text:"Government-backed personal loans of up to £25,000, with 12 months of free mentoring for first-time recipients. From 6 April 2026 the fixed rate is 7.5% and businesses trading for up to five years can apply.",figure:"up to £25,000; 7.5%; up to 60 months trading",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Terms change; check the live page before relying on them.",src:[SRCQ("Start Up Loans (British Business Bank) home page","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","We offer all of our first Start Up Loan recipients 12 months of free business mentoring."),SRCQ("Start Up Loans: changes to interest rate and eligibility criteria","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/support-and-guidance/frequently-asked-questions/changes-to-interest-rate-and-eligibility","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","As of 6th April 2026, the fixed interest rate on a Start Up Loan is 7.5%."),SRCQ("Start Up Loans: changes to interest rate and eligibility criteria","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/support-and-guidance/frequently-asked-questions/changes-to-interest-rate-and-eligibility","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Businesses will be able to apply if they have been trading for up to 60 months (five years).")]},
  {h:"One in five Start Up Loans to ethnic minority founders",text:"Of the first £1 billion lent since 2012, 20% (£201 million) went to people from Black, Asian and other ethnic minority backgrounds. The British Business Bank compares its 21% share with 4% of the overall SME population.",figure:"20%, £201 million",year:"",disputed:false,note:"\"Black, Asian and Other Ethnic Minority\" excludes white minorities. No Black-only figure was found. The milestone release is undated on the page; press coverage dates it 13 September 2023. The Bank's factsheet uses 21% and is undated.",src:[SRCQ("Start Up Loans: Start Up Loans hits £1bn milestone of loans to UK small businesses (September 2023)","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/media-centre/start-up-loans-hits-1bn-milestone-of-loans-to-uk-small-businesses","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","20% have been lent to people from Black, Asian and Other Ethnic Minority backgrounds (not including White Minorities)"),SRCQ("British Business Bank: Unlocking the potential of diverse entrepreneurs, factsheet","https://www.british-business-bank.co.uk/unlocking-the-potential-of-diverse-entrepreneurs-factsheet","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","21% of Start Up Loans go to Ethnic Minority entrepreneurs")]},
  {h:"Almost half of London's Start Up Loans",text:"In London, 7,366 loans worth more than £50 million, almost half of all loans issued in the capital since 2012, went to people from ethnic minority backgrounds.",figure:"48% of 15,423 London loans",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Data to end of October 2019. The release uses the term ‘BAME’.",src:[SRCQ("Start Up Loans: £50 million of Start Up Loans in London go to ethnic minority entrepreneurs (data to end October 2019)","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/media-centre/50-million-of-start-up-loans-in-london-go-to-ethnic-minority-entrepreneurs","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","almost half (48%) of all the 15,423 Start Up Loans issued in the capital since 2012 went to people from BAME backgrounds.")]},
  {h:"Mayor of London: Ascend programme",text:"A 12-week programme funded by the UK Shared Prosperity Fund (£748,000) and run by Foundervine, for under-represented founders including women and ethnic minorities. It addresses barriers to raising investment.",figure:"55 ethnic minority founders helped",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Foundervine is described here as the delivery body only, not endorsed. UKSPF activity ends on 30 September 2026, according to the whole-journey page.",src:[SRCQ("Mayor of London: UK Shared Prosperity Fund case study: Ascend programme","https://www.london.gov.uk/programmes-strategies/business-and-economy/mayors-priorities-londons-economy-and-business/uk-shared-prosperity-fund-ukspf/uk-shared-prosperity-fund-ukspf-case-studies/ascend-programme","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","The programme was run by Foundervine."),SRCQ("Mayor of London: UK Shared Prosperity Fund case study: Ascend programme","https://www.london.gov.uk/programmes-strategies/business-and-economy/mayors-priorities-londons-economy-and-business/uk-shared-prosperity-fund-ukspf/uk-shared-prosperity-fund-ukspf-case-studies/ascend-programme","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","underrepresented founders - including women, ethnic minorities, and other marginalised groups")]},
  {h:"Mayor of London: Investment Readiness Programme (2020)",text:"A one-year grant of up to £44,000 to Funding London to help women-owned and ethnic minority-owned SMEs understand the finance available to them.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"A historical example; check whether it is still running before linking to it.",src:[SRCQ("Mayor of London: ADD2434 Investment Readiness Programme (decision of 3 March 2020)","https://www.london.gov.uk/decisions/add2434-investment-readiness-programme","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","women and Black Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) owned SMEs")]},
  {h:"Innovate UK Women in Innovation",text:"In 2026, Innovate UK backed 100 women founders: 61 award winners each received a £75,000 grant plus 12 months of support. 29% of the cohort identify as being from an ethnic minority background.",figure:"29% of cohort",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"No dedicated ethnic minority strand was found. Whether one exists is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("UKRI: Innovate UK backs 100 women founders shaping the UK’s future industries (5 August 2026)","https://www.ukri.org/news/innovate-uk-backs-100-women-shaping-the-uks-future-industries/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","29% identify as being from an ethnic minority background (excluding white minorities)")]},
  {h:"Regional example: START, West of England",text:"A free early-stage programme for tech and digital start-ups in the West of England. The provider states it wants to support community-led and ethnic minority-led entrepreneurship.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Applicants must be over 18 and live in the combined authority area.",src:[SRCQ("TechSpark: START (West of England)","https://techspark.co/start/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","We are really keen to support community and ethnic minority led Entrepreneurship through this initiative.")]},
  {h:"Equal treatment in services and lending",text:"A provider of services to the public, including finance, must not discriminate by refusing the service or by the terms it offers.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Section 13 defines direct discrimination; section 29 applies it to services.",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 29 (provision of services)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/29","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","must not discriminate against a person requiring the service by not providing the person with the service")]},
  {h:"Networks led by and for Black business",text:"Black business networks exist across the UK. Examples include the London Chamber of Commerce and Industry's Black Business Association (launched 2020), Coding Black Females and Colorintech, which runs Black Tech Fest.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Described, not endorsed; no government register of such networks exists. The Black Business Network is not described, as no authoritative source for it is cited.",src:[SRCQ("London Chamber of Commerce and Industry: LCCI to launch Black Business Association (19 October 2020)","https://www.londonchamber.co.uk/news-insights/news/press-releases/lcci-to-launch-black-business-association/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","providing a forum to collectively discuss barriers to equality in London's economy"),SRCQ("Coding Black Females","https://codingblackfemales.com/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Coding Black Females is the largest community of Black women in tech in the UK."),SRCQ("Black Tech Fest (BTF); blacktechfest.com now redirects here","https://www.joinbtf.com/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Brought to you by Colorintech, BTF is our annual October festival held in London, UK.")]}]},
 {k:"finance",h:"Finance: mainstream, faith-compliant and community",intro:"Ethnic minority-led businesses are more open to using finance for growth than white-led businesses, but they face more refusals. Faith-compliant and community-based finance has a long history and some legal recognition, and is less visible in the public offer.",points:[
  {h:"Appetite for growth finance",text:"Half of ethnic minority-led businesses are open to using finance for growth, against around three in ten white-led businesses.",figure:"half against about 30%",year:"",disputed:false,note:"From the 2021/22 Small Business Finance Markets factsheet.",src:[SRCQ("British Business Bank: Small Business Finance Markets Report 2021/22, factsheet","https://british-business-bank.co.uk/about/research-and-publications/small-business-finance-markets-report-2021/factsheet","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Half of Ethnic Minority-led businesses are open to using finance for growth compared to around three in 10 White-led")]},
  {h:"Alternative finance is recognised in tax law",text:"UK tax law treats returns under alternative (for example Sharia-compliant) finance arrangements as interest for tax purposes.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Income Tax Act 2007, Part 10A: alternative finance arrangements","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2007/3/part/10A","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","contains provisions about the treatment as interest for certain income tax purposes of alternative finance return")]},
  {h:"Sharia-compliant Start Up Loans route has closed to new applicants",text:"Start Up Loans offered Sharia-compliant funding through Financing Sharia Enterprise. It stopped taking new applications from 29 December 2025, and the standard Start Up Loan is not Sharia compliant.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Relevant to Black Muslim founders, including many of Somali, Nigerian and other African heritage; no source gives numbers. Whether a replacement route exists is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Start Up Loans: Sharia compliant finance","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/sharia-compliant-finance","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Please note that Financing Sharia Enterprise will stop taking new applications for funding from 29 December 2025."),SRCQ("Start Up Loans: Sharia compliant finance","https://www.startuploans.co.uk/sharia-compliant-finance","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Please note that this option isn’t Sharia compliant.")]},
  {h:"Pardner: community savings as a response to exclusion",text:"Pardner is a rotating savings and credit arrangement. Members pay in together and take turns to receive the pot. Caribbean communities used it when Windrush-generation migrants faced barriers to UK banking.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Similar rotating schemes exist in many African communities (for example susu or esusu), but the sources cited do not confirm this.",src:[SRCQ("Bank of England Museum: Pardner Hand: a Caribbean answer to British banking exclusion (exhibition with Museumand, closed 20 December 2024)","https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/museum/whats-on/exhibitions/pardner-hand","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","a kind of community-based saving method also known as a Rotating Savings and Credit Association (ROSCA)"),SRCQ("Bank of England Museum: Community savings and the Pardner Hand (16 June 2023)","https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/museum/online-collections/blog/community-savings-and-the-pardner-hand","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Each person, or 'pardner', taking it in turns to receive the pot, or 'hand,' of money.")]}]},
 {k:"diaspora",h:"Diaspora: trade and networks with Africa and the Caribbean",intro:"Diaspora founders often bring international networks, languages and market knowledge. The FSB found ethnic minority businesses more likely to export. This section gives context; it does not advise on any route.",points:[
  {h:"International networks as an asset",text:"The FSB described ethnic minority and migrant communities as forging trading links by drawing on international networks.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","forging vital trading links by leveraging international networks"),SRCQ("Federation of Small Businesses: Unlocking opportunity: the value of ethnic minority firms to UK economic activity and enterprise (2020), hosted by the Enterprise Research Centre","https://www.enterpriseresearch.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/Unlocking-Opportunity_FSB-Report-.2020.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","EMB owners in every UK region were more likely to export than non EMB owners")]},
  {h:"Developing Countries Trading Scheme",text:"Since 19 June 2023, the UK's Developing Countries Trading Scheme has given preferential access to UK imports from 65 eligible countries, many of them in Africa. It replaced the UK Generalised Scheme of Preferences.",figure:"65 countries",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The scheme benefits UK importers buying from eligible countries. Tier and tariff details are on linked GOV.UK guidance and are not covered here here.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Developing Countries Trading Scheme (DCTS)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/trading-with-developing-nations/uk-generalised-scheme-of-preferences","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","The DCTS applies to 65 countries that are:"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Developing Countries Trading Scheme (DCTS)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/trading-with-developing-nations/uk-generalised-scheme-of-preferences","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","entered into force on 19 June 2023")]},
  {h:"African Continental Free Trade Area",text:"Trading under the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) started on 1 January 2021. 54 African Union member states have signed the agreement.",figure:"54 signatories",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Ratification figures are from January 2021 (30 countries) and have since changed; the current count is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("African Union: press briefing on the status of the AfCFTA (12 January 2021)","https://au.int/en/newsevents/20210112/press-briefing-status-afcfta","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Trading under the African Continental Free Trade Area started in earnest on 1 January 2021.")]},
  {h:"UK trade with African partners: an example",text:"UK–Kenya trade in goods and services was £2.1 billion in the four quarters to the end of the first quarter of 2026. The Department for Business and Trade publishes a factsheet like this for each partner country.",figure:"£2.1 billion",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"One country shown as an example. A total for UK–Africa trade is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Business and Trade: Trade and investment factsheet, Kenya (24 September 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ab254aba9c3d267bcccecc9/kenya-trade-and-investment-factsheet-2026-09-24.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Total trade in goods and services (exports plus imports) between the UK and Kenya was £2.1 billion")]},
  {h:"Remittances and money service businesses",text:"Remittances from diaspora communities are a major economic link, and money service businesses that carry them are themselves diaspora enterprises. A 2015 government factsheet estimated at least US$1.3 billion a year was remitted to Somalia, mainly through such businesses.",figure:"at least US$1.3 billion a year (Somalia)",year:"2015",disputed:false,note:"Dated, single-country and quoting an Oxfam estimate. No current official figure for UK remittances to Africa as a whole was confirmed.",src:[SRCQ("HM Government: UK-Somalia Remittance Factsheet (March 2015)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/418690/15-03-11_UK-Somalia_Remittance_Factsheet.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","It is estimated that a minimum of US$1.3 billion is remitted annually to Somalia by diaspora mainly via money service businesses")]}]},
 {k:"history",h:"History: Black British enterprise",intro:"Black enterprise in Britain is long-standing, and much of it began as a response to exclusion from jobs and banking.",points:[
  {h:"Dr Harold Moody's practice, Peckham, 1913",text:"Refused hospital work because of his colour, Harold Moody set up his own medical practice in Peckham in February 1913. He later co-founded the League of Coloured Peoples in 1931.",figure:"",year:"1913",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("King’s College London: Dr Harold Moody","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/people/dr-harold-moody","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","After three years of unsuccessful job-hunting, Harold set up his own practice in Peckham, south London, in February 1913.")]},
  {h:"Brixton's markets in the Windrush years",text:"In the 1950s, Brixton became a hub for the African Caribbean community, and its markets became the community's commercial and social heart, selling Caribbean produce.",figure:"",year:"1956",disputed:false,note:"Historic England does not say who owned the stalls or shops. Black ownership of specific Windrush-era Brixton businesses is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Historic England blog: Places that tell stories of the Windrush generation (22 June 2020)","https://heritagecalling.com/2020/06/22/places-that-tell-stories-of-the-windrush-generation/","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Brixton's markets soon formed the commercial and social heart of the new community")]},
  {h:"Pardner and Caribbean entrepreneurship",text:"The Bank of England Museum and Museumand presented pardner as Caribbean entrepreneurship in the face of financial discrimination: Windrush-generation migrants were often denied basic banking services.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Exhibition closed 20 December 2024.",src:[SRCQ("Bank of England Museum: Pardner Hand: a Caribbean answer to British banking exclusion (exhibition with Museumand, closed 20 December 2024)","https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/museum/whats-on/exhibitions/pardner-hand","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","highlights Caribbean entrepreneurship in the face of financial discrimination"),SRCQ("Bank of England Museum: Community savings and the Pardner Hand (16 June 2023)","https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/museum/online-collections/blog/community-savings-and-the-pardner-hand","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","When trying to access financial services in the UK, the recently arrived Windrush Generation faced many barriers.")]},
  {h:"New Beacon Books, 1966",text:"New Beacon Books, established in 1966, describes itself as Britain's first Black publisher and bookshop.",figure:"",year:"1966",disputed:false,note:"The \"first\" claim is the organisation's own and was not independently verified.",src:[SRCQ("New Beacon Books: The George Padmore Institute","https://www.newbeaconbooks.com/the-george-padmore-institute","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","Britain's first black publisher and bookshop")]}]},
 {k:"limits",h:"Limits of this evidence and of this page",intro:"Read the evidence on this page with these limits in mind.",points:[
  {h:"Black-specific data is thin",text:"Official surveys mostly report \"ethnic minority\" as one group. The British Business Bank's 2020 study is the main source with Black-specific figures, and it notes small samples for some groups.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Treat Black African and Black female figures as indicative.",src:[SRCQ("Alone, together: entrepreneurship and diversity in the UK, full report PDF (British Business Bank and Oliver Wyman, 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Alone-together-Entrepreneurship-and-diversity-in-the-UK-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","in others we are restricted to groupings of ethnicities such as ‘Black’, ‘Asian and Other Ethnic Minority’ and ‘White’.")]},
  {h:"Much of the evidence dates from 2018 to 2021",text:"The key studies use data from before or during COVID-19. No newer study with comparable Black-specific outcome figures is cited here.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Department for Business and Trade: Longitudinal Small Business Survey 2024: SME employers (published 25 September 2025)","https://gov.uk/government/statistics/small-business-survey-2024-businesses-with-employees/longitudinal-small-business-survey-2024-sme-employers-businesses-with-1-to-249-employees","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","This compared to 7% in 2023 and there has been little change since 2015.")]},
  {h:"No immigration advice",text:"Whether a person may work or run a business in the UK depends on their immigration status. This page gives no immigration advice and says nothing about any individual's status. Employers must check that a job applicant is allowed to work in the UK before employing them.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Check a job applicant's right to work","https://www.gov.uk/check-job-applicant-right-to-work","Supports the Black enterprise: starting and growing a business page","You must check that a job applicant is allowed to work for you in the UK before you employ them.")]},
  {h:"Naming is not endorsing",text:"Networks, programmes and private bodies are described so people can find them. No government register of community networks exists, and inclusion here is not a recommendation.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Unconfirmed items",text:"These items are not confirmed by the sources cited: the Pathways recommendation on ethnicity data; the Black Business Network; any Innovate UK ethnic minority strand; Black ownership of named Windrush-era Brixton businesses; the minimum Start Up Loan amount; a current UK–Africa trade or remittance total.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Start Up Loans offers up to £25,000 with a year of free mentoring, and reaches ethnic minority founders well above their share of businesses.",refs:["en-support-start-up-loans-up-to-25-000-with-mentori", "en-support-one-in-five-start-up-loans-to-ethnic-min"]},{t:"If you need Sharia-compliant finance, the Start Up Loans route has closed to new applicants; the tax rules for alternative finance still apply.",refs:["en-finance-sharia-compliant-start-up-loans-route-ha", "en-finance-alternative-finance-is-recognised-in-tax"]},{t:"Lenders and other service providers must not discriminate against you.",refs:["en-support-equal-treatment-in-services-and-lending"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Networks led by and for Black founders carry much of the support; mentor, buy from and introduce them.",refs:["en-barriers-discrimination-and-distance-from-formal-", "en-support-networks-led-by-and-for-black-business"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Black founders start up as successfully as white founders. The gaps open after launch: in turnover, profit and finance.",refs:["en-picture-black-founders-start-up-as-successfully-", "en-barriers-lower-median-turnover", "en-barriers-higher-rejection-and-discouragement"]},{t:"Gender and ethnicity compound: Black women founders face the widest profit gap.",refs:["en-barriers-gender-and-ethnicity-compound"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Design support for after launch and for access to finance, not only for starting up.",refs:["en-barriers-systemic-disadvantage-not-only-circumsta", "en-barriers-finance-stops-ideas", "en-finance-appetite-for-growth-finance"]},{t:"Include faith-compliant and community finance, such as pardner, in how services are designed.",refs:["en-finance-alternative-finance-is-recognised-in-tax", "en-finance-pardner-community-savings-as-a-response-"]},{t:"Diaspora founders bring trade links: trade schemes with Africa are part of the picture.",refs:["en-diaspora-developing-countries-trading-scheme", "en-diaspora-african-continental-free-trade-area", "en-diaspora-international-networks-as-an-asset"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Publish start-up and lending outcomes by ethnicity, with Black African and Black Caribbean shown separately.",refs:["en-limits-black-specific-data-is-thin", "en-picture-about-6-of-sme-employers-are-ethnic-mino"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Black founders start businesses as successfully as white founders.",refs:["en-picture-black-founders-start-up-as-successfully-"]},{t:"The gaps come after launch: lower turnover, less profit and more refusals for finance.",refs:["en-barriers-lower-median-turnover", "en-barriers-profit-and-aims", "en-barriers-higher-rejection-and-discouragement"]},{t:"Start Up Loans lend up to £25,000, with a year of free mentoring.",refs:["en-support-start-up-loans-up-to-25-000-with-mentori"]},{t:"The Sharia-compliant Start Up Loans route closed to new applicants on 29 December 2025.",refs:["en-finance-sharia-compliant-start-up-loans-route-ha"]},{t:"Black enterprise in Britain is long-standing, and often began as a response to exclusion.",refs:["en-history-dr-harold-moody-s-practice-peckham-1913", "en-finance-pardner-community-savings-as-a-response-"]}]}]};
LP["younglives"]={name:"Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET",pfx:"yl",kpis:[{s:"neet",h:"Milburn interim report (2026)",n:"15.2%",l:"of Black, African and Caribbean 16 to 24 year olds were NEET in 2024, the highest rate",ic:"briefcase"},{s:"neet",h:"16 and 17 year olds, England",n:"1.7%",l:"of Black 16 and 17 year olds were NEET, against 3.9% of White peers",ic:"chart"},{s:"strengths",h:"University entry",n:"48.0%",l:"of Black 18 year olds entered higher education in 2024, against 29.8% of White",ic:"spark"},{s:"care",h:"Care leavers and NEET",n:"40%",l:"of care leavers aged 19 to 21 were NEET, against about 15% of all",ic:"handheart"}],sections:[
 {k:"terms",h:"NEET, BEET and CHEET",intro:"NEET is the official label, used at 16 to 24. BEET and CHEET are terms used by this hub, describing earlier states in which a young person is held, lost or unseen by the system.",points:[
  {h:"BEET",text:"Being in Education, Employment or Training: the default engaged state, universal and not individually tracked once things go well.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"A term used by this hub; no external source.",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"CHEET",text:"Child Held in Early years, Education or Training: held by the state or someone other than their parents, such as care, guardianship, treatment, custody of a parent, or licensed work.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"A term used by this hub; no external source.",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"NEET",text:"Not in Education, Employment or Training, officially measured at 16 to 24 from the Labour Force Survey. DfE also tracks 16 and 17 year olds.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Departure events",text:"The point where a child leaves a system, or a system stops seeing them: for example exclusion, deregistration for home education, leaving care at 18, or leaving custody. This page uses them to show where Black, African and Caribbean young people's experiences differ.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Linking statement is an editorial synthesis, based on the sections below.",ed:true,src:[]}]},
 {k:"school",h:"School: exclusions, adultification and children not in school",intro:"Exclusion and suspension are 'departure events' from BEET. Some Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean pupils are suspended and excluded at higher rates than the average, while Black African pupils are not.",points:[
  {h:"Suspensions: Mixed White and Black Caribbean",text:"Pupils from the Mixed White and Black Caribbean group had a suspension rate of 17.36 (per 100 pupils), almost twice the England rate of 9.33.",figure:"17.36 v 9.33",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Rate = suspensions per 100 pupils; one pupil can be suspended more than once.",src:[SRCQ("Pupil suspensions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 18 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/pupil-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","pupils from the Mixed White and Black Caribbean ethnic group were almost 2 times more likely to be suspended"),SRCQ("Pupil suspensions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 18 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/pupil-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","787,000 suspensions in state-funded schools in England – 933 suspensions for every 10,000 pupils (a rate of 9.33)")]},
  {h:"Suspensions: Black Caribbean",text:"Black Caribbean pupils had a suspension rate of 13.58, above the England rate. Black African pupils had a rate of 5.54, below it.",figure:"13.58 (Black Caribbean); 5.54 (Black African)",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Black African figure is from the page's data table, not its text.",src:[SRCQ("Pupil suspensions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 18 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/pupil-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black Caribbean pupils had a suspension rate of 13.58 (1,358 suspensions for every 10,000 pupils)")]},
  {h:"Permanent exclusions",text:"The England rate was 0.11. In the data table, Mixed White and Black Caribbean was 0.24 and Black Caribbean 0.18; Black African was 0.06, below average. Highest rates were for Gypsy or Roma and Irish Traveller pupils.",figure:"0.24 / 0.18 / 0.06 v 0.11",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Group rates are table values; the page warns they rest on very small numbers.",src:[SRCQ("Permanent exclusions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 11 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/permanent-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","there were 11 permanent exclusions for every 10,000 pupils in England (a rate of 0.11)"),SRCQ("Permanent exclusions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 11 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/permanent-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","these rates are based on very small numbers of exclusions and are less reliable as a result")]},
  {h:"Latest year (2024/25)",text:"Overall rates in 2024/25 (suspensions 10.88, permanent exclusions 0.12) were higher than in 2022/23 but lower than in 2023/24 (11.31 and 0.13). The release names Gypsy, Roma and Irish Traveller pupils as having the highest rates; 2024/25 rates for Black groups are not shown here.",figure:"10.88 / 0.12",year:"2024/25",disputed:false,note:"Ethnicity breakdown for 2024/25 not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Suspensions and permanent exclusions in England, 2024/25 (DfE, Explore education statistics)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/suspensions-and-permanent-exclusions-in-england","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","The rate of suspensions in the 2024/25 academic year was 10.88"),SRCQ("Suspensions and permanent exclusions in England, 2024/25 (DfE, Explore education statistics)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/suspensions-and-permanent-exclusions-in-england","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","The rate of permanent exclusions in the 2024/25 academic year was 0.12")]},
  {h:"Timpson review (2019)",text:"The government response to the Timpson review accepted that some groups, including Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean pupils, are more likely to be permanently excluded, even when other factors are taken into account.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"The often-cited '1.7 times' figure for Black Caribbean pupils is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Timpson review of school exclusion: government response (DfE, May 2019; copy held in the UCL IOE Digital Education Resource Archive)","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk//33359/3/Timpson_review_of_school_exclusion_government_response.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","in particular Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean pupils"),SRCQ("Timpson review of school exclusion: government response (DfE, May 2019; copy held in the UCL IOE Digital Education Resource Archive)","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk//33359/3/Timpson_review_of_school_exclusion_government_response.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","children from some groups (such as Black Caribbean children) are more likely to be excluded than White British children")]},
  {h:"Adultification",text:"Adultification is when children are not given the presumption of innocence and vulnerability that childhood should bring. Inspectorate and charity sources link it to racialised discrimination against Black children.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Definition originates with Davis and Marsh (2020), as cited by Missing People.",src:[SRCQ("Adultification and ethnicity (Missing People, 19 October 2022)","https://missingpeople.org.uk/?p=15052","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","adultification is when notions of innocence and vulnerability are not afforded to certain children."),SRCQ("Adultification bias within child protection and safeguarding, Academic Insights (HM Inspectorate of Probation, 24 June 2022)","https://hmiprobation.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/document/adultification-bias-within-child-protection-and-safeguarding","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","highlighting adultification bias, its links to racialised discrimination")]},
  {h:"Child Q (City and Hackney, 2022)",text:"The safeguarding practice review into the strip search of a Black schoolgirl at school found racism and adultification bias were likely influencing factors. A 2023 update was positive about progress but found concerns extend to all statutory services.",figure:"",year:"2022; update 2023",disputed:false,note:"Findings are as reported in Hackney Council's summary; the full review text is not cited here.",src:[SRCQ("Child Q update report: Hackney Council responds (London Borough of Hackney, 20 June 2023)","https://news.hackney.gov.uk/child-q-update-report-hackney-council-responds","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","And it found that racism and ‘adultification bias’ were ‘likely’ influencing factors in what happened."),SRCQ("Child Q update report: Hackney Council responds (London Borough of Hackney, 20 June 2023)","https://news.hackney.gov.uk/child-q-update-report-hackney-council-responds","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","The report concludes that concerns about safeguarding, racism and disproportionality extend to all statutory services.")]},
  {h:"Strip searches of children (2026)",text:"The Children's Commissioner found that between July 2023 and June 2024 Black children in England and Wales were almost eight times more likely than White children to be strip searched by police: 35% of the children strip searched were Black, although Black children were 6% of 10–17-year-olds in the 2021 census (report published April 2026). Earlier reports measured against population figures: Black children were up to six times more likely to be strip searched than population figures would suggest in 2018–mid-2022 (March 2023 report) and over four times more likely in July 2022–June 2023 (August 2024 report).",figure:"~8x",year:"Jan 2018 to Jun 2024 data; published April 2026",disputed:false,note:"Police searches, not school; included because Child Q prompted the series.",src:[SRCQ("Press notice: falling strip searches 'masking' ongoing safeguarding failures (Children's Commissioner for England, 22 April 2026)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/news/press-notice-falling-strip-searches-masking-ongoing-safeguarding-failures-as-almost-a-third-of-children-searched-have-been-repeatedly-searched-and-eth/","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black children now almost eight times more likely to be strip searched than White children"),SRCQ("Press notice: falling strip searches 'masking' ongoing safeguarding failures (Children's Commissioner for England, 22 April 2026)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/news/press-notice-falling-strip-searches-masking-ongoing-safeguarding-failures-as-almost-a-third-of-children-searched-have-been-repeatedly-searched-and-eth/","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","today's report suggests a reversal of this trend for Black children"),SRCQ("Children's Commissioner for England, Police powers and children – strip searching and use of force (April 2026)","https://assets.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/wpuploads/2026/04/CC-police-powers-on-children-strip-search-and-use-of-force.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","almost 8 times more likely to be strip searched than White children and around 5 times more likely than Asian children"),SRCQ("Children's Commissioner for England, Police powers and children – strip searching and use of force (April 2026)","https://assets.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/wpuploads/2026/04/CC-police-powers-on-children-strip-search-and-use-of-force.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Although only 6% of the population of 10-17 year olds in the 2021 census were Black, 35% of the"),SRCQ("Children's Commissioner, Strip searching of children in England and Wales: first complete dataset for 2018–2023, including new data July 2022–June 2023 (August 2024)","https://assets.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/wpuploads/2024/08/Strip-searching-of-children-in-England-and-Wales-2024.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black children are now four times more likely to be strip searched compared to national population figures,"),SRCQ("Children's Commissioner, Strip search of children in England and Wales – analysis by the Children's Commissioner for England (March 2023)","https://assets.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/wpuploads/2023/03/cc-strip-search-of-children-in-england-and-wales.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black children in England and Wales were up to 6 times more likely to be strip searched when compared to national population")]},
  {h:"Children not in school",text:"England has legislated for local authority registers of children not in school, not yet in force when checked. No ethnicity breakdown of home-educated children or children missing education was found.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"House of Commons Library SN05108. Ethnicity of children not in school: not confirmed by the sources cited.",ed:true,src:[]}]},
 {k:"care",h:"Care and youth justice",intro:"Care and custody are CHEET states: the state holds the child. Black and Mixed children are over-represented in youth justice, and Black children are over-represented among unaccompanied asylum-seeking children in care.",points:[
  {h:"Children looked after",text:"In 2025, 81,770 children were looked after in England: 8% Black, African, Caribbean or Black British and 11% Mixed. DfE says Mixed children were over-represented.",figure:"8% Black; 11% Mixed",year:"31 March 2025",disputed:false,note:"DfE text does not state whether Black children overall are over-represented compared with the child population.",src:[SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, 2025 (DfE, Explore education statistics)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions/2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","8% Black, African, Caribbean or Black British, 5% were Asian or Asian British, 4% other ethnicities,"),SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, 2025 (DfE, Explore education statistics)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions/2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Children from Mixed ethnic groups were over-represented and children from Asian ethnic groups were under- represented")]},
  {h:"Unaccompanied children in care",text:"A third of unaccompanied asylum-seeking children looked after were of Black or Black British ethnicity.",figure:"33%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, 2025 (DfE, Explore education statistics)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions/2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","33% were of Black or Black British ethnicity, 24% of Asian or Asian British ethnicity, 3% White, 1% Mixed ethnicity")]},
  {h:"Care leavers and NEET",text:"40% of care leavers aged 19 to 21 were NEET, against about 15% of all 19 to 21 year olds (the 'care cliff').",figure:"40% v 15%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Not broken down by ethnicity in the cited text.",src:[SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, 2025 (DfE, Explore education statistics)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions/2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","40% were not in education, employment or training (NEET)")]},
  {h:"Children in custody",text:"Around 420 children were in custody at any one time. Black and Mixed children remained over-represented, at 22% and 18% of the custody population, although numbers fell.",figure:"22% Black; 18% Mixed",year:"2024/25",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black and Mixed children remained overrepresented (accounting for 22% and 18% respectively)."),SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","There was an average of around 420 children in custody at any one time during the year")]},
  {h:"Stop and search",text:"Black children were involved in 18% of stop and searches of children, 12 points above their share of 10 to 17 year olds in the 2021 Census (about 6%).",figure:"18% v ~6%",year:"year ending March 2025",disputed:false,note:"Population share derived by subtraction from the source sentence.",src:[SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black children were involved in 18% of stop and searches where ethnicity was known in the year ending March 2025."),SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","This was 12 percentage points higher than the proportion of Black 10 to 17-year-olds in the 2021 population")]},
  {h:"First-time entrants",text:"In the year ending December 2024, first-time entrants to the youth justice system were just over 8,100, but the number of Black first-time entrants rose by 8%, the first rise for this group in eight years.",figure:"+8%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","the number for Black children increased by 8%, the first year-on-year increase for this group in eight years")]},
  {h:"Black care-experienced young adults in prison",text:"Barnardo's research, carried out by a Black-led organisation, found 20 of 22 Black care-experienced young adults in prison reported racism or differential treatment.",figure:"20 of 22",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Small qualitative sample; findings are not national estimates.",src:[SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Twenty out of 22 young people disclosed experiences of racism or differential treatment")]}]},
 {k:"neet",h:"NEET and employment by ethnicity",intro:"The picture depends on age. At 16 and 17, Black young people in England are less likely than average to be NEET. Across 16 to 24, the Milburn interim report finds Black, African and Caribbean young people have the highest NEET rate of the large groups surveyed.",points:[
  {h:"Milburn interim report (2026)",text:"In 2024 Black, African and Caribbean young people had the highest NEET rate of the ethnic groups in large national surveys, at 15.2%, against 13.2% for White young people.",figure:"15.2% v 13.2%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Survey-based (Annual Population Survey); confidence intervals not stated in the text read.",src:[SRCQ("Young people and work: interim report (DWP, GOV.UK, May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/young-people-and-work-interim-report/young-people-and-work-interim-report","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","In 2024 Black, African and Caribbean young people had the highest NEET rates of all ethnic groups included in large scale national surveys, at 15.2%.")]},
  {h:"Gaps widen with age",text:"Milburn notes NEET rates for most broad groups are fairly similar, but unemployment gaps widen as people grow older, so longer-term trajectories matter.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Young people and work: interim report (DWP, GOV.UK, May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/young-people-and-work-interim-report/young-people-and-work-interim-report","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","the gap in unemployment rates widens as individuals grow older.")]},
  {h:"Older pooled estimate (2017 to 2019)",text:"Ethnicity facts and figures found 11.5% of 16 to 24 year olds NEET on average, with Black young people also at 11.5% and no reliable difference from the average.",figure:"11.5% v 11.5%",year:"2017-2019",disputed:true,note:"Differs from Milburn's 2024 figure; different periods and pooling. Treat the gap as recent and survey-based.",src:[SRCQ("Young people not in employment, education or training (NEET), 2017 to 2019 (Ethnicity facts and figures, updated 3 August 2021)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/work-pay-and-benefits/unemployment-and-economic-inactivity/young-people-not-in-employment-education-or-training-neet/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","an average of 11.5% of young people aged 16 to 24 in the UK were not in employment, education, or training (NEET)")]},
  {h:"16 and 17 year olds, England",text:"In the Department for Education's tracking of 16 and 17 year olds by councils, 1.7% of Black young people were NEET, against 3.9% of White young people and 3.4% of all. Black is a broad group here; the figures are from the latest year in the data file.",figure:"1.7% v 3.9%",year:"2026 (Dec 2025 to Feb 2026 average)",disputed:false,note:"Figures read from the department's data file; groups with more young people whose activity is not known can look different.",src:[SRCQ("NEET by characteristics: Participation in education, training and NEET age 16 to 17 (Department for Education)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/data-catalogue/data-set/a7bc111a-9503-40df-99fa-764fcb13a2e9","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","National, regional and local authority NEET estimates by (academic) age, gender, ethnic group and special educational needs and disability (SEND).")]},
  {h:"Discrimination at work",text:"In a Youth Futures Foundation survey of over 2,200 ethnic minority young people, 7 in 10 had experienced workplace or labour market discrimination.",figure:"71%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"No White comparison group in the survey.",src:[SRCQ("Survey reveals that 71% of young people from Black, Asian and minority ethnic backgrounds have experienced discrimination in the workplace (Youth Futures Foundation, 10 November 2022)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/news/survey-reveals-that-71-of-young-people-from-black-asian-and-minority-ethnic-backgrounds-have-experienced-discrimination-in-the-workplace/","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","7 in 10 (71%) young people from ethnic minority backgrounds have experienced some form of")]},
  {h:"Ethnicity as a barrier",text:"57% of young Black respondents saw their ethnicity as a barrier to career success. Black men were most concerned about its effect on their prospects.",figure:"57%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Rapid survey with young people from ethnic minorities (Youth Futures Foundation, fieldwork by Savanta, 7 November 2022)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Youth-Futures-Foundation_Savanta_Ethnic-Disparities-Survey-report_final.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","This rises to 57% of young Black people"),SRCQ("Rapid survey with young people from ethnic minorities (Youth Futures Foundation, fieldwork by Savanta, 7 November 2022)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Youth-Futures-Foundation_Savanta_Ethnic-Disparities-Survey-report_final.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black men stand out as being most concerned about how their ethnicity could affect their employment prospects.")]},
  {h:"Pandemic effect",text:"Youth Futures Foundation reports that the fall in employment during the pandemic was significantly higher for young Black and Asian people.",figure:"",year:"2020-2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Narrowing the gap: tackling ethnic disparities in youth employment (Youth Futures Foundation)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Youth-Futures-Foundation-Narrowing-the-gap-tackling-ethnic-disparities.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","the fall in employment was significantly higher for young Black and Asian people")]},
  {h:"Data gap",text:"ONS says its standard Labour Force Survey sample is too small for reliable NEET rates by ethnicity; its published figures use a three-year pooled dataset, and bespoke tables can be commissioned.",figure:"",year:"2020; 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("FOI response: NEET aged 16 to 24 by ethnicity in 2019 (ONS, 7 July 2020)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/aboutus/transparencyandgovernance/freedomofinformationfoi/youngpeoplenotinemploymenteducationortrainingneetaged16to24yearsbyethnicityin2019","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","a sufficient sample to produce reliable results")]}]},
 {k:"strengths",h:"Strengths and progress",intro:"Black young people are not only a story of risk. Many groups stay in learning longer and go on to university at higher rates than White peers, and youth justice involvement has fallen sharply over a decade.",points:[
  {h:"University entry",text:"48.0% of Black 18 year olds were accepted into higher education in 2024, up from 21.6% in 2006, against 29.8% of White 18 year olds, the lowest rate.",figure:"48.0% v 29.8%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"UCAS data via Ethnicity facts and figures; broad 'Black' group only.",src:[SRCQ("Entry rates into higher education, 2024 (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 9 July 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/higher-education/entry-rates-into-higher-education/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","the entry rate for Black people went up from 21.6% to 48.0%"),SRCQ("Entry rates into higher education, 2024 (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 9 July 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/higher-education/entry-rates-into-higher-education/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","29.8% of White people were accepted – the lowest entry rate")]},
  {h:"Staying in learning at 16 and 17",text:"Black 16 and 17 year olds in England are less than half as likely as White peers to be NEET.",figure:"1.7% v 3.9%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("NEET by characteristics: Participation in education, training and NEET age 16 to 17 (Department for Education)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/data-catalogue/data-set/a7bc111a-9503-40df-99fa-764fcb13a2e9","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","National, regional and local authority NEET estimates by (academic) age, gender, ethnic group and special educational needs and disability (SEND).")]},
  {h:"Black African pupils and exclusion",text:"Black African pupils had below-average suspension (5.54) and permanent exclusion (0.06) rates, so averages for 'Black' pupils hide large differences.",figure:"5.54; 0.06",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Values are from the Ethnicity facts and figures data tables (suspensions and permanent exclusions pages), not page text, so no quote is given.",src:[SRCQ("Pupil suspensions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 18 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/pupil-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black Caribbean pupils had a suspension rate of 13.58 (1,358 suspensions for every 10,000 pupils)")]},
  {h:"Destinations after GCSEs (older data)",text:"96% of Black African and 95% of Black pupils who finished GCSEs in 2017 went into sustained education, apprenticeships or employment, about the same as or above the 94% national average.",figure:"96% / 95% v 94%",year:"2017 leavers",disputed:false,note:"Older data; table values.",src:[SRCQ("Destinations of school pupils after GCSEs, 2017 leavers (Ethnicity facts and figures, updated 4 March 2021)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/after-education/destinations-of-school-pupils-after-key-stage-4-usually-aged-16-years/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","94% of pupils went into education, apprenticeships or employment for at least 2 terms")]},
  {h:"Confidence and enterprise",text:"Black women were among the most confident respondents about reaching their goals (82%). Young Black people were the most likely to see self-employment as a route forward.",figure:"82%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Rapid survey with young people from ethnic minorities (Youth Futures Foundation, fieldwork by Savanta, 7 November 2022)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Youth-Futures-Foundation_Savanta_Ethnic-Disparities-Survey-report_final.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","most likely to see self-employment as a way to overcome discrimination.")]},
  {h:"Fewer children in custody",text:"All ethnic groups have seen a fall in the custody population over ten years, and Black and Mixed children in custody fell on the previous year.",figure:"",year:"2024/25",disputed:false,note:"Over-representation persists despite falls.",src:[SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","While all ethnic groups have seen a decrease in the average custody population over the last 10 years"),SRCQ("Youth Justice Statistics: 2024 to 2025 (Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice, January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Despite decreases in the number of Black and Mixed children in custody compared to the previous year")]}]},
 {k:"voices",h:"Lived experience, as reported",intro:"These are findings and quotations as published by the cited reports. No new testimony was gathered. Names are left out on purpose.",points:[
  {h:"'Employers underestimate us'",text:"Youth Futures Foundation reports that more than half of young Black respondents feel employers underestimate their abilities because of their ethnic background.",figure:"55%",year:"2022",disputed:true,note:"The survey report's chart may show 59% for Black respondents; check the original before citing a number.",src:[SRCQ("Survey reveals that 71% of young people from Black, Asian and minority ethnic backgrounds have experienced discrimination in the workplace (Youth Futures Foundation, 10 November 2022)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/news/survey-reveals-that-71-of-young-people-from-black-asian-and-minority-ethnic-backgrounds-have-experienced-discrimination-in-the-workplace/","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","More than half (55%) of young Black people feel that employers underestimate their abilities")]},
  {h:"Silence after applying",text:"Young people told the Milburn review about applying for many jobs and hearing nothing back.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Not ethnicity-specific.",src:[SRCQ("Young people and work: interim report (DWP, GOV.UK, May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/young-people-and-work-interim-report/young-people-and-work-interim-report","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","I heard young people describe applying for dozens, sometimes hundreds, of jobs and hearing nothing back.")]},
  {h:"Changing social workers",text:"A young Black care-experienced adult in Barnardo's research described the effect of frequent changes of social worker.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Quoted by Barnardo's; 15 of 22 reported inconsistent social worker support.",src:[SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","I don't feel comfortable talking to him, because like you're just going to [go] one day"),SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","15 out of the 22 young people interviewed reported inconsistent support from their social workers.")]},
  {h:"Police as discipline in care",text:"Young people believed care staff often called the police unnecessarily as a disciplinary measure.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Young people's perception as reported by Barnardo's.",src:[SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","care staff often involved the police unnecessarily as a disciplinary measure")]},
  {h:"Going missing",text:"Missing People's research found Black children felt seen as less valuable or important than White children when they go missing.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Adultification and ethnicity (Missing People, 19 October 2022)","https://missingpeople.org.uk/?p=15052","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","a profound sense of Black children being seen as less valuable or important than White children when they go missing.")]},
  {h:"Get the small things right",text:"Black care-experienced young people asked services to get the small things right first.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("3 things our research discovered about Black children in care (Barnardo's, 23 October 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/blog/3-things-our-research-discovered-about-black-children-care","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black care experienced young people ask that we get the small things right first")]}]},
 {k:"help",h:"What helps",intro:"These are evidence-based or recommended approaches from What Works, statutory and charity sources. Few are tested specifically for Black young people, so treat them as promising rather than proven for this group.",points:[
  {h:"Personalised, holistic employment support",text:"Youth Futures Foundation's evidence review points to personalised, flexible provision and holistic support, plus access to learning or paid work experience.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Narrowing the gap: tackling ethnic disparities in youth employment (Youth Futures Foundation)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Youth-Futures-Foundation-Narrowing-the-gap-tackling-ethnic-disparities.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","personalised, flexible provision"),SRCQ("Narrowing the gap: tackling ethnic disparities in youth employment (Youth Futures Foundation)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Youth-Futures-Foundation-Narrowing-the-gap-tackling-ethnic-disparities.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","holistic employment support")]},
  {h:"Mentoring and coaching, well matched",text:"Mentoring has a low but positive direct effect on youth employment when part of a wider programme; the quality of the match and a length of over six months matter.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Evidence base is thin, as the toolkit says.",src:[SRCQ("Mentoring: Youth Employment Toolkit Unwrapped for Employers (Youth Futures Foundation, September 2024)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Mentoring-Toolkit-Unwrapped-1.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Mentoring has a low but positive direct impact on youth employment outcomes"),SRCQ("Mentoring: Youth Employment Toolkit Unwrapped for Employers (Youth Futures Foundation, September 2024)","https://youthfuturesfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Mentoring-Toolkit-Unwrapped-1.pdf","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","the quality of interactions and of the 'match' between mentors and mentees is important")]},
  {h:"Timely mental health support",text:"Only 4 of 22 Black care-experienced young adults in Barnardo's study were offered or accessed mental health support; Barnardo's calls for timely access.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","access to timely mental health support"),SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","just four out of 22 participants were offered, or accessed, mental health support")]},
  {h:"Black Foster Care Network and fair treatment",text:"Barnardo's recommends a Black Foster Care Network and full implementation of the Lammy Review to reduce over-criminalisation of children in care.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Recommendation, not an evaluated intervention.",src:[SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","the introduction of a Black Foster Care Network"),SRCQ("Double discrimination: Barnardo's calls for change as report highlights young Black people who have been in care (Barnardo's, 21 September 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/news/double-discrimination-barnardos-calls-change-report-highlights-young-black-people-who-have","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","full implementation of the Lammy Review to reduce the over-criminalisation of children in the care system")]},
  {h:"Black-led research and listening",text:"Barnardo's research was commissioned from a Black-led organisation so Black young people were talking to Black researchers.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("3 things our research discovered about Black children in care (Barnardo's, 23 October 2023)","https://barnardos.org.uk/blog/3-things-our-research-discovered-about-black-children-care","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","Black young people were talking to Black researchers.")]},
  {h:"Recognising adultification",text:"Inspectorate and safeguarding sources ask practitioners to recognise adultification bias as linked to racialised discrimination.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Adultification bias within child protection and safeguarding, Academic Insights (HM Inspectorate of Probation, 24 June 2022)","https://hmiprobation.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/document/adultification-bias-within-child-protection-and-safeguarding","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","highlighting adultification bias, its links to racialised discrimination")]}]},
 {k:"limits",h:"Limits of the evidence",intro:"Read the figures with care. The strongest case against this section is that it mixes ages, years, nations and data types; these limits answer that.",points:[
  {h:"Broad groups hide differences",text:"'Black' combines Black African and Black Caribbean pupils, whose exclusion rates differ a lot. Mixed White and Black Caribbean is often reported only inside 'Mixed'.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Age changes the picture",text:"At 16 and 17, Black young people in England are less likely than average to be NEET; at 16 to 24, Milburn finds the highest rate. These are different measures (council tracking v survey) and both can be true.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Do not compare the two figures directly.",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Small samples",text:"Rates for small groups, such as permanent exclusion rates, are less reliable than rates for large groups, and survey estimates for small groups need pooling over several years.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Permanent exclusions, 2022 to 2023 school year (Ethnicity facts and figures, published 11 December 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/permanent-exclusions/latest","Supports the Young lives: NEET, BEET and CHEET page","these rates are based on very small numbers of exclusions and are less reliable as a result")]},
  {h:"Dated ethnicity pages",text:"Ethnicity facts and figures exclusion pages cover 2022/23; its NEET page covers 2017 to 2019; its destinations page covers 2017 leavers. Ethnicity rates for 2024/25 exclusions are not shown here.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Disparity is not cause",text:"Higher rates show where systems treat or serve groups differently; they do not show why. Poverty, SEND, place and racism interact, and DfE notes some differences remain after other factors are taken into account.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Not confirmed by the sources cited",text:"These are not confirmed by the sources cited: the Timpson review's 'times as likely' figure; the Commission on Young Lives (2022) reports, for which no official copy was found; the full Child Q review text, as only the council's summary is cited; the ethnicity of home-educated children; and Black-specific NEET data for Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Survey design",text:"The Youth Futures Foundation survey covered ethnic minority young people only, so it cannot compare with White peers. Barnardo's study had 22 participants.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[]}]}],vant:[{who:"Young people and families",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black young people go to university at higher rates than White peers, and at 16 and 17 are less likely to be NEET.",refs:["yl-strengths-university-entry", "yl-strengths-staying-in-learning-at-16-and-17"]},{t:"Inspectors and safeguarding bodies recognise adultification bias: being treated as older, and less in need of protection, than a child is. It can be named and challenged.",refs:["yl-school-adultification", "yl-help-recognising-adultification"]}]},{who:"Practitioners: teachers, social workers, youth workers",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Black care-experienced young people ask services to get the small things right, and to keep the same social worker where possible.",refs:["yl-voices-get-the-small-things-right", "yl-voices-changing-social-workers"]},{t:"Calling the police as discipline in care feeds the youth justice figures.",refs:["yl-voices-police-as-discipline-in-care", "yl-care-black-care-experienced-young-adults-in-p"]}]},{who:"Employers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"More than half of young Black respondents feel employers underestimate them. Reply to applicants, and offer well-matched mentoring.",refs:["yl-voices-employers-underestimate-us", "yl-voices-silence-after-applying", "yl-help-mentoring-and-coaching-well-matched"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Focus on departure events: suspension, exclusion, leaving care and custody are where the differences show.",refs:["yl-terms-departure-events", "yl-school-suspensions-mixed-white-and-black-caribb", "yl-care-care-leavers-and-neet", "yl-care-children-in-custody"]},{t:"Fund timely mental health support, and personalised, holistic help into work.",refs:["yl-help-timely-mental-health-support", "yl-help-personalised-holistic-employment-support"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Report Black Caribbean, Black African and Mixed White and Black Caribbean separately: a single 'Black' average hides the differences.",refs:["yl-limits-broad-groups-hide-differences", "yl-strengths-black-african-pupils-and-exclusion"]},{t:"State the age range and the measure: council tracking at 16 and 17 and surveys at 16 to 24 give different pictures.",refs:["yl-limits-age-changes-the-picture", "yl-neet-milburn-interim-report-2026", "yl-neet-16-and-17-year-olds-england"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"BEET, CHEET and NEET describe where a young person is held, lost or unseen by the system.",refs:["yl-terms-beet", "yl-terms-cheet", "yl-terms-neet"]},{t:"Black 18 year olds go to university at higher rates than White 18 year olds.",refs:["yl-strengths-university-entry"]},{t:"Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean pupils are suspended more often than average.",refs:["yl-school-suspensions-mixed-white-and-black-caribb", "yl-school-suspensions-black-caribbean"]},{t:"Black and Mixed children are over-represented among children in custody.",refs:["yl-care-children-in-custody"]},{t:"Young people ask services to get the small things right.",refs:["yl-voices-get-the-small-things-right"]}]}]};
LP["lenses"]={name:"Through other lenses",pfx:"ln",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"ai",h:"Responsible AI and data use",intro:"Who algorithms see, misjudge or miss, and the duties that apply",points:[
  {h:"Face analysis was least accurate for darker-skinned women",text:"The Gender Shades study tested three commercial gender-classification systems. Error rates reached 34.7% for darker-skinned women, compared with no more than 0.8% for lighter-skinned men. The training and benchmark datasets were mostly lighter-skinned faces.",figure:"Up to 34.7% error (darker-skinned women) vs at most 0.8% (lighter-skinned men)",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"This study tested gender classification, not identity matching. It shows bias in commercial systems at that time, not in today's products.",src:[SRCQ("Gender Shades: Intersectional Accuracy Disparities in Commercial Gender Classification (PMLR 81, 2018)","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a.html","Supports the Through other lenses page","darker-skinned females are the most misclassified group (with error rates of up to 34.7%)."),SRCQ("Gender Shades (PMLR 81, 2018)","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a.html","Supports the Through other lenses page","The maximum error rate for lighter-skinned males is 0.8%."),SRCQ("MIT News: Study finds gender and skin-type bias in commercial artificial-intelligence systems (11 Feb 2018)","https://news.mit.edu/2018/study-finds-gender-skin-type-bias-artificial-intelligence-systems-0212","Supports the Through other lenses page","the error rates ballooned — to more than 20 percent in one case and more than 34 percent in the other two.")],view:"Citizen on the receiving end of the technology. The study also shows a Black woman researcher setting the evidence agenda, which is relevant to careers and leadership.",who:"Black women and other darker-skinned women; anyone checked by face-analysis tools",themes:["belonging", "careers", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"A US government test found large demographic differences across face recognition algorithms",text:"In 2019 the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) tested 189 algorithms from 99 developers. Many produced more false positives for Asian and African American faces than for white faces, often by 10 to 100 times. Results varied widely between algorithms.",figure:"False-positive differences often 10 to 100 times; 189 algorithms tested",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"The source is a US study using US image sets. The news release also says the most accurate algorithms showed much smaller differences.",src:[SRCQ("NIST: NIST Study Evaluates Effects of Race, Age, Sex on Face Recognition Software (19 Dec 2019)","https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2019/12/nist-study-evaluates-effects-race-age-sex-face-recognition-software","Supports the Through other lenses page","higher rates of false positives for Asian and African American faces relative to images of Caucasians"),SRCQ("NIST news release (19 Dec 2019)","https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2019/12/nist-study-evaluates-effects-race-age-sex-face-recognition-software","Supports the Through other lenses page","The differentials often ranged from a factor of 10 to 100 times, depending on the individual algorithm."),SRCQ("NIST news release (19 Dec 2019)","https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2019/12/nist-study-evaluates-effects-race-age-sex-face-recognition-software","Supports the Through other lenses page","higher rates of false positives for African American females")],view:"Data scientist and buyer: the bias depends on which algorithm is bought, so a buyer who tests and chooses carefully can reduce it.",who:"Black and Asian people; African American women in one-to-many searches",themes:["diaspora", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"The Court of Appeal found South Wales Police had not met the equality duty on facial recognition",text:"In R (Bridges) v Chief Constable of South Wales Police, judgment handed down on 11 August 2020, the Court of Appeal found the force had not complied with the Public Sector Equality Duty. It had not checked whether the software was biased by race or sex. The court did not find that the software was in fact biased.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Only the judgment's cover page was readable on judiciary.uk, so the paragraph [199] wording is quoted from a barrister's case note. It should be checked against the full judgment before publication.",src:[SRCQ("Judiciary UK: R (Bridges) v CC South Wales","https://www.judiciary.uk/judgments/r-bridges-v-cc-south-wales/","Supports the Through other lenses page","The date and time of handing down shall be deemed for all purposes to be 10:00am on Tuesday 11 August 2020."),SRCQ("Inforrm case note: R (Bridges) v Chief Constable of South Wales Police (17 Aug 2020)","https://inforrm.org/2020/08/17/case-law-r-on-the-application-of-bridges-v-chief-constable-of-south-wales-police-use-of-automatic-facial-recognition-technology-unlawful-hugh-tomlinson-qc/","Supports the Through other lenses page","never sought to satisfy themselves that the software program did not have an unacceptable bias on the grounds of race or sex"),SRCQ("Law Gazette: Court of Appeal warns of race bias in facial recognition technology","https://www.lawgazette.co.uk/news/court-of-appeal-warns-of-race-bias-in-facial-recognition-technology/5105315.article","Supports the Through other lenses page","South Wales Police did not comply with the public sector equality duty (PSED)."),SRCQ("R (Bridges) v Chief Constable of South Wales Police, Court of Appeal judgment (2020)","https://www.judiciary.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/R-Bridges-v-CC-South-Wales-ors-Judgment.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","The Respondent did not comply with the Public Sector Equality Duty in section 149 of the Equality Act 2010"),SRCQ("R (Bridges) v Chief Constable of South Wales Police, Court of Appeal judgment (2020)","https://www.judiciary.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/R-Bridges-v-CC-South-Wales-ors-Judgment.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","SWP have never sought to satisfy themselves, either directly or by way of independent verification, that the software program in this case does not have an unacceptable bias on grounds of race or sex.")],view:"Civil servant building services: the duty is to check for bias before and during use, not to wait until bias is proven.",who:"Black and minority ethnic people and women in public spaces where live facial recognition is used",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Police live facial recognition: fairness depends on the setting used",text:"Testing by the National Physical Laboratory for the Metropolitan Police and South Wales Police (2023) found that the algorithm can be run at settings where there is no statistically significant difference in performance between demographic groups. At lower settings, differences between groups appear.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:true,note:"Disputed. Civil liberties group Big Brother Watch said the report confirms live facial recognition has significant race and sex biases (as reported by The Register). The full report is not cited here, so the exact thresholds are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("College of Policing: Operational testing of facial recognition technology","https://science.police.uk/research/resources/operational-testing-of-facial-recognition-technology/","Supports the Through other lenses page","There are settings our algorithm can be operated at where there is no statistical significance"),SRCQ("The Register: UK police facial recognition (6 Apr 2023)","https://www.theregister.com/2023/04/06/uk_police_facial_recognition/","Supports the Through other lenses page","confirms that live facial recognition does have significant race and sex biases")],view:"Leader and operator: the fairness of the system depends on how it is configured. The threshold is a policy decision as well as a technical one.",who:"Black Londoners and others scanned at live facial recognition deployments",themes:["diaspora", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"The rules on police facial recognition are being rewritten",text:"On 4 December 2025 the Home Office launched a 10-week consultation on a new legal framework for law enforcement use of biometrics and facial recognition.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The ministerial statement does not mention bias or fairness.",src:[SRCQ("UK Parliament written statement HLWS1128 (4 Dec 2025)","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/1860229","Supports the Through other lenses page","Consultation on a new legal framework for law enforcement use of biometrics, facial recognition and similar technologies")],view:"Citizen and staff network: consultations are a formal way for communities to have a say.",who:"Communities with more police contact, including Black communities",themes:["knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"A visa-streaming algorithm that used nationality was withdrawn under legal challenge",text:"A Home Office tool risk-scored visa applications partly by nationality. After a legal challenge by the Joint Council for the Welfare of Immigrants (JCWI), supported by Foxglove, the Home Office agreed to stop using it from 7 August 2020 and to redesign it.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:true,note:"The source is a specialist immigration law publisher; no GOV.UK or Parliament source is cited. Which nationalities were affected, and any racial effect, are not confirmed by the sources cited. Campaigners called the tool racist; the Home Office did not accept that.",src:[SRCQ("Free Movement: Government to redesign controversial visa algorithm (4 Aug 2020)","https://freemovement.org.uk/government-to-redesign-controversial-visa-algorithm","Supports the Through other lenses page","scored visa applicants for risk based in part on their nationality"),SRCQ("Free Movement (4 Aug 2020)","https://freemovement.org.uk/government-to-redesign-controversial-visa-algorithm","Supports the Through other lenses page","Earlier this year JCWI, with the help of Foxglove, launched a legal challenge")],view:"Diaspora family member and sponsor: the tool affected families sponsoring relatives and visitors from Africa and the Caribbean.",who:"Visa applicants from countries scored as higher risk, including many African and Caribbean nationalities. Their UK-based families, sponsors and employers were also affected.",themes:["africa", "diaspora", "enterprise", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"The 2020 A-level algorithm: no sourced ethnicity effect",text:"In August 2020 Ofqual reported that its equalities analysis, which included ethnicity, found no evidence that the grade-awarding process had introduced systemic bias. No source found shows an ethnicity-specific effect from the algorithm.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:true,note:"Any ethnicity effect is not confirmed by the sources cited. The concern at the time was mainly about disadvantage by school and socio-economic background. The later reversal of the algorithm's grades is not covered by the sources cited here.",src:[SRCQ("Ofqual / GOV.UK: Guide to AS and A level results for England, 2020 (13 Aug 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/guide-to-as-and-a-level-results-for-england-2020","Supports the Through other lenses page","The analyses conducted shows no evidence that this year’s process of awarding grades has introduced any systemic bias.")],view:"Young person and parent: what a regulator's own analysis found, compared with what people believed at the time.",who:"Students who sat A levels in 2020, including Black students",themes:["knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Algorithms learn yesterday's decisions, and the data to check for bias is often missing",text:"The CDEI review (November 2020) covered recruitment, financial services, policing and local government. It found that algorithms trained on past decisions can encode existing bias, and that data on protected characteristics is often not available to monitor outcomes. It recommended a mandatory transparency obligation for the public sector.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The CDEI is now the Responsible Technology Adoption Unit.",src:[SRCQ("CDEI review into bias in algorithmic decision-making (27 Nov 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cdei-publishes-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making/main-report-cdei-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making","Supports the Through other lenses page","The introduction of algorithms can encode existing biases into algorithms, if they are trained from existing decisions."),SRCQ("CDEI review (27 Nov 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cdei-publishes-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making/main-report-cdei-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making","Supports the Through other lenses page","Data is needed to monitor outcomes and identify bias, but data on protected characteristics is not available often enough.")],view:"Data scientist and civil servant: collecting good ethnicity data is a precondition for fairness, not a threat to it.",who:"Groups over-represented in historical policing, credit and local-service records, which often include Black communities",themes:["careers", "enterprise", "knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Pulse oximeters overestimate oxygen levels for people with darker skin",text:"The independent review on equity in medical devices, chaired by Professor Dame Margaret Whitehead and published on 11 March 2024, found that pulse oximeters overestimate true oxygen levels in people with darker skin. This can delay treatment. The review also warned of bias in AI-enabled medical devices.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Pulse oximeters are not AI. They are included because they show how biased data and design harm people physically. The summary page's AI warning names women; it describes the risks to ethnic minority groups only in general terms.",src:[SRCQ("DHSC: Equity in medical devices: independent review, summary report (11 Mar 2024)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/equity-in-medical-devices-independent-review-final-report/equity-in-medical-devices-independent-review-summary-report","Supports the Through other lenses page","Pulse oximeters overestimate true oxygen levels in people with darker skin tones."),SRCQ("DHSC: Equity in medical devices summary report (11 Mar 2024)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/equity-in-medical-devices-independent-review-final-report/equity-in-medical-devices-independent-review-summary-report","Supports the Through other lenses page","inherent bias against certain groups in the population, notably women")],view:"Patient and community health: bias in AI sits within a longer history of devices designed and tested on lighter skin.",who:"Black, Asian and other darker-skinned patients; women and disadvantaged groups for AI devices",themes:["diaspora", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"The equality duty applies from the moment a public body considers using AI",text:"The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) says the Public Sector Equality Duty applies from the start, when a public body is deciding whether to use AI. It also applies to AI already in use and to systems that others develop. Northern Ireland has its own duty under section 75, and Scotland and Wales have specific duties that cover AI procurement.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("EHRC: Artificial intelligence: meeting the Public Sector Equality Duty (1 Sep 2022)","https://www.equalityhumanrights.com/guidance/artificial-intelligence-meeting-public-sector-equality-duty-psed","Supports the Through other lenses page","Public bodies need to consider the PSED from the very start when thinking about whether to use AI."),SRCQ("EHRC PSED and AI guidance (1 Sep 2022)","https://www.equalityhumanrights.com/guidance/artificial-intelligence-meeting-public-sector-equality-duty-psed","Supports the Through other lenses page","The PSED also applies to any AI systems that public bodies are already using or that others may be developing.")],view:"Civil servant and leader: this is a legal duty, not an optional extra, and it covers suppliers' systems too.",who:"Everyone with protected characteristics, including race, who uses public services",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Communities can look up the algorithmic tools government uses",text:"The Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard (ATRS) is mandatory for central government. It applies to algorithmic tools that significantly influence decisions with public effect, or that interact directly with the public. Few records had been published by January 2025, according to the Committee of Public Accounts (Public Accounts Committee).",figure:"33 ATRS records published as at January 2025 (Committee of Public Accounts)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"The record count changes over time. Read the live count on the ATRS hub at the point of use.",src:[SRCQ("ATRS mandatory scope and exemptions policy (17 Dec 2024)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-mandatory-scope-and-exemptions-policy/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-atrs-mandatory-scope-and-exemptions-policy","Supports the Through other lenses page","The mandatory requirement to complete ATRS records currently applies to central government."),SRCQ("ATRS mandatory scope and exemptions policy (17 Dec 2024)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-mandatory-scope-and-exemptions-policy/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-atrs-mandatory-scope-and-exemptions-policy","Supports the Through other lenses page","have a significant influence on a decision-making process with public effect, or"),SRCQ("Public Accounts Committee report HC 356","https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm5901/cmselect/cmpubacc/356/report.html","Supports the Through other lenses page","at January 2025, only 33 records had been published on the ATRS website.")],view:"Citizen and staff network: a public record to cite when asking how a decision affecting your community was made.",who:"Any community affected by automated or AI-assisted decisions",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Black people are slightly under-represented among IT specialists",text:"BCS analysis of the ONS Labour Force Survey for 2023 shows people of Black, African, Caribbean or Black British ethnicity making up 3% of IT specialists, compared with 4% of all occupations. Ethnic minority people overall made up 21% of IT specialists.",figure:"3% of IT specialists vs 4% of all occupations (2023)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"This is not an official statistics release. It is a professional body's analysis of ONS survey data. No official figure for Black people in AI-specific roles was found, so that is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("BCS Diversity Report 2024: Ethnicity and IT employment","https://www.bcs.org/policy-and-influence/equity-diversity-and-inclusion/bcs-diversity-report-2024-ethnicity/ethnicity-and-it-employment/","Supports the Through other lenses page","Analysis of ONS Quarterly Labour Force Survey by BCS"),SRCQ("BCS Diversity Report 2024: Ethnicity and IT employment","https://www.bcs.org/policy-and-influence/equity-diversity-and-inclusion/bcs-diversity-report-2024-ethnicity/ethnicity-and-it-employment/","Supports the Through other lenses page","representing 21% of all IT specialists in the UK at that time")],view:"Careers: who builds AI shapes whose faces and voices it is tested on.",who:"Black people working in, or aiming for, tech and AI careers",themes:["careers", "enterprise", "knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Africa's own AI strategy puts development, languages and heritage first",text:"The African Union Executive Council endorsed a Continental AI Strategy in Accra in July 2024. It describes an Africa-centric, development-focused approach and says AI must reflect Africa's languages and cultural heritage. The Ethics Atlas sets out a 'Global South reading' of shared AI ethics words. In that reading, wellbeing means development, inclusion and closing the AI divide, and control means sovereign control of one's own data and infrastructure.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"The Ethics Atlas labels its African Union row S (found in search, primary not opened). Its reference to Ubuntu ethics is labelled K (unsourced background) and is not used here.",src:[SRCQ("African Union press release: AU committed to developing AI capabilities in Africa (28 Aug 2024)","https://au.int/en/pressreleases/20240828/african-union-committed-developing-ai-capabilities-africa","Supports the Through other lenses page","an Africa-centric, development-focused approach to AI"),SRCQ("African Union press release (28 Aug 2024)","https://au.int/en/pressreleases/20240828/african-union-committed-developing-ai-capabilities-africa","Supports the Through other lenses page","AI systems must be adapted to local realities, reflecting Africa's diversity, languages, cultural heritage")],view:"African and diaspora perspective: Africa sets its own AI agenda and is not only a subject of Western debates about AI ethics.",who:"African nations and the African diaspora in the UK",themes:["africa", "belonging", "diaspora", "enterprise", "language"]},
  {h:"Who ethics frameworks protect: colonial exemptions and missing frameworks",text:"The Ethics Atlas notes that every AI ethics framework it found limits 'whose wellbeing' to the state that wrote it. Its history of war ethics records that Grotius's law of war between states left colonial wars outside it. It also records an Algerian statement at the UN Security Council that the AI divide is about sovereignty itself. It flags that public ethics frameworks are absent in Sudan and the Sahel, which it describes as where most deaths occur.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"These are low-confidence research labels. The colonial-exemption row is K (standard history, unsourced). The Algeria quote is S (UN). The Sudan and Sahel line is marked 'to verify'. These points should not be presented as established facts until they are sourced.",ed:true,src:[],view:"Historian: AI ethics continues an older pattern of deciding whose rights count.",who:"Formerly colonised peoples; communities in African conflict zones",themes:["africa", "diaspora", "history"]}]},
 {k:"data",h:"Data, evidence and being counted",intro:"Whether a community is counted, how, and who is left out",points:[
  {h:"Government's Standards for Ethnicity Data (2023)",text:"Following a public consultation, the government published Standards for Ethnicity Data on 17 April 2023. This was Inclusive Britain action 6. They set out best practice for collecting, analysing and reporting ethnicity data in government and public bodies. Data collected for the whole UK should use the GSS harmonised standard. The standards also point to RDU style guidance, which avoids terms such as 'BAME'.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Following the standards is good practice, not a legal requirement.",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Through other lenses page","They describe best practice when collecting, analysing and reporting ethnicity data."),SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Through other lenses page","If you are collecting data for the whole of the UK, you should use the UK harmonised standard."),SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Through other lenses page","words and phrases RDU avoids, such as 'BAME'"),SRCQ("Inclusive Britain action plan: updates (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/inclusive-britain-action-plan-updates","Supports the Through other lenses page","new standards for ethnicity data published, following a public consultation")],view:"The official rulebook for how the state records and describes ethnicity.",who:"Black, African, Caribbean and Mixed people, and every other ethnic group described in public sector data",themes:["belonging", "knowledge", "language"]},
  {h:"Census 2021: African, Caribbean and Mixed options, with write-ins",text:"The 2021 Census in England and Wales offered 19 tick-box responses. Under 'Black, Black British, Caribbean or African' these were Caribbean, African and a write-in 'Any other' option. Under Mixed they included White and Black Caribbean and White and Black African. In Wales the heading also included 'Black Welsh'. The Black African group grew from 1.8% (990,000) in 2011 to 2.5% (1.5 million) in 2021.",figure:"Black African: 2.5% (1.5 million) in 2021, up from 1.8% (990,000) in 2011",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Detailed write-in results (for example, single countries or peoples of origin) are not confirmed by the sources cited here. The Ethnicity facts and figures page lists 19 categories, but its page description says 18. Scotland and Northern Ireland use different questions.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Through other lenses page","Respondents could choose one out of 19 tick-box response categories, including write-in response options."),SRCQ("List of ethnic groups (Ethnicity facts and figures)","https://ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/ethnic-groups","Supports the Through other lenses page","Any other Black, Black British, or Caribbean background"),SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Through other lenses page","both ethnic groups had the option to write in")],view:"Self-identification: the Census asks people to describe themselves, including in their own words.",who:"African, Caribbean, Black British, Black Welsh and Mixed-heritage people in England and Wales",themes:["africa", "belonging", "diaspora", "history", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Government stopped using 'BAME'",text:"The Commission on Race and Ethnic Disparities recommended dropping the term 'BAME', and the Inclusive Britain plan (March 2022, 74 actions) committed government to stop using it. The Equality Hub explained that grouping all ethnic minorities together can hide large differences in outcomes. It also said many ethnic minority people dislike the term. Government now uses specific Census groups where it can.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Some staff networks and organisations outside government still use the term, and preferences differ. The hub should explain why it uses the terms it does, rather than enforce one.",src:[SRCQ("Why we no longer use the term 'BAME' in government (Equality Hub blog)","https://equalities.blog.gov.uk/2022/04/07/why-we-no-longer-use-the-term-bame-in-government/","Supports the Through other lenses page","This can disguise huge differences in outcomes between ethnic groups."),SRCQ("Why we no longer use the term 'BAME' in government (Equality Hub blog)","https://equalities.blog.gov.uk/2022/04/07/why-we-no-longer-use-the-term-bame-in-government/","Supports the Through other lenses page","many ethnic minorities themselves say they do not like the term 'BAME'"),SRCQ("Inclusive Britain action plan: updates (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/inclusive-britain-action-plan-updates","Supports the Through other lenses page","Inclusive Britain was published in March 2022. It sets out 74 actions")],view:"Language view: a label that lumps groups together also lumps their outcomes together.",who:"Black, African, Caribbean, Mixed and all other ethnic minority people who were grouped together as 'BAME'",themes:["belonging", "knowledge", "language"]},
  {h:"The Commission on Race and Ethnic Disparities (2021) and the dispute about it",text:"The Commission reported in March 2021. Its foreword said it no longer saw a Britain where the system is deliberately rigged against ethnic minorities. UN human rights experts rejected the report, saying it distorted historic facts and misused data. The government's Inclusive Britain response followed in 2022.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:true,note:"Both the Commission's findings and the criticism of it are cited. The hub should present both and should not adopt either side's conclusion.",src:[SRCQ("Commission on Race and Ethnic Disparities: foreword, introduction and full recommendations (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-report-of-the-commission-on-race-and-ethnic-disparities/foreword-introduction-and-full-recommendations","Supports the Through other lenses page","Put simply we no longer see a Britain where the system is deliberately rigged against ethnic minorities."),SRCQ("UN OHCHR blog: UN experts condemn reprehensible racism report (Equally Ours)","https://www.equallyours.org.uk/un-ohcr-blog-un-experts-condemn-reprehensible-racism-report","Supports the Through other lenses page","saying the review further distorted and falsified historic facts"),SRCQ("UN OHCHR blog: UN experts condemn reprehensible racism report (Equally Ours)","https://www.equallyours.org.uk/un-ohcr-blog-un-experts-condemn-reprehensible-racism-report","Supports the Through other lenses page","twisting data and misapplying statistics")],view:"Contested evidence: the same data can lead to very different conclusions about racism.",who:"People of African descent and all ethnic minority communities in the UK",themes:["belonging", "history", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"The Inclusive Data Taskforce: everyone counts and is counted",text:"The Inclusive Data Taskforce reported in September 2021, with 46 recommendations under 8 Inclusive Data Principles. The National Statistician's response and the January 2022 implementation plan aim for everyone to count and be counted in UK data. This includes work to build trust with the people who supply data.",figure:"46 recommendations, 8 principles",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"How far the recommendations have been delivered by 2026 is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Inclusive Data Taskforce implementation plan (UK Statistics Authority)","https://uksa.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/publication/inclusive-data-taskforce-implementation-plan/","Supports the Through other lenses page","The report made 46 recommendations categorised under 8 Inclusive Data Principles."),SRCQ("Inclusive Data Taskforce implementation plan (UK Statistics Authority)","https://uksa.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/publication/inclusive-data-taskforce-implementation-plan/","Supports the Through other lenses page","so that everyone counts and is counted in UK data and evidence")],view:"The statistical system's own commitment to inclusion.",who:"Groups that are under-represented in UK data and evidence, including ethnic minority communities",themes:["knowledge"]},
  {h:"Ethnicity in NHS records often differs from the Census",text:"In May 2024 the ONS compared ethnicity in four health data sources with what people told the 2021 Census. Black African was among the groups with the highest agreement. Mixed groups and 'Other' groups had the lowest. In hospital and talking-therapy records, the 'Other Black' category agreed less than 20% of the time.",figure:"'Other Black' agreement under 20% (Hospital Episode Statistics and Talking Therapies)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Covers England only. The ONS gives agreement for each data source and group, not a single overall rate.",src:[SRCQ("Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","the ethnic groups with the lowest level of agreement are the Mixed groups, Other groups"),SRCQ("Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","Other Black (less than 20%)")],view:"Data quality view: the record can say something different from what the person says about themselves.",who:"Mixed-heritage people and people recorded as 'Other Black' or 'Any other ethnic group', whose health statistics may be unreliable as a result",themes:["belonging", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"NHS Race and Health Observatory: poor ethnicity monitoring data",text:"In February 2022 a rapid evidence review of ethnic inequalities in healthcare, funded by the NHS Race and Health Observatory, found large inequalities across health services. One of its authors pointed to years of poor-quality ethnic monitoring data in NHS systems.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The source is a university news release about the review, not the review itself. When the Observatory was founded is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Review highlights stark ethnic healthcare inequalities in the UK (University of Manchester)","https://www.manchester.ac.uk/about/news/review-highlights-stark-ethnic-healthcare-inequalities-in-the-uk/","Supports the Through other lenses page","The review, funded by the NHS Race and Health Observatory"),SRCQ("Review highlights stark ethnic healthcare inequalities in the UK (University of Manchester)","https://www.manchester.ac.uk/about/news/review-highlights-stark-ethnic-healthcare-inequalities-in-the-uk/","Supports the Through other lenses page","a lack of high-quality ethnic monitoring data recorded in NHS systems")],view:"Health equity view: without good data, inequalities cannot be seen or fixed.",who:"Black, Asian and other ethnic minority patients and NHS staff",themes:["knowledge"]},
  {h:"Black people are under-represented among survey respondents",text:"The ONS says ethnic minority groups tend to be less likely to take part in government surveys. On its Opinions and Lifestyle Survey, Asian, Black and Mixed groups were under-represented compared with population estimates (as of April 2025). The ONS tested a targeted £10 incentive to improve response and representativeness.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The ONS page was last revised on 27 July 2026. Under-coverage in other named surveys, such as the Labour Force Survey, is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Targeted incentives on the Opinions and Lifestyle Survey (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthandlifeexpectancies/methodologies/targetedincentivesontheopinionsandlifestylesurvey","Supports the Through other lenses page","ethnic minority groups, tend to be less likely to participate"),SRCQ("Targeted incentives on the Opinions and Lifestyle Survey (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthandlifeexpectancies/methodologies/targetedincentivesontheopinionsandlifestylesurvey","Supports the Through other lenses page","ethnic minority groups (including Asian, Black and Mixed) are underrepresented")],view:"Coverage view: when fewer people respond, the estimates for their group are less certain.",who:"Black, Asian and Mixed people, and especially young people aged 16 to 29",themes:["knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Trust, willingness and fear of discrimination in sharing health data",text:"Research for Understanding Patient Data found that many Black and South Asian people have low trust in the NHS and worry about how their data is protected. Respondents from all ethnic groups were willing to share most types of personal data with the NHS. Black and South Asian respondents were more concerned that sharing data could lead to discrimination. Suggested reasons for gaps in ethnicity data include structural racism, lack of training and mistrust.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The findings are mixed: low trust in the NHS sits alongside willingness to share. The Ada Lovelace Institute is not covered here. Any finding attributed to it is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Bringing together our work on equitable data collection with Black and South Asian communities (Understanding Patient Data)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/node/311","Supports the Through other lenses page","Many Black and South Asian people have low levels of trust in the NHS"),SRCQ("Insights from Black & South Asian people on patient data (Understanding Patient Data)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/node/295","Supports the Through other lenses page","Respondents from all ethnic groups were willing to share most types of personal data with the NHS."),SRCQ("Insights from Black & South Asian people on patient data (Understanding Patient Data)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/node/295","Supports the Through other lenses page","greater concern for the potential for discrimination as a result of sharing their data")],view:"Lived experience: willingness to share depends on believing the data will be used fairly.",who:"Black and South Asian patients and communities in England",themes:["belonging", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Ethnicity facts and figures: government's public evidence base",text:"The Race Disparity Audit was published in October 2017. Its data is kept up to date on the GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures service, built by the Race Disparity Unit. The service brings together government data on how different ethnic groups experience public services.",figure:"",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"The page does not give a launch date for the website itself.",src:[SRCQ("Background (Ethnicity facts and figures)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/background","Supports the Through other lenses page","The Race Disparity Audit was published in October 2017."),SRCQ("Background (Ethnicity facts and figures)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/background","Supports the Through other lenses page","Data from the audit is continually updated on the Ethnicity facts and figures website.")],view:"An open, reusable evidence base the hub can link to instead of copying.",who:"All ethnic groups. Staff networks and leaders use it to evidence disparities",themes:["careers", "knowledge", "younglives"]}]},
 {k:"hist",h:"History, reparation, recognition and pay",intro:"From the 1833 compensation to honours and pay today",points:[
  {h:"Start from a fixed point, with no hierarchy of blame or suffering",text:"The ledger rests on numbered principles. Its fixed point is that human beings were made property and nothing excuses it. Every actor (European, African, Arab, Ottoman and others) is held to one standard of evidence; complicity explains scale but never moves responsibility. The gravity of enslavement is not ranked against other atrocities, and harm and achievement are held together, so the record does not define a continent or a people.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",ed:true,src:[],view:"A principled, non-partisan footing for BHM content that pre-empts both denial and 'whataboutery' while keeping dignity central.",who:"Descendants of enslaved Africans in the Caribbean, the Americas and the UK; African and diaspora communities; all readers, including descendants of enslavers",themes:["africa", "belonging", "diaspora", "history"]},
  {h:"Language with dignity: ‘enslaved people’, not ‘slaves’",text:"The ledger names people as people: 'enslaved people', not 'slaves'; 'enslavers', not 'owners'. Euphemisms are replaced with what happened, slurs survive only inside titles and quotations, and ancestors are named before their remains. Government's own style guide does not use 'BAME' or 'BME', because the terms hide differences between groups. The hub can adopt both rules and enforce them with a language check.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The ledger records that its language was revised after four independent sensitivity reads and a second editor.",src:[SRCQ("Writing about ethnicity — Ethnicity facts and figures style guide","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Through other lenses page","We do not use the terms BAME (Black, Asian and minority ethnic) and BME (Black and minority ethnic)")],view:"Language is a design rule with a check behind it, not just a style preference.",who:"Black, African, Caribbean and Mixed communities; content authors across government",themes:["belonging", "knowledge", "language"]},
  {h:"Abolition paid £20 million to enslavers, not to the enslaved",text:"The Slavery Abolition Act 1833 funded £20 million of compensation to enslavers. The enslaved received nothing and were forced into unpaid 'apprenticeship'. The UCL Legacies of British Slavery database records the compensation claims, with enslavers, estates and later investments, and is the authoritative public source.",figure:"£20 million",year:"1833",disputed:false,note:"The ledger says 'about 46,000 claims' and 'about 40% of 1833 government spending'. Neither figure is confirmed by the sources cited here; the UCL page gives 'more than forty thousand slave-owners' (a later phase added another twenty thousand).",src:[SRCQ("Legacies of British Slave-ownership — UCL Centre for the Study of the Legacies of British Slavery","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social-historical-sciences/history/research/research-projects-and-centres/centre-study-legacies-british-slavery-cslbs/cslbs-projects-and-partners/legacies-british-slave-ownership","Supports the Through other lenses page","parliament granted £20 million in compensation to former slave-owners"),SRCQ("Legacies of British Slave-ownership — UCL Centre for the Study of the Legacies of British Slavery","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social-historical-sciences/history/research/research-projects-and-centres/centre-study-legacies-british-slavery-cslbs/cslbs-projects-and-partners/legacies-british-slave-ownership","Supports the Through other lenses page","more than forty thousand slave-owners in the British Caribbean, Mauritius or the Cape"),SRCQ("HM Treasury FOI2018/00186: Slavery Abolition Act 1833","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/680456/FOI2018-00186_-_Slavery_Abolition_Act_1833_-_pdf_for_disclosure_log__003_.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","The Government used £20 million to fund the Slavery Abolition Act 1833.")],view:"Shows the state put a price on freedom and paid it to the enslavers, which is the core of today's reparations arguments.",who:"Descendants of people enslaved in the British Caribbean, Mauritius and the Cape; Caribbean-heritage communities in the UK",themes:["diaspora", "equity", "history", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"The abolition loan was finally repaid in 2015, but the sum is unknown",text:"Money borrowed in 1835 to fund the compensation was rolled into other government debt and the last of it was redeemed in 2015. The Treasury has no record of how much of the original loan was still outstanding when it was consolidated in 1927, so the amount repaid in 2015 cannot be stated. Avoid claims that taxpayers 'paid off' a specific sum.",figure:"Repaid 2015; amount not known",year:"2015",disputed:true,note:"The fact of 2015 repayment is confirmed; the amount repaid is unknown, and popular claims often overstate it.",src:[SRCQ("HM Treasury FOI2018/00186: Slavery Abolition Act 1833","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/680456/FOI2018-00186_-_Slavery_Abolition_Act_1833_-_pdf_for_disclosure_log__003_.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","Money borrowed to fund the Slavery Abolition Act (1835) was therefore fully repaid in 2015."),SRCQ("Full Fact: the government loan to pay slave owners compensation after 1833","https://fullfact.org/economy/slavery-abolition-act-loan/","Supports the Through other lenses page","Therefore, it's not known how much of this loan was repaid in 2015.")],view:"A precise, myth-proof version of a widely shared fact.",who:"UK taxpayers today, including Black British taxpayers whose ancestors were enslaved",themes:["equity", "history", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"CARICOM's Ten Point Plan for Reparatory Justice",text:"The CARICOM Reparations Commission's plan has ten points: full formal apology, repatriation, an Indigenous peoples development programme, cultural institutions, the public health crisis, illiteracy eradication, an African knowledge programme, psychological rehabilitation, technology transfer and debt cancellation. The ledger books it as a claim with no monetary amount and nothing paid, and records that heads of government have approved a revised plan.",figure:"",year:"2014",disputed:true,note:"The ten headings are confirmed on caricom.org. The 2014 approval date is from the ledger (it cites a Leigh Day press release of 11 March 2014). The revised plan and the September 2026 Barbados conference are from the ledger only and are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("CARICOM Ten Point Plan for Reparatory Justice (caricom.org)","https://caricom.org/?p=19846","Supports the Through other lenses page","1. FULL FORMAL APOLOGY"),SRCQ("CARICOM Ten Point Plan for Reparatory Justice (caricom.org)","https://caricom.org/?p=19846","Supports the Through other lenses page","10. DEBT CANCELLATION")],view:"Reparation defined as development, health, education and dignity, not only cash.",who:"Caribbean nations and their diaspora in the UK",themes:["africa", "diaspora", "history"]},
  {h:"Where the UK state stands, and what is live in 2026",text:"The ledger records the UK government's position as no reparations: it says the Prime Minister in October 2024 preferred to work on 'future-facing challenges'. It also tracks live moves, including Jamaica's petition of 7 September 2026 asking the King to refer three questions on slavery to the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council, without a money claim, and the African Union's Decade of Justice through Reparations, 2026–2035.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:true,note:"From the ledger only (it cites Al Jazeera and Jamaica's Ministry of Culture); not confirmed by the sources cited. A politically live area: present it as reported positions, not as government policy statements.",ed:true,src:[],view:"Officials need a neutral, current factual position, set apart from advocacy.",who:"Caribbean and African states; diaspora communities in the UK; civil servants who brief on this",themes:["africa", "careers", "diaspora", "history"]},
  {h:"Return of ancestors and looted heritage",text:"UK law lets nine named national museums transfer human remains out of their collections (Human Tissue Act 2004, section 47). The British Museum Act 1963 bars most other disposals and is the main legal barrier to restitution. The ledger tracks 28 returns and requests of ancestral remains, and cases such as the sack of Benin City in 1897, when thousands of brass and ivory works were taken.",figure:"",year:"1897",disputed:false,note:"From the ledger only (it links legislation.gov.uk for s.47); the statute wording was not rechecked. The ledger's rule: ancestors are named before their remains, and burial sites are never mapped precisely.",ed:true,src:[],view:"Repair includes the return of the dead and of cultural property, and the hub can point people to how that works.",who:"African and Caribbean source communities; diaspora communities seeking ancestors and heritage",themes:["africa", "belonging", "history"]},
  {h:"Windrush: the empire's legacy inside the UK state",text:"Commonwealth-born residents, many of whom arrived as children before 1973, were denied work, housing and healthcare, and some were detained or removed. The Windrush Lessons Learned Review (2020) identified 164 people who had been in the country before 1973 and had been detained or removed, or both, since 2002.",figure:"164 detained or removed, or both, since 2002",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"The ledger also records the 2018 apology and the Windrush Compensation Scheme; see the hub's inequalities page for the scheme's figures.",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Lessons Learned Review (Wendy Williams, March 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e74984fd3bf7f4684279faa/6.5577_HO_Windrush_Lessons_Learned_Review_WEB_v2.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","It identified 164 people in the country before 1973 who'd either been detained or removed – or both – since 2002.")],view:"Links colonial history directly to recent failures in public administration and in data and records, which civil servants own.",who:"The Windrush generation and their families, mainly Caribbean heritage",themes:["diaspora", "history", "knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Black civil servants: present across the service, scarcer at the top",text:"At 31 March 2026, 19.2% of civil servants with a known ethnicity were from an ethnic minority background, a record high (11.2% in 2016). Representation falls with seniority: the Senior Civil Service is lowest at 10.6%. Black civil servants are the second-largest ethnic minority group, at 26,655 people. The bulletin gives no separate Black share by grade.",figure:"SCS ethnic minority representation 10.6% (all ethnic minorities); Black headcount 26,655",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The percentages exclude unknown and undeclared ethnicity. A Black-only SCS percentage is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Through other lenses page","with those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%,"),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Through other lenses page","followed by Black civil servants (26,655).")],view:"The leadership pipeline is the gap, not overall entry.",who:"Black, African, Caribbean and Mixed civil servants; staff networks; leaders",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Civil service pay by ethnicity: a 4.5% gap nationally, 27.8% in London",text:"The Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics 2025 give a median hourly pay gap of 4.5% for Black civil servants against White civil servants across the UK, and 27.8% in London. Annual medians at 31 March 2024 were £33,980 for Black and £34,300 for White civil servants; at junior grades Black civil servants had among the highest median pay of any group, but in London the lowest. A service-wide median says more about where people sit in the grades than about equal pay for equal work.",figure:"4.5% UK-wide; 27.8% in London",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Unadjusted gaps partly reflect grade mix and London location. The grade-mix argument comes from the hub's pay work.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service pay — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/public-sector-pay/civil-service-pay/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","among junior grades (AA, AO and EO), black civil servants had among the highest median annual pay out of all ethnic groups"),SRCQ("Civil Service pay — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/public-sector-pay/civil-service-pay/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","lowest for black civil servants (£35,060)"),SRCQ("Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Through other lenses page","The median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 4.5%."),SRCQ("Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Through other lenses page","In London the median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 27.8%.")],view:"Read pay gaps alongside grade mix and location, never as a single number.",who:"Black civil servants, especially in London and at mid and senior grades",themes:["careers", "equity", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Ethnicity pay gaps across the economy: where people were born matters",text:"In 2022, Black, African, Caribbean or Black British employees earned a median £13.53 an hour against £14.35 for White employees, a gap that has held since 2012. UK-born Black employees earned more (£15.18) than UK-born White employees (£14.26), while non-UK-born Black employees earned less (£12.95). UK-born Black African and Black Caribbean employees earned less than UK-born White British employees.",figure:"£13.53 vs £14.35 median hourly pay (2022)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The ONS article (released 29 November 2023) does not give a separate median for Black African employees against White British, so that exact comparison is not confirmed by the sources cited. The latest ONS release covers 2012 to 2022.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity pay gaps in Great Britain: 2012 to 2022 (ONS, 29 November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/earningsandworkinghours/articles/ethnicitypaygapsingreatbritain/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","Black, African, Caribbean or Black British employees earned less (£13.53) median gross hourly pay than White employees (£14.35),"),SRCQ("Ethnicity pay gaps in Great Britain: 2012 to 2022 (ONS, 29 November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/earningsandworkinghours/articles/ethnicitypaygapsingreatbritain/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","UK-born Black Caribbean and Black African employees earned less than White British employees")],view:"Splitting the Black category by birthplace and by detailed group reverses some conclusions, so aggregate 'Black' figures hide diaspora differences.",who:"Black African, Black Caribbean and Mixed workers; migrants and first-generation workers",themes:["diaspora", "enterprise", "equity", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Honours: Black recipients slightly below their population share",text:"In the 2025 New Year Honours list, 3.7% of recipients were Black, against a Black population share of 4.0% in England and Wales (Census 2021). Since June 2015 the share of recipients from Asian, Black, Mixed and Other backgrounds has risen from 7.0% to 12.5%.",figure:"3.7% Black recipients (2025 New Year Honours) vs 4.0% Black population (E&W 2021)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The geographies differ: UK honours compared with an England and Wales population, so treat the comparison as indicative. Ethnicity facts and figures makes no direct population comparison.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","3.7% were Black."),SRCQ("Honours recipients — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","the total percentage of honours recipients from Asian, Black, Mixed and Other backgrounds went up from 7.0% to 12.5%."),SRCQ("Population of England and Wales — Ethnicity facts and figures (Census 2021)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest/","Supports the Through other lenses page","followed by black (4.0%), mixed (2.9%) and other (2.1%) ethnic groups.")],view:"Recognition is an equality outcome the hub can act on, by nominating people.",who:"Black community leaders, staff-network volunteers and public servants who could be nominated",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]}]},
 {k:"other",h:"Public services and systems",intro:"Where systems hand people over, and where they fall through",points:[
  {h:"The handover from children's to adult services is where people fall out of view",text:"The hub's social care work names the transition between children's and adult social care as the point where records and support most often break. Young Black people in care and care leavers face that handover too. The hub can name the seam rather than treat childhood and adulthood as separate topics.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The hub's work frames this as interpretation, not a measured rate. No figure for how many people are lost at the seam has been checked.",ed:true,src:[],view:"System design: the failure is structural, at the join between two systems, not only in either one.",who:"Care-experienced young people and care leavers; local authority children's and adult services; staff networks supporting care-experienced colleagues",themes:["belonging", "knowledge", "lives", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Children looked after in England by ethnicity, 2025",text:"At 31 March 2025, 8% of children looked after were Black, African, Caribbean or Black British, and 11% were Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups. DfE says children from Mixed groups were over-represented. Among unaccompanied asylum-seeking children, 33% were Black or Black British.",figure:"8% Black; 11% Mixed; 33% of UASC Black",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The DfE text that was checked names Mixed groups as over-represented. It does not say that Black children are over-represented, so that claim is not confirmed by the sources cited. Comparing with the share in the child population needs ONS population figures, which are not covered here here.",src:[SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, reporting year 2025 (DfE, released 26 November 2025)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions","Supports the Through other lenses page","8% Black, African, Caribbean or Black British, 5% were Asian or Asian British, 4% other ethnicities,"),SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, reporting year 2025 (DfE)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions","Supports the Through other lenses page","Children from Mixed ethnic groups were over-represented"),SRCQ("Children looked after in England including adoptions, reporting year 2025 (DfE)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions","Supports the Through other lenses page","33% were of Black or Black British ethnicity, 24% of Asian or Asian British ethnicity, 3% White,")],view:"Official statistics: show the over-representation together with its denominator, and split out asylum-seeking children.",who:"Black and Mixed-heritage children in care, unaccompanied asylum-seeking children, foster carers, local authorities",themes:["africa", "diaspora", "knowledge", "lives", "younglives"]},
  {h:"Ethnic minority and internationally recruited workers in adult social care",text:"Skills for Care reported that about a third of the adult social care workforce come from ethnic minority backgrounds. In 2023/24, 105,000 international recruits started direct care roles in the independent sector, while the number of British workers fell.",figure:"32% ethnic minority; 105,000 international recruits (2023/24)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"This is a secondary source quoting the Skills for Care 2024 report. The 2026 report (2025/26 data) exists, but its ethnicity tables sit in an Excel appendix that was not read. The share of Black workers specifically, and Nigeria or Zimbabwe as leading source countries, are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Learning Disability Today, reporting Skills for Care, State of the adult social care sector and workforce in England 2024","https://www.learningdisabilitytoday.co.uk/news/adult-social-care-workforce-steadily-growing-but-domestic-recruitment-struggles-persist/","Supports the Through other lenses page","nearly one third (32%) of the adult social care workforce come from ethnic minority backgrounds"),SRCQ("Learning Disability Today, reporting Skills for Care 2024","https://www.learningdisabilitytoday.co.uk/news/adult-social-care-workforce-steadily-growing-but-domestic-recruitment-struggles-persist/","Supports the Through other lenses page","In total, 105,000 international recruits started direct care providing roles in the independent sector over the past year")],view:"Workforce: care services depend on migrant and ethnic minority workers.",who:"Care workers, including Black African and Caribbean workers and recent international recruits; people who draw on care; employers",themes:["africa", "careers", "diaspora", "equity", "lives"]},
  {h:"A race equality standard already exists for the social care workforce",text:"The Social Care Workforce Race Equality Standard (SC-WRES) collects workforce data across nine metrics so that organisations can plan action. Social Work England publishes SC-WRES data, including on fitness-to-practise referrals.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"No finding or figure on referral disparities was confirmed on the page checked (data labelled as at December 2025).",src:[SRCQ("Social Work England, Social Care Workforce Race Equality Standard data","https://www.socialworkengland.org.uk/about/data-and-research/data-and-insight/social-care-workforce-race-equality-standard-data/","Supports the Through other lenses page","intended to support organisations to make progress towards race equality.")],view:"Governance: an existing equality instrument the hub can point to instead of building a new one.",who:"Black and minoritised social workers and care staff; local authority employers; the regulator",themes:["careers", "equity", "knowledge", "lives"]},
  {h:"Windrush: a records and data failure, not only a policy failure",text:"The Windrush Lessons Learned Review (March 2020) found that the state gave people no documents and kept no records, and then demanded an unreasonable level of proof. It described institutional ignorance and thoughtlessness towards the issue of race. For a data-governance audience, this is the clearest case of harm done by missing records.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The GOV.UK page shows 'first published 19 July 2018' (when the review was commissioned). The report itself was ordered to be printed on 19 March 2020 and makes 30 recommendations.",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Lessons Learned Review, Wendy Williams (HC 93, March 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e74984fd3bf7f4684279faa/6.5577_HO_Windrush_Lessons_Learned_Review_WEB_v2.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","the factors that I identified demonstrate an institutional ignorance and thoughtlessness towards the issue of race"),SRCQ("Windrush Lessons Learned Review, Wendy Williams (March 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e74984fd3bf7f4684279faa/6.5577_HO_Windrush_Lessons_Learned_Review_WEB_v2.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","gave them no documents to demonstrate this status. Nor did it keep records."),SRCQ("Windrush Lessons Learned Review, Wendy Williams (March 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e74984fd3bf7f4684279faa/6.5577_HO_Windrush_Lessons_Learned_Review_WEB_v2.pdf","Supports the Through other lenses page","The Home Office demanded an unreasonable level of proof for them to be able to demonstrate their status.")],view:"Data governance: records management and the burden of proof are equality issues.",who:"The Windrush generation and their descendants; Caribbean and African Commonwealth communities; Home Office and records staff",themes:["belonging", "diaspora", "equity", "history", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Windrush landing card slips destroyed in 2010",text:"In 2018 it was reported that landing card slips recording arrival dates were destroyed when a Home Office office in Croydon closed in October 2010. The Home Office said the decision was operational and that the slips were not reliable evidence of residence.",figure:"",year:"2010",disputed:true,note:"The claim comes from a former employee, reported by The Guardian and Civil Service World. The Home Office disputed the slips' value as evidence. The review's text, as checked by automated extraction, did not mention landing cards, so their role in the review's findings is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service World, 18 April 2018: Fury over revelation that Home Office destroyed thousands of Windrush landing cards","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/fury-over-revelation-that-home-office-destroyed-thousands-of-windrush-landing-cards","Supports the Through other lenses page","a horde of landing card slips recording the arrival dates of immigrants were culled in an office move."),SRCQ("Civil Service World, 18 April 2018 (Home Office response)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/fury-over-revelation-that-home-office-destroyed-thousands-of-windrush-landing-cards","Supports the Through other lenses page","did not provide any reliable evidence relating to ongoing residence in the UK.")],view:"Records retention: decisions to destroy records need equality impact and retention review.",who:"The Windrush generation; anyone whose status depended on records the state held",themes:["belonging", "equity", "history", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"How government should record ethnicity: ask people, and avoid 'BAME'",text:"The 2023 Standards for ethnicity data say the best way to collect ethnicity is to ask people, and the Race Disparity Unit avoids the term 'BAME'. The hub's own forms, analytics and statistics should follow these standards, and use aggregated groups only when reliable detailed data is not available.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Published 17 April 2023. The page refers to the GSS harmonised standards. The ONS review of the harmonised standard is ongoing and is not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (Equality Hub / Race Disparity Unit, GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Through other lenses page","The best and most practical way of collecting data about people's ethnicity is to ask them"),SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Through other lenses page","words and phrases RDU avoids, such as 'BAME'")],view:"Standards: the language and classification the hub uses are themselves a data standard.",who:"Everyone whose ethnicity is recorded; analysts; service designers; staff networks",themes:["belonging", "knowledge", "language"]},
  {h:"Ethnic minority representation in the civil service is at a record high, and lowest in the SCS",text:"The Civil Service statistics bulletin for 31 March 2026 shows ethnic minority representation at a record high overall and lowest in the Senior Civil Service. Its figures, including the Black headcount, are set out under the history lens.",figure:"19.2% ethnic minority; 10.6% at SCS; 26,655 Black civil servants",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The 2026 bulletin does not give official Senior Civil Service headline figures or a Black share as a percentage. Ethnicity pay gaps are published separately, in the Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics 2025 (4.5% UK-wide and 27.8% in London, Black against White).",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Through other lenses page","Of those with a known ethnicity, the percentage who are from an ethnic minority background is at a record high of 19.2%."),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Through other lenses page","with those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%"),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Through other lenses page","followed by Black civil servants (26,655)."),SRCQ("Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Through other lenses page","The median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 4.5%.")],view:"Workforce and leadership: a pipeline that narrows towards the top.",who:"Black civil servants, staff networks, leaders and talent programmes",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Housing costs more than double persistent low income in Black households",text:"Over 2019 to 2023, 9% of people in Black households were in persistent low income before housing costs, against 22% after housing costs. This was the biggest gap of any ethnic group.",figure:"9% before housing costs, 22% after",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Data from DWP Understanding Society analysis, page last updated 17 September 2025. These are Black households in aggregate, and the data does not separate Black African from Black Caribbean.",src:[SRCQ("Persistent low income, Ethnicity facts and figures (GOV.UK)","https://ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/work-pay-and-benefits/pay-and-income/low-income/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","people in Black households had the biggest difference in persistent low income rates before (9%) and after (22%) housing costs")],view:"Household economics: housing is where the squeeze falls.",who:"Black households, especially renters; children in low-income households",themes:["equity", "knowledge", "younglives"]},
  {h:"An average can describe nobody",text:"The hub's household-costs work found two gradients running in opposite directions: the Brexit output loss falls mostly on richer regions, while the price shock falls mostly on poorer households, who spend more on food and energy. A national average hides both. The hub should apply the same rule to ethnicity: show the distribution and the group, not one headline figure.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The hub's Brexit work did not break costs down by ethnicity. The link to Black households is drawn here by inference and needs its own sourced data.",ed:true,src:[],view:"Analytical honesty: break figures down by group and do not add up overlapping estimates.",who:"Low-income households, including Black households (see the low-income point); readers of any statistic on the hub",themes:["enterprise", "equity", "knowledge"]},
  {h:"Black people are detained under the Mental Health Act at nearly four times the White rate",text:"In 2024-25 the detention rate for the Black or Black British group was 262.4 per 100,000 people, nearly four times the White rate of 65.8. Detentions involve health services, police and local authority social care, so the issue crosses the same service boundaries as the hub's interoperability work.",figure:"262.4 vs 65.8 per 100,000",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Rates are crude per-population rates. The role of Approved Mental Health Professionals in local authorities is general knowledge and is not covered here against a source here.",src:[SRCQ("Mental Health Act Statistics, Annual Figures 2024-25: detentions, differences between groups (NHS England)","https://digital.nhs.uk/data-and-information/publications/statistical/mental-health-act-statistics-annual-figures/2024-25-annual-figures/detentions-differences-between-groups-of-people","Supports the Through other lenses page","detention rates for the ‘Black or Black British’ group (262.4 detentions per 100,000 population) were highest,")],view:"Cross-service: the disparity sits across several services, so no single service owns it.",who:"Black adults and families; mental health, police and local authority social care services",themes:["equity", "knowledge", "lives"]},
  {h:"Ethnicity data that doesn't match across systems is a semantic interoperability problem",text:"The hub's interoperability work has four layers: technical, syntactic, semantic, and organisational and legal. Ethnicity recorded with different categories in schools, care, the NHS and HR is the semantic layer failing. A disparity cannot be traced across a person's journey if 'Black African' means different things in different systems. The hub's data dictionary should use the GSS harmonised categories and give crosswalks to them.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The four layers come from the hub's work. Applying them to ethnicity coding is the hub's inference, and no cross-system mismatch rate has been checked.",ed:true,src:[],view:"Interoperability: shared meaning is the requirement before equality analysis can work across systems.",who:"Analysts, service owners, and communities whose outcomes are measured",themes:["belonging", "equity", "knowledge", "lives"]},
  {h:"Equality analysis of the life events where systems fail people",text:"The hub's pay and reward analysis found that its recommendations were about life events the system fails, such as an allowance reassessment window falling during parental leave. It called for equality analysis of outcomes for people returning from leave. For careers and leadership, the hub can apply the same test by ethnicity: look at progression, assessment and reward at each transition, not just headcount.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The hub's analysis was not broken down by ethnicity. The civil service does publish ethnicity pay gaps: 4.5% UK-wide and 27.8% in London for Black against White civil servants (2025).",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Through other lenses page","In London the median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 27.8%.")],view:"Reward and progression: disparities build up at transitions.",who:"Black civil servants at career transitions (promotion, assessment, leave, transfer)",themes:["careers", "knowledge"]}]},
 {k:"life",h:"Across the life course",intro:"One disparity at each stage of life, from birth to remembrance, so the pattern can be seen as a whole. Each row is sourced; rates are compared with the White or overall rate stated in the source.",points:[
  {h:"Birth: Maternal deaths",text:"Black women's maternal mortality rate was nearly three times that of White women in the MBRRACE-UK report covering 2022–24. The overall rate was 12.80 per 100,000 maternities, and women in the most deprived areas had around twice the rate of those in the least deprived.",figure:"Nearly 3 times the White women's rate (2022–24)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("NPEU: UK maternal death rate 20% higher than in 2009-11, new MBRRACE-UK report finds (10 September 2026)","https://www.npeu.ox.ac.uk/news/2899-uk-maternal-death-rate-20-higher-than-in-2009-11-new-mbrrace-uk-report-finds","Supports the Through other lenses page","The maternal mortality rate for Black women was nearly three times that for White women.")]},
  {h:"Work: Pay across the economy",text:"Black employees' median hourly pay was £13.53 against £14.35 for White employees in 2022. UK-born Black employees earned more than UK-born White employees; non-UK-born Black employees earned less.",figure:"£13.53 vs £14.35 (2022)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity pay gaps in Great Britain: 2012 to 2022 (ONS, 29 November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/earningsandworkinghours/articles/ethnicitypaygapsingreatbritain/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","Black, African, Caribbean or Black British employees earned less (£13.53) median gross hourly pay than White employees (£14.35),")]},
  {h:"Work: Leadership in the civil service",text:"Ethnic minority representation is lowest in the Senior Civil Service, at 10.6% in 2026, against 19.2% across the whole service.",figure:"SCS 10.6% vs 19.2% overall (2026)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Through other lenses page","with those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%,")]},
  {h:"Health: Detention under the Mental Health Act",text:"In the year to March 2023, Black people were 3.5 times as likely as White people to be detained: 228 detentions per 100,000 Black people against 64 per 100,000 White people.",figure:"3.5 times; 228 vs 64 per 100,000 (year to March 2023)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Detentions under the Mental Health Act — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/health/mental-health/detentions-under-the-mental-health-act/latest/","Supports the Through other lenses page","in the year to March 2023, black people were 3.5 times as likely as white people to be detained under the Mental Health Act")]},
  {h:"Justice: Stop and search",text:"In the year ending 31 March 2023 there were 24.5 stop and searches for every 1,000 Black people and 5.9 for every 1,000 White people in England and Wales. That is about 4 times the rate, but the ratio is our own arithmetic: the service no longer publishes relative likelihoods.",figure:"24.5 vs 5.9 per 1,000 (year ending March 2023)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Stop and search — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/crime-justice-and-the-law/policing/stop-and-search/latest/","Supports the Through other lenses page","there were 24.5 stop and searches for every 1,000 black people, and 5.9 for every 1,000 white people")]},
  {h:"Housing: Home ownership",text:"In the two years to March 2023, 70% of White British households in England owned their home. Mixed White and Black African households had the lowest rate, at 14%. The data table gives 22% for Black African, 31% for Black Caribbean and 24% for all Black households.",figure:"70% White British vs 22% Black African (table value), 2021–23",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Home ownership — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/housing/owning-and-renting/home-ownership/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","70% of White British households owned their own homes"),SRCQ("Home ownership — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/housing/owning-and-renting/home-ownership/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","14% of households in the Mixed White and Black African ethnic group owned their own homes")]},
  {h:"Recognition (later career): Honours",text:"3.7% of recipients in the 2025 New Year Honours list were Black, against 4.0% of the population of England and Wales.",figure:"3.7% vs 4.0%",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients — Ethnicity facts and figures","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/latest","Supports the Through other lenses page","3.7% were Black."),SRCQ("Population of England and Wales — Ethnicity facts and figures (Census 2021)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest/","Supports the Through other lenses page","followed by black (4.0%), mixed (2.9%) and other (2.1%) ethnic groups.")]}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Facial recognition and some medical devices have worked less well for darker skin; you can ask how a system was tested.",refs:["ln-ai-face-analysis-was-least-accurate-for-dar", "ln-ai-pulse-oximeters-overestimate-oxygen-leve"]},{t:"Being counted starts with being asked: official guidance says to ask people their ethnicity rather than assign it.",refs:["ln-data-government-s-standards-for-ethnicity-dat", "ln-other-how-government-should-record-ethnicity-a"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Use 'enslaved people', not 'slaves', and keep no hierarchy of suffering between histories.",refs:["ln-hist-language-with-dignity-enslaved-people-no", "ln-hist-start-from-a-fixed-point-with-no-hierarc"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Black staff are present across the civil service but scarcer at the top; look at the pipeline, not only headcount.",refs:["ln-hist-black-civil-servants-present-across-the-"]},{t:"Honours and awards are an equality outcome too.",refs:["ln-hist-honours-black-recipients-slightly-below-"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"The equality duty applies from the moment a public body considers an AI tool; check for bias before use, not after.",refs:["ln-ai-the-equality-duty-applies-from-the-momen", "ln-ai-the-court-of-appeal-found-south-wales-po"]},{t:"Watch the seams: the handover from children's to adult services is where people fall out of view.",refs:["ln-other-the-handover-from-children-s-to-adult-se"]},{t:"Windrush shows that destroying or failing to keep records can strip people of their rights.",refs:["ln-other-windrush-a-records-and-data-failure-not-"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Ethnicity in NHS and other records often differs from what people say in the Census; check before you compare.",refs:["ln-data-ethnicity-in-nhs-records-often-differs-f", "ln-other-ethnicity-data-that-doesn-t-match-across"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Some face recognition systems have been least accurate for darker-skinned women.",refs:["ln-ai-face-analysis-was-least-accurate-for-dar"]},{t:"Official guidance says to ask people their ethnicity, not to assign it.",refs:["ln-data-government-s-standards-for-ethnicity-dat"]},{t:"In 1833, £20 million was paid to enslavers. The enslaved received nothing.",refs:["ln-hist-abolition-paid-20-million-to-enslavers-n"]},{t:"People fall out of view where services hand them on, such as when they leave care.",refs:["ln-other-the-handover-from-children-s-to-adult-se"]}]}]};
LP["represent"]={name:"Representation across the state",pfx:"rq",kpis:[{s:"executive",h:"The Black gradient by grade",n:"1.6%",l:"of the Senior Civil Service is Black, against 7.7% of the most junior grades (2026)",ic:"flag"},{s:"judicial",h:"Senior judiciary",n:"1 of 147",l:"High Court and senior judges with a known ethnicity is Black (2026)",ic:"scale"},{s:"legislative",h:"House of Commons 2024",n:"90",l:"MPs from minority ethnic groups were elected in 2024 (14%); Black MPs are not officially counted",ic:"flag"},{s:"services",h:"NHS",n:"12.7%",l:"of very senior NHS trust managers are from ethnic minorities, against 28.6% of staff (2024)",ic:"heart"}],sections:[
 {k:"executive",h:"Executive: ministers, the civil service and public appointments",intro:"Black civil servants are over-represented at entry grades and under-represented at the top. The SCS figure has barely moved since 2025.",points:[
  {h:"Civil service overall",text:"Ethnic minority representation in the civil service reached a record 19.2% in March 2026, close to the 19.6% economically active benchmark.",figure:"19.2%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","19.2% are from an ethnic minority background, up from 18.0% in 2025.")]},
  {h:"The Black gradient by grade",text:"Black staff make up 7.7% of AA/AO and 7.2% of EO grades, but 2.8% of Grades 6 and 7 and 1.6% of the SCS (105 people). These shares are worked out from the official data table.",figure:"7.7% to 1.6%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Worked out from the official data; see the ladder below.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026")]},
  {h:"SCS is the lowest-represented grade",text:"Ethnic minority representation in the SCS is 10.6%, the lowest of any grade.",figure:"10.6%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%")]},
  {h:"Ethnicity pay gap, London",text:"In London, Black civil servants earn a median of 27.8% less per hour than White civil servants. This matches the figure on the hub's inequalities page. The 2026 pay gap statistics are due on 28 October 2026.",figure:"27.8%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2025","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Representation across the state page","In London the median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 27.8%.")]},
  {h:"Ethnicity pay gap, UK-wide, and bonuses",text:"Across the UK the Black–White median pay gap is 4.5%. 62.1% of Black civil servants received a bonus, against 73.1% of White civil servants.",figure:"4.5%; 62.1% vs 73.1%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2025","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Representation across the state page","The median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 4.5%."),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2025","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the Representation across the state page","In 2025, 73.1% of White civil servants received a bonus, compared to 62.1% of Black civil servants.")]},
  {h:"Fast Stream funnel",text:"Black applicants are about 15.7% of known-ethnicity Fast Stream applicants, but the Black African success rate is 0.3%, against 1.3% for White British applicants. Worked out from the official data table.",figure:"0.3% vs 1.3%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Worked out from the official data; Black Caribbean recommendations are suppressed.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Fast Stream Annual Report 2026","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6aaa4fcd086ce7c57b818545/Fast_Stream_Annual_Report_2026_V5.pdf","Supports the Representation across the state page","Fast Stream recruitment by ethnic group, all applicants, 2026")]},
  {h:"Public appointments",text:"20% of 2024-25 public appointments went to ethnic minority candidates, against 27% of applicants. Ethnic minority people hold 8% of chairs. No Black breakdown is published, and no ethnicity target was found in the report.",figure:"20% / 8% of chairs",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Public Appointments Data Report 2024-25 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Representation across the state page","20% of public appointments were made to candidates from an ethnic minority background"),SRCQ("Public Appointments Data Report 2024-25 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Representation across the state page","8% of chairs were from an ethnic minority background, 13% of members and 18% of individual office holders")]},
  {h:"Cabinet",text:"No official count of ethnic minority or Black members of the current Cabinet was found. The latest Commons Library count (2022) covered the previous government.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:true,note:"Current figure not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","Seven Cabinet Ministers are from a minority ethnic background.")]}]},
 {k:"privateoffice",h:"Private offices: what is published, and what is not",intro:"No published data on the ethnicity of ministerial private office staff (private secretaries, principal private secretaries, diary or correspondence teams) was found.",points:[
  {h:"No private office diversity data",text:"Civil Service Statistics report ethnicity by grade, department and profession, not by role such as private secretary. No departmental or Cabinet Office publication on private office diversity was found.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Finding: data gap. Private offices draw mostly on HEO/SEO, Grade 7 and SCS staff. At those grades Black representation is 4.7%, 2.8% and 1.6% (computed).",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026")]},
  {h:"Talent schemes as the pipeline",text:"The Fast Stream is a common route into private office roles. It publishes ethnicity outcomes, and the Black African success rate is about a quarter of the White British rate. Equivalent outcome data for the Future Leaders Scheme, the Senior Leaders Scheme and positive action schemes was not found.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Scheme outcome data beyond the Fast Stream not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Fast Stream Annual Report 2026","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6aaa4fcd086ce7c57b818545/Fast_Stream_Annual_Report_2026_V5.pdf","Supports the Representation across the state page","Fast Stream recruitment by ethnic group, all applicants, 2026")]}]},
 {k:"legislative",h:"Legislatures",intro:"The Commons is the most diverse it has ever been on ethnic minority measures. No official body publishes a count of Black MPs, and the devolved and Lords figures are dated.",points:[
  {h:"House of Commons 2024",text:"An estimated 90 MPs (14%) elected in 2024 are from minority ethnic groups, up from 66 in 2019. The Commons Library takes this from British Future data.",figure:"90 (14%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("General election 2024 results (House of Commons Library, CBP-10009)","https://researchbriefings.files.parliament.uk/documents/CBP-10009/CBP-10009.pdf","Supports the Representation across the state page","It is estimated that 90 MPs elected in 2024 are from minority ethnic groups, up from 66 MPs in 2019.")]},
  {h:"Black MPs not officially counted",text:"Neither the Commons Library briefing nor British Future’s release gives a count of Black MPs.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:true,note:"Not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Milestone for representation as diversity of Parliament matches electorate (British Future, 5 July 2024)","https://www.britishfuture.org/milestone-for-representation-as-diversity-of-parliament-matches-electorate/","Supports the Representation across the state page","A record number of 90 ethnic minority MPs have been elected to sit in the UK’s most diverse parliament ever.")]},
  {h:"House of Lords",text:"The latest official figure is from September 2022: 55 members (7.3%) from minority ethnic groups.",figure:"7.3%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Dated.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","In September 2022, 55 or 7.3% of Members of the House of Lords were from minority ethnic groups.")]},
  {h:"Devolved legislatures",text:"In the 2021 sessions, ethnic minority members numbered 6 of 129 at Holyrood (4.5%), 3 of 60 in the Senedd (5%) and none of 90 in the NI Assembly.",figure:"",year:"2021-22",disputed:false,note:"Dated; official figures for the 2026 Holyrood and Senedd intakes not confirmed.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","Six (4.5%) of the Scottish Parliament's 129 members"),SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","None of the 90 Members of the Northern Ireland Assembly were recorded as being from a minority ethnic group.")]},
  {h:"Parliamentary staff",text:"Scottish Parliament staff: minority ethnic representation rose to 4%, on a definition that includes some White groups. Commons and Lords administration figures are not cited here.",figure:"4%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity monitoring and pay gaps report 2024-25 (Scottish Parliament)","https://www.parliament.scot/about/how-parliament-works/equity-diversity-and-inclusion/diversity-monitoring-and-pay-gap-reports/diversity-monitoring-and-pay-gaps-report-2024-25","Supports the Representation across the state page","The representation of Minority Ethnic staff has increased to 4%")]}]},
 {k:"judicial",h:"Judiciary and the legal pipeline",intro:"Black lawyers are present in the professions roughly in line with the working-age population, at 3% to 4%. That share narrows sharply at each step towards the senior bench.",points:[
  {h:"Black judges barely moved in ten years",text:"The Black share of all judges went from 1.4% in 2016 to 1.6% in 2026, while the Asian and Mixed shares rose.",figure:"1.6%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","for the Black ethnic group (from 1.4% to 1.6%) there was a comparatively smaller increase")]},
  {h:"Hub figure (1%) reconciled",text:"The 2025 bulletin, which the hub cites, reported Black judges at 1% of all judges, a rounded figure. The 2026 unrounded figure is 1.6%. Both describe a flat trend.",figure:"1% (2025, rounded) / 1.6% (2026)",year:"2025-2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary - 2025 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2025-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2025-statistics--2","Supports the Representation across the state page","Representation of Black judges has remained at 1% of all judges.")]},
  {h:"Senior judiciary",text:"Of 147 High Court, Court of Appeal and Heads of Division judges with known ethnicity, one is Black and ten are ethnic minority. Worked out from the official data table.",figure:"1 of 147",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Worked out from the official data; the numbers are small.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals")]},
  {h:"Professions",text:"Black representation in the three legal professions is 3% to 4%, comparable to the working-age population. KC status is less common among ethnic minority barristers (8%) than among White barristers (13%).",figure:"3% to 4%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Representation of Black individuals was similar for all three professions (3% to 4%)"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Of ethnic minority barristers 8% were King’s Counsel, compared to 13% of White barristers.")]},
  {h:"JAC selection",text:"Black candidates were 7% of applicants but 3% of those recommended, which matches their 3% share of the eligible pool. MoJ finds no statistically significant disparity from eligible pool to recommendation (RRI 1.27).",figure:"7% to 3%",year:"2025-26",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","The likelihood of success from eligible pool to recommendation was higher for Black candidates than for White candidates")]},
  {h:"Magistrates",text:"Ethnic minority people make up 14% of magistrates. The Black share, worked out from the official data table, is 4.6%, above the Black population benchmark.",figure:"14% / Black 4.6%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Over half (57%) of all magistrates were female. Ethnic minorities made up 14%.")]}]},
 {k:"services",h:"Public services: police, armed forces, NHS and schools",intro:"The same pattern holds across the public services: Black staff are present or over-represented at entry level and thin out towards the top.",points:[
  {h:"Police",text:"Black officers remain 1.3% of officers in England and Wales in 2026. Ethnic minority representation is 6.9% at chief inspector and above, against 9.2% of constables.",figure:"1.3%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Police workforce, England and Wales: 31 March 2026 (Home Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","Black officers made up 1.3% of officers, Asian officers 3.9%, mixed officers 2.7%, and officers from another ethnic group made up 0.8%")]},
  {h:"Armed forces",text:"Ethnic minorities are 15.0% of other ranks but 3.9% of officers. Computed Black shares are 7.5% of other ranks, 0.8% of officers and 0.6% at Lieutenant Colonel and above. Many ethnic minority personnel are Commonwealth nationals.",figure:"15.0% vs 3.9%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2026 (MOD)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","3.9 per cent of Officers and 15.0 per cent of Other Ranks in the UK Regular Forces."),SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2026 (MOD)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","43.5 per cent of ethnic minorities (excluding white minorities) personnel in UK Regular forces did not have UK Nationality")]},
  {h:"NHS",text:"Staff from Black and minority ethnic groups, as the race equality standard names them, were 28.6% of the NHS trust workforce but 12.7% of very senior managers (WRES 2024). EFF (2022) gives Black staff at 7.4% overall and 3.4% of senior managers.",figure:"28.6% vs 12.7%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("One in eight in senior NHS staff from black and minority ethnic backgrounds (NHS England, June 2025; WRES 2024 report)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/2025/06/one-in-eight-in-senior-nhs-staff-from-black-and-minority-ethnic-backgrounds/","Supports the Representation across the state page","12.7% of staff at very senior manager level are from these backgrounds."),SRCQ("NHS workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, June 2022 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/nhs-workforce/latest","Supports the Representation across the state page","black staff made up 7.4% of the NHS workforce, and 4.4% of the working age population")]},
  {h:"Teachers and headteachers",text:"Black teachers are 2.9% of teachers, falling to 1.2% of headteachers, against a 4.4% working-age benchmark. These match the hub figures.",figure:"2.9% / 1.2%",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("School teacher workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, 2024 to 2025 academic year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest","Supports the Representation across the state page","91.8% of headteachers were White British")]},
  {h:"Local government",text:"Ethnicity results from the LGA councillor census (2022, 30% response) is not cited here, and no official data on ethnic minority council chief executives was found.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:true,note:"Not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("National census of local authority councillors 2022 (LGA)","https://www.local.gov.uk/publications/national-census-local-authority-councillors-2022","Supports the Representation across the state page","a response rate of 30 per cent")]}]},
 {k:"gaps",h:"Data gaps",intro:"Several of the posts closest to power have no published ethnicity data, or no Black breakdown.",points:[
  {h:"Gaps at the top",text:"There is no official published count of Black Cabinet ministers, Black MPs, ethnic minority permanent secretaries, ambassadors or heads of mission, or private office staff.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Each is recorded in gaps[].",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Ethnic minority only",text:"Public appointments, WRES summaries, police senior ranks and JAC exercise-level data publish ethnic minority totals only. Black shares are hidden in the aggregate.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Public Appointments Data Report 2024-25 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Representation across the state page","13% (293) were from an ethnic minority background, compared to 9% in 2024 and 12% in 2023")]}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black people are well represented at the entry to many public institutions; the gap is at the top.",refs:["rq-executive-the-black-gradient-by-grade", "rq-services-teachers-and-headteachers", "rq-judicial-senior-judiciary"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Sponsor and nominate: talent schemes are a main route into private offices and senior roles, and the Fast Stream shows a steep drop for Black African applicants.",refs:["rq-privateoffice-talent-schemes-as-the-pipeline", "rq-executive-fast-stream-funnel"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Publish your private office's make-up against your department's grade profile; no department does today.",refs:["rq-privateoffice-no-private-office-diversity-data", "rq-gaps-gaps-at-the-top"]},{t:"Look at the pay gap where it is widest: London.",refs:["rq-executive-ethnicity-pay-gap-london"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Public appointments, police senior ranks and NHS summaries publish only an ethnic minority total; ask for Black, African and Caribbean breakdowns.",refs:["rq-gaps-ethnic-minority-only", "rq-executive-public-appointments"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Compare with the right benchmark: the whole population, the working-age population and the eligible pool give different answers.",refs:["rq-judicial-jac-selection", "rq-executive-civil-service-overall", "rq-services-teachers-and-headteachers"]},{t:"State the year, and whether a figure is rounded: the hub's 1% and the newer 1.6% for Black judges describe the same flat trend.",refs:["rq-judicial-hub-figure-1-reconciled"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Black people are well represented in junior civil service grades, but only 1.6% of the Senior Civil Service is Black.",refs:["rq-executive-the-black-gradient-by-grade"]},{t:"One of the 147 most senior judges is Black.",refs:["rq-judicial-senior-judiciary"]},{t:"No one publishes the ethnicity of private office staff.",refs:["rq-privateoffice-no-private-office-diversity-data"]},{t:"In London, Black civil servants earn 27.8% less per hour than White civil servants.",refs:["rq-executive-ethnicity-pay-gap-london"]}]}],ladder:[{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"All civil servants",order:0,black:"5.8%",mixed:"2.6%",em:"19.2%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 (headcount, known ethnicity) of the Civil Service Statistics 2026 ODS file: Black 26,655 and Mixed 11,965 of 459,545 with known ethnicity; ethnic minority share as published in the table. Bulletin: Black civil servants 26,655 headcount. EFF (March 2024) published Black 4.5%; see notes.reconcile.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026"),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","19.2% are from an ethnic minority background, up from 18.0% in 2025."),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","followed by Black civil servants (26,655)")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"Administrative Officers and Assistants (AA/AO)",order:1,black:"7.7%",mixed:"2.3%",em:"21.0%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 (headcount, known ethnicity) of the Civil Service Statistics 2026 ODS file: Black 7,910 and Mixed 2,355 of 103,220 with known ethnicity; ethnic minority share as published in the table.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"Executive Officers (EO)",order:2,black:"7.2%",mixed:"2.5%",em:"23.5%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 (headcount, known ethnicity) of the Civil Service Statistics 2026 ODS file: Black 8,315 and Mixed 2,885 of 115,085 with known ethnicity; ethnic minority share as published in the table.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"Higher and Senior Executive Officers (HEO/SEO)",order:3,black:"4.7%",mixed:"2.6%",em:"17.7%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 (headcount, known ethnicity) of the Civil Service Statistics 2026 ODS file: Black 6,565 and Mixed 3,635 of 139,010 with known ethnicity; ethnic minority share as published in the table.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"Grades 6 and 7",order:4,black:"2.8%",mixed:"2.8%",em:"14.0%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 (headcount, known ethnicity) of the Civil Service Statistics 2026 ODS file: Black 2,085 and Mixed 2,105 of 74,725 with known ethnicity; ethnic minority share as published in the table.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"Senior Civil Service (SCS)",order:5,black:"1.6%",mixed:"2.8%",em:"10.6%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 (headcount, known ethnicity) of the Civil Service Statistics 2026 ODS file: Black 105 and Mixed 190 of 6,700 with known ethnicity; ethnic minority share as published in the table. Black share is roughly 1 in 64 SCS members against 1 in 13 AA/AO staff. 2025 equivalent: Black 105 of 6,800 (1.5%).",src:[SRCQ("Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics 2026 (Table 2: employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, 31 March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2: Civil Service employment by ethnicity and responsibility level, as at 31 March 2026"),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin: Civil Service Statistics 2026","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"UK Civil Service",level:"Permanent secretaries",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"",year:"",status:"not confirmed",note:"No official count of ethnic minority or Black permanent secretaries was found. Not confirmed by the sources cited. Numbers would be very small (tens of posts) and likely suppressed for disclosure reasons.",src:[]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"HM Government",level:"Cabinet ministers",order:8,black:"",mixed:"",em:"",year:"2022 (dated)",status:"not confirmed",note:"Only a 2022 count (previous government) was found: seven Cabinet ministers from a minority ethnic background. No official count for the current Cabinet, and no official count of Black Cabinet ministers, was found. Current figure not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","Seven Cabinet Ministers are from a minority ethnic background.")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"Civil Service Fast Stream",level:"Applicants (2026 campaign)",order:0,black:"15.7%",mixed:"",em:"43.2%",year:"2026 campaign",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 3 of the Fast Stream Annual Report 2026: Black African 9,143 + Black Caribbean 798 + Any other Black 358 = 10,299 of 65,429 applications with known ethnicity. Ethnic minority 43.2% of applicants as published.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Fast Stream Annual Report 2026","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6aaa4fcd086ce7c57b818545/Fast_Stream_Annual_Report_2026_V5.pdf","Supports the Representation across the state page","Fast Stream recruitment by ethnic group, all applicants, 2026")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"Civil Service Fast Stream",level:"Recommended for appointment (2026 campaign)",order:1,black:"at least 3.9%",mixed:"",em:"29.7%",year:"2026 campaign",status:"computed",note:"Black African recommended: 26 (3.9% of known); Black Caribbean and Any other Black are suppressed (*). Success rate Black African 0.3% against White British 1.3% (Table 3). Ethnic minority 29.7% of recommendations as published.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Fast Stream Annual Report 2026","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6aaa4fcd086ce7c57b818545/Fast_Stream_Annual_Report_2026_V5.pdf","Supports the Representation across the state page","Fast Stream recruitment by ethnic group, all applicants, 2026")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"Public appointments (ministerial departments)",level:"All public appointees in post",order:3,black:"",mixed:"",em:"13%",year:"31 March 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Ethnicity known for 51% of appointees. No Black or Mixed breakdown published. Benchmark in the report is the economically active population.",src:[SRCQ("Public Appointments Data Report 2024-25 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Representation across the state page","13% (293) were from an ethnic minority background, compared to 9% in 2024 and 12% in 2023")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"Public appointments (ministerial departments)",level:"New appointments made in 2024-25",order:2,black:"",mixed:"",em:"20%",year:"2024-25",status:"confirmed",note:"Ethnic minority share falls from 27% of applicants to 20% of shortlisted, appointable and appointed candidates. No Black breakdown published.",src:[SRCQ("Public Appointments Data Report 2024-25 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Representation across the state page","20% of public appointments were made to candidates from an ethnic minority background")]},{branch:"Executive",inst:"Public appointments (ministerial departments)",level:"Chairs of public bodies",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"8%",year:"31 March 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Ethnic minority chairs 8%, members 13%, individual office holders 18%.",src:[SRCQ("Public Appointments Data Report 2024-25 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Representation across the state page","8% of chairs were from an ethnic minority background, 13% of members and 18% of individual office holders")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"House of Commons",level:"MPs elected July 2024",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"14% (90 MPs)",year:"2024",status:"confirmed",note:"No official count of Black MPs found in the HoC Library briefing; the Library uses British Future data for ethnicity. Black MP count not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("General election 2024 results (House of Commons Library, CBP-10009)","https://researchbriefings.files.parliament.uk/documents/CBP-10009/CBP-10009.pdf","Supports the Representation across the state page","It is estimated that 90 MPs elected in 2024 are from minority ethnic groups, up from 66 MPs in 2019."),SRCQ("Milestone for representation as diversity of Parliament matches electorate (British Future, 5 July 2024)","https://www.britishfuture.org/milestone-for-representation-as-diversity-of-parliament-matches-electorate/","Supports the Representation across the state page","A record number of 90 ethnic minority MPs have been elected to sit in the UK’s most diverse parliament ever.")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"House of Lords",level:"Members",order:7,black:"",mixed:"",em:"7.3% (55 members)",year:"September 2022 (dated)",status:"confirmed",note:"Latest official figure found is from 2022. No Black breakdown. Treat as dated.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","In September 2022, 55 or 7.3% of Members of the House of Lords were from minority ethnic groups.")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"Scottish Parliament",level:"MSPs (Session 6, elected 2021)",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"4.5% (6 of 129)",year:"2021 (dated)",status:"confirmed",note:"Session 7 (elected May 2026) official diversity data not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","Six (4.5%) of the Scottish Parliament's 129 members")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"Senedd Cymru",level:"Members (Sixth Senedd, elected 2021)",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"5% (3 of 60)",year:"2021 (dated)",status:"confirmed",note:"Senedd Research says limited diversity information exists on Members. Data for the expanded 96-member Senedd elected in 2026 not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","three (5%) of the 60 Members of the Welsh Parliament"),SRCQ("Who speaks for Wales? How could the 2026 Senedd election be more representative? (Senedd Research, 26 November 2025)","https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/who-speaks-for-wales-how-could-the-2026-senedd-election-be-more-representative/","Supports the Representation across the state page","There is limited information available about the diversity of Members of the Senedd.")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"Northern Ireland Assembly",level:"MLAs",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"0 of 90",year:"2022 (dated)",status:"confirmed",note:"As recorded by the Commons Library in 2022.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic diversity in politics and public life (House of Commons Library, SN01156; 2022 data)","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/sn01156/","Supports the Representation across the state page","None of the 90 Members of the Northern Ireland Assembly were recorded as being from a minority ethnic group.")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"Scottish Parliament Corporate Body",level:"Parliamentary staff",order:0,black:"",mixed:"",em:"4%",year:"2025 report",status:"confirmed",note:"Minority ethnic here includes some White groups (Polish, Gypsy/Traveller, Roma, Showpeople). Scotland census 2022 minority ethnic share given as 12.9% on the same page.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity monitoring and pay gaps report 2024-25 (Scottish Parliament)","https://www.parliament.scot/about/how-parliament-works/equity-diversity-and-inclusion/diversity-monitoring-and-pay-gap-reports/diversity-monitoring-and-pay-gaps-report-2024-25","Supports the Representation across the state page","The representation of Minority Ethnic staff has increased to 4%")]},{branch:"Legislative",inst:"House of Commons and House of Lords administrations",level:"Parliamentary staff",order:0,black:"",mixed:"",em:"",year:"",status:"not confirmed",note:"No current published workforce ethnicity figure for the Commons or Lords administrations was retrieved. Not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Legal professions (Bar)",level:"Practising barristers",order:0,black:"3.6%",mixed:"4.0%",em:"17%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 1.1, Black 604 and Mixed 683 of 16,932. Data source Bar Standards Board.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 1.1: Legal professions in England and Wales"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Representation of Black individuals was similar for all three professions (3% to 4%)")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Legal professions (Bar)",level:"King's Counsel",order:4,black:"1.3%",mixed:"3.3%",em:"11.6%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 1.1, Black 27 and Mixed 68 of 2,066 KCs. 8% of ethnic minority barristers are KCs against 13% of White barristers.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 1.1: Legal professions in England and Wales"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Of ethnic minority barristers 8% were King’s Counsel, compared to 13% of White barristers.")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Legal professions (solicitors)",level:"Solicitors",order:0,black:"3.1%",mixed:"2.1%",em:"20%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 1.1, Black 3,632 and Mixed 2,470 of 115,833. Declaration rate 64%. Data source SRA.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 1.1: Legal professions in England and Wales")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Legal professions (solicitors)",level:"Partners (owners and managers)",order:3,black:"2.6%",mixed:"",em:"18%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 1.1, Black 762 of 28,968 partners with known ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 1.1: Legal professions in England and Wales")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Judicial Appointments Commission",level:"Applicants, legal exercises 2025-26",order:1,black:"7%",mixed:"4%",em:"30%",year:"2025-26",status:"confirmed",note:"Table 2.2 representation percentages. Black share of eligible pool is 3%.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2.2: Display data for selected exercise or group of exercises"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Ethnic minority candidates made up a much higher percentage of applications (30%) than their representation in the eligible pool.")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Judicial Appointments Commission",level:"Recommended for appointment, legal exercises 2025-26",order:2,black:"3%",mixed:"5%",em:"18%",year:"2025-26",status:"confirmed",note:"Table 2.2: 18 Black candidates recommended (3%); Black share drops from 7% of applicants to 4% shortlisted and 3% recommended, in line with the 3% eligible pool.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2.2: Display data for selected exercise or group of exercises"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Across all legal exercises in 2025-26, ethnic minority candidates accounted for 16% of the eligible pool and 18% of recommendations.")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Magistracy",level:"Magistrates in post",order:1,black:"4.6%",mixed:"1.9%",em:"14%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.6, Black 610 and Mixed 251 of 13,377 with known ethnicity. New appointments 2025-26: Black 74 of 1,624 (4.6%), ethnic minority 17%.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.6: Primary appointment of serving Magistrates in England and Wales"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","Over half (57%) of all magistrates were female. Ethnic minorities made up 14%.")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Courts and tribunals judiciary",level:"All judges (courts and tribunals)",order:3,black:"1.6%",mixed:"3.3%",em:"13%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.1, Black 79 and Mixed 159 of 4,868. Bulletin: Black share 1.4% (2016) to 1.6% (2026). Hub publishes 1% from the 2025 bulletin; see notes.reconcile.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals"),SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","for the Black ethnic group (from 1.4% to 1.6%) there was a comparatively smaller increase")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Courts judiciary",level:"All court judges",order:3,black:"1.4%",mixed:"3.3%",em:"12%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.1, Black 43 of 3,185 court judges with known ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Tribunals judiciary",level:"All tribunal judges",order:3,black:"2.1%",mixed:"3.3%",em:"14%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.1, Black 36 of 1,683. Non-legal tribunal members: Black 93 of 2,920 (3.2%), ethnic minority 18%.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Courts judiciary",level:"Recorders (fee-paid, entry to Crown Court bench)",order:3,black:"1.5%",mixed:"4.0%",em:"12%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.1, Black 12 and Mixed 33 of 822.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Courts judiciary",level:"Circuit Judges",order:4,black:"0.8%",mixed:"3.0%",em:"10%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.1, Black 6 and Mixed 21 of 706.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Senior judiciary",level:"High Court and above (High Court, Court of Appeal, Heads of Division)",order:6,black:"0.7% (1 judge)",mixed:"2.7%",em:"6.8%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from the Diversity of the judiciary 2026 bulletin tables (ODS), share of those with known ethnicity: Table 3.1, 149 posts (5 Heads of Division, 39 Court of Appeal, 105 High Court), 147 with known ethnicity: 1 Black, 4 Mixed, 10 ethnic minority. Supreme Court not covered by these statistics. Very small numbers.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary 2026: all bulletin tables (Tables 1.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.6)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e19886a73e62ba10299c0/All_bulletin_tables_2026_-_FINAL_v3.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 3.1: Primary appointment of Judges and Non-legal Members in Courts and Tribunals")]},{branch:"Judicial",inst:"Tribunals judiciary",level:"Tribunal Presidents, Chamber Presidents, Deputy and Vice Presidents",order:7,black:"0",mixed:"0",em:"0% (0 of 13)",year:"1 April 2026",status:"confirmed",note:"No ethnic minority judge holds a primary appointment at the most senior tribunal level.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of the judiciary: legal professions, new appointments and current post-holders - 2026 statistics (MoJ)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Representation across the state page","no ethnic minority judges whose primary appointment was in the most senior tribunal positions (Presidents)")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"Police (43 territorial forces, England and Wales)",level:"All police officers",order:0,black:"1.3%",mixed:"2.7%",em:"8.7%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"confirmed",note:"Same 1.3% Black figure as the hub (eqi-policing-1). Ethnic minority joiners 10.8% in 2025-26; minority ethnic share of promotions fell to 6.5% from 8.4%.",src:[SRCQ("Police workforce, England and Wales: 31 March 2026 (Home Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","Black officers made up 1.3% of officers, Asian officers 3.9%, mixed officers 2.7%, and officers from another ethnic group made up 0.8%")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"Police (43 territorial forces, England and Wales)",level:"Joiners 2025-26",order:0,black:"",mixed:"",em:"10.8%",year:"2025-26",status:"confirmed",note:"No Black breakdown of joiners in the bulletin text.",src:[SRCQ("Police workforce, England and Wales: 31 March 2026 (Home Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","932 or 10.8% identified themselves as ethnic minorities (excluding White minorities)")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"Police (43 territorial forces, England and Wales)",level:"Chief inspector and above",order:5,black:"",mixed:"",em:"6.9%",year:"31 March 2026",status:"confirmed",note:"Black share at senior ranks is not given in the bulletin text or the summary tables checked (Table D1 is ethnic minority only). Black senior-rank share not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Police workforce, England and Wales: 31 March 2026 (Home Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","6.9% of officers of rank chief inspector or above identified as ethnic minorities (excluding White minorities), compared with 9.2% of constables")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"UK Regular Armed Forces",level:"Other ranks",order:0,black:"7.5%",mixed:"2.4%",em:"15.0%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 of the MOD April 2026 ODS tables (UK Regulars, known ethnicity): Black 8,180 and Mixed 2,590 of 109,700. Caveat: 43.5% of ethnic minority Regulars do not hold UK nationality (Commonwealth recruitment), so this is not a measure of UK-resident Black representation.",src:[SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics April 2026, accessible ODS tables (Table 2: UK Regulars by ethnicity and paid rank)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4cf4f7e50f952c9b89e6ad/UK_armed_forces_biannual_diversity_statistics_April_2026_Accessible_ODS_Tables.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2 - UK Regulars by Ethnicity and Paid Rank"),SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2026 (MOD)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","3.9 per cent of Officers and 15.0 per cent of Other Ranks in the UK Regular Forces.")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"UK Regular Armed Forces",level:"All UK Regulars",order:0,black:"6.1%",mixed:"2.2%",em:"12.8%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 of the MOD April 2026 ODS tables (UK Regulars, known ethnicity): Black 8,420 and Mixed 3,010 of 137,150.",src:[SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics April 2026, accessible ODS tables (Table 2: UK Regulars by ethnicity and paid rank)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4cf4f7e50f952c9b89e6ad/UK_armed_forces_biannual_diversity_statistics_April_2026_Accessible_ODS_Tables.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2 - UK Regulars by Ethnicity and Paid Rank"),SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2026 (MOD)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","accounted for 12.8 per cent of the UK Regular Forces (17,570 personnel), at 1 April 2026")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"UK Regular Armed Forces",level:"Officers (all)",order:3,black:"0.8%",mixed:"1.5%",em:"3.9%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 of the MOD April 2026 ODS tables (UK Regulars, known ethnicity): Black 230 and Mixed 420 of 27,450 officers.",src:[SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics April 2026, accessible ODS tables (Table 2: UK Regulars by ethnicity and paid rank)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4cf4f7e50f952c9b89e6ad/UK_armed_forces_biannual_diversity_statistics_April_2026_Accessible_ODS_Tables.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2 - UK Regulars by Ethnicity and Paid Rank"),SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2026 (MOD)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026","Supports the Representation across the state page","3.9 per cent of Officers and 15.0 per cent of Other Ranks in the UK Regular Forces.")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"UK Regular Armed Forces",level:"Lieutenant Colonel (OF-4) and above",order:6,black:"0.6%",mixed:"0.9%",em:"2.4%",year:"1 April 2026",status:"computed",note:"Computed from Table 2 of the MOD April 2026 ODS tables (UK Regulars, known ethnicity): Black 30 and Mixed 50 of 5,450 (figures rounded to nearest 10 in the source).",src:[SRCQ("UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics April 2026, accessible ODS tables (Table 2: UK Regulars by ethnicity and paid rank)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4cf4f7e50f952c9b89e6ad/UK_armed_forces_biannual_diversity_statistics_April_2026_Accessible_ODS_Tables.ods","Supports the Representation across the state page","Table 2 - UK Regulars by Ethnicity and Paid Rank")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"NHS (England)",level:"All NHS staff (EFF)",order:0,black:"7.4%",mixed:"",em:"25.7%",year:"June 2022 (dated)",status:"confirmed",note:"Latest Ethnicity facts and figures page uses June 2022 data.",src:[SRCQ("NHS workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, June 2022 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/nhs-workforce/latest","Supports the Representation across the state page","black staff made up 7.4% of the NHS workforce, and 4.4% of the working age population")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"NHS (England)",level:"Senior managers (NHS infrastructure support group)",order:5,black:"3.4%",mixed:"1.4%",em:"11.3%",year:"June 2022 (dated)",status:"confirmed",note:"Black 420 of 11,902 senior managers with known ethnicity (published table CSV).",src:[SRCQ("NHS workforce by ethnicity and grade (managers and senior managers), CSV","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/nhs-workforce/latest/downloads/by-ethnicity-and-grade-managers-and-senior-managers-table.csv","Supports the Representation across the state page","Data file: figures read from the published table."),SRCQ("NHS workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, June 2022 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/nhs-workforce/latest","Supports the Representation across the state page","ethnic minority staff made up 15.0% of people in managerial level positions, and 11.3% of senior managerial level positions")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"NHS trusts (WRES)",level:"All trust staff",order:0,black:"",mixed:"",em:"28.6% (Black and minority ethnic, as published)",year:"March 2024",status:"confirmed",note:"WRES reports a combined ‘BME’ group; no Black figure in the summary. The WRES 2025 report (published April 2026) was not cited here as text; its figures are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("One in eight in senior NHS staff from black and minority ethnic backgrounds (NHS England, June 2025; WRES 2024 report)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/2025/06/one-in-eight-in-senior-nhs-staff-from-black-and-minority-ethnic-backgrounds/","Supports the Representation across the state page","has also increased from 26.4% in 2023 to 28.6% as of March 2024")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"NHS trusts (WRES)",level:"Very senior managers (VSM)",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"12.7% (Black and minority ethnic, as published)",year:"March 2024",status:"confirmed",note:"The Black and minority ethnic share of very senior managers is less than half the share of all trust staff.",src:[SRCQ("One in eight in senior NHS staff from black and minority ethnic backgrounds (NHS England, June 2025; WRES 2024 report)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/2025/06/one-in-eight-in-senior-nhs-staff-from-black-and-minority-ethnic-backgrounds/","Supports the Representation across the state page","12.7% of staff at very senior manager level are from these backgrounds.")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"State-funded schools (England)",level:"All teachers",order:0,black:"2.9%",mixed:"1.9%",em:"11.4%",year:"2024 to 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Published table (EFF, by ethnicity and role CSV), share of known ethnicity. Black 12,992. Ethnic minority computed as 100 minus White 88.6% (includes Other). Matches hub eqi-education-2.",src:[SRCQ("School teacher workforce by ethnicity and role, CSV (2024 to 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest/downloads/by-ethnicity-and-role-table.csv","Supports the Representation across the state page","Data file: figures read from the published table."),SRCQ("School teacher workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, 2024 to 2025 academic year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest","Supports the Representation across the state page","88.6% of all teachers in state-funded schools were White, and 5.8% were Asian")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"State-funded schools (England)",level:"Classroom teachers",order:0,black:"3.1%",mixed:"1.9%",em:"12.2%",year:"2024 to 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Published table (EFF, by ethnicity and role CSV), share of known ethnicity. Black 11,745. Ethnic minority computed as 100 minus White 87.8%.",src:[SRCQ("School teacher workforce by ethnicity and role, CSV (2024 to 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest/downloads/by-ethnicity-and-role-table.csv","Supports the Representation across the state page","Data file: figures read from the published table.")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"State-funded schools (England)",level:"Assistant headteachers",order:3,black:"2.4%",mixed:"1.7%",em:"8.8%",year:"2024 to 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Published table (EFF, by ethnicity and role CSV), share of known ethnicity. Black 723. Ethnic minority computed as 100 minus White 91.2%.",src:[SRCQ("School teacher workforce by ethnicity and role, CSV (2024 to 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest/downloads/by-ethnicity-and-role-table.csv","Supports the Representation across the state page","Data file: figures read from the published table.")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"State-funded schools (England)",level:"Deputy headteachers",order:4,black:"1.6%",mixed:"1.4%",em:"6.4%",year:"2024 to 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Published table (EFF, by ethnicity and role CSV), share of known ethnicity. Black 276. Ethnic minority computed as 100 minus White 93.6%.",src:[SRCQ("School teacher workforce by ethnicity and role, CSV (2024 to 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest/downloads/by-ethnicity-and-role-table.csv","Supports the Representation across the state page","Data file: figures read from the published table.")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"State-funded schools (England)",level:"Headteachers",order:5,black:"1.2%",mixed:"1.1%",em:"4.7%",year:"2024 to 2025",status:"confirmed",note:"Published table (EFF, by ethnicity and role CSV), share of known ethnicity. Black 248 of 20,310 known. Ethnic minority computed as 100 minus White 95.3%. Matches hub.",src:[SRCQ("School teacher workforce by ethnicity and role, CSV (2024 to 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest/downloads/by-ethnicity-and-role-table.csv","Supports the Representation across the state page","Data file: figures read from the published table."),SRCQ("School teacher workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, 2024 to 2025 academic year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/school-teacher-workforce/latest","Supports the Representation across the state page","91.8% of headteachers were White British")]},{branch:"Public services",inst:"Local government (England)",level:"Councillors",order:6,black:"",mixed:"",em:"",year:"2022",status:"not confirmed",note:"The LGA 2022 councillor census exists (30% response rate) but its ethnicity results is not cited here. Not confirmed by the sources cited. Council chief executives by ethnicity: no published official data found.",src:[SRCQ("National census of local authority councillors 2022 (LGA)","https://www.local.gov.uk/publications/national-census-local-authority-councillors-2022","Supports the Representation across the state page","a response rate of 30 per cent")]}],poom:[{"ring_or_role": "Principal Private Secretary and the private office core (inner ring)", "interfaces_with": "Executive: permanent secretary, SCS directors, Cabinet Office (Propriety and Ethics, Public Appointments)", "data_it_could_hold": "Ethnicity profile of the private office team by grade, set against departmental Table 2 grade shares; Black and ethnic minority shares of public appointment shortlists put to the minister.", "note": "Analysis, not fact. No published private office diversity data exists."}, {"ring_or_role": "Parliamentary clerk / PQ and correspondence units (second ring)", "interfaces_with": "Legislative: MPs, peers, select committees (PQs, letters, evidence sessions)", "data_it_could_hold": "Counts of PQs and correspondence on race equality topics; links to Commons Library and EFF tables for answers; tracking of commitments made in the House.", "note": "Analysis, not fact."}, {"ring_or_role": "Diary and visits secretaries", "interfaces_with": "Public services and communities (police forces, NHS trusts, schools, councils)", "data_it_could_hold": "Whether visits and stakeholder roundtables reach Black-led organisations; local representation benchmarks (force-level police data, trust-level WRES).", "note": "Analysis, not fact."}, {"ring_or_role": "Private secretaries for specific policy portfolios", "interfaces_with": "Executive agencies and arm’s-length bodies; public appointments; departmental workforce leads", "data_it_could_hold": "Board diversity of sponsored bodies; ministerial appointments data by stage (applied, shortlisted, appointed); departmental ethnicity pay gap.", "note": "Analysis, not fact."}, {"ring_or_role": "Outer ring: legal advisers and the Law Officers interface", "interfaces_with": "Judicial: very limited, because of judicial independence; mainly via MoJ, the Lord Chancellor and the JAC", "data_it_could_hold": "Read-only reference: MoJ judicial diversity tables and JAC outcomes. A private office should not hold or seek data on individual judges.", "note": "Analysis, not fact. Only the Lord Chancellor’s private office has a formal role (judicial appointments concurrence)."}],gaps:["No published ethnicity data on ministerial private office staff (private secretaries, PPSs, diary and correspondence teams).", "No official count of ethnic minority or Black members of the current Cabinet or ministerial team.", "No official count of Black MPs; the Commons Library uses British Future estimates for ethnic minority MPs.", "No sourced count of ethnic minority or Black permanent secretaries.", "No sourced ethnicity data for ambassadors or heads of mission (FCDO).", "Ethnicity outcome data for the Future Leaders Scheme, Senior Leaders Scheme and positive action development schemes not found.", "House of Lords, Holyrood, Senedd and NI Assembly figures are dated (2021-22); official figures for the 2026 devolved intakes not confirmed.", "House of Commons and House of Lords administration workforce ethnicity not cited here.", "WRES 2025 (published April 2026) headline figures not cited here; WRES reports a combined ‘BME’ group only.", "Police senior ranks published as ethnic minority only, with no Black breakdown in the bulletin.", "Ethnicity results of the LGA councillor census not cited here; no data on ethnic minority council chief executives.", "Public appointments publish ethnic minority totals only, with no Black breakdown and no target found.", "The Supreme Court is outside the MoJ judicial diversity statistics."]};
LP["deeia"]={name:"The DEEIA lens",pfx:"dx",kpis:[{s:"measure",h:"Civil Service ethnicity pay gap",n:"4.5%",l:"median hourly pay gap, Black against White civil servants (2025)",ic:"scale"},{s:"policy",h:"May 2024: EDI spending review and controls",n:"£27.1m",l:"spent on equality, diversity and inclusion across government in 2022-23, before spending controls",ic:"chart"},{s:"measure",h:"People Survey: inclusion and fair treatment by ethnicity (2025)",n:"80%",l:"inclusion and fair treatment score for ethnic minority staff, against 81% for White staff (2025)",ic:"hands"},{s:"access",h:"Disability by ethnic group (Census 2021)",n:"22.4%",l:"of people of Mixed White and Black Caribbean ethnicity in England were disabled (age-standardised, 2021)",ic:"heart"}],sections:[
 {k:"terms",h:"What the five terms mean",intro:"Official and academic sources broadly agree on diversity, equality and inclusion. Equity is the term most often disputed. Accessibility is the one with the clearest legal test.",points:[
  {h:"Equality means fair treatment and equal opportunity",text:"Acas defines workplace equality as equal job opportunities and fairness, and links it to not treating people unfairly because of a protected characteristic.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("What they are - Equality, diversity and inclusion (Acas)","https://www.acas.org.uk/improving-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Equality in the workplace means equal job opportunities and fairness for employees and job applicants."),SRCQ("What they are - Equality, diversity and inclusion (Acas)","https://www.acas.org.uk/improving-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","You must not treat people unfairly because of reasons protected by discrimination law ('protected characteristics').")]},
  {h:"Diversity describes the mix of people",text:"Acas defines diversity as the range of people in a workforce, and as valuing those differences.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("What they are - Equality, diversity and inclusion (Acas)","https://www.acas.org.uk/improving-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Diversity is the range of people in your workforce.")]},
  {h:"Inclusion is about being valued and safe to speak up",text:"Acas says an inclusive workplace lets everyone feel valued and safe to raise ideas and issues.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("What they are - Equality, diversity and inclusion (Acas)","https://www.acas.org.uk/improving-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","An inclusive workplace means everyone feels valued at work.")]},
  {h:"Equity is not the same as equality",text:"A university glossary separates equality (the same opportunities) from equity (giving people what they need because starting points differ). It also says equity aims at equal outcomes.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Contested. Defining equity as \"equality of outcomes\" is the point critics object to. UK statutory duties are framed as \"equality of opportunity\" (s149), not equity.",src:[SRCQ("Glossary of terms - Equality, diversity and inclusion (University of Bath)","https://www.bath.ac.uk/guides/glossary-of-terms/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Equality is ensuring everyone has the same opportunities, whereas equity is about giving people what they need")]},
  {h:"Belonging is now part of how inclusion is defined",text:"University and professional-body sources describe inclusion as a sense of belonging in which people can feel safe and do their best work.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Most of the CIPD factsheet is behind a members' login. Only the open summary was checked.",src:[SRCQ("Glossary of terms - Equality, diversity and inclusion (University of Bath)","https://www.bath.ac.uk/guides/glossary-of-terms/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Inclusion is a sense of belonging, where individuals feel valued and able to thrive in their environment"),SRCQ("Equality, diversity and inclusion (EDI) in the workplace - CIPD factsheet (11 Feb 2026)","https://www.cipd.org/uk/knowledge/factsheets/diversity-factsheet/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","every individual can feel safe, experience a sense of belonging, and empowered to perform to the best of their abilities.")]},
  {h:"Accessibility has a technical standard",text:"For public sector digital services, accessibility means meeting WCAG 2.2 AA and publishing an accessibility statement.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Understanding accessibility requirements for public sector bodies (GOV.UK, GDS)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","meet the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 AA accessibility standard"),SRCQ("Understanding accessibility requirements for public sector bodies (GOV.UK, GDS)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","publish an accessibility statement that explains how accessible your website or mobile app is")]},
  {h:"The vocabulary itself is politically contested",text:"The 2025 US executive orders treat \"equity\" programmes as discriminatory. A 2024 UK ministerial statement warned against seeing opportunity \"through the prism of identity politics\". The UK statutory duty still requires public bodies to advance equality of opportunity.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Presented to show the range of official positions. The hub does not take a side.",src:[SRCQ("Ending Radical and Wasteful Government DEI Programs and Preferencing (White House, 20 January 2025)","https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/ending-radical-and-wasteful-government-dei-programs-and-preferencing/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","all “equity action plans,” “equity” actions, initiatives, or programs, “equity-related” grants or contracts"),SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","lack of opportunity should not be seen through the prism of identity politics"),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 149 (Public sector equality duty)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","advance equality of opportunity between persons who share a relevant protected characteristic and persons who do not share it")]}]},
 {k:"law",h:"UK legal frame",intro:"The Equality Act 2010 covers England, Wales and Scotland. Northern Ireland has its own section 75 duty. The equality duties and the digital accessibility regulations apply directly to public bodies.",points:[
  {h:"Nine protected characteristics, including race",text:"Section 4 lists nine protected characteristics. Race is one of them.",figure:"",year:"2010",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 4 (The protected characteristics)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/4","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The following characteristics are protected characteristics— age; disability; gender reassignment; marriage and civil partnership; pregnancy and maternity; race; religion or belief; sex; sexual orientation.")]},
  {h:"What \"race\" means in law",text:"Section 9 defines race to include colour, nationality and ethnic or national origins.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 9 (Race)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/9","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Race includes— (a) colour; (b) nationality; (c) ethnic or national origins.")]},
  {h:"Positive action is lawful within limits (s158)",text:"Organisations may take proportionate action where a group faces disadvantage, has different needs, or is under-represented.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 158 (Positive action: general)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/158","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","This Act does not prohibit P from taking any action which is a proportionate means of achieving the aim of—"),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 158 (Positive action: general)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/158","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","participation in an activity by persons who share a protected characteristic is disproportionately low.")]},
  {h:"The recruitment \"tie-break\" (s159)",text:"Preferring a candidate because of a protected characteristic is allowed only if they are as qualified, there is no blanket policy of preference, and it is proportionate.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Where positive action ends and unlawful positive discrimination begins is a frequent point of dispute. The 2024 UK ministerial statement cited \"positive discrimination\" as a concern.",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 159 (Positive action: recruitment and promotion)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/159","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","A is as qualified as B to be recruited or promoted,"),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 159 (Positive action: recruitment and promotion)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/159","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","P does not have a policy of treating persons who share the protected characteristic more favourably in connection with recruitment or promotion")]},
  {h:"Public sector equality duty (s149)",text:"Public authorities must have due regard to eliminating discrimination, advancing equality of opportunity and fostering good relations. The duty may involve treating some people more favourably.",figure:"",year:"2010",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 149 (Public sector equality duty)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","A public authority must, in the exercise of its functions, have due regard to the need to—"),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 149 (Public sector equality duty)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Compliance with the duties in this section may involve treating some persons more favourably than others")]},
  {h:"Scotland: specific duties",text:"Scottish listed authorities have extra duties, including setting equality outcomes, assessing policies, using employee information and publishing in accessible ways.",figure:"",year:"2012",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties) (Scotland) Regulations 2012 (SSI 2012/162)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ssi/2012/162/contents/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Duty to publish equality outcomes and report progress"),SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties) (Scotland) Regulations 2012 (SSI 2012/162)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ssi/2012/162/contents/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Duty to gather and use employee information"),SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties) (Scotland) Regulations 2012 (SSI 2012/162)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ssi/2012/162/contents/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Duty to publish in a manner that is accessible, etc.")]},
  {h:"Wales: statutory duties",text:"Welsh listed authorities must set equality objectives, engage people, assess impact, gather employment and pay information, and publish Strategic Equality Plans.",figure:"",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Statutory Duties) (Wales) Regulations 2011 (WSI 2011/1064)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2011/1064/contents/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Equality objectives"),SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Statutory Duties) (Wales) Regulations 2011 (WSI 2011/1064)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2011/1064/contents/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Strategic Equality Plans"),SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Statutory Duties) (Wales) Regulations 2011 (WSI 2011/1064)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2011/1064/contents/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Impact and monitoring of policies and practices")]},
  {h:"Northern Ireland: section 75",text:"Public authorities in Northern Ireland must have due regard to promoting equality of opportunity between groups, including by racial group.",figure:"",year:"1998",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Northern Ireland Act 1998, section 75 (Statutory duty on public authorities)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/47/section/75","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","A public authority shall in carrying out its functions relating to Northern Ireland have due regard to the need to promote equality of opportunity"),SRCQ("Northern Ireland Act 1998, section 75 (Statutory duty on public authorities)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/47/section/75","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","between persons of different religious belief, political opinion, racial group, age, marital status or sexual orientation")]},
  {h:"Digital accessibility regulations",text:"Public sector websites and apps must meet WCAG 2.2 AA and publish an accessibility statement. The regulations came into force on 23 September 2018.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"The correct title includes \"(No. 2)\".",src:[SRCQ("The Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018 (SI 2018/952)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2018/952/contents","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018"),SRCQ("Understanding accessibility requirements for public sector bodies (GOV.UK, GDS)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The accessibility regulations came into force for public sector bodies on 23 September 2018."),SRCQ("Understanding accessibility requirements for public sector bodies (GOV.UK, GDS)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The accessibility regulations build on your existing obligations to people who have a disability under the Equality Act 2010")]},
  {h:"WCAG 2.2 is the current W3C Recommendation",text:"The current published version of WCAG 2.2 is dated 12 December 2024. GOV.UK updated its guidance for the new WCAG 2.2 criteria on 5 October 2023.",figure:"WCAG 2.2 AA",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 (W3C)","https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","W3C Recommendation 12 December 2024"),SRCQ("Understanding accessibility requirements for public sector bodies (GOV.UK, GDS)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","5 October 2023 Updated guidance as result of the new success criteria in WCAG 2.2.")]},
  {h:"Socio-economic duty (s1)",text:"Section 1 requires listed authorities to consider reducing inequalities of outcome caused by socio-economic disadvantage when making strategic decisions.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 1 (Public sector duty regarding socio-economic inequalities)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/1","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","designed to reduce the inequalities of outcome which result from socio-economic disadvantage")]},
  {h:"Socio-economic duty: commenced in Scotland (2018) and Wales (2021)",text:"The duty came into force in Scotland on 1 April 2018 and in Wales on 31 March 2021. The legislation.gov.uk commencement notes list only Scotland and Wales.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Commencement in England is not confirmed by the sources cited. The legislation.gov.uk annotations record commencement for Scotland and Wales only. The Scottish duty is widely called the Fairer Scotland Duty, but that name is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Authorities subject to the Socio-economic Inequality Duty) (Scotland) Regulations 2018 (SSI 2018/101)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ssi/2018/101/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Coming into force 1st April 2018"),SRCQ("The Equality Act 2010 (Commencement No. 15) (Wales) Order 2021 (WSI 2021/298)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2021/298/made","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","come into force on 31 March 2021"),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 1 (commencement information)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/1","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","S. 1 in force at 31.3.2021 for specified purposes for W. by S.I. 2021/298")]},
  {h:"Socio-economic status is not a protected characteristic",text:"The Welsh Government notes that the Act does not make socio-economic status a protected characteristic, so the EHRC cannot use its full enforcement powers on this duty.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Relevant to the young lives (NEET/BEET/CHEET) content, where class and race interact.",src:[SRCQ("Socio-economic Duty: an overview (Welsh Government, last updated 12 July 2024)","https://www.gov.wales/socio-economic-duty-overview","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","the 2010 Act does not establish ‘socio-economic discrimination’ – socio-economic status – as a protected characteristic in the Act")]}]},
 {k:"policy",h:"Civil Service and government policy, 2022 to 2026",intro:"The Civil Service D&I Strategy covered 2022 to 2025. From May 2024, EDI spending controls applied. These were still in operation in 2026. No successor strategy has been found.",points:[
  {h:"Civil Service D&I Strategy 2022 to 2025",text:"The strategy set four objectives: a Civil Service that draws from the communities it serves, is visible, is flexible, and welcomes talent from all backgrounds.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy: 2022 to 2025 (policy paper, 24 February 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The strategy contains 4 objectives"),SRCQ("Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy: 2022 to 2025 (policy paper, 24 February 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","This strategy sets the standard for diversity and inclusion in the Civil Service from 2022 to 2025."),SRCQ("Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy: 2022 to 2025 (policy paper, 24 February 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","welcomes talent from wherever it comes – attracting the best talent from all backgrounds")]},
  {h:"May 2024: EDI spending review and controls",text:"A Cabinet Office review found £27.1 million of EDI spend across 95 Civil Service organisations in 2022-23. Ministers ended external EDI spending unless they approved it, and folded standalone EDI roles into HR.",figure:"£27.1m (2022-23, 95 organisations)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","total EDI expenditure for the 2022-23 financial year was £27.1 million."),SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Standalone EDI roles will also cease to exist outside of HR"),SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","an end to all external spending on EDI activity, unless cleared and authorised by Ministers.")]},
  {h:"The guidance overrode part of the 2022 strategy",text:"The 2024 expenditure guidance says it supersedes the strategy's earlier rules on authorising spend.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"The GOV.UK page was live and not marked as withdrawn when checked on 24 October 2026.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Expenditure Guidance (Cabinet Office, May 2024; deposited paper DEP2024-0493)","https://data.parliament.uk/DepositedPapers/Files/DEP2024-0493/Civil_Service_Equality_Diversity_and_Inclusion_Guidance.docx.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Cease all external EDI spend in the Civil Service unless cleared and authorised by Ministers;"),SRCQ("Civil Service Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Expenditure Guidance (Cabinet Office, May 2024; deposited paper DEP2024-0493)","https://data.parliament.uk/DepositedPapers/Files/DEP2024-0493/Civil_Service_Equality_Diversity_and_Inclusion_Guidance.docx.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","This supersedes the policy on authorisation originally set out in the Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2022 - 2025."),SRCQ("Civil Service Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Expenditure Guidance (GOV.UK page)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-equality-diversity-and-inclusion-expenditure-guidance","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Published: 14 May 2024")]},
  {h:"Disability support was exempt",text:"The 2024 changes excluded support for disabled staff.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Support for disability will be excluded from these changes, recognising the particular needs of disabled staff.")]},
  {h:"The spending controls continued after the change of government",text:"In September 2026 the Cabinet Office said it had published external EDI spend for 2024/25 and would do the same for 2025/26. The reported 2024/25 total was about £80,000.",figure:"£79,888.15 (2024/25 external, reported)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The £79,888 covers only external spend that departments reported. The £27.1m in 2022-23 covered all EDI spend, including roles. The two figures are not comparable.",src:[SRCQ("Arms Length Bodies: Equality - written question 20656, answered 10 September 2026","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-questions/detail/2026-08-28/20656","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","All external Equality, Diversity and Inclusion expenditure for the financial year 2024/2025 that was reported to the Cabinet Office has been published."),SRCQ("Arms Length Bodies: Equality - written question 20656, answered 10 September 2026","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-questions/detail/2026-08-28/20656","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The same principle will be applied for 2025/2026."),SRCQ("Civil Service 2024/2025 External Expenditure on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (Cabinet Office, last updated 29 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-equality-diversity-and-inclusion-external-expenditure/civil-service-20242025-external-expenditure-on-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Total Expenditure £79,888.15 + VAT where applicable")]},
  {h:"Staff networks: new policy in September 2025",text:"A Civil Service Staff Network Policy was launched on 23 September 2025.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"A successor to the 2022 to 2025 D&I Strategy is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service: Equality - written question 31205, answered 19 October 2026","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-questions/detail/2026-10-09/31205","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The Civil Service Staff Network Policy was launched on 23 September 2025.")]},
  {h:"Ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: government response (March 2026)",text:"On 25 March 2026 the government published its consultation response and draft clauses. Reporting would apply to employers with 250 or more staff, mirror gender pay gap reporting, include workforce composition and declaration rates, and require action plans.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Public bodies will be encouraged, not required, to report by grade, recruitment, retention and progression.",src:[SRCQ("Equality (Race and Disability) Bill: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting - consultation and outcome (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","draft clauses of primary legislation to introduce mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting"),SRCQ("Equality (Race and Disability) Bill: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting - consultation and outcome (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","apply mandatory reporting to employers with 250 or more employees"),SRCQ("Equality (Race and Disability) Bill: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting - consultation and outcome (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","we will not be mandating the additional reporting requirements for public bodies as proposed in the consultation")]},
  {h:"Equality (Race and Disability) Bill: not yet introduced",text:"A March 2026 law-firm briefing said the Bill was still awaited. A search of the UK Parliament Bills database on 24 October 2026 found no bill with this title.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Introduction to Parliament is not confirmed by the sources cited. The quote comes from a secondary source (a law firm). The Bills API search was for \"Equality\", sorted by date updated.",src:[SRCQ("Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting (Linklaters Employment Links blog, March 2026) - secondary source","https://www.linklaters.com/insights/blogs/employmentlinks/2026/march/mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","sitting alongside the promised Equality (Race and Disability) Bill (which is still awaited)")]},
  {h:"A bill to repeal the PSED is before Parliament",text:"A private member's bill to repeal section 149 was presented in the House of Commons on 22 June 2026. Its second reading is listed for 5 March 2027.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:true,note:"Sponsor: Joy Morrissey MP. Second reading is listed for 5 March 2027. It is a private member's bill, not government policy, and not law.",src:[SRCQ("Public Sector Equality Duty (Repeal) Bill (UK Parliament Bills)","https://bills.parliament.uk/bills/4189","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","A Bill to repeal section 149 and schedule 18 of the Equality Act 2010"),SRCQ("Public Sector Equality Duty (Repeal) Bill (UK Parliament Bills)","https://bills.parliament.uk/bills/4189","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Joy Morrissey"),SRCQ("Public Sector Equality Duty (Repeal) Bill (UK Parliament Bills)","https://bills.parliament.uk/bills/4189","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","2nd reading")]}]},
 {k:"debate",h:"The debate: for and against DEI programmes",intro:"This section sets out the strongest case on each side, taken from official and professional sources. Most critics target particular interventions, such as unconscious bias training and preferential treatment, or their value for money. The legal duties stay in place either way.",points:[
  {h:"US: Executive Order 14151 (20 January 2025)",text:"Directs federal agencies to end DEI and DEIA offices, posts, equity action plans and related performance requirements.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Neutral summary of the order's text. Later litigation and implementation are not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("Ending Radical and Wasteful Government DEI Programs and Preferencing (White House, 20 January 2025)","https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/ending-radical-and-wasteful-government-dei-programs-and-preferencing/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","terminate, to the maximum extent allowed by law, all DEI, DEIA, and “environmental justice” offices and positions"),SRCQ("Executive Order 14151, 90 FR 8339 (Federal Register)","https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/01/29/2025-01953/ending-radical-and-wasteful-government-dei-programs-and-preferencing","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Ending Radical and Wasteful Government DEI Programs and Preferencing")]},
  {h:"US: Executive Order 14173 (21 January 2025)",text:"Revokes earlier orders, including Executive Order 11246 on equal employment opportunity for federal contractors. Says illegal DEI policies undermine merit.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"This is the order's own stated reasoning, quoted as the case made by its authors.",src:[SRCQ("Ending Illegal Discrimination and Restoring Merit-Based Opportunity (White House, 21 January 2025)","https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/ending-illegal-discrimination-and-restoring-merit-based-opportunity/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Executive Order 11246 of September 24, 1965 (Equal Employment Opportunity), is hereby revoked."),SRCQ("Ending Illegal Discrimination and Restoring Merit-Based Opportunity (White House, 21 January 2025)","https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/01/ending-illegal-discrimination-and-restoring-merit-based-opportunity/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","deny, discredit, and undermine the traditional American values of hard work, excellence, and individual achievement"),SRCQ("Executive Order 14173, 90 FR 8633 (Federal Register)","https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/01/31/2025-02097/ending-illegal-discrimination-and-restoring-merit-based-opportunity","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Ending Illegal Discrimination and Restoring Merit-Based Opportunity")]},
  {h:"UK official response to the US orders",text:"No official UK government statement responding to the US executive orders was found.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Not confirmed by the sources cited. The UK Parliament written questions search and a web search found only press and law-firm reports, which are excluded under the official-sources rule.",ed:true,src:[]},
  {h:"Against: value for money and evidence (UK, 2024)",text:"Ministers said £27.1m of EDI spend was poor value. They cited the Inclusion at Work Panel finding that some interventions were counterproductive.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:true,note:"The Inclusion at Work Panel report is cited from the statement only. It is not covered here directly.",src:[SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","We do not believe that this represents value for money."),SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion in the Civil Service - written statement HCWS464, 14 May 2024","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2024-05-14/HCWS464","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","EDI interventions are proving to be counterproductive.")]},
  {h:"Against: unconscious bias training phased out (2020)",text:"The Cabinet Office phased out unconscious bias training in the Civil Service after an evidence review.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Unconscious Bias Training - written statement HCWS652, Cabinet Office, 15 December 2020","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2020-12-15/HCWS652","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Ministers have concluded that unconscious bias training does not achieve its intended aims."),SRCQ("Unconscious Bias Training - written statement HCWS652, Cabinet Office, 15 December 2020","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-statements/detail/2020-12-15/HCWS652","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","It will therefore be phased out in the Civil Service.")]},
  {h:"The evidence behind that decision",text:"The Behavioural Insights Team review found no evidence that unconscious bias training changes behaviour or improves representation. It described possible backfire effects.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:true,note:"Strongest point for critics. The same review also notes that awareness effects exist in the short term.",src:[SRCQ("Unconscious bias and diversity training - what the evidence says (Behavioural Insights Team for GEO, December 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5fd8c859e90e071be5f30531/20-12-14_UBT_BIT_report.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","there is currently no evidence that this training changes behaviour or improves workplace equality"),SRCQ("Unconscious bias and diversity training - what the evidence says (Behavioural Insights Team for GEO, December 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5fd8c859e90e071be5f30531/20-12-14_UBT_BIT_report.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","UBT can be effective for reducing implicit bias, but there is no evidence that it can eliminate it.")]},
  {h:"The CIPD agrees on the limits of training",text:"The CIPD found that teaching about unconscious bias can backfire. It recommends perspective-taking and treats training as one part of a wider programme.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","this has been seen to make people more comfortable with their biases and lead to greater observed bias."),SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","not as a magic bullet that will change behaviour by itself.")]},
  {h:"For: what does work, according to the evidence",text:"The CIPD found that training works under certain conditions and that positive action should be central to D&I strategy.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:true,note:"Strongest point for supporters: the evidence criticises particular methods, not the goal.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","diversity training can be effective in promoting knowledge and skills when certain conditions are met."),SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Positive action programmes should form a central part of any D&I strategy.")]},
  {h:"For: performance and problem-solving",text:"The Civil Service strategy and Acas link diversity and inclusion to performance, problem-solving and preventing discrimination.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"These are claims of benefit made by the bodies themselves. The hub has not independently tested them.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2022 to 2025 (HTML)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025-html","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Team diversity is crucial to organisational performance."),SRCQ("What they are - Equality, diversity and inclusion (Acas)","https://www.acas.org.uk/improving-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","improve ideas and problem-solving"),SRCQ("What they are - Equality, diversity and inclusion (Acas)","https://www.acas.org.uk/improving-equality-diversity-and-inclusion","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","prevent serious or legal issues arising, such as bullying, harassment and discrimination")]},
  {h:"Common ground: the legal floor remains",text:"However programmes are judged, the PSED and the Equality Act duties still bind public bodies. Disability support was exempted from the 2024 cuts.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"A private member's bill to repeal s149 exists (see policy). It is not law.",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 149 (Public sector equality duty)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","A public authority must, in the exercise of its functions, have due regard to the need to—"),SRCQ("Civil Service Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Expenditure Guidance (Cabinet Office, May 2024; deposited paper DEP2024-0493)","https://data.parliament.uk/DepositedPapers/Files/DEP2024-0493/Civil_Service_Equality_Diversity_and_Inclusion_Guidance.docx.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","legally sound and in line with the Equality Act 2010.")]}]},
 {k:"access",h:"Accessibility, disability and ethnicity",intro:"Disability rates vary by ethnic group. Sickle cell disorder, which mainly affects people of African and Caribbean heritage, can count as a disability. Special educational needs are identified at different rates for different ethnic groups.",points:[
  {h:"Disability by ethnic group (Census 2021)",text:"Among ethnic groups, the second-highest age-standardised disability rate was for people of White and Black Caribbean ethnicity: 22.4% in England. The highest was for White Gypsy or Irish Traveller people.",figure:"22.4% (England, Mixed White and Black Caribbean)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Black African and Black Caribbean figures are in the ONS data download and were not extracted. Lowest: Chinese, 8.6% in England.",src:[SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","(22.4% in England and 26.7% in Wales)"),SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","(33.2% in England and 39.1% in Wales)"),SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","(8.6% in England and 9.3% in Wales)"),SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The highest age-standardised rates of disability occurred for people who identified with the ethnic groups")]},
  {h:"Why the figures are age-standardised",text:"Disability rises with age and ethnic groups have different age profiles, so the ONS standardises for age. These figures cannot be compared with crude rates.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The second quote is cut short to match the page text exactly.",src:[SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Percentages in this article have been age-standardised because of the close relationship between disability and age."),SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Standardised data are not comparable with other publishe")]},
  {h:"The census definition matches the Equality Act",text:"The census counts people as disabled if a long-term condition limits their day-to-day activities. The ONS says this aligns with the Equality Act.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group differences in health, employment, education and housing shown in England and Wales' Census 2021 (ONS, 15 March 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/articles/ethnicgroupdifferencesinhealthemploymenteducationandhousingshowninenglandandwalescensus2021/2023-03-15","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","This aligns with the Equality Act (2010) definition."),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 6 (Disability)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/6","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","the impairment has a substantial and long-term adverse effect on P's ability to carry out normal day-to-day activities")]},
  {h:"Is sickle cell a disability in law?",text:"Not automatically. A university guide produced with the sickle cell community says most people with the diagnosis are likely to meet the Equality Act definition.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:true,note:"Disability status depends on the individual case: the Section 6 test of substantial and long-term adverse effect applies.",src:[SRCQ("Sickle Cell Work and Employment: A Guide for Employers and Employees (De Montfort University, v1.1 2019)","https://sicklecellwork.dmu.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/PDF-Version_Guide-To-Sickle-Cell-and-Employment-Version_1.1_2019.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","In law, a diagnosis of sickle cell disorder (SCD) is not automatically a disability"),SRCQ("Sickle Cell Work and Employment: A Guide for Employers and Employees (De Montfort University, v1.1 2019)","https://sicklecellwork.dmu.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/PDF-Version_Guide-To-Sickle-Cell-and-Employment-Version_1.1_2019.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","it is likely that most people with the diagnosis will fall within the definition of disabled for the purposes of the Equality Act 2010")]},
  {h:"'No One's Listening' (APPG, November 2021)",text:"The APPG on Sickle Cell and Thalassaemia inquiry found sub-standard secondary care. It said negative attitudes towards patients are often underpinned by racism.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The Sickle Cell Society's own link to the report returned 'page not found' when checked. The copy cited is held as evidence by the Infected Blood Inquiry.",src:[SRCQ("No One's Listening: an inquiry into the avoidable deaths and failures of care for sickle cell patients in secondary care (APPG on Sickle Cell and Thalassaemia, Nov 2021; copy held by the Infected Blood Inquiry, ref RLIT0001928)","https://www.infectedbloodinquiry.org.uk/sites/default/files/documents/RLIT0001928%20-%20NO%20ONE%E2%80%99S%20LISTENINGAN%20INQUIRY%20INTO%20THE%20AVOIDABLE%20DEATHS%20AND%20FAILURES%20OF%20CARE%20FOR%20SICKLE%20CELL%20PATIENTS%20IN%20SECONDARY%20CARE%20-%2001%20Nov%202021.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The weight of the evidence suggests that such negative attitudes towards sickle cell patients are also often underpinned by racism."),SRCQ("No One's Listening: an inquiry into the avoidable deaths and failures of care for sickle cell patients in secondary care (APPG on Sickle Cell and Thalassaemia, Nov 2021; copy held by the Infected Blood Inquiry, ref RLIT0001928)","https://www.infectedbloodinquiry.org.uk/sites/default/files/documents/RLIT0001928%20-%20NO%20ONE%E2%80%99S%20LISTENINGAN%20INQUIRY%20INTO%20THE%20AVOIDABLE%20DEATHS%20AND%20FAILURES%20OF%20CARE%20FOR%20SICKLE%20CELL%20PATIENTS%20IN%20SECONDARY%20CARE%20-%2001%20Nov%202021.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","AN INQUIRY INTO THE AVOIDABLE DEATHS AND FAILURES OF CARE FOR SICKLE CELL PATIENTS IN SECONDARY CARE")]},
  {h:"Autism and SEMH: identified at different rates by ethnic group",text:"DfE-funded research found that Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean pupils were twice as likely as White British pupils to be identified with SEMH needs. Asian pupils were half as likely to be identified with autism.",figure:"2x SEMH identification (Black Caribbean vs White British)",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"Under-identification of autism among Black pupils is not confirmed by the sources cited. This research found autism under-identification for Asian pupils.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic minority children disproportionately identified with Special Education Needs (University of Oxford Department of Education, 12 February 2019) - on DfE-funded research by Prof Steve Strand","https://www.education.ox.ac.uk/ethnic-minority-children-disproportionately-identified-with-special-education-needs-sen","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Black Caribbean and Mixed White & Black Caribbean pupils are twice as likely to be identified with Social, Emotional and Mental Health (SEMH) needs"),SRCQ("Ethnic minority children disproportionately identified with Special Education Needs (University of Oxford Department of Education, 12 February 2019) - on DfE-funded research by Prof Steve Strand","https://www.education.ox.ac.uk/ethnic-minority-children-disproportionately-identified-with-special-education-needs-sen","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Asian pupils (Indian, Pakistani, Bangladeshi and Other Asian) are half as likely to be identified with Autistic Spectrum Disorders (ASD)")]},
  {h:"What over-identification may mean",text:"The researcher warned that over-identification of SEMH may narrow the curriculum for some Black Caribbean children. Differences between schools mattered most.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"This interpretation comes from the researcher. The DfE's own position is not shown in the quotes.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic minority children disproportionately identified with Special Education Needs (University of Oxford Department of Education, 12 February 2019) - on DfE-funded research by Prof Steve Strand","https://www.education.ox.ac.uk/ethnic-minority-children-disproportionately-identified-with-special-education-needs-sen","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","some Black Caribbean children may be suffering an inappropriate or narrowed curriculum from unwarranted over-identification"),SRCQ("Ethnic minority children disproportionately identified with Special Education Needs (University of Oxford Department of Education, 12 February 2019) - on DfE-funded research by Prof Steve Strand","https://www.education.ox.ac.uk/ethnic-minority-children-disproportionately-identified-with-special-education-needs-sen","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","variation between schools is more influential in the over-representation of Black Caribbean and Mixed White & Black Caribbean students for SEMH")]},
  {h:"Latest SEN statistics (2025/26)",text:"DfE official statistics break SEN down by ethnicity. The highest rate is in the Traveller of Irish heritage group and the lowest is in the Chinese group.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Figures for Black ethnic groups are in the DfE table tool and were not extracted.",src:[SRCQ("Special educational needs in England, academic year 2025/26 (DfE, accredited official statistics, published 11 June 2026)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/special-educational-needs-in-england/2025-26","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","SEN is most prevalent in the Traveller of Irish heritage ethnic group"),SRCQ("Special educational needs in England, academic year 2025/26 (DfE, accredited official statistics, published 11 June 2026)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/special-educational-needs-in-england/2025-26","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The lowest rate for SEN is in the Chinese ethnic group")]}]},
 {k:"measure",h:"Measuring DEEIA: published metrics",intro:"This section uses only published, official metrics: Civil Service representation and pay gaps, the People Survey and the NHS WRES.",points:[
  {h:"Ethnic minority representation in the Civil Service",text:"Overall, the Civil Service is close to parity: 19.2% of staff with a known ethnicity are from an ethnic minority, against 19.6% of the economically active working-age population.",figure:"19.2%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (Cabinet Office, 29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","19.2% are from an ethnic minority background, up from 18.0% in 2025."),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (Cabinet Office, 29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","economically active working age population in the UK (19.6%)")]},
  {h:"Representation falls with seniority",text:"Ethnic minority representation is lowest in the Senior Civil Service, at 10.6%.",figure:"10.6% (SCS)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (Cabinet Office, 29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%")]},
  {h:"Black civil servants",text:"Black civil servants are the second-largest ethnic minority group by headcount.",figure:"26,655",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (Cabinet Office, 29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","followed by Black civil servants (26,655)")]},
  {h:"Civil Service ethnicity pay gap",text:"Median hourly pay for Black civil servants was 4.5% lower than for White civil servants. Black civil servants were the least likely to receive a bonus.",figure:"4.5% median gap (Black vs White)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Data is as at 31 March 2025. A 2026 ethnicity pay gap bulletin was not listed on the 2026 statistics page when checked.",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office, 29 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","the median hourly earnings of Black civil servants is 4.5% lower than that of White civil servants"),SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Ethnicity Pay Gap Statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office, 29 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2025","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","with Black civil servants the least likely (62.1%)"),SRCQ("Civil Service statistics: 2025 (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","detailed information on the UK Civil Service workforce as at 31 March 2025")]},
  {h:"People Survey: inclusion and fair treatment by ethnicity (2025)",text:"Inclusion and fair treatment scores were 80% for ethnic minority staff and 81% for White staff. Reported discrimination was 11% for ethnic minority staff and 8% for White staff.",figure:"80% vs 81%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Column order: all civil servants, then Ethnic Minority, then White. The highlights combine all ethnic minority groups. Figures for Black staff are in the ethnicity ODS file and were not extracted.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service People Survey 2025 - Results Highlights (Cabinet Office, updated 28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results-highlights","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Inclusion and fair treatment 78% (-1pp) 80% (0pp) 81% (-1pp)"),SRCQ("Civil Service People Survey 2025 - Results Highlights (Cabinet Office, updated 28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results-highlights","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Discrimination (a low value is good) 9% (0pp) 11% (-1pp) 8% (0pp)")]},
  {h:"NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard (WRES)",text:"The 2025 WRES is the tenth annual publication and uses nine indicators. Representation of staff from Black and minority ethnic groups is rising, but gaps in career progression continue.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Indicator values are not shown here; see the report for them.",src:[SRCQ("Workforce Race Equality Standard report published (NHS Employers, 17 April 2026)","https://www.nhsemployers.org/news/workforce-race-equality-standard-report-published","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","tracks progress across nine indicators used to measure workplace equality in NHS trusts."),SRCQ("Workforce Race Equality Standard report published (NHS Employers, 17 April 2026)","https://www.nhsemployers.org/news/workforce-race-equality-standard-report-published","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","While representation of Black and minority ethnic (BME) staff continues to increase, the report highlights ongoing disparities in career progression"),SRCQ("Workforce Race Equality Standard: 2025 data analysis report for NHS trusts (NHS England, 7 April 2026)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/publication/workforce-race-equality-standard-2025-data-analysis-report-for-nhs-trusts/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The 2025 Workforce Race Equality Standard (WRES) dataset is the 10th publication since the WRES was mandated")]},
  {h:"Future metrics: ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting",text:"The proposed regime adds workforce composition and declaration rates to the six pay gap calculations.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Equality (Race and Disability) Bill: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting - consultation and outcome (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","require employers to report on the overall composition of their workforce by ethnicity and disability"),SRCQ("Equality (Race and Disability) Bill: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting - consultation and outcome (GOV.UK)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","require the same 6 calculations")]},
  {h:"Evaluate, do not assume",text:"The CIPD recommends evaluating every intervention against clear aims, using before-and-after measures and control groups.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Continually evaluate the effectiveness of diversity and awareness training."),SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Creating an intervention and control group will help measure the effectiveness of the training intervention.")]}]},
 {k:"intersect",h:"Intersectionality",intro:"The term was coined in 1989. In UK law, protection against combined (dual) discrimination exists on paper in section 14 but has never been brought into force.",points:[
  {h:"Crenshaw's 1989 paper",text:"Kimberlé Crenshaw introduced the idea in a 1989 University of Chicago Legal Forum article.",figure:"",year:"1989",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Crenshaw, K. (1989) Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex, University of Chicago Legal Forum","https://chicagounbound.uchicago.edu/uclf/vol1989/iss1/8/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex: A Black Feminist Critique of Antidiscrimination Doctrine, Feminist Theory and Antiracist Politics"),SRCQ("Crenshaw, K. (1989) Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex, University of Chicago Legal Forum","https://chicagounbound.uchicago.edu/uclf/vol1989/iss1/8/","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","University of Chicago Legal Forum : Vol. 1989, Article 8")]},
  {h:"Coined to explain the experience of Black women",text:"Columbia Law School says Crenshaw coined \"intersectionality\" to explain the oppression of African-American women.",figure:"",year:"1989",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Kimberlé Crenshaw on Intersectionality, More than Two Decades Later (Columbia Law School)","https://www.law.columbia.edu/news/archive/kimberle-crenshaw-intersectionality-more-two-decades-later","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Kimberlé Crenshaw coined the term “intersectionality” in a paper as a way to help explain the oppression of African-American women.")]},
  {h:"Section 14 was never brought into force",text:"Section 14 of the Equality Act 2010 provides for combined discrimination on two characteristics. legislation.gov.uk still marks it \"Prospective\".",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"legislation.gov.uk explains that \"Prospective\" covers provisions that have never come into force. The page was up to date to 20 October 2026.",src:[SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 14 (Combined discrimination: dual characteristics)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/14","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Prospective 14 Combined discrimination: dual characteristics"),SRCQ("Equality Act 2010, section 14 (Combined discrimination: dual characteristics)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/14","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","because of a combination of two relevant protected characteristics")]},
  {h:"Government position, October 2026",text:"The government says it is considering how best to strengthen protection against dual discrimination, informed by its 2025 Call for Evidence.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"No commencement date for section 14 was given.",src:[SRCQ("Discrimination - written question HL3652 (Women and Equalities), answered 22 October 2026","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-questions/detail/2026-09-16/HL3652","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The Government is considering how to strengthen protections against dual discrimination in the most effective way."),SRCQ("Discrimination - written question HL3652 (Women and Equalities), answered 22 October 2026","https://questions-statements.parliament.uk/written-questions/detail/2026-09-16/HL3652","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","In our manifesto, we committed to strengthening protections against dual discrimination.")]},
  {h:"Intersection in the data: sex, ethnicity and disability",text:"Census 2021 found women had higher disability rates than men in every ethnic group.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Females had higher rates of disability than males and this remained true for all ethnic groups.")]},
  {h:"Intersection in practice: sickle cell",text:"Employers may have both disability duties (reasonable adjustments) and race duties for staff with sickle cell disorder.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Sickle Cell Work and Employment: A Guide for Employers and Employees (De Montfort University, v1.1 2019)","https://sicklecellwork.dmu.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/PDF-Version_Guide-To-Sickle-Cell-and-Employment-Version_1.1_2019.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","since SCD disproportionately affects BME communities, not to engage in direct or indirect racist discrimination")]},
  {h:"EHRC position on intersectionality",text:"An Equality and Human Rights Commission statement on intersectionality is not confirmed by the sources cited.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Not confirmed by the sources cited. Requests to equalityhumanrights.com returned blocking pages.",ed:true,src:[]}]},
 {k:"limits",h:"Limits of this evidence",intro:"These are known gaps and caveats. Read them before reusing any figure.",points:[
  {h:"Self-reported data and non-declaration",text:"Civil Service ethnicity percentages leave out staff who did not declare their ethnicity. Training evidence relies mostly on self-report.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (Cabinet Office, 29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Percentages exclude those with an unknown ethnicity and those who have elected to not declare their ethnicity."),SRCQ("Diversity management that works: an evidence-based view (CIPD and Westminster Business School, October 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/globalassets/media/knowledge/knowledge-hub/reports/7926-diversity-and-inclusion-report-revised_tcm18-65334.pdf","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","evidence is mostly based on self-report methods, which means the results may be subject to social desirability bias.")]},
  {h:"\"Ethnic minority\" hides big differences",text:"Several headline sources group all ethnic minorities together. Black African and Black Caribbean outcomes differ, as the SEN and Census data show.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Disaggregated tables (ODS/XLSX) exist for the Census, the People Survey and SEN. They were not parsed for this frame.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service People Survey 2025 - Results Highlights (Cabinet Office, updated 28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results-highlights","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","Key survey results by the grouped responses to J03 ('Ethnic Minority' and 'White') are presented in Table 8.")]},
  {h:"Survey method changed in 2025",text:"Five People Survey themes were recalculated in 2025 and should not be compared with earlier years.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"\"Inclusion and fair treatment\" is not one of the five changed themes. Even so, the 2025 discrimination figure for ethnic minority staff (11%, -1pp) does not match the 2024 highlight (11%, unchanged), so check the source before citing a trend.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service People Survey 2025 - Results Highlights (Cabinet Office, updated 28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results-highlights","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","The scores of these five themes should not be compared with those published in previous publications."),SRCQ("Civil Service People Survey 2024 - Results Highlights (Cabinet Office)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2024-results/civil-service-people-survey-2024-results-highlights","Supports the The DEEIA lens page","remained the same (11% and 8% respectively)")]}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Race is a protected characteristic: you are protected from discrimination at work and in services, and positive action to help under-represented groups is lawful within limits.",refs:["dx-law-nine-protected-characteristics-including", "dx-law-positive-action-is-lawful-within-limits-"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Staff networks have their own civil service policy since September 2025; use it to agree how your network works.",refs:["dx-policy-staff-networks-new-policy-in-september-2"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Training on its own rarely changes behaviour; the evidence favours structured processes, positive action and checking what works.",refs:["dx-debate-the-evidence-behind-that-decision", "dx-debate-for-what-does-work-according-to-the-evid", "dx-measure-evaluate-do-not-assume"]},{t:"Watch the published measures: representation by grade, the pay gap and the People Survey.",refs:["dx-measure-representation-falls-with-seniority", "dx-measure-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap", "dx-measure-people-survey-inclusion-and-fair-treatme"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"The public sector equality duty applies to every decision; follow both the pay gap reporting plans and the bill that would repeal the duty.",refs:["dx-law-public-sector-equality-duty-s149", "dx-policy-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporti", "dx-policy-a-bill-to-repeal-the-psed-is-before-parl"]},{t:"Accessibility is a legal floor: public websites must meet WCAG 2.2 AA, and disability support was exempt from spending controls.",refs:["dx-law-digital-accessibility-regulations", "dx-policy-disability-support-was-exempt"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"'Ethnic minority' totals hide large differences; report Black African, Black Caribbean and Mixed groups separately.",refs:["dx-limits-ethnic-minority-hides-big-differences"]},{t:"Intersections need combined data: sex, ethnicity and disability together, as with sickle cell.",refs:["dx-intersect-intersection-in-the-data-sex-ethnicity-a", "dx-intersect-intersection-in-practice-sickle-cell", "dx-access-autism-and-semh-identified-at-different-"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Diversity is who is present. Equality is fair treatment. Equity is fair chances. Inclusion is belonging. Accessibility is being able to use things.",refs:["dx-terms-diversity-describes-the-mix-of-people", "dx-terms-equality-means-fair-treatment-and-equal-", "dx-terms-equity-is-not-the-same-as-equality", "dx-terms-inclusion-is-about-being-valued-and-safe", "dx-terms-accessibility-has-a-technical-standard"]},{t:"UK law protects race as one of nine protected characteristics.",refs:["dx-law-nine-protected-characteristics-including"]},{t:"Positive action to help under-represented groups is lawful, within limits.",refs:["dx-law-positive-action-is-lawful-within-limits-"]},{t:"People disagree about diversity programmes. This page sets out both sides.",refs:["dx-debate-for-what-does-work-according-to-the-evid", "dx-debate-against-value-for-money-and-evidence-uk-"]}]}]};
LP["journeys"]={name:"Africa's knowledge and its journeys",pfx:"jy",kpis:[{s:"nile",h:"Egyptian writing",n:"3300 BC",l:"about when writing developed independently in Egypt",ic:"edit"},{s:"early",h:"Blombos Cave engraved ochre",n:"c. 75,000 to 100,000 years ago",l:"engraved ochre at Blombos Cave, among the oldest symbolic objects known",ic:"spark"},{s:"west",h:"Djenné-Djenno: a city before Islam",n:"c. 250 BC to AD 1100",l:"Djenné-Djenno, a town in West Africa before Islam",ic:"columns"},{s:"t-numbers",h:"Fibonacci in Bejaia (Bugia), Algeria",n:"Bejaia",l:"where Fibonacci learned the numerals Europe now uses",ic:"sigma"}],sections:[
 {k:"early",h:"Early evidence: symbols, counting, monuments and farming",intro:"Southern and central Africa hold some of the oldest known evidence of symbolic marks and notched bones. The evidence is real, but what the marks meant is often unknown. Claims of early astronomy and of African firsts in farming need care.",points:[
  {h:"Blombos Cave engraved ochre",text:"Pieces of ochre deliberately engraved with cross-hatched designs were found in Middle Stone Age layers at Blombos Cave, South Africa. They are among the oldest known abstract designs made by humans. When first reported in 2002, the closest comparable European evidence was much younger. Later work dated other engraved pieces as far back as about 100,000 years.",figure:"c. 75,000 to 100,000 years ago",year:"c. 73,000 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced as deliberate engraving. What the designs meant is unknown, so calling them \"art\" or \"writing\" goes beyond the evidence. The 2002 comparison with Europe is from that date; newer finds elsewhere are not covered by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Henshilwood, d'Errico and Watts (2009) Engraved ochres from the Middle Stone Age levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa. Journal of Human Evolution 57(1) - abstract","https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/kip_articles/1784","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","thirteen additional pieces of incised ochre recovered from c. 75,000–100,000 year old levels at Blombos Cave"),SRCQ("Henshilwood, d'Errico and Watts (2009) Engraved ochres from the Middle Stone Age levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa. Journal of Human Evolution 57(1) - abstract","https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/kip_articles/1784","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","In Africa, this includes two deliberately engraved ochre pieces from c. 75,000 year old levels at Blombos Cave"),SRCQ("ScienceDaily (2002), National Science Foundation release: Abstract engravings show modern behavior emerged earlier than previously thought","https://sciencedaily.com/releases/2002/01/020114073704.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","evidence of abstract representations is from the Eurasian Upper Paleolithic period mainly in France and dated to less than 35,000 years ago")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Blombos ochre-processing workshop",text:"A toolkit for making and storing ochre paint in shells, about 100,000 years old, was found at the same cave.",figure:"c. 100,000 years ago",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Shows planning and simple chemistry (mixing pigments). It does not by itself show \"science\" in the modern sense.",src:[SRCQ("AAAS (2011) Science: Early ochre workshop represents critical point in human evolution","https://www.aaas.org/news/science-early-ochre-workshop-represents-critical-point-human-evolution","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","researchers have discovered a 100,000-year-old workshop that may have been used by early Homo sapiens to make, mix, and store ochre")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Lebombo bone and Border Cave notched bones",text:"A notched baboon fibula from Border Cave, on the South Africa-Eswatini border, is about 42,000 to 43,000 years old. A peer-reviewed reanalysis of Border Cave finds concluded that people there used notched bones for notational (record-keeping) purposes.",figure:"c. 42,000 to 43,000 years ago",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Cautious reading: a tally or record of some kind. Bolder readings (a lunar calendar, for example) are not confirmed by the sources cited. The Lebombo bone is often confused with the younger Ishango bone.",src:[SRCQ("d'Errico et al. (2012) Early evidence of San material culture represented by organic artifacts from Border Cave, South Africa. PNAS - abstract","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3421171","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","used notched bones for notational purposes"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Lebombo bone (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebombo_bone","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is between 42,000 and 43,000 years old, according to 24 radiocarbon datings"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Lebombo bone (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebombo_bone","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The bone has been conjectured to be a tally stick")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Ishango bone: the cautious reading",text:"A baboon fibula with groups of notches in three columns, found in 1950 on the shore of Lake Edward (Democratic Republic of the Congo). An ICOMOS-IAU study dates it to about 25,000 to 16,000 years ago and describes it as possible evidence of early number knowledge beyond simple counting. It is held in Brussels.",figure:"c. 25,000 to 16,000 years ago",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The age range is wide because the dating has been revised. Older engraved bones also exist in Europe, so it is not unique as a counting object.",src:[SRCQ("ICOMOS-IAU Thematic Study on Astronomical Heritage, Case Study 1.3: The Ishango Bone, Democratic Republic of the Congo","https://web.astronomicalheritage.org/images/astronomicalheritage.org/thematic-study/ch01cs3.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","A subsequent re-evaluation of the archaeological dating places the bone between c. 25,000 BP and 16,000 BP."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ishango bone (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishango_bone","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","although older engraved bones are also known, such as the approximately 26,000-year-old \"Wolf Bone\" from Dolni Vestonice")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Ishango bone: the bolder readings",text:"Some researchers read the columns as a number system (bases 10 and 12), a list of prime numbers or a lunar calendar of about six months. A 2025 preprint found further patterns. These readings are hypotheses.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Disputed. A small number of notches can produce patterns by chance; see the myths section. The 2025 study is a preprint, not peer reviewed.",src:[SRCQ("ICOMOS-IAU Thematic Study on Astronomical Heritage, Case Study 1.3: The Ishango Bone, Democratic Republic of the Congo","https://web.astronomicalheritage.org/images/astronomicalheritage.org/thematic-study/ch01cs3.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the numbers contained in the first column (G) are precisely the four prime numbers between 10 and 20"),SRCQ("ICOMOS-IAU Thematic Study on Astronomical Heritage, Case Study 1.3: The Ishango Bone, Democratic Republic of the Congo","https://web.astronomicalheritage.org/images/astronomicalheritage.org/thematic-study/ch01cs3.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It has also been suggested that the bone could have been used for time reckoning"),SRCQ("arXiv preprint 2504.06412 (2025) on numerical patterns of the Ishango Bone (not peer reviewed)","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.06412","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Two columns comprise all prime and odd numbers between 9 and 21, with the sole exception of 15"),SRCQ("Parkinson-Coombs et al. (2026), Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Cognitive Science Society 48","https://escholarship.org/content/qt5sk7g51r/qt5sk7g51r.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","and the concept of prime numbers was understood' (de Heinzelin, 1962, p. 114)."),SRCQ("Parkinson-Coombs et al. (2026), Proceedings of the Cognitive Science Society 48","https://escholarship.org/content/qt5sk7g51r/qt5sk7g51r.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","As suggestive as the patterns of notch collections are, they alone are insufficient to support the claim of a numerical")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Nabta Playa stone circle and megaliths",text:"In the Egyptian Sahara, a small stone circle and lines of standing stones were built by Neolithic communities who used the area seasonally. A 1998 Nature paper reported alignments to north-south and to the summer solstice horizon. The university press release said it predates Stonehenge by about 1,000 years.",figure:"c. 4500 to 4000 BC (6,500 to 6,000 years ago)",year:"c. 4500 BC",disputed:true,note:"The stones and the dates of the settlement are well evidenced. Whether the alignments were intended as astronomy is an interpretation. The press release language (\"oldest\") is stronger than the paper. Comparison: the Stonehenge stone circle went up about 2500 BC; its first earthwork about 3000 BC.",src:[SRCQ("Malville, Wendorf, Mazar and Schild (1998) Megaliths and Neolithic astronomy in southern Egypt. Nature 392 - abstract","https://ideas.repec.org/a/nat/nature/v392y1998i6675d10.1038_33131.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","which has alignments to cardinal and solstitial directions, was a very early megalithic expression of ideology and astronomy"),SRCQ("University of Colorado Boulder (1998) Oldest astronomical megalith alignment discovered in southern Egypt by science team","https://www.colorado.edu/today/1998/03/30/oldest-astronomical-megalith-alignment-discovered-southern-egypt-science-team","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Nabta predates Stonehenge and similar prehistoric sites around the world by about 1,000 years"),SRCQ("English Heritage: History of Stonehenge","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/stonehenge/history-and-stories/history/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the unique stone circle was erected in the late Neolithic period about 2500 BC")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Early pottery in the Sahara (Ounjougou, Mali)",text:"Pottery and small arrowheads at Ounjougou in Mali date to before 9400 BC, during a wetter phase of the Sahara. This is one of the earliest pottery traditions in Africa, linked to cooking wild grasses.",figure:"before 9400 BC",year:"c. 9400 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced and likely an independent invention. It is not the oldest pottery in the world: the same paper notes very early ceramics in East Asia. Comparison: pottery reached Europe much later, but the exact date is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Huysecom et al. (2009) The emergence of pottery in Africa during the tenth millennium cal BC: new evidence from Ounjougou (Mali). Antiquity 83 - abstract, University of Geneva","https://archive-ouverte.unige.ch/unige:12635","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a lithic and ceramic assemblage that dates from before 9400 cal BC"),SRCQ("Huysecom et al. (2009) The emergence of pottery in Africa during the tenth millennium cal BC: new evidence from Ounjougou (Mali). Antiquity 83 - abstract, University of Geneva","https://archive-ouverte.unige.ch/unige:12635","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","As in East Asia, where very early ceramics are also known")],evidence:"dating"},
  {h:"Sorghum domestication",text:"Sorghum, a staple grain today, was domesticated from wild sorghum in eastern Sudan. A UCL-led team found evidence at a site near Kassala dated 3500 to 3000 BC.",figure:"3500 to 3000 BC",year:"c. 3500 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced as an African domestication. Dates for full domestication vary between studies.",src:[SRCQ("Sci.News (2017) Earliest evidence of domesticated sorghum discovered (report on UCL-led research)","https://www.sci.news/archaeology/earliest-evidence-domesticated-sorghum-05271.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Sorghum was domesticated from its wild ancestor more than 5,000 years ago"),SRCQ("Sci.News (2017) Earliest evidence of domesticated sorghum discovered (report on UCL-led research)","https://www.sci.news/archaeology/earliest-evidence-domesticated-sorghum-05271.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","dating from 3500 to 3000 BC")],evidence:"natural"},
  {h:"African rice",text:"African rice (Oryza glaberrima) was domesticated in Africa, separately from Asian rice. Older work placed its origin in the inner Niger delta; genetic studies now suggest several origins across West and Central Africa.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The African origin is secure. Where and when it happened is debated, and a date is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Ndjiondjop et al. (2021) Domestication of African rice: a review. Rice - abstract","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7790969","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is one of the two independently domesticated rice species"),SRCQ("Choi et al. (2019) The complex geography of domestication of the African rice Oryza glaberrima. PLOS Genetics - abstract","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6424484","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","we hypothesize a non-centric (i.e. multiregional) domestication origin for African rice")],evidence:"natural"},
  {h:"Coffee",text:"Wild coffee plants come from Ethiopia. The drink itself is first documented in Yemen in the 15th century.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Popular stories say coffee was \"discovered\" in Ethiopia by a goatherd; that legend is not evidence. A date for domestication is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: History of coffee","https://www.britannica.com/topic/history-of-coffee","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Wild coffee plants are thought to have been native to an Ethiopian plateau region known as Kefa (Kaffa)"),SRCQ("Folger Shakespeare Library: Early modern coffee culture and history in the Islamic world","https://www.folger.edu/blogs/shakespeare-and-beyond/islamic-history-of-coffee/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Historically, coffee as a hot beverage was introduced to the world by the Sufi saints in 15th-century Yemen."),SRCQ("JSTOR Daily (Livia Gershon, summarising Topik 2009, Cultural Critique)","https://daily.jstor.org/how-coffee-went-from-a-mystical-sacrament-to-an-everyday-drink/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Sufi Muslims in Yemen particularly prized the drink, which became part of their nighttime chanting rituals."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: History of coffee (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_coffee","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Wild coffee plants originated in Ethiopia , while the beverage itself has its roots in Yemen")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Cattle",text:"Early cattle remains at Nabta Playa were once taken as evidence that Africans domesticated cattle independently. Later studies argue these were wild aurochs, with domestic cattle arriving from south-west Asia; this is still debated.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Contested: some researchers still argue for local domestication. Do not present either view as settled. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Brass, Journal of World Prehistory (doi 10.1007/s10963-017-9112-9), UCL Discovery","https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10045701/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Bos remains from the early Holocene at Nabta Playa—Bir Kiseiba were those of hunted aurochs."),SRCQ("Brass, Journal of World Prehistory (doi 10.1007/s10963-017-9112-9), UCL Discovery","https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10045701/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","were descendants of Bos taurus domesticated in the Middle Euphrates area of the Middle East"),SRCQ("Linseele et al. 2014, PLOS ONE 9(10): e108517","https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0108517","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","extant African unhumped cattle are descendants of domesticated cattle from the Near East"),SRCQ("Pitt et al. (2019) 'Domestication of cattle: Two or three events?', Evolutionary Applications 12(1), doi:10.1111/eva.12674 – abstract","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6304694/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","may be explained by hybridization with local aurochs in Africa after the domestication of taurine cattle in the Fertile Crescent"),SRCQ("Pitt et al. (2019) 'Domestication of cattle: Two or three events?', Evolutionary Applications 12(1) – introduction","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6304694/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a third domestication event has been hypothesized to have occurred in north‐east Africa about 8,000–9,000 YA"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Nabta Playa (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabta_Playa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the cattle remains found at Nabta have been shown to be morphologically wild in several studies")],evidence:"natural"}]},
 {k:"nile",h:"The Nile Valley: Egypt, Kerma and Kush",intro:"Egypt and its southern neighbours in Nubia (modern Sudan) produced some of the earliest writing, mathematics, medicine and large-scale engineering in the world. These are African civilisations by geography. Egypt also traded and mixed with south-west Asia.",points:[
  {h:"Egyptian writing",text:"Writing was invented independently in only a few places, usually counted as four: Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica. Egypt's earliest writing, labels from the tombs of its first kings, dates to about 3300 BC (dates given range from about 3320 to 3250 BC), close in date to Mesopotamia (Iraq).",figure:"c. 3320 BC",year:"c. 3250 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Comparison: Europe's earliest deciphered script, Linear B in Greece, dates from about 1450 BC; it was adapted from the earlier, undeciphered Linear A, whose date is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("University of Chicago News: Oriental Institute 'Visible Language' exhibition","https://news.uchicago.edu/story/origins-writing-make-engaging-new-exhibition-oriental-institute-museum","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","There are four instances and places in human history when writing was invented from scratch."),SRCQ("University of Chicago News: Oriental Institute 'Visible Language' exhibition","https://news.uchicago.edu/story/origins-writing-make-engaging-new-exhibition-oriental-institute-museum","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","tags and labels from the tombs of the first kings (about 3320 BC)"),SRCQ("ISAC Museum, University of Chicago (Google Arts & Culture): The Invention of Writing in Egypt","https://Artsandculture.google.com/story/4AXR09XM5lg0hw","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It is possible that the Mesopotamians and Egyptians both independently developed writing around the same time."),SRCQ("British Museum: Egyptian hieroglyphs decipherment timeline","https://www.britishmuseum.org/exhibitions/hieroglyphs-unlocking-ancient-egypt/egyptian-hieroglyphs-decipherment-timeline","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Of these original writing systems, Egyptian and Sumerian are the oldest known (from 3300–3250 BC onwards)."),SRCQ("The Past / Ancient Egypt magazine (Andrew Fulton)","https://the-past.com/shorts/the-picture-desk/the-discovery-of-tomb-u-j","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Jar labels from Tomb U-j are the earliest examples of written hieroglyphs."),SRCQ("University of Chicago News (27 September 2010): Origins of writing make for engaging new exhibition at Oriental Institute Museum","https://news.uchicago.edu/story/origins-writing-make-engaging-new-exhibition-oriental-institute-museum","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","On display will be early Egyptian writing, which includes tags and labels from the tombs of the first kings (about 3320 BC),"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: History of writing (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_writing","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Four independent inventions of writing are most commonly recognized")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Rhind Mathematical Papyrus",text:"A scroll of mathematical tables and problems, copied by the scribe Ahmes about 1650 BC. It includes a table for working with unit fractions (fractions with 1 on top) and a method of multiplication by doubling.",figure:"c. 1650 BC",year:"c. 1650 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Britannica also gives an earlier date for the original text (c. 2000 BC); the dating of the original is uncertain. This is practical mathematics for administration and building, not abstract proof.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Rhind papyrus","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Rhind-papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the scribe who copied it about 1650 bc"),SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Mathematics in Egyptian papyri","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Egyptian_papyri/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Rhind papyrus gives a table for doubling unit fractions"),SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Mathematics in Egyptian papyri","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Egyptian_papyri/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","illustrates the Egyptian method of multiplication")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Moscow Mathematical Papyrus: volume of a frustum",text:"Problem 14 calculates the volume of a truncated pyramid (a frustum) correctly for a worked example. Historians often call this the high point of Egyptian mathematics.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The calculation is correct, but it is a worked example, not a general proof. The papyrus date (Middle Kingdom, about 1850 BC) is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Mathematics in Egyptian papyri","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Egyptian_papyri/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","many historians argue is the most impressive achievement of Egyptian mathematics"),SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Mathematics in Egyptian papyri","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Egyptian_papyri/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Example of calculating a truncated pyramid."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Moscow Mathematical Papyrus (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moscow_Mathematical_Papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Problems 10 and 14 compute a surface area and the volume of a frustum respectively.")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Egyptian number writing and the state",text:"By about 3000 BC Egypt was a single state with hieroglyphic writing and numerals, needed for tax, grain and building records.",figure:"c. 3000 BC",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Shows the link between writing, number and administration.",src:[SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: An overview of Egyptian mathematics","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Egyptian_mathematics/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","By 3000 BC the Egyptians had already developed their hieroglyphic writing"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Memphis and its Necropolis - the Pyramid Fields from Giza to Dahshur","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/86/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Traditionally believed to have been founded in 3000 BC as the capital of a politically unified Egypt")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus",text:"The oldest known surgical text. It sets out 48 injury cases, each with examination, diagnosis, prognosis and treatment, with almost no magic.",figure:"c. 1600s BC copy",year:"c. 1600 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. The copy dates from about the 17th century BC; the content may be about 1,000 years older, which is an estimate.",src:[SRCQ("US National Library of Medicine Technical Bulletin (2010): An ancient medical treasure at your fingertips (Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus)","https://www.nlm.nih.gov/pubs/techbull/ma10/ma10_hmd_reprint_papyrus.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the world's oldest known surgical document"),SRCQ("US National Library of Medicine Technical Bulletin (2010): An ancient medical treasure at your fingertips (Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus)","https://www.nlm.nih.gov/pubs/techbull/ma10/ma10_hmd_reprint_papyrus.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","written in Egyptian hieratic script around the 17 th century BCE but probably based on material from a thousand years earlier"),SRCQ("US National Library of Medicine Technical Bulletin (2010): An ancient medical treasure at your fingertips (Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus)","https://www.nlm.nih.gov/pubs/techbull/ma10/ma10_hmd_reprint_papyrus.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","consists of 48 cases dealing with wounds and trauma"),SRCQ("US National Library of Medicine Technical Bulletin (2010): An ancient medical treasure at your fingertips (Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus)","https://www.nlm.nih.gov/pubs/techbull/ma10/ma10_hmd_reprint_papyrus.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it is chiefly rational and does not usually bring the supernatural into the explanations")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Ebers Papyrus",text:"A long Egyptian medical papyrus of remedies and medical texts from about 1550 BC (the end of the 16th century BC), now held by Leipzig University Library.",figure:"c. 1550 BC",year:"",disputed:false,note:"It mixes remedies with spells; how much of it worked is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Ebers papyrus","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ebers-papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Ebers papyrus, Egyptian compilation of medical texts dated about 1550 bc, one of the oldest known medical works."),SRCQ("Leipzig University Library: Ebers Papyrus","https://www.ub.uni-leipzig.de/en/about-us/exhibitions/permanent-exhibition/ebers-papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Leipzig University Library owns the biggest papyrus scroll devoted to ancient Egyptian medicine."),SRCQ("Leipzig University Library: Ebers Papyrus","https://www.ub.uni-leipzig.de/en/about-us/exhibitions/permanent-exhibition/ebers-papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It contains nearly 900 prescriptions and educational texts concentrating on general and internal medicine."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ebers Papyrus (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebers_Papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is an Egyptian medical papyrus of herbal knowledge dating to c. 1550 BCE")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Astronomy and the 365-day civil calendar",text:"Egypt used a 365-day civil calendar: 12 months of 30 days plus five extra days. It ran alongside a lunar calendar throughout the pharaonic period.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The calendar is well evidenced. How it was set up is debated: some link it to the star Sirius, others to averaging Nile flood records. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Egyptian calendar (print version; same publisher as original)","https://www.britannica.com/print/article/180708","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","both systems of reckoning were maintained throughout the pharaonic period"),SRCQ("Metropolitan Museum of Art: Telling Time in Ancient Egypt","https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/telling-time-in-ancient-egypt","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","with five epagomenal days (days outside the regular months) added at the end of the year"),SRCQ("UCL Digital Egypt: Calendar","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt//chronology/calendar.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the five epagomenal days"),SRCQ("Alexandra V. Mironova, 'Ancient Egyptian calendar', Calendars and Festivals of the Ancient Near East, St Petersburg State University","https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/162-ancient-egyptian-calendar.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Beside it, Egyptians used a lunar calendar with 12 month also,"),SRCQ("Alexandra V. Mironova, 'Ancient Egyptian calendar', St Petersburg State University","https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/162-ancient-egyptian-calendar.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Egyptians added five more days to such 12 months, getting a 365-day long calendar year approximately equal to the solar year."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Egyptian calendar (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_calendar","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a solar calendar with a 365-day year")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Pyramid engineering at Giza",text:"The Great Pyramid of Khufu is the only one of the ancient Seven Wonders still standing. Excavations found a purpose-built town for the workforce.",figure:"c. 2575 to 2467 BC",year:"c. 2560 BC",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Comparison: Giza (4th Dynasty, about 2575 to 2467 BC) is roughly contemporary with the Stonehenge stone circle (about 2500 BC).",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Memphis and its Necropolis - the Pyramid Fields from Giza to Dahshur","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/86/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the Great Pyramid of Khufu is the only surviving wonder of the ancient world"),SRCQ("Ancient Egypt Research Associates (AERA): The Lost City of the Pyramids (Heit el-Ghurab)","https://aeraweb.org/projects/lost-city/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","this city served as the base of operations for constructing the great pyramid complexes of kings Menkaure, Khafre, and probably Khufu"),SRCQ("NBC News / Associated Press (2010): Egypt discovers workers' tombs near pyramids","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna34794254","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Egypt's 4th Dynasty (2575 B.C. to 2467 B.C.) when the great pyramids were built")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Kerma",text:"The Kingdom of Kerma in Upper Nubia (Sudan) flourished from about 2500 BC. It was the most powerful Nubian state of the early 2nd millennium BC, until Egypt took control by about 1500 BC.",figure:"c. 2500 to 1500 BC",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced archaeologically.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Kerma tentative list entry","https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6594","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Kerma was the capital of the first Kingdom of Kush"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Kerma tentative list entry","https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6594","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","flourished between 2500 and 1500 BC"),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Karmah","https://www.britannica.com/place/Karmah","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Thutmose I (1493–c. 1482 bc) mounted a major offensive, capturing Karmah and destroying the Cushite state."),SRCQ("British Museum, Room 65: Sudan, Egypt and Nubia (gallery page)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/galleries/sudan-egypt-and-nubia","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","By around 2500 BC, a powerful kingdom had developed in northern Sudan, centred on the town of Kerma."),SRCQ("British Museum, Room 65: Sudan, Egypt and Nubia (gallery page)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/galleries/sudan-egypt-and-nubia","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The culture and urban society of Kerma flourished between about 2500–1500 BC."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Kerma culture (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerma_culture","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The culture developed from around 2500 BC, reaching its peak between 1750 BC and 1500 BC.")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Kush rules Egypt",text:"Kings from Kush (Sudan) ruled Egypt as the 25th Dynasty in the 8th and 7th centuries BC. UNESCO describes their power reaching the Egyptian Delta and Palestine.",figure:"c. 744 to 656 BC",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. A reminder that influence ran south to north as well as north to south.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Gebel Barkal and the Sites of the Napatan Region","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1073/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","whose political power extended up to the Egyptian Delta and Palestine"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twenty-fifth_Dynasty_of_Egypt","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The 25th dynasty was a line of pharaohs who originated in the Kingdom of Kush")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Meroë: pyramids and iron-working",text:"Meroë was the heartland of Kush. Its pyramids and iron-working remains show wealth and production.",figure:"8th century BC to 4th century AD",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Iron-working at Meroë is well evidenced. Popular phrases like \"the Birmingham of Africa\" and claims about the exact scale and start date are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Archaeological Sites of the Island of Meroe","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1336/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a major power in the ancient world from the 8th century BCE to the 4th century CE."),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Archaeological Sites of the Island of Meroe","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1336/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","evidence of production and trade, including ceramics and iron-works,"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Archaeological Sites of the Island of Meroe","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1336/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The pyramids at Meroe are outstanding examples of Kushite funerary monuments,")],evidence:"structure"}]},
 {k:"iron",h:"Iron metallurgy",intro:"Africa south of the Sahara moved into iron-working early, in some places skipping a copper or bronze age. Whether it was invented there or spread from elsewhere is still debated.",points:[
  {h:"Nok and Taruga (Nigeria)",text:"The Nok culture of central Nigeria, known for its terracotta sculptures, gives some of the earliest evidence of iron smelting in West Africa, about 500 BC, from excavations at Taruga.",figure:"c. 500 BC",year:"c. 500 BC",disputed:true,note:"Dates of about 500 BC are well supported; claims of much earlier dates are less secure. Comparison: Britain's Iron Age began about 800 BC, central Europe's about 800 BC, the Aegean's about 1200 BC.",src:[SRCQ("Rupp, Breunig and Kahlheber, Exploring the Nok enigma. Antiquity Project Gallery (Goethe University Frankfurt project)","https://www.antiquity.ac.uk/projgall/kahlheber316/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","providing some of the earliest evidence of iron smelting in West Africa around 500 BC as demonstrated by excavations in Taruga"),SRCQ("Britannica: Nok culture","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nok-culture","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","existed on the Benue Plateau of Nigeria between about 500 bce and 200 ce."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Iron Age Europe (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Age_Europe","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Great Britain (800 BC – 100 AD)")],evidence:"dating"},
  {h:"Invented in Africa or brought in?",text:"Scholars still debate whether iron-working south of the Sahara came from the Mediterranean or was invented there. Very early dates from the Termit Massif in Niger have been used to argue for independent invention, but remain disputed.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Open question. Very early claims (some as far back as 3600 BC) are outliers and are not accepted as established. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Alpern (2005), 'Did They or Didn't They Invent It? Iron in Sub-Saharan Africa', History in Africa 32","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/abs/did-they-or-didnt-they-invent-it-iron-in-subsaharan-africa/DB40377A90535C7041DC10159B43C50F","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the French archeologist to whom we owe very early dates for iron metallurgy from the Termit Massif in Niger"),SRCQ("Alpern (2005), History in Africa 32 (quoting Quéchon)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/abs/did-they-or-didnt-they-invent-it-iron-in-subsaharan-africa/DB40377A90535C7041DC10159B43C50F","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","indisputably, in the present state of knowledge, the hypothesis of an autochthonous invention is convincing."),SRCQ("Chemistry World (Royal Society of Chemistry), 'The archaeologists saving Africa's ironworking heritage'","https://www.chemistryworld.com/features/the-archaeologists-saving-africas-ironworking-heritage/4018411.article","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","if it was independently invented or imported from elsewhere via North Africa"),SRCQ("Killick, van der Merwe, Gordon and Grébénart (1988) 'Reassessment of the Evidence for Early Metallurgy in Niger, West Africa', Journal of Archaeological Science 15: 367–394 (PDF reprint)","https://ressources.ingall-niger.org/documents/livres/lectures/metallurgie_Killick_1988.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Some scholars have argued that these are evidence of the independent invention of iron smelting in Africa."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Iron metallurgy in Africa (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_metallurgy_in_Africa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it is still not known whether this technology diffused into sub-Saharan Africa from the Mediterranean region, or whether it was invented there independently")],evidence:"dating"},
  {h:"Great Lakes: Haya iron and the carbon steel claim",text:"Peter Schmidt's fieldwork in the 1970s, including work with Donald Avery published in 1978, studied Haya smelting in north-west Tanzania and argued that furnaces reached high temperatures by preheating the air blast. Popular accounts go further and say the Haya made carbon steel for 2,000 years, hotter than anything in Europe.",figure:"Early Iron Age (dates not confirmed by the sources cited)",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Contested. The preheating idea is reported as a suggestion. The \"hotter than Europe\" claim comes from a popular radio script citing a 1983 anthology, not a technical study. The 1978 paper and any published scientific critique of it are not confirmed by the sources cited, so treat the bold claim as unproven.",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Kemondo Iron Age Sites (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kemondo_Iron_Age_Sites","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Preheating has been suggested to be a distinct feature of African Early Iron Age smelting techniques"),SRCQ("University of Houston, The Engines of Our Ingenuity No. 385: African Steel Making (John H. Lienhard, radio script c.1990)","https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/385","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The result was a far hotter process than anything known in Europe before modern times.")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Spread to the east and south",text:"Iron-working spread through eastern Africa and reached South Africa by about AD 200, with the ancestors of today's Bantu-speaking peoples. Djenné-Djenno in Mali worked iron from its first settlement, about 250 BC.",figure:"c. 250 BC to AD 200",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Broad dates from a secondary source.",src:[SRCQ("Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline: Inland Niger Delta","https://www.metmuseum.org/ko/essays/inland-niger-delta","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was populated as early as 250 B.C. and expanded to become a major urban center by 850 A.D."),SRCQ("Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline: Inland Niger Delta","https://www.metmuseum.org/ko/essays/inland-niger-delta","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the original inhabitants of Jenne-jeno worked iron from the earliest days of the site's occupation."),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: South Africa, The Iron Age","https://www.britannica.com/place/South-Africa/The-Iron-Age","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","New groups of people arriving in South Africa at that time had strong connections to East Africa."),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: South Africa, The Iron Age","https://www.britannica.com/place/South-Africa/The-Iron-Age","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","They were directly ancestral to the Bantu-speaking peoples who form the majority of South Africa's population today."),SRCQ("South African History Online","https://sahistory.org.za/article/when-why-and-where-first-african-farmers-settled-southern-africa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The African farmers who moved to southern Africa were Bantu-speakers. They are the ancestors of many South African people."),SRCQ("McIntosh and McIntosh (1981) 'The Inland Niger Delta before the Empire of Mali: Evidence from Jenne-Jeno', Journal of African History 22(1) – abstract","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-african-history/article/abs/inland-niger-delta-before-the-empire-of-mali-evidence-from-jennejeno/111CBA0644CA0D9EE7B3210C7A8F50F6","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","iron-using and manufacturing peoples were occupying the site in the third century B.C."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Iron metallurgy in Africa (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_metallurgy_in_Africa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Bantu expansion spread the technology to Eastern and Southern Africa between 500 BCE and 400 CE")],evidence:"dating"},
  {h:"Europe for comparison",text:"In Europe iron came into use first in the south-east, from about 1200 BC; in Greece the Iron Age is usually dated from about 1050 BC, and in Britain from about 800 BC.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"West African iron (about 500 BC) is later than the Aegean and similar to northern Europe. It does not support a claim that Africa had iron \"before Europe\" in general.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Iron Age","https://www.britannica.com/event/Iron-Age","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in the Middle East and southeastern Europe about 1200 BCE"),SRCQ("British Museum: Room 50, Britain and Europe 800 BC–AD 43","https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/galleries/britain-and-europe-800-bc-ad-43","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Iron Age was a time of dramatic change for the people of Britain and Europe."),SRCQ("Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford: Iron Age","https://britisharchaeology.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/collections/iron-age.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the Iron Age is divided into Early (800- 400 BC), Middle (400-120 BC) and Late (120 BC-AD 43)."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Iron Age Europe (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Age_Europe","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Aegean (1200–700 BC) Italy (1100–700 BC)")],evidence:"dating"}]},
 {k:"aksum",h:"Aksum (Ethiopia and Eritrea)",intro:"Aksum was a trading power on the Red Sea, linking Africa, Arabia and the Roman world.",points:[
  {h:"A great power",text:"UNESCO calls Aksum the most powerful state between the Eastern Roman Empire and Persia.",figure:"1st to 7th centuries AD (approximate)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"UNESCO summary of the historical record.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","and was the most powerful state between the Eastern Roman Empire and Persia"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The kingdom was at the crossroads of the three continents: Africa, Arabia and the Greco-Roman World")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Monumental stelae",text:"Aksum's carved granite stelae date from the 3rd and 4th centuries AD. The largest may be the biggest single-stone stele anyone ever tried to raise.",figure:"3rd to 4th centuries AD",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Several stelae survive in the town of Aksum dating between the 3rd and 4th centuries AD."),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It is possibly the largest monolithic stele that ancient human beings ever attempted to erect.")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Coinage",text:"Aksum issued its own coins, mainly in gold and silver, from the reign of King Endubis (about AD 270 to 300).",figure:"from c. AD 270",year:"c. AD 270",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Which metals were used and how unusual this was in Africa at the time are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("British Museum (via Smarthistory): Aksumite coins","https://smarthistory.org/aksumite-coins","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","These coins, bearing the name of King Endubis, were mainly struck in gold and silver."),SRCQ("British Museum (via Smarthistory): Aksumite coins","https://smarthistory.org/aksumite-coins","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It is generally thought that the first Aksumite coins were intended for international trade."),SRCQ("British Museum collection: Endubis (ruler)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/term/BIOG90446","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Minted in:Aksum (town)"),SRCQ("American Numismatic Society collection 1966.22.1","https://numismatics.org/collection/1966.22.1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Gold Coin of Endubis, Aksum, 270–300."),SRCQ("Fitzwilliam Museum, Cambridge: CM.2-2000","https://data.fitzmuseum.cam.ac.uk/id/object/265723","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Aksum, Endubis (c.AD 270/90), gold unit, type 1"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Aksumite currency (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aksumite_currency","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Its mintages were issued and circulated from the reign of King Endybis around AD 270")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Christianity in the 4th century",text:"Aksum adopted Christianity in the 4th century AD and built churches.",figure:"4th century AD",year:"c. AD 330",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Popular accounts link the conversion to King Ezana and compare it with Rome's adoption in the same century; those details are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The introduction of Christianity in the 4th century AD resulted in the building of churches")],evidence:"documentary"}]},
 {k:"west",h:"West Africa: cities, empires, scholarship and art",intro:"West Africa had towns before Islam arrived, then large trading empires linked to the Islamic world. Timbuktu became a centre of learning. Ife and Benin produced world-class metal sculpture.",points:[
  {h:"Djenné-Djenno: a city before Islam",text:"Djenné-Djenno in Mali was settled from about 250 BC and became a large town. Archaeologists found no city walls, temples or signs of a state bureaucracy in the pre-Islamic period. UNESCO calls it evidence of pre-Islamic urban life.",figure:"c. 250 BC to AD 1100",year:"c. 250 BC",disputed:true,note:"The early town is well evidenced. Population figures (up to about 42,000 for the whole complex around AD 900) are estimates.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Old Towns of Djenné","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Inhabited since 250 B.C.,"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Old Towns of Djenné","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","bears witness to a pre-Islamic urban structure"),SRCQ("ORIAS, University of California Berkeley: From Djenné to Mali - state and empire (lecture notes)","https://orias.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/from_djenne_to_mali_state_empire_lecture.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","perhapts 42,000 for entire urban complex"),SRCQ("ORIAS, University of California Berkeley: From Djenné to Mali - state and empire (lecture notes)","https://orias.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/from_djenne_to_mali_state_empire_lecture.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","No ancient city walls")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Ghana (Wagadu)",text:"The Ghana Empire, or Wagadu, was based in what is now south-east Mauritania and western Mali.",figure:"c. AD 300 to mid-13th century (start uncertain)",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Its start date is uncertain, and the identification of its capital (Koumbi Saleh) is debated. Its role in the gold trade is widely reported but not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Ghana, historical West African empire","https://www.britannica.com/place/Ghana-historical-West-African-empire","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in an area that now comprises southeastern Mauritania and part of Mali"),SRCQ("Metropolitan Museum of Art: Western Sudan, 500–1000 A.D.","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/06/afu.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Kumbi Saleh, an important political and commercial center of the Ghana empire located in modern-day Mauritania, is occupied."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ghana Empire (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana_Empire","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was an ancient western-Sahelian empire based in the modern-day southeast of Mauritania and western Mali")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Mali and Mansa Musa's hajj (1324)",text:"Mansa Musa ruled Mali from 1307 or 1312. His pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 was so lavish that his gold gifts are said to have affected the value of gold in Egypt.",figure:"1324",year:"1324",disputed:true,note:"The hajj is well documented by Arabic writers. \"Wealthiest person in history\" cannot be measured and should be avoided. The same sources note that his reign also involved warfare and slave raiding.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Musa I of Mali","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Musa-I-of-Mali","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the splendor of his pilgrimage to Mecca (1324)."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Mansa Musa (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansa_Musa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","his lavish gift-giving is said to have noticeably affected the value of gold in Egypt"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Mansa Musa (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansa_Musa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","may have captured over 6,000 slaves per year for this purpose")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Songhai",text:"Songhai, ruled from Gao, was at its peak one of the largest empires in West African history, until its defeat at the Battle of Tondibi in 1591.",figure:"c. 1430s to 1591",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Tomb of Askia","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1139","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was built when Gao became the capital of the Songhai Empire"),SRCQ("History Today: Fall of Africa's Greatest Empire","https://www.historytoday.com/node/55948","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Founded in 1464 out of the ruins of the Malian Empire, Songhay was the largest of the indigenous empires in Africa."),SRCQ("History Today: Fall of Africa's Greatest Empire","https://www.historytoday.com/node/55948","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Battle of Tondibi, which resulted in the defeat of the Songhay army, took place on 13 March 1591."),SRCQ("Issihak II – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Issihak-II","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","when Moroccan forces, using firearms, advanced into the Songhai empire to rout his forces, first at Tondibi"),SRCQ("Songhai empire summary – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/summary/Songhai-empire","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it reached its greatest extent in the 16th century before falling to Moroccan forces in 1591."),SRCQ("Julianna Tesfu, 'Songhai Empire (ca. 1375-1591)', BlackPast.org","https://new.blackpast.org/?p=63596","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Songhai Empire was the largest and last of the three major pre-colonial empires to emerge in West Africa."),SRCQ("Western and Central Sudan, 1400–1600 A.D. – Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History, The Metropolitan Museum of Art","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/08/afu.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Songhai have only handheld weapons, while the Moroccan invaders possess firearms,"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Songhai Empire (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Songhai_Empire","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","At its peak, it was one of the largest African empires in history.")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Timbuktu as a centre of learning",text:"UNESCO describes Timbuktu as an intellectual and spiritual capital, with Sankore and other schools, and a market where manuscripts were traded.",figure:"15th to 16th centuries",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Timbuktu","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/119/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Timbuktu was an intellectual and spiritual capital and a centre for the propagation of Islam"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Timbuktu","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/119/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","an important market place where the trading of manuscripts was negotiated")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Sankore: a \"university\"?",text:"Sankore is often called a university, but some scholars say the term is misleading. Timbuktu's scholars learned through informal teaching, often within families, not in universities like those of medieval Europe. Many of the city's manuscripts are still held in private libraries.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The learning was real; the word \"university\" overstates its structure. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Cleaveland, 'Timbuktu and Walata: lineages and higher education', in The Meanings of Timbuktu (CODESRIA/HSRC Press)","https://publication.codesria.org/index.php/pub/catalog/download/141/1225/4143?inline=1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Nor did students and teachers in towns such as Timbuktu and Walata form corporations in the European sense."),SRCQ("Cleaveland, in The Meanings of Timbuktu (CODESRIA/HSRC Press)","https://publication.codesria.org/index.php/pub/catalog/download/141/1225/4143?inline=1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","But these scholars did not acquire their skills in universities"),SRCQ("Stewart (2021), 'What's in the Manuscripts of Timbuktu? A Survey of the Contents of 31 Private Libraries', History in Africa 48","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/89202946609FB726FE85A2D7EABC75DB/S0361541320000182a.pdf/whats-in-the-manuscripts-of-timbuktu-a-survey-of-the-contents-of-31-private-libraries.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Since 2012, an inventory of nearly 350,000 Timbuktu manuscripts in private libraries has been underway,"),SRCQ("Library of Congress: Timbuktu, an Islamic cultural center","https://www.loc.gov/collections/islamic-manuscripts-from-mali/articles-and-essays/timbuktu-an-islamic-cultural-center/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The ancient manuscripts preserved at Timbuktu's Ahmed Baba Center and in its private family libraries"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Sankore Madrasah (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sankore_Madrasah","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","there is no evidence of a centralized teaching institution such as in European medieval universities")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Subjects of the Timbuktu manuscripts",text:"Library of Congress exhibits from Timbuktu family libraries include an astronomy text, an arithmetic primer, a medical compilation and many works of Islamic law.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Most surviving copies date from the 16th to 19th centuries (for example an astronomy text copied 1733); much of the science follows the wider Islamic tradition rather than being unique to Timbuktu.",src:[SRCQ("Library of Congress exhibition: Ancient Manuscripts from the Desert Libraries of Timbuktu - Exhibition items","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/mali/mali-exhibit.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","This text was written to train scholars in the field of astronomy"),SRCQ("Library of Congress exhibition: Ancient Manuscripts from the Desert Libraries of Timbuktu - Exhibition items","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/mali/mali-exhibit.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","demonstrates the rules of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division"),SRCQ("Library of Congress exhibition: Ancient Manuscripts from the Desert Libraries of Timbuktu - Exhibition items","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/mali/mali-exhibit.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","instructing students of Islamic law about the rights of orphans and married women")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"How many manuscripts?",text:"Figures vary. Librarians said almost 400,000 manuscripts were moved to Bamako in 2012 to 2013; another account says about 350,000. About 4,203 were lost from the Ahmed Baba Institute. An early report that 25,000 were burned was false.",figure:"c. 350,000 to 400,000 (estimates)",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Numbers vary by source and are estimates; private family collections were never fully catalogued.",src:[SRCQ("Mail & Guardian (2014): Scroll smugglers - how Timbuktu's secret treasures were saved","https://mg.co.za/article/2014-05-30-scroll-smugglers-how-timbuktus-secret-treasures-were-saved","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","they had almost 400 000 manuscripts to move"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Timbuktu Manuscripts (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbuktu_Manuscripts","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Some 350,000 manuscripts were transported to safety"),SRCQ("Mail & Guardian (2014): Scroll smugglers - how Timbuktu's secret treasures were saved","https://mg.co.za/article/2014-05-30-scroll-smugglers-how-timbuktus-secret-treasures-were-saved","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Maïga estimates 4 203 manuscripts belonging to the Ahmed Baba Institute were lost"),SRCQ("MPR News / Associated Press (2013): Timbuktu manuscripts mostly safe, university says","https://www.mprnews.org/story/2013/01/30/news/timbuktu-manuscripts","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","25,000 manuscripts had been burned was false")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Ife sculpture",text:"Between about 1200 and 1500, Ife (Nigeria) produced naturalistic heads in bronze, terracotta and stone. Their discovery challenged European assumptions about African art.",figure:"c. 1200 to 1500",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. The Met calls them \"bronzes\"; the exact alloys are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Ife (from ca. 6th century)","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/ife/hd_ife.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Between ca. 1200 and 1500, Ife developed into a flourishing artistic center."),SRCQ("The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Ife (from ca. 6th century)","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/ife/hd_ife.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","challenged European assumptions about the nature of African art.")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Benin Bronzes and brass casting",text:"The Benin Bronzes, made of brass and bronze by royal guilds for the Oba of Benin, date from at least the 16th century. British forces looted them in 1897.",figure:"from 16th century; looted 1897",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. The British Museum is one of several museums holding looted pieces; their return is a live public debate.",src:[SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The 'Benin Bronzes' (made of brass and bronze) are a group of sculptures"),SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","by specialist guilds working for the royal court of the Oba (king) in Benin City"),SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","They were created from at least the 16th century onwards"),SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Its shrines and associated compounds were looted by British forces")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Benin City walls and earthworks",text:"Benin City and its region are crossed by earthen walls and ditches built between about AD 700 and 1400. Patrick Darling estimated in 1977 a total length of about 16,000 km, the longest earthworks of the pre-mechanical era.",figure:"c. AD 700 to 1400; 16,000 km (estimate)",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The earthworks are real. The 16,000 km length is one researcher's estimate, mostly of low banks, and has not been fully re-measured. Comparisons such as \"four times the Great Wall of China\" rest on this estimate.",src:[SRCQ("Guinness World Records: Longest earthworks of the pre-mechanical era","https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/97959-longest-earthworks-of-the-pre-mechanical-era","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","estimated by Dr Patrick Darling that the total length of the earthworks was probably 16,000 km"),SRCQ("Guinness World Records: Longest earthworks of the pre-mechanical era","https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/97959-longest-earthworks-of-the-pre-mechanical-era","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Radio carbon research indicates that they were built from about 700 AD to 1400 AD."),SRCQ("NASA Earth Observatory (2025): A glimpse of history in Benin City","https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/images/154894/a-glimpse-of-history-in-benin-city","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Most of the earthworks consisted of relatively narrow and shallow linear ramparts and ditches")],evidence:"structure"}]},
 {k:"east",h:"The Swahili coast",intro:"Swahili city-states on the East African coast traded across the Indian Ocean with Arabia, Persia, India and China.",points:[
  {h:"Kilwa's own coins",text:"Kilwa Kisiwani (Tanzania) minted its own currency from the 11th to the 14th century. Kilwa coins have even been found in northern Australia.",figure:"11th to 14th centuries",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The coins are well evidenced. How they reached Australia is unknown.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Kilwa Kisiwani minted its own currency in the 11th to 14th centuries."),SRCQ("Australian Geographic (2014): Unravelling the mystery of Arnhem Land's ancient African coins","https://www.australiangeographic.com.au/history-culture/2014/08/mystery-of-ancient-african-coins-found-in-australia/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Kilwa sultanate coins were minted between the 10th and 14th centuries")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Husuni Kubwa palace and the Great Mosque",text:"The Husuni Kubwa palace was built about 1310 to 1333 with a large octagonal bathing pool. Kilwa's Great Mosque is the oldest standing mosque on the East African coast.",figure:"c. 1310 to 1333",year:"c. 1310",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the palace Husuni Kubwa built between c1310 and 1333 with its large octagonal bathing pool."),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Great Mosque of Kilwa Kisiwani is the oldest standing mosque on the East African coast.")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Trade with China",text:"Gold and ivory were traded for Chinese porcelain and other goods. A Ming coin of the Yongle emperor (1403 to 1425) was excavated on Manda island, Kenya.",figure:"14th to 15th centuries",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The porcelain trade is well evidenced. A visit by Admiral Zheng He to this coast is possible but not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","traded for silver, carnelians, perfumes, Persian faience and Chinese porcelain."),SRCQ("Field Museum press release (2013): Ancient Chinese coin found on Kenyan island by Field Museum expedition","https://www.fieldmuseum.org/about/press/ancient-chinese-coin-found-kenyan-island-field-museum-expedition","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","issued by Emperor Yongle who reigned from 1403-1425AD during the Ming Dynasty")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Slave trading on the coast",text:"Kilwa's medieval wealth came mainly from trading gold and ivory from the interior. By the late 18th century it had become a key port in the Indian Ocean slave trade.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Included for balance: African states were sophisticated, and some also traded in enslaved people.",src:[SRCQ("Metropolitan Museum of Art: Historic Towns of Kilwa Kisiwani and Songo Mnara","https://www.metmuseum.org/en/perspectives/kilwa-kisiwani-songo-mnara","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The wealth of both towns stemmed from Kilwa's strategic position"),SRCQ("Metropolitan Museum of Art: Historic Towns of Kilwa Kisiwani and Songo Mnara","https://www.metmuseum.org/en/perspectives/kilwa-kisiwani-songo-mnara","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","became a critical node in the Indian Ocean slave and ivory trades"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the merchants of Kilwa dealt in gold, silver, pearls, perfumes"),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: eastern Africa","https://www.britannica.com/place/eastern-Africa/The-Shirazi-migration","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","These began with a marked growth in the previously marginal slave trade, particularly at first in the Kilwa region"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Kilwa Sultanate (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilwa_Sultanate","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Kilwa’s economy also saw significant involvement in the slave trade")],evidence:"documentary"}]},
 {k:"south",h:"Southern Africa: Mapungubwe and Great Zimbabwe",intro:"Inland states in southern Africa built stone towns and traded gold to the coast.",points:[
  {h:"Mapungubwe",text:"Mapungubwe (South Africa), which rose and fell between about 900 and 1300, grew into the largest kingdom in the subcontinent, trading through East African ports to India and China, before it was abandoned.",figure:"c. 900 to 1300",year:"c. 1075",disputed:true,note:"UNESCO's own page gives two end dates (end of 13th century and 14th century). Dates are approximate.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Mapungubwe Cultural Landscape","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1099/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","between 900 and 1,300 AD"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Mapungubwe Cultural Landscape","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1099/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Mapungubwe developed into the largest kingdom in the sub-continent before it was abandoned in the 14th century."),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Mapungubwe Cultural Landscape","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1099/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","through the East African ports to India and China."),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Mapungubwe Cultural Landscape","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1099/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Until its demise at the end of the 13th century AD.")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Great Zimbabwe",text:"Great Zimbabwe was built by the Shona between about 1100 and 1450. Its granite walls were fitted without mortar. Its population may have topped 10,000.",figure:"c. 1100 to 1450",year:"c. 1100",disputed:false,note:"Builders and dates are well evidenced. The population figure is an estimate.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Great Zimbabwe National Monument","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","built between 1100 and 1450 AD"),SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Great Zimbabwe National Monument","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","by a Bantu population of the Iron Age, the Shona."),SRCQ("The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Great Zimbabwe (11th-15th century)","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/zimb/hd_zimb.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","All of Great Zimbabwe's walls were fitted without the use of mortar"),SRCQ("The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Great Zimbabwe (11th-15th century)","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/zimb/hd_zimb.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","At its height, Great Zimbabwe is estimated to have had a population greater than 10,000")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"Trade",text:"Finds at Great Zimbabwe show long-distance trade with the outside world.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Great Zimbabwe National Monument","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","which testify to the extent of long-standing trade with the outer world.")],evidence:"artefact"}]},
 {k:"writing",h:"African writing systems",intro:"Africa has several writing systems of its own, from ancient to modern. Some are full scripts; others are symbol systems.",points:[
  {h:"Egyptian hieroglyphs",text:"One of the world's few independent inventions of writing. Egypt's earliest writing dates to about 3300 BC (dates given range from about 3320 to 3250 BC).",figure:"c. 3320 BC",year:"",disputed:false,note:"See the Nile Valley section.",src:[SRCQ("University of Chicago News: Oriental Institute 'Visible Language' exhibition","https://news.uchicago.edu/story/origins-writing-make-engaging-new-exhibition-oriental-institute-museum","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","There are four instances and places in human history when writing was invented from scratch."),SRCQ("ISAC Museum, University of Chicago (Google Arts & Culture): The Invention of Writing in Egypt","https://Artsandculture.google.com/story/4AXR09XM5lg0hw","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The earliest known Egyptian writing was discovered in Tomb U-j at Abydos dating to 3320 BC."),SRCQ("British Museum: Egyptian hieroglyphs decipherment timeline","https://www.britishmuseum.org/exhibitions/hieroglyphs-unlocking-ancient-egypt/egyptian-hieroglyphs-decipherment-timeline","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Of these original writing systems, Egyptian and Sumerian are the oldest known (from 3300–3250 BC onwards)."),SRCQ("The Past / Ancient Egypt magazine (Andrew Fulton)","https://the-past.com/shorts/the-picture-desk/the-discovery-of-tomb-u-j","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Jar labels from Tomb U-j are the earliest examples of written hieroglyphs."),SRCQ("University of Chicago News (27 September 2010): Origins of writing make for engaging new exhibition at Oriental Institute Museum","https://news.uchicago.edu/story/origins-writing-make-engaging-new-exhibition-oriental-institute-museum","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","four instances and places in human history when writing was invented from scratch - in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: History of writing (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_writing","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in Egypt c. 3250 BC")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Meroitic",text:"Kush developed its own script, Meroitic: a cursive form appeared in the 3rd century BC and a hieroglyphic form in the 2nd century BC. It was deciphered (can be sounded out) in 1911 by F. Ll. Griffith, but the language is still only partly understood.",figure:"from the 3rd century BC",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced.",src:[SRCQ("UCL Petrie Museum, Digital Egypt: Meroitic script","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt//nubia/mwriting.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Meroitic is an alphabetic script with 23 signs used in a hieroglyphic form (mainly on monumental art) and in a cursive form."),SRCQ("Rilly, 'Meroitic Writing', UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology","https://escholarship.org/content/qt2ts0n9p0/qt2ts0n9p0.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","This local Demotic was very likely the ancestor of the Meroitic cursive script, which appeared in the third century BCE."),SRCQ("Rilly, 'Meroitic Writing', UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology","https://escholarship.org/content/qt2ts0n9p0/qt2ts0n9p0.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It was deciphered in 1911 by the British Egyptologist Francis Llewellyn Griffith."),SRCQ("Rilly, 'Meroitic Writing', UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology","https://escholarship.org/content/qt2ts0n9p0/qt2ts0n9p0.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the vocabulary and grammar of the language remain only partially known"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Meroitic script (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meroitic_script","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The script was deciphered in 1909 by Francis Llewellyn Griffith")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Ge'ez",text:"Aksum's inscriptions use Greek, Sabaean and Ge'ez. Ge'ez added vowel marks in the 4th century AD and is still used in Ethiopia and Eritrea.",figure:"4th century AD (vowel marks)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Ge'ez grew out of the South Arabian script; it was not invented from nothing.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Some of them include trilingual text in Greek, Sabaean and Ge'ez (Classical Ethiopian)"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ge'ez script (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ge%CA%BDez_script","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Vocalization of Geʻez occurred in the 4th century"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ge'ez script (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ge%CA%BDez_script","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Geʽez script was derived from the Ancient South Arabian script")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Libyco-Berber and Tifinagh",text:"Peoples of ancient North Africa wrote inscriptions in the Libyco-Berber script; Tuareg Tifinagh is related to it and a modern form (Neo-Tifinagh) is used today.",figure:"antiquity to present",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Its origin (possibly linked to the Phoenician alphabet) is uncertain.",src:[SRCQ("British Museum African Rock Art: Written in stone, the Libyco-Berber scripts","https://africanrockart.britishmuseum.org/thematic/written-in-stone/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Tifinagh script, the modern script of Tuareg people shares some symbols with the Libyco-Berber alphabet"),SRCQ("British Museum African Rock Art: Written in stone, the Libyco-Berber scripts","https://africanrockart.britishmuseum.org/thematic/written-in-stone/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a new alphabet was developed in the 1960s to be used by Berber people"),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Tifinagh","https://www.britannica.com/topic/tifinagh","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","This script is still used by the Tuaregs."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Tifinagh (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tifinagh","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","many inscriptions were engraved using the Libyco-Berber script")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Nsibidi",text:"Nsibidi is a system of symbols of the Ejagham and neighbouring peoples of the Cross River region of Nigeria and Cameroon. Designs on pottery from Calabar dated to the 5th to 15th centuries AD may foreshadow it.",figure:"possible forerunners 5th to 15th century (debated)",year:"",disputed:true,note:"It is usually classed as proto-writing (symbols), not a full script for writing a language. Early dates are debated.",src:[SRCQ("Slogar (2005), PhD dissertation, University of Maryland: Calabar terracottas","https://drum.lib.umd.edu/items/94d90e1e-2e32-481e-9bb7-3a726a05a19a","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","date to the fifth-fifteenth century A.D."),SRCQ("Slogar (2005), PhD dissertation, University of Maryland: Calabar terracottas","https://drum.lib.umd.edu/items/94d90e1e-2e32-481e-9bb7-3a726a05a19a","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","foreshadowing in particular the ideographic script called nsibidi (or nsibiri)"),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Ekoi","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ekoi","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Ekoi, group of peoples situated in extreme southeastern Nigeria and extending eastward into neighbouring Cameroon."),SRCQ("Adekunle Temilade Adeniji, 'The Akwanshi Stone Monoliths of the Cross River Region in Nigeria and Cameroon', Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History, The Metropolitan Museum of Art (25 Sept 2025)","https://www.metmuseum.org/en/essays/akwanshi-stone-monoliths","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Additional excavations in Calabar, where pottery inscriptions related to designs found on the monoliths"),SRCQ("Adeniji, 'The Akwanshi Stone Monoliths …', The Metropolitan Museum of Art","https://www.metmuseum.org/en/essays/akwanshi-stone-monoliths","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","These markings also resemble motifs employed in nsibidi, an ancient and graphic system of writing"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Proto-writing (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proto-writing","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","most researchers agree that use of the earliest symbols date back between the 5th and 15th centuries")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Vai",text:"The Vai script of Liberia was created in the early 1830s by a small group of Vai speakers. Momolu Duwalu Bukele was one of its main inventors.",figure:"1830s",year:"1833",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. It is a 19th-century invention, not an ancient one.",src:[SRCQ("Tykhostup and Kelly (2018), 'A Diachronic Comparison of the Vai Script of Liberia (1834-2005)', Journal of Open Humanities Data","https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_2550476_1/component/file_2550475/shh947.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Created by between six and eight non-literate Vai-speakers in Liberia in about 1833"),SRCQ("Tykhostup and Kelly (2018), Journal of Open Humanities Data","https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_2550476_1/component/file_2550475/shh947.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Mɔmɔlu Duwalu Bukɛlɛ, one of the principle inventors of the script"),SRCQ("Tuchscherer & Hair 2002, History in Africa 29 (Cambridge)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/abs/cherokee-and-west-africa-examining-the-origins-of-the-vai-script/5123DB37F287706DF5F0EAB6E0A3B283","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it seems safer to suppose the invention to have begun in either 1832 or 1833."),SRCQ("Unicode Technical Committee document L2/05-053 (2005), Vai code chart proposal","https://www.unicode.org/L2/L2005/05053r-vai-chart.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Since Momolu Duwalu Bukele devised the Vai syllabary in the early 1800s"),SRCQ("Max Planck Institute for the Science of Human History press release, 'Rare African Script Offers Clues to the Evolution of Writing' (13 Jan 2022)","https://www.shh.mpg.de/2089042/evolution-of-writing","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Vai script of Liberia was created from scratch in about 1834 by eight completely illiterate men"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Vai syllabary (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vai_syllabary","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","devised for the Vai language by Momolu Duwalu Bukele")],evidence:"documentary"}]},
 {k:"maths",h:"African mathematics beyond Egypt",intro:"Mathematical ideas appear in counting systems, design and scholarship across Africa. The evidence ranges from firm (written treatises) to interpretive (design analysed by modern mathematicians).",points:[
  {h:"Ron Eglash, African Fractals (1999)",text:"Eglash argued that many African settlement plans and designs use repeating patterns at different scales (fractals), sometimes on purpose.",figure:"1999",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Critique: a reviewer notes that all complex settlements may look fractal for natural reasons, and Eglash himself says the patterns do not prove knowledge of fractal geometry.",src:[SRCQ("Bangura (2000) review of Eglash, African Fractals. Nexus Network Journal 2(4)","https://www.maths.tcd.ie/EMIS/journals/NNJ/reviews_v2n4-Bangura.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","African Fractals: Modern Computing and Indigenous Design (New Brunswick, New Jersey: Rutgers University Press, 1999)"),SRCQ("Bangura (2000) review of Eglash, African Fractals. Nexus Network Journal 2(4)","https://www.maths.tcd.ie/EMIS/journals/NNJ/reviews_v2n4-Bangura.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","we cannot just assume that African fractals show an understanding of fractal geometry, nor can we dismiss that possibility"),SRCQ("Bangura (2000) review of Eglash, African Fractals. Nexus Network Journal 2(4)","https://www.maths.tcd.ie/EMIS/journals/NNJ/reviews_v2n4-Bangura.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","all cities (historic, primitive and modern) are fractal precisely because they are complex natural systems")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Yoruba counting in twenties",text:"Yoruba counts in base 20 and builds numbers by adding, subtracting and multiplying, for example 60 as \"three twenties\".",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well known, but the source page is flagged as needing more citations. Base-20 counting is also found elsewhere; the source compares one feature with Danish.",src:[SRCQ("University at Buffalo, Mathematicians of the African Diaspora: early Nigeria","https://www.math.buffalo.edu/mad/Ancient-Africa/mad_nigeria_pre-colonial.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The numeral system itself weighs heavily upon subtraction."),SRCQ("University at Buffalo, Mathematicians of the African Diaspora: early Nigeria","https://www.math.buffalo.edu/mad/Ancient-Africa/mad_nigeria_pre-colonial.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the vigesimal (base 20) scale of the West African Yoruba people"),SRCQ("Akinadé and Ọdẹjọbí (2014) 'Computational modelling of Yorùbá numerals in a number-to-text conversion system', Journal of Language Modelling 2(1): 167–211 (repository copy, BazTech)","https://yadda.icm.edu.pl/baztech/element/bwmeta1.element.baztech-7ac043b4-5f7d-4a03-87ca-bcb98011cbae/c/Akinade_Computational_modelling_of_Yoruba_numerals_1_2014.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the use of base 20 (vigesimal), which likely stems from the counting of cowry shells"),SRCQ("Akinadé and Ọdẹjọbí (2014), Journal of Language Modelling 2(1)","https://yadda.icm.edu.pl/baztech/element/bwmeta1.element.baztech-7ac043b4-5f7d-4a03-87ca-bcb98011cbae/c/Akinade_Computational_modelling_of_Yoruba_numerals_1_2014.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the use of three of the standard arithmetic operations (i.e., multiplication, subtraction, and addition)"),SRCQ("Akinadé and Ọdẹjọbí (2014), Journal of Language Modelling 2(1)","https://yadda.icm.edu.pl/baztech/element/bwmeta1.element.baztech-7ac043b4-5f7d-4a03-87ca-bcb98011cbae/c/Akinade_Computational_modelling_of_Yoruba_numerals_1_2014.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","ọgọ́ta is formed by multiplying an MP (20 – formed by MP→ M) and an NP (3"),SRCQ("Agbeyangi, Eludiora and Popoola (2016) 'Web-Based Yorùbá Numeral Translation System', IAES International Journal of Artificial Intelligence 5(4)","https://ijai.iaescore.com/index.php/IJAI/article/download/4243/6743","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Yorùbá numeral uses vigesimal (based 20) extensively."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Yoruba numerals (secondary source; flagged by Wikipedia as needing more citations)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoruba_numerals","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the Yoruba language has a rather elaborate vigesimal (base-20) numeral system that involves addition, subtraction and multiplication")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Sona sand drawings",text:"Chokwe sona are continuous one-line drawings made in sand around a grid of dots. Mathematicians, notably Paulus Gerdes, have analysed the rules used to make them.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The drawings are real; describing them as \"algorithms\" is a modern mathematical reading. How old the tradition is is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Chavey (2010) Strip symmetry groups of African sona designs. Bridges 2010 Proceedings (Beloit College)","https://static1.bridgesmathart.org/2010/cdrom/proceedings/69/paper_69.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The sona drawings of the Chokwe people of Angola/Congo are continuous one-line drawings, generally made in the sand"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Lusona (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lusona","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Paulus Gerdes identified six algorithms")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"Al-Kishnawi on magic squares",text:"Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Kishnawi, a Fulani scholar from northern Nigeria, wrote on how to construct magic squares. He died in Cairo in 1741.",figure:"18th century",year:"",disputed:true,note:"His magic squares sat within numerology and esoteric study, not only pure mathematics. The often-quoted treatise date of 1732 is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("University at Buffalo, Mathematicians of the African Diaspora: Muhammad ibn Muhammad","https://www.math.buffalo.edu/mad/muhammad_ibn_muhammad.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Fullani al-Kishnawi was a Falani from northern Nigeria."),SRCQ("University at Buffalo, Mathematicians of the African Diaspora: Muhammad ibn Muhammad","https://www.math.buffalo.edu/mad/muhammad_ibn_muhammad.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","procedures for constructing magic squares up to order 11"),SRCQ("University at Buffalo, Mathematicians of the African Diaspora: Muhammad ibn Muhammad","https://www.math.buffalo.edu/mad/muhammad_ibn_muhammad.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Muhammad died in Cairo in 1741."),SRCQ("Muslim Heritage (FSTC), Natty Mark Samuels 2015 (non-academic)","https://muslimheritage.com/people/scholars/muhammed-ibn-muhammed-al-fulani-al-kishnawi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","He died in Cairo, in 1741."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Kishnawi (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhammad_ibn_Muhammad_al-Fulani_al-Kishnawi","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","studied the art of magic squares , arithmetic , and chronology")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Arithmetic teaching in Timbuktu",text:"A Timbuktu arithmetic primer explained the four operations and the history of mathematics, and was used by students in Timbuktu and North Africa.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced as a teaching text, in the wider Islamic scholarly tradition.",src:[SRCQ("Library of Congress exhibition: Ancient Manuscripts from the Desert Libraries of Timbuktu - Exhibition items","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/mali/mali-exhibit.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The text was used extensively by students in Timbuktu and North Africa.")],evidence:"documentary"}]},
 {k:"medicine",h:"Medicine and other knowledge",intro:"African medical knowledge appears in Egyptian papyri, Timbuktu manuscripts and in the knowledge enslaved Africans carried abroad.",points:[
  {h:"Onesimus and smallpox inoculation in Boston",text:"Onesimus, an enslaved African man, told Cotton Mather about inoculation against smallpox practised in his homeland. Mather reported it to the Royal Society in 1716 and promoted inoculation in the Boston epidemic of 1721.",figure:"1716 to 1721",year:"1721",disputed:false,note:"Well evidenced. Balance: Mather also learned the practice was used in Turkey and China, so it was not uniquely African. The account first reached Mather by 1716, not in 1721.",src:[SRCQ("HISTORY (A&E): How an enslaved African man in Boston helped save generations from smallpox","https://www.history.com/articles/smallpox-vaccine-onesimus-slave-cotton-mather","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","had undergone an operation, which had given him something of the smallpox and would forever preserve him from it"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Onesimus (Bostonian) (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onesimus_(Bostonian)","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","After a smallpox outbreak began in Boston in 1721"),SRCQ("HISTORY (A&E): How an enslaved African man in Boston helped save generations from smallpox","https://www.history.com/articles/smallpox-vaccine-onesimus-slave-cotton-mather","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","learned that the practice had been used in Turkey and China")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Medicine in the Timbuktu manuscripts",text:"A Timbuktu compilation explains how to diagnose and treat the sick using animal, plant and mineral substances, alongside prayers.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Mixes practical remedies with religious practice, as most medicine of the period did.",src:[SRCQ("Library of Congress exhibition: Ancient Manuscripts from the Desert Libraries of Timbuktu - Exhibition items","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/mali/mali-exhibit.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","This compilation of cures instructs the reader about methods of diagnosing and medicating the sick.")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"Kano and Zaria manuscripts",text:"Northern Nigeria has an Arabic manuscript tradition. The Kano Chronicle, an Arabic history of Kano's kings written in the 1890s, is the best-known local history of the Hausa.",figure:"late 19th century copy",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The surviving Kano Chronicle was written in the late 1880s, so it is a late source for early history. Details of Zaria manuscript collections are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Kano, historical kingdom","https://www.britannica.com/place/Kano-historical-kingdom-Nigeria","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","According to the Kano Chronicle (1890s), the best-known native history of the Hausa people,"),SRCQ("Hiskett (1957), 'The Kano Chronicle', Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/89BD4E84EB34C55E00EED1C129E84E66/S0035869X00107385a.pdf/the-kano-chronicle.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The several Arabic MSS. of the work had, for years, been lost sight of,"),SRCQ("British Library Endangered Archives: Arabic manuscripts held by the Kano State History and Culture Bureau","https://eap.bl.uk/collection/EAP087-1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The majority of the manuscripts are letters written by prominent judges, Emirs and leaders."),SRCQ("Hunwick 1994, History in Africa 21 (Cambridge), quoting Palmer","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/abs/historical-whodunit-the-socalled-kano-chronicle-and-its-place-in-the-historiography-of-kano/8DC5438DE86A0CF05C1E6FCE707FE67E","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","must on internal evidence have been written during the latter part of the decade 1883-1893"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Kano Chronicle (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kano_Chronicle","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Arabic-language manuscript that lists the rulers of Kano")],evidence:"documentary"}]},
 {k:"myths",h:"Testing the claims: what holds and what does not",intro:"A credible hub must test claims that flatter the case as hard as claims against it. Each item gives a verdict: false, exaggerated, unproven or partly true.",points:[
  {h:"\"The Dogon knew about Sirius B\" - unproven, probably false",text:"The French anthropologist Marcel Griaule reported that the Dogon of Mali knew of an invisible companion star of Sirius. In 1991 Walter van Beek restudied the Dogon and found no shared knowledge of this; his informants said they learned about the star from Griaule. Britannica describes a Dogon ceremony as resting on a belief that beings from Sirius visited the Dogon, but does not discuss this dispute.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: unproven and probably the result of contact with the researcher. Griaule's daughter disputed van Beek's method, so the debate is not fully closed.",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Dogon people (secondary source quoting van Beek 1991, Current Anthropology 32(2))","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dogon_people","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","All agree, however, that they learned about the star from Griaule."),SRCQ("Dogon and Bangime Linguistics bibliography: van Beek (1991) Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule","https://dogonlanguages.info/sources/vanbeek1991","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule"),SRCQ("Britannica: Dogon","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Dogon","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","amphibious beings from Sirius visited the Dogon."),SRCQ("The Skeptic's Dictionary: Dogon","https://skepdic.com/dogon.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Temple has either misinterpreted Dogon beliefs, or distorted Griaule and Dieterlen's claims")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"\"Ishango proves prime numbers\" - unproven",text:"One column of the Ishango bone has groups of 11, 13, 17 and 19 notches, the primes between 10 and 20. Some scholars doubt this shows knowledge of primes, as there is no evidence of the idea before classical Greece.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: unproven. The bone is evidence of counting or recording; \"proves primes\" goes beyond the evidence.",src:[SRCQ("ICOMOS-IAU Thematic Study on Astronomical Heritage, case study 1.3: Ishango bone","https://web.astronomicalheritage.org/images/astronomicalheritage.org/thematic-study/ch01cs3.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the numbers contained in the first column (G) are precisely the four prime numbers between 10 and 20"),SRCQ("Pletser, 'Does the Ishango Bone Indicate Knowledge of the Base 12?' (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.1019","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the hypothesis of mastering the knowledge of prime numbers appears too audacious"),SRCQ("Pletser, 'Does the Ishango Bone Indicate Knowledge of the Base 12?' (arXiv)","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.1019","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","no awareness of the notion of prime numbers has been discovered before the classical Greek period"),SRCQ("Parkinson-Coombs, Courtenay, Geis, d'Errico and Núñez (2026) 'Was the Ishango Bone — \"The Oldest Numerical Device\" — a Mathematical Tool? An Empirical Approach', Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Cognitive Science Society 48 (eScholarship, University of California)","https://escholarship.org/content/qt5sk7g51r/qt5sk7g51r.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Some, while dismissing the invocation of prime numbers, have drawn attention to 'what seems less coincidental... [that]"),SRCQ("Parkinson-Coombs et al. (2026), Proceedings of the Cognitive Science Society 48","https://escholarship.org/content/qt5sk7g51r/qt5sk7g51r.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","As suggestive as the patterns of notch collections are, they alone are insufficient to support the claim of a numerical"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ishango bone (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishango_bone","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","but this might be a coincidence")],evidence:"artefact"},
  {h:"\"Africans invented algebra\" - false as stated",text:"Algebra as a named subject comes from al-Khwarizmi's book, written in Baghdad, where he worked until about 850. North Africans contributed later: Abu Kamil in Egypt (about 850 to 930) and Ibn al-Banna and al-Qalasadi, who used symbols in the 15th century. Egyptian papyri solved some equation-like problems much earlier.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: false as a blanket claim; Africa's real contributions are Egyptian problem-solving and later North African work in the Islamic tradition.",src:[SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Al-Khwarizmi biography","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Khwarizmi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","founded an academy called the House of Wisdom"),SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Al-Khwarizmi biography","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Khwarizmi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","His algebra treatise Hisab al-jabr w'al-muqabala gives us the word algebra"),SRCQ("Britannica: al-Khwarizmi","https://www.britannica.com/biography/al-Khwarizmi","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","live on in the terms algorithm and algebra."),SRCQ("Wikipedia: History of algebra (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_algebra","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Egyptian mathematician Abū Kāmil Shujā ibn Aslam (c. 850–930)"),SRCQ("MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews: Arabic mathematics - forgotten brilliance?","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Arabic_mathematics/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Ibn al-Banna and al-Qalasadi used symbols in the 15 th century")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"\"The pyramids were built by slaves\" - not supported",text:"Excavations at Giza found a town for workers and tombs for builders beside the pyramids, with regular meat supplies and three-month work rotations. Egypt's antiquities chief concluded they were paid labourers.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: the slave story is not supported. But \"free volunteers\" also overstates it: rotated labour may have been compulsory service for the state. The \"paid\" reading is the excavators' interpretation.",src:[SRCQ("NBC News / Associated Press (2010): Egypt discovers workers' tombs near pyramids","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna34794254","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","show that the workers were paid laborers, rather than the slaves of popular imagination"),SRCQ("NBC News / Associated Press (2010): Egypt discovers workers' tombs near pyramids","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna34794254","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the workers were rotated every three months"),SRCQ("Ancient Egypt Research Associates (AERA): The Lost City of the Pyramids (Heit el-Ghurab)","https://aeraweb.org/projects/lost-city/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Purpose-built by the crown")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"\"Egypt was not African\" and the race debate - frame by ancestry and geography",text:"Egypt is in Africa, at its junction with Asia. Ancient DNA is still scarce: one Old Kingdom genome is mostly North African Neolithic ancestry with about 20% from the eastern Fertile Crescent. Later mummies from Middle Egypt share more ancestry with Near Eastern peoples than today's Egyptians, who have more recent sub-Saharan ancestry. Mainstream scholars say modern \"black\" and \"white\" labels do not fit ancient Egypt.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: \"Egypt was not African\" is false on geography and misleading on ancestry. Equally, claims that Egypt was a single \"race\" are not supported. Limits: very few genomes, mostly from northern and middle Egypt, none from Upper Egypt or Nubia in these studies.",src:[SRCQ("Schuenemann et al. (2017) Ancient Egyptian mummy genomes suggest an increase of Sub-Saharan African ancestry in post-Roman periods. Nature Communications - abstract","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5459999/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Egypt, located on the isthmus of Africa"),SRCQ("Morez Jacobs et al. (2025) Whole-genome ancestry of an Old Kingdom Egyptian. Nature - abstract","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09195-5","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Most of his genome is best represented by North African Neolithic ancestry"),SRCQ("Morez Jacobs et al. (2025) Whole-genome ancestry of an Old Kingdom Egyptian. Nature - abstract","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09195-5","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","approximately 20% of his genetic ancestry can be traced to genomes representing the eastern Fertile Crescent"),SRCQ("Schuenemann et al. (2017) Ancient Egyptian mummy genomes suggest an increase of Sub-Saharan African ancestry in post-Roman periods. Nature Communications - abstract","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5459999/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","ancient Egyptians shared more ancestry with Near Easterners than present-day Egyptians"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ancient Egyptian race controversy (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egyptian_race_controversy","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","applying modern notions of black or white races to ancient Egypt is anachronistic")],evidence:"natural"},
  {h:"\"Africa was more advanced than Europe\" - partly true for some fields and dates, false as a blanket claim",text:"In some fields Africa was earlier: Egyptian writing (about 3250 BC) came about 1,800 years before Europe's earliest deciphered script (Linear B, about 1450 BC), and Giza's pyramids match Stonehenge in date. In others Europe was level or earlier: farming in Britain began about 4100 BC, the Aegean used iron before West Africa, and Europe has engraved bones as old as Ishango. Egypt itself drew on contacts with south-west Asia, including farming and writing ideas.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: compare like with like, by date and by region. \"Africa\" and \"Europe\" were never single societies, so league tables mislead.",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Linear B (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_B","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the earliest known examples dating to around 1450 BC"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ishango bone (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishango_bone","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","approximately 26,000-year-old \"Wolf Bone\" from Dolni Vestonice"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Neolithic British Isles (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neolithic_British_Isles","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","British, Irish and Manx history c. 4100–2500 BC"),SRCQ("English Heritage: History of Stonehenge","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/stonehenge/history-and-stories/history/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the earliest known major event was the construction of a circular ditch with an inner and outer bank, built about 3000 BC"),SRCQ("Morez Jacobs et al. (2025) Whole-genome ancestry of an Old Kingdom Egyptian. Nature - abstract","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09195-5","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","contacts between Egypt and the eastern Fertile Crescent were not limited to objects and imagery")],evidence:"dating"},
  {h:"\"Europeans brought civilisation to Africa\" - false",text:"Every section of this page records cities, states, writing, metalwork, coinage, scholarship and trade in Africa long before European colonial rule. The idea that Africa had no history before Europeans arrived was voiced in the 20th century by the Oxford historian Hugh Trevor-Roper. Colonial authorities in Rhodesia pressed archaeologists to deny that Africans built Great Zimbabwe. UNESCO's General History of Africa (from 1964) was created to correct this.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Verdict: false. Balance: African states were not ideal societies; several (Mali, Kilwa) also took part in slavery, as did European states.",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Hugh Trevor-Roper (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Trevor-Roper","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","there is only the history of Europeans in Africa. The rest is darkness"),SRCQ("Wikipedia: Great Zimbabwe (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the white government of Rhodesia pressured archaeologists to deny its construction by black Africans"),SRCQ("The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Great Zimbabwe (11th-15th century)","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/zimb/hd_zimb.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","attributed the architecture to foreign powers."),SRCQ("UNESCO: General History of Africa","https://www.unesco.org/en/general-history-africa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","to reframe the continent’s history from an African perspective")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"\"Mansa Musa was the richest person ever\" - unmeasurable",text:"Britannica repeats that he is widely considered the wealthiest man in history. There is no way to compare medieval wealth with modern fortunes reliably.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: exaggerated. Say instead that his 1324 hajj displayed enormous wealth in gold.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Musa I of Mali","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Musa-I-of-Mali","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Widely considered to be the wealthiest man in history")],evidence:"documentary"},
  {h:"\"Benin's walls were four times longer than the Great Wall\" - exaggerated precision",text:"The total length rests on a single 1977 estimate of about 16,000 km, mostly of low banks and ditches, not a measured wall.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: impressive and real, but the comparison treats an estimate of low earthworks as if it were a measured stone wall.",src:[SRCQ("Guinness World Records: Longest earthworks of the pre-mechanical era","https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/97959-longest-earthworks-of-the-pre-mechanical-era","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","estimated by Dr Patrick Darling"),SRCQ("NASA Earth Observatory (2025): A glimpse of history in Benin City","https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/images/154894/a-glimpse-of-history-in-benin-city","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Many sections have been destroyed or are too small or too obscured by modern development to be easily detected")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"\"Nabta Playa is the world's first observatory, older than Stonehenge\" - partly true",text:"Nabta's stones are older than Stonehenge's stone circle, and some alignments to the solstice are reported in Nature. \"First observatory\" goes beyond what the paper claims.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: older, yes; an astronomical observatory, unproven.",src:[SRCQ("Malville, Wendorf, Mazar and Schild (1998) Megaliths and Neolithic astronomy in southern Egypt. Nature 392 - abstract","https://ideas.repec.org/a/nat/nature/v392y1998i6675d10.1038_33131.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The organization of the megaliths suggests a symbolic geometry that integrated death, water, and the Sun.")],evidence:"structure"},
  {h:"\"The Haya made steel hotter than anything in Europe\" - unproven",text:"This comes from popular sources based on 1970s fieldwork and recreated smelts in the 20th century, not on dated ancient steel analysed in a lab.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Verdict: unproven. Present Haya smelting as skilled and inventive, without the superlative.",src:[SRCQ("University of Houston, The Engines of Our Ingenuity No. 385: African Steel Making (John H. Lienhard, radio script c.1990)","https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/385","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It was the same steel that'd served the subsaharan peoples for two millinea before it was almost forgotten.")],evidence:"artefact"}]},
 {k:"t-ancient",h:"The ancient Mediterranean: Egypt, Alexandria, Carthage and Roman Africa",intro:"Long before Islam, Africa was part of the Mediterranean world of learning and power. Greek writers themselves said they owed things to Egypt, Alexandria was a leading centre of Greek-language science, and Roman North Africa produced an emperor and one of Christianity's most influential thinkers. These are reports and attributions, not proofs of a single \"origin\" of knowledge.",points:[
  {h:"Herodotus on Egypt and the Greek gods",text:"Herodotus (5th century BC) wrote that the names of most Greek gods came from Egypt, while making clear he was repeating what Egyptians told him.",figure:"",year:"-440",disputed:true,note:"This is Herodotus's own claim, reported neutrally. Modern scholars treat his Egyptian derivations with caution; the passage shows what Greeks believed, not a proven borrowing.",src:[SRCQ("Herodotus, Histories 2.50 (trans. A. D. Godley) - Perseids / Perseus Digital Library text","https://fgh.perseids.org/read/greekLit/tlg0016/tlg001/perseus-eng2/2.50.1-2.50.3","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the names of all the gods have always existed in Egypt"),SRCQ("Herodotus, Histories 2.50 (trans. A. D. Godley) - Perseids / Perseus Digital Library text","https://fgh.perseids.org/read/greekLit/tlg0016/tlg001/perseus-eng2/2.50.1-2.50.3","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","I only say what the Egyptians themselves say")],origin:"Egypt"},
  {h:"Plato's Egyptian priest",text:"In Plato's Timaeus, an elderly priest at Sais in Egypt tells the Athenian lawmaker Solon that Greeks are \"always children\" compared with Egypt's long records.",figure:"",year:"-360",disputed:true,note:"A literary dialogue, not a historical record. It shows Greek respect for Egyptian antiquity, not a documented transfer of specific knowledge.",src:[SRCQ("Plato, Timaeus 22b - Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University","https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0180:text=Tim.:section=22b","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","O Solon, Solon, you Greeks are always children: there is not such a thing as an old Greek")],origin:"Egypt"},
  {h:"Thales and Egyptian geometry",text:"Later Greek writers said Thales learned geometry in Egypt and brought it to Greece. MacTutor adds that Egyptian geometry was largely practical rules, not deductive proof.",figure:"",year:"-585",disputed:true,note:"The tradition is late (\"is said to\"). Both sides are shown: Egyptian practice influenced Greeks; formal proof is credited to later Greeks.",src:[SRCQ("Thales of Miletus - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Thales/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Thales is said to have travelled in Egypt, and to have thence brought to the Greeks the science of geometry"),SRCQ("Thales of Miletus - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Thales/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","What Egyptians knew of geometry was mainly rules of thumb")],origin:"Egypt"},
  {h:"Euclid of Alexandria",text:"Euclid, author of the Elements, worked in Alexandria, Egypt, a Greek-speaking city on African soil founded by Alexander the Great.",figure:"",year:"-300",disputed:false,note:"Alexandria was an African city, but its scholarship was written in Greek and drew on the whole eastern Mediterranean. Euclid's own ethnicity is unknown.",src:[SRCQ("Euclid of Alexandria - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Euclid/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Euclid of Alexandria is the most prominent mathematician of antiquity best known for his treatise on mathematics The Elements")],origin:"Egypt"},
  {h:"Ptolemy in Alexandria",text:"Ptolemy made astronomical observations from Alexandria. His great work reached medieval Europe through Arabic as the \"Almagest\".",figure:"",year:"150",disputed:false,note:"A clear example of a two-step route: Greek text written in Egypt, translated into Arabic, then from Arabic into Latin (see Gerard of Cremona).",src:[SRCQ("Claudius Ptolemy - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Ptolemy/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","He made astronomical observations from Alexandria in Egypt during the years AD 127-41"),SRCQ("Claudius Ptolemy - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Ptolemy/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","This was translated into Arabic as \"al-majisti\" and from this the title Almagest was given to the work")],origin:"Egypt"},
  {h:"Hypatia of Alexandria",text:"Hypatia, a mathematician and philosopher taught by her father Theon, worked in Alexandria and was killed in 415.",figure:"",year:"415",disputed:false,note:"MacTutor calls her the first woman (to make a substantial contribution to mathematics, in its fuller sentence).",src:[SRCQ("Hypatia of Alexandria - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Hypatia/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Hypatia of Alexandria was the first woman")],origin:"Egypt"},
  {h:"Carthage",text:"Carthage, founded by Phoenician colonists in present-day Tunisia, built a Mediterranean trading empire until Rome destroyed it in 146 BC; it was later rebuilt as capital of Roman Africa.",figure:"9th century BC founding; destroyed 146 BC",year:"-146",disputed:false,note:"Carthage was founded by settlers from the Levant (Phoenicians) and mixed with local Berber/Libyan populations; it is African in place, Levantine in origin. UNESCO page also gives \"146 AD\" in one section, an evident typo.",src:[SRCQ("Archaeological Site of Carthage - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/37","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Carthage was founded in the 9th century B.C. on the Gulf of Tunis."),SRCQ("Western North Africa, 1000 B.C.-1 A.D. - Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History, The Met","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/04/afw.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Carthaginian ships carry metals, oil, wine, grain, and other products to ports throughout the western Mediterranean.")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Septimius Severus, emperor from Africa (193-211)",text:"Septimius Severus was born at Lepcis (Leptis) Magna in present-day Libya, ruled Rome from 193 and died at York in 211.",figure:"",year:"193",disputed:true,note:"English Heritage says his father's family was Punic (of Phoenician descent) and his mother's Italian. He is sometimes called \"Rome's Black Emperor\"; Historic Environment Scotland says his skin colour cannot be verified. Present him as the first emperor from Africa, not as Black.",src:[SRCQ("Septimius Severus - English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/hadrians-wall/hadrians-wall-history-and-stories/septimius-severus/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Severus was born in Lepcis Magna (Khoms in present-day Libya)."),SRCQ("Septimius Severus - English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/hadrians-wall/hadrians-wall-history-and-stories/septimius-severus/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Severus died at York on 4 February AD 211"),SRCQ("Scotland's African Emperor - Historic Environment Scotland Blog","https://blog.historicenvironment.scot/2017/10/scotlands-african-emperor/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","We can’t verify the exact tone of his skin, but we can say Severus was born and brought up in Libya")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Augustine of Hippo",text:"Augustine, among the most influential Christian thinkers, was born in Thagaste in Roman Africa (modern Souk Ahras, Algeria) and was bishop of Hippo.",figure:"",year:"354",disputed:false,note:"Born to a Roman-African family; often described as of Berber descent, but that is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Saint Augustine - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/augustine/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","He was born in Thagaste in Roman Africa (modern Souk Ahras in Algeria)."),SRCQ("Saint Augustine - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/augustine/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Augustine of Hippo was perhaps the greatest Christian philosopher of Antiquity")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Africans in Roman Britain",text:"North Africans served and lived in Roman Britain; English Heritage notes a tombstone of Victor, a former slave from North Africa, at South Shields.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Covered in the hub already (Black Romans in Britain); link there rather than repeat.",src:[SRCQ("Septimius Severus - English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/hadrians-wall/hadrians-wall-history-and-stories/septimius-severus/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Romans of North African origin are well represented in the historical record")],origin:"North Africa"}]},
 {k:"t-andalus",h:"Al-Andalus, 711-1492",intro:"Muslim rule in Iberia began with an army from North Africa led by a Berber commander, and two later Berber dynasties from Morocco ruled al-Andalus. Much of the science that flourished there came from Baghdad, Persia and India, carried west through North Africa. Its greatest European impact came when Christian scholars translated Arabic texts into Latin, above all in Toledo.",points:[
  {h:"Tariq ibn Ziyad and 711",text:"In 711 North African Muslim forces led by the Berber general Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed into the Iberian Peninsula.",figure:"",year:"711",disputed:false,note:"Conquest under the Umayyad caliphate of Damascus; the rank and file were largely Berber, leadership mixed Arab and Berber.",src:[SRCQ("Who were the Moors? It's complicated - National Geographic","https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/who-were-moors","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a group of North African Muslims led by the Berber general, Tariq ibn-Ziyad, captured the Iberian Peninsula")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Cordoba as a caliphal capital",text:"UNESCO records that in 929 Abd al-Rahman III made Cordoba the seat of an independent caliphate, and that the Great Mosque was begun in the 8th century.",figure:"",year:"929",disputed:false,note:"The Umayyads of Cordoba were a Syrian Arab dynasty; the mosque's style draws on Syrian, Visigothic and local traditions.",src:[SRCQ("Historic Centre of Cordoba - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/313","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","In 929, Abderraman III established it as the headquarters of the independent Caliphate."),SRCQ("Historic Centre of Cordoba - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/313","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It was built in the 8th century, over the remains of the Visigoth Basilica of San Vicente.")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"The Cordoba library: how big?",text:"Medieval sources credit Caliph al-Hakam II (r. 961-976) with a palace library of over 400,000 books. A peer-reviewed study calls the figure likely hyperbole, while agreeing the library was a major centre.",figure:"400,000 volumes (reported, likely exaggerated)",year:"970",disputed:true,note:"Report the figure only as a claim in later sources. The library drew scholars from Baghdad and Constantinople, per the same article.",src:[SRCQ("U. Bongianino, The page architecture of a deluxe Arabic dictionary from Islamic Spain - Manuscript and Text Cultures (peer-reviewed)","https://mtc-journal.org/index.php/mtc/article/download/27/61?inline=1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a number of sources credit him with the establishment of a palatine library containing more than 400,000 books"),SRCQ("U. Bongianino, The page architecture of a deluxe Arabic dictionary from Islamic Spain - Manuscript and Text Cultures (peer-reviewed)","https://mtc-journal.org/index.php/mtc/article/download/27/61?inline=1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","While the number 400,000 is likely a hyperbole")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"New crops and irrigation",text:"Andrew Watson (1974, book 1983) argued for an \"Arab Agricultural Revolution\": new crops spread, especially from India, across the Middle East, North Africa and al-Andalus.",figure:"",year:"1974",disputed:true,note:"Crops such as sugar cane, rice, citrus and cotton are often cited; a crop-by-crop list is not confirmed by the sources cited here. Many came from India or South-East Asia, with North Africa as route.",src:[SRCQ("D. Varisco and D. Fuks, Andrew Watson's contribution to the history of agriculture - Journal of Economic History, In Memoriam (Cambridge Core)","https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/announcements/in-memoriam","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","diffusion of new crops and medicinal plants, especially from India, to the Middle East, North Africa and Andalusia")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Critics of the \"revolution\"",text:"Later scholars accept that Watson's survey is valuable but argue several crops were already present before Islam and that \"revolution\" overstates change. Michael Decker (2009) and others have challenged the thesis.",figure:"",year:"2009",disputed:true,note:"Decker, \"Plants and Progress: Rethinking the Arab Agricultural Revolution\", Journal of World History 20(2), 2009, is cited in the Squatriti reference list; its argument is summarised here from the Varisco and Fuks piece, not from Decker directly.",src:[SRCQ("D. Varisco and D. Fuks, Andrew Watson's contribution to the history of agriculture - Journal of Economic History, In Memoriam (Cambridge Core)","https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/announcements/in-memoriam","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","A major problem with Watson's analysis is the suggestion of a unique \"agricultural revolution\" stimulated by the Arab conquests"),SRCQ("D. Varisco and D. Fuks, Andrew Watson's contribution to the history of agriculture - Journal of Economic History, In Memoriam (Cambridge Core)","https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/announcements/in-memoriam","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","A number of the crops he discussed were present before the Islamic era"),SRCQ("P. Squatriti, Of Seeds, Seasons, and Seas: Andrew Watson's Medieval Agrarian Revolution Forty Years Later - Journal of Economic History (2014)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/of-seeds-seasons-and-seas-andrew-watsons-medieval-agrarian-revolution-forty-years-later/B714D229B1C2DA502612680FD3B5C17C","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","has been used and cited widely by historians and archaeologists, many working in fields far removed from Watson's")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"The Alhambra",text:"UNESCO describes the Alhambra and Generalife as exceptional testimony to Muslim Spain, built mainly under the Nasrid emirs of Granada.",figure:"",year:"1238",disputed:false,note:"Nasrid art is Andalusi: Iberian, with roots in North African and eastern Islamic traditions. Quote reproduced as the page gives it (missing \"of\").",src:[SRCQ("Alhambra, Generalife and Albayzin, Granada - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/314","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Alhambra and Generalife bear exceptional testimony to Muslim Spain the 13th and 15th centuries.")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Toledo translations",text:"In the second half of the 12th century, Toledo became a centre for translating Arabic works, and Greek works preserved in Arabic, into Latin. Gerard of Cremona and Dominicus Gundisalvi were the most prolific translators.",figure:"",year:"1150",disputed:false,note:"Other named translators include the Jewish scholar Avendauth and, later, Michael Scot (SEP). The texts were mostly by Persian, Iraqi and Greek authors (Avicenna, al-Farabi, Ptolemy).",src:[SRCQ("Influence of Arabic and Islamic Philosophy on the Latin West - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arabic-islamic-influence/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in Toledo in the second half of the twelfth century, where two very prolific translators worked: Gerard of Cremona and Dominicus Gundisalvi")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Gerard of Cremona and the Almagest",text:"Gerard went to Toledo to learn Arabic so he could read and translate Ptolemy's Almagest, and made Arabic science available in Latin.",figure:"",year:"1175",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Gherard of Cremona - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Gherard/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Gherard went to Toledo in Spain where his intention was to learn Arabic so he could read Ptolemy's Almagest")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Ibn Rushd (Averroes)",text:"Ibn Rushd was born in Cordoba in 1126, served the Almohad court in Marrakesh as judge and physician, and became the Latin West's great commentator on Aristotle.",figure:"",year:"1126",disputed:true,note:"Andalusi-born; career split between Iberia and Morocco. Death in Marrakesh (1198) is given by one account (SEP); IEP says he returned to Cordoba and died the following year without naming the place.",src:[SRCQ("Ibn Rushd [Averroes] - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-rushd/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Abū al-Walīd Muḥammad ibn Ahmad Ibn Rushd was born in Cordova in 1126"),SRCQ("Ibn Rushd [Averroes] - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-rushd/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","According to one account, his death took place while he was confined to a residence in Marrakesh")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Maimonides",text:"Moses Maimonides was born in Cordoba in 1138; his family later left, and he settled in Fustat (old Cairo), Egypt, where he worked as a physician and scholar.",figure:"",year:"1138",disputed:false,note:"A Jewish Andalusi scholar who wrote mainly in Arabic: Iberian-born, career in Morocco and Egypt.",src:[SRCQ("Maimonides - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/maimonides/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Maimonides was born to a distinguished family in Cordova, Spain in 1138"),SRCQ("Maimonides - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/maimonides/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Maimonides arrived in Egypt in 1166 and eventually settled in Fustat")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Al-Zarqali and the Toledan Tables",text:"Al-Zarqali (Arzachel), an instrument maker and astronomer in 11th-century Toledo, is best known for the Toledan Tables, which circulated in Latin.",figure:"",year:"1070",disputed:false,note:"Andalusi, working in Islamic Toledo before its conquest by Castile in 1085.",src:[SRCQ("Al-Zarqali - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Zarqali/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Perhaps he is even more famous for the Toledan Tables"),SRCQ("Al-Zarqali - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Zarqali/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","We do, however, have two versions of copies of the Tables which were translated into Latin")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Abbas ibn Firnas and flight",text:"Ibn Firnas, a 9th-century Cordoban scholar, is said to have attempted a glide with feathered wings about 875. The accounts survive through al-Maqqari, a 17th-century Moroccan scholar.",figure:"",year:"875",disputed:true,note:"The sources are late and brief; \"first aviator\" claims go beyond the evidence. Often described as of Berber descent, but that is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Flights of Fancy on Manmade Wings - AramcoWorld, Nov/Dec 2008","https://archive.aramcoworld.com/issue/200806/flights.of.fancy.on.manmade.wings.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Thanks to the 17th-century Moroccan scholar al-Maqqari, two accounts of Ibn Firnas’s flight survive."),SRCQ("Flights of Fancy on Manmade Wings - AramcoWorld, Nov/Dec 2008","https://archive.aramcoworld.com/issue/200806/flights.of.fancy.on.manmade.wings.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is all that the scant historical descriptions tell us")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Ziryab",text:"Ziryab, a musician from Baghdad, reached the Cordoban court in the 9th century. Later writers credit him with changes to the lute and to music, dress and dining customs.",figure:"",year:"822",disputed:true,note:"Came from Baghdad via North Africa. Much of his reputation rests on Ibn Hayyan (11th century) and later sources; specific \"innovations\" are often embellished. His ethnic origin is debated; the dark-complexion report is from Ibn Hayyan.",src:[SRCQ("R. W. Lebling Jr, Flight of the Blackbird - AramcoWorld, July/August 2003","https://archive.aramcoworld.com/issue/200304/flight.of.the.blackbird.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Ziryab knew thousands of songs by heart and revolutionized the design of the musical instrument that became the lute"),SRCQ("R. W. Lebling Jr, Flight of the Blackbird - AramcoWorld, July/August 2003","https://archive.aramcoworld.com/issue/200304/flight.of.the.blackbird.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was called Blackbird because of his extremely dark complexion")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Almoravids",text:"The Almoravids were a confederation of Berber (Sanhaja) peoples from the western Sahara who founded Marrakesh and ruled both Morocco and al-Andalus.",figure:"",year:"1086",disputed:false,note:"Marrakesh founding date (c.1070) and conquest of al-Andalus (from 1086) are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Almoravids - Encyclopedia.com (Europe, 1450 to 1789 / Encyclopedia of Islam and the Muslim World entry)","https://www.encyclopedia.com/history/modern-europe/spanish-and-portuguese-history/almoravids","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","confederation of Berber"),SRCQ("Ibn Rushd (Averroes) - Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://iep.utm.edu/ibn-rushd/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Almohads, like the Almoravids they had supplanted, were a Northwest African Kharijite-influenced Berber reform movement.")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Almohads",text:"The Almohads, a rival Berber movement from the Moroccan High Atlas, replaced the Almoravids and patronised scholars such as Ibn Rushd and Ibn Tufayl.",figure:"",year:"1147",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Almoravids - Encyclopedia.com (Europe, 1450 to 1789 / Encyclopedia of Islam and the Muslim World entry)","https://www.encyclopedia.com/history/modern-europe/spanish-and-portuguese-history/almoravids","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","a confederation of rival Berber"),SRCQ("Ibn Rushd (Averroes) - Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://iep.utm.edu/ibn-rushd/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Ibn Rushd traveled to Marrakesh and came under the patronage of the caliph ‘Abd al-Mu’min")],origin:"North Africa"}]},
 {k:"t-numbers",h:"Numbers and mathematics",intro:"The numerals Europe uses today began in India. They reached Europe through the Arabic-speaking world, with North Africa and al-Andalus as the main western route. North African mathematicians also developed the subject in their own right.",points:[
  {h:"Indian origin of the numerals",text:"The numerals originated in India; the scholar al-Biruni (11th century) said Arabs used a selection of Indian figures.",figure:"",year:"1030",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Indian numerals - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Indian_numerals/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","What we [the Arabs] use for numerals is a selection of the best and most regular figures in India")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"The western route: North Africa and Spain",text:"MacTutor states the numerals reached Europe through the western Arabic world, mainly North Africa and Spain.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Arabic numeral system - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Arabic_numerals/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Transmission to Europe came through this western Arabic route, coming into Europe first through Spain"),SRCQ("The Arabic numeral system - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Arabic_numerals/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","By the western part of the Arabic world we mean the regions comprising mainly North Africa and Spain")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Ghubar (\"dust\") numerals",text:"In the Maghreb and al-Andalus, the western forms were called ghubar (\"dust\") numerals, after the dust board used for calculating.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"European numeral shapes descend from these western forms.",src:[SRCQ("The Arabic numeral system - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Arabic_numerals/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in the western part of the Arabic world the Indian numerals came to be known as Guba (or Gubar or Ghubar")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Codex Vigilanus, 976",text:"The earliest known appearance of these numerals in a European manuscript is in the Codex Vigilanus, copied by a monk in Spain in 976.",figure:"",year:"976",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Arabic numeral system - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Arabic_numerals/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The numerals appear in the Codex Vigilanus copied by a monk in Spain in 976")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Gerbert of Aurillac (Pope Sylvester II)",text:"Gerbert studied in Catalonia in the 960s, learned the Hindu-Arabic numerals there and promoted them, with the abacus, in Europe.",figure:"",year:"967",disputed:false,note:"He studied in Christian Catalonia, which was in contact with al-Andalus; he did not study in Muslim Cordoba, despite popular claims.",src:[SRCQ("Gerbert of Aurillac - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Gerbert/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","he had learnt about the Hindu-Arabic numerals while in Spain")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Fibonacci in Bejaia (Bugia), Algeria",text:"Leonardo of Pisa (Fibonacci) learned the \"nine Indian figures\" as a boy in Bugia (now Bejaia, Algeria), where his father represented Pisan merchants. He said so in the Liber Abaci (1202).",figure:"",year:"1202",disputed:false,note:"The clearest single example of North Africa as the route: Indian numerals, learned from North African teachers, published in Italy.",src:[SRCQ("Leonardo Pisano Fibonacci - MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fibonacci/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","merchants of the Republic of Pisa who were trading in Bugia, later called Bougie and now called Bejaia"),SRCQ("Leonardo Pisano Fibonacci - MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fibonacci/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","I had been introduced to the art of the Indians' nine symbols through remarkable teaching")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Al-Khwarizmi's algebra",text:"Al-Khwarizmi, working at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad around 820, wrote the book that gave us the word \"algebra\"; his name gave us \"algorithm\".",figure:"",year:"820",disputed:false,note:"Baghdad and Central Asia (Khwarazm), not Africa. The route to Europe ran through Spain.",src:[SRCQ("Al-Khwarizmi - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Khwarizmi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","His algebra treatise Hisab al-jabr w'al-muqabala gives us the word algebra"),SRCQ("Al-Khwarizmi - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Khwarizmi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","founded an academy called the House of Wisdom where Greek philosophical and scientific works were translated")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Robert of Chester, 1145, Segovia",text:"The English scholar Robert of Chester translated al-Khwarizmi's algebra into Latin in 1145, working in Segovia.",figure:"",year:"1145",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Robert of Chester - Muslim Heritage","https://muslimheritage.com/people/scholars/robert-of-chester/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Liber algebrae et almucabola, Al-Khwārizmī’s book about algebra, translated in 1145"),SRCQ("Robert of Chester - Muslim Heritage","https://muslimheritage.com/people/scholars/robert-of-chester/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Robert of Chester is known to have worked in Segovia")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Ibn al-Banna of Marrakesh",text:"Ibn al-Banna (born Marrakesh) taught mathematics and astronomy in Fez and used some symbolic notation.",figure:"",year:"1300",disputed:true,note:"MacTutor argues that neither al-Banna nor al-Qalasadi invented algebraic notation; earlier attempts existed.",src:[SRCQ("Ibn al-Banna - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Banna/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","At the university in Fez Al-Banna taught all branches of mathematics"),SRCQ("Ibn al-Banna - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Banna/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","al-Banna introduces some mathematical notation")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Al-Qalasadi and algebraic symbols",text:"Al-Qalasadi (1412-1486), born at Baza in al-Andalus and died at Beja in Tunisia, used short Arabic words or letters as algebraic symbols.",figure:"",year:"1460",disputed:true,note:"Andalusi-born, career ended in Tunisia (Ifriqiya). \"First\" claims are qualified by MacTutor itself.",src:[SRCQ("Al-Qalasadi - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Qalasadi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","took the first steps toward the introduction of algebraic symbolism by using letters in place of numbers"),SRCQ("Al-Qalasadi - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Qalasadi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","without taking into consideration similar previous attempts both in Eastern and in Western Islam")],origin:"mixed"}]},
 {k:"t-univ",h:"Universities and places of learning",intro:"North Africa hosts some of the oldest continuously working places of higher learning. Whether they count as \"universities\" depends on the definition: the word comes from medieval European legal corporations such as Bologna, while al-Qarawiyyin and al-Azhar began as mosque-colleges.",points:[
  {h:"Al-Qarawiyyin, Fez (859)",text:"Guinness World Records lists the University of al-Qarawiyyin, founded in 859 in Fez, as the oldest existing, continually operating educational institution. Its founder is traditionally named as Fatima al-Fihri.",figure:"859",year:"859",disputed:true,note:"UNESCO's own wording is not confirmed by the sources cited. \"First degree-granting\" is a contested claim; it began as a mosque with teaching and became a state university in 1963 (not confirmed by the sources cited). Fatima al-Fihri as founder is reported by the NYU page.",src:[SRCQ("Oldest higher-learning institution - Guinness World Records","https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/oldest-university","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The oldest existing and continually operating educational institution in the world is the University of Karueein,"),SRCQ("Islam and Knowledge - The Muslim Experience at NYU Tandon, Poly Archives (New York University)","https://polyarchives.hosting.nyu.edu/exhibits/show/the-muslim-experience-poly/islam-and-knowledge","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","According to UNESCO and Guinness World Records, the world's oldest continuously operating and first degree-granting educational institution")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Al-Azhar, Cairo (970)",text:"Al-Azhar was founded in 970 by the Fatimids, a dynasty that came from North Africa, and became a leading seat of Sunni learning.",figure:"",year:"970",disputed:false,note:"Fatimids rose in Ifriqiya (Tunisia) before conquering Egypt. Teaching began 972 per Muslim Heritage.",src:[SRCQ("Al-Azhar - Encyclopedia.com","https://www.encyclopedia.com/environment/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/al-azhar","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","from North Africa, founded this mosque on Saturday, 24 Jumādā I 359/April 4, 970"),SRCQ("Al-Azhar University - Muslim Heritage","https://muslimheritage.com/al-azhar-university/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It was founded by the Fatimids in 970 CE and was formally organized by 988")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"Bologna (1088)",text:"The University of Bologna dates itself to 1088, which it describes as a conventional date.",figure:"",year:"1088",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Our history - University of Bologna","https://www.unibo.it/en/university/who-we-are/our-history","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","from as far back as 1088, conventionally referred to as the year in which the Studium of Bologna was founded")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Oxford (c.1096-1167)",text:"Oxford has no founding date; teaching existed by 1096 and grew rapidly from 1167.",figure:"",year:"1096",disputed:false,note:"Not confirmed by the sources cited; this is the range commonly stated.",ed:true,src:[],origin:"mixed"}]},
 {k:"t-sicily",h:"Sicily under Muslim rule and after",intro:"Sicily was conquered from Ifriqiya (Tunisia) by the Aghlabid dynasty and ruled by Muslim emirs until the Norman conquest. Under Norman kings, Arabic learning continued at court.",points:[
  {h:"Conquest from Ifriqiya",text:"The Aghlabid emir Ziyadat Allah, governing Ifriqiya (Tunisia) for the Baghdad caliph, intervened in Sicily; Palermo fell in 831.",figure:"",year:"831",disputed:false,note:"French-language source; quotes in French.",src:[SRCQ("N. Hautemaniere, La Sicile islamique - Les cles du Moyen-Orient","https://www.lesclesdumoyenorient.com/La-Sicile-islamique.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","l’émir Ziâdat-Allâh, membre de la dynastie aghlabide gouvernant la province d’Ifriqiya au nom du calife de Bagdad"),SRCQ("N. Hautemaniere, La Sicile islamique - Les cles du Moyen-Orient","https://www.lesclesdumoyenorient.com/La-Sicile-islamique.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","De la prise de Palerme en 831 à la conquête de Taormine")],origin:"North Africa"},
  {h:"End of Muslim rule",text:"Norman leaders Robert Guiscard and Roger de Hauteville took Sicily in stages from 1061; this source dates the end of the conquest to 1071.",figure:"",year:"1071",disputed:true,note:"The commonly cited end date of 1091 (fall of Noto) is not confirmed by the sources cited; this source gives 1071 (Palermo fell 1072).",src:[SRCQ("N. Hautemaniere, La Sicile islamique - Les cles du Moyen-Orient","https://www.lesclesdumoyenorient.com/La-Sicile-islamique.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Dès 1071, Robert Guiscard et Roger de Hauteville achevèrent la conquête d’une Sicile politiquement éclatée")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Al-Idrisi and the Tabula Rogeriana",text:"Al-Idrisi, probably born in Ceuta (North Africa) in 1100, joined the court of the Norman king Roger II around 1138 and produced a world geography and map, completed in 1153/1154.",figure:"",year:"1154",disputed:true,note:"Date is given as 1154 in many sources and 1153 by Factum; Les clés du Moyen-Orient gives 1154. Al-Idrisi was a Moroccan-Andalusi Arab of Idrisid descent.",src:[SRCQ("al-Idrisi and Roger II: Mapping the World in the 12th Century - Factum Foundation","https://factumfoundation.org/our-projects/digital-restorations/re-creating-the-lost-map-of-al-idrisi/al-idrisi-and-roger-ii-mapping-the-world-in-the-twelfth-century/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was probably born in Ceuta in 1100"),SRCQ("al-Idrisi and Roger II: Mapping the World in the 12th Century - Factum Foundation","https://factumfoundation.org/our-projects/digital-restorations/re-creating-the-lost-map-of-al-idrisi/al-idrisi-and-roger-ii-mapping-the-world-in-the-twelfth-century/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it is often known as the Tabula Rogeriana or Book of Roger; it was completed in AH 548/1153 CE")],origin:"North Africa"}]},
 {k:"t-medicine",h:"Medicine",intro:"Europe's first large body of medical theory in Latin came from a North African translator working in southern Italy. The texts he translated were mostly by Persian and other eastern authors.",points:[
  {h:"Constantine the African",text:"Constantine left his North African homeland, reached Salerno, then joined Monte Cassino (about 1077), translating more than two dozen medical texts from Arabic into Latin.",figure:"",year:"1077",disputed:false,note:"Usually said to come from Ifriqiya (Kairouan/Carthage area); exact birthplace is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Medicine at Monte Cassino: Constantine the African and the oldest manuscript of his Pantegni (Kwakkel and Newton, Brepols 2019) - Garrison-Morton-Norman","https://historyofmedicine.com/id/15265","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Fleeing his North-African homeland for Italy, Constantine the African arrived in Salerno and then joined the abbey of Monte Cassino"),SRCQ("Medicine at Monte Cassino: Constantine the African and the oldest manuscript of his Pantegni (Kwakkel and Newton, Brepols 2019) - Garrison-Morton-Norman","https://historyofmedicine.com/id/15265","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","He dedicated his life to the translation of more than two dozen medical texts from Arabic into Latin.")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"The Pantegni: Persian source, African translator",text:"His main work, the Pantegni, adapted the Complete Book of the Medical Art by the Persian physician Ali ibn al-Abbas al-Majusi.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"A model case for this hub: the translator was African, the source text Persian.",src:[SRCQ("Medicine at Monte Cassino: Constantine the African and the oldest manuscript of his Pantegni (Kwakkel and Newton, Brepols 2019) - Garrison-Morton-Norman","https://historyofmedicine.com/id/15265","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","translated and adapted from the Complete Book of the Medical Art by the Persian physician")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Hiding the Arabic origin",text:"Constantine tried to disguise the Arabic origin of his texts.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"One reason the African and Islamic route was later under-recognised in Europe.",src:[SRCQ("Influence of Arabic and Islamic Philosophy on the Latin West - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arabic-islamic-influence/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","tried to disguise the Arabic origin of his texts")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Salerno school",text:"Salerno was the leading medical school of medieval Europe, and Constantine's translations fed its teaching.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The direct link between Constantine's translations and the Salerno curriculum is widely stated but not confirmed in detail by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Medicine at Monte Cassino: Constantine the African and the oldest manuscript of his Pantegni (Kwakkel and Newton, Brepols 2019) - Garrison-Morton-Norman","https://historyofmedicine.com/id/15265","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","These great efforts produced the first substantial written body of medical theory and practice in medieval Europe.")],origin:"mixed"}]},
 {k:"t-seen",h:"Africa as Europe saw it before colonisation",intro:"Before the colonial era, Europeans knew Africa through gold, diplomacy, religion and individual Africans who lived and worked in Europe. These images were often respectful, though Europe also held Africans in slavery throughout this period.",points:[
  {h:"The Catalan Atlas (1375) and Mansa Musa",text:"The Catalan Atlas, made in Majorca about 1375 and now in the Bibliotheque nationale de France, shows Mansa Musa of Mali holding gold.",figure:"",year:"1375",disputed:false,note:"BnF holding: the BnF attribution is not confirmed by the sources cited. Attributed to Abraham Cresques.",src:[SRCQ("New Exhibition Highlights Story of the Richest Man Who Ever Lived - Smithsonian Magazine","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/richest-man-who-ever-lived-180971409/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","One of these items is a reproduction of the Catalan Atlas, produced on the island of Majorca around 1375,"),SRCQ("The Catalan Atlas - Carleton College Library","https://www.carleton.edu/library-exhibitions/facsimilies/catalan-atlas/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","to explore the extent of European knowledge of the world and the representation of Africa (especially king Mansa Musa")],origin:"sub-Saharan"},
  {h:"Ethiopian monks at Florence (1441)",text:"Ethiopian monks from the Jerusalem community attended the Council of Florence in 1441 at the pope's invitation.",figure:"",year:"1441",disputed:false,note:"Other Ethiopian embassies to Europe (e.g. to Aragon, Venice, Lisbon) are not confirmed by the sources cited here.",src:[SRCQ("Ethiopia - Contact with European Christendom (Library of Congress Country Studies)","https://countrystudies.us/ethiopia/12.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Ethiopian monks from the Jerusalem community attended the Council of Florence in 1441 at the invitation of the pope")],origin:"Ethiopia"},
  {h:"Black St Maurice, Magdeburg (c.1240)",text:"The statue of St Maurice in Magdeburg Cathedral, about 1240, is the earliest known depiction of the saint as a Black African, shown as a dignified knight.",figure:"",year:"1240",disputed:false,note:"Maurice was said to be an Egyptian (Theban) soldier; depicting him as Black was a 13th-century European choice, not evidence of his appearance.",src:[SRCQ("J. Bowersox, St. Maurice in Magdeburg (ca. 1240) - Black Central Europe","https://blackcentraleurope.com/sources/medieval/st-maurice-in-magdeburg-ca-1240/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","In this statue, the earliest depiction of St. Maurice as a black man"),SRCQ("Statue of St. Maurice - Art and Architecture of the Middle Ages (Caskey, Cohen, Safran)","https://artofthemiddleages.com/s/main/item/1546","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The cathedral's sculpture of St. Maurice is the earliest representation of the martyr as a black African.")],origin:"sub-Saharan"},
  {h:"Juan Latino in Granada",text:"Juan Latino (c.1517-c.1594), born to parents from Guinea and once enslaved, became a Latin professor at Granada and published Latin poetry in 1573 and 1576.",figure:"",year:"1573",disputed:true,note:"Often called \"African-born\"; Cambridge University Library says he was most likely born in Spain. Use \"of African descent\".",src:[SRCQ("Juan Latino, 16th-century Afro-Spaniard freed slave, poet and Latin professor - Cambridge University Library, Languages across Borders","https://languagecollections-blog.lib.cam.ac.uk/2020/07/10/juan-latino-16th-century-afro-spaniard-freed-slave-poet-and-latin-professor","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was born most likely in Baena (southern Spain), descendent of Guinea born parents"),SRCQ("Juan Latino, 16th-century Afro-Spaniard freed slave, poet and Latin professor - Cambridge University Library, Languages across Borders","https://languagecollections-blog.lib.cam.ac.uk/2020/07/10/juan-latino-16th-century-afro-spaniard-freed-slave-poet-and-latin-professor","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","He was the first Afro-European to write in Latin and thus, have a literary career.")],origin:"sub-Saharan"},
  {h:"Abba Gregorius and Hiob Ludolf",text:"The Ethiopian monk Abba Gregorius (Gorgoryos) worked with the German scholar Hiob Ludolf; in 1652 he was invited to Gotha. Ludolf's Ge'ez-Latin dictionary owed much to him.",figure:"",year:"1652",disputed:false,note:"Founding of Ethiopian Studies in Europe was a collaboration with an Ethiopian scholar. German quote: \"At his request the prince invited Gregorius to Gotha in 1652.\"",src:[SRCQ("Ethiopia and Ethiopian Studies - Thomas Fisher Rare Book Library, University of Toronto","https://fisherdigitus.library.utoronto.ca/exhibits/show/blackhistory/earlyprintandmanuscripts/ethiopia","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","his Ge‘ez-Latin dictionary, for example, owed much to the learning of the Ethiopian monk Abba Gorgoryos"),SRCQ("Ludolf, Hiob - Deutsche Biographie (NDB/ADB)","https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/pnd118817167.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Auf seinen Wunsch hin lud der Fürst 1652 Gregorius nach Gotha ein")],origin:"Ethiopia"},
  {h:"Africans in Renaissance Europe",text:"The Walters Art Museum's 2012-13 exhibition used European art to reveal the presence of Africans in Renaissance society.",figure:"",year:"2012",disputed:false,note:"Exhibition ran 14 October 2012 to 21 January 2013 (Walters page).",src:[SRCQ("Revealing the African Presence in Renaissance Europe - The Walters Art Museum","https://thewalters.org/?p=839","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","explores the wealth of European art to reveal the hidden presence of Africans in Renaissance society")],origin:"mixed"}]},
 {k:"t-beyond",h:"Beyond Europe: the Red Sea, Indian Ocean and Sahara",intro:"African states traded and travelled east as well as north. Aksum linked Rome and India, East African city-states exchanged envoys with Ming China, West African gold fed the medieval economy, and Africans rose to power in India.",points:[
  {h:"Aksum and the Rome-India trade",text:"Aksum (Ethiopia/Eritrea) sought to control sea trade between the Roman Empire and India, and minted its own coins for about 300 years.",figure:"",year:"100",disputed:false,note:"The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea names King Zoskales as promoting commerce with Rome, Arabia and India.",src:[SRCQ("Ethiopia - The Aksumite State (Library of Congress Country Studies)","https://countrystudies.us/ethiopia/5.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Much of the impetus for foreign conquest lay in the desire to control the maritime trade between the Roman Empire and India"),SRCQ("Ethiopia - The Aksumite State (Library of Congress Country Studies)","https://countrystudies.us/ethiopia/5.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Even more remarkable and wholly unique for ancient Africa was the minting of coins over an approximately 300-year period")],origin:"Ethiopia"},
  {h:"Malindi's giraffe for the Ming court (1414)",text:"In 1414 Zheng He's fleet met envoys from Malindi (Kenya) in Bengal, who gave a giraffe that was taken to China; Malindi sent another the next year.",figure:"",year:"1414",disputed:false,note:"The first giraffe came via Bengal, not directly from Africa.",src:[SRCQ("The giraffes that sailed to medieval China - Science News","https://www.sciencenews.org/blog/wild-things/giraffes-sailed-medieval-china","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in 1414 they met envoys from the African coastal state of Malindi (now part of Kenya)")],origin:"sub-Saharan"},
  {h:"Zheng He on the East African coast (1417-19)",text:"On the fifth voyage (1417-19) the fleet reached the east coast of Africa, returning ambassadors from Mogadishu, Brawa and Malindi.",figure:"",year:"1418",disputed:false,note:"The brief's \"1418\" falls within this voyage.",src:[SRCQ("History 104, Lecture 02: Zheng He's Treasure Ship Expeditions - University of Delaware","https://www1.udel.edu/History-old/figal/Hist104/text/02LecOutline.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The fleet explored the Arabian coast from Hormuz to Aden and the east coast of Africa, returning ambassadors from Mogadishu, Brawa, and Malinda")],origin:"sub-Saharan"},
  {h:"West African gold",text:"West African gold made the region indispensable to the medieval world; Mansa Musa of Mali is remembered as one of the wealthiest people in history.",figure:"",year:"1324",disputed:true,note:"\"Richest person ever\" rankings are not measurable; present as reputation. Exhibition: Caravans of Gold, Fragments in Time, Block Museum 2019.",src:[SRCQ("New Exhibition Highlights Story of the Richest Man Who Ever Lived - Smithsonian Magazine","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/richest-man-who-ever-lived-180971409/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","And gold was what made West Africa indispensable to the rest of the world during the Middle Ages."),SRCQ("Medieval Africa as a cultural force is subject of major exhibition at Block Museum - Block Museum, Northwestern University","https://nublockmuseum.blog/2019/01/09/medieval-africa-as-a-cultural-force-is-subject-of-major-exhibition-at-block-museum/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is the first major exhibition to highlight West Africa's global reach in the medieval period of the 8th to 16th centuries")],origin:"sub-Saharan"},
  {h:"Habshi and Sidi in India; Malik Ambar",text:"Africans, many from Ethiopia, reached South Asia from as early as the 7th century as merchants, sailors and enslaved people. Malik Ambar (1548-1626), born in Ethiopia, rose to rule in the Deccan.",figure:"",year:"1600",disputed:false,note:"Siddi communities in India today descend from these and later East African arrivals.",src:[SRCQ("C. A. Stewart, Habshi and Sidi: Africans in the Deccan - The Met","https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/habshi-and-sidi","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The integration of Africans into the subcontinent took place as early as the seventh century"),SRCQ("C. A. Stewart, Habshi and Sidi: Africans in the Deccan - The Met","https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/habshi-and-sidi","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Malik 'Ambar (1548–1626), who was born Chapu, in Ethiopia.")],origin:"Ethiopia"}]},
 {k:"t-myths",h:"Where claims go too far",intro:"This history is often told in ways that either erase Africa or claim too much for it. These notes set out the strongest objections and what the sources actually support.",points:[
  {h:"\"The Moors were all Black Africans\"",text:"\"Moor\" was never a single ethnicity. It first meant people of Roman Mauretania (North-west Africa), later Muslims in Europe, and from the Renaissance any dark-skinned person. Al-Andalus's Muslims were mainly Berbers, Arabs and Iberian converts, with some sub-Saharan Africans.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Both erasure (treating Moors as non-African) and overclaim (treating them all as Black) are wrong. The proportion of sub-Saharan Africans in al-Andalus is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Who were the Moors? It's complicated - National Geographic","https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/who-were-moors","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it does not actually describe a specific ethnicity or race"),SRCQ("Who were the Moors? It's complicated - National Geographic","https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/who-were-moors","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Beginning in the Renaissance, “Moor” and “blackamoor” were also used to describe any person with dark skin.")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Library sizes",text:"Figures like 400,000 books for Cordoba are repeated in popular writing as fact, sometimes with comparisons to small Christian libraries. Specialist scholarship calls the number likely hyperbole.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Say \"sources claim\" and give the scholarly caveat.",src:[SRCQ("The ornament of the world - The Peninsula (Qatar), 2013","https://thepeninsulaqatar.com/article/19/09/2013/the-ornament-of-the-world","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The caliph’s library (one of 70 in the town) contained 400,000 volumes"),SRCQ("U. Bongianino, The page architecture of a deluxe Arabic dictionary from Islamic Spain - Manuscript and Text Cultures (peer-reviewed)","https://mtc-journal.org/index.php/mtc/article/download/27/61?inline=1","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","While the number 400,000 is likely a hyperbole")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"\"The Arabs invented zero\"",text:"Rules for calculating with zero were set out by the Indian mathematician Brahmagupta in the 7th century. The word \"zero\" comes, through Arabic sifr, from the transmitting culture, not the inventing one.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("A history of Zero - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Zero/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Brahmagupta attempted to give the rules for arithmetic involving zero and negative numbers in the seventh century"),SRCQ("A history of Zero - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Zero/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Our name \"zero\" derives ultimately from the Arabic sifr")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Crediting Africa with Persian or Indian knowledge",text:"Much \"African\" learning in medieval Europe was African in route, not in origin: Indian numerals, Baghdad algebra, Persian medicine. Credit North Africa for transmitting, teaching and extending it (Fibonacci's teachers, Ibn al-Banna, Constantine), not for inventing all of it.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Medicine at Monte Cassino: Constantine the African and the oldest manuscript of his Pantegni (Kwakkel and Newton, Brepols 2019) - Garrison-Morton-Norman","https://historyofmedicine.com/id/15265","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","translated and adapted from the Complete Book of the Medical Art by the Persian physician"),SRCQ("A history of Zero - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Zero/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The brilliant work of the Indian mathematicians was transmitted to the Islamic and Arabic mathematicians further west")],origin:"wider Islamic world via North Africa"},
  {h:"Calling famous figures \"Black\" without evidence",text:"Septimius Severus's skin colour cannot be verified; Juan Latino was probably born in Spain; Ziryab's origin is debated. Use place of birth and documented ancestry rather than assumed appearance.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Scotland's African Emperor - Historic Environment Scotland Blog","https://blog.historicenvironment.scot/2017/10/scotlands-african-emperor/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","We can’t verify the exact tone of his skin")],origin:"mixed"},
  {h:"Single heroic \"firsts\"",text:"Claims such as \"first aviator\" (Ibn Firnas), \"first algebraic notation\" (al-Qalasadi) or \"first university\" (al-Qarawiyyin) rest on thin, late or definition-dependent evidence.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ibn al-Banna - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Banna/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","neither al-Banna nor al-Qalasadi were the inventors of mathematical notation"),SRCQ("Flights of Fancy on Manmade Wings - AramcoWorld, Nov/Dec 2008","https://archive.aramcoworld.com/issue/200806/flights.of.fancy.on.manmade.wings.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is all that the scant historical descriptions tell us")],origin:"mixed"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Writing, mathematics, medicine and monumental building were developed in Africa thousands of years ago, and the evidence is in documents, objects and buildings that survive.",refs:["jy-nile-egyptian-writing", "jy-nile-rhind-mathematical-papyrus", "jy-nile-edwin-smith-surgical-papyrus", "jy-south-great-zimbabwe"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Avoid the claims that do not hold, such as the Dogon and Sirius B or 'Africans invented algebra': they weaken a case the evidence already makes.",refs:["jy-myths-the-dogon-knew-about-sirius-b-unproven-p", "jy-myths-africans-invented-algebra-false-as-state"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"When you tell this history, say where knowledge came from and how it travelled: much reached Europe through North Africa and al-Andalus from India, Persia and Baghdad, as well as from Africa itself.",refs:["jy-t-numbers-fibonacci-in-bejaia-bugia-algeria", "jy-t-numbers-indian-origin-of-the-numerals", "jy-t-andalus-toledo-translations"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Teaching and exhibitions can show African achievement alongside, not below, other civilisations: the timeline on the History page sets them side by side.",refs:["jy-myths-europeans-brought-civilisation-to-africa", "jy-myths-africa-was-more-advanced-than-europe-par"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Label evidence by type and strength: much early African history rests on archaeology and dating rather than written records, and some popular figures cannot be measured.",refs:["jy-early-ishango-bone-the-cautious-reading", "jy-west-how-many-manuscripts", "jy-myths-mansa-musa-was-the-richest-person-ever-u"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Africa has some of the oldest evidence of symbolic thought, at Blombos Cave in South Africa.",refs:["jy-early-blombos-cave-engraved-ochre"]},{t:"Egypt developed writing, mathematics and surgery thousands of years ago.",refs:["jy-nile-egyptian-writing", "jy-nile-rhind-mathematical-papyrus", "jy-nile-edwin-smith-surgical-papyrus"]},{t:"West and southern Africa built cities, states and centres of learning, such as Djenné-Djenno, Timbuktu and Great Zimbabwe.",refs:["jy-west-djenn-djenno-a-city-before-islam", "jy-west-timbuktu-as-a-centre-of-learning", "jy-south-great-zimbabwe"]},{t:"Knowledge reached Europe through North Africa and al-Andalus, often from India and Baghdad as well as from Africa.",refs:["jy-t-numbers-fibonacci-in-bejaia-bugia-algeria", "jy-t-andalus-toledo-translations"]},{t:"Some popular claims go too far; this page says which.",refs:["jy-myths-the-dogon-knew-about-sirius-b-unproven-p"]}]}],time:[{ys:-98000,y:"c. 100,000 years ago",t:"Ochre-processing workshop at Blombos Cave, South Africa",src:[SRCQ("AAAS (2011) Science: Early ochre workshop represents critical point in human evolution","https://www.aaas.org/news/science-early-ochre-workshop-represents-critical-point-human-evolution","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","researchers have discovered a 100,000-year-old workshop")]},{ys:-73000,y:"c. 75,000 years ago",t:"Engraved ochre at Blombos Cave",src:[SRCQ("Henshilwood, d'Errico and Watts (2009) Engraved ochres from the Middle Stone Age levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa. Journal of Human Evolution 57(1) - abstract","https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/kip_articles/1784","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","two deliberately engraved ochre pieces from c. 75,000 year old l")]},{ys:-41000,y:"c. 43,000 to 42,000 years ago",t:"Lebombo bone, Border Cave",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Lebombo bone (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebombo_bone","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","is between 42,000 and 43,000 years old")]},{ys:-20000,y:"c. 25,000 to 16,000 years ago",t:"Ishango bone, DR Congo",src:[SRCQ("ICOMOS-IAU Thematic Study on Astronomical Heritage, Case Study 1.3: The Ishango Bone, Democratic Republic of the Congo","https://web.astronomicalheritage.org/images/astronomicalheritage.org/thematic-study/ch01cs3.pdf","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","places the bone between c. 25,000 BP and 16,000 BP.")]},{ys:-9400,y:"before 9400 BC",t:"Pottery at Ounjougou, Mali",src:[SRCQ("Huysecom et al. (2009) The emergence of pottery in Africa during the tenth millennium cal BC: new evidence from Ounjougou (Mali). Antiquity 83 - abstract, University of Geneva","https://archive-ouverte.unige.ch/unige:12635","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","dates from before 9400 cal BC")]},{ys:-4500,y:"c. 4500 to 4000 BC",t:"Nabta Playa megaliths",src:[SRCQ("University of Colorado Boulder (1998) Oldest astronomical megalith alignment discovered in southern Egypt by science team","https://www.colorado.edu/today/1998/03/30/oldest-astronomical-megalith-alignment-discovered-southern-egypt-science-team","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","date from about 6,500 years to 6,000 years ago")]},{ys:-4100,y:"c. 4100 BC",t:"Europe comparison: Neolithic begins in Britain and Ireland",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Neolithic British Isles (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neolithic_British_Isles","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","British, Irish and Manx history c. 4100–2500 BC")]},{ys:-3500,y:"3500 to 3000 BC",t:"Sorghum cultivation and domestication, eastern Sudan",src:[SRCQ("Sci.News (2017) Earliest evidence of domesticated sorghum discovered (report on UCL-led research)","https://www.sci.news/archaeology/earliest-evidence-domesticated-sorghum-05271.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","dating from 3500 to 3000 BC")]},{ys:-3250,y:"c. 3250 BC",t:"Writing in Egypt",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: History of writing (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_writing","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in Egypt c. 3250 BC")]},{ys:-3000,y:"c. 3000 BC",t:"Europe comparison: first Stonehenge earthwork",src:[SRCQ("English Heritage: History of Stonehenge","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/stonehenge/history-and-stories/history/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","built about 3000 BC")]},{ys:-2575,y:"c. 2575 to 2467 BC",t:"Giza pyramids (4th Dynasty)",src:[SRCQ("NBC News / Associated Press (2010): Egypt discovers workers' tombs near pyramids","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna34794254","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Egypt's 4th Dynasty (2575 B.C. to 2467 B.C.) when the great pyramids were built")]},{ys:-2510,y:"c. 2510 BC",t:"365-day civil calendar probably in use",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Egyptian calendar (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_calendar","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","probable evidence of its use early in the reign of Shepseskaf ( c. 2510 BC")]},{ys:-2500,y:"c. 2500 BC",t:"Kerma culture develops in Nubia",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Kerma culture (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerma_culture","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The culture developed from around 2500 BC")]},{ys:-2499,y:"c. 2500 BC",t:"Europe comparison: Stonehenge stone circle",src:[SRCQ("English Heritage: History of Stonehenge","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/stonehenge/history-and-stories/history/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","erected in the late Neolithic period about 2500 BC")]},{ys:-1650,y:"c. 1650 BC",t:"Rhind Mathematical Papyrus copied",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Rhind papyrus","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Rhind-papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the scribe who copied it about 1650 bc")]},{ys:-1600,y:"c. 17th century BC",t:"Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus written",src:[SRCQ("US National Library of Medicine Technical Bulletin (2010): An ancient medical treasure at your fingertips (Edwin Smith Surgical Papyrus)","https://www.nlm.nih.gov/pubs/techbull/ma10/ma10_hmd_reprint_papyrus.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","written in Egyptian hieratic script around the 17 th century BCE")]},{ys:-1550,y:"c. 1550 BC",t:"Ebers medical papyrus",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Ebers Papyrus (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebers_Papyrus","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","dating to c. 1550 BCE")]},{ys:-1450,y:"c. 1450 BC",t:"Europe comparison: Linear B, earliest deciphered writing in Europe",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Linear B (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_B","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the earliest known examples dating to around 1450 BC")]},{ys:-1200,y:"c. 1200 BC",t:"Europe comparison: Iron Age in the Aegean",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Iron Age Europe (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Age_Europe","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Aegean (1200–700 BC)")]},{ys:-814,y:"9th century BC",t:"Phoenician settlers found Carthage (Tunisia)",src:[SRCQ("Archaeological Site of Carthage - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/37","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Carthage was founded in the 9th century B.C. on the Gulf of Tunis.")]},{ys:-800,y:"c. 800 BC",t:"Europe comparison: Iron Age in Britain",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Iron Age Europe (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_Age_Europe","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Great Britain (800 BC – 100 AD)")]},{ys:-744,y:"c. 744 BC",t:"Kushite 25th Dynasty rules Egypt",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twenty-fifth_Dynasty_of_Egypt","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The 25th dynasty was a line of pharaohs who originated in the Kingdom of Kush")]},{ys:-500,y:"c. 500 BC",t:"Iron smelting at Taruga (Nok), Nigeria",src:[SRCQ("Rupp, Breunig and Kahlheber, Exploring the Nok enigma. Antiquity Project Gallery (Goethe University Frankfurt project)","https://www.antiquity.ac.uk/projgall/kahlheber316/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","earliest evidence of iron smelting in West Africa around 500 BC")]},{ys:-440,y:"c.440 BC",t:"Herodotus says Greek gods' names came from Egypt",src:[SRCQ("Herodotus, Histories 2.50 (trans. A. D. Godley) - Perseids / Perseus Digital Library text","https://fgh.perseids.org/read/greekLit/tlg0016/tlg001/perseus-eng2/2.50.1-2.50.3","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","I only say what the Egyptians themselves say")]},{ys:-300,y:"c. 300 BC",t:"Meroitic script in use",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Meroitic script (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meroitic_script","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Period 300 BC – 600 AD")]},{ys:-300,y:"c.300 BC",t:"Euclid writes the Elements in Alexandria",src:[SRCQ("Euclid of Alexandria - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Euclid/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Euclid of Alexandria is the most prominent mathematician of antiquity")]},{ys:-250,y:"c. 250 BC",t:"Djenné-Djenno settled",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Old Towns of Djenné","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Inhabited since 250 B.C.,")]},{ys:-146,y:"146 BC",t:"Rome destroys Carthage",src:[SRCQ("Western North Africa, 1000 B.C.-1 A.D. - Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History, The Met","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/ht/04/afw.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","When it surrenders in 146 B.C., the Romans wreak merciless destruction, leveling the once proud city")]},{ys:127,y:"AD 127-141",t:"Ptolemy observes from Alexandria",src:[SRCQ("Claudius Ptolemy - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Ptolemy/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","He made astronomical observations from Alexandria in Egypt during the years AD 127-41")]},{ys:193,y:"193",t:"Septimius Severus, born in Libya, becomes emperor",src:[SRCQ("Septimius Severus - English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/hadrians-wall/hadrians-wall-history-and-stories/septimius-severus/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","became Roman emperor in AD 193 after a ruthless campaign against his rivals")]},{ys:211,y:"211",t:"Severus dies at York",src:[SRCQ("Septimius Severus - English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/hadrians-wall/hadrians-wall-history-and-stories/septimius-severus/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Severus died at York on 4 February AD 211")]},{ys:270,y:"c. AD 270",t:"Aksum issues coins",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Aksumite currency (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aksumite_currency","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","from the reign of King Endybis around AD 270")]},{ys:330,y:"4th century AD",t:"Aksum adopts Christianity",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Aksum","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The introduction of Christianity in the 4th century AD")]},{ys:354,y:"354",t:"Augustine born at Thagaste (Algeria)",src:[SRCQ("Saint Augustine - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/augustine/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Augustine (Aurelius Augustinus) lived from 13 November 354 to 28 August 430")]},{ys:700,y:"c. AD 700 to 1400",t:"Benin earthworks built",src:[SRCQ("Guinness World Records: Longest earthworks of the pre-mechanical era","https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/97959-longest-earthworks-of-the-pre-mechanical-era","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","they were built from about 700 AD to 1400 AD.")]},{ys:711,y:"711",t:"Tariq ibn Ziyad leads North African forces into Iberia",src:[SRCQ("Who were the Moors? It's complicated - National Geographic","https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/who-were-moors","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","led by the Berber general, Tariq ibn-Ziyad, captured the Iberian Peninsula")]},{ys:820,y:"c.820",t:"Al-Khwarizmi writes on algebra in Baghdad",src:[SRCQ("Al-Khwarizmi - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Khwarizmi/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","His algebra treatise Hisab al-jabr w'al-muqabala gives us the word algebra")]},{ys:831,y:"831",t:"Palermo taken by Aghlabid forces from Ifriqiya",src:[SRCQ("N. Hautemaniere, La Sicile islamique - Les cles du Moyen-Orient","https://www.lesclesdumoyenorient.com/La-Sicile-islamique.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","De la prise de Palerme en 831")]},{ys:859,y:"859",t:"Al-Qarawiyyin founded in Fez",src:[SRCQ("Oldest higher-learning institution - Guinness World Records","https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/oldest-university","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","founded in 859 AD in Fez, Morocco.")]},{ys:929,y:"929",t:"Cordoba becomes seat of an independent caliphate",src:[SRCQ("Historic Centre of Cordoba - UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/313","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","In 929, Abderraman III established it as the headquarters of the independent Caliphate.")]},{ys:970,y:"970",t:"Al-Azhar founded in Cairo by the Fatimids",src:[SRCQ("Al-Azhar University - Muslim Heritage","https://muslimheritage.com/al-azhar-university/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","It was founded by the Fatimids in 970 CE")]},{ys:976,y:"976",t:"Numerals appear in the Codex Vigilanus, Spain",src:[SRCQ("The Arabic numeral system - MacTutor, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Arabic_numerals/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The numerals appear in the Codex Vigilanus copied by a monk in Spain in 976")]},{ys:1000,y:"11th to 14th centuries",t:"Kilwa mints its own coins",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Kilwa Kisiwani minted its own currency in the 11th to 14th centuries.")]},{ys:1077,y:"c.1077",t:"Constantine the African joins Monte Cassino",src:[SRCQ("Medicine at Monte Cassino: Constantine the African and the oldest manuscript of his Pantegni (Kwakkel and Newton, Brepols 2019) - Garrison-Morton-Norman","https://historyofmedicine.com/id/15265","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","joined the abbey of Monte Cassino south of Rome in c. 1077")]},{ys:1088,y:"1088",t:"Conventional founding date of Bologna",src:[SRCQ("Our history - University of Bologna","https://www.unibo.it/en/university/who-we-are/our-history","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","conventionally referred to as the year in which the Studium of Bologna was founded")]},{ys:1100,y:"c. 1100 to 1450",t:"Great Zimbabwe built",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Great Zimbabwe National Monument","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","built between 1100 and 1450 AD")]},{ys:1126,y:"1126",t:"Ibn Rushd born in Cordoba",src:[SRCQ("Ibn Rushd [Averroes] - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-rushd/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","was born in Cordova in 1126")]},{ys:1138,y:"1138",t:"Maimonides born in Cordoba",src:[SRCQ("Maimonides - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/maimonides/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Maimonides was born to a distinguished family in Cordova, Spain in 1138")]},{ys:1145,y:"1145",t:"Robert of Chester translates al-Khwarizmi's algebra",src:[SRCQ("Robert of Chester - Muslim Heritage","https://muslimheritage.com/people/scholars/robert-of-chester/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","translated in 1145")]},{ys:1153,y:"1153/1154",t:"Al-Idrisi completes the Book of Roger",src:[SRCQ("al-Idrisi and Roger II: Mapping the World in the 12th Century - Factum Foundation","https://factumfoundation.org/our-projects/digital-restorations/re-creating-the-lost-map-of-al-idrisi/al-idrisi-and-roger-ii-mapping-the-world-in-the-twelfth-century/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","it was completed in AH 548/1153 CE")]},{ys:1166,y:"1166",t:"Maimonides arrives in Egypt",src:[SRCQ("Maimonides - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/maimonides/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Maimonides arrived in Egypt in 1166 and eventually settled in Fustat")]},{ys:1200,y:"c. 1200 to 1500",t:"Ife sculpture flourishes",src:[SRCQ("The Met, Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History: Ife (from ca. 6th century)","https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/ife/hd_ife.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Between ca. 1200 and 1500, Ife developed into a flourishing artistic center.")]},{ys:1202,y:"1202",t:"Fibonacci's Liber Abaci",src:[SRCQ("Leonardo Pisano Fibonacci - MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fibonacci/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","published in 1202 after Fibonacci's return to Italy")]},{ys:1240,y:"c.1240",t:"Black St Maurice statue, Magdeburg",src:[SRCQ("J. Bowersox, St. Maurice in Magdeburg (ca. 1240) - Black Central Europe","https://blackcentraleurope.com/sources/medieval/st-maurice-in-magdeburg-ca-1240/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","This statue of St. Maurice in Magdeburg Cathedral dates from around 1240")]},{ys:1310,y:"c. 1310 to 1333",t:"Husuni Kubwa palace, Kilwa",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","the palace Husuni Kubwa built between c1310 and 1333")]},{ys:1324,y:"1324",t:"Mansa Musa's pilgrimage to Mecca",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Musa I of Mali","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Musa-I-of-Mali","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","his pilgrimage to Mecca (1324).")]},{ys:1375,y:"c.1375",t:"Catalan Atlas shows Mansa Musa",src:[SRCQ("New Exhibition Highlights Story of the Richest Man Who Ever Lived - Smithsonian Magazine","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/richest-man-who-ever-lived-180971409/","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","produced on the island of Majorca around 1375,")]},{ys:1403,y:"1403 to 1425",t:"Ming coin of Yongle reaches the Kenyan coast",src:[SRCQ("Field Museum press release (2013): Ancient Chinese coin found on Kenyan island by Field Museum expedition","https://www.fieldmuseum.org/about/press/ancient-chinese-coin-found-kenyan-island-field-museum-expedition","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","issued by Emperor Yongle who reigned from 1403-1425AD")]},{ys:1414,y:"1414",t:"Malindi envoys give a giraffe to Zheng He's fleet in Bengal",src:[SRCQ("The giraffes that sailed to medieval China - Science News","https://www.sciencenews.org/blog/wild-things/giraffes-sailed-medieval-china","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","in 1414 they met envoys from the African coastal state of Malindi")]},{ys:1417,y:"1417-19",t:"Zheng He's fleet on the East African coast",src:[SRCQ("History 104, Lecture 02: Zheng He's Treasure Ship Expeditions - University of Delaware","https://www1.udel.edu/History-old/figal/Hist104/text/02LecOutline.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","returning ambassadors from Mogadishu, Brawa, and Malinda")]},{ys:1441,y:"1441",t:"Ethiopian monks at the Council of Florence",src:[SRCQ("Ethiopia - Contact with European Christendom (Library of Congress Country Studies)","https://countrystudies.us/ethiopia/12.htm","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","attended the Council of Florence in 1441")]},{ys:1500,y:"from 16th century",t:"Benin brass castings",src:[SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","They were created from at least the 16th century onwards")]},{ys:1546,y:"1546",t:"Juan Latino graduates at Granada",src:[SRCQ("Juan Latino, 16th-century Afro-Spaniard freed slave, poet and Latin professor - Cambridge University Library, Languages across Borders","https://languagecollections-blog.lib.cam.ac.uk/2020/07/10/juan-latino-16th-century-afro-spaniard-freed-slave-poet-and-latin-professor","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Latino obtained a bachelor’s degree in arts in 1546")]},{ys:1591,y:"1591",t:"Songhai falls after the Battle of Tondibi",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Sankore Madrasah (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sankore_Madrasah","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","its decline following the Battle of Tondibi in 1591")]},{ys:1652,y:"1652",t:"Abba Gregorius invited to Gotha",src:[SRCQ("Ludolf, Hiob - Deutsche Biographie (NDB/ADB)","https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/pnd118817167.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","lud der Fürst 1652 Gregorius nach Gotha ein")]},{ys:1716,y:"1716",t:"Mather reports Onesimus's account of inoculation to the Royal Society",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Onesimus (Bostonian) (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onesimus_(Bostonian)","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Mather, in a 1716 letter to the Royal Society of London")]},{ys:1721,y:"1721",t:"Smallpox outbreak in Boston; inoculation promoted",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Onesimus (Bostonian) (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onesimus_(Bostonian)","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","After a smallpox outbreak began in Boston in 1721")]},{ys:1733,y:"1733",t:"Timbuktu astronomy text copied (Library of Congress exhibit)",src:[SRCQ("Library of Congress exhibition: Ancient Manuscripts from the Desert Libraries of Timbuktu - Exhibition items","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/mali/mali-exhibit.html","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","The Important Stars Among the Multitude of the Heavens), copied 1733")]},{ys:1833,y:"1830s",t:"Vai syllabary first documented",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Vai syllabary (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vai_syllabary","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","first documented in the 1830s")]},{ys:1897,y:"1897",t:"British forces loot Benin City",src:[SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Its shrines and associated compounds were looted by British forces")]},{ys:1964,y:"1964",t:"UNESCO launches the General History of Africa",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO: General History of Africa","https://www.unesco.org/en/general-history-africa","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","In 1964, as African nations gained independence")]},{ys:2013,y:"2012 to 2013",t:"Timbuktu manuscripts moved to safety in Bamako",src:[SRCQ("Wikipedia: Timbuktu Manuscripts (secondary source)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbuktu_Manuscripts","Supports the Africa's knowledge and its journeys page","Some 350,000 manuscripts were transported to safety")]}],dx:false};
LP["whatwaslost"]={name:"What was taken, lost and might have been",pfx:"wl",kpis:[{s:"scale",h:"Transatlantic trade: about 12.5 million embarked",n:"12.5 million",l:"people embarked on the transatlantic slave trade",ic:"route"},{s:"scale",h:"Four trades together: about 18 million",n:"about 18 million",l:"people taken across four slave trades",ic:"chart"},{s:"destruction",h:"British Museum holdings",n:"over 900",l:"objects from Benin City held by the British Museum",ic:"doc"},{s:"destruction",h:"Horniman Museum returns ownership",n:"72 objects",l:"returned to Nigeria by the Horniman Museum (2022)",ic:"hands"}],sections:[
 {k:"scale",h:"How many people were taken",intro:"The numbers come from shipping records and historical estimates. The transatlantic figures are the best documented. The other trades, deaths before embarkation and Africa’s “missing” population are less certain and are modelled or estimated.",points:[
  {h:"Transatlantic trade: about 12.5 million embarked",text:"Research based on the Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade Database (SlaveVoyages) estimates that about 12.5 million Africans were forced aboard slave ships over some 350 years.",figure:"12.5 million",year:"1525–1866",disputed:false,note:"These figures are taken from the National Endowment for the Humanities (a funder of the database) and from PBS, both citing the database.",src:[SRCQ("Gross Injustice (Humanities magazine, National Endowment for the Humanities, 2010)","https://www.neh.gov/humanities/2010/septemberoctober/feature/gross-injustice","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","12.5 million Africans forced aboard European and American slave ships."),SRCQ("How Many Slaves Landed in the U.S.? (PBS, The African Americans: Many Rivers to Cross)","https://www.pbs.org/wnet/african-americans-many-rivers-to-cross/history/how-many-slaves-landed-in-the-us/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","12.5 million Africans were shipped to the New World.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"About 10.7 million survived the crossing",text:"About 10.7 million people survived the Middle Passage and were landed in the Americas.",figure:"10.7 million",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Gross Injustice (Humanities magazine, National Endowment for the Humanities, 2010)","https://www.neh.gov/humanities/2010/septemberoctober/feature/gross-injustice","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","10.7 million survivors of the Middle Passage disembarked in the New World.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Deaths at sea",text:"The same source says that nearly 1.5 million Africans died because of conditions on the Middle Passage.",figure:"about 1.5 million",year:"",disputed:true,note:"12.5 million embarked minus 10.7 million landed gives about 1.8 million. The difference from 1.5 million is not explained by the source cited; treat both as estimates.",src:[SRCQ("Gross Injustice (Humanities magazine, National Endowment for the Humanities, 2010)","https://www.neh.gov/humanities/2010/septemberoctober/feature/gross-injustice","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","nearly 1.5 million Africans died as a result of the horrendous conditions of the Middle Passage")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Older totals have been revised",text:"Very different totals were once quoted. Decades of research have narrowed the range, so modern figures are more reliable than older claims of 20 or 100 million.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Gross Injustice (Humanities magazine, National Endowment for the Humanities, 2010)","https://www.neh.gov/humanities/2010/septemberoctober/feature/gross-injustice","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Wildly speculative sums once bandied about—3.5 million, 20 million, even 100 million—have been set aside")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Four trades together: about 18 million",text:"Nathan Nunn’s 2008 paper counts about 12 million people exported in the transatlantic trade and about 6 million in the trans-Saharan, Red Sea and Indian Ocean trades.",figure:"about 18 million (12 + 6)",year:"1400–1900",disputed:false,note:"Nunn’s figures are for people exported. They do not include people killed in raids or who died before reaching the coast or the desert routes.",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","During the trans-Atlantic slave trade alone, approximately 12 million slaves were exported from Africa."),SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Another 6 million were exported in the other three slave trades.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Deaths before embarkation",text:"Many people died before reaching the coast. Estimates of deaths during the march to the coast vary widely, from 10% to 50%.",figure:"10% to 50%",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The wide range shows how uncertain this figure is.",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Death rates during the trek to the coast are known with less certainty, but estimates range from 10% to 50%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Deaths at sea by period",text:"Nunn also reports that deaths during the Atlantic crossing ranged from 7% to 20%, depending on the period and the length of the voyage.",figure:"7% to 20%",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Estimates of cross-Atlantic mortality rates ranged from 7% to 20% depending on the time period and the length of the voyage")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Africa’s “missing” population (Manning)",text:"Patrick Manning modelled what Africa’s population might have been without the slave trades. Nunn reports Manning’s finding that, by 1850, Africa’s population was only half of what it would otherwise have been.",figure:"about 50% of the counterfactual population by 1850",year:"1850",disputed:true,note:"This is a modelled counterfactual estimate (Manning, Slavery and African Life, 1990, p. 171, as cited by Nunn), not a count. Manning’s other estimates and his method in detail are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","by 1850 Africa’s population was only half of what it would have been had the slave trades not taken place")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"How reliable are Manning’s models?",text:"A 2019 review by the economic historian Ewout Frankema finds that Manning’s models capture the likely negative effect of the transatlantic trade on Africa’s population well, but notes how hard it is to project African population back in time.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Frankema also points to the difficulty of projecting African population series back into earlier centuries.",src:[SRCQ("Ewout Frankema, Africa and the Demographic Consequences of the Columbian Exchange, Asian Review of World Histories 7 (2019)","https://www.ewoutfrankema.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankema_African-Demography-and-Columbian-Exchange_ARWH.2019.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","capture the potential negative consequences of the trans-Atlantic slave trade on African population development since 1500 well")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"economics",h:"What the economics research says",intro:"Since 2008, economists have used statistics to test whether the slave trades still affect Africa today. The research finds lasting harm. Critics accept much of this but question how far it can explain the whole of African history.",points:[
  {h:"The areas that exported most people are poorest today",text:"Nunn built country-by-country estimates of the number of people exported and found that the countries that lost the most people are poorer today.",figure:"",year:"2008",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa's Slave Trades (NBER Working Paper 13367, abstract)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w13367","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","I find a robust negative relationship between the number of slaves exported from a country and current economic performance.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Testing for cause, not just correlation",text:"Critics asked whether poorer areas were simply more likely to be raided (selection), or whether poverty caused the trade (reverse causality). Nunn tested this with historical evidence and with instrumental variables, such as sailing distances to slave markets.",figure:"",year:"2008",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa's Slave Trades (NBER Working Paper 13367, abstract)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w13367","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","I examine the historical evidence on selection into the slave trades, and use instrumental variables.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"More developed areas were hit hardest",text:"Nunn's evidence suggests that it was the more developed and more densely populated parts of Africa that were drawn most into the slave trades. Nunn reads this as support for the idea that the trades held back places that were advancing; it does not show what those places would otherwise have become.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"This is Nunn’s interpretation of historical population density data. It is relevant to the counterfactual but does not show what those areas would have achieved.",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it was actually the most developed areas of Africa that tended to select into the slave trades")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"State building interrupted",text:"Nunn cites historians who found that state systems in Senegambia were developing before the slave trades, and that this development stalled after the Portuguese arrived in the 15th century.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","this evolution stagnated soon after the arrival of the Portuguese in the 15th century")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Lower trust today",text:"Nunn and Wantchekon (2011) linked survey data to records of slave shipments by ethnic group. People whose ancestors were heavily raided are less trusting today.",figure:"",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nunn and Wantchekon, The Slave Trade and the Origins of Mistrust in Africa, AER 101(7), 2011 (abstract)","https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.101.7.3221","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","individuals whose ancestors were heavily raided during the slave trade are less trusting today.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Effects passed on through culture",text:"The same study finds that most of the effect works through norms, beliefs and values passed down within communities.",figure:"",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nunn and Wantchekon, The Slave Trade and the Origins of Mistrust in Africa, AER 101(7), 2011 (abstract)","https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.101.7.3221","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","most of the impact of the slave trade is through factors that are internal to the individual, such as cultural norms, beliefs, and values.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"More ethnic division",text:"Whatley and Gillezeau (2011) find that areas that exported more people are more ethnically divided today.",figure:"",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Whatley and Gillezeau, The Impact of the Transatlantic Slave Trade on Ethnic Stratification in Africa, AER 101(3), 2011 (abstract)","https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.101.3.571","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","we find an economically and statistically significant positive relationship between slave exports and ethnic heterogeneity.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Critique: “compression of history”",text:"The economic historian Gareth Austin argues that these long-run studies squash different periods and paths of history together and so oversimplify the causes.",figure:"",year:"2008",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Gareth Austin, The ‘reversal of fortune’ thesis and the compression of history, Journal of International Development 20(8), 2008 (abstract, RePEc)","https://ideas.repec.org/a/wly/jintdv/v20y2008i8p996-1027.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By compressing different historical periods and paths, the 'reversal' thesis over-simplifies the causation.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Critique: poverty came first, then was made worse",text:"Austin argues that low labour productivity in Africa was a starting condition for the external slave trades, while also accepting that the trades greatly reinforced African poverty.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Austin’s view supports harm from the slave trades but challenges the idea that Africa started from a position of overall economic advantage.",src:[SRCQ("Gareth Austin, The ‘reversal of fortune’ thesis and the compression of history, Journal of International Development 20(8), 2008 (abstract, RePEc)","https://ideas.repec.org/a/wly/jintdv/v20y2008i8p996-1027.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Relatively low labour productivity was a premise of the external slave trades"),SRCQ("Gareth Austin, The ‘reversal of fortune’ thesis and the compression of history, Journal of International Development 20(8), 2008 (abstract, RePEc)","https://ideas.repec.org/a/wly/jintdv/v20y2008i8p996-1027.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the latter greatly reinforced the relative poverty of many Sub-Saharan economies.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"What the economics can and cannot show",text:"These studies explain differences within Africa today. They do not measure what Africa as a whole would have achieved without the trades.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The research question is about part of present-day underdevelopment, not a full counterfactual history.",src:[SRCQ("Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa's Slave Trades (NBER Working Paper 13367, abstract)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w13367","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Can part of Africa's current underdevelopment be explained by its slave trades?")],kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"debate",h:"History and political economy",intro:"Historians and economists have long argued about how the slave trades and colonial rule shaped Africa and Europe. These are the main positions and the main replies to them.",points:[
  {h:"Rodney: Europe underdeveloped Africa",text:"Walter Rodney’s 1972 book argues that European exploitation actively underdeveloped Africa while enriching Europe.",figure:"",year:"1972",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Gerald L. Caplan, 'The Use of African History: How Europe Underdeveloped Africa by Walter Rodney' (review article, African Review, University of Dar es Salaam), quoting Rodney","https://journals.udsm.ac.tz/index.php/ar/article/download/545/526","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Africa helped to develop Western Europe in the same proportion as Western Europe helped to underdevelop Africa."),SRCQ("Rodney, Walter (International Encyclopedia of the Social Sciences, via Encyclopedia.com)","https://www.encyclopedia.com/social-sciences/applied-and-social-sciences-magazines/rodney-walter","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1972 he published his best-known work How Europe Underdeveloped Africa."),SRCQ("Walter Rodney papers – finding aid, Robert W. Woodruff Library of the Atlanta University Center","https://findingaids.auctr.edu/repositories/2/resources/9","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Walter Rodney is widely known for his seminal work, How Europe Underdeveloped Africa, first published in 1972."),SRCQ("How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/How_Europe_Underdeveloped_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Africa developed Europe at the same rate that Europe underdeveloped Africa.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Criticism of Rodney",text:"Some scholars have criticised Rodney for relying too heavily on the dependency theory of the 1960s, and for not looking enough at factors inside Africa that drove the slave trade.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"The source page flags parts of this criticism as poorly referenced. It is reported here as a position held by some historians. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Rodney, Walter (International Encyclopedia of the Social Sciences, via Encyclopedia.com)","https://www.encyclopedia.com/social-sciences/applied-and-social-sciences-magazines/rodney-walter","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Some scholars argue that Rodney relies too heavily on the dependency theory of the 1960s"),SRCQ("Rodney, Walter (International Encyclopedia of the Social Sciences, via Encyclopedia.com)","https://www.encyclopedia.com/social-sciences/applied-and-social-sciences-magazines/rodney-walter","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","has been criticized for not looking sufficiently at the internal factors in Africa that accounted for the slave trade"),SRCQ("Ana Lucia Araujo (2022) 'Did Rodney Get It Wrong? Europe Underdeveloped Africa but Enslaved People Were Not Always Purchased with Rubbish', African Economic History 50(2): 22–32 (author's PDF)","https://analuciaaraujo.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Araujo.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","While undervaluing Africans' agency, Rodney also tended to underestimate the importance of indigenous slavery"),SRCQ("Araujo (2022), African Economic History 50(2)","https://analuciaaraujo.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Araujo.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Rodney basically argued that the deportation of enslaved Africans to the Americas was a response to external influences."),SRCQ("How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/How_Europe_Underdeveloped_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","criticized his portrayal of Africans as powerless or easily-duped victims of the whites.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Williams: slavery helped pay for industrialisation",text:"Eric Williams's Capitalism and Slavery (1944) argued that wealth from slavery helped finance Britain's Industrial Revolution, and that economic self-interest, not humanitarianism, drove abolition.",figure:"",year:"1944",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Slavery and the British Industrial Revolution (UBS Center, University of Zurich)","https://www.ubscenter.uzh.ch/de/news_events/insights/2023-04-slavery_british_industrial_revolution.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Britain accumulated vast wealth from the triangular trade and used this wealth to finance its industrial revolution"),SRCQ("David Richardson, review of Kenneth Morgan, Slavery, Atlantic Trade and the British Economy (Institute of Historical Research, Reviews in History)","https://archives.history.ac.uk/ihr/Focus/Empire/reviews/richardson.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it was economic self interest, not humanitarianism, that drove nineteenth-century British antislavery."),SRCQ("Brandon 2017, International Review of Social History 62 (Cambridge), quoting Williams","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/9B01C9532A93D678129A5FD60B61829E/S0020859017000190a.pdf/from_williamss_thesis_to_williams_thesis_an_anticolonial_trajectory.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","one of the main streams of that accumulation of capital in England which financed the Industrial Revolution"),SRCQ("Capitalism and Slavery (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitalism_and_Slavery","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the high profits from slavery, he wrote, helped finance the Industrial Revolution .")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Reply: the profits were small",text:"The economic historian Stanley Engerman (1972) estimated that profits from the slave trade and West Indian plantations were less than 5% of the British economy in any year of the Industrial Revolution. This figure is reported here from a secondary source.",figure:"a small share",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Slavery and the British Industrial Revolution (UBS Center, University of Zurich), citing Engerman (1972) and others","https://www.ubscenter.uzh.ch/de/news_events/insights/2023-04-slavery_british_industrial_revolution.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","absolute levels of profit from the slave trade were small relative to the size of the British economy"),SRCQ("Heblich, Redding and Voth, Slavery and the British Industrial Revolution (CEPR VoxEU), citing Engerman (1972)","https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/slavery-and-british-industrial-revolution","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","profits from the slave trade were no higher than from other lines of business"),SRCQ("Eltis & Engerman 2000, Journal of Economic History 60(1)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/abs/importance-of-slavery-and-the-slave-trade-to-industrializing-britain/3E5AFD0ABB8FC4C63467800064158645","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it is apparent that sugar cultivation and the slave trade were not particularly large,"),SRCQ("Capitalism and Slavery (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitalism_and_Slavery","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the total profits from the slave trade and of West Indian plantations amounted to less than 5% of the British economy")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Reply: the decline thesis",text:"Many later historians have rejected Williams's claim that the Caribbean colonies were in economic decline before abolition, though the wider debate about Capitalism and Slavery continues.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Seymour Drescher, Econocide: British Slavery in the Era of Abolition (University of North Carolina Press)","https://uncpress.org/book/9780807871799/econocide","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","was detrimental to Britain's economic interests at a time when British slavery was actually at the height of its potential."),SRCQ("Seymour Drescher, Econocide (University of North Carolina Press)","https://uncpress.org/book/9780807871799/econocide","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","resulted not from the diminishing value of slavery for Great Britain but instead from the British public's mobilization against the slave trade."),SRCQ("Brandon 2017, International Review of Social History 62 (Cambridge)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/9B01C9532A93D678129A5FD60B61829E/S0020859017000190a.pdf/from_williamss_thesis_to_williams_thesis_an_anticolonial_trajectory.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The all-out attack of economic historians on Williams's decline thesis was launched, to great effect, by Seymour Drescher"),SRCQ("Capitalism and Slavery (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitalism_and_Slavery","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","More recent research has rejected this conclusion")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Inikori: Africans and England’s industrialisation",text:"Joseph Inikori (2002) argues that Africans, on the continent and in the diaspora, made a major contribution to England’s industrialisation through Atlantic trade.",figure:"",year:"2002",disputed:false,note:"“disaporic” is spelled as on the source page.",src:[SRCQ("Africans and the Industrial Revolution in England (Business History Explorer, Business Archives Council)","https://businesshistoryexplorer.businessarchivescouncil.org.uk/publications/13165","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","focuses on the contribution of Africans (continental and disaporic) to England's industrialization process from mid 17th to mid 19th century")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Thornton: African agency",text:"John Thornton argues that African states resisted early European raids and were not forced into the Atlantic trade by economic pressure. This complicates a story in which Africa was only acted upon.",figure:"",year:"1992",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("John Thornton, Africa and Africans in the Making of the Atlantic World, 1400-1800, ch. 4 summary (Cambridge University Press)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/africa-and-africans-in-the-making-of-the-atlantic-world-14001800/process-of-enslavement-and-the-slave-trade/777DA26E18B1DD2682A29F900AFD8CAF","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","We have established so far that Africans were not under any direct commercial or economic pressure to deal in slaves."),SRCQ("John Thornton, Africa and Africans in the Making of the Atlantic World, 1400-1800, ch. 2 summary (Cambridge University Press)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/africa-and-africans-in-the-making-of-the-atlantic-world-14001800/development-of-commerce-between-europeans-and-africans/A90464F5DB35DEB7651FF8979BCB1085","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","There would be no dramatic European conquests in Africa."),SRCQ("J.C. Sharman (2023) 'Something New out of Africa: States Made Slaves, Slaves Made States', International Organization 77(3) (quoting Thornton)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/international-organization/article/something-new-out-of-africa-states-made-slaves-slaves-made-states/6E27318C9103B59F54C44EB8748333EB","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Europeans did not possess the military power to force Africans to participate in any type of trade"),SRCQ("J.C. Sharman (2023), International Organization 77(3) (quoting Thornton)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/international-organization/article/something-new-out-of-africa-states-made-slaves-slaves-made-states/6E27318C9103B59F54C44EB8748333EB","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Therefore all African trade in the Atlantic, including the slave trade, had to be voluntary."),SRCQ("John Thornton (historian) (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Thornton_(historian)","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","arguing controversially that African economic strength and power were sufficient to force Europeans to deal with them on their own terms.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Reversal of fortune",text:"Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson found that, among colonised countries, those that were relatively rich in 1500 are relatively poor today. They link this to the institutions that colonisers set up.",figure:"",year:"2002",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson, Reversal of Fortune (NBER Working Paper 8460, abstract; published QJE 2002)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w8460","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Among countries colonized by European powers during the past 500 years those that were relatively rich in 1500 are now relatively poor.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Institutions, not geography",text:"The same authors argue that colonial powers set up extractive institutions in places that were already prosperous, and that this caused the reversal.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson, Reversal of Fortune (NBER Working Paper 8460, abstract; published QJE 2002)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w8460","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the reversal is consistent with the role of institutions in economic development.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Diamond: geography",text:"Jared Diamond argues that differences in power and technology between societies come mainly from geography and environment. On this view, Africa's long north–south layout made it harder for crops and animals to spread.",figure:"",year:"1997",disputed:true,note:"Diamond explicitly rejects any idea of inherent superiority. His explanation is a counter-argument to the claim mainly because it places the causes much earlier than the slave trades.",src:[SRCQ("Guns, Germs, and Steel (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://britannica.com/topic/Guns-Germs-and-Steel","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","argued that geography can explain the inequality that exists between different societies"),SRCQ("Guns, Germs, and Steel (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://britannica.com/topic/Guns-Germs-and-Steel","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Africa and the Americas stretch far further north to south than they do east to west."),SRCQ("PBS: Guns, Germs and Steel, episode 3 transcript","https://www.pbs.org/gunsgermssteel/show/transcript3.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","I conclude that geography, and guns, germs and steel, have been the strongest forces to shape the history of our world."),SRCQ("Laitin, Moortgat and Robinson (2012) 'Geographic axes and the persistence of cultural diversity', PNAS 109(26): 10263 – abstract as reproduced by National Taiwan University","https://www.hisp.ntu.edu.tw/abstract_literature?id=520","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","is oriented more north-south than east-west"),SRCQ("Guns, Germs, and Steel (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guns,_Germs,_and_Steel","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Diamond argues that the gaps in power and technology between human societies originate primarily in environmental differences")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Critics of the geographic explanation",text:"Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson argue that their reversal-of-fortune evidence is hard to square with a purely geographic explanation, because places that were rich in 1500 did not stay rich. This is their argument, aimed at geographic explanations in general.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"This criticism is directed at geographic explanations in general, not only at Diamond.",src:[SRCQ("Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson, Reversal of Fortune (NBER Working Paper 8460, abstract; published QJE 2002)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w8460","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","This reversal is inconsistent with a view that links economic development to geographic factors.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"The Great Divergence: Europe’s lead came late",text:"Kenneth Pomeranz (2000) argues that living standards in the most advanced parts of Europe and East Asia were comparable as late as 1750. If he is right, Europe's global lead was recent. Whether it was inevitable is a counterfactual that this evidence cannot settle.",figure:"",year:"2000",disputed:false,note:"Pomeranz compares Europe with East Asia, not with Africa. His work is used here as a frame, not as evidence about Africa.",src:[SRCQ("The Great Divergence (Princeton University Press)","https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691217185/the-great-divergence","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","as recently as 1750, life expectancy, consumption, and product and factor markets were comparable in Europe and East Asia.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"The Great Divergence is debated",text:"Pomeranz’s argument is influential but contested. Some economic historians date the divergence earlier.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Martin Daunton, 'Why Did Europe's Economies Diverge from Asia?' (Gresham College lecture, 2022)","https://www.gresham.ac.uk/node/15339","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Historians agree it happened – not about when or why"),SRCQ("Martin Daunton (Gresham College lecture, 2022), on Stephen Broadberry and colleagues","https://www.gresham.ac.uk/node/15339","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","argue the divergence was much earlier"),SRCQ("Stephen Broadberry, 'Historical national accounting and dating the Great Divergence' (University of Oxford, Department of Economics)","https://www.economics.ox.ac.uk/node/2208241","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","argues for a return to the Pomeranz (2000) version of the Great Divergence, beginning only after 1800."),SRCQ("The Great Divergence (book) (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Divergence_(book)","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The book has been the subject of intense academic debate")],kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"darkages",h:"The “Dark Ages” and the medieval world",intro:"The claim compares Africa with Europe in the early and high middle ages. The phrase “Dark Ages” is itself contested. Comparisons between regions depend on what is measured: city size, literacy, metalworking, trade or the size of states. No single measure shows one region as “more advanced” overall.",points:[
  {h:"Historians rarely use the term",text:"Britannica notes that historians now rarely use the term “Dark Ages” because of the value judgement it carries.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Dark Ages (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Dark-Ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The term “Dark Ages” is now rarely used by historians because of the value judgment it implies.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Where the idea came from",text:"The idea of a European “Dark Age” came from Italian Renaissance humanists, who saw a thousand years of darkness between ancient Rome and their own time. Historians now rarely use the term.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Middle Ages (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Middle-Ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The term and its conventional meaning were introduced by Italian humanists with invidious intent."),SRCQ("Middle Ages (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Middle-Ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the notion of a thousand-year period of darkness and ignorance separating them from the ancient Greek and Roman world"),SRCQ("Dark Ages (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Dark-Ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The term “Dark Ages” is now rarely used by historians because of the value judgment it implies."),SRCQ("Europeana: The not-so-dark Middle Ages (blog)","https://europeana.eu/da/stories/the-not-so-dark-middle-ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Humanists, like Giorgio Vasari, believed that the period that had preceded them had been a dark intermediary time"),SRCQ("Naoíse Mac Sweeney, 'The Myth of the \"Dark Ages\" Ignores How Classical Traditions Flourished Around the World', Smithsonian Magazine (18 May 2023)","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/the-myth-of-the-dark-ages-ignores-how-classical-traditions-flourished-around-the-world-180982190/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","recent scholarship has largely dispelled the myth of a European medieval dark age"),SRCQ("Dark Ages (historiography) (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Ages_(historiography)","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Dark Ages is a term, now deprecated by most historians, for the Early Middle Ages")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Western Europe, about 500 to 1000",text:"In its narrower meaning, the term describes Western Europe from about 500 to 1000, a period of frequent warfare and much reduced town life.",figure:"",year:"c. 500–1000",disputed:false,note:"This describes Western Europe only. Other parts of Europe, such as the Byzantine Empire, are not covered by it.",src:[SRCQ("Dark Ages (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Dark-Ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the period between about 500 and 1000, which was marked by frequent warfare and a virtual disappearance of urban life.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The House of Wisdom, Baghdad",text:"Under the Abbasid caliphs, Baghdad’s House of Wisdom was a royal library. The caliph al-Ma’mun (813–833) made it a centre for translating and studying classical works. The Mongols largely destroyed Baghdad in 1258.",figure:"",year:"9th century",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Bayt al-Hikmah (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Bayt-al-Hikmah","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","royal library maintained by the Abbasid caliphs during their reign in Baghdad"),SRCQ("Hülegü (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/print/article/275219","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","and in 1258 he seized and largely destroyed Baghdad"),SRCQ("Berggren, Biographical Encyclopedia of Astronomers (Springer 2007): Ma'mun","https://islamsci.mcgill.ca/RASI/BEA/Ma%27mun_BEA.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","When he came to the throne as the seventh caliph of the ʿAbbāsid Empire in 813"),SRCQ("Smithsonian Magazine, 'How an Artist Is Rebuilding a Baghdad Library Destroyed During the Iraq War'","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/artist-hopes-rebuild-baghdad-library-destroyed-during-iraq-war-180969979/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a grand library possibly founded by the Abbasid caliph Al-Mansur in the 8th century."),SRCQ("Linda Komaroff, 'The Art of the Ilkhanid Period (1256–1353)', Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History, The Metropolitan Museum of Art (2001)","https://www.metmuseum.org/en/essays/the-art-of-the-ilkhanid-period-1256-1353","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","when Mongol forces subjugated all of Iran and by 1258 had also taken Baghdad,"),SRCQ("House of Wisdom (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Wisdom","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it was turned into a public academy and a library.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Aksum",text:"Aksum, in present-day Ethiopia and Eritrea, sat at the crossroads of Africa, Arabia and the Greco-Roman world. Its evidence is set out in full on the journeys page.",figure:"",year:"1st–8th centuries AD",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Aksum (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/15/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The kingdom was at the crossroads of the three continents: Africa, Arabia and the Greco-Roman World")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ghana Empire: the “Land of Gold”",text:"From the 8th century, trade caravans crossed the Sahara on routes later described by Arabic-language writers. The Ghana Empire in West Africa grew wealthy from gold and became known as the “Land of Gold”.",figure:"",year:"8th century",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Trans-Saharan Gold Trade (7th–14th Century), Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History (Metropolitan Museum of Art)","https://www.metmuseum.org/es/essays/the-trans-saharan-gold-trade-7th-14th-century","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","From the eighth century onward, annual trade caravans followed routes later described by Arabic authors"),SRCQ("The Trans-Saharan Gold Trade (Metropolitan Museum of Art)","https://www.metmuseum.org/es/essays/the-trans-saharan-gold-trade-7th-14th-century","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","referred to as the “Land of Gold.”"),SRCQ("The Trans-Saharan Gold Trade (Metropolitan Museum of Art)","https://www.metmuseum.org/es/essays/the-trans-saharan-gold-trade-7th-14th-century","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Leaders of the ancient kingdom of Ghana accumulated wealth by keeping the core of pure metal"),SRCQ("Ghana Empire (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana_Empire","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it was already regarded as a wealthy state")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mali Empire and Mansa Musa",text:"The Mali Empire (about 1235 to 1610) became the largest empire in West Africa. The Arab writer al-Umari, writing about twelve years later, recorded that the gold Mansa Musa's party spent in Egypt during his 1324 pilgrimage lowered the value of gold there, and that it had stayed low since.",figure:"",year:"1324–1326",disputed:false,note:"Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Mali (historical empire), Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Mali-historical-empire-Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Mali empire was a trading empire in western Africa from the 13th to the 16th century."),SRCQ("The Trans-Saharan Gold Trade (Metropolitan Museum of Art)","https://www.metmuseum.org/es/essays/the-trans-saharan-gold-trade-7th-14th-century","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Mansa Musa's arrival in Cairo carrying a ton of the metal (1324–25) caused the market in gold to crash."),SRCQ("Al-Umari on Mansa Musa's visit to Cairo (Digital History, University of Houston)","https://www.digitalhistory.uh.edu/active_learning/explorations/1492/mansa_musa_visit.cfm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","They exchanged gold until they depressed its value in Egypt and caused its price to fall."),SRCQ("'The Empires of the Western Sudan: Mali Empire', Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History, The Metropolitan Museum of Art (Oct 2000)","https://metmuseum.org/essays/the-empires-of-the-western-sudan-mali-empire","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In the early thirteenth century, the exiled prince Sundiata Keita"),SRCQ("'The Empires of the Western Sudan: Mali Empire', The Metropolitan Museum of Art","https://metmuseum.org/essays/the-empires-of-the-western-sudan-mali-empire","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Following Mansa Musa's death, Mali went into a long decline, shrinking to the size of its original territory by 1645."),SRCQ("Boston University African Studies Center: Kingdom of Mali (primary source extract, al-Umari)","https://www.bu.edu/africa/outreach/k_o_mali","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","They exchanged gold until they depressed its value in Egypt and caused its price to fall."),SRCQ("Mali Empire (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mali_Empire","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","At its peak, Mali was the largest empire in West Africa")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Kilwa and Indian Ocean trade",text:"From the 13th to the 16th century, the Swahili city of Kilwa Kisiwani traded gold and ivory for Persian pottery and Chinese porcelain. The traveller Ibn Battuta called it one of the most beautiful cities in the world.",figure:"",year:"13th–16th centuries",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","described Kilwa as one of the most beautiful cities of the world."),SRCQ("Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","much of the trade in the Indian Ocean thus passed through their hands.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Metalworking: Igbo-Ukwu",text:"Excavations at Igbo-Ukwu in present-day Nigeria found hundreds of highly skilled bronze castings, which may date from as early as the 9th century AD.",figure:"",year:"9th century",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Igbo-Ukwu (ca. 9th century), Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History (Metropolitan Museum of Art)","https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/igbo-ukwu-ca-9th-century","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Three sites have been excavated, revealing hundreds of ritual vessels and regalia castings of bronze"),SRCQ("Igbo-Ukwu (Metropolitan Museum of Art)","https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/igbo-ukwu-ca-9th-century","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","flourished as early as the ninth century"),SRCQ("Smarthistory: Igbo-Ukwu","https://smarthistory.org/?p=62761","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Over the next two decades, hundreds more bronze objects would be discovered"),SRCQ("Igbo-Ukwu (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Igbo-Ukwu","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a highly sophisticated bronze metal-working culture dating to the 9th century AD")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Great Zimbabwe",text:"UNESCO describes Great Zimbabwe as a unique testimony to the Shona civilisation between the 11th and 15th centuries, and an important trading centre.",figure:"",year:"11th–15th centuries",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe National Monument (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","are a unique testimony to the Bantu civilization of the Shona between the 11th and 15th centuries.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"City sizes: Cairo",text:"In Tertius Chandler's historical estimates, as tabulated on Wikipedia, Cairo was the largest city in the world in 1325, with about 500,000 people. These are rough estimates and are not confirmed by an institutional source.",figure:"estimates vary",year:"1325",disputed:true,note:"Colin McEvedy’s estimates, as reproduced by Fordham University’s Internet History Sourcebooks. Pre-modern city populations are rough estimates and other scholars give different figures.",src:[SRCQ("Medieval urban population estimates from Colin McEvedy, New Penguin Atlas of Medieval History (Internet Medieval Sourcebook, Fordham University) – 1346, 50,000–125,000 band","https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/Halsall/source/europe-populationurbanareas500-1500.asp","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Cairo Constantinople Florence Genoa Ghent Milan Paris Tabriz Venice"),SRCQ("Egypt: A Country Study, U.S. Library of Congress (Federal Research Division), 'Major Cities' (countrystudies.us reproduction)","https://countrystudies.us/egypt/57.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","One of its more illustrious periods ran from 1170 to 1345, when Cairo became one of the world's largest cities"),SRCQ("André Raymond (2003) 'Al-Maqrīzī's Khiṭaṭ and the Urban Structure of Mamluk Cairo', Mamluk Studies Review 7(2), University of Chicago","https://knowledge.uchicago.edu/record/1086/files/MSR_VII-2_2003-Raymond_pp145-167.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Cairo still was one of the major cities in the Mediterranean basin."),SRCQ("List of largest cities throughout history (Wikipedia; Chandler 1987 column)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_cities_throughout_history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Cairo Egypt 1325 500,000")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"City sizes: Paris, London and Córdoba",text:"Estimates vary widely. Britannica puts Córdoba, under Muslim rule, at about 100,000 people in 1000, making it the largest city in Europe. Colin McEvedy put Paris at 50,000 to 125,000 people in 1346.",figure:"Córdoba about 100,000 (1000)",year:"1300",disputed:true,note:"Córdoba from Britannica; Paris from Colin McEvedy’s estimates as reproduced by Fordham University. Figures are rough estimates. Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Córdoba, Spain (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Cordoba-Spain","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it became the largest and probably the most cultured city in Europe"),SRCQ("Córdoba, Spain (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Cordoba-Spain","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","with a population of some 100,000 in 1000"),SRCQ("McEvedy estimates (Internet Medieval Sourcebook, Fordham University) – 1346, 50,000–125,000 band, which includes Paris","https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/Halsall/source/europe-populationurbanareas500-1500.asp","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Cairo Constantinople Florence Genoa Ghent Milan Paris Tabriz Venice"),SRCQ("Historic Centre of Cordoba – UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/313/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the main urban and cultural focus in the western world"),SRCQ("List of largest European cities in history (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_European_cities_in_history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Paris France 25,000 20,000 30,000 50,000 110,000 160,000 228,000")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"destruction",h:"Destruction, removal and erasure of evidence",intro:"Some evidence of African achievement was looted, destroyed, removed abroad or explained away. Not all loss was caused by Europeans, and a great deal has survived or been recovered.",points:[
  {h:"Benin City, 1897",text:"In February 1897 British forces captured Benin City in a punitive expedition. Houses, shrines and palaces were looted and many buildings were burned.",figure:"",year:"1897",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Horniman to return ownership of Benin bronzes to Nigeria (Horniman Museum, 7 August 2022)","https://www.horniman.ac.uk/story/horniman-to-return-ownership-of-benin-bronzes-to-nigeria/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","forcibly removed from Benin City during the British military incursion in February 1897"),SRCQ("Benin Expedition of 1897 (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin_Expedition_of_1897","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","many buildings were burned down, including the Palace building itself")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"How many objects were taken",text:"The British Museum says that thousands of objects of ceremonial and ritual value were taken to the UK in 1897, as official spoils of war or shared among members of the expedition. They include over a thousand metal plaques and sculptures known as the Benin Bronzes. One secondary source says about 40% went to the British Museum.",figure:"thousands taken in 1897",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Totals vary by source and by how objects are counted.",src:[SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","thousands of objects of ceremonial and ritual value were taken to the UK as official 'spoils of war'"),SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Over 900 objects from the historic Kingdom of Benin are currently cared for by the British Museum."),SRCQ("The British raid on Benin, 1897 (National Museums Scotland)","https://nms.ac.uk/explore-our-collections/stories/global-arts-cultures-and-design/benin-collections-at-national-museums-scotland/dive-in/the-british-raid-on-benin-1897","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","auctioned off by the British Admiralty to help cover the costs of the venture."),SRCQ("Neil Brodie (University of Oxford), 'Problematizing the Encyclopedic Museum: The Benin Bronzes and Ivories in Historical Context', book chapter pp. 112–137 (Oxford University Research Archive)","https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:bb6056bc-332d-4287-987d-370dc78afa1a/files/mfd313f532f1ef245f8ddf199a1e2ba3a","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","where they were sold to help defray the costs of the military expedition."),SRCQ("Sani Yakubu Adam, 'The Benin Plaque and Competing Narratives of the African Art', 100 Histories of 100 Worlds in One Object","https://100histories100worlds.org/the-benin-plaque/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The British Admiralty decided to sell the objects at a public auction, and the British Museum had to face competition from German museums."),SRCQ("British Museum: Benin Bronzes","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","thousands of objects of ceremonial and ritual value were taken to the UK"),SRCQ("Benin Expedition of 1897 (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin_Expedition_of_1897","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","They include over a thousand metal plaques and sculptures collectively known as the Benin Bronzes .")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"British Museum holdings",text:"The British Museum says it holds over 900 objects from the historic Kingdom of Benin.",figure:"over 900",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","There are over 900 objects from the historic Kingdom of Benin in the British Museum's collection.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Horniman Museum returns ownership",text:"In 2022 the Horniman Museum in London agreed to transfer ownership of 72 objects taken from Benin City to Nigeria, and formally did so in November 2022.",figure:"72 objects",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Horniman to return ownership of Benin bronzes to Nigeria (Horniman Museum, 7 August 2022)","https://www.horniman.ac.uk/story/horniman-to-return-ownership-of-benin-bronzes-to-nigeria/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Ownership of 72 objects, which were forcibly removed from Benin City during the British military incursion in February 1897"),SRCQ("Six objects to return to Nigeria as Horniman formally transfers ownership of ‘Benin Bronzes’ (Horniman Museum, 28 November 2022)","https://www.horniman.ac.uk/story/six-objects-to-return-to-nigeria-as-horniman-formally-transfers-ownership-of-benin-bronzes/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a ceremony marking the transfer of ownership of 72 items taken by force from Benin City in 1897.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Jesus College, Cambridge",text:"Jesus College, Cambridge, handed a bronze cockerel (okukor) to Nigerian representatives on 27 October 2021, the first return of a Benin Bronze by a UK institution. The University of Aberdeen handed over a Benin Bronze head of an Oba the next day.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"This return took place in 2021, before the Horniman transfer of 2022.",src:[SRCQ("Benin Bronze October return date agreed (Jesus College, Cambridge)","https://www.jesus.cam.ac.uk/articles/benin-bronze-october-return-date-agreed","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","will receive a Benin Bronze from Jesus College on Wednesday 27 October."),SRCQ("University to return Benin bronze (University of Aberdeen, 25 March 2021)","https://www.abdn.ac.uk/about/inclusive/news/14790/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","On Tuesday the University's governing body supported the unconditional return of the Benin bronze to Nigeria."),SRCQ("Associated Press (via WDIV), 27 Oct 2021","https://www.clickondetroit.com/news/world/2021/10/27/colonial-art-cambridge-hands-over-looted-bronze-to-nigeria/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Jesus College is the first U.K. institution to give back one of the artifacts known as the Benin Bronzes."),SRCQ("The Art Newspaper, 'University of Aberdeen to return Benin bronze looted by British troops to Nigeria' (25 March 2021)","https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2021/03/25/university-of-aberdeen-to-return-benin-bronze-looted-by-british-troops-to-nigeria","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The University of Aberdeen has agreed to return to Nigeria a sculpture looted by British soldiers in a raid in 1897,"),SRCQ("The Art Newspaper (28 October 2021): Cambridge University college becomes first UK institution to return looted Benin bronze to Nigeria","https://theartnewspaper.com/2021/10/28/cambridge-university-college-becomes-first-uk-institution-to-return-looted-benin-bronze-to-nigeria","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Jesus College at the University of Cambridge has become the first UK institution to restitute a Benin bronze to Nigeria"),SRCQ("Benin Expedition of 1897 (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin_Expedition_of_1897","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","On 27 October 2021, the okukor was received by Nigeria's National Commission for Museums and Monuments (NCMM)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The British Museum’s position",text:"The British Museum Act 1963 allows the Trustees to dispose of objects only in limited cases, such as duplicates. The Museum says it is committed to working with Nigerian institutions and lends Benin objects around the world.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Section 5 lists the limited conditions (duplicates, certain objects made after 1850, objects unfit to keep). UK Parliament petitions have asked for the Act to be changed.",src:[SRCQ("British Museum Act 1963, section 5 (legislation.gov.uk)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1963/24/section/5","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Trustees of the British Museum may sell, exchange, give away or otherwise dispose of any object vested in them"),SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Museum is committed to active engagement with Nigerian institutions concerning the Benin Bronzes.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Calls to change the law",text:"Petitions to the UK Parliament have asked for the British Museum Act 1963 to be amended so that the Trustees can accept requests to return objects.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"This petition opened in November 2021 and closed without a government response or debate.",src:[SRCQ("Petition: Amend the British Museum Act 1963 (UK Parliament petitions)","https://petition.parliament.uk/petitions/600509","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","We ask Parliament to amend the British Museum Act 1963 to allow the Trustees to accept repatriation requests")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A contested counterpoint on Benin",text:"Some US campaigners, led by the Restitution Study Group, link the Bronzes to the Kingdom of Benin’s role in selling enslaved people and have tried to stop them being returned to Nigeria. The British Museum notes that Benin played a role in the trade, though its extent is debated.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Link Between Benin Bronzes and Slave Trade Snarls Transfers to Nigeria (Institute of Art and Law, 2023)","https://ial.uk.com/link-between-benin-bronzes-and-slave-trade-snarls-transfers-to-nigeria/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the goal being to keep certain Bronzes from being transferred back to Nigeria."),SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","African kingdoms including the Benin Kingdom, played a role in capturing and selling enslaved people"),SRCQ("Center for Art Law, 'Case Review: Farmer-Paellmann v Smithsonian Inst.' (11 July 2024)","https://itsartlaw.org/2024/07/11/case-review-farmer-paellmann-v-smithsonian-inst","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The plaintiffs sought to prevent and terminate the Institute's plans to return the objects."),SRCQ("Center for Art Law, 'Case Review: Farmer-Paellmann v Smithsonian Inst.' (2024)","https://itsartlaw.org/2024/07/11/case-review-farmer-paellmann-v-smithsonian-inst","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The complaint alleged that as persons descending from those trafficked under the Kingdom"),SRCQ("Benin Expedition of 1897 (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin_Expedition_of_1897","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the Kingdom of Benin was a slave trading state into the late 19th century")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Maqdala, 1868",text:"After defeating Emperor Tewodros II at Maqdala, British troops looted the fortress, its church and its treasury, taking hundreds of manuscripts and religious objects.",figure:"",year:"1868",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Legacy of Maqdala 1868 at the Bodleian Library (School of Anthropology and Museum Ethnography, University of Oxford)","https://www.anthro.ox.ac.uk/legacy-maqdala-1868-bodleian-library","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","They looted a huge amount of Ethiopian treasures, including hundreds of manuscripts, royal jewelry, crowns, crosses and other religious artifacts")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Manuscripts now in British libraries",text:"Over 300 manuscripts were acquired on the spot for the British Museum. Many are now in the British Library, with others in Oxford and Cambridge.",figure:"over 300 manuscripts",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Legacy of Maqdala 1868 at the Bodleian Library (School of Anthropology and Museum Ethnography, University of Oxford)","https://www.anthro.ox.ac.uk/legacy-maqdala-1868-bodleian-library","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","over 300 manuscripts were acquired on the spot by the British Museum."),SRCQ("The Legacy of Maqdala 1868 at the Bodleian Library (School of Anthropology and Museum Ethnography, University of Oxford)","https://www.anthro.ox.ac.uk/legacy-maqdala-1868-bodleian-library","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","many of which are now housed in the British Library")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The British Museum’s Maqdala holdings",text:"The British Museum cares for more than 90 objects from Maqdala, including 11 tabots (consecrated altar tablets). It says it remains open to dialogue.",figure:"more than 90 objects; 11 tabots",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Maqdala collection (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/objects-news/maqdala-collection","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The British Museum cares for more than 90 objects from the northern Ethiopian site of Maqdala"),SRCQ("Maqdala collection (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/objects-news/maqdala-collection","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Museum remains open to continuing dialogue around the tabots and the wider Maqdala collections.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Kumasi, 1874: Asante gold",text:"In 1874 British troops raided the Asante capital Kumasi. Several hundred items of gold regalia were looted from the royal palace and later sold in London.",figure:"",year:"1874",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Homecoming – Exhibition of Asante Gold Regalia at Manhyia Palace Museum, Kumasi (V&A, 2 May 2024)","https://www.vam.ac.uk/blog/news/homecoming-exhibition-of-asante-gold-regalia-at-manhiya-palace-museum-kumasi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Most of the items of Asante regalia in the V&A were among several hundred looted from the royal palace in Kumasi")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Asante gold goes on show in Kumasi",text:"In 2024 items from the V&A and the British Museum went on show in Kumasi on loan for three years, with an option to extend. This is a loan, not a return of ownership.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Homecoming – Exhibition of Asante Gold Regalia at Manhyia Palace Museum, Kumasi (V&A, 2 May 2024)","https://www.vam.ac.uk/blog/news/homecoming-exhibition-of-asante-gold-regalia-at-manhiya-palace-museum-kumasi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The V&A items are on loan to Manhyia Palace Museum for three years with the option to extend for a further three")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Great Zimbabwe: denial of African builders",text:"When the German explorer Karl Mauch reached Great Zimbabwe in 1871, he credited it to outsiders, linking it to the Queen of Sheba and to Phoenicians.",figure:"",year:"1871",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Mysteries of Great Zimbabwe (NOVA, PBS)","https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/article/mysteries-of-great-zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","must have been built by the Queen of Sheba."),SRCQ("Preben Kaarsholm, 'The past as battlefield in Rhodesia and Zimbabwe' (SAS-Space, School of Advanced Study, University of London)","https://sas-space.sas.ac.uk/4213/1/Preben_Kaarsholm_-_The_past_as_battlefield_in_Rhodesia_and_Zimbabwe.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Phoenicians and, possibly, Israelites in their thousands had been working here"),SRCQ("Webber Ndoro, 'Great Zimbabwe', Scientific American (special edition, Vol. 15 No. 1s)","https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/great-zimbabwe-2005-01/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","A German explorer, Karl Mauch, was first to arrive, in 1871."),SRCQ("Webber Ndoro, 'Great Zimbabwe', Scientific American","https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/great-zimbabwe-2005-01/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","It looked to Mauch to be the result of Phoenician or Israelite settlers."),SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Karl Mauch recorded the ruins and immediately discounted any possibility of native construction")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Great Zimbabwe: damage to the evidence",text:"In the early colonial period, diggers such as Richard Hall stripped away up to 12 feet of layered deposits while looking for non-African builders, destroying evidence archaeologists use to date the site.",figure:"",year:"early colonial period",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Mysteries of Great Zimbabwe (NOVA, PBS)","https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/article/mysteries-of-great-zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","discarding from three to 12 feet of stratified archeological deposits throughout Great Zimbabwe"),SRCQ("Roderick J. McIntosh, 'Riddle of Great Zimbabwe', Archaeology (Archaeological Institute of America), 1998","https://archive.archaeology.org/9807/abstracts/africa.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","to free it from the filth and decadence of the Kaffir [South African] occupation"),SRCQ("Shadreck Chirikure and Innocent Pikirayi (2008) 'Inside and outside the dry stone walls: revisiting the material culture of Great Zimbabwe', Antiquity 82: 976–993","https://core-cms.cambridgecore.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/5C9929FB69CE65473F2FD3879FF6B853/S0003598X00097726a.pdf/inside-and-outside-the-dry-stone-walls-revisiting-the-material-culture-of-great-zimbabwe.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the men who removed all the most important finds from the ruins and stripped them of so much of their deposits"),SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","European antiquarians looted and pillaged Great Zimbabwe and similar structures from the 1890s to 1920s")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Rhodesia: censorship",text:"Under white minority rule, the Rhodesian government suppressed archaeologists’ findings that Africans built Great Zimbabwe. Official publications were barred from stating plainly that it was an African creation.",figure:"",year:"1960s–1970s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Mysteries of Great Zimbabwe (NOVA, PBS)","https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/article/mysteries-of-great-zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the white government of Rhodesia set about suppressing the findings of prehistorians"),SRCQ("Preben Kaarsholm, 'The past as battlefield in Rhodesia and Zimbabwe' (University of London), citing Garlake","https://sas-space.sas.ac.uk/4213/1/Preben_Kaarsholm_-_The_past_as_battlefield_in_Rhodesia_and_Zimbabwe.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","that no official publication may state unequivocally that Great Zimbabwe was an African creation"),SRCQ("Webber Ndoro, 'Great Zimbabwe', Scientific American","https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/great-zimbabwe-2005-01/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","From 1965 until independence in 1980, the Rhodesian Front censored all books and other materials available on Great Zimbabwe."),SRCQ("Chirikure and Pikirayi (2008), Antiquity 82","https://core-cms.cambridgecore.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/5C9929FB69CE65473F2FD3879FF6B853/S0003598X00097726a.pdf/inside-and-outside-the-dry-stone-walls-revisiting-the-material-culture-of-great-zimbabwe.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Rhodesian settler community did not accept an African authorship of the monuments."),SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Archaeologists who disputed the official statement were censored by the government.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Peter Garlake forced out",text:"The archaeologist Peter Garlake, a senior inspector with Rhodesia’s Historical Monuments Commission, condemned government interference over Great Zimbabwe. He was forced out of the country and had left by early 1971.",figure:"",year:"had left by 1971",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Editorial, Antiquity 45 (177), March 1971","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/15E527FDFF38A0BB003FB0D804EC76D5/S0003598X00068976a.pdf/editorial.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Peter Garlake, until recently Senior Inspector of the Rhodesian Historical Monuments Commission, has left for West Africa."),SRCQ("Editorial, Antiquity 45 (177), March 1971, quoting Garlake","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/15E527FDFF38A0BB003FB0D804EC76D5/S0003598X00068976a.pdf/editorial.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a personal attack on the integrity of archaeologists who have studied the subject in great detail."),SRCQ("Mysteries of Great Zimbabwe (NOVA, PBS)","https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/article/mysteries-of-great-zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Garlake, for one, was forced out of the country."),SRCQ("Chirikure and Pikirayi (2008), Antiquity 82: 976–993","https://core-cms.cambridgecore.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/5C9929FB69CE65473F2FD3879FF6B853/S0003598X00097726a.pdf/inside-and-outside-the-dry-stone-walls-revisiting-the-material-culture-of-great-zimbabwe.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","exiled professional archaeologists"),SRCQ("Peter Garlake (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Garlake","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Garlake was forced to leave the country in 1970.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Science corrected the record",text:"Professional archaeologists, starting with David Randall-MacIver and Gertrude Caton-Thompson in the early 20th century, confirmed that Great Zimbabwe was of African origin.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"This shows that the erasure was challenged, though official denial in Rhodesia continued afterwards.",src:[SRCQ("Roderick J. McIntosh, 'Riddle of Great Zimbabwe', Archaeology (Archaeological Institute of America), 1998","https://archive.archaeology.org/9807/abstracts/africa.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","of British archaeologists David Randall-MacIver and Gertrude Caton-Thompson early in this century"),SRCQ("Roderick J. McIntosh, 'Riddle of Great Zimbabwe', Archaeology (AIA), 1998","https://archive.archaeology.org/9807/abstracts/africa.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page",", which confirmed that the ruins were of African origin."),SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe National Monument (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","is unquestionably of Bantu origin"),SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Its African origin only became consensus by the 1950s.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Most heritage is outside Africa",text:"The Sarr–Savoy report (2018) relies on an expert estimate that over 90% of the material cultural heritage of sub-Saharan Africa is held outside the continent.",figure:"over 90%",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"The report presents this as a figure “often reiterated by experts” rather than its own count.",src:[SRCQ("Felwine Sarr and Bénédicte Savoy, The Restitution of African Cultural Heritage (2018), English translation (PDF mirror)","https://www.about-africa.de/images/sonstiges/2018/sarr_savoy_en.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","over 90% of the material cultural legacy of sub-Saharan Africa remains preserved and housed outside of the African continent.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Size of European collections",text:"The same report gives examples: about 69,000 objects from sub-Saharan Africa in the British Museum and about 70,000 in the Musée du quai Branly in Paris.",figure:"69,000 (British Museum)",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Felwine Sarr and Bénédicte Savoy, The Restitution of African Cultural Heritage (2018), English translation (PDF mirror)","https://www.about-africa.de/images/sonstiges/2018/sarr_savoy_en.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","From the British Museum (69,000 objects from sub-Saharan Africa)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Timbuktu, 2012–2013: a different kind of loss",text:"During the occupation of northern Mali by armed jihadist groups, a UNESCO specialist reported that 4,203 manuscripts held by the Ahmed Baba Institute were burned or stolen. This loss was not caused by Europeans. Local people moved many more manuscripts to safety; a widely repeated estimate is about 350,000.",figure:"4,203 lost; about 300,000 saved",year:"2012–2013",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Damage to Timbuktu's cultural heritage worse than first estimated (UNESCO)","https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/damage-timbuktus-cultural-heritage-worse-first-estimated-reports-unesco-mission","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","We estimate that 4,203 manuscripts from the Ahmed Baba research centre were lost"),SRCQ("Damage to Timbuktu's cultural heritage (UNESCO)","https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/damage-timbuktus-cultural-heritage-worse-first-estimated-reports-unesco-mission","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","another 300,000 were exfiltrated - mainly to Bamako"),SRCQ("Endangered Libraries in Timbuktu (British Library Endangered Archives Programme)","https://eap.bl.uk/node/2441","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","hundreds of thousands of Timbuktu manuscripts were removed clandestinely to Bamako"),SRCQ("PRI The World (3 July 2013): Islamists have destroyed one-tenth of Timbuktu's ancient manuscripts, UN finds","https://theworld.org/stories/2013/07/03/islamists-have-destroyed-one-tenth-timbuktus-ancient-manuscripts-un-finds","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","4,203 manuscripts were either burned by the Islamists or stolen"),SRCQ("Timbuktu manuscripts (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbuktu_manuscripts","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","4,203 of Timbuktu's manuscripts were burned or stolen following between 2012 and 2013.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A war crimes conviction",text:"The International Criminal Court convicted Ahmad Al Faqi Al Mahdi in 2016 of the war crime of attacking religious and historic buildings in Timbuktu.",figure:"",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Prosecutor v. Ahmad Al Faqi Al Mahdi (International Criminal Court)","https://www.icc-cpi.int/mali/al-mahdi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Found guilty as a co-perpetrator of the war crime consisting in intentionally directing attacks against religious and historic buildings in Timbuktu")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Oral tradition as an archive",text:"In West Africa, griots (jeli) have traditionally preserved genealogies, historical narratives and oral traditions. Historians writing UNESCO’s General History of Africa used oral traditions as a source.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Where the written record was lost or never made, oral tradition is a source of evidence in its own right.",src:[SRCQ("Griot (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/art/griot","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","role has traditionally been to preserve the genealogies, historical narratives, and oral traditions of their people"),SRCQ("UNESCO's work on General History of Africa helps restore forgotten history (Africa Renewal, United Nations)","https://africarenewal.un.org/en/magazine/unescos-work-general-history-africa-helps-restore-forgotten-history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The writers integrated information from oral traditions, written archives, and scientific and archaeological findings."),SRCQ("Hakimah Abdul-Fattah, 'How Griots Tell Legendary Epics Through Stories and Songs in West Africa', The Metropolitan Museum of Art (20 April 2020)","https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/sahel-sunjata-stories-songs","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","As well as being storytellers, they are poets, historians, genealogists, and musicians."),SRCQ("UNESCO, 'A new page in the General History of Africa' (29 March 2019, updated 20 April 2023)","https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/new-page-general-history-africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","recognizing the work of African researchers, oral traditions and new data, notably from archaeological sources"),SRCQ("Griot (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Griot","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The griot is a repository of oral tradition")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Losses elsewhere: Baghdad and Alexandria",text:"Great libraries were lost in other places too. The Mongols largely destroyed Baghdad, home of the House of Wisdom, in 1258. Later writers say a fire in 48 BC destroyed the Library of Alexandria, but how much was lost is unclear.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"This is included for balance: the loss of archives is not unique to Africa.",src:[SRCQ("Hülegü (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/print/article/275219","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","and in 1258 he seized and largely destroyed Baghdad"),SRCQ("The fate of the Library of Alexandria (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Library-of-Alexandria/The-fate-of-the-Library-of-Alexandria","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Caesar was forced to repel the danger by using fire, which spread from the dockyards and destroyed the Great Library."),SRCQ("The fate of the Library of Alexandria (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Library-of-Alexandria/The-fate-of-the-Library-of-Alexandria","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Yet he is remarkably silent regarding the extent of the destruction caused by the fire in the city itself."),SRCQ("Linda Komaroff, 'The Art of the Ilkhanid Period (1256–1353)', The Metropolitan Museum of Art (2001)","https://www.metmuseum.org/en/essays/the-art-of-the-ilkhanid-period-1256-1353","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","when Mongol forces subjugated all of Iran and by 1258 had also taken Baghdad,"),SRCQ("Smithsonian Magazine, 'How an Artist Is Rebuilding a Baghdad Library Destroyed During the Iraq War'","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/artist-hopes-rebuild-baghdad-library-destroyed-during-iraq-war-180969979/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Legend has it that when the Mongols laid siege to Baghdad in 1258,"),SRCQ("Erin Blakemore, 'What really happened to the Library of Alexandria? These are the theories.', National Geographic (16 Oct 2025)","https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/who-burned-library-of-alexandria-egypt","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 48 B.C., at least part of the library became a casualty of an Egyptian civil war."),SRCQ("Erin Blakemore, National Geographic (2025)","https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/who-burned-library-of-alexandria-egypt","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Historical sources disagree on how much damage resulted;"),SRCQ("House of Wisdom (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Wisdom","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","It was destroyed in 1258 during the Mongol siege of Baghdad .")],kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"narratives",h:"Colonial-era narratives",intro:"From the 19th century, some European writers claimed that Africa had no history of its own. These claims were used to justify colonial rule and have since been widely challenged.",points:[
  {h:"The “civilising mission”",text:"European powers presented colonisation as a duty to “civilise” other peoples. Historians treat this as a political justification for conquest.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Carey A. Watt, Introduction, Civilizing Missions in Colonial and Postcolonial South Asia (Anthem/Cambridge Core)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/civilizing-missions-in-colonial-and-postcolonial-south-asia/introduction/15B1055E5108F4F68163AE96DF9118FA","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","was used to justify and legitimize the establishment and continuation of overseas colonies."),SRCQ("Margaret Kohn and Kavita Reddy, 'Colonialism', Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (substantive revision 17 Jan 2023)","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The idea of a “civilizing mission” was by no means the invention of the British in the nineteenth century."),SRCQ("Kohn and Reddy, 'Colonialism', Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (2023)","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","proved useful in justifying imperialism."),SRCQ("Civilizing mission (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civilizing_mission","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The civilizing mission ( French : mission civilisatrice ) is a political rationale for military intervention and for colonization")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Hegel: Africa outside history",text:"The German philosopher G.W.F. Hegel, in lectures given in the 1820s, dismissed Africa from world history. Many scholars have strongly criticised him for this.",figure:"",year:"1820s",disputed:false,note:"The exact wording “no historical part of the world” is widely quoted from an English translation, but is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Tom C. McCaskie, Exiled from History: Africa in Hegel’s Academic Practice, History in Africa 46 (2019) (abstract)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/exiled-from-history-africa-in-hegels-academic-practice/182C63265C32C1A213269BAFA65DBFDA","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Many scholars, African and otherwise, have excoriated G.W.F. Hegel for his dismissal of Africa from history and progress")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Hegel used and misused his sources",text:"A historian of Africa has shown that Hegel read, misread and misused the texts about Africa that were available to him.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Tom C. McCaskie, Exiled from History: Africa in Hegel’s Academic Practice, History in Africa 46 (2019) (abstract)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/exiled-from-history-africa-in-hegels-academic-practice/182C63265C32C1A213269BAFA65DBFDA","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","read, misread, excerpted, used, and misused in support of his theorizing and apriorism.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Trevor-Roper’s remark",text:"The Oxford historian Hugh Trevor-Roper said in 1963 that there was “only the history of Europeans in Africa” and that the rest was “the unrewarding gyrations of barbarous tribes in picturesque but irrelevant corners of the globe”.",figure:"",year:"1963",disputed:false,note:"The 1963 date (a lecture series later published as The Rise of Christian Europe) is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Enocent Msindo (Rhodes University), keynote to the Zimbabwe Historical Association, 2019, quoting Trevor-Roper","https://www.africaknows.eu/pdfs/D19-11.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","there is only the history of Europeans in Africa. The rest is largely darkness"),SRCQ("Fuglestad 1992, History in Africa 19 (Cambridge)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/abs/trevorroper-trap-or-the-imperialism-of-history-an-essay1/8E05630B0B459F9F1BDB6EEE819AFC9C","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","that at least precolonial Black Africa had no history"),SRCQ("The Spectator, Michela Wrong, 23 Jan 2025 (magazine)","https://spectator.com/article/never-underestimate-the-complexities-of-african-history/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","unrewarding gyrations of barbarous tribes"),SRCQ("Stephen Ellis, review of Finn Fuglestad, The Ambiguities of History (2005), African Affairs (2006) – Leiden University Scholarly Publications","https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A3144963/view","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Africa's record did not demonstrate this, he thought, but consisted of only 'the unrewarding gyrations"),SRCQ("Tunde Adeleke (2000) 'Guerilla Intellectualism: Walter A. Rodney and the Weapon of Knowledge in the Struggle for Black Liberation', Journal of Thought 35(1): 37–59 (PDF hosted by Princeton University)","https://bgc.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/175/2022/01/clean_GI.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the unrewarding gyrations of barbarous tribes on picturesque but irrelevant corners of the globe."),SRCQ("Hugh Trevor-Roper (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Trevor-Roper","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the unedifying gyrations of barbarous tribes in picturesque but irrelevant corners of the globe")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The response to Trevor-Roper",text:"The remark was strongly challenged by Africanist historians such as J.D. Fage, who argued that Africa’s past showed complex societies and change, so Africa did have a history.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Enocent Msindo (Rhodes University), keynote to the Zimbabwe Historical Association, 2019","https://www.africaknows.eu/pdfs/D19-11.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","soon came under serious attack from the emerging Africanists"),SRCQ("Stephen Ellis, review of Finn Fuglestad, The Ambiguities of History (2005), African Affairs (2006) – Leiden University Scholarly Publications","https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A3144963/view","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","(he studied under John Fage, one of the main respondents to Hugh Trevor-Roper, and is the author"),SRCQ("Stephen Ellis, review in African Affairs (2006)","https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A3144963/view","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The gist of the Africanist response was that Trevor-Roper was wrong since Africa's past demonstrated"),SRCQ("Hugh Trevor-Roper (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Trevor-Roper","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","spurred intense debate between historians, anthropologists , sociologists")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"UNESCO’s General History of Africa",text:"In 1964 UNESCO began work on the General History of Africa, reflecting its growing number of African member states. Its writers used oral traditions and archaeology as well as written archives.",figure:"",year:"1964",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO's work on General History of Africa (Africa Renewal, United Nations)","https://africarenewal.un.org/en/magazine/unescos-work-general-history-africa-helps-restore-forgotten-history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1964, the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) envisioned an ambitious project"),SRCQ("General History of Africa (UNESCO Library guide)","https://unesco.libguides.com/general_history_africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The GHA was intended to reflect UNESCO's growing number of African member states."),SRCQ("UNESCO's work on General History of Africa (Africa Renewal, United Nations)","https://africarenewal.un.org/en/magazine/unescos-work-general-history-africa-helps-restore-forgotten-history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The writers integrated information from oral traditions, written archives, and scientific and archaeological findings."),SRCQ("UNESCO, General History of Africa (programme page)","https://www.unesco.org/en/general-history-africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1964, as African nations gained independence, UNESCO brought together leading African and international experts"),SRCQ("UNESCO press release, 'General History of Africa: UNESCO completes pioneering historical oeuvre …' (17 Oct 2025)","https://articles.unesco.org/sites/default/files/medias/fichiers/2025/10/PR_General_History_of_Africa_UNESCO_completes_pioneering_historical_oeuvre_and_launches_new_educational_resources_en.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Launched in 1964 by UNESCO and several newly independent African states"),SRCQ("UNESCO, 'A new page in the General History of Africa' (2019)","https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/new-page-general-history-africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","recognizing the work of African researchers, oral traditions and new data, notably from archaeological sources"),SRCQ("General History of Africa (UNESCO project) (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_History_of_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The General History of Africa ( GHA ) is a two-phase project launched by UNESCO in 1964")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Hamitic hypothesis",text:"The “Hamitic hypothesis” claimed that progress in Africa was the work of “Hamitic” migrants from the north and north-east. C.G. Seligman set it out in Races of Africa (1930). It has since been discredited.",figure:"",year:"1930",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Charles Seligman (LSE Department of Anthropology)","https://www.lse.ac.uk/anthropology/Old-Anthropology-Library/Seligman/Seligman","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Here he asserted that members of the 'Hamitic race', who had migrated into central Africa from North and Northeast Africa"),SRCQ("Charles Seligman (LSE Department of Anthropology)","https://www.lse.ac.uk/anthropology/Old-Anthropology-Library/Seligman/Seligman","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Later discredited, this theory – known as the Hamitic hypothesis"),SRCQ("Encyclopaedia Britannica: Hamitic hypothesis","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Hamitic-hypothesis","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","This theory has been thoroughly debunked and is considered a pseudoscientific and racist construct."),SRCQ("Hamites / Hamitic hypothesis (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamitic_race","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","virtually all significant achievements in African history were the work of \"Hamites\".")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Scramble for Africa",text:"By the early 20th century European powers controlled most of Africa. Only Ethiopia and Liberia remained outside European rule.",figure:"",year:"early 20th century",disputed:false,note:"See also the hub’s peoples page for the Scramble and its effects on communities.",src:[SRCQ("Scramble for Africa (Encyclopaedia Britannica), map caption","https://www.britannica.com/event/Scramble-for-Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","except for Ethiopia and Liberia."),SRCQ("Scramble for Africa (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Scramble-for-Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","claimed control of most African territory"),SRCQ("EBSCO Research Starters: Scramble for Africa","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/scramble-africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1914, almost all of Africa was under European control."),SRCQ("Scramble for Africa (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scramble_for_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1914, only Ethiopia and Liberia remained outside European control")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"limits",h:"What can and cannot be known",intro:"The claim is a counterfactual: it asks what would have happened if history had been different. Counterfactuals cannot be proven. The evidence can show harm and lost potential. It cannot show for certain what Africa would have become.",points:[
  {h:"The harm is well evidenced",text:"The scale of the trades, the population loss and the long-term economic effects are supported by several independent kinds of evidence.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa's Slave Trades (NBER Working Paper 13367, abstract)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w13367","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Together the evidence suggests that the slave trades have had an adverse effect on economic development.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"The counterfactual is not testable",text:"Estimates of the “missing” population are modelled, and projecting African population back in time is hard. No method can measure what Africa would have achieved.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Frankema discusses the difficulty of extending African population series into earlier centuries.",src:[SRCQ("Ewout Frankema, Africa and the Demographic Consequences of the Columbian Exchange, Asian Review of World Histories 7 (2019)","https://www.ewoutfrankema.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankema_African-Demography-and-Columbian-Exchange_ARWH.2019.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","main challenges of extrapolating")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"“More advanced” depends on the measure",text:"On some measures, such as city size around the year 1000, the Islamic world led: Córdoba, under Muslim rule, was then the largest city in Europe. Population figures are rough estimates, and no single measure supports an overall ranking.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Part of this rests on one independent source.",src:[SRCQ("Córdoba, Spain (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Cordoba-Spain","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it became the largest and probably the most cultured city in Europe"),SRCQ("McEvedy estimates (Internet Medieval Sourcebook, Fordham University) – c.1000, 23,000–49,000 band (Paris and London not listed)","https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/Halsall/source/europe-populationurbanareas500-1500.asp","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Alexandria Antioch Basra Cairo Cordoba"),SRCQ("Historic Centre of Cordoba – UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/313/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the main urban and cultural focus in the western world"),SRCQ("Egypt: A Country Study, U.S. Library of Congress, 'Major Cities' (countrystudies.us reproduction)","https://countrystudies.us/egypt/57.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","One of its more illustrious periods ran from 1170 to 1345, when Cairo became one of the world's largest cities"),SRCQ("List of largest cities throughout history (Wikipedia; Chandler 1987 column)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_cities_throughout_history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Cairo Egypt 1325 500,000")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Africa’s economic position before the trades",text:"Some economic historians argue that low labour productivity already existed before the external slave trades. This challenges the idea that Africa was on course to overtake Europe.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Gareth Austin, The ‘reversal of fortune’ thesis and the compression of history, Journal of International Development 20(8), 2008 (abstract, RePEc)","https://ideas.repec.org/a/wly/jintdv/v20y2008i8p996-1027.html","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Relatively low labour productivity was a premise of the external slave trades")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Europe’s lead was late and not inevitable",text:"Great Divergence research led by Kenneth Pomeranz argues that Europe's lead over other advanced regions came only after about 1750; some economic historians date it earlier. If the lead came late, other paths of development were possible, but the evidence does not show that Africa would have taken one.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Great Divergence (Princeton University Press)","https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691217185/the-great-divergence","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","as recently as 1750, life expectancy, consumption, and product and factor markets were comparable in Europe and East Asia.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"African states were also involved",text:"The trades involved African rulers and merchants as well as Europeans and others. This does not lessen the harm, but it complicates a simple Europe-versus-Africa account.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"“that” is as written on the source page.",src:[SRCQ("Bristol Museums (Bristol City Council)","https://museums.bristol.gov.uk/narratives.php?irn=2356","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Those selling enslaved people were usually the chiefs and important men of the local area"),SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","African kingdoms including the Benin Kingdom, played a role in capturing and selling enslaved people"),SRCQ("John Thornton, Africa and Africans in the Making of the Atlantic World (Cambridge University Press)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/africa-and-africans-in-the-making-of-the-atlantic-world-14001800/process-of-enslavement-and-the-slave-trade/777DA26E18B1DD2682A29F900AFD8CAF","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","At the beginning, at least, Europeans were only tapping existing slave markets."),SRCQ("John Thornton (historian) (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Thornton_(historian)","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Africans had been more active participants in the trade that was previously believed")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Not all losses were caused by Europeans",text:"The trans-Saharan, Red Sea and Indian Ocean trades were not European. The destruction in Timbuktu in 2012–2013 was carried out by jihadist occupiers.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Another 6 million were exported in the other three slave trades."),SRCQ("The Prosecutor v. Ahmad Al Faqi Al Mahdi (International Criminal Court)","https://www.icc-cpi.int/mali/al-mahdi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","intentionally directing attacks against religious and historic buildings in Timbuktu")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Much evidence survives",text:"Despite looting and loss, substantial evidence survives: World Heritage sites, hundreds of thousands of manuscripts, museum collections and oral traditions.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Endangered Libraries in Timbuktu (British Library)","https://eap.bl.uk/node/2441","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","hundreds of thousands of Timbuktu manuscripts were removed clandestinely to Bamako"),SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe National Monument (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The ruins of Great Zimbabwe bear a unique testimony to the lost civilisation of the Shona"),SRCQ("Benin Bronzes (British Museum)","https://www.britishmuseum.org/about-us/british-museum-story/contested-objects-collection/benin-bronzes","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Over 900 objects from the historic Kingdom of Benin are currently cared for by the British Museum."),SRCQ("Griot (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/art/griot","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","role has traditionally been to preserve the genealogies, historical narratives, and oral traditions of their people"),SRCQ("Timbuktu manuscripts (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbuktu_manuscripts","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Some 350,000 manuscripts were transported to safety")],kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"d-before",h:"Disease before colonisation and in the so-called \"Age of Discovery\" (about 1400-1600)",intro:"Epidemics did not begin with colonisation. Plague crossed Africa, Asia and Europe in the 14th century, and some diseases were long established in Africa. From the 15th century, sea voyages and the slave trade moved disease in more than one direction. The phrase \"Age of Discovery\" is a European-centred label; we use it in quotation marks.",points:[
  {h:"The Black Death in Egypt",text:"Plague reached Egypt in the mid-14th century. The historian Michael Dols concluded that between a third and a half of Egypt's people died in this first outbreak. His work is still the standard reference.",figure:"One-third to one-half of Egypt's population (Dols's estimate)",year:"14th century",disputed:false,note:"The exact dates 1346-1353 are commonly given for the pandemic but are not confirmed by the sources cited here.",src:[SRCQ("Stuart Borsch, The Black Death in the Middle East and Central Asia (Islamic Law Blog, Harvard Law School Program in Islamic Law, 29 May 2020)","https://islamiclaw.blog/2020/05/29/stuart-borsch/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","between one-third and one-half of the inhabitants of Egypt were carried off by this outbreak"),SRCQ("Stuart Borsch, The Black Death in the Middle East and Central Asia (Islamic Law Blog, Harvard Law School Program in Islamic Law, 29 May 2020)","https://islamiclaw.blog/2020/05/29/stuart-borsch/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","His work remains the standard source of reference for the Black Death in the Middle East")]},
  {h:"The Black Death in the Maghreb",text:"Eyewitnesses and historians agree plague devastated North-West Africa, but it is much less studied than Europe or Egypt.",figure:"",year:"14th century",disputed:false,note:"Death rates for the Maghreb are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Rachel Singer, The Black Death in the Maghreb: A Call to Action (University of Chicago Lexicon Project PDF)","https://voices.uchicago.edu/lexiconproject/files/2021/09/singer_death_maghreb.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Despite detailed eyewitness accounts and a scholarly consensus that the outbreak"),SRCQ("Rachel Singer, The Black Death in the Maghreb: A Call to Action (University of Chicago Lexicon Project PDF)","https://voices.uchicago.edu/lexiconproject/files/2021/09/singer_death_maghreb.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","we are more ignorant of the Black Death in the Maghreb than we are of the Black Death in regions")]},
  {h:"Sleeping sickness was known in Africa long before colonial rule",text:"Sleeping sickness is old in Africa. A 14th-century Arab historian recorded a ruler of Mali dying of it. Historians argue that African societies had learned to live alongside the tsetse fly before colonial upheaval broke that balance.",figure:"",year:"14th century",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Headrick (2014) Sleeping Sickness Epidemics and Colonial Responses in East and Central Africa, 1900-1940, PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases (PMC)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3998934/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In the 14th century, the Arab historian Ibn Khaldun wrote that King Diata II of Mali had died of it."),SRCQ("Headrick (2014) Sleeping Sickness Epidemics and Colonial Responses in East and Central Africa, 1900-1940, PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases (PMC)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3998934/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Africans before the colonial era had established a rough equilibrium between two ecosystems")]},
  {h:"Malaria and sickle-cell trait",text:"Malaria shaped human populations in Africa long before European contact. Sickle-cell trait gives some protection against it. The hub's genome page covers this in depth.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"See the hub genome page; not repeated here.",src:[SRCQ("David S. Jones (2003) Virgin Soils Revisited, William and Mary Quarterly 60(4) (PDF via MITx course)","https://courses.edx.org/asset-v1:MITx+21H.899x+3T2020+type%40asset+block%40VirginSoilWMQ.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The protective benefit of sickle cell trait against malaria became a centerpiece of the burgeoning field of evolutionary genetics.")]},
  {h:"Yellow fever began in Africa",text:"Yellow fever virus came from Africa and reached the Americas with the slave ships, as did the mosquito that carries it. Today yellow fever is still a risk in both regions.",figure:"27 African and 13 Latin American countries classified high-risk today (WHO)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The African origin is stated by a history blog (We're History); WHO gives the present-day distribution only.",src:[SRCQ("Zika Virus, Yellow Fever, and the Mosquito Behind Them (We're History, 2 February 2016)","https://werehistory.org/zika/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The virus that causes it also came from Africa and arrived in the New World via the slave trade"),SRCQ("Yellow fever fact sheet (World Health Organization, 20 October 2025)","https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/yellow-fever","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Twenty-seven African countries and 13 Latin American countries are classified as high-risk for yellow fever outbreaks.")]},
  {h:"The Columbian Exchange as an idea",text:"In 1972 the historian Alfred Crosby named the \"Columbian Exchange\": the movement of crops, animals, people and germs across the Atlantic after 1492. His thesis is still debated.",figure:"",year:"1972",disputed:true,note:"Historians of medicine have challenged parts of Crosby's later argument (see \"Conditions\").",src:[SRCQ("European Exploration, Perception of the Other, and the Columbian Exchange (Oxford University Press, Backman, Cultures of the West, student resources)","https://learninglink.oup.com/access/content/history-in-practice-western-civ/backman3e-student-resources-volume-1-chapter-14-section-3-background","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","to describe the exchange of goods, people, animals, and pathogens across the Atlantic during the age of exploration"),SRCQ("The Columbian Exchange - seminar page (Department of History, University of Warwick)","https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/arts/history/students/modules/hi34i/seminars/columbianexchange/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Crosby’s thesis has been debated by historians of medicine, empire, and the environment")]},
  {h:"Portugal, the Canaries and the West African coast",text:"Portuguese voyages along the African Atlantic coast in the 15th century were driven by gold and then by trade in enslaved people. Canary Islanders were among the first taken.",figure:"",year:"1440s",disputed:false,note:"Disease at the early trading forts (for example Elmina) is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Brookbanks & Mbambo (2025) Reimaging, from a decolonised perspective, the Grade 10 intended curriculum..., Yesterday & Today No 33 (SciELO South Africa)","https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/yt/n33/15.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Canarians were ruthlessly abducted and shipped off to Europe, where they fed a highly lucrative market in slaves"),SRCQ("Brookbanks & Mbambo (2025) Reimaging, from a decolonised perspective, the Grade 10 intended curriculum..., Yesterday & Today No 33 (SciELO South Africa)","https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/yt/n33/15.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","was first mobilised in the 1440s"),SRCQ("Brookbanks & Mbambo (2025) Reimaging, from a decolonised perspective, the Grade 10 intended curriculum..., Yesterday & Today No 33 (SciELO South Africa)","https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/yt/n33/15.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","superseded by a new lucrative trade in Black African slaves")]},
  {h:"Syphilis: did it travel from the Americas to Europe?",text:"A 2011 review found no reliable skeletal proof of syphilis in Europe before 1492, which supports an American origin. A later study claimed earlier European cases. The question stays open.",figure:"",year:"1493-1495",disputed:true,note:"Debated. Neither side is settled by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Skeletons point to Columbus voyage for syphilis origins (ScienceDaily, source: Emory University, 20 December 2011)","https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2011/12/111220134144.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","None of this skeletal evidence, including 54 published reports, holds up when subjected to standardized analyses for both diagnosis and dating"),SRCQ("Did Columbus Bring Syphilis To Europe? Study Raises Doubts (Scripps News)","https://scrippsnews.com/science-and-tech/did-columbus-bring-syphilis-to-europe-study-raises-doubts","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","A new study claims to have found evidence of syphilis in the Old World before Columbus' voyage to the Americas")]},
  {h:"Smallpox on Hispaniola, 1518",text:"In December 1518 smallpox broke out among enslaved Africans in the mines of Hispaniola. It then killed many of the remaining Taíno and spread to other islands and the mainland.",figure:"About one-third of the remaining Indigenous people of Hispaniola by May 1519 (as reported)",year:"1518",disputed:false,note:"Secondary website; figure is an estimate.",src:[SRCQ("Smallpox Comes to the Americas (1507-1524) (Indigenous Mexico, secondary website citing Jonathan B. Tucker)","https://www.indigenousmexico.org/articles/smallpox-comes-to-the-americas-1507-1524","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In December 1518, smallpox appeared again, initially among the enslaved Africans in the mines of Hispaniola."),SRCQ("Smallpox Comes to the Americas (1507-1524) (Indigenous Mexico, secondary website citing Jonathan B. Tucker)","https://www.indigenousmexico.org/articles/smallpox-comes-to-the-americas-1507-1524","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By May 1519, one-third of the remaining native people on Hispaniola died of smallpox")]},
  {h:"Smallpox moved through the slave trade",text:"The historian Jonathan B. Tucker argues that ships carrying enslaved Africans from places where smallpox was common brought the disease to the Americas again and again.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Quoted from Tucker via a secondary website.",src:[SRCQ("Smallpox Comes to the Americas (1507-1524) (Indigenous Mexico, secondary website citing Jonathan B. Tucker)","https://www.indigenousmexico.org/articles/smallpox-comes-to-the-americas-1507-1524","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Since many African slaves came from regions where smallpox was endemic, the slave trade introduced the disease to the Americas.")]},
  {h:"Yellow fever reaches the Caribbean, 1647",text:"The first clear yellow fever epidemic in the Americas struck Barbados and Yucatán in 1647. The mosquito had crossed in water casks on slave ships.",figure:"",year:"1647",disputed:false,note:"Malaria also crossed with the slave trade; its timing is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Zika Virus, Yellow Fever, and the Mosquito Behind Them (We're History, 2 February 2016)","https://werehistory.org/zika/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The first unmistakable evidence of its presence came in 1647 when yellow fever struck on Barbados and the Yucatan Peninsula."),SRCQ("Zika Virus, Yellow Fever, and the Mosquito Behind Them (We're History, 2 February 2016)","https://werehistory.org/zika/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The mosquitoes made the transatlantic passage as stowaways, breeding in the containers"),SRCQ("The Columbian Exchange - seminar page (Department of History, University of Warwick)","https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/arts/history/students/modules/hi34i/seminars/columbianexchange/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Yellow Fever, Malaria and Development: Atlantic Africa and the New World, 1647-1928")]}]},
 {k:"d-americas",h:"The Americas",intro:"After 1492 the Indigenous peoples of the Americas suffered one of the largest population collapses in human history. Disease was the main killer, but war, forced labour, hunger and displacement made it far worse.",points:[
  {h:"The scale of the collapse",text:"A 2019 UCL study pooled 119 regional estimates. It put the population before 1492 at about 60.5 million and found that about 90% died within a century.",figure:"About 60.5 million before 1492 (range 44.8-78.2 million); about 90% died",year:"1492-1600",disputed:true,note:"Estimates of the pre-1492 population vary widely between scholars.",src:[SRCQ("Koch, Brierley, Maslin & Lewis (2019) Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492, Quaternary Science Reviews (White Rose Research Online record)","https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/154711/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","From 119 published regional population estimates we calculate a pre-1492 CE population of 60.5 million"),SRCQ("Koch, Brierley, Maslin & Lewis (2019) Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492, Quaternary Science Reviews (White Rose Research Online record)","https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/154711/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","European epidemics removed 90% (IQR 87–92%) of the indigenous population over the next century.")]},
  {h:"\"The Great Dying\" and a cooler climate",text:"The same study estimates that European epidemics removed about 90% of an estimated 60.5 million people within a century of 1492. UCL puts the deaths at about 55 million; press coverage rounded this to 56 million. The study links the loss to a dip in atmospheric carbon dioxide: abandoned farmland returned to forest and drew down carbon, which contributed to the cooling known as the \"Little Ice Age\".",figure:"About 56 million deaths by 1600; CO2 fell 7-10 ppm",year:"1600",disputed:true,note:"The authors attribute 47-67% of the carbon dioxide fall to the Americas; natural causes also played a part. The 56 million figure is from press coverage of the study.",src:[SRCQ("UCL Geography news (8 February 2019): The 'Great Dying' of 1492 led to lower CO2 and a cooler planet","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/geography/news/2019/feb/great-dying-1492-led-lower-co2-and-cooler-planet","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","calculated that 55 million indigenous people died after Europeans spread epidemics, warfare and famines across the Americas in the 1500s"),SRCQ("European colonization of Americas killed so many it cooled Earth's climate (eceee news summary of The Guardian, 31 January 2019)","https://www.eceee.org/all-news/news/european-colonization-of-americas-killed-so-many-it-cooled-earths-climate/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","causing deaths of around 56 million by 1600"),SRCQ("Koch, Brierley, Maslin & Lewis (2019) Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492, Quaternary Science Reviews (White Rose Research Online record)","https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/154711/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","47–67% of the atmospheric CO2 decline"),SRCQ("European colonization of Americas killed so many it cooled Earth's climate (eceee news summary of The Guardian, 31 January 2019)","https://www.eceee.org/all-news/news/european-colonization-of-americas-killed-so-many-it-cooled-earths-climate/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The drop in temperature during this period is known as the “Little Ice Age”")]},
  {h:"Smallpox in Tenochtitlan, 1520",text:"Smallpox arrived in Mexico in 1520 with a Spanish expedition. It killed perhaps a third to a half of the population, including Moctezuma's successor, weakening the Mexica just as they resisted the Spanish.",figure:"One-third to one-half of the population",year:"1520",disputed:true,note:"Some accounts name the carrier as Francisco de Eguía and describe him as an enslaved African; that description is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Aztec Empire smallpox epidemic (EBSCO Research Starters)","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/aztec-empire-smallpox-epidemic","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","resulting in devastating fatalities estimated to be between one-third and one-half of the population"),SRCQ("Aztec Empire smallpox epidemic (EBSCO Research Starters)","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/aztec-empire-smallpox-epidemic","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","emperor named to replace Moctezuma died of smallpox"),SRCQ("Aztec Empire smallpox epidemic (EBSCO Research Starters)","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/aztec-empire-smallpox-epidemic","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","A soldier who had an active case of smallpox came with the expedition to arrest Cortés.")]},
  {h:"Cocoliztli: the great epidemics of 1545 and 1576",text:"Two waves of a disease called cocoliztli (\"pest\" in Nahuatl) swept Mexico. The 1545-1548 wave alone may have killed 5 to 15 million people.",figure:"5-15 million deaths; up to 80% of the Indigenous population (1545-1548)",year:"1545",disputed:true,note:"The cause is debated; see next point.",src:[SRCQ("Acuna-Soto et al. (2002) Megadrought and Megadeath in 16th Century Mexico, Emerging Infectious Diseases 8(4), CDC","https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/8/4/01-0175_article","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","killed an estimated 5 million to 15 million people, or up to 80% of the native population")]},
  {h:"Cocoliztli: Salmonella or an Indigenous fever?",text:"In 2002 researchers argued cocoliztli was a native haemorrhagic fever made worse by severe drought. In 2018 ancient DNA from victims in Oaxaca found Salmonella Paratyphi C. The researchers themselves called it a possible cause.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"The DNA came from one burial site; it does not rule out other pathogens.",src:[SRCQ("Acuna-Soto et al. (2002) Megadrought and Megadeath in 16th Century Mexico, Emerging Infectious Diseases 8(4), CDC","https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/8/4/01-0175_article","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","These symptoms are not consistent with known European or African diseases present in Mexico during the 16th century."),SRCQ("Possible cause of early colonial-era Mexican epidemic identified (University of Tübingen, 2018)","https://uni-tuebingen.de/en/newsfullview-landingpage/article/possible-cause-of-early-colonial-era-mexican-epidemic-identified/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","identify Salmonella enterica Paratyphi C, a pathogen that causes enteric fever, in the skeletons of victims of the 1545-1550 cocoliztli epidemic"),SRCQ("Possible cause of early colonial-era Mexican epidemic identified (Max Planck Society, 19 January 2018)","https://www.mpg.de/11884269/possible-cause-of-early-colonial-era-mexican-epidemic-identified","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","may be the long-debated cause of the 1545-1550 AD")]},
  {h:"The Taíno: collapse on Hispaniola",text:"The Taíno population of the Caribbean fell sharply within a generation of 1492. Forced labour, hunger, disease and violence all played a part.",figure:"Some 85% lost by the early 1500s (contested extrapolation)",year:"1492-1520s",disputed:true,note:"Pre-contact numbers are uncertain; some scholars estimate over three million on Hispaniola.",src:[SRCQ("What Became of the Taíno? (Smithsonian Magazine article by Robert M. Poole, 2011, as reproduced by the CARICOM Reparations Commission)","https://caricomreparations.org/what-became-of-the-taino/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","some 85 percent of the Taíno population—had vanished by the early 1500s, according to a controversial extrapolation from Spanish records"),SRCQ("What Became of the Taíno? (Smithsonian Magazine article by Robert M. Poole, 2011, as reproduced by the CARICOM Reparations Commission)","https://caricomreparations.org/what-became-of-the-taino/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","They began to starve; many thousands fell prey to smallpox, measles and other European diseases")]},
  {h:"The encomienda system",text:"Under the encomienda, Spanish settlers were granted the labour of Indigenous people. In practice it was a system of forced labour and exploitation on Hispaniola.",figure:"",year:"1500s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies (Magdalen College, University of Oxford, Old Library blog, 28 October 2020)","https://www.magd.ox.ac.uk/blog/short-account-of-the-destruction-of-the-indies/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","This system enslaved the native population and distributed them amongst the conquistadors"),SRCQ("What Became of the Taíno? (Smithsonian Magazine article by Robert M. Poole, 2011, as reproduced by the CARICOM Reparations Commission)","https://caricomreparations.org/what-became-of-the-taino/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Spaniards removed men from villages to work in gold mines and colonial plantations.")]},
  {h:"Las Casas's testimony",text:"Bartolomé de las Casas, a Spanish friar who reached Hispaniola in 1502, became a fierce critic of Spanish cruelty. His figures are polemical and not precise counts.",figure:"",year:"1542",disputed:true,note:"Historians treat Las Casas's numbers as testimony, not census data.",src:[SRCQ("Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies (Magdalen College, University of Oxford, Old Library blog, 28 October 2020)","https://www.magd.ox.ac.uk/blog/short-account-of-the-destruction-of-the-indies/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","travelling to the island of Hispaniola in 1502"),SRCQ("Bartolomé de las Casas, Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1542; excerpts, National Humanities Center primary source set)","https://nationalhumanitiescenter.org/pds/amerbegin/contact/text7/casas_destruction.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","of the above three millions souls that we once saw, today there be no more than two hundred of those native people remaining")]},
  {h:"Were the Taíno \"extinct\"?",text:"No. A 2018 ancient-genome study found Taíno-related ancestry in present-day Puerto Ricans. Many Caribbean people have long claimed Taíno heritage.",figure:"10-15% Native American ancestry in a sample of 104 Puerto Ricans",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"Genetic ancestry is not the same as cultural or community identity.",src:[SRCQ("Traces of indigenous Taíno found in present-day Caribbean populations (University of Cambridge press release on Schroeder et al., PNAS 2018, republished by Brewminate)","https://brewminate.com/traces-of-indigenous-taino-found-in-present-day-caribbean-populations/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","despite some historical narratives that inaccurately brand them “extinct”"),SRCQ("Traces of indigenous Taíno found in present-day Caribbean populations (University of Cambridge press release on Schroeder et al., PNAS 2018, republished by Brewminate)","https://brewminate.com/traces-of-indigenous-taino-found-in-present-day-caribbean-populations/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","who have long claimed indigenous Taíno heritage"),SRCQ("Traces of indigenous Taíno found in present-day Caribbean populations (University of Cambridge press release on Schroeder et al., PNAS 2018, republished by Brewminate)","https://brewminate.com/traces-of-indigenous-taino-found-in-present-day-caribbean-populations/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The 10-15% of Native American ancestry in this group was shown to be closely related to the ancient Bahamian genome.")]},
  {h:"The Beothuk of Newfoundland",text:"European settlement cut the Beothuk off from food. Tuberculosis, other diseases and violence reduced their numbers. Shanawdithit, often called the last Beothuk, died in 1829.",figure:"",year:"1829",disputed:true,note:"Mi'kmaq oral histories say Beothuk people married into other Indigenous nations.",src:[SRCQ("Shawnadithit (The Canadian Encyclopedia)","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/shawnadithit","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Beothuk’s access to food and traditional resources was reduced."),SRCQ("Shawnadithit (The Canadian Encyclopedia)","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/shawnadithit","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","So too did conflict."),SRCQ("Shawnadithit (The Canadian Encyclopedia)","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/shawnadithit","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Tuberculosis , and other diseases brought by the Europeans to North America, significantly reduced the Beothuk population."),SRCQ("Shawnadithit (The Canadian Encyclopedia)","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/shawnadithit","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","died 6 June 1829"),SRCQ("Shawnadithit (The Canadian Encyclopedia)","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/shawnadithit","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","While it is popularly believed that Shawnadithit was the last Beothuk, Mi’kmaq oral histories reject that claim.")]},
  {h:"Enslaved Africans brought in as Indigenous people died",text:"As the Indigenous labour force collapsed, Spanish colonists imported enslaved Africans to work the mines and plantations.",figure:"",year:"1500s",disputed:false,note:"Quoted from Tucker via a secondary website.",src:[SRCQ("Smallpox Comes to the Americas (1507-1524) (Indigenous Mexico, secondary website citing Jonathan B. Tucker)","https://www.indigenousmexico.org/articles/smallpox-comes-to-the-americas-1507-1524","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The resulting high death rates depleted the indigenous labor pool, leading the Spanish to import slaves from West Africa as replacements.")]}]},
 {k:"d-africa",h:"Africa",intro:"In Africa, colonial conquest spread old and new diseases, destroyed herds and food supplies, and in some places was accompanied by mass killing. Disease also limited European settlement for centuries.",points:[
  {h:"Smallpox at the Cape, 1713",text:"Smallpox reached the Cape on a Dutch ship's laundry in 1713. The Khoikhoi and San suffered terrible losses.",figure:"",year:"1713",disputed:false,note:"Precise death tolls are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Smallpox Epidemic Strikes at the Cape, 13 February 1713 (South African History Online)","https://sahistory.org.za/node/10850","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","On 13 February 1713 something as unlikely as the dirty linen of the crew of a Dutch ship"),SRCQ("Smallpox Epidemic Strikes at the Cape, 13 February 1713 (South African History Online)","https://sahistory.org.za/node/10850","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","They appear to have much less resistance to the disease than the slaves and the colonists have and suffer huge losses to their numbers.")]},
  {h:"Sleeping sickness in Uganda, 1901-1905",text:"A severe epidemic broke out around Lake Victoria in 1901. Historians link the wider epidemics to the trade and movement that colonial rule set in motion.",figure:"Over 200,000 deaths, one-third of the regional population, by 1905",year:"1901",disputed:true,note:"The parasite responsible for the Uganda epidemic has been questioned by later researchers.",src:[SRCQ("Headrick (2014) Sleeping Sickness Epidemics and Colonial Responses in East and Central Africa, 1900-1940, PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases (PMC)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3998934/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1905 over 200,000 inhabitants, one-third of the population of the region, had died of the disease."),SRCQ("Headrick (2014) Sleeping Sickness Epidemics and Colonial Responses in East and Central Africa, 1900-1940, PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases (PMC)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3998934/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","flared into epidemics as the expansion of colonial rule increased trade and migrations throughout Africa")]},
  {h:"Sleeping sickness in the Congo basin",text:"Cases grew along the Sangha and Ubangi rivers in the 1890s and spread through the Congo region. Roger Casement saw it as one cause of population loss.",figure:"",year:"1890s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Headrick (2014) Sleeping Sickness Epidemics and Colonial Responses in East and Central Africa, 1900-1940, PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases (PMC)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3998934/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","increasing numbers along the Sangha and Ubangi rivers in the 1890s"),SRCQ("Report of the British Consul, Roger Casement, on the Administration of the Congo Free State (1904; excerpts, Vancouver Island University course page)","https://web.viu.ca/davies/H479B.Imperialism.Nationalism/Br.report.Congo.atrocities.1904.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a prominent place must be assigned to this malady")]},
  {h:"Rinderpest, 1887-1897",text:"Rinderpest arrived in 1887 with cattle shipped from India for Italian troops at Massawa. It swept the continent, killed most cattle and brought famine. There is no evidence it was spread on purpose.",figure:"About 90% of Africa's cattle in a decade; perhaps a third of Ethiopians dead by 1892",year:"1887",disputed:true,note:"The one-third figure for Ethiopia is described by the source as an estimate from \"some sources\".",src:[SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1887, an Italian ship docked at the Red Sea port of Massawa"),SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a pathogen that once killed 90% of the cattle on the African continent in a single decade"),SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1892, some sources estimate that as much as one-third of the population of Ethiopia had died of starvation"),SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","There is no evidence that the Italian would-be colonisers smuggled in the disease on purpose")]},
  {h:"The Congo Free State and the Casement Report",text:"Under King Leopold II's rule, people were forced to gather rubber under threat of violence. Roger Casement's 1904 report described villages emptied since his previous visit in 1887.",figure:"",year:"1904",disputed:true,note:"Overall population-loss estimates for the Congo Free State vary widely between historians; no figure is confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Report of the British Consul, Roger Casement, on the Administration of the Congo Free State (1904; excerpts, Vancouver Island University course page)","https://web.viu.ca/davies/H479B.Imperialism.Nationalism/Br.report.Congo.atrocities.1904.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The colonial regime of the Belgian King Leopold II"),SRCQ("Casement in the Congo Free State, exhibition panel 4 (National Museum of Ireland)","https://source.museum.ie/getmedia/bbc5ec22-153c-4051-8cef-9ec710f3b635/Panel-4-Casement-in-the-Congo-Free-State.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","punishment inflicted on men forced to collect rubber by sentries of the Anglo-Belgian India Rubber Company"),SRCQ("Roger Casement - voice of the voiceless (History Ireland)","https://www.historyireland.com/?p=24399","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","in comparison with a previous trip in 1887 he found a diminished and brutalised population. His report was published in 1904"),SRCQ("Report of the British Consul, Roger Casement, on the Administration of the Congo Free State (1904; excerpts, Vancouver Island University course page)","https://web.viu.ca/davies/H479B.Imperialism.Nationalism/Br.report.Congo.atrocities.1904.htm","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Communities I had formerly known as large and flourishing centers of population are to-day entirely gone"),SRCQ("Roger Casement - voice of the voiceless (History Ireland)","https://www.historyireland.com/?p=24399","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The cause of the atrocities was uncontrolled greed for rubber")]},
  {h:"Herero and Nama, 1904–1908: recognised by Germany as genocide in 2021",text:"German forces in what is now Namibia drove Herero people into the desert and cut them off from water. Survivors were held in camps. According to the Kupferberg Holocaust Center (CUNY), up to 80,000 Herero (about 80%) and 10,000 Nama (about 50%) died. The German government has officially called these events a genocide since 28 May 2021.",figure:"Up to 80,000 Herero (about 80%) and 10,000 Nama (about 50%)",year:"1904",disputed:false,note:"Figures from the Kupferberg Holocaust Center (CUNY), which calls it the first genocide of the 20th century.",src:[SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","directing troops to kill all Herero men and to drive women and children into the desert"),SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","German forces shot refugees, poisoned wells, and cut off access to water"),SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1908, German colonial forces had murdered as many as 80,000 Herero, roughly 80 percent of their population, and 10,000 Nama, roughly 50 percent")]},
  {h:"Shark Island camp",text:"The camp on Shark Island, off the Namibian coast, held Herero and Nama prisoners from 1905 to 1907. Beatings, hunger and disease killed many.",figure:"About 1,000-3,000 deaths (Wiener Holocaust Library estimate)",year:"1905",disputed:true,note:"Death-rate estimates for Shark Island vary between sources; higher figures are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Shark Island Concentration Camp (The Holocaust Explained, The Wiener Holocaust Library)","https://theholocaustexplained.org/?p=6758","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The camp was opened by the German colonial forces in 1905"),SRCQ("Shark Island Concentration Camp (The Holocaust Explained, The Wiener Holocaust Library)","https://theholocaustexplained.org/?p=6758","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","It closed in April 1907. In total, approximately 1000-3000 people died in the camp."),SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The most notorious was Shark Island, off the coast of Namibia, where both Herero and Nama prisoners were held.")]},
  {h:"Germany's recognition, 2021",text:"On 28 May 2021 Germany's Foreign Minister, Heiko Maas, said Germany would officially call these events a genocide and ask forgiveness. Germany pledged 1.1 billion euro but said no legal claim for compensation follows from it.",figure:"1.1 billion euro programme",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The term \"genocide\" here is the German government's own, from 28 May 2021.",src:[SRCQ("Foreign Minister Maas on the conclusion of negotiations with Namibia (German Federal Foreign Office, 28 May 2021)","https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/-/2463598","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","We will now officially call these events what they are from today’s perspective: a genocide."),SRCQ("Foreign Minister Maas on the conclusion of negotiations with Namibia (German Federal Foreign Office, 28 May 2021)","https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/-/2463598","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a substantial programme to the tune of 1.1 billion euro"),SRCQ("Foreign Minister Maas on the conclusion of negotiations with Namibia (German Federal Foreign Office, 28 May 2021)","https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/-/2463598","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Legal claims for compensation cannot be derived from it.")]},
  {h:"\"The white man's grave\" and quinine",text:"Disease, above all malaria, killed so many Europeans in West Africa that the coast became known as \"the white man's grave\", a phrase examined by the historian Philip Curtin. From the mid-19th century, regular quinine doses changed this. In 1854 Baikie's Niger expedition returned without a single death.",figure:"",year:"1854",disputed:false,note:"This disease barrier slowed European settlement; it did not protect Africans from the slave trade.",src:[SRCQ("Philip D. Curtin, \"The White Man's Grave\": Image and Reality, 1780-1850, Journal of British Studies 1(1) (Cambridge Core)","https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-british-studies/article/white-mans-grave-image-and-reality-17801850/3D5A35C18B1B4D2A4956641007FB4572","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Image and Reality, 1780-1850"),SRCQ("\"The forgotten man of Africa\" (British Society for the History of Medicine, Lee Coppack, 14 May 2024)","https://bshm.org.uk/?p=18422","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1847 Dr Alexander Bryson, an Assistant Surgeon in the West Africa Squadron, presented the Admiralty with a report"),SRCQ("\"The forgotten man of Africa\" (British Society for the History of Medicine, Lee Coppack, 14 May 2024)","https://bshm.org.uk/?p=18422","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","for the first time in the history of African exploration, they had completed the mission without the loss of a single life")]}]},
 {k:"d-passage",h:"The Middle Passage and the plantations",intro:"Captives died in large numbers on the voyage across the Atlantic and after arrival. Conditions on Caribbean sugar plantations were so harsh that enslaved populations often fell rather than grew.",points:[
  {h:"Deaths on the Middle Passage fell over time",text:"Most historians find that death rates on slave ships fell between the 17th and early 19th centuries, though data are patchy and one study of the Dutch trade found no trend.",figure:"",year:"1600s-1800s",disputed:true,note:"SlaveVoyages percentages by period are not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Deaths of Slaves in the Middle Passage, Journal of Economic History 45(3), 1985 (abstract, Cambridge Core)","https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/abs/deaths-of-slaves-in-the-middle-passage/29A728FC6E348ACECB6EF98BAB480BD3","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","slave mortality during the Middle Passage fell between the seventeenth and early nineteenth centuries"),SRCQ("Deaths of Slaves in the Middle Passage, Journal of Economic History 45(3), 1985 (abstract, Cambridge Core)","https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/abs/deaths-of-slaves-in-the-middle-passage/29A728FC6E348ACECB6EF98BAB480BD3","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","no discernible trend toward decrease or increase in the overall pattern")]},
  {h:"Death rates by period",text:"One large study measured deaths per month at sea, per 1,000 captives. The average fell from about 98 (1676-1700) to about 46 (1776-1800). It rose again in the illegal trade after 1850 (about 197, from only 20 voyages).",figure:"Monthly deaths per 1,000: 97.6 (1676-1700); 46.0 (1776-1800); 60.5 (1826-1850); 197.4 (1851-1864)",year:"1676-1864",disputed:false,note:"These are monthly rates, not the share of captives who died on a whole voyage.",src:[SRCQ("Klein, Engerman, Haines & Shlomowitz (2001) Transoceanic Mortality: The Slave Trade in Comparative Perspective, William and Mary Quarterly, supplement Table A.1","https://oieahc.wm.edu/wp-content/uploads/klein_table1.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Death rates per month were calculated for each voyage and the mean was taken of the distribution of death rates per month."),SRCQ("Klein, Engerman, Haines & Shlomowitz (2001) Transoceanic Mortality: The Slave Trade in Comparative Perspective, William and Mary Quarterly, supplement Table A.1","https://oieahc.wm.edu/wp-content/uploads/klein_table1.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","1676-1700 105 77.7 46 85.81 339.4 300 178.19 73 74 27.71 97.63"),SRCQ("Klein, Engerman, Haines & Shlomowitz (2001) Transoceanic Mortality: The Slave Trade in Comparative Perspective, William and Mary Quarterly, supplement Table A.1","https://oieahc.wm.edu/wp-content/uploads/klein_table1.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","1776-1800 650 27.4 12 40.19 332.3 324.5 138.06 60.8 58 22.25 45.98"),SRCQ("Klein, Engerman, Haines & Shlomowitz (2001) Transoceanic Mortality: The Slave Trade in Comparative Perspective, William and Mary Quarterly, supplement Table A.1","https://oieahc.wm.edu/wp-content/uploads/klein_table1.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","1826-1850 491 32.9 19 43.4 427.3 426 171.83 39.8 35 14.88 60.47"),SRCQ("Klein, Engerman, Haines & Shlomowitz (2001) Transoceanic Mortality: The Slave Trade in Comparative Perspective, William and Mary Quarterly, supplement Table A.1","https://oieahc.wm.edu/wp-content/uploads/klein_table1.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","1851-1864 20 194.3 139 227.6 738 656.5 310.86 47.1 46 17.11 197.39")]},
  {h:"Caribbean plantations: populations shrank",text:"Richard Dunn compared a Jamaican sugar estate with a Virginia plantation. Mesopotamia in Jamaica lost population while Mount Airy in Virginia grew.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("A Tale of Two Plantations: a comparative approach to Caribbean and US slavery, Richard S. Dunn (Case Western Reserve University, Baker-Nord Center event page)","https://case.edu/artsci/bakernord/events/past-events/2015-2016/tale-two-plantations-comparative-approach-caribbean-and-us-slavery","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","drastic population loss at Mesopotamia and vigorous population growth at Mount Airy")]}]},
 {k:"d-oceania",h:"Oceania and Asia",intro:"In Australia, New Zealand and the Pacific islands, newly introduced diseases, violence and land loss caused steep population falls in the 18th and 19th centuries. Coverage of Asia here is limited to the Black Death.",points:[
  {h:"Smallpox around Sydney, 1789",text:"Smallpox broke out among Aboriginal people around Sydney in April 1789, the year after the British arrived. Where it came from is debated.",figure:"",year:"1789",disputed:true,note:"One theory says Macassan traders in the north brought it earlier. Most researchers think First Nations people had no earlier exposure; whether the British fleet brought it is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Huang & Bashford (2024) Smallpox Geographies: vaccination, borders and Indigenous peoples in Australia's coastal north (open access, Europe PMC)","https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11046008","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Most historical and epidemiological researchers, however, still understand First Nations people on the Australian continent to have had no prior exposure to smallpox"),SRCQ("Huang & Bashford (2024) Smallpox Geographies: vaccination, borders and Indigenous peoples in Australia's coastal north (open access, Europe PMC)","https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11046008","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","mortality and morbidity beginning in April 1789, the year after the British arrived in Sydney Cove"),SRCQ("Huang & Bashford (2024) Smallpox Geographies: vaccination, borders and Indigenous peoples in Australia's coastal north (open access, Europe PMC)","https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11046008","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","One thesis argues that smallpox pre-existed British colonisation in 1788")]},
  {h:"Tasmania and Truganini",text:"Truganini (Trukanini), a Nuennone woman who died in 1876, lost her mother, sister and intended husband to colonial violence. She was wrongly called \"the last Tasmanian\".",figure:"",year:"1876",disputed:false,note:"Wider frontier violence across Australia is not quantified by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Aborigines, the last of the race, Tasmania, c. 1866 (National Portrait Gallery of Australia)","https://www.portrait.gov.au/portraits/2006.31","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Trukanini (c. 1812–1876) is arguably nineteenth century Australia’s most celebrated Indigenous leader."),SRCQ("Aborigines, the last of the race, Tasmania, c. 1866 (National Portrait Gallery of Australia)","https://www.portrait.gov.au/portraits/2006.31","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","all as a result of white violence")]},
  {h:"Tasmanian Aboriginal people today",text:"Tasmanian Aboriginal people did not die out. Descendants of Truganini's contemporaries live in Tasmania today.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"AIATSIS was not reachable to check; the National Portrait Gallery is cited.",src:[SRCQ("Aborigines, the last of the race, Tasmania, c. 1866 (National Portrait Gallery of Australia)","https://www.portrait.gov.au/portraits/2006.31","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a false notion attested to by the many descendants of her contemporaries still living in Tasmania today")]},
  {h:"Hawaii after 1778",text:"One estimate puts the Native Hawaiian population at 683,000 when Cook arrived in 1778. Other estimates range from 200,000 to 1 million. Introduced diseases caused steep falls over the next 60 years.",figure:"Down 48% by 1800, 71% by 1820, 84% by 1840 (Swanson's estimate)",year:"1778",disputed:true,note:"The pre-contact estimate is highly disputed.",src:[SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","estimates that there were 683,000 Native Hawaiians on the island in 1778"),SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","which have ranged from as low as 200,000 to as high as 1 million"),SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1800, the population had declined by 48% since Cook set foot on Hawaii."),SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1820, it had declined 71%; by 1840, it declined 84%."),SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Over the years, many other infectious diseases and illnesses such as measles, chicken pox, polio and tuberculosis killed thousands of Hawaiians.")]},
  {h:"Māori population decline",text:"The Māori population fell from about 100,000 in 1769 to about 42,000 in 1896. Introduced diseases were the main cause, more than warfare. The fall sped up after 1840 as settlers arrived.",figure:"About 100,000 (1769) to about 42,000 (1896)",year:"1896",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Epidemics and Pandemics: Impact on Māori (National Library of New Zealand Services to Schools, DigitalNZ; quotes Te Ara)","https://digitalnz.org/stories/5e83d713c56cf9000bcf2cd2","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","It is estimated to have been about 100,000 in 1769."),SRCQ("Epidemics and Pandemics: Impact on Māori (National Library of New Zealand Services to Schools, DigitalNZ; quotes Te Ara)","https://digitalnz.org/stories/5e83d713c56cf9000bcf2cd2","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","At its lowest point in 1896 it was around 42,000."),SRCQ("Epidemics and Pandemics: Impact on Māori (National Library of New Zealand Services to Schools, DigitalNZ; quotes Te Ara)","https://digitalnz.org/stories/5e83d713c56cf9000bcf2cd2","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Introduced diseases were the major reason for the Māori population decrease."),SRCQ("Epidemics and Pandemics: Impact on Māori (National Library of New Zealand Services to Schools, DigitalNZ; quotes Te Ara)","https://digitalnz.org/stories/5e83d713c56cf9000bcf2cd2","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","war deaths were not great in number compared with the deaths from other causes"),SRCQ("Epidemics and Pandemics: Impact on Māori (National Library of New Zealand Services to Schools, DigitalNZ; quotes Te Ara)","https://digitalnz.org/stories/5e83d713c56cf9000bcf2cd2","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Decline accelerated after the Treaty of Waitangi was signed in 1840")]},
  {h:"The Pacific labour trade (\"blackbirding\")",text:"Pacific islanders, such as Solomon Islanders, were recruited or taken to work in the sugar fields of Fiji and Queensland. Many died.",figure:"",year:"19th century",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health (PDF)","https://jmvh.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/Pacific-Island-Societies.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The necessity to import other island peoples such as Solomon Islanders through contract labour ships (black-birders) also resulted in mass mortality")]},
  {h:"Measles in Fiji, 1875",text:"Measles arrived in January 1875 on HMS Dido, carrying the chief Cakobau back from Sydney. It killed a fifth to a quarter of Fijians in five months, and almost all the chiefs.",figure:"One-fifth to one-quarter of the population",year:"1875",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health (PDF)","https://jmvh.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/Pacific-Island-Societies.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","From February to June 1875 measles swept all of Fiji killing an estimated one-fifth to one-fourth of the general population."),SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health (PDF)","https://jmvh.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/Pacific-Island-Societies.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","All or nearly all (records are uncertain) of the 69 chiefs died"),SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health (PDF)","https://jmvh.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/Pacific-Island-Societies.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In January 1875 the warship HMS Dido returned from Sydney carrying the chief’s group")]},
  {h:"Pacific islands exposed from the 18th century",text:"Many isolated Pacific islands lost large parts of their populations when new infections first reached them.",figure:"",year:"18th century",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health","https://jmvh.org/article/pacific-island-societies-destabilised-by-infectious-diseases/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Infectious diseases de-populated many isolated Pacific islands when they were first exposed to global pathogen circulation from the 18th century.")]},
  {h:"Asia: the Black Death in the Middle East and Central Asia",text:"Plague also struck Central Asia and Iraq in the 14th century. Research on mortality there is still being developed.",figure:"",year:"14th century",disputed:false,note:"Colonial-era epidemics in South and South-East Asia are not covered by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("Stuart Borsch, The Black Death in the Middle East and Central Asia (Islamic Law Blog, Harvard Law School Program in Islamic Law, 29 May 2020)","https://islamiclaw.blog/2020/05/29/stuart-borsch/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","new data for the Black Death’s mortality in Samarqand, Khurasan, Iraq, Andalusia, and North Africa")]}]},
 {k:"d-conditions",h:"Conditions: why \"germs alone\" is not enough",intro:"Disease did not strike in a vacuum. Hunger, forced labour, displacement, war and land loss left people weaker and less able to care for the sick.",points:[
  {h:"Virgin Soils Revisited",text:"The historian David S. Jones argued in 2003 that what happened to each Indigenous population depended on its circumstances, not only on a lack of immunity.",figure:"",year:"2003",disputed:true,note:"Jones challenges the \"virgin soil\" explanation; others still stress immunity.",src:[SRCQ("David S. Jones (2003) Virgin Soils Revisited, William and Mary Quarterly 60(4) (PDF via MITx course)","https://courses.edx.org/asset-v1:MITx+21H.899x+3T2020+type%40asset+block%40VirginSoilWMQ.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the fates of individual populations depended on contingent factors of their physical, economic, social, and political environments"),SRCQ("Virgin Soils Revisited - excerpt (Gladue Rights Research Database, University of Saskatchewan)","https://gladue.usask.ca/index.php/node/6164","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","one of the most shocking demographic events of the last millennium")]},
  {h:"Poverty and stress drive epidemics",text:"Jones suggests Indigenous epidemics were driven by the same forces that drive epidemics everywhere.",figure:"",year:"2003",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("David S. Jones (2003) Virgin Soils Revisited, William and Mary Quarterly 60(4) (PDF via MITx course)","https://courses.edx.org/asset-v1:MITx+21H.899x+3T2020+type%40asset+block%40VirginSoilWMQ.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","were caused by the same forces of poverty, social stress, and environmental vulnerability that cause epidemics in all other times and places")]},
  {h:"Hunger and disease feed each other",text:"Epidemics stopped people working their land, which caused famine, which left survivors weaker against the next disease.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Colin Calloway, quoted by Jones.",src:[SRCQ("David S. Jones (2003) Virgin Soils Revisited, William and Mary Quarterly 60(4) (PDF via MITx course)","https://courses.edx.org/asset-v1:MITx+21H.899x+3T2020+type%40asset+block%40VirginSoilWMQ.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","cut down economic productivity, generating hunger and famine, which rendered those who survived one disease more vulnerable to affliction by the next")]},
  {h:"\"No immunity\" hides too much",text:"Jones warns that saying Indigenous people had \"no immunity\" blurs very different causes.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("David S. Jones (2003) Virgin Soils Revisited, William and Mary Quarterly 60(4) (PDF via MITx course)","https://courses.edx.org/asset-v1:MITx+21H.899x+3T2020+type%40asset+block%40VirginSoilWMQ.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","These claims obscure crucial distinctions between different mechanisms that might have left American Indians vulnerable.")]},
  {h:"Drought and disease in Mexico",text:"The cocoliztli epidemics coincided with the worst drought in 500 years, which magnified their effect.",figure:"",year:"1545",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acuna-Soto et al. (2002) Megadrought and Megadeath in 16th Century Mexico, Emerging Infectious Diseases 8(4), CDC","https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/8/4/01-0175_article","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","These droughts appear to have interacted with ecologic and sociologic conditions, magnifying the human impact of infectious disease")]},
  {h:"Famine used to force labour",text:"After rinderpest and famine, some mine owners in Southern Africa saw hunger as a way to push down wages.",figure:"",year:"1890s",disputed:false,note:"Quote attributed by the source to a mine managers' association president.",src:[SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","urged colleagues to drop wages and extend hours"),SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the natives have to work because they cannot obtain food otherwise")]},
  {h:"Camps as a system",text:"In Namibia, the camps combined beatings, hunger, disease and sexual violence.",figure:"",year:"1904-1908",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Beatings, starvation, disease, sexual violence, and forced restraint formed a deliberate system of subjugation and exploitation")]},
  {h:"Loss of leaders",text:"When epidemics killed chiefs and elders, communities lost their leaders at the moment they were most needed.",figure:"",year:"1875",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health (PDF)","https://jmvh.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/Pacific-Island-Societies.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The destabilisation caused by the sudden death of island leadership has had profound social and political implications")]}]},
 {k:"d-words",h:"Words and limits",intro:"How we describe these events matters. Numbers are uncertain, terms carry history, and many peoples once called \"extinct\" are still here.",points:[
  {h:"\"Extinct\" is often wrong",text:"Peoples described as extinct, including the Taíno, Beothuk and Tasmanian Aboriginal people, have living descendants or living communities with oral histories of survival.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"See the Taíno, Beothuk and Tasmania points.",src:[SRCQ("Traces of indigenous Taíno found in present-day Caribbean populations (University of Cambridge press release on Schroeder et al., PNAS 2018, republished by Brewminate)","https://brewminate.com/traces-of-indigenous-taino-found-in-present-day-caribbean-populations/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","despite some historical narratives that inaccurately brand them “extinct”"),SRCQ("Aborigines, the last of the race, Tasmania, c. 1866 (National Portrait Gallery of Australia)","https://www.portrait.gov.au/portraits/2006.31","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a false notion attested to by the many descendants of her contemporaries still living in Tasmania today")]},
  {h:"Recovery as well as loss",text:"Native Hawaiians, for example, have grown in number again since their low point.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback")]},
  {h:"Numbers come with wide ranges",text:"Estimates for the Americas, Hawaii and Mexico span wide ranges. We give ranges and name who gives them.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","highly disputed historical estimates"),SRCQ("Koch, Brierley, Maslin & Lewis (2019) Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492, Quaternary Science Reviews (White Rose Research Online record)","https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/154711/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","interquartile range, IQR 44.8–78.2 million")]},
  {h:"\"Age of Discovery\" is a Eurocentric label",text:"Lands \"discovered\" by Europeans were already home to millions of people. Two South African history teachers, drawing on the journalist-historian Howard French (2021), argue that a Eurocentric narrative has sidelined Africa's role in this era.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"We keep the phrase in quotation marks.",src:[SRCQ("Brookbanks & Mbambo (2025) Reimaging, from a decolonised perspective, the Grade 10 intended curriculum..., Yesterday & Today No 33 (SciELO South Africa)","https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/yt/n33/15.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","A Eurocentric historical narrative means people in the West have long been conditioned to believe that Africa has little pre-modern history"),SRCQ("Brookbanks & Mbambo (2025) Reimaging, from a decolonised perspective, the Grade 10 intended curriculum..., Yesterday & Today No 33 (SciELO South Africa)","https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/yt/n33/15.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the so-termed ‘Age of Discovery’")]},
  {h:"Who says \"genocide\", and when",text:"Germany's government formally used \"genocide\" for the Herero and Nama killings on 28 May 2021. The Kupferberg Holocaust Center and the Wiener Holocaust Library also use the term. The 2019 UCL study uses \"Great Dying\" for the Americas, not \"genocide\".",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"We attribute the word to the speaker rather than applying it ourselves.",src:[SRCQ("Foreign Minister Maas on the conclusion of negotiations with Namibia (German Federal Foreign Office, 28 May 2021)","https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/-/2463598","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a genocide"),SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Germany perpetrated the first genocide of the 20th century"),SRCQ("Koch, Brierley, Maslin & Lewis (2019) Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492, Quaternary Science Reviews (White Rose Research Online record)","https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/154711/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Great Dying of the Indigenous Peoples of the Americas")]},
  {h:"Disease moved in more than one direction",text:"Germs travelled from Europe and Africa to the Americas and the Pacific, and possibly from the Americas to Europe (syphilis, debated).",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("European Exploration, Perception of the Other, and the Columbian Exchange (Oxford University Press, Backman, Cultures of the West, student resources)","https://learninglink.oup.com/access/content/history-in-practice-western-civ/backman3e-student-resources-volume-1-chapter-14-section-3-background","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the exchange of goods, people, animals, and pathogens across the Atlantic"),SRCQ("Did Columbus Bring Syphilis To Europe? Study Raises Doubts (Scripps News)","https://scrippsnews.com/science-and-tech/did-columbus-bring-syphilis-to-europe-study-raises-doubts","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","reopening an old debate")]},
  {h:"Disease is not the same as intent",text:"Some introductions were accidental, as the rinderpest source notes. That does not lessen the harm, or the responsibility for the conditions that made it worse.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","There is no evidence that the Italian would-be colonisers smuggled in the disease on purpose")]}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"The harm is well evidenced: about 18 million people were taken in four slave trades, and the areas that lost most are poorer today.",refs:["wl-scale-four-trades-together-about-18-million", "wl-economics-the-areas-that-exported-most-people-are-"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Present the 'what might have been' question as a debate: the harm is measurable, but the counterfactual cannot be tested.",refs:["wl-limits-the-counterfactual-is-not-testable", "wl-limits-the-harm-is-well-evidenced"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Museums and institutions can follow the Horniman and Jesus College in returning looted objects; the law limits what national museums can do.",refs:["wl-destruction-horniman-museum-returns-ownership", "wl-destruction-jesus-college-cambridge", "wl-destruction-calls-to-change-the-law"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Colonial narratives such as the 'civilising mission' and the Hamitic hypothesis shaped what was taught; correct them with the evidence.",refs:["wl-narratives-the-civilising-mission", "wl-narratives-the-hamitic-hypothesis", "wl-narratives-unesco-s-general-history-of-africa"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Compare like with like: 'more advanced' depends on the measure, and city sizes and population figures are estimates with wide ranges.",refs:["wl-limits-more-advanced-depends-on-the-measure", "wl-darkages-city-sizes-cairo"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"About 12.5 million people were taken across the Atlantic, and about 18 million across four slave trades.",refs:["wl-scale-transatlantic-trade-about-12-5-million-e", "wl-scale-four-trades-together-about-18-million"]},{t:"Research links the slave trades to lower incomes and lower trust in the areas most affected today.",refs:["wl-economics-the-areas-that-exported-most-people-are-", "wl-economics-lower-trust-today"]},{t:"Objects were looted, and evidence of African achievement was denied, as at Benin City and Great Zimbabwe.",refs:["wl-destruction-benin-city-1897", "wl-destruction-great-zimbabwe-denial-of-african-builder"]},{t:"Whether Africa would have become 'more advanced' cannot be tested; this page sets out both sides.",refs:["wl-limits-the-counterfactual-is-not-testable"]}]}],time:[{ys:300,y:"c. 300",t:"Ghana Empire established in West Africa (traditional dating c. 300).",src:[SRCQ("Ghana Empire (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana_Empire","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Ghana Empire غانا Wagadu واغادو c. 300– c. mid-13th century")]},{ys:500,y:"c. 500–1000",t:"Western Europe’s so-called “Dark Ages” in the narrow sense.",src:[SRCQ("Dark Ages (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/event/Dark-Ages","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","the period between about 500 and 1000")]},{ys:813,y:"813–833",t:"Baghdad’s House of Wisdom becomes a public academy under al-Ma’mun.",src:[SRCQ("House of Wisdom (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Wisdom","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","it was turned into a public academy and a library.")]},{ys:850,y:"9th century",t:"Bronze casting at Igbo-Ukwu (present-day Nigeria).",src:[SRCQ("Igbo-Ukwu (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Igbo-Ukwu","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a highly sophisticated bronze metal-working culture dating to the 9th century AD")]},{ys:1000,y:"c. 1000",t:"Córdoba estimated at about 450,000 people.",src:[SRCQ("List of largest European cities in history (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_European_cities_in_history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Córdoba Spain 160,000 200,000 450,000")]},{ys:1050,y:"11th–15th centuries",t:"Great Zimbabwe flourishes.",src:[SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe National Monument (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","are a unique testimony to the Bantu civilization of the Shona between the 11th and 15th centuries.")]},{ys:1258,y:"1258",t:"Mongols destroy Baghdad and the House of Wisdom.",src:[SRCQ("House of Wisdom (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Wisdom","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","It was destroyed in 1258 during the Mongol siege of Baghdad .")]},{ys:1324,y:"1324–1326",t:"Mansa Musa of Mali makes his pilgrimage to Mecca.",src:[SRCQ("Mali Empire (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mali_Empire","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","He made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca from 1324 to 1326")]},{ys:1325,y:"1325",t:"Cairo estimated at about 500,000 people (Chandler).",src:[SRCQ("List of largest cities throughout history (Wikipedia; Chandler 1987 column)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_cities_throughout_history","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Cairo Egypt 1325 500,000")]},{ys:1331,y:"1331–1332",t:"Ibn Battuta visits Kilwa.",src:[SRCQ("Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","described Kilwa as one of the most beautiful cities of the world.")]},{ys:1348,y:"14th c.",t:"Black Death in Egypt: a third to a half of the population dies (Dols)",src:[SRCQ("Stuart Borsch, The Black Death in the Middle East and Central Asia (Islamic Law Blog, Harvard Law School Program in Islamic Law, 29 May 2020)","https://islamiclaw.blog/2020/05/29/stuart-borsch/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","between one-third and one-half of the inhabitants of Egypt were carried off by this outbreak")]},{ys:1441,y:"1440s",t:"Skin colour first used to justify enslaving Africans (argument of Howard French, 2021)",src:[SRCQ("Brookbanks & Mbambo (2025) Reimaging, from a decolonised perspective, the Grade 10 intended curriculum..., Yesterday & Today No 33 (SciELO South Africa)","https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/yt/n33/15.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","was first mobilised in the 1440s")]},{ys:1502,y:"1502",t:"Las Casas arrives on Hispaniola",src:[SRCQ("Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies (Magdalen College, University of Oxford, Old Library blog, 28 October 2020)","https://www.magd.ox.ac.uk/blog/short-account-of-the-destruction-of-the-indies/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","travelling to the island of Hispaniola in 1502")]},{ys:1518,y:"1518",t:"Smallpox on Hispaniola",src:[SRCQ("Smallpox Comes to the Americas (1507-1524) (Indigenous Mexico, secondary website citing Jonathan B. Tucker)","https://www.indigenousmexico.org/articles/smallpox-comes-to-the-americas-1507-1524","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In December 1518, smallpox appeared again, initially among the enslaved Africans in the mines of Hispaniola.")]},{ys:1520,y:"1520",t:"Smallpox in Tenochtitlan",src:[SRCQ("Aztec Empire smallpox epidemic (EBSCO Research Starters)","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/aztec-empire-smallpox-epidemic","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","emperor named to replace Moctezuma died of smallpox")]},{ys:1525,y:"1525–1866",t:"Period of the transatlantic slave trade covered by the database.",src:[SRCQ("How Many Slaves Landed in the U.S.? (PBS, The African Americans: Many Rivers to Cross)","https://www.pbs.org/wnet/african-americans-many-rivers-to-cross/history/how-many-slaves-landed-in-the-us/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Between 1525 and 1866, in the entire history of the slave trade to the New World")]},{ys:1542,y:"1542",t:"Las Casas writes his Brief Account",src:[SRCQ("Bartolomé de las Casas, Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1542; excerpts, National Humanities Center primary source set)","https://nationalhumanitiescenter.org/pds/amerbegin/contact/text7/casas_destruction.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","of the above three millions souls that we once saw, today there be no more than two hundred of those native people remaining")]},{ys:1545,y:"1545-1548",t:"Cocoliztli epidemic in Mexico",src:[SRCQ("Acuna-Soto et al. (2002) Megadrought and Megadeath in 16th Century Mexico, Emerging Infectious Diseases 8(4), CDC","https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/8/4/01-0175_article","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","killed an estimated 5 million to 15 million people, or up to 80% of the native population")]},{ys:1647,y:"1647",t:"Yellow fever in Barbados and Yucatán",src:[SRCQ("Zika Virus, Yellow Fever, and the Mosquito Behind Them (We're History, 2 February 2016)","https://werehistory.org/zika/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The first unmistakable evidence of its presence came in 1647 when yellow fever struck on Barbados and the Yucatan Peninsula.")]},{ys:1713,y:"1713",t:"Smallpox at the Cape",src:[SRCQ("Smallpox Epidemic Strikes at the Cape, 13 February 1713 (South African History Online)","https://sahistory.org.za/node/10850","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Hardest hit are the indigenous Khoisan people.")]},{ys:1750,y:"1750",t:"Living standards in advanced parts of Europe and East Asia still comparable (Pomeranz).",src:[SRCQ("The Great Divergence (Princeton University Press)","https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691217185/the-great-divergence","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","as recently as 1750, life expectancy, consumption, and product and factor markets were comparable in Europe and East Asia.")]},{ys:1778,y:"1778",t:"Cook reaches Hawaii",src:[SRCQ("After 200 years, Native Hawaiians make a comeback (Pew Research Center, 6 April 2015)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2015/04/06/native-hawaiian-population/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","estimates that there were 683,000 Native Hawaiians on the island in 1778")]},{ys:1789,y:"1789",t:"Smallpox around Sydney",src:[SRCQ("Huang & Bashford (2024) Smallpox Geographies: vaccination, borders and Indigenous peoples in Australia's coastal north (open access, Europe PMC)","https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11046008","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","mortality and morbidity beginning in April 1789, the year after the British arrived in Sydney Cove")]},{ys:1829,y:"1829",t:"Death of Shanawdithit",src:[SRCQ("Shawnadithit (The Canadian Encyclopedia)","https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/shawnadithit","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","died 6 June 1829")]},{ys:1850,y:"1850",t:"Africa’s population estimated at half its counterfactual level (Manning, modelled).",src:[SRCQ("Nathan Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades, Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1), 2008 (author copy)","https://nathannunn.sites.olt.ubc.ca/files/2022/07/nathannunn-the-long-term-effect.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","by 1850 Africa’s population was only half of what it would have been had the slave trades not taken place")]},{ys:1854,y:"1854",t:"Baikie's quinine expedition up the Niger",src:[SRCQ("\"The forgotten man of Africa\" (British Society for the History of Medicine, Lee Coppack, 14 May 2024)","https://bshm.org.uk/?p=18422","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","for the first time in the history of African exploration, they had completed the mission without the loss of a single life")]},{ys:1868,y:"1868",t:"British troops loot Maqdala, Ethiopia.",src:[SRCQ("The Legacy of Maqdala 1868 at the Bodleian Library (School of Anthropology and Museum Ethnography, University of Oxford)","https://www.anthro.ox.ac.uk/legacy-maqdala-1868-bodleian-library","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","in 1868 no less than 13,000 troops were deployed to the mountain fortress and treasury at Maqdala")]},{ys:1871,y:"1871",t:"Karl Mauch records Great Zimbabwe and rules out African builders.",src:[SRCQ("Great Zimbabwe (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Zimbabwe","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1871 he showed the ruins to Karl Mauch")]},{ys:1874,y:"1874",t:"British forces raid Kumasi; Asante gold regalia looted.",src:[SRCQ("Homecoming – Exhibition of Asante Gold Regalia at Manhyia Palace Museum, Kumasi (V&A, 2 May 2024)","https://www.vam.ac.uk/blog/news/homecoming-exhibition-of-asante-gold-regalia-at-manhiya-palace-museum-kumasi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The V&A’s items all date from the third Anglo-Asante War of 1874.")]},{ys:1875,y:"1875",t:"Measles in Fiji",src:[SRCQ("Shanks, Pacific Island Societies Destabilised by Infectious Diseases, Journal of Military and Veterans' Health (PDF)","https://jmvh.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/Pacific-Island-Societies.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","From February to June 1875 measles swept all of Fiji killing an estimated one-fifth to one-fourth of the general population.")]},{ys:1876,y:"1876",t:"Death of Truganini",src:[SRCQ("Aborigines, the last of the race, Tasmania, c. 1866 (National Portrait Gallery of Australia)","https://www.portrait.gov.au/portraits/2006.31","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Trukanini (c. 1812–1876) is arguably nineteenth century Australia’s most celebrated Indigenous leader.")]},{ys:1887,y:"1887",t:"Rinderpest arrives at Massawa",src:[SRCQ("How was rinderpest eradicated? (Gavi VaccinesWork, Maya Prabhu, 24 September 2026)","https://www.gavi.org/vaccineswork/how-was-rinderpest-eradicated","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1887, an Italian ship docked at the Red Sea port of Massawa")]},{ys:1896,y:"1896",t:"Māori population at its lowest",src:[SRCQ("Epidemics and Pandemics: Impact on Māori (National Library of New Zealand Services to Schools, DigitalNZ; quotes Te Ara)","https://digitalnz.org/stories/5e83d713c56cf9000bcf2cd2","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","At its lowest point in 1896 it was around 42,000.")]},{ys:1897,y:"1897",t:"British punitive expedition to Benin City; the Benin Bronzes are looted.",src:[SRCQ("Horniman to return ownership of Benin bronzes to Nigeria (Horniman Museum, 7 August 2022)","https://www.horniman.ac.uk/story/horniman-to-return-ownership-of-benin-bronzes-to-nigeria/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","forcibly removed from Benin City during the British military incursion in February 1897")]},{ys:1901,y:"1901-1905",t:"Sleeping sickness epidemic in Uganda",src:[SRCQ("Headrick (2014) Sleeping Sickness Epidemics and Colonial Responses in East and Central Africa, 1900-1940, PLoS Neglected Tropical Diseases (PMC)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3998934/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1905 over 200,000 inhabitants, one-third of the population of the region, had died of the disease.")]},{ys:1904,y:"1904",t:"Casement Report published",src:[SRCQ("Roger Casement - voice of the voiceless (History Ireland)","https://www.historyireland.com/?p=24399","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","His report was published in 1904")]},{ys:1904,y:"1904-1908",t:"Herero and Nama genocide",src:[SRCQ("Namibia (1904-1908) (Kupferberg Holocaust Center, Queensborough Community College, CUNY)","https://khc.qcc.cuny.edu/blog/namibia-cs/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Between 1904 and 1908, Germany perpetrated the first genocide of the 20th century")]},{ys:1914,y:"1914",t:"Only Ethiopia and Liberia remain outside European control.",src:[SRCQ("Scramble for Africa (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scramble_for_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","By 1914, only Ethiopia and Liberia remained outside European control")]},{ys:1944,y:"1944",t:"Eric Williams publishes Capitalism and Slavery.",src:[SRCQ("Capitalism and Slavery (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitalism_and_Slavery","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Publication of Capitalism and Slavery happened finally in the United States, in 1944.")]},{ys:1963,y:"1963",t:"British Museum Act passed, limiting disposal of objects.",src:[SRCQ("British Museum Act 1963, section 5 (legislation.gov.uk)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1963/24/section/5","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Trustees of the British Museum may sell, exchange, give away or otherwise dispose of any object vested in them")]},{ys:1964,y:"1964",t:"UNESCO launches the General History of Africa.",src:[SRCQ("General History of Africa (UNESCO project) (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_History_of_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The General History of Africa ( GHA ) is a two-phase project launched by UNESCO in 1964")]},{ys:1970,y:"1970",t:"Archaeologist Peter Garlake forced to leave Rhodesia.",src:[SRCQ("Peter Garlake (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Garlake","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Garlake was forced to leave the country in 1970.")]},{ys:1972,y:"1972",t:"Walter Rodney publishes How Europe Underdeveloped Africa.",src:[SRCQ("How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/How_Europe_Underdeveloped_Africa","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","How Europe Underdeveloped Africa is a 1972 book written by Walter Rodney")]},{ys:1972,y:"1972",t:"Crosby names the Columbian Exchange",src:[SRCQ("European Exploration, Perception of the Other, and the Columbian Exchange (Oxford University Press, Backman, Cultures of the West, student resources)","https://learninglink.oup.com/access/content/history-in-practice-western-civ/backman3e-student-resources-volume-1-chapter-14-section-3-background","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","In 1972 Alfred Crosby wrote The Columbian Exchange")]},{ys:2003,y:"2003",t:"Jones publishes Virgin Soils Revisited",src:[SRCQ("Virgin Soils Revisited - excerpt (Gladue Rights Research Database, University of Saskatchewan)","https://gladue.usask.ca/index.php/node/6164","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Virgin Soils Revisited")]},{ys:2008,y:"2008",t:"Nathan Nunn publishes The Long-Term Effects of Africa’s Slave Trades.",src:[SRCQ("Nunn, The Long-Term Effects of Africa's Slave Trades (NBER Working Paper 13367, abstract)","https://www.nber.org/papers/w13367","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","I find a robust negative relationship between the number of slaves exported from a country and current economic performance.")]},{ys:2011,y:"2011",t:"Nunn and Wantchekon link the slave trades to lower trust.",src:[SRCQ("Nunn and Wantchekon, The Slave Trade and the Origins of Mistrust in Africa, AER 101(7), 2011 (abstract)","https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.101.7.3221","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","individuals whose ancestors were heavily raided during the slave trade are less trusting today.")]},{ys:2012,y:"2012–2013",t:"Manuscripts in Timbuktu burned or stolen during the jihadist occupation.",src:[SRCQ("Timbuktu manuscripts (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbuktu_manuscripts","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","4,203 of Timbuktu's manuscripts were burned or stolen following between 2012 and 2013.")]},{ys:2016,y:"2016",t:"ICC convicts Al Mahdi for attacks on Timbuktu’s heritage.",src:[SRCQ("The Prosecutor v. Ahmad Al Faqi Al Mahdi (International Criminal Court)","https://www.icc-cpi.int/mali/al-mahdi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","Found guilty and sentenced to 9 years imprisonment on 27 September 2016.")]},{ys:2018,y:"2018",t:"Sarr–Savoy report on restitution published in France.",src:[SRCQ("Felwine Sarr and Bénédicte Savoy, The Restitution of African Cultural Heritage (2018), English translation (PDF mirror)","https://www.about-africa.de/images/sonstiges/2018/sarr_savoy_en.pdf","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","over 90% of the material cultural legacy of sub-Saharan Africa remains preserved and housed outside of the African continent.")]},{ys:2018,y:"2018",t:"Ancient DNA links Taíno to present-day Puerto Ricans",src:[SRCQ("Traces of indigenous Taíno found in present-day Caribbean populations (University of Cambridge press release on Schroeder et al., PNAS 2018, republished by Brewminate)","https://brewminate.com/traces-of-indigenous-taino-found-in-present-day-caribbean-populations/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The new research finally provides concrete proof that indigenous ancestry in the region has survived to the present day.")]},{ys:2019,y:"2019",t:"UCL study links the Great Dying to global cooling",src:[SRCQ("Koch, Brierley, Maslin & Lewis (2019) Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492, Quaternary Science Reviews (White Rose Research Online record)","https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/154711/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","The Great Dying of the Indigenous Peoples of the Americas resulted in a human-driven global impact on the Earth System")]},{ys:2021,y:"2021",t:"Jesus College, Cambridge returns a Benin bronze cockerel.",src:[SRCQ("Benin Expedition of 1897 (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin_Expedition_of_1897","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","On 27 October 2021, the okukor was received by Nigeria's National Commission for Museums and Monuments (NCMM)")]},{ys:2021,y:"2021",t:"Germany recognises the genocide",src:[SRCQ("Foreign Minister Maas on the conclusion of negotiations with Namibia (German Federal Foreign Office, 28 May 2021)","https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/-/2463598","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","We will now officially call these events what they are from today’s perspective: a genocide.")]},{ys:2022,y:"2022",t:"Horniman Museum transfers ownership of 72 Benin objects to Nigeria.",src:[SRCQ("Six objects to return to Nigeria as Horniman formally transfers ownership of ‘Benin Bronzes’ (Horniman Museum, 28 November 2022)","https://www.horniman.ac.uk/story/six-objects-to-return-to-nigeria-as-horniman-formally-transfers-ownership-of-benin-bronzes/","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","a ceremony marking the transfer of ownership of 72 items taken by force from Benin City in 1897.")]},{ys:2024,y:"2024",t:"Asante gold from the V&A and British Museum shown in Kumasi on loan.",src:[SRCQ("Homecoming – Exhibition of Asante Gold Regalia at Manhyia Palace Museum, Kumasi (V&A, 2 May 2024)","https://www.vam.ac.uk/blog/news/homecoming-exhibition-of-asante-gold-regalia-at-manhiya-palace-museum-kumasi","Supports the What was taken, lost and might have been page","has gone on show in Ghana for the first time in 150 years")]}]};
LP["stereotypes"]={name:"Who built the stereotypes",pfx:"sb",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"enlight",h:"Enlightenment naturalists and philosophers",intro:"In the 18th century, naturalists and philosophers began sorting humanity into ranked groups. Some of their claims were challenged by contemporaries at the time, and their legacy is still being reassessed by universities today.",points:[
  {h:"Linnaeus ranks human 'varieties' by continent",text:"In his classification of nature, Linnaeus divided humans into 'varieties' linked to the continents. In the 1758 edition of Systema Naturae he added the four temperaments, or 'humours', to each variety. The African variety was always placed last, and the descriptions as a whole favoured Europeans.",figure:"",year:"1758 (10th edition of Systema Naturae)",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Linnaeus and Race (Linnean Society of London)","https://www.linnean.org/learning/who-was-linnaeus/linnaeus-and-race","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","One variety never varied in this hierarchy, however: Africanus consistently remained at the bottom of the list."),SRCQ("Linnaeus and Race (Linnean Society of London)","https://www.linnean.org/learning/who-was-linnaeus/linnaeus-and-race","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Linnaeus’ work on the classification of man forms one of the 18th-century roots of modern scientific racism."),SRCQ("Linnaeus, Carolus (1707–1778) – Understanding RACE glossary (American Anthropological Association)","https://understandingrace.org/glossary/linnaeus-carolus-1707-1778/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","based on physical and cultural descriptions that favored Europeans.")],person:"Carl Linnaeus (1707–1778), Swedish naturalist",impact:"The Linnean Society of London describes his classification of humans as one of the 18th-century roots of modern scientific racism.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Blumenbach names five varieties, including 'Caucasian'",text:"In 1795 Blumenbach set out a five-part naming system for human varieties, including 'Caucasian'. He held that all humans were one species and did not propose a hierarchy, but he treated the white 'Caucasian' form as the most beautiful. Later writers misused his labels to support racist ideas.",figure:"",year:"1795",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Blumenbach and the concept of race (University of Göttingen)","https://uni-goettingen.de/en/650077.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","He is best known for establishing a five-part naming system in 1795"),SRCQ("Blumenbach and the concept of race (University of Göttingen)","https://uni-goettingen.de/en/650077.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","his appraisal of the aesthetic of whites as Caucasians was (ab)used to legitimize and inspire racist interpretations"),SRCQ("Race (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/race/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The “science” of race was furthered by the man sometimes considered to be the father of modern anthropology, Johann Friedrich Blumenbach (1752–1840).")],person:"Johann Friedrich Blumenbach (1752–1840), German naturalist",impact:"The University of Göttingen notes that 'Caucasian' is still frequently used as a synonym for white, and that his view of whites as Caucasians was misused to legitimise racist interpretations.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Hume's footnote on 'national characters'",text:"In a footnote to his essay 'Of National Characters', added in the 1753–54 edition of his essays, Hume wrote that he was 'apt to suspect' Black people to be 'naturally inferior' to white people. He claimed there were no arts or sciences among them, and dismissed an educated Black man in Jamaica as admired only for 'slender accomplishments'.",figure:"",year:"1753–54",disputed:false,note:"Sources differ on the edition date: the University of Edinburgh appendix refers to 'the 1753 version' and the Stanford Encyclopedia to 'the 1754 edition'; the collected essays were dated 1753 and issued 1753–54.",src:[SRCQ("James Beattie (1735—1803) (Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://iep.utm.edu/james-beattie/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","I am apt to suspect the negroes to be naturally inferior to the whites."),SRCQ("Appendix 2: The University of Edinburgh, Theories of Race and Civilisation, and British Imperialism, by Ian Stewart (University of Edinburgh)","https://www.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2025-07/Appendix%202:%20The%20University%20of%20Edinburgh,%20Theories%20of%20Race%20and%20Civilisation,%20and%20British%20Imperialism,%20by%20Ian%20Stewart.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","but it is likely he is admired for slender accomplishments, like a parrot, who speaks a few words plainly."),SRCQ("Race (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/race/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Amidst this argument against crude naturalism, Hume inserts a footnote in the 1754 edition, wherein he writes:")],person:"David Hume (1711–1776), Scottish philosopher and historian",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Beattie answers Hume in 1770",text:"The Scottish philosopher James Beattie replied to Hume's footnote in his Essay on Truth (1770). He argued that Africans and Americans had many ingenious arts and manufactures, and that the condition of slavery was not favourable to genius. Beattie was a committed abolitionist who called slavery 'a barbarous piece of policy'.",figure:"",year:"1770",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("James Beattie (1735—1803) (Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://iep.utm.edu/james-beattie/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The Africans and Americans are known to have many ingenious manufactures and arts among them"),SRCQ("James Beattie (1735—1803) (Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://iep.utm.edu/james-beattie/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","These assertions are strong; but I know not whether they have anything else to recommend them."),SRCQ("James Beattie (1735-1803), by Dr Julie Watt (Brycchan Carey, Abolition website)","https://www.brycchancarey.com/abolition/beattie.htm","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Part III of Beattie's Essay (published in May 1770) demolishes Hume's racist argument line by line.")],person:"James Beattie (1735–1803), Scottish philosopher",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Kant defines 'race' and ranks humanity",text:"Kant wrote a series of essays defining 'race' as a scientific concept from the 1770s onward. In his lectures he said humanity reached its 'highest degree of perfection in the white race' and placed Africans much lower. How central this was to his philosophy, and whether he changed his mind, is disputed.",figure:"",year:"1770s–1790s",disputed:true,note:"Robert Bernasconi (with Tommy Lott) presents Kant as 'the first to propose a rigorous scientific concept of race', and treats race as part of Kant's system. Pauline Kleingeld (2007) argues that Kant ranked white people above others in the 1780s but gave up the racial hierarchy in the 1790s for a more egalitarian, cosmopolitan view. Inder Marwah (2022) notes that Kleingeld and others see Kant as having 'ultimately' retracted his predictions, but argues racial ideas still shaped his account of human progress.",src:[SRCQ("Inder S. Marwah, 'White Progress: Kant, Race and Teleology', Kantian Review 27 (2022), 615–634 (Cambridge University Press)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D226D581B530439C81DF60228DAF3C29/S1369415422000334a.pdf/div-class-title-white-progress-kant-race-and-teleology-div.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","[h]umanity has its highest degree of perfection in the white race."),SRCQ("'Kant, Race, and Natural History' (Kingston University research repository), citing Bernasconi 2001","https://eprints.kingston.ac.uk/40732/1/Sandford-S-40732-AAM.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the first to propose a rigorous scientific concept of race"),SRCQ("Pauline Kleingeld, 'Kant's Second Thoughts on Race', Philosophical Quarterly 57 (2007) (abstract on PhilArchive)","https://philarchive.org/rec/KLEKST","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","During the 1780s, as Kant was developing his universalistic moral theory")],person:"Immanuel Kant (1724–1804), German philosopher",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Edinburgh removes Hume's name from a building",text:"In September 2020 the University of Edinburgh renamed its David Hume Tower as 40 George Square, as an interim decision. It cited Hume's 'comments on matters of race', which it said, 'though not uncommon at the time, rightly cause distress today'. The decision drew international scrutiny and debate.",figure:"",year:"September 2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Making sense of silenced archives: Hume, Scotland and the 'debate' about the humanity of Black people (University of Edinburgh, Race and Decoloniality blog, 2021)","https://www.race.ed.ac.uk/blog/2021/making-sense-silenced-archives-hume-scotland-and-debate-about-humanity-black-people","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","found itself at the centre of international scrutiny after temporarily renaming the David Hume Tower (now referred to by its street designation 40 George Square)."),SRCQ("Edinburgh Uni renames David Hume tower over 'racist views' (STV News, 13 September 2020)","https://news.stv.tv/east-central/edinburgh-uni-renames-david-hume-tower-over-racist-views","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The University of Edinburgh has renamed its David Hume Tower over the philosopher’s “comments on matters of race”.")],impact:"A UK university changed how it publicly honours an Enlightenment figure because of what he wrote about race.",kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"plantation",h:"Plantation writers and the first Black answers",intro:"Writers with a stake in slavery used natural history to argue that Black people were a separate and lower kind of human. Black writers in Britain and America answered them directly in print.",points:[
  {h:"Edward Long's History of Jamaica",text:"Edward Long, a Jamaican planter and enslaver, published The History of Jamaica in 1774. He was a polygenist, believing that human races did not share a common origin. Historian Catherine Hall shows that the book tried to define Black and white people as 'essentially different and unequal', using natural history to build a fixed racial hierarchy.",figure:"",year:"1774",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Enlightenment Race Science in the Colonies: Edward Long and the… (University of Michigan, Science, Technology and Society Program)","https://prod.lsa.umich.edu/sts/news-events/all-events/archived-events/2014/01/enlightenment-race-science-in-the-colonies--edward-long-and-the-.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Long was a polygenist, believing that the different races of Man did not share a common origin."),SRCQ("Catherine Hall, Lucky Valley: Edward Long and the History of Racial Capitalism (Cambridge University Press, 2024)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/lucky-valley/8487455BCEEB6E3585784D0AC99D32B6","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","attempted to define White and Black as essentially different and unequal"),SRCQ("Catherine Hall, Lucky Valley (Cambridge University Press, 2024) – book description","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/lucky-valley/8487455BCEEB6E3585784D0AC99D32B6","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","exposing the insidious myths and stereotypes that have poisoned social relations over generations")],person:"Edward Long, planter, enslaver and historian of Jamaica",impact:"Hall argues that the myths and stereotypes Long helped to spread have damaged social relations for generations.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Banneker challenges Jefferson's doubts",text:"In Notes on the State of Virginia (1785), Thomas Jefferson set out his doubts about the intellect of Black people. In August 1791 Benjamin Banneker sent Jefferson, then Secretary of State, a manuscript copy of his almanac with a letter challenging those views. Jefferson replied that 'no body wishes more than I do to see such proofs as you exhibit'.",figure:"",year:"1785–1791",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Letter to Thomas Jefferson from Benjamin Banneker, August 19, 1791 (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Letter-to-Thomas-Jefferson-from-Benjamin-Banneker-August-19-1791","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","he had detailed his skepticism of Black intellect in his 1785 work Notes on the State of Virginia"),SRCQ("Letter to Thomas Jefferson from Benjamin Banneker (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Letter-to-Thomas-Jefferson-from-Benjamin-Banneker-August-19-1791","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","It was with this understanding that Banneker sent a manuscript copy of his first almanac"),SRCQ("Thomas Jefferson to Benjamin Banneker, 30 August 1791 (Library of Congress exhibition)","https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/jefferson/79.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","no body wishes more than I do to see such proofs as you exhibit")],person:"Benjamin Banneker, Black American almanac author; Thomas Jefferson (1743–1826), US Secretary of State",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Equiano's Interesting Narrative",text:"Olaudah Equiano's autobiography, published in London in 1789, described his life as an enslaved person. It was timed to coincide with the May 1789 parliamentary debate on the slave trade, and Equiano toured the country promoting it. By writing his own life, he directly contradicted the claim that Black minds were inferior.",figure:"",year:"1789",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Olaudah Equiano (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Olaudah-Equiano","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","whose 1789 autobiography, detailing his life as an enslaved person, was immensely popular and influential"),SRCQ("The Interesting Narrative by Olaudah Equiano – Ten-Minute Book Club (Faculty of English, University of Oxford)","https://www.english.ox.ac.uk/ten-minute-book-club/equiano-the-interesting-narrative","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","published to coincide with the May 1789 parliamentary debate on the slave trade"),SRCQ("The Interesting Narrative by Olaudah Equiano (University of Oxford)","https://www.english.ox.ac.uk/ten-minute-book-club/equiano-the-interesting-narrative","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","entirely opposed to the ideology of slavery's insistence that black minds were inferior")],person:"Olaudah Equiano (c.1745–1797), writer and abolitionist",impact:"The book became a bestseller and was part of the anti-slavery campaign; Britain abolished the slave trade in 1807, ten years after his death.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Cugoano demands immediate abolition",text:"In 1787 Ottobah Cugoano published Thoughts and Sentiments on the Evil and Wicked Traffic of the Slavery and Commerce of the Human Species. It is one of the first anti-slavery books by a Black author published in Britain, and the most radical of its time, calling for immediate abolition and the emancipation of enslaved people. He was a leading member of the Sons of Africa and a friend of Equiano.",figure:"",year:"1787",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ottobah Cugoano – blue plaque (English Heritage)","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/ottobah-cugoano/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","calling for its immediate abolition and the emancipation of enslaved peoples."),SRCQ("Ottobah Cugoano – blue plaque (English Heritage)","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/ottobah-cugoano/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","OTTOBAH CUGOANO born c.1757 Author and anti-slavery campaigner lived and worked in Schomberg House 1785-1791"),SRCQ("Learning about Britain's Black abolitionists (University of Wolverhampton blog, October 2022)","https://www.wlv.ac.uk/news-and-events/wlv-blog/2022/october-2022/learning-about-britains-black-abolitionists.php","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","By the end of the year, Cugoano was also a leading member of the Sons of Africa,")],person:"Ottobah Cugoano (born c.1757), writer and anti-slavery campaigner",impact:"English Heritage has marked Schomberg House in London, where he lived and worked from 1785 to 1791, with a blue plaque.",kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"victorian",h:"Victorian writers and anthropologists",intro:"After slavery was abolished in the British Caribbean, some leading Victorian writers and new learned societies argued that Black people were naturally suited to subordination. Other public figures opposed them at the time.",points:[
  {h:"Carlyle's 'Occasional Discourse'",text:"In December 1849 Carlyle published an essay in Fraser's Magazine arguing that emancipation had left Black Jamaicans in idleness and that freed workers should be compelled to work. He reissued it in 1853 as a pamphlet with an even more offensive title.",figure:"",year:"1849; reissued 1853",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Speech beyond Toleration: On Carlyle and Moral Controversialism Now (Oxford University Research Archive)","https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:ea29f182-95b8-4061-8bbf-67513e6b6fb4/files/m60e7ce9522815995a4307da35b29c1a2","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the divine right of being compelled (if \"permitted\" will not answer) to do what work they are appointed for."),SRCQ("The War on Woke is 200 years old (Snapshots of Empire blog, University of Sussex, 2022)","https://blogs.sussex.ac.uk/snapshotsofempire/2022/09/06/the-war-on-woke-is-200-years-old/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Carlyle argued that emancipation had condemned Black Jamaicans to idle pauperism, just as greater freedom had the Irish."),SRCQ("The War on Woke is 200 years old (University of Sussex)","https://blogs.sussex.ac.uk/snapshotsofempire/2022/09/06/the-war-on-woke-is-200-years-old/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Carlyle’s unabashed racism sharpened and articulated the British public’s sense of disappointment")],person:"Thomas Carlyle, Victorian essayist and historian",impact:"Historian Alan Lester argues that the essay sharpened and gave voice to British public disappointment with emancipation.",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Mill's reply: 'The Negro Question'",text:"In January 1850 John Stuart Mill replied in the same magazine. He said Carlyle's picture of Jamaican labour was 'copied from the wildest prophecies of the slavery party before emancipation'. Their different views on race and slavery helped to end their friendship.",figure:"",year:"1850",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Speech beyond Toleration: On Carlyle and Moral Controversialism Now (Oxford University Research Archive)","https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:ea29f182-95b8-4061-8bbf-67513e6b6fb4/files/m60e7ce9522815995a4307da35b29c1a2","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","copied from the wildest prophecies of the slavery party before emancipation"),SRCQ("Sheila McIntosh, 'Carlyle and the Caribbean', Carlyle Society Occasional Papers 21 (Edinburgh, 2008)","https://edwebcontent.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/atoms/files/carlyle_papers_no._21.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Mill in his rebuttal of the “Discourse” in a letter to Fraser’s Magazine 1850"),SRCQ("Sheila McIntosh, 'Carlyle and the Caribbean' (Carlyle Society, 2008)","https://edwebcontent.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/atoms/files/carlyle_papers_no._21.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the different attitudes the two men adopted towards race and slavery.")],person:"John Stuart Mill, philosopher and economist",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Morant Bay and the Governor Eyre controversy",text:"After the Morant Bay rising in Jamaica in 1865, Governor Edward John Eyre declared martial law and hanged Paul Bogle and the assembly member George William Gordon. In Britain, the Jamaica Committee, led by Mill, sought to have Eyre prosecuted, while Carlyle joined the Eyre Defence Committee in 1866.",figure:"",year:"1865–1866",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Jamaica – British rule (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Jamaica/British-rule","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","who declared martial law, suppressed the rioters, and hanged the principal instigator, Paul Bogle,"),SRCQ("Jamaica – British rule (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Jamaica/British-rule","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","and in 1866 Parliament declared the island a crown colony."),SRCQ("Sheila McIntosh, 'Carlyle and the Caribbean' (Carlyle Society, 2008)","https://edwebcontent.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/atoms/files/carlyle_papers_no._21.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Carlyle joined the latter in August 1866 in spite of the fact that Welsh Carlyle had died only a few months previously."),SRCQ("When Intellectuals Split: The Eyre Case (JSTOR Daily)","https://daily.jstor.org/when-intellectuals-split-the-eyre-case","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The famed John Stuart Mill, who had become an MP that same year, led the charge.")],impact:"Eyre was recalled after public outcry and an official investigation, and in 1866 Parliament made Jamaica a crown colony.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Robert Knox and The Races of Men",text:"Knox, an Edinburgh surgeon and anatomist, became a popular lecturer on race. In The Races of Men (1850) he classified all known human races and argued that race was the greatest single factor shaping behaviour and character. He also played on fears that Europeans could be driven out of their colonies.",figure:"",year:"1850",disputed:false,note:"Birkbeck notes that, despite his racism, some scholars have recently argued Knox used his theories to criticise colonialism.",src:[SRCQ("Colonialism and science (University of Glasgow)","https://www.gla.ac.uk/media/Media_834239_smxx.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In his book, 'The Races of Men', Dr Robert Knox, a Scottish anatomist and ethnologist, highlighted recent rebellions")],person:"Robert Knox (born 1791), Scottish surgeon and anatomist",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"James Hunt and the Anthropological Society",text:"In 1863 James Hunt led about a dozen members out of the Ethnological Society to found the Anthropological Society of London. His paper 'On the Negro's Place in Nature' (1863) defended the subjection and even the enslavement of Black people and argued that humans were several species.",figure:"",year:"1863–64",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Paul Turnbull, 'British Anthropological Thought in Colonial Practice' (ANU Press)","https://press.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/p53561/mobile/ch04.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","A faction of about a dozen members led by James Hunt (1833-1869)"),SRCQ("Paul Turnbull (ANU Press)","https://press.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/p53561/mobile/ch04.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In this widely circulated pamphlet, Hunt outlined a prima facie case for classifying African people"),SRCQ("On the Negro's place in nature – catalogue record (Conway Hall Library and Archives)","https://conwayhallcollections.omeka.net/items/show/780","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","attracted much attention, as it defended the subjection and even slavery of the negro,")],person:"James Hunt (1833–1869), anthropologist",impact:"The paper was published as a widely circulated pamphlet.",kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"racescience",h:"Race science, eugenics and those who answered them",intro:"In the 19th century, skull measurement, 'types' of humanity and theories of heredity were presented as science. Black scholars and campaigners challenged them at the time, and some institutions have since publicly reckoned with this history.",points:[
  {h:"Samuel Morton's skull collection",text:"Morton, who trained in medicine at Edinburgh, built the largest skull collection in North America. In Crania Americana (1839) he used skull capacity to rank races, with Caucasians at the top. He believed different races had separate origins.",figure:"",year:"1839",disputed:true,note:"Stephen Jay Gould (1981) argued that Morton skewed his data to fit his preconceptions. Lewis et al. (PLoS Biology, 2011) remeasured the skulls and concluded that Morton 'did not manipulate data to support his preconceptions, contra Gould'. Weisberg (2014) argues most of Gould's arguments are sound and that Morton did mismeasure in ways that matched 19th-century racial bias; Kaplan and Pigliucci (2015) argue the data cannot properly answer the question. All sides agree Morton's racial views were racist.",src:[SRCQ("Appendix 2: The University of Edinburgh, Theories of Race and Civilisation, and British Imperialism, by Ian Stewart (University of Edinburgh)","https://www.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2025-07/Appendix%202:%20The%20University%20of%20Edinburgh,%20Theories%20of%20Race%20and%20Civilisation,%20and%20British%20Imperialism,%20by%20Ian%20Stewart.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the largest skull collection in North America"),SRCQ("Lewis et al., 'The Mismeasure of Science: Stephen Jay Gould versus Samuel George Morton on Skulls and Bias', PLoS Biology (2011)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3110184","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","assigned a plethora of different attributes to various groups, often in highly racist fashion"),SRCQ("Race – Gobineau's Essay on the Inequality of Human Races (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/race-human/Gobineaus-Essay-on-the-Inequality-of-Human-Races","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","He imported some of his arguments from the polygenists, especially the American Samuel Morton."),SRCQ("Michael Weisberg, 'Remeasuring man', Evolution and Development (2014) (ANU research portal abstract)","https://researchportalplus.anu.edu.au/en/publications/remeasuring-man/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Samuel George Morton (1799-1851) was the most highly regarded American scientist of the early and middle 19th century."),SRCQ("Kaplan and Pigliucci, 'Gould on Morton, Redux', Studies in History and Philosophy of Biological and Biomedical Sciences 52 (2015) (PhilArchive abstract)","https://philarchive.org/rec/KAPGOM-2","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","We maintain that Gould was right to reject Morton's analysis as inappropriate and misleading,")],person:"Samuel George Morton (1799–1851), American physician",impact:"Britannica notes that Arthur de Gobineau took some of his arguments from Morton.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Nott and Gliddon's Types of Mankind",text:"Physician Josiah Nott and George Gliddon published Types of Mankind in 1854. It promoted the idea that human races were separate species, included a contribution by Louis Agassiz and ran through ten editions.",figure:"",year:"1854",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Polygenism – 'This Abominable Traffic': Physicians on Slavery (Center for the History of Medicine, Countway Library, Harvard)","https://collections.countway.harvard.edu/onview/exhibits/show/this-abominable-traffic/agassiz-and-polygenism/polygenism","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Physician Josiah Clark Nott (1804-1873) used early anthropological science to promote the idea of polygenism."),SRCQ("Polygenism (Countway Library, Harvard)","https://collections.countway.harvard.edu/onview/exhibits/show/this-abominable-traffic/agassiz-and-polygenism/polygenism","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","...which was first published in 1854 and ran through ten editions."),SRCQ("The Claims of the Negro Ethnologically Considered, 12 July 1854 (Frederick Douglass Papers Project, Digital Edition)","https://frederickdouglasspapersproject.com/s/digitaledition/item/15927","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the most compendious and barefaced is the book, entitled Types of Mankind, by Nott and Glidden."),SRCQ("The Claims of the Negro Ethnologically Considered (Frederick Douglass Papers Project)","https://frederickdouglasspapersproject.com/s/digitaledition/item/15927","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","were duly consulted by our slavery propagating statesmen.")],person:"Josiah Clark Nott (1804–1873), physician; George Gliddon, co-author",impact:"Frederick Douglass said such ethnologists were 'duly consulted by our slavery propagating statesmen'.",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Douglass answers the ethnologists",text:"On 12 July 1854 Frederick Douglass gave a commencement address at Western Reserve College in Ohio, 'The Claims of the Negro Ethnologically Considered'. He named Morton and Nott and Gliddon, argued that the evidence favoured the unity of the human species, and said that the pull to 'read the negro out of the human family' came from pride and self-interest.",figure:"",year:"1854",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Frederick Douglass – Our Stories (Case Western Reserve University)","https://case.edu/bicentennial/history/our-stories/frederick-douglass","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In 1854, the civil rights leader delivered an address to the literary societies during commencement week at Western Reserve College."),SRCQ("The Claims of the Negro Ethnologically Considered (Frederick Douglass Papers Project)","https://frederickdouglasspapersproject.com/s/digitaledition/item/15927","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The temptation, therefore, to read the negro out of the human family is exceedingly strong"),SRCQ("Frederick Douglass (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Summer 2021 edition)","https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2021/entries/frederick-douglass/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","I sincerely believe, that the weight of the argument is in favor of the unity of origin of the human race, or species")],person:"Frederick Douglass, abolitionist, writer and orator",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Gobineau's Essay on the Inequality of Human Races",text:"Gobineau's Essay (1853–55) claimed that all civilisations were the work of white races, that 'Aryans' were the purest, and that mixing with other races caused civilisations to decline. It was widely read and publicised, with admirers in the United States and Germany, including the composer Richard Wagner.",figure:"",year:"1853–55",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Race – Gobineau's Essay on the Inequality of Human Races (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/race-human/Gobineaus-Essay-on-the-Inequality-of-Human-Races","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","were all products of the white races and that no civilization could emerge without their cooperation."),SRCQ("Race – Gobineau's Essay (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/race-human/Gobineaus-Essay-on-the-Inequality-of-Human-Races","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","who had an almost incalculable effect on late 19th-century social theory."),SRCQ("Race (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/race/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Nevertheless, Gobineau lent credence to the white racial supremacy that Nott supported (Brace 2005, 120–121).")],person:"Arthur de Gobineau (Joseph-Arthur, comte de Gobineau), French writer and diplomat",impact:"Britannica describes Gobineau as the most important promoter of racial ideology in mid-19th-century Europe, with an almost incalculable effect on late 19th-century social theory.",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Firmin's The Equality of the Human Races",text:"The Haitian scholar Anténor Firmin wrote De l'égalité des races humaines (1885) as a response to Gobineau. He used the scientific methods of his day to argue that the human races were equal. The book was sidelined for its 'radical' position and was out of print even in Haiti until 1968.",figure:"",year:"1885",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Equality of the Human Races (University of Illinois Press)","https://www.press.uillinois.edu/books/?id=p071027","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Marginalized for its \"radical\" position that the human races were equal,"),SRCQ("The Equality of the Human Races (University of Illinois Press)","https://www.press.uillinois.edu/books/?id=p071027","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a foundational text in critical anthropology first published in 1885"),SRCQ("Russell, 'Positivism and Progress in Firmin's Equality of the Human Races', Journal of Pan African Studies 7:2 (2014)","https://mail.jpanafrican.org/docs/vol7no2/Firmin-5-Russell-ready.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","quickly disappeared and was out of print even in Haiti until 1968."),SRCQ("Anténor Firmin (1850-1911) (BlackPast.org)","https://new.blackpast.org/global-african-history/antenor-firmin-1850-1911","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Firmin wrote De l’égalité des races Humaines as a response to the essay of anthropologist Arthur de Gobineau")],person:"Anténor Firmin (1850–1911), Haitian anthropologist and diplomat",impact:"It is now regarded as a foundational text in critical anthropology.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Galton and the founding of eugenics",text:"In Hereditary Genius (1869) Galton argued that mental ability is inherited, and proposed arranged marriages between 'men of distinction' and wealthy women to produce a 'gifted race'. He coined the term 'eugenics' in 1883.",figure:"",year:"1869–1883",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Hereditary Genius (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Hereditary-Genius","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Galton, in Hereditary Genius (1869), proposed that a system of arranged marriages between men of distinction"),SRCQ("Eugenic Thinking and the Cognitive Sciences (Open Encyclopedia of Cognitive Science, MIT)","https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/ptlnp5h2/release/1","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The term eugenics was coined by Sir Francis Galton in England in his 1883 book"),SRCQ("Eugenic Thinking and the Cognitive Sciences (MIT)","https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/ptlnp5h2/release/1","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a social movement that led many countries and their states or provinces to adopt policies and laws"),SRCQ("Bricks + Mortals: a history of eugenics told through buildings (UCL Culture)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-collections/programmes-and-exhibitions/about-us/projects/bricks-mortals-history-eugenics-told-through-buildings","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In 1883, Galton coined the term \"eugenics\", the study of human characteristics passed on through the generations")],person:"Francis Galton (1822–1911), English scientist",impact:"The eugenics movement led many countries to adopt policies and laws aimed at changing the traits of future generations.",kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"eugenics",h:"Eugenics and colonial science in Britain and the empire",intro:"In the early 20th century, ideas of racial hierarchy were built into British universities, colonial government and public exhibitions. Several institutions have since reviewed that record.",points:[
  {h:"Karl Pearson framed race as a struggle won by the 'fitter race'",text:"In National Life from the Standpoint of Science (1900), the statistician Karl Pearson argued that a nation is kept efficient by competition, including war with what he called 'inferior races'. UCL's eugenics inquiry found earlier correspondence in which he ranked peoples on a scale he linked to 'civilisation'.",figure:"",year:"1895–1900",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Modern History Sourcebook: Karl Pearson, National Life from the Standpoint of Science, 1900 (Fordham University)","https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/mod/1900pearsonl.asp","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","kept up to a high pitch of external efficiency by contest, chiefly by way of war with inferior races"),SRCQ("Inquiry into the History of Eugenics at UCL: final report, February 2020 (University College London)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/about/sites/about/files/2025-10/UCL-History-of-Eugenics-Inquiry-Report-Final-Feb20.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a singularly low place on a scale which appears to conform somewhat to the scale of civilisation")],person:"Karl Pearson (1857–1936), statistician and eugenicist, University College London",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Galton's money created a eugenics laboratory and chair at UCL",text:"Francis Galton funded a eugenics laboratory in the University of London, which Pearson directed from 1907, and left money in his will for a professorship of eugenics. Pearson became the first Galton Professor of Eugenics at University College in 1911; the chair was renamed Human Genetics only in 1963.",figure:"",year:"1907–1963",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Oxford DNB article: Pearson, Karl, by Joanne Woiak (Oxford University Press, reproduced by University of St Andrews MacTutor)","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/DNB/Pearson.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In 1911 he was appointed the first Galton professor of eugenics at University College."),SRCQ("Inquiry into the History of Eugenics at UCL: final report, February 2020 (University College London)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/about/sites/about/files/2025-10/UCL-History-of-Eugenics-Inquiry-Report-Final-Feb20.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","1963: Penrose renames the Galton Professor of Eugenics the Galton Professor of Human Genetics.")],person:"Karl Pearson (1857–1936); Francis Galton (1822–1911)",impact:"Eugenics became an established university discipline in London, with its own laboratory, chair and journal, for more than half a century.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A eugenics journal set out to study 'racial problems'",text:"Pearson founded the Annals of Eugenics in 1925, published for the Galton Laboratory by Cambridge University Press. Its original subtitle described it as a journal for the scientific study of racial problems. It was renamed the Annals of Human Genetics in 1954.",figure:"",year:"1925–1954",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Catalogue record: Annals of eugenics (Wellcome Collection)","https://content.www.wellcomecollection.org/works/q6z3mjfn","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a journal for the scientific study of racial problems"),SRCQ("Inquiry into the History of Eugenics at UCL: final report, February 2020 (University College London)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/about/sites/about/files/2025-10/UCL-History-of-Eugenics-Inquiry-Report-Final-Feb20.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The journal's original subtitle had been 'a journal for the scientific study of racial problems'.")],person:"Karl Pearson (1857–1936), founding editor",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The 1930 Fletcher Report stigmatised mixed-heritage children",text:"A 1930 report on mixed-heritage children in Liverpool, written for the a Liverpool association for the welfare of mixed-heritage children (its name used a term now considered offensive) with the backing of a eugenicist, described the children of Black men and white women as having inherited defects. It was criticised at the time for its biased methods.",figure:"",year:"1930",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Mark Christian, 'The Fletcher Report 1930: A Historical Case Study of Contested Black Mixed Heritage Britishness', Journal of Historical Sociology 21(2–3), 2008 (Wiley; abstract and extracts reproduced by Mixed Race Studies)","https://www.mixedracestudies.org/?p=639","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the official outset in defining Liverpool's 'half castes' as a problem"),SRCQ("The Fletcher report, timeline entry (Mix-d: Museum)","https://exhibition.mixedmuseum.org.uk/museum/timeline/the-fletcher-report","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the report was highly influential and would shape perceptions of mixed race people and families for many years to come")],impact:"Historians judge that the report helped define Liverpool's mixed-heritage population as a 'problem' and shaped perceptions of mixed-race people and families for many years.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Indirect rule governed Africans through fixed 'customary' units",text:"Lord Lugard's The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922) set out indirect rule: governing through chiefs and 'native' courts. Later scholars argue that this system defined people by ethnic group and let state-appointed authorities decide what counted as 'custom'.",figure:"",year:"1922",disputed:true,note:"Assessments differ. Britannica describes indirect rule as 'cooperative in spirit and economical in staff and expense' and says it 'helped bridge the gap between tribal systems' and modern government, though it was 'sometimes misapplied or overprolonged'. Mamdani (Princeton University Press) argues it was a form of 'decentralized despotism' that hardened ethnic divisions.",src:[SRCQ("Frederick Lugard (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/F-D-Lugard","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","It greatly influenced British administration in Africa and beyond."),SRCQ("Mahmood Mamdani, Citizen and Subject: Contemporary Africa and the Legacy of Late Colonialism, book description (Princeton University Press)","https://press.princeton.edu/node/56579","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","through tribally organized local authorities, reproducing racial identity in citizens and ethnic identity in subjects")],person:"Frederick Lugard (1858–1945), colonial administrator",impact:"Britannica records that the Dual Mandate greatly influenced British administration in Africa and beyond.",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Africans were put on show at the 1924 British Empire Exhibition",text:"At Wembley in 1924, about sixty West African craftspeople and workers lived and worked in a mock walled city and 'native village' for visitors to watch. Press coverage leaned on stereotypes of African 'backwardness'.",figure:"",year:"1924–1925",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Marc Matera, Afro-Metropolis: Black Life and Political Activism in Interwar London, chapter 1 (University of California Press)","https://content.ucpress.edu/chapters/12865.ch01.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Symbols of African backwardness and sexual innuendo pervaded press coverage of Baa."),SRCQ("Philip Grant, When Nigeria came to Wembley, 1924 (Brent Museum and Archives, London Borough of Brent, 2014)","https://www.brent.gov.uk/media/9293973/When%20Nigeria%20came%20to%20Wembley%201924.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","He was much photographed by visitors to the Walled City in 1924.")],impact:"According to historian Marc Matera, West African students in London, including the Union of Students of African Descent, protested at the press coverage, saying it held African peoples up to public ridicule.",kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"iq",h:"IQ, heredity and race after 1960",intro:"From the late 1960s a small group of researchers argued that differences in test scores between racial groups were largely genetic. Their work was funded and published through a distinct network, and journals and universities have since retracted or disowned parts of it.",points:[
  {h:"Arthur Jensen claimed group IQ gaps were largely inherited",text:"In 1969 the Harvard Educational Review published Arthur Jensen's article 'How Much Can We Boost IQ and Scholastic Achievement?'. It argued that compensatory education had apparently failed and that differences in intelligence between groups had a substantial genetic basis.",figure:"",year:"1969",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("In Memoriam: Arthur R. Jensen (University of California Academic Senate)","https://senate.universityofcalifornia.edu/_files/inmemoriam/html/ArthurR.Jensen.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","there is a substantial genetic contribution to both individual and group differences in intelligence."),SRCQ("Jensen, Arthur Robert, Scribner Encyclopedia of American Lives: The 1960s (Charles Scribner's Sons, via Encyclopedia.com)","https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/jensen-arthur-robert","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Compensatory education has been tried and it apparently has failed.")],person:"Arthur Jensen (1923–2012), educational psychologist, University of California, Berkeley",impact:"The article provoked a long public controversy; the same journal printed critiques by seven psychologists, and critics called it scientific racism.",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"A Nobel physicist campaigned on race and heredity",text:"From the late 1960s, William Shockley, who shared the 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics, argued that Black Americans scored lower on IQ tests because of genetics, and advocated eugenics while a professor at Stanford.",figure:"",year:"late 1960s–1980s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Guide to the William Shockley papers (Stanford University Libraries, via Online Archive of California)","https://oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/kt3t1nf3ft/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","While working at Stanford, Shockley embraced and advocated for eugenics."),SRCQ("William B. Shockley (Britannica Student Encyclopedia)","https://kids.britannica.com/students/article/William-B-Shockley/313517","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","He was widely condemned for his beliefs.")],person:"William Shockley (1910–1989), physicist, Stanford University",impact:"Britannica records that he was widely condemned for these views.",kind:"argument"},
  {h:"The Pioneer Fund and Mankind Quarterly sustained race science",text:"The Pioneer Fund, a US foundation set up in 1937 with Wickliffe Draper's money, financed opponents of racial integration. Mankind Quarterly, founded in Edinburgh in 1960 with Robert Gayre as editor, described itself as a journal of 'race and inheritance'.",figure:"",year:"1937 onwards",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Francesco Cassata, 'Against UNESCO: Gedda, Gini and American Scientific Racism', Medicina nei Secoli 20(3), 2008 (Sapienza University of Rome)","https://rosa.uniroma1.it/rosa01/medicina_nei_secoli/article/download/391/365/741","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","made ample contributions to economically sustain the main adversaries of the American integrationist system"),SRCQ("Catalogue record: The mankind quarterly, Edinburgh, 1960– (Wellcome Collection)","https://wellcomecollection.org/works/c2qucqsb","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","An international quarterly journal dealing with race and inheritance in the fields of ethnology, ethno- and human genetics,"),SRCQ("Charles Lane, 'The Tainted Sources of The Bell Curve', New York Review of Books, 1 December 1994","https://www.nybooks.com/articles/1994/12/01/the-tainted-sources-of-the-bell-curve/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","...a New York foundation established in 1937 with the money of Wickliffe Draper.")],impact:"Historians link both to organised scientific opposition to US desegregation after the 1954 Brown v. Board of Education ruling.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A journal retracted a Rushton paper as unsound in 2020",text:"In 2020 the Elsevier journal Personality and Individual Differences retracted a 2012 paper by J. Philippe Rushton and a co-author linking skin pigmentation to aggression and sexuality. The notice said the paper misrepresented the literature and ignored social and educational explanations. That year his former department at Western University in Canada said much of his research was racist.",figure:"",year:"2012; retracted 2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Retraction notice for Rushton & Templer (2012), Personality and Individual Differences (Elsevier), as reproduced in Retraction Watch, 29 June 2020","https://retractionwatch.com/2020/06/29/journal-calls-2012-paper-deeply-offensive-to-particular-minorities","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Rushton & Templer ignored obvious social and educational explanations for higher levels of violence, HIV infection etc."),SRCQ("Department statement on Dr. Philippe Rushton (Department of Psychology, Western University, June 2020)","https://psychology.uwo.ca/people/faculty/remembrance/rushton.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","much of his research was racist, and attempted to find differences in intelligence between racialized groups")],person:"J. Philippe Rushton (1943–2012), psychologist, Western University, Canada",impact:"Western's psychology department said his work continues to be misused by white supremacists and promoted by eugenic organisations.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Richard Lynn's 'national IQ' data led to a Royal Society retraction",text:"In 2024 Proceedings of the Royal Society B retracted a 2010 paper because it relied on between-country IQ data published in 2004 by Tatu Vanhanen and the British psychologist Richard Lynn. Critics had shown that the data contained substantial inaccuracies and biases. In 2018 Ulster University withdrew Lynn's emeritus title after a student campaign.",figure:"",year:"2004–2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Retraction: 'Parasite prevalence and the worldwide distribution of cognitive ability' (2010), Proceedings of the Royal Society B 291, 2024 (The Royal Society)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11060651/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the manifest problems in the data warranted retraction"),SRCQ("Do some IQ data need a 'public health warning?' (Retraction Watch, 18 March 2024)","https://retractionwatch.com/2024/03/18/do-some-iq-data-need-a-public-health-warning-a-paper-based-on-a-controversial-psychologists-data-is-retracted/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","data published in 2004 by Tatu Vanhanen and Richard Lynn, who died in 2023:"),SRCQ("UU students call for removal of former professor's title (Irish News, 14 February 2018)","https://www.irishnews.com/news/2018/02/14/news/uu-students-calls-for-removal-of-former-professor-s-title-1255299/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The move against Professor Lynn came after the Students Union passed a motion calling for the \"revocation\" of his title.")],person:"Richard Lynn (1930–2023), psychologist, Ulster University",impact:"The editors weighed the potential harm of data portraying some regional populations as having below-normal average intelligence.",kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"britain",h:"Britain: schools, labelling and the media",intro:"In Britain, ideas about ability and race shaped how Caribbean children were assessed in schools and how young Black people were portrayed in the press. Parents, campaigners and scholars documented and challenged this.",points:[
  {h:"Caribbean children were over-placed in 'ESN' schools",text:"In the 1960s and 1970s, schools for the 'educationally subnormal' (ESN) took a disproportionate share of Caribbean children. The Inner London Education Authority noted in 1967 that 28% of children in its ESN schools were 'immigrant', and a 1973 Department of Education and Science letter found West Indian children four times over-represented.",figure:"",year:"1960s–1970s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Sally Tomlinson, 'Disgraceful Labelling: Race, Special Education and Exclusion' (Excluded Lives project, Department of Education, University of Oxford, 14 July 2021)","https://excludedlives.education.ox.ac.uk/disgraceful-labelling-race-special-education-and-exclusion/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The Inner London Education Authority noted in 1967 that 28% of children in ESN schools were 'immigrant'."),SRCQ("A history of education campaigning (Black Cultural Archives, via Google Arts & Culture)","https://artsandculture.google.com/story/a-history-of-education-campaigning-black-cultural-archives/cQXhZVzwN7aDKg?hl=en","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Coard looked at the high numbers of Black children who were being placed in schools")],impact:"Caribbean parents' concern about these placements grew into organised campaigns during the 1970s.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A 1971 pamphlet exposed the labelling and mobilised parents",text:"How the West Indian Child is Made Educationally Subnormal in the British School System (1971) argued that schools and teachers, not children, were the source of the problem, through low expectations and biased assessment. It recommended Black supplementary schools.",figure:"",year:"1971",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("A history of education campaigning (Black Cultural Archives, via Google Arts & Culture)","https://artsandculture.google.com/story/a-history-of-education-campaigning-black-cultural-archives/cQXhZVzwN7aDKg?hl=en","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Many activists and parents were galvanised into action by the publication of"),SRCQ("Sally Tomlinson, 'Disgraceful Labelling' (University of Oxford Excluded Lives project, 2021)","https://excludedlives.education.ox.ac.uk/disgraceful-labelling-race-special-education-and-exclusion/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the bitterness and concern of Caribbean parents over these placements became vocal during the decade."),SRCQ("West Indian Children in Our Schools (Rampton interim report), HMSO 1981, full text hosted by education-uk.org","https://education-uk.org/documents/rampton/rampton1981.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","placed the blame for misclassification squarely on the schools and teachers")],impact:"Black Cultural Archives records that the publication galvanised activists and parents and contributed to the growth of the supplementary (Saturday) school movement.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"A government inquiry named low teacher expectations and prejudice",text:"The government's Rampton inquiry reported in 1981 (West Indian Children in Our Schools). It found West Indian children as a group were underachieving and said teachers' low expectations could become a self-fulfilling prophecy. It recommended ethnic monitoring of special-needs assessments.",figure:"",year:"1981",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("West Indian Children in Our Schools: Interim report of the Committee of Inquiry into the Education of Children from Ethnic Minority Groups (HMSO, 1981), full text hosted by education-uk.org","https://education-uk.org/documents/rampton/rampton1981.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","low expectations of the academic ability of West Indian pupils by teachers can often prove a self-fulfilling prophecy."),SRCQ("Multi-cultural Education, catalogue description (BFI Replay, British Film Institute)","https://replay.bfi.org.uk/video/129/543edc70-be30-54dc-8532-c563d1106b32","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","have not sought to provide West Indian children with a robust learning environment")],impact:"The report led to official recommendations that cultural differences and the effects of discrimination be taken into account when assessing pupils for special education.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Policing the Crisis showed how a 'mugging' panic was built",text:"Policing the Crisis: Mugging, the State and Law and Order (1978), by Stuart Hall and colleagues at Birmingham's Centre for Contemporary Cultural Studies, analysed the 1972–73 wave of press and police attention to 'mugging'. It traced how race, immigration and crime were linked in 1970s public debate.",figure:"",year:"1978",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Kieran Connell, 'Policing the Crisis 35 Years On', Contemporary British History 29(2), 2015 (Queen's University Belfast research portal)","https://pure.qub.ac.uk/en/publications/policing-the-crisis-35-years-on/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The new edition is demonstrative of the book's enduring influence."),SRCQ("Open University programme record presented by Stuart Hall on 'mugging' (The Open University Digital Archive)","https://www.open.ac.uk/library/digital-archive/video:00525_2161","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Between August 1972 and October 1973 Britain experienced a wave of 'muggings'"),SRCQ("News Media Criminology, in The SAGE Handbook of Criminological Theory, 2010 (City, University of London repository)","https://openaccess.city.ac.uk/id/eprint/2013/1/2010%20-%20Sage%20Handbook%20-%20News%20Media%20Criminology.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the young black street criminal"),SRCQ("Policing the Crisis in the 21st Century; the making of 'knife crime youths' in Britain, Crime, Law and Social Change, 2023 (University of Greenwich repository)","https://gala.gre.ac.uk/id/eprint/42590/7/42590_WILLIAMS_%20Policing_the_crisis_in_the_21st_Century_The_making_of_knife_crime_youths_in_Britain.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","unambiguously assigned as a black crime"),SRCQ("Policing the Crisis: Mugging, the State and Law and Order, 2nd edition (Open Research Online, The Open University)","https://oro.open.ac.uk/37679","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","fuelled and indeed, to a certain extent, created by the very institutions")],person:"Stuart Hall (1932–2014), cultural theorist, with co-authors",impact:"A 2015 review in Contemporary British History describes the book's enduring influence and its 'remarkably prescient' ideas.",kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"services",h:"Government and public services",intro:"Official records and reviews show that stereotypes about Black people shaped immigration policy, schooling, policing and healthcare in the UK, and that some clinical tools carried 19th-century ideas into modern practice.",points:[
  {h:"1950s Home Office reports repeated stereotypes of Caribbean workers",text:"By December 1953 the Home Office had produced five internal reports on colonial migration that linked Black and Asian workers with supposed laziness, poor discipline and low intellect. A 1953 Ministry of Labour note listed employers' objections such as 'low output' and 'irresponsibility' without testing them.",figure:"",year:"1950–1954",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The historical roots of the Windrush scandal: independent research report (Home Office, published 26 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal-independent-research-report-accessible","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","each of which had drawn associations between the supposed laziness, ill-discipline and low intellect of 'coloured workers'."),SRCQ("Race relations in industry 1953, HO 344/105 (The National Archives)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/commonwealth-migration-since-1945/race-relations-in-industry-1953/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","their irresponsibility, quarrelsomeness and lack of discipline")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"1960s–70s immigration laws aimed to reduce migration of people who were not white",text:"A history written for Home Office staff after the Windrush scandal concluded that major immigration laws in 1962, 1968 and 1971 were designed to reduce the proportion of people in the UK who did not have white skin. The Home Office published it only in September 2024, after a tribunal ordered its release.",figure:"",year:"1962–1971 (report published 2024)",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The historical roots of the Windrush scandal, executive summary point 2 (Home Office, 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal-independent-research-report-accessible","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","designed to reduce the proportion of people living in the United Kingdom"),SRCQ("Home Office internal 'roots of Windrush' report published after FoI battle (Civil Service World, 2024)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/home-office-internal-roots-of-windrush-report-published-foi-battle","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","openly devised to discriminate on the basis of ethnic and racial ancestry")],impact:"The report links the Windrush scandal to decades of race-based immigration law and was written to teach Home Office staff that history.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Windrush review: 'institutional ignorance' about race and history",text:"The independent Windrush Lessons Learned Review (2020) found that the Home Office had shown institutional ignorance and thoughtlessness about race and about the history of the Windrush generation. It recommended that staff learn about Britain's colonial history and the history of Black Britons.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Lessons Learned Review (HC 93, March 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e74984fd3bf7f4684279faa/6.5577_HO_Windrush_Lessons_Learned_Review_WEB_v2.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","institutional ignorance and thoughtlessness towards the issue of race and the history of the Windrush generation"),SRCQ("Statement on Windrush lessons learned review (UK Parliament, March 2020)","https://webtest.parliament.uk/business/news/2020/march/statement-windrush-lessons-learned-review","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","institutional ignorance and thoughtlessness towards the issue of race")],impact:"The review led the Home Office to commission the historical report on the roots of the scandal, published in 2024.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Swann Report: stereotyped views affected West Indian pupils",text:"The Swann Report, 'Education for All' (1985), and its 1981 interim report concluded that racism, intentional and unintentional, had an important bearing on the performance of West Indian children, partly through teachers' stereotyped or patronising views. When the report was published the Education Secretary, Sir Keith Joseph, told Parliament he did not accept that racism explained underachievement.",figure:"",year:"1981–1985",disputed:true,note:"The committee concluded that racism had a direct and important bearing on West Indian pupils' performance (Swann Report, chapter 2). The Education Secretary said in the Commons on 14 March 1985 that he had seen 'precious little evidence of any racist prejudice' among teachers.",src:[SRCQ("Education for All: report of the Committee of Inquiry into the Education of Children from Ethnic Minority Groups (HMSO, 1985), Annex A para 9 and chapter 2","https://www.education-uk.org/documents/swann/swann1985.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","both intentional and unintentional, has a direct and important bearing on the performance of West Indian children in our schools"),SRCQ("Ethnic Minority Children (Education), Hansard HC Deb 14 March 1985 (UK Parliament)","https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1985/mar/14/ethnic-minority-children-education","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","in whom I have seen precious little evidence of any racist prejudice.")],impact:"The report recommended that education should combat racism and challenge inherited myths and stereotypes, but the government of the day disputed its findings on teacher prejudice.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"False beliefs about biology linked to under-rating Black pain",text:"A 2016 study in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences found that half of a sample of white US medical students and residents endorsed at least one false belief about biological differences, such as that Black people have thicker skin. Those who held such beliefs rated a Black patient's pain lower and made less accurate treatment recommendations.",figure:"",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Racial bias in pain assessment and treatment recommendations, and false beliefs about biological differences between blacks and whites, PNAS 2016;113(16):4296–301 (PubMed)","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27044069/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","half of a sample of white medical students and residents endorsed these beliefs"),SRCQ("University of Virginia news release on the study (University of Virginia, 2016)","https://news.virginia.edu/node/211661","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","half of the sample endorsed at least one of the false beliefs")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black women's pain dismissed in UK maternity care",text:"A 2022 inquiry by the charity Birthrights heard that health professionals described Black women and babies as having 'thick, tough skin', and that women's pain was often dismissed. A 2023 House of Commons committee report on Black maternal health recorded that staff sometimes repeated stereotypes about Black women's pain.",figure:"",year:"2022–2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Systemic racism, not broken bodies: executive summary (Birthrights, May 2022)","https://www.birthrights.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Birthrights-inquiry-systemic-racism_exec-summary_May-22-web.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","thick, tough skin"),SRCQ("Black maternal health, Third Report of Session 2022–23, para 13 (House of Commons Women and Equalities Committee, 2023)","https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm5803/cmselect/cmwomeq/94/report.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","such as black women having lower pain thresholds")],impact:"A parliamentary committee accepted concerns about racial bias from medical staff and called for action on disparities in maternal health.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Lung tests carried a 'race correction' rooted in slavery-era claims",text:"Lung-function tests long applied lower 'normal' values for Black patients. Historians trace this to Thomas Jefferson's speculation about the lungs of enslaved people and to Samuel Cartwright, a pro-slavery physician who used a spirometer to claim a 20 per cent 'deficiency'. In 2023 the American Thoracic Society recommended race-neutral equations, and in 2025 the UK's Association for Respiratory Technology and Physiology backed the race-neutral GLI Global equations.",figure:"",year:"1785–2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Race and Ethnicity in Pulmonary Function Test Interpretation: An Official American Thoracic Society Statement, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2023;207(8):978–995","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10112445","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","PFT laboratories should adopt a race-neutral approach to PFT interpretation"),SRCQ("Race, ethnicity and lung function: A brief history, Canadian Journal of Respiratory Therapy 2015;51(4):99–101","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4631137","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","It is time to rethink the problematic practice of race correction in light of this history."),SRCQ("ARTP race neutral position statement (Respiratory Futures, August 2025)","https://www.respiratoryfutures.org.uk/programmes-pages/health-inequalities/health-inequalities-resources/artp-race-neutral-position-statement/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Self-identified race/ethnicity will no longer be required for lung function interpretation.")],impact:"UK lung-function services are moving to equations that no longer require a patient's race or ethnicity.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Child Q: 'adultification' named as a likely factor",text:"A 2022 safeguarding practice review into the strip-search of a Black 15-year-old girl at a Hackney school found that racism was likely an influencing factor and named 'adultification', where Black children are treated as older and less in need of protection than their white peers.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Child Q: powerful set of actions undertaken by Council (Hackney Council, 2022)","https://news.hackney.gov.uk/news/child-q-powerful-set-of-actions-undertaken-by-council-to-better-protect-residents-from-harm-and-racism","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","where Black children are treated more harshly than their white peers"),SRCQ("Local Child Safeguarding Practice Review: comment by BASW (British Association of Social Workers, 21 March 2022)","https://basw.co.uk/articles/local-child-safeguarding-practice-review-comment-basw","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","specifically notes 'adultification' as a key factor")],impact:"Hackney Council expanded adultification training to all schools and pressed ministers to review strip-search guidance.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black children far more likely to be strip-searched by police",text:"After Child Q, the Children's Commissioner for England analysed police strip-searches of children. Black children were about six times more likely to be strip-searched than their share of the population suggested between 2018 and 2022, and about four times more likely in 2022–23.",figure:"",year:"2023–2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Child strip searched every 14 hours by police in England and Wales (Children's Commissioner for England, 19 August 2024)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/news-and-blogs/child-strip-searched-every-14-hours-by-police-in-england-and-wales/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","six times more likely between 2018 and 2022"),SRCQ("Black children six times likelier to be strip-searched: UK report (Al Jazeera, 27 March 2023)","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/3/27/black-children-six-times-likelier-to-be-strip-searched-uk-report","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","More than a third of the 2,847 searches were of Black children")],impact:"The Commissioner reported widespread non-compliance with safeguards, including searches without an appropriate adult present.",kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"perception",h:"Public perception",intro:"Surveys and media studies show that racial prejudice and racialised stereotypes remain part of British life, even though open prejudice has declined since the 1980s.",points:[
  {h:"A quarter or more of Britons admit racial prejudice",text:"The British Social Attitudes survey has asked people since the 1980s whether they are prejudiced against people of other races. The share saying 'very' or 'a little' prejudiced was 38% in 1987, 30% in 2013 and 26% in 2017.",figure:"",year:"1987–2017",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Would you describe yourself as very or a little prejudiced? (LSE Equity, Diversity and Inclusion blog, June 2014)","https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/diversity/2014/06/would-you-describe-yourself-as-very-or-a-little-prejudiced-report-from-the-british-social-attitudes-survey-2013","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","30% people self-declared prejudice on the basis of race"),SRCQ("NatCen report: Racial Prejudice in Britain Today (Equally Ours, summarising NatCen, September 2017)","https://www.equallyours.org.uk/natcen-report-racial-prejudice-in-britain-today/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","26% of respondents to the 2017 British Social Attitudes Survey describe themselves as 'very' or 'a little' racially prejudiced."),SRCQ("Racial prejudice in Britain today (Runnymede Trust, 2017)","https://www.runnymedetrust.org/publications/racial-prejudice-in-britain-today","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","one in four people in Britain admitted to being prejudiced towards people of other races.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Survey of 14,000 people finds widespread racism against Black groups",text:"The Evidence for Equality National Survey (2023), run by university researchers, found that almost one in six minority ethnic respondents had suffered a racially motivated physical assault. Around half of Black Caribbean and other Black respondents reported racial discrimination in education, and over a third reported discrimination by the police.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("More than a third of people from minority groups in the UK have experienced racist assaults, survey finds (King's College London, April 2023)","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/news/more-than-a-third-of-people-from-minority-groups-in-the-uk-have-experienced-racist-assaults-survey-finds","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","almost one in six respondents had experienced a racially motivated physical assault"),SRCQ("Over a third of people from minority groups have experienced racist assaults, survey finds (University of Manchester, April 2023)","https://www.manchester.ac.uk/discover/news/racist-assaults-survey-finds/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","over a third of people from the Black Caribbean, Any Other Black, Roma and Gypsy/Traveller groups")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"answers",h:"Answering back: 20th-century counter-scholarship",intro:"Throughout the century, scholars and international bodies challenged racial hierarchy with evidence. Some of these debates continue.",points:[
  {h:"Du Bois in London, 1900: 'the problem of the colour line'",text:"At the 1900 Pan-African Conference in London, W.E.B. Du Bois's address 'To the Nations of the World' declared that the problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the colour line. He went on to organise four Pan-African Congresses between 1919 and 1927.",figure:"",year:"1900–1927",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("'To the Nations of the World (1900)', in A. Getachew and J. Pitts (eds), W. E. B. Du Bois: International Thought (Cambridge University Press, 2022)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/w-e-b-du-bois-international-thought/to-the-nations-of-the-world-1900/F39373DE0C7CF0C12002FED832742882","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the color line."),SRCQ("W.E.B. Du Bois (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/W-E-B-Du-Bois","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the architect of four Pan-African Congresses held between 1919 and 1927")],person:"W.E.B. Du Bois (1868–1963), sociologist and historian",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Black Reconstruction overturned a racist school of history",text:"In Black Reconstruction (1935), Du Bois challenged the dominant 'Dunning School', which held that Black Americans had been unfit for the rights granted after the US Civil War. His chapter on 'the propaganda of history' criticised how textbooks portrayed the period.",figure:"",year:"1935",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Eric Gardner, 'Black Reconstructions: Introduction', African American Literature in Transition, 1865–1880 (Cambridge University Press), excerpt","https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/27470/excerpt/9781108427470_excerpt.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The treatment of the period of Reconstruction reflects small credit upon American historians as scientists."),SRCQ("The Dunning School: The Biased Study of Reconstruction that Shaped 'Gone With the Wind' (Atlanta History Center)","https://www.atlantahistorycenter.com/blog/the-dunning-school-the-biased-study-of-reconstruction-that-shaped-gone-with-the-wind/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Today, Du Bois' work still forms the basis of most historians' interpretations of Reconstruction.")],person:"W.E.B. Du Bois (1868–1963)",impact:"Historians now treat his interpretation as the basis of most modern accounts of Reconstruction.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Cheikh Anta Diop placed Africa at the origin of civilisations",text:"In Nations nègres et culture (1954) and later work, the Senegalese scholar Cheikh Anta Diop argued that Africa was the source of many forms of human civilisation, including that of ancient Egypt. He proposed the 1974 UNESCO symposium in Cairo on the peopling of ancient Egypt.",figure:"",year:"1954–1986",disputed:true,note:"Diop's thesis that ancient Egyptian civilisation was Black African remains contested among Egyptologists and historians; Encyclopaedia Universalis notes 'Des jugements contradictoires, souvent passionnels, ont été portés sur ses travaux' (contradictory, often passionate judgements). His challenge to the idea that Africa had no history is widely recognised.",src:[SRCQ("Jean Devisse, 'Diop Cheikh Anta (1923–1986)' (Encyclopaedia Universalis)","https://www.universalis.fr/encyclopedie/cheikh-anta-diop/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","l'Afrique était à l'origine de bon nombre de formes des civilisations humaines"),SRCQ("Cheikh Anta Diop's short biography (Musée des Civilisations noires, Dakar)","https://mcn.sn/?p=1424","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","an international conference was held in Cairo from January 28 to February 3, 1974")],person:"Cheikh Anta Diop (1923–1986), historian and physicist",impact:"Encyclopaedia Universalis records that French academia was slow and reluctant to recognise his work.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Psychologists found no direct evidence for a genetic race gap",text:"After a decade of controversy, the American Psychological Association set up a task force whose 1996 report, 'Intelligence: Knowns and Unknowns', reviewed group differences in test scores. It found no direct evidence for a genetic explanation of the Black–White average score gap.",figure:"",year:"1996",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Neisser et al., 'Intelligence: Knowns and Unknowns', American Psychologist 51(2), 1996 (University of North Florida research portal record)","https://scholars.unf.edu/en/publications/intelligence-knowns-and-unknowns-2/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a task force established by the American Psychological Association"),SRCQ("Ulric Neisser, 'Never a Dull Moment', American Psychologist, January 1997 (reply to commentaries on the task force report)","https://www.Gwern.net/doc/iq/1997-neisser.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","no direct evidence for a genetic interpretation of the Black-White IQ difference")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Gould's Mismeasure of Man and the skull dispute",text:"In The Mismeasure of Man (1981), Stephen Jay Gould argued that racial science, from 19th-century skull measurement to IQ testing, was shaped by its authors' prejudices. His case against Samuel Morton's skull data has been re-examined since 2011.",figure:"",year:"1981–2015",disputed:true,note:"Lewis et al. (PLOS Biology, 2011) re-measured Morton's skulls and concluded that Morton did not manipulate his data and that Gould's own analysis was biased. Weisberg (2014) and Kaplan, Pigliucci and Banta (2015) argue that most of Gould's criticisms stand and that Morton's analysis was inappropriate and misleading, though Kaplan et al. also say Gould was wrong to think a better analysis was available.",src:[SRCQ("J. E. Lewis et al., 'The Mismeasure of Science: Stephen Jay Gould versus Samuel George Morton on Skulls and Bias', PLOS Biology, 2011","https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.1001071","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","demonstrating that Morton did not manipulate data to support his preconceptions, contra Gould"),SRCQ("M. Weisberg, 'Remeasuring man', Evolution and Development 16(3), 2014","https://researchportalplus.anu.edu.au/en/publications/remeasuring-man/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Morton did indeed mismeasure his skulls in ways that conformed to 19th century racial biases."),SRCQ("J. M. Kaplan, M. Pigliucci and J. A. Banta, 'Gould on Morton, Redux', Studies in History and Philosophy of Biological and Biomedical Sciences 52, 2015","https://philarchive.org/rec/KAPGOM-2","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","We take strong exception to Lewis et al.'s thesis that Morton was \"right.\"")],person:"Stephen Jay Gould (1941–2002), palaeontologist, Harvard University",kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"academia",h:"Universities and learned societies face their past",intro:"Since 2018 several UK universities and learned societies have investigated how they helped create, honour or profit from ideas of racial hierarchy, and have changed names, published reports or withdrawn research as a result.",points:[
  {h:"UCL inquiry into eugenics and removal of Galton and Pearson names",text:"University College London commissioned an independent inquiry into its history of eugenics, which reported in February 2020. In June 2020 UCL removed the names of Francis Galton, who coined the word 'eugenics', and Karl Pearson, the first holder of the eugenics chair Galton funded, from a lecture theatre and a building. In January 2021 UCL issued a formal public apology. UCL formally apologised for its history and legacy of eugenics on 7 January 2021.",figure:"",year:"2020–2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UCL denames buildings named after eugenicists (UCL News, 19 June 2020)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2020/jun/ucl-denames-buildings-named-after-eugenicists","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Francis Galton coined the term eugenics"),SRCQ("UCL to begin process of renaming buildings named after eugenicists (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/690052","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","rename spaces and awards where the namesake is associated with eugenics"),SRCQ("UCL makes formal public apology for its history and legacy of eugenics (UCL News, 7 January 2021)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2021/jan/ucl-makes-formal-public-apology-its-history-and-legacy-eugenics","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","We apologise for honouring individuals who were leading eugenicists through the naming of spaces on campus."),SRCQ("Bricks + Mortals (UCL Culture)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-collections/programmes-and-exhibitions/about-us/projects/bricks-mortals-history-eugenics-told-through-buildings","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","On 07 January 2021, UCL issued a formal public apology for its history and legacy of eugenics"),SRCQ("UCL makes formal apology for its history and legacy of eugenics (The Boar, January 2021)","https://theboar.org/2021/01/ucl-makes-formal-apology-for-its-history-and-legacy-of-eugenics","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In a statement issued on 7 January, the university expressed “deep regret” for its involvement.")],impact:"The inquiry's main recommendation, accepted in principle, was to rename spaces and awards linked to eugenics; the Galton Lecture Theatre became Lecture Theatre 115 and the Pearson Building the North-West Wing.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Galton Institute renames itself the Adelphi Genetics Forum",text:"The learned society founded as the Eugenics Education Society, later the Galton Institute, changed its name to the Adelphi Genetics Forum in 2021. Its trustees said Galton's name had become a barrier because of his racism and eugenics, and the society now explicitly rejects those ideas.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Adelphi Genetics Forum: an evolving organisation (The Biologist, Royal Society of Biology)","https://thebiologist.rsb.org.uk/biologist-interviews/adelphi-genetics-forum-an-evolving-organisation","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","growing public awareness of Galton's racism and eugenics made his name toxic,"),SRCQ("Why the Galton Institute is now called the Adelphi Genetics Forum (Adelphi Genetics Forum)","https://adelphigenetics.org/adelphi-genetics-forum-name/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Keeping the Galton name suggests that, at best, we are insensitive to the connotations")],impact:"A society that once existed to promote eugenics now runs public conferences on 'living with the eugenic past' and argues that biology students should learn about eugenics and ethics.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Glasgow: first UK university to count its gains from slavery",text:"In 2018 the University of Glasgow published 'Slavery, Abolition and the University of Glasgow', which estimated that gifts and bequests linked to slavery were worth up to almost £200 million in today's money. It began a programme of 'reparative justice', including a partnership with the University of the West Indies and a centre for the study of slavery.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Slavery in Britain: making reparation (University of Glasgow)","https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/avenue/world-changingresearch/theirstoryisourstory/makingreparation/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","estimated as up to almost £200 million in today's money"),SRCQ("Universities urged to follow Glasgow's lead on slave trade past (Times Higher Education, 2018)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/659531","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the University of Glasgow may have benefited by up to £200 million")],impact:"The report prompted calls for every UK university to examine how it profited from slavery.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cambridge finds its scholars helped justify racism and slavery",text:"The University of Cambridge's Advisory Group on Legacies of Enslavement reported in September 2022. Besides money from slavery, it found that Cambridge writers had produced ideas used to justify enslavement, including an early claim that differences between white and Black people were biological, and that 20th-century Cambridge thought connected to rigid ideas of race within eugenics.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Advisory Group on Legacies of Enslavement: Final Report, section 4.2.6 (University of Cambridge, 2022)","https://www.legaciesofenslavement.cam.ac.uk/sites/default/files/University%20of%20Cambridge%20Advisory%20Group%20on%20Legacies%20of%20Enslavement%20Final%20Report%2021-09-2022.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the start of a long line of Cambridge thought that was used to justify racism and slavery"),SRCQ("Cambridge University received 'significant benefits' from slavery, inquiry finds (ITV News, 22 September 2022)","https://www.itv.com/news/anglia/2022-09-22/university-received-significant-benefits-from-slavery-inquiry-finds","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","promoting some of the ideas that underpinned the practice of enslavement")],impact:"The university committed to a permanent research centre, studentships for Black British students and scholarships for African and Caribbean students, and set up a working group on renaming the Haddon and Seeley libraries.",kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"reckoning",h:"Counter-moves: science bodies, museums and schools",intro:"Scientific organisations have publicly rejected race science, and museums and curricula in the UK have changed what they display and teach.",points:[
  {h:"UNESCO 1950: race 'less a biological fact than a social myth'",text:"In July 1950 UNESCO published 'The Race Question', drafted mainly by the anthropologist Ashley Montagu with a panel of experts. It said that all humans belong to one species and put group differences in measured intelligence down mainly to environment. A revised 1951 statement by geneticists and physical anthropologists partly stepped back, while still rejecting the use of mental traits to classify races.",figure:"",year:"1950–1951",disputed:true,note:"The 1950 text was criticised at the time for its statements on intelligence and its sociological approach; UNESCO's 1951 revision accepted a division of humanity into major groups on physical traits only (Duedahl; Alberta Eugenics Archive).",src:[SRCQ("From Racial Strangers to Ethnic Minorities: on the socio-political impact of UNESCO, 1945–60 (Aalborg University repository)","https://vbn.aau.dk/files/61248534/From_Racial_Strangers_to_Ethnic_Minorities_Paper_Poul_Duedahl.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","race is less a biological fact than a social myth"),SRCQ("UNESCO 'The Race Question', 18 July 1950 (Eugenics Archives, Canada)","https://eugenicsarchive.ca/discover/timeline/53235f3a132156674b000254","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","more properly explained in terms of environmental factors"),SRCQ("M. C. Maio and R. V. Santos, 'Antiracism and the uses of science in the post-World War II: An analysis of UNESCO's first statements on race (1950 and 1951)', Vibrant 12(2), 2015","https://new.scielo.br/j/vb/a/LDkfPSxqXPbcdH49DBXCQGP/?lang=en","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Race is less a biological fact than a social myth")],person:"Ashley Montagu (died 1999), anthropologist, rapporteur",impact:"The statement was widely reported in the press and proposed replacing the idea of 'race' with that of 'ethnic groups'.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"US human genetics society apologises for its eugenic past",text:"In January 2023 the American Society of Human Genetics apologised for the part some of its founders, past presidents and other leaders played in eugenics, and for staying silent when genetics was used to support racism. Its report found that nine early leaders held posts in the American Eugenics Society.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("ASHG documents and apologizes for past harms of human genetics research (American Society of Human Genetics, 24 January 2023)","https://www.ashg.org/?p=22783","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","ASHG acknowledges and apologizes for the participation of some ASHG founders, past presidents, and other leaders"),SRCQ("Genetics society issues apology for ties to eugenics and racism (Smithsonian Magazine, 2023)","https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/genetics-society-issues-apology-for-ties-to-eugenics-and-racism-180981530/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Nine of its early leaders had leadership positions within the American Eugenics Society")],impact:"The society suspended naming its awards after individuals while it reviewed their links to eugenics or discrimination.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Pitt Rivers Museum removes human remains that fed racist thinking",text:"Over the summer of 2020, Oxford's Pitt Rivers Museum removed 120 human remains, including shrunken heads, from open display after a three-year ethical review. The museum said audience research showed visitors read the displays as showing other cultures as 'savage' or 'primitive'.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Critical Changes (Pitt Rivers Museum, University of Oxford)","https://www.prm.ox.ac.uk/node/1268656","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","reinforced racist and stereotypical thinking that goes against the Museum's values today"),SRCQ("Pitt Rivers Museum removes shrunken heads from display after ethical review (Museums Association, 18 September 2020)","https://www.museumsassociation.org/museums-journal/news/2020/09/pitt-rivers-museum-removes-shrunken-heads-from-display-after-ethical-review/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","three-year ethical review of the museum's displays and programming")],impact:"The museum said its displays had reinforced racist and stereotypical thinking, and that removal brought it into line with sector guidelines.",kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Wellcome Collection closes its 'Medicine Man' gallery",text:"In November 2022 the Wellcome Collection in London closed its long-running 'Medicine Man' display. It said the gallery still presented a version of medical history based on racist, sexist and ableist theories, in which Black people, Indigenous peoples and disabled people were exoticised and exploited.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Wellcome Collection speaks out on Medicine Man closure (Museums Association, November 2022)","https://www.museumsassociation.org/museums-journal/news/2022/11/wellcome-collection-speaks-out-on-medicine-man-closure","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","still perpetuates a version of medical history that is based on racist, sexist and ableist theories and language"),SRCQ("London museum Wellcome Collection closes 'racist, sexist and ableist' exhibit (Euronews, 28 November 2022)","https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/28/london-museum-wellcome-collection-closes-racist-sexist-and-ableist-exhibit","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","exoticised, marginalised and exploited")],kind:"counter-argument"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"These ideas were made by named people, often with interests in slavery or empire, and Black writers answered them from the start.",refs:["sb-plantation-edward-long-s-history-of-jamaica", "sb-plantation-equiano-s-interesting-narrative", "sb-racescience-firmin-s-the-equality-of-the-human-races"]},{t:"If a test, a school report or a clinician seems to see you through a stereotype, inquiries such as Rampton and Swann named the same pattern.",refs:["sb-britain-a-government-inquiry-named-low-teacher-e", "sb-services-swann-report-stereotyped-views-affected-"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"When you hear a 'common sense' claim about Black people, ask where it comes from: much race science has been retracted or disproved.",refs:["sb-iq-a-journal-retracted-a-rushton-paper-as-u", "sb-iq-richard-lynn-s-national-iq-data-led-to-a", "sb-answers-psychologists-found-no-direct-evidence-f"]},{t:"Quote the people who answered the stereotypes, not only those who made them.",refs:["sb-answers-du-bois-in-london-1900-the-problem-of-th", "sb-racescience-douglass-answers-the-ethnologists"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Low expectations can become a self-fulfilling prophecy. Check how assessment, appraisal and promotion treat Black staff.",refs:["sb-britain-a-government-inquiry-named-low-teacher-e", "sb-britain-caribbean-children-were-over-placed-in-e"]},{t:"Institutions can face their past openly, as UCL, Glasgow and Cambridge have done.",refs:["sb-academia-ucl-inquiry-into-eugenics-and-removal-of", "sb-academia-glasgow-first-uk-university-to-count-its", "sb-academia-cambridge-finds-its-scholars-helped-just"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Audit rules and clinical tools for race adjustments and stereotypes; lung tests and maternity care show how old ideas persist.",refs:["sb-services-lung-tests-carried-a-race-correction-roo", "sb-services-black-women-s-pain-dismissed-in-uk-mater", "sb-services-false-beliefs-about-biology-linked-to-un"]},{t:"Learn the history behind a policy before changing it, as the Windrush review recommended.",refs:["sb-services-windrush-review-institutional-ignorance-", "sb-services-1960s-70s-immigration-laws-aimed-to-redu"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Data can carry prejudice: check where a dataset came from and who built it before using it.",refs:["sb-iq-richard-lynn-s-national-iq-data-led-to-a", "sb-eugenics-a-eugenics-journal-set-out-to-study-raci"]},{t:"Report attitude measures with their exact question and year; self-reported prejudice understates the experience reported by Black people.",refs:["sb-perception-a-quarter-or-more-of-britons-admit-racia", "sb-perception-survey-of-14-000-people-finds-widespread"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Hume and Kant wrote that Black people were inferior; how central this was to Kant is debated.",refs:["sb-enlight-hume-s-footnote-on-national-characters", "sb-enlight-kant-defines-race-and-ranks-humanity"]},{t:"Later, race science ranked races by skull size and claimed civilisation was the work of white races.",refs:["sb-racescience-samuel-morton-s-skull-collection", "sb-racescience-gobineau-s-essay-on-the-inequality-of-hu"]},{t:"Galton founded eugenics, and UCL apologised for its history and legacy of eugenics in 2021.",refs:["sb-racescience-galton-and-the-founding-of-eugenics", "sb-academia-ucl-inquiry-into-eugenics-and-removal-of"]},{t:"In the 1960s and 1970s, Caribbean children in Britain were over-placed in schools for the 'educationally subnormal'.",refs:["sb-britain-caribbean-children-were-over-placed-in-e"]},{t:"False beliefs about Black people's skin and pain still affect health care.",refs:["sb-services-false-beliefs-about-biology-linked-to-un", "sb-services-black-women-s-pain-dismissed-in-uk-mater"]},{t:"Black writers answered at every stage, from Equiano and Firmin to Du Bois and Diop.",refs:["sb-plantation-equiano-s-interesting-narrative", "sb-racescience-firmin-s-the-equality-of-the-human-races", "sb-answers-cheikh-anta-diop-placed-africa-at-the-or"]}]}],time:[{ys:1753,y:"1753–54",t:"Hume's footnote on 'national characters'",src:[SRCQ("James Beattie (1735—1803) (Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://iep.utm.edu/james-beattie/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","I am apt to suspect the negroes to be naturally inferior to the whites.")]},{ys:1758,y:"1758 (10th edition of Systema Naturae)",t:"Linnaeus ranks human 'varieties' by continent",src:[SRCQ("Linnaeus and Race (Linnean Society of London)","https://www.linnean.org/learning/who-was-linnaeus/linnaeus-and-race","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","One variety never varied in this hierarchy, however: Africanus consistently remained at the bottom of the list.")]},{ys:1770,y:"1770",t:"Beattie answers Hume in 1770",src:[SRCQ("James Beattie (1735—1803) (Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","https://iep.utm.edu/james-beattie/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The Africans and Americans are known to have many ingenious manufactures and arts among them")]},{ys:1770,y:"1770s–1790s",t:"Kant defines 'race' and ranks humanity",src:[SRCQ("Inder S. Marwah, 'White Progress: Kant, Race and Teleology', Kantian Review 27 (2022), 615–634 (Cambridge University Press)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D226D581B530439C81DF60228DAF3C29/S1369415422000334a.pdf/div-class-title-white-progress-kant-race-and-teleology-div.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","[h]umanity has its highest degree of perfection in the white race.")]},{ys:1774,y:"1774",t:"Edward Long's History of Jamaica",src:[SRCQ("Enlightenment Race Science in the Colonies: Edward Long and the… (University of Michigan, Science, Technology and Society Program)","https://prod.lsa.umich.edu/sts/news-events/all-events/archived-events/2014/01/enlightenment-race-science-in-the-colonies--edward-long-and-the-.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Long was a polygenist, believing that the different races of Man did not share a common origin.")]},{ys:1785,y:"1785–1791",t:"Banneker challenges Jefferson's doubts",src:[SRCQ("Letter to Thomas Jefferson from Benjamin Banneker, August 19, 1791 (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Letter-to-Thomas-Jefferson-from-Benjamin-Banneker-August-19-1791","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","he had detailed his skepticism of Black intellect in his 1785 work Notes on the State of Virginia")]},{ys:1785,y:"1785–2025",t:"Lung tests carried a 'race correction' rooted in slavery-era claims",src:[SRCQ("Race and Ethnicity in Pulmonary Function Test Interpretation: An Official American Thoracic Society Statement, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2023;207(8):978–995","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10112445","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","PFT laboratories should adopt a race-neutral approach to PFT interpretation")]},{ys:1787,y:"1787",t:"Cugoano demands immediate abolition",src:[SRCQ("Ottobah Cugoano – blue plaque (English Heritage)","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/ottobah-cugoano/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","calling for its immediate abolition and the emancipation of enslaved peoples.")]},{ys:1789,y:"1789",t:"Equiano's Interesting Narrative",src:[SRCQ("Olaudah Equiano (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Olaudah-Equiano","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","whose 1789 autobiography, detailing his life as an enslaved person, was immensely popular and influential")]},{ys:1795,y:"1795",t:"Blumenbach names five varieties, including 'Caucasian'",src:[SRCQ("Blumenbach and the concept of race (University of Göttingen)","https://uni-goettingen.de/en/650077.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","He is best known for establishing a five-part naming system in 1795")]},{ys:1839,y:"1839",t:"Samuel Morton's skull collection",src:[SRCQ("Appendix 2: The University of Edinburgh, Theories of Race and Civilisation, and British Imperialism, by Ian Stewart (University of Edinburgh)","https://www.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2025-07/Appendix%202:%20The%20University%20of%20Edinburgh,%20Theories%20of%20Race%20and%20Civilisation,%20and%20British%20Imperialism,%20by%20Ian%20Stewart.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the largest skull collection in North America")]},{ys:1849,y:"1849; reissued 1853",t:"Carlyle's 'Occasional Discourse'",src:[SRCQ("Speech beyond Toleration: On Carlyle and Moral Controversialism Now (Oxford University Research Archive)","https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:ea29f182-95b8-4061-8bbf-67513e6b6fb4/files/m60e7ce9522815995a4307da35b29c1a2","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the divine right of being compelled (if \"permitted\" will not answer) to do what work they are appointed for.")]},{ys:1850,y:"1850",t:"Mill's reply: 'The Negro Question'",src:[SRCQ("Speech beyond Toleration: On Carlyle and Moral Controversialism Now (Oxford University Research Archive)","https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:ea29f182-95b8-4061-8bbf-67513e6b6fb4/files/m60e7ce9522815995a4307da35b29c1a2","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","copied from the wildest prophecies of the slavery party before emancipation")]},{ys:1850,y:"1850",t:"Robert Knox and The Races of Men",src:[SRCQ("Colonialism and science (University of Glasgow)","https://www.gla.ac.uk/media/Media_834239_smxx.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In his book, 'The Races of Men', Dr Robert Knox, a Scottish anatomist and ethnologist, highlighted recent rebellions")]},{ys:1853,y:"1853–55",t:"Gobineau's Essay on the Inequality of Human Races",src:[SRCQ("Race – Gobineau's Essay on the Inequality of Human Races (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/race-human/Gobineaus-Essay-on-the-Inequality-of-Human-Races","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","were all products of the white races and that no civilization could emerge without their cooperation.")]},{ys:1854,y:"1854",t:"Nott and Gliddon's Types of Mankind",src:[SRCQ("Polygenism – 'This Abominable Traffic': Physicians on Slavery (Center for the History of Medicine, Countway Library, Harvard)","https://collections.countway.harvard.edu/onview/exhibits/show/this-abominable-traffic/agassiz-and-polygenism/polygenism","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Physician Josiah Clark Nott (1804-1873) used early anthropological science to promote the idea of polygenism.")]},{ys:1854,y:"1854",t:"Douglass answers the ethnologists",src:[SRCQ("Frederick Douglass – Our Stories (Case Western Reserve University)","https://case.edu/bicentennial/history/our-stories/frederick-douglass","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In 1854, the civil rights leader delivered an address to the literary societies during commencement week at Western Reserve College.")]},{ys:1863,y:"1863–64",t:"James Hunt and the Anthropological Society",src:[SRCQ("Paul Turnbull, 'British Anthropological Thought in Colonial Practice' (ANU Press)","https://press.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/p53561/mobile/ch04.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","A faction of about a dozen members led by James Hunt (1833-1869)")]},{ys:1865,y:"1865–1866",t:"Morant Bay and the Governor Eyre controversy",src:[SRCQ("Jamaica – British rule (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Jamaica/British-rule","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","who declared martial law, suppressed the rioters, and hanged the principal instigator, Paul Bogle,")]},{ys:1869,y:"1869–1883",t:"Galton and the founding of eugenics",src:[SRCQ("Hereditary Genius (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Hereditary-Genius","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Galton, in Hereditary Genius (1869), proposed that a system of arranged marriages between men of distinction")]},{ys:1885,y:"1885",t:"Firmin's The Equality of the Human Races",src:[SRCQ("The Equality of the Human Races (University of Illinois Press)","https://www.press.uillinois.edu/books/?id=p071027","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Marginalized for its \"radical\" position that the human races were equal,")]},{ys:1895,y:"1895–1900",t:"Karl Pearson framed race as a struggle won by the 'fitter race'",src:[SRCQ("Modern History Sourcebook: Karl Pearson, National Life from the Standpoint of Science, 1900 (Fordham University)","https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/mod/1900pearsonl.asp","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","kept up to a high pitch of external efficiency by contest, chiefly by way of war with inferior races")]},{ys:1900,y:"1900–1927",t:"Du Bois in London, 1900: 'the problem of the colour line'",src:[SRCQ("'To the Nations of the World (1900)', in A. Getachew and J. Pitts (eds), W. E. B. Du Bois: International Thought (Cambridge University Press, 2022)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/w-e-b-du-bois-international-thought/to-the-nations-of-the-world-1900/F39373DE0C7CF0C12002FED832742882","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the color line.")]},{ys:1907,y:"1907–1963",t:"Galton's money created a eugenics laboratory and chair at UCL",src:[SRCQ("Oxford DNB article: Pearson, Karl, by Joanne Woiak (Oxford University Press, reproduced by University of St Andrews MacTutor)","https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/DNB/Pearson.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","In 1911 he was appointed the first Galton professor of eugenics at University College.")]},{ys:1922,y:"1922",t:"Indirect rule governed Africans through fixed 'customary' units",src:[SRCQ("Frederick Lugard (Encyclopaedia Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/biography/F-D-Lugard","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","It greatly influenced British administration in Africa and beyond.")]},{ys:1924,y:"1924–1925",t:"Africans were put on show at the 1924 British Empire Exhibition",src:[SRCQ("Marc Matera, Afro-Metropolis: Black Life and Political Activism in Interwar London, chapter 1 (University of California Press)","https://content.ucpress.edu/chapters/12865.ch01.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Symbols of African backwardness and sexual innuendo pervaded press coverage of Baa.")]},{ys:1925,y:"1925–1954",t:"A eugenics journal set out to study 'racial problems'",src:[SRCQ("Catalogue record: Annals of eugenics (Wellcome Collection)","https://content.www.wellcomecollection.org/works/q6z3mjfn","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a journal for the scientific study of racial problems")]},{ys:1930,y:"1930",t:"The 1930 Fletcher Report stigmatised mixed-heritage children",src:[SRCQ("Mark Christian, 'The Fletcher Report 1930: A Historical Case Study of Contested Black Mixed Heritage Britishness', Journal of Historical Sociology 21(2–3), 2008 (Wiley; abstract and extracts reproduced by Mixed Race Studies)","https://www.mixedracestudies.org/?p=639","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the official outset in defining Liverpool's 'half castes' as a problem")]},{ys:1935,y:"1935",t:"Black Reconstruction overturned a racist school of history",src:[SRCQ("Eric Gardner, 'Black Reconstructions: Introduction', African American Literature in Transition, 1865–1880 (Cambridge University Press), excerpt","https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/27470/excerpt/9781108427470_excerpt.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The treatment of the period of Reconstruction reflects small credit upon American historians as scientists.")]},{ys:1937,y:"1937 onwards",t:"The Pioneer Fund and Mankind Quarterly sustained race science",src:[SRCQ("Francesco Cassata, 'Against UNESCO: Gedda, Gini and American Scientific Racism', Medicina nei Secoli 20(3), 2008 (Sapienza University of Rome)","https://rosa.uniroma1.it/rosa01/medicina_nei_secoli/article/download/391/365/741","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","made ample contributions to economically sustain the main adversaries of the American integrationist system")]},{ys:1950,y:"1950–1954",t:"1950s Home Office reports repeated stereotypes of Caribbean workers",src:[SRCQ("The historical roots of the Windrush scandal: independent research report (Home Office, published 26 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal-independent-research-report-accessible","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","each of which had drawn associations between the supposed laziness, ill-discipline and low intellect of 'coloured workers'.")]},{ys:1950,y:"1950–1951",t:"UNESCO 1950: race 'less a biological fact than a social myth'",src:[SRCQ("From Racial Strangers to Ethnic Minorities: on the socio-political impact of UNESCO, 1945–60 (Aalborg University repository)","https://vbn.aau.dk/files/61248534/From_Racial_Strangers_to_Ethnic_Minorities_Paper_Poul_Duedahl.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","race is less a biological fact than a social myth")]},{ys:1954,y:"1954–1986",t:"Cheikh Anta Diop placed Africa at the origin of civilisations",src:[SRCQ("Jean Devisse, 'Diop Cheikh Anta (1923–1986)' (Encyclopaedia Universalis)","https://www.universalis.fr/encyclopedie/cheikh-anta-diop/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","l'Afrique était à l'origine de bon nombre de formes des civilisations humaines")]},{ys:1960,y:"late 1960s–1980s",t:"A Nobel physicist campaigned on race and heredity",src:[SRCQ("Guide to the William Shockley papers (Stanford University Libraries, via Online Archive of California)","https://oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/kt3t1nf3ft/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","While working at Stanford, Shockley embraced and advocated for eugenics.")]},{ys:1960,y:"1960s–1970s",t:"Caribbean children were over-placed in 'ESN' schools",src:[SRCQ("Sally Tomlinson, 'Disgraceful Labelling: Race, Special Education and Exclusion' (Excluded Lives project, Department of Education, University of Oxford, 14 July 2021)","https://excludedlives.education.ox.ac.uk/disgraceful-labelling-race-special-education-and-exclusion/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The Inner London Education Authority noted in 1967 that 28% of children in ESN schools were 'immigrant'.")]},{ys:1962,y:"1962–1971 (report published 2024)",t:"1960s–70s immigration laws aimed to reduce migration of people who were not white",src:[SRCQ("The historical roots of the Windrush scandal, executive summary point 2 (Home Office, 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal/the-historical-roots-of-the-windrush-scandal-independent-research-report-accessible","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","designed to reduce the proportion of people living in the United Kingdom")]},{ys:1969,y:"1969",t:"Arthur Jensen claimed group IQ gaps were largely inherited",src:[SRCQ("In Memoriam: Arthur R. Jensen (University of California Academic Senate)","https://senate.universityofcalifornia.edu/_files/inmemoriam/html/ArthurR.Jensen.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","there is a substantial genetic contribution to both individual and group differences in intelligence.")]},{ys:1971,y:"1971",t:"A 1971 pamphlet exposed the labelling and mobilised parents",src:[SRCQ("A history of education campaigning (Black Cultural Archives, via Google Arts & Culture)","https://artsandculture.google.com/story/a-history-of-education-campaigning-black-cultural-archives/cQXhZVzwN7aDKg?hl=en","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Many activists and parents were galvanised into action by the publication of")]},{ys:1978,y:"1978",t:"Policing the Crisis showed how a 'mugging' panic was built",src:[SRCQ("Kieran Connell, 'Policing the Crisis 35 Years On', Contemporary British History 29(2), 2015 (Queen's University Belfast research portal)","https://pure.qub.ac.uk/en/publications/policing-the-crisis-35-years-on/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","The new edition is demonstrative of the book's enduring influence.")]},{ys:1981,y:"1981",t:"A government inquiry named low teacher expectations and prejudice",src:[SRCQ("West Indian Children in Our Schools: Interim report of the Committee of Inquiry into the Education of Children from Ethnic Minority Groups (HMSO, 1981), full text hosted by education-uk.org","https://education-uk.org/documents/rampton/rampton1981.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","low expectations of the academic ability of West Indian pupils by teachers can often prove a self-fulfilling prophecy.")]},{ys:1981,y:"1981–1985",t:"Swann Report: stereotyped views affected West Indian pupils",src:[SRCQ("Education for All: report of the Committee of Inquiry into the Education of Children from Ethnic Minority Groups (HMSO, 1985), Annex A para 9 and chapter 2","https://www.education-uk.org/documents/swann/swann1985.html","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","both intentional and unintentional, has a direct and important bearing on the performance of West Indian children in our schools")]},{ys:1981,y:"1981–2015",t:"Gould's Mismeasure of Man and the skull dispute",src:[SRCQ("J. E. Lewis et al., 'The Mismeasure of Science: Stephen Jay Gould versus Samuel George Morton on Skulls and Bias', PLOS Biology, 2011","https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.1001071","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","demonstrating that Morton did not manipulate data to support his preconceptions, contra Gould")]},{ys:1987,y:"1987–2017",t:"A quarter or more of Britons admit racial prejudice",src:[SRCQ("Would you describe yourself as very or a little prejudiced? (LSE Equity, Diversity and Inclusion blog, June 2014)","https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/diversity/2014/06/would-you-describe-yourself-as-very-or-a-little-prejudiced-report-from-the-british-social-attitudes-survey-2013","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","30% people self-declared prejudice on the basis of race")]},{ys:1996,y:"1996",t:"Psychologists found no direct evidence for a genetic race gap",src:[SRCQ("Neisser et al., 'Intelligence: Knowns and Unknowns', American Psychologist 51(2), 1996 (University of North Florida research portal record)","https://scholars.unf.edu/en/publications/intelligence-knowns-and-unknowns-2/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","a task force established by the American Psychological Association")]},{ys:2004,y:"2004–2024",t:"Richard Lynn's 'national IQ' data led to a Royal Society retraction",src:[SRCQ("Retraction: 'Parasite prevalence and the worldwide distribution of cognitive ability' (2010), Proceedings of the Royal Society B 291, 2024 (The Royal Society)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11060651/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the manifest problems in the data warranted retraction")]},{ys:2012,y:"2012; retracted 2020",t:"A journal retracted a Rushton paper as unsound in 2020",src:[SRCQ("Retraction notice for Rushton & Templer (2012), Personality and Individual Differences (Elsevier), as reproduced in Retraction Watch, 29 June 2020","https://retractionwatch.com/2020/06/29/journal-calls-2012-paper-deeply-offensive-to-particular-minorities","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Rushton & Templer ignored obvious social and educational explanations for higher levels of violence, HIV infection etc.")]},{ys:2016,y:"2016",t:"False beliefs about biology linked to under-rating Black pain",src:[SRCQ("Racial bias in pain assessment and treatment recommendations, and false beliefs about biological differences between blacks and whites, PNAS 2016;113(16):4296–301 (PubMed)","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27044069/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","half of a sample of white medical students and residents endorsed these beliefs")]},{ys:2018,y:"2018",t:"Glasgow: first UK university to count its gains from slavery",src:[SRCQ("Slavery in Britain: making reparation (University of Glasgow)","https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/avenue/world-changingresearch/theirstoryisourstory/makingreparation/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","estimated as up to almost £200 million in today's money")]},{ys:2020,y:"September 2020",t:"Edinburgh removes Hume's name from a building",src:[SRCQ("Making sense of silenced archives: Hume, Scotland and the 'debate' about the humanity of Black people (University of Edinburgh, Race and Decoloniality blog, 2021)","https://www.race.ed.ac.uk/blog/2021/making-sense-silenced-archives-hume-scotland-and-debate-about-humanity-black-people","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","found itself at the centre of international scrutiny after temporarily renaming the David Hume Tower (now referred to by its street designation 40 George Square).")]},{ys:2020,y:"2020",t:"Windrush review: 'institutional ignorance' about race and history",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Lessons Learned Review (HC 93, March 2020)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e74984fd3bf7f4684279faa/6.5577_HO_Windrush_Lessons_Learned_Review_WEB_v2.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","institutional ignorance and thoughtlessness towards the issue of race and the history of the Windrush generation")]},{ys:2020,y:"2020–2021",t:"UCL inquiry into eugenics and removal of Galton and Pearson names",src:[SRCQ("UCL denames buildings named after eugenicists (UCL News, 19 June 2020)","https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2020/jun/ucl-denames-buildings-named-after-eugenicists","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","Francis Galton coined the term eugenics")]},{ys:2020,y:"2020",t:"Pitt Rivers Museum removes human remains that fed racist thinking",src:[SRCQ("Critical Changes (Pitt Rivers Museum, University of Oxford)","https://www.prm.ox.ac.uk/node/1268656","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","reinforced racist and stereotypical thinking that goes against the Museum's values today")]},{ys:2021,y:"2021",t:"The Galton Institute renames itself the Adelphi Genetics Forum",src:[SRCQ("Adelphi Genetics Forum: an evolving organisation (The Biologist, Royal Society of Biology)","https://thebiologist.rsb.org.uk/biologist-interviews/adelphi-genetics-forum-an-evolving-organisation","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","growing public awareness of Galton's racism and eugenics made his name toxic,")]},{ys:2022,y:"2022–2023",t:"Black women's pain dismissed in UK maternity care",src:[SRCQ("Systemic racism, not broken bodies: executive summary (Birthrights, May 2022)","https://www.birthrights.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Birthrights-inquiry-systemic-racism_exec-summary_May-22-web.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","thick, tough skin")]},{ys:2022,y:"2022",t:"Child Q: 'adultification' named as a likely factor",src:[SRCQ("Child Q: powerful set of actions undertaken by Council (Hackney Council, 2022)","https://news.hackney.gov.uk/news/child-q-powerful-set-of-actions-undertaken-by-council-to-better-protect-residents-from-harm-and-racism","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","where Black children are treated more harshly than their white peers")]},{ys:2022,y:"2022",t:"Cambridge finds its scholars helped justify racism and slavery",src:[SRCQ("Advisory Group on Legacies of Enslavement: Final Report, section 4.2.6 (University of Cambridge, 2022)","https://www.legaciesofenslavement.cam.ac.uk/sites/default/files/University%20of%20Cambridge%20Advisory%20Group%20on%20Legacies%20of%20Enslavement%20Final%20Report%2021-09-2022.pdf","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","the start of a long line of Cambridge thought that was used to justify racism and slavery")]},{ys:2022,y:"2022",t:"Wellcome Collection closes its 'Medicine Man' gallery",src:[SRCQ("Wellcome Collection speaks out on Medicine Man closure (Museums Association, November 2022)","https://www.museumsassociation.org/museums-journal/news/2022/11/wellcome-collection-speaks-out-on-medicine-man-closure","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","still perpetuates a version of medical history that is based on racist, sexist and ableist theories and language")]},{ys:2023,y:"2023–2024",t:"Black children far more likely to be strip-searched by police",src:[SRCQ("Child strip searched every 14 hours by police in England and Wales (Children's Commissioner for England, 19 August 2024)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/news-and-blogs/child-strip-searched-every-14-hours-by-police-in-england-and-wales/","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","six times more likely between 2018 and 2022")]},{ys:2023,y:"2023",t:"Survey of 14,000 people finds widespread racism against Black groups",src:[SRCQ("More than a third of people from minority groups in the UK have experienced racist assaults, survey finds (King's College London, April 2023)","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/news/more-than-a-third-of-people-from-minority-groups-in-the-uk-have-experienced-racist-assaults-survey-finds","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","almost one in six respondents had experienced a racially motivated physical assault")]},{ys:2023,y:"2023",t:"US human genetics society apologises for its eugenic past",src:[SRCQ("ASHG documents and apologizes for past harms of human genetics research (American Society of Human Genetics, 24 January 2023)","https://www.ashg.org/?p=22783","Supports the Who built the stereotypes page","ASHG acknowledges and apologizes for the participation of some ASHG founders, past presidents, and other leaders")]}]};
LP["curriculum"]={name:"Curriculum and reference works",pfx:"cr",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"references",h:"Reference works",intro:"A century of attempts to build reference works on African and African-descended history, from Du Bois's 1909 idea to UNESCO's completed General History of Africa - and what the UK still lacks.",points:[
  {h:"Du Bois proposes an 'Encyclopaedia Africana' (1909)",text:"The idea of a reference work on the history and culture of people of African descent goes back to W.E.B. Du Bois, who first announced it in a 1909 letter to the Sierra Leone-based Pan-Africanist Edward Wilmot Blyden. Later efforts, including an 'Encyclopedia of the Negro' begun in 1931, were never completed for lack of funding.",figure:"",year:"1909",disputed:true,note:"Date of the first idea: the article title and the Emory and BlackPast texts give 1909; the indexed abstract of the same author's 2000 journal article says 1901. 1909 is the date supported by two independent sources; 1901 may be an abstract error but is not ruled out.",src:[SRCQ("Emory Report, October 1999 (Emory University)","https://www.emory.edu/EMORY_REPORT/erarchive/1999/October/eroctober.4/10_4_99gates.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","DuBois first had the idea for a black encyclopedia in 1909"),SRCQ("W.E.B. Du Bois and the Making of the Encyclopedia Africana, 1909-1963 - introduction to Africana by Gates and Appiah, republished (BlackPast.org)","https://new.blackpast.org/w-e-b-dubois-and-making-encyclopedia-africana-1909-1963","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","in a letter to Edward Wilmot Blyden, the Pan Africanist intellectual, in Sierra Leone in 1909"),SRCQ("H.L. Gates Jr, 'W.E.B. Du Bois and the Encyclopedia Africana, 1909-63', The ANNALS of the American Academy of Political and Social Science 568 (2000) - abstract (SAGE, via RePEc)","https://ideas.repec.org/a/sae/anname/v568y2000i1p203-219.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","In 1901, W.E.B. Du Bois, the African American intellectual and activist, conceived the idea of an encyclopedia Africana.")],person:"W.E.B. Du Bois (1868–1963), historian and sociologist",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ghana launches the Encyclopaedia Africana (1962)",text:"At the invitation of Kwame Nkrumah, Du Bois moved to Ghana and in March 1962 set up an Encyclopaedia Africana secretariat under the Ghana Academy of Sciences. In December 1962 he told a conference at the University of Ghana that the work should be based in Africa and compiled by Africans.",figure:"",year:"1960–1962",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("L.H. Ofosu-Appiah, 'The Encyclopaedia Africana', Journal of Modern African Studies 5:4 (1967) (Cambridge University Press)","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-modern-african-studies/article/abs/encyclopaedia-africana/07C71AFDFAA5F166D9DC47E549B86088","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","In March 1962 the late Dr W. E. B. Du Bois set up under the sponsorship of the Ghana Academy of Sciences"),SRCQ("W.E.B. Du Bois Address at the Conference on the Encyclopaedia Africana, 1962 - collection record (University of Virginia Library)","https://archives.lib.virginia.edu/ark:/59853/196322","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","He argues for his project of an Encyclopedia Africana based in Africa and compiled by Africans."),SRCQ("'Let's tell our story through Encyclopaedia Africana' (Graphic Online, 12 June 2026)","https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/general-news/ghana-news-lets-tell-our-story-through-encyclopaedia-africana-prof-damba-to-africans.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","conceived by Dr W.E.B. Du Bois and championed by Ghana's first President, Dr Kwame Nkrumah.")],person:"W.E.B. Du Bois (1868–1963); Kwame Nkrumah (1909–1972), first President of Ghana",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Three volumes published, then a long pause",text:"The project's published output is a Dictionary of African Biography in three volumes: Ethiopia–Ghana (1977), Sierra Leone–Zaire (1979) and South Africa–Botswana–Lesotho–Swaziland (1995). It was planned as a 20-volume series. In 2016 the African Union said progress had slowed for decades and sought to revive it under Agenda 2063.",figure:"",year:"1977–2016",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Biography: Africa - research guide (Harvard Library)","https://guides.library.harvard.edu/biography/africa","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","v. 3. South Africa-Botswana-Lesotho-Swaziland (1995)."),SRCQ("CIDO Holds Technical Workshop to Fast-Track Implementation of Encyclopaedia Africana Project (African Union, 27 Oct 2016)","https://au.int/en/pressreleases/20161026-1","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","the project has successfully published three volumes of the Encyclopaedia Africana"),SRCQ("Dictionary of African biography: In 20 volumes - catalogue record (African Union Library)","https://library.au.int/fr/node/237132","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Dictionary of African biography: In 20 volumes")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Volume IV under way (2026)",text:"In June 2026 scholars from Africa and the diaspora met at Peduase, Ghana, for a three-day workshop to develop the conceptual framework, thematic structure, editorial standards and contributor database for Encyclopaedia Africana Volume IV. The African Union had earlier made the project one of its Agenda 2063 objectives.",figure:"",year:"June 2026",disputed:false,note:"The June 2026 workshop itself is reported only by Graphic Online - not confirmed by a second source cited. The African Union source confirms the revival context, not the 2026 workshop.",src:[SRCQ("'Let's tell our story through Encyclopaedia Africana' (Graphic Online, 12 June 2026)","https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/general-news/ghana-news-lets-tell-our-story-through-encyclopaedia-africana-prof-damba-to-africans.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","develop the conceptual framework, thematic structure, editorial standards and contributor database"),SRCQ("CIDO Holds Technical Workshop to Fast-Track Implementation of Encyclopaedia Africana Project (African Union, 27 Oct 2016)","https://au.int/en/pressreleases/20161026-1","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","its designation as one of the key continental objectives of AU Agenda 2063")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Encarta Africana and the print Africana (1999)",text:"In January 1999 Microsoft launched Encarta Africana, a multimedia encyclopedia of African and African-descended history edited by Henry Louis Gates Jr and Kwame Anthony Appiah, with over 3,000 articles. A print version, Africana, followed in 1999; Oxford University Press published an expanded second edition in 2005 with more than 4,000 articles.",figure:"",year:"1999–2005",disputed:false,note:"Publishers Weekly gives conflicting page counts (2,144 and 1,200) for the 1999 print edition, so no page count is stated.",src:[SRCQ("Encarta Africana ... Launches Today (Microsoft press release, 8 Jan 1999)","https://news.microsoft.com/1999/01/08/encarta-africana-the-first-comprehensive-encyclopedia-of-black-history-and-culture-launches-today/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Encarta Africana, the First Comprehensive Encyclopedia Of Black History and Culture, Launches Today"),SRCQ("Africana: The Encyclopedia of the African and African American Experience, 2nd edn - faculty publications record (New York University)","https://gretchen.law.nyu.edu/fac-books-edited-works/772","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","the equivalent of a black Encyclopedia Britannica"),SRCQ("Emory Report, October 1999 (Emory University)","https://www.emory.edu/EMORY_REPORT/erarchive/1999/October/eroctober.4/10_4_99gates.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","But Microsoft had one condition: They wanted a 2 million-word encyclopedia in 18 months."),SRCQ("Review of Africana (Publishers Weekly, 4 Oct 1999)","https://www.publishersweekly.com/9780465000715","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","this book belongs on every family's reference shelf.")],person:"Henry Louis Gates Jr and Kwame Anthony Appiah, editors (public role)",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Dictionary of African Biography (OUP, 2011)",text:"Oxford University Press published the six-volume Dictionary of African Biography in November 2011, with about 2,100 entries of 750–2,000 words, each reviewed by an editorial board. Its editors aimed to grow it to 10,000 biographies online, through the subscription Oxford African American Studies Center.",figure:"",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"Sources differ on the exact count (nearly 2,100 vs 2,129) and year (November 2011 vs 2012 imprint); the page uses 'about 2,100' and 2011. The two Harvard pages count as one publisher; The Root is the independent second source.",src:[SRCQ("Dictionary of African Biography (Hutchins Center, Harvard University)","https://hutchinscenter.fas.harvard.edu/DAB.md","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Oxford University Press published the six-volume, 2100-entry print edition of the DAB in November 2011."),SRCQ("Dictionary of African Biography (Hutchins Center, Harvard University)","https://hutchinscenter.fas.harvard.edu/DAB.md","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The DAB continues to solicit entries, with a goal of reaching 10,000 biographies."),SRCQ("New Book Lists More Than 2,000 African Bios (The Root)","https://www.theroot.com/new-book-lists-more-than-2000-african-bios","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","An online edition of 10,000 entries will follow."),SRCQ("Biography: Africa - research guide (Harvard Library)","https://guides.library.harvard.edu/biography/africa","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Oxford; NY: Oxford University Press, 2012, 6 v.")],person:"Emmanuel K. Akyeampong and Henry Louis Gates Jr, editors (public role)",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Dictionary of Caribbean and Afro-Latin American Biography (2016)",text:"In 2016 Oxford University Press published a companion Dictionary of Caribbean and Afro-Latin American Biography, covering historically significant people across the whole Caribbean and Afro-descended populations throughout Latin America.",figure:"",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Dictionary of Caribbean and Afro-Latin American Biography (Hutchins Center, Harvard University)","https://hutchinscenter.fas.harvard.edu/publications/dictionary-caribbean-and-afro-latin-american-biography","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","the entire Caribbean, and the Afro-descended populations throughout Latin America"),SRCQ("Dictionary of Caribbean and Afro-Latin American biography - catalogue record (WorldCat/OCLC)","https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/927363773","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Oxford University Press, New York ... 2016")],person:"Franklin W. Knight and Henry Louis Gates Jr, editors (public role)",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"UNESCO General History of Africa",text:"UNESCO began the General History of Africa in 1964 with newly independent African states. Eight volumes appeared up to 1993. In October 2025 UNESCO announced the completion of three further volumes (IX, X and XI), written with more than 200 experts.",figure:"",year:"1964–2025",disputed:false,note:"The often-cited 2013 decision to add volumes IX–XI was not found in any source opened, so no date for that decision is given.",src:[SRCQ("General History of Africa (UNESCO)","https://en.unesco.org/general-history-africa","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","In 1964, UNESCO launched the elaboration of the General History of Africa"),SRCQ("General History of Africa (UNESCO)","https://en.unesco.org/general-history-africa","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","In recent years, UNESCO has embarked on the preparation and drafting of three new volumes of the GHA (Volumes IX, X and XI)."),SRCQ("Historia general de África: la UNESCO culmina una obra pionera (Permanent Delegation of Spain to UNESCO, 17 Oct 2025)","https://www.exteriores.gob.es/RepresentacionesPermanentes/unesco/es/Comunicacion/Noticias/Paginas/Articulos/Historia-general-de-%C3%81frica-la-UNESCO-culmina-una-obra-pionera-y-presenta-nuevos-recursos-educativos.aspx","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Los volúmenes IX, X y XI completan los primeros ocho volúmenes publicados hasta 1993."),SRCQ("L'UNESCO achève l'Histoire générale de l'Afrique (AllAfrica, 20 Oct 2025)","https://fr.allafrica.com/stories/202510200336.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Plus de 200 experts africains et internationaux ont collaboré à cette dernière phase.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Oxford Companion to Black British History (2007)",text:"The main single-volume reference on Black British history is The Oxford Companion to Black British History, edited by David Dabydeen, John Gilmore and Cecily Jones and published by Oxford University Press in March 2007. It covers Black people in Britain from classical times to the present.",figure:"",year:"2007",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Oxford companion to Black British history - catalogue record (WorldCat/OCLC)","https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/76936652","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Searchable online reference covering the history of black people in Britain from classical times to the present day."),SRCQ("Pathak, 'Centre for Contemporary Cultural Studies', in The Oxford Companion to Black British History - repository record (University of Southampton)","https://eprints.soton.ac.uk/187139","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The Oxford Companion to Black British History (Oxford Companions) ... Oxford University Press ... 22 March 2007")],person:"David Dabydeen, John Gilmore and Cecily Jones, editors (public role)",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Oxford DNB adds Black British lives",text:"The Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, the national record of people who shaped British history, has published updates focused on people of Black and African descent, including an 'Early Black Lives' release marked with a University of Oxford seminar in October 2019.",figure:"",year:"2019–2021",disputed:false,note:"Number of entries added and the date of the University College item are not stated in the sources opened. University College is a separate legal body from the University, but both are Oxford; OUP is the publisher of the ODNB itself.",src:[SRCQ("Early Black Lives Special Seminar, 16 October 2019 (Oxford Talks, University of Oxford)","https://talks.ox.ac.uk/talks/id/780b7155-778b-4abc-ac3c-8e198fde3f7d?format=txt","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Black History Month and Oxford Dictionary of National Biography's Early Black Lives release"),SRCQ("Christian Cole enters the ODNB (University College, Oxford)","https://www.univ.ox.ac.uk/?p=14063","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","has a specific focus on the lives of people of Black/African descent who have had an impact on British History"),SRCQ("Black History Month: celebrating 10 people who made British history (OUPblog, 30 Sep 2021)","https://blog.oup.com/2021/09/black-history-month-celebrating-10-people-who-made-british-history/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","a collection of Oxford Dictionary of National Biography articles exploring the lives of people of Black/African descent")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Open online references: BlackPast",text:"BlackPast.org, created by University of Washington historian Quintard Taylor, is a free online reference on African American and global Black history; by 2016 it held about 4,000 encyclopedia entries. It is US-based and US-focused.",figure:"",year:"2004/2007–",disputed:true,note:"Founding date: a University of Washington library guide says founded in 2004; UW News (2016) says Taylor started it in 2007 with fewer than 100 entries. Both are University of Washington pages. Year left open.",src:[SRCQ("Quintard Taylor's BlackPast.org history site gets redesign (UW News, 9 Mar 2016)","https://www.washington.edu/news/2016/03/09/quintard-taylors-blackpast-org-history-site-gets-redesign-new-executive-director/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","It now has 13,000 pages of material, with about 4,000 online encyclopedia entries in all."),SRCQ("BlackPast.org review (History Matters, George Mason University)","https://historymatters.gmu.edu/d/8131","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Created and maintained by Quintard Taylor."),SRCQ("BIS 256 reference sources guide (University of Washington Libraries)","https://guides.lib.uw.edu/bothell/bis256/ref","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","the most extensive online source on worldwide Black history currently available")],person:"Quintard Taylor, historian and founder (public role)",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"What is missing: a current, open, UK-focused reference",text:"Hub reading: the major references are either US-centred (BlackPast, Africana), continental (Encyclopaedia Africana, UNESCO), behind subscriptions (the Dictionary of African Biography online sits in a restricted Oxford database), or now nearly 20 years old (the 2007 Oxford Companion). There is no current, openly licensed reference focused on African, Caribbean and Black British history in the UK.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This is the hub's own assessment, not a finding of any source. The claim that no such reference exists is an absence claim: it reflects the sources searched, not an exhaustive survey.",src:[SRCQ("Oxford African American Studies Center - database record (University at Buffalo Libraries)","https://research.lib.buffalo.edu/oxford-african-american-studies-center","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Remote access to this resource requires a UBITName and password."),SRCQ("Dictionary of African Biography (Hutchins Center, Harvard University)","https://hutchinscenter.fas.harvard.edu/DAB.md","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","All online DAB entries are accessible at http://www.oxfordaasc.com/"),SRCQ("The Oxford companion to Black British history - catalogue record (WorldCat/OCLC)","https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/76936652","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Searchable online reference covering the history of black people in Britain from classical times to the present day.")],kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"curriculum",h:"Where the four nations stand",intro:"Each UK nation handles Black history in schools differently. England is rewriting its national curriculum; Wales has made it mandatory; Scotland and Northern Ireland leave most choices to schools.",points:[
  {h:"England: the Curriculum and Assessment Review (Nov 2025)",text:"The independent Curriculum and Assessment Review's final report (November 2025) recommended that curriculum reforms reflect the diversity of society and the contributions that shaped it. It reported that young people said not seeing themselves in the curriculum could be disempowering.",figure:"",year:"November 2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Curriculum and Assessment Review final report (deposited paper DEP2025-0723, UK Parliament)","https://data.parliament.uk/DepositedPapers/Files/DEP2025-0723/Curriculum_and_Assessment_Review_final_report.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Young people have told us that not seeing themselves in the curriculum, or encountering negative portrayals, can be disempowering and demotivating"),SRCQ("The school curriculum in England, Research Briefing CBP06798 (House of Commons Library, 21 Nov 2025)","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk/id/eprint/41839/1/SN06798.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The curriculum should appropriately reflect the diversity of modern society.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"England: the government's response and timetable",text:"The government accepted the direction of the review. It will publish a revised national curriculum in 2027 for first teaching in 2028, and update GCSEs for first teaching from 2029. For history it committed that teachers can 'reflect the innate diversity of British history, including British Black and Asian history'.",figure:"",year:"November 2025",disputed:false,note:"The government response quote was returned by the fetcher in two parts across a line break; it is reproduced here as one sentence.",src:[SRCQ("Government response to the Curriculum and Assessment Review (DfE, November 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/690b2a4a14b040dfe82922ea/Government_response_to_the_Curriculum_and_Assessment_Review.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","publish a revised national curriculum in 2027 for first teaching in 2028"),SRCQ("Government response to the Curriculum and Assessment Review (DfE, November 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/690b2a4a14b040dfe82922ea/Government_response_to_the_Curriculum_and_Assessment_Review.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","In reforming the curriculum, we will ensure that teachers can reflect the innate diversity of British history, including British Black and Asian history."),SRCQ("The school curriculum in England, Research Briefing CBP06798 (House of Commons Library, 21 Nov 2025)","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk/id/eprint/41839/1/SN06798.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","a revised national curriculum will be published in 2027, for first teaching in 2028."),SRCQ("What has the government committed to for history? (Historical Association)","https://www.history.org.uk/primary/module/8908/curriculum-review-support-module-primary/13644/what-has-the-government-committed-to-for-history","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","reflect the innate diversity of British history, including British Black and Asian history")],impact:"Sets the first statutory window (2027–2029) in which British Black history could be written into England's national curriculum and GCSEs.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Historical Association: welcome, with a warning on time",text:"The Historical Association, the subject association for history, supported the recommendation for a curriculum reflecting British Black and Asian history, and welcomed that it reaches beyond Key Stage 3. It warned that wider changes and more testing could leave less time for history.",figure:"",year:"6 November 2025",disputed:false,note:"The HA's view is its own statement; the government response confirms the commitment it responds to.",src:[SRCQ("Curriculum and Assessment Review Final Report - response (Historical Association, 6 Nov 2025)","https://history.org.uk/primary/news/4477/curriculum-and-assessment-review-final-report-re","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","We support the recommendation for a curriculum that reflects the innate diversity of British history, including British Black and Asian history."),SRCQ("Curriculum and Assessment Review Final Report - response (Historical Association, 6 Nov 2025)","https://history.org.uk/primary/news/4477/curriculum-and-assessment-review-final-report-re","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","proposed changes across the curriculum and increased testing could lead to less time for history"),SRCQ("Government response to the Curriculum and Assessment Review (DfE, November 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/690b2a4a14b040dfe82922ea/Government_response_to_the_Curriculum_and_Assessment_Review.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","In reforming the curriculum, we will ensure that teachers can reflect the innate diversity of British history, including British Black and Asian history.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Black Curriculum: 'sidesteps the most pressing issues'",text:"The Black Curriculum, a social enterprise campaigning for Black British history in schools, called the review cautious and said it contained no explicit mention of race, migration or colonialism. It welcomed the emphasis on local stories but said teachers need protected time and training to teach contested histories.",figure:"",year:"November 2025",disputed:true,note:"Views differ: the Historical Association welcomed the diversity recommendation; The Black Curriculum judged the review too cautious. TBC's statement that the review never names race or colonialism was not independently checked against the full report (the history annex was not extractable); the Commons Library briefing likewise mentions diversity but not Black or Asian history.",src:[SRCQ("The Black Curriculum Response to the Curriculum and Assessment Review (The Black Curriculum, Nov 2025)","https://theblackcurriculum.com/blog/the-black-curriculum-response-to-the-curriculum-and-assessment-review-november-2025","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","a review contains no explicit mention of race, migration, white supremacy, or colonialism."),SRCQ("The Black Curriculum Response to the Curriculum and Assessment Review (The Black Curriculum, Nov 2025)","https://theblackcurriculum.com/blog/the-black-curriculum-response-to-the-curriculum-and-assessment-review-november-2025","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","teachers need time, protected training, and ongoing support to address contested histories confidently and safely."),SRCQ("The school curriculum in England, Research Briefing CBP06798 (House of Commons Library, 21 Nov 2025)","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk/id/eprint/41839/1/SN06798.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The curriculum should appropriately reflect the diversity of modern society.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"England before the review: 'flexibility' (2020–21)",text:"In July 2020 the then Schools Minister, Nick Gibb, said the flexibility of the history curriculum meant Black British history could already be included. The government gave the same answer to a 2020 e-petition asking for a more inclusive curriculum, and did not change the statutory curriculum.",figure:"",year:"July 2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Black history and cultural diversity of the curriculum, Debate Pack CDP 2021/0102 (House of Commons Library, 24 Jun 2021)","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk//38130/1/CDP-2021-0102.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The flexibility within the history curriculum means that Black British history can already be included."),SRCQ("E-petition 323961 and Government response, 30 Jul 2020 (UK Parliament Petitions)","https://petition.parliament.uk/petitions/323961","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","the National Curriculum for England already has the flexibility to be inclusive of")],person:"Nick Gibb, Minister of State for School Standards (acting officially)",impact:"Black British history remained optional in England's statutory curriculum until the 2025 review.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wales: mandatory since 2022",text:"Wales became the first UK nation to make teaching Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic histories and experiences mandatory, through the Curriculum for Wales rolled out from September 2022. Schools design their own curriculum within that requirement.",figure:"",year:"2021–2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Learning Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic histories included in new Welsh curriculum (Welsh Government, 1 Oct 2021)","https://www.gov.wales/learning-black-asian-and-minority-ethnic-histories-included-new-welsh-curriculum","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","make teaching of Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic histories and experiences mandatory in the school curriculum"),SRCQ("Written Question WQ95452 and answer, 21 Jan 2025 (Senedd Cymru)","https://record.senedd.wales/WrittenQuestion/95452","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","to make it mandatory to teach Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic histories and experiences in the new Curriculum for Wales.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Scotland: open curriculum, new slavery lessons (Oct 2025)",text:"Scotland has an open curriculum, so teachers choose topics. Research found fewer than two-thirds of secondary schools taught even one lesson on the transatlantic slave trade. In October 2025 the universities of Stirling, Edinburgh and Glasgow launched Teaching Slavery in Scotland lesson plans, funded by the Scottish Government and mapped to BGE, National 5 and Higher.",figure:"",year:"October 2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Lesson plans launched to teach slavery in Scottish schools (University of Stirling, 24 Oct 2025)","https://www.stir.ac.uk/news/2025/october-2025-news/lesson-plans-launched-to-teach-slavery-in-scottish-schools/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Because we have an open curriculum in Scotland, teachers are responsible for the topics they teach."),SRCQ("Lesson plans launched to teach slavery in Scottish schools (University of Stirling, 24 Oct 2025)","https://www.stir.ac.uk/news/2025/october-2025-news/lesson-plans-launched-to-teach-slavery-in-scottish-schools/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Less than two-thirds of Scottish secondary schools taught at least one history lesson about the transatlantic slave trade."),SRCQ("New resources for teaching slavery (Royal Historical Society blog, 17 Feb 2026)","https://blog.royalhistsoc.org/2026/02/17/new-resources-for-teaching-slavery-connecting-academic-history-and-the-classroom/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","lesson plans that are directly mapped to the Scottish curriculum (Broad General Education [BGE], National 5, and Higher)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Northern Ireland: no named Black history requirement",text:"Northern Ireland's statutory minimum content for history requires study of Irish, British, European and global contexts and the ability to challenge stereotypical, biased or distorted viewpoints. No source opened showed a specific statutory requirement to teach Black history; slavery appears only in non-statutory guidance examples.",figure:"",year:"2007–",disputed:false,note:"Absence of a requirement is stated as 'not found in the sources cited', not as proven. CCEA notes bold type marks statutory items and that formatting was lost in extraction.",src:[SRCQ("The Education (Curriculum Minimum Content) Order (Northern Ireland) 2007 (legislation.gov.uk)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisr/2007/46/data.xht","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","an ability to challenge stereotypical, biased or distorted viewpoints"),SRCQ("Guidance for History at Key Stage 3 (CCEA, 2007)","https://ccea.org.uk/document/2465","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","the Troubles, slavery, apartheid, Arab/Israeli conflict")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"evidence",h:"Why it matters",intro:"Evidence on who studies and teaches history, and how few pupils take the GCSE units that cover Black British history.",points:[
  {h:"Royal Historical Society (2018): history's diversity gap",text:"The Royal Historical Society's 2018 report on race, ethnicity and equality found that Black historians made up less than 1% of UK university history staff, and that only 11% of undergraduate history students were from ethnic minority backgrounds (2.4% Black, 3.8% Asian).",figure:"",year:"October 2018",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("10 key findings from the RHS Race, Ethnicity & Equality report (Institute of Historical Research blog, Oct 2018)","https://blog.history.ac.uk/2018/10/10-key-findings-from-the-rhs-race-ethnicity-equality-report/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The figure is especially stark for Black historians, who make up less than 1% of university history staff."),SRCQ("10 key findings from the RHS Race, Ethnicity & Equality report (Institute of Historical Research blog, Oct 2018)","https://blog.history.ac.uk/2018/10/10-key-findings-from-the-rhs-race-ethnicity-equality-report/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Only 11% of undergraduate history students in the UK come from"),SRCQ("Report on Race, Ethnicity and Equality in UK History (Historical Association, 19 Oct 2018)","https://www.history.org.uk/ha-news/news/3647/report-on-race-ethnicity-and-equality-in-uk-histo","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","with just 2.4% from Black and 3.8% from Asian backgrounds.")],impact:"Few Black historians and history students means fewer people trained to write the reference works and curricula this page describes.",kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Only about 4% of GCSE history pupils took 'Migration to Britain'",text:"A 2019 Runnymede Trust and University of Liverpool report found that about 4% of GCSE History pupils took the AQA or OCR migration units launched in 2016 (2018 entries). It also found 78% of teachers surveyed wanted training on teaching migration and 71% on empire.",figure:"",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"The figure is from 2018 Ofqual entries; no more recent uptake figure was found. The Runnymede report (with a university partner) is the authoritative source; Al Jazeera and Schools Week report it independently.",src:[SRCQ("Teaching Migration, Belonging and Empire in Secondary Schools (Runnymede Trust and University of Liverpool TIDE project, 2019)","https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/61488f992b58e687f1108c7c/61bcc9eca927205637e401b8_TIDERunnymedeTeachingMigrationReport.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Overall, about 4 per cent of pupils taking GCSE History are taking ‘Migration to Britain’ with AQA and OCR."),SRCQ("Teaching Migration, Belonging and Empire in Secondary Schools - publication page (Runnymede Trust)","https://www.runnymedetrust.org/publications/teaching-migration-belonging-and-empire-in-secondary-schools","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","78 percent of teachers surveyed wanted training on teaching migration and 71 per cent on teaching empire."),SRCQ("British schools should teach migration and empire: Runnymede (Al Jazeera, Jul 2019)","https://www.aljazeera.com/indepth/features/british-schools-teach-migration-empire-runnymede-190703182458909.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","only four percent of students studying GCSE History are taking one of these courses."),SRCQ("How curriculum reform can help in the fight against racism (Schools Week)","https://schoolsweek.co.uk/?p=108823","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","just 4 per cent of GCSE history students studied the “migration to Britain” module")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Schools are changing, but often only by a lesson or two",text:"A 2021 Historical Association survey of history teachers in England found most schools had made substantial curriculum changes to address diversity, with switching to a GCSE migration unit the most commonly mentioned change. But only 23% devoted a series of lessons to Black or Asian British history; 57% gave it one or two lessons.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The two sources describe the same Historical Association survey (316 responses). The HA report gives 83% for Key Stage 3 changes; the Oxford/Reading summary gives 87% - the scope differs, so the page says 'most schools'. The 23% and 57% figures come from the HA report only.",src:[SRCQ("HA Survey of History in Schools in England 2021 (Historical Association)","https://history.org.uk/files/download/25253/1638962602/HA_Survey_of_History_in_Schools_in_England_2021.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Only 23% of schools reported devoting a series of lessons to teaching an aspect of Black or Asian British history."),SRCQ("History teaching has substantially changed to address diversity, say teachers (University of Oxford, 22 Oct 2021)","https://www.ox.ac.uk/news/2021-10-22-history-teaching-has-substantially-changed-address-diversity-say-teachers","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Some 87% of UK secondary schools report making substantial changes to history teaching to address issues of diversity")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"GCSE options that cover Black British history",text:"All three main English exam boards now offer an optional migration unit: OCR's 'Migrants to Britain, c.1250 to present', AQA's 'Britain: Migration, empires and the people, c790 to the present day' (including Windrush and Caribbean migrants), and Pearson Edexcel's 'Migrants in Britain, c800–present and Notting Hill, c1948–c1970', first assessed in June 2022.",figure:"",year:"2016–2022",disputed:false,note:"Impact sentence source: Pearson page - 'Teaching guide produced in partnership with The Black Curriculum on the new Notting Hill historic environment'. In 2019 Runnymede noted Pearson had then made no such offer; its unit came later. All are optional, not compulsory.",src:[SRCQ("GCSE History B (Schools History Project) J411 specification (OCR)","https://www.ocr.org.uk/Images/207164-specification-accredited-gcse-history-b-.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","This thematic study should enable learners to understand changes and continuities in migration to Britain from c.1250 to the present."),SRCQ("GCSE History 8145 specification (AQA)","https://filestore.aqa.org.uk/resources/history/specifications/AQA-8145-SP-2016.PDF","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","'Windrush' and the Caribbean migrants"),SRCQ("New Migration thematic study (Pearson Edexcel)","https://qualifications.pearson.com/en/qualifications/edexcel-gcses/history-2016/teaching-support/new-migration-thematic-study.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The new topic is available for teaching and was first assessed in June 2022."),SRCQ("Teaching Migration, Belonging and Empire in Secondary Schools (Runnymede Trust and University of Liverpool, 2019)","https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/61488f992b58e687f1108c7c/61bcc9eca927205637e401b8_TIDERunnymedeTeachingMigrationReport.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The exam boards OCR and AQA launched GCSE-level units on ‘Migration to Britain’ in 2016")],impact:"Pearson produced a teaching guide for its Notting Hill unit in partnership with The Black Curriculum.",kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"framework",h:"A proposed content framework",intro:"The hub's own proposals for a UK framework and an open reference, linked to where the hub already covers each topic. These are proposals, not findings.",points:[
  {h:"Proposal: past, present and future themes",text:"Hub proposal: a UK framework could run from African civilisations and knowledge, through enslavement, abolition and empire, early Black Britons, Windrush and post-war migration, to present-day representation, enterprise, health and young people, and future-facing themes such as reparative history and data. Each theme links to hub pages that already hold verified points. Existing GCSE content already covers parts of this arc.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Hub proposal, not a finding of any source. Suggested links: African civilisations; Knowledge transfer; What was lost; Who built the stereotypes; Peoples of our places; Settlers, borders, births; Slavery and the gremio; Representation; Enterprise; Youth; Blackness and identity.",src:[SRCQ("GCSE History 8145 specification (AQA)","https://filestore.aqa.org.uk/resources/history/specifications/AQA-8145-SP-2016.PDF","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Expansion in Africa: causes and impact of British involvement"),SRCQ("GCSE History B J411 specification (OCR)","https://www.ocr.org.uk/Images/207164-specification-accredited-gcse-history-b-.pdf","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Early African and Indian migrants to Britain including free Africans, ayahs and child servants")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Proposal: cross-cutting skills",text:"Hub proposal: every theme should build the same skills - reading and questioning sources, using archives, oral history, local history and comparing the four UK nations. The government has already committed to strengthen source criticism in history, and both curriculum campaigners and the review stress local case studies.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Hub proposal. Four-nations comparison links to this page's curriculum section; archives links to the hub's archives page.",src:[SRCQ("What has the government committed to for history? (Historical Association, quoting the government response)","https://www.history.org.uk/primary/module/8908/curriculum-review-support-module-primary/13644/what-has-the-government-committed-to-for-history","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","we will improve the programme of study to support pupils to critically appraise historical sources"),SRCQ("The Black Curriculum Response to the Curriculum and Assessment Review (Nov 2025)","https://theblackcurriculum.com/blog/the-black-curriculum-response-to-the-curriculum-and-assessment-review-november-2025","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Local stories and case studies are rightly highlighted as powerful tools"),SRCQ("The Education (Curriculum Minimum Content) Order (Northern Ireland) 2007","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisr/2007/46/data.xht","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Investigate how history has been selectively interpreted to create stereotypical perceptions")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Proposal: seed an open encyclopaedia from verified hub content",text:"Hub proposal: the hub's sourced, triangulated points could seed an openly licensed UK reference, using the governance that existing works rely on - named editors, editorial standards, signed entries with bibliographies and board review. Encyclopaedia Africana Volume IV and the Dictionary of African Biography show the model.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Hub proposal; the sources describe how other reference works are governed, not this proposal.",src:[SRCQ("Dictionary of African Biography (Hutchins Center, Harvard University)","https://hutchinscenter.fas.harvard.edu/publications/dictionary-african-biography","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","DAB contains nearly 2 million words in nearly 2,100 entries, each with bibliography, ranging from 750 to 2,000 words."),SRCQ("'Let's tell our story through Encyclopaedia Africana' (Graphic Online, 12 June 2026)","https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/general-news/ghana-news-lets-tell-our-story-through-encyclopaedia-africana-prof-damba-to-africans.html","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","develop the conceptual framework, thematic structure, editorial standards and contributor database")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Proposal: partners and funders",text:"Hub proposal: possible partners include Black Cultural Archives (founded 1981, the national heritage centre for African and Caribbean histories in Britain), the George Padmore Institute (an archive and research centre set up in 1991), the Institute of Historical Research, the Historical Association, The Black Curriculum and the University of the West Indies. Possible funders include the National Lottery Heritage Fund, whose Heritage 2033 programme offers grants from £10,000 to £10 million, and the AHRC, which has co-funded work on legacies of race.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Hub proposal; naming an organisation does not imply it has agreed to anything. The 1991 founding date of the George Padmore Institute comes from New Beacon Books only (the University of London page gives no date). Funding programmes change - check the NLHF and UKRI pages for current status before citing; an NLHF page showed the smaller grants band temporarily closed in Nov 2023 before Heritage 2033 opened. No sourced factual claims are made about the IHR, HA, The Black Curriculum or UWI beyond those elsewhere on this page.",src:[SRCQ("BCA receives £200,000 to plug funding gap (Museums Association, 19 Dec 2018)","https://www.museumsassociation.org/museums-journal/news/2018/12/19122018-bca-receives-200000-to-plug-funding-gap/","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","The archives were founded in 1981"),SRCQ("BCA statement on £200,000 government funding (Black Cultural Archives)","https://blackculturalarchives.org/blog/bca-200000governmentcashboost","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Founded in 1981, BCA is the first of its kind."),SRCQ("The George Padmore Institute (New Beacon Books)","https://www.newbeaconbooks.com/the-george-padmore-institute","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","an archive, educational research and information centre"),SRCQ("George Padmore Institute (University of London exhibitions)","https://exhibitions.london.ac.uk/s/ials-trans-people/item/3276","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","a community heritage archive of Black British history"),SRCQ("Our new Heritage 2033 National Lottery programme is open for grants from £10,000 to £10million (National Lottery Heritage Fund, Jan 2024)","https://heritagefund.org.uk/news/our-new-heritage-2033-national-lottery-programme-open-grants-ps10000-ps10million","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","we’ve increased our entry level grants to £10,000"),SRCQ("Funding listing (Bath and North East Somerset Council)","https://www.bathnes.gov.uk/node/88885","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","National Lottery Heritage Grants - £250,000 to £10 million."),SRCQ("Hidden histories of environmental science programme (UKRI)","https://www.ukri.org/what-we-do/our-main-funds-and-areas-of-support/browse-our-areas-of-investment-and-support/hidden-histories-programme","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","AHRC and NERC are co-funding a programme called Hidden histories of environmental science"),SRCQ("UKRI 'Hidden histories of environmental science' seed fund (IHBC NewsBlogs)","https://newsblogs.ihbc.org.uk/?p=29917","Supports the Curriculum and reference works page","Acknowledging legacies of race, social injustice and exclusion to inform the future")],kind:"argument"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Reference works on African and Caribbean history exist, but most are US-centred, continental or behind a subscription.",refs:["cr-references-what-is-missing-a-current-open-uk-focuse", "cr-references-dictionary-of-african-biography-oup-2011"]},{t:"From 2028, English schools are expected to be able to reflect British Black history; ask your child's school what it plans.",refs:["cr-curriculum-england-the-government-s-response-and-ti"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Check facts in the established references before you share them, and say where views differ.",refs:["cr-references-oxford-companion-to-black-british-histor", "cr-references-oxford-dnb-adds-black-british-lives"]},{t:"The review was welcomed by the Historical Association and criticised by The Black Curriculum; both views are here.",refs:["cr-curriculum-historical-association-welcome-with-a-wa", "cr-curriculum-the-black-curriculum-sidesteps-the-most-"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Give teachers and staff time and training: surveys show many diversity changes amount to a lesson or two.",refs:["cr-evidence-schools-are-changing-but-often-only-by-a", "cr-curriculum-historical-association-welcome-with-a-wa"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Respond to England's curriculum consultation with evidence, and learn from Wales, where these histories are mandatory.",refs:["cr-curriculum-england-the-government-s-response-and-ti", "cr-curriculum-wales-mandatory-since-2022"]},{t:"An open UK reference needs editors, standards and funding; partners and funders already exist.",refs:["cr-framework-proposal-seed-an-open-encyclopaedia-from", "cr-framework-proposal-partners-and-funders"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Report subject choices by ethnicity: few pupils take the GCSE migration units, and few Black students study history at university.",refs:["cr-evidence-only-about-4-of-gcse-history-pupils-took", "cr-evidence-royal-historical-society-2018-history-s-"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Du Bois first proposed an encyclopaedia of African-descended history; Ghana launched the project in 1962.",refs:["cr-references-du-bois-proposes-an-encyclopaedia-africa", "cr-references-ghana-launches-the-encyclopaedia-african"]},{t:"Oxford's Dictionary of African Biography (2011) has about 2,100 entries.",refs:["cr-references-dictionary-of-african-biography-oup-2011"]},{t:"England will publish a revised national curriculum in 2027, for first teaching in 2028.",refs:["cr-curriculum-england-the-government-s-response-and-ti"]},{t:"Wales has made these histories mandatory since 2022; Scotland leaves topics to teachers.",refs:["cr-curriculum-wales-mandatory-since-2022", "cr-curriculum-scotland-open-curriculum-new-slavery-les"]},{t:"About 4% of GCSE history pupils took a migration unit in 2018.",refs:["cr-evidence-only-about-4-of-gcse-history-pupils-took"]},{t:"The hub proposes an open, UK-focused reference built from verified, sourced content.",refs:["cr-framework-proposal-seed-an-open-encyclopaedia-from"]}]}]};
LP["global"]={name:"Black communities around the world",pfx:"gl",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"latam-overview",h:"Latin America: the largest African diaspora",intro:"Latin America is home to the largest populations of African descent outside Africa. The UN has made people of African descent a focus of two international decades and a permanent forum.",points:[
  {h:"About 1 in 5 to 1 in 4 Latin Americans is of African descent",text:"The World Bank estimates that around 133 million people in Latin America identify as Afro-descendant, most of them in Brazil. The UN regional commission ECLAC (CEPAL) gives a similar figure of 134 million, or 21% of the population. Estimates differ because countries ask different census questions and some do not ask at all.",figure:"133–134 million",year:"2018–2020",disputed:true,note:"World Bank says 'one in four'; CEPAL says 21%. Both are estimates built from national censuses that use different categories.",src:[SRCQ("World Bank: Eliminating Afro-descendant exclusion in Latin America is vital for development (2018)","https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2018/08/29/eliminating-afro-descendant-exclusion-in-latin-america-is-vital-for-development","Supports the Black communities around the world page","One in four Latin Americans identifies as an Afro-descendant, some 133 million people."),SRCQ("CEPAL: Afrodescendientes y la matriz de la desigualdad social en América Latina","https://www.cepal.org/es/notas/sintesis-documento-afrodescendientes-la-matriz-la-desigualdad-social-america-latina-retos-la","Supports the Black communities around the world page","que asciende actualmente a 134 millones de personas, que representan un 21% de la población total")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-descendants are more likely to live in chronic poverty",text:"The World Bank found Afro-descendants are 2.5 times more likely to be chronically poor than white or mestizo people. In 6 countries they make up 38% of the population but about half of people in extreme poverty.",figure:"2.5 times",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"The 2.5 times figure rests on the World Bank alone; CEPAL supports the wider point that race is a structural axis of inequality.",src:[SRCQ("World Bank press release (2018)","https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2018/08/29/eliminating-afro-descendant-exclusion-in-latin-america-is-vital-for-development","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Afro-descendants are 2.5 times more likely to be chronically poor than whites or mestizos."),SRCQ("CEPAL synthesis document","https://www.cepal.org/es/notas/sintesis-documento-afrodescendientes-la-matriz-la-desigualdad-social-america-latina-retos-la","Supports the Black communities around the world page","constituyen, sin duda, uno de los ejes estructurantes de la matriz de la desigualdad social")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The UN has declared a second Decade for People of African Descent",text:"The first International Decade for People of African Descent was proclaimed by UN General Assembly resolution 68/237 in 2013. In 2024 the Assembly proclaimed a second decade from 1 January 2025 to 31 December 2034, keeping the theme 'recognition, justice and development'.",figure:"2025–2034",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UN General Assembly resolution A/RES/79/193","https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4070482/files/A_RES_79_193-EN.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Proclaims the Second International Decade for People of African Descent, commencing on 1 January 2025"),SRCQ("UN: International Decade for People of African Descent","https://www.un.org/en/node/47712","Supports the Black communities around the world page","People of African descent: recognition, justice and development")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A UN Permanent Forum now advises on people of African descent",text:"The UN General Assembly set up the Permanent Forum on People of African Descent in August 2021 (resolution 75/314). It is a consultative body that also advises the Human Rights Council. Its first session met in Geneva on 5 to 8 December 2022.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UN: Permanent Forum on People of African Descent","https://www.un.org/en/node/48483","Supports the Black communities around the world page","On 2 August 2021, the General Assembly formally operationalized the Permanent Forum of People of African Descent"),SRCQ("Quaker UN Office: first session of the Permanent Forum","https://quno.org/timeline/first-session-permanent-forum-people-african-descent/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","took place from 5 to 8 December 2022 at the Palais des Nations, in Geneva")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"brazil",h:"Brazil: Afro-Brazilian communities",intro:"Brazil has the largest population of African descent of any country outside Africa. Afro-Brazilian culture, resistance and history are now marked in law, but deep racial inequality remains.",points:[
  {h:"More than half of Brazilians identify as Black or brown",text:"In the 2022 census, 45.3% of Brazilians identified as pardo (brown, of mixed heritage) and 10.2% as preto (Black), about 20.7 million people. The Brazilian state and the Black movement usually count both groups together as negro (Black), making them a majority.",figure:"55.5%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Figures are from the IBGE 2022 census; the IBGE news page could not be read directly, so these rest on two press reports of the IBGE release. Not every pardo person identifies as Afro-Brazilian.",src:[SRCQ("Jornal do Brasil: Censo 2022 – população parda supera a branca","https://www.jb.com.br/brasil/2023/12/amp/1047975-censo-2022-populacao-parda-supera-a-branca-pela-1-vez-desde-1872.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","A população preta saltou de 7,6% para 10,2%."),SRCQ("O Povo (Agência Brasil report of IBGE results)","https://www.opovo.com.br/noticias/economia/2023/12/22/censo-2022-populacao-parda-supera-a-branca-pela-1-vez-desde-1872.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","os pardos aumentaram a participação de 43,1% para 45,3%.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"1.3 million quilombolas were counted for the first time",text:"Quilombolas are people living in communities descended from settlements founded by people who escaped slavery (quilombos). The 2022 census counted 1,327,802 quilombolas for the first time, 0.65% of the population; nearly a third live in Bahia.",figure:"1,327,802",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Census count rests on IBGE as reported by CNN Brasil; IBGE's own page could not be read. The ISA source supports the definition, not the count.",src:[SRCQ("CNN Brasil: Brasil tem mais de 1,3 milhão de quilombolas (IBGE)","https://www.cnnbrasil.com.br/nacional/brasil-tem-mais-de-13-milhao-de-quilombolas-revela-retrato-inedito-do-ibge/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","a população quilombola residente no Brasil é de 1.327.802 pessoas"),SRCQ("Instituto Socioambiental teaching kit on quilombos (quoting Decree 4.887/2003)","https://acervo.socioambiental.org/sites/default/files/documents/kit_didatico_ISA_R4.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","com presunção de ancestralidade negra relacionada com a resistência à opressão histórica sofrida")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Quilombos were promised land in 1988, but few hold titles",text:"Article 68 of the transitional provisions of Brazil's 1988 Constitution recognises quilombo communities' permanent ownership of their land and requires the state to issue titles. In practice only 12.6% of quilombolas live in officially delimited territories, and 4.3% in fully titled ones.",figure:"4.3% in titled land",year:"1988; 2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Instituto Socioambiental teaching kit on quilombos","https://acervo.socioambiental.org/sites/default/files/documents/kit_didatico_ISA_R4.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Aos remanescentes das comunidades dos quilombos que estejam ocupando suas terras é reconhecida a propriedade definitiva,"),SRCQ("CNN Brasil reporting IBGE census data","https://www.cnnbrasil.com.br/nacional/brasil-tem-mais-de-13-milhao-de-quilombolas-revela-retrato-inedito-do-ibge/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Dessa forma, 12,6% reside em terras oficialmente delimitadas.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Zumbi dos Palmares Day became a national holiday in 2024",text:"Zumbi led Palmares, the largest quilombo, and was killed when Portuguese forces destroyed it on 20 November 1695. Law 14.759 of December 2023 made 20 November, the National Day of Zumbi and Black Consciousness, a national holiday, first observed nationwide in 2024.",figure:"20 November",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Câmara dos Deputados: Lei nº 14.759, de 21 de dezembro de 2023","https://www2.camara.leg.br/legin/fed/lei/2023/lei-14759-21-dezembro-2023-795091-publicacaooriginal-170522-pl.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Fica declarado feriado nacional o dia 20 de novembro, para a celebração do Dia Nacional de Zumbi e da Consciência Negra."),SRCQ("Agência Brasil (EBC): Zumbi dos Palmares, a 17th century leader's death is celebrated","https://memoria.ebc.com.br/agenciabrasil/noticia/2003-11-20/zumbi-dos-palmares-17th-century-negro-leaders-death-celebrated","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In a such a battle, in November 1695, Palmares was destroyed and Zumbi, as Francisco was now called, was killed."),SRCQ("Congresso em Foco: Dia de Zumbi e da Consciência Negra","https://congressoemfoco.com.br/area/pais/dia-de-zumbi-e-da-consciencia-negra-a-historia-por-tras-do-novo-feriado-nacional","Supports the Black communities around the world page","O Quilombo dos Palmares era uma sociedade complexa e autossuficiente, organizada em pequenos povoados chamados mocambos.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Capoeira and samba de roda are world heritage",text:"UNESCO lists 2 Afro-Brazilian traditions as intangible cultural heritage of humanity. Samba de roda of the Recôncavo of Bahia grew from the dances of enslaved Africans and influenced urban samba in Rio (proclaimed 2005, inscribed 2008). The capoeira circle, both fight and dance, was inscribed in 2014.",figure:"2008; 2014",year:"2014",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO: Capoeira circle","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/capoeira-in-brazil-00892","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Capoeira is an Afro-Brazilian cultural practice – simultaneously a fight and a dance"),SRCQ("Britannica: capoeira","https://www.britannica.com/sports/capoeira","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The basic aesthetic elements of capoeira were brought to Brazil by enslaved people"),SRCQ("UNESCO: Samba de Roda of the Recôncavo of Bahia","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/samba-de-roda-of-the-reconcavo-of-bahia-00101","Supports the Black communities around the world page","drew heavily on the dances and cultural traditions of the region's African slaves"),SRCQ("Estudos Avançados (SciELO): Samba de roda, supernal heritage of humanity","https://scielo.br/j/ea/a/dBfRTxGdpcQh4VffwpLjhMx/?lang=en","Supports the Black communities around the world page","was included in Unesco's Third Proclamation of the Intangible Heritage of Humanity")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-Brazilian history must be taught in every school",text:"Since Law 10.639 of 2003, all primary and secondary schools in Brazil, public and private, must teach Afro-Brazilian history and culture. The same law puts 20 November in the school calendar as Black Consciousness Day.",figure:"",year:"2003",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Lei nº 10.639, de 9 de janeiro de 2003 (text published by Santos city council)","https://www.santos.sp.gov.br/static/files_www/conselhos/CMPDCNPIR/lei_nr_10.639_de_09_01_2003.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","O calendário escolar incluirá o dia 20 de novembro como 'Dia Nacional da Consciência Negra'."),SRCQ("Câmara dos Deputados: Lei nº 10.639/2003","https://www2.camara.leg.br/legin/fed/lei/2003/lei-10639-9-janeiro-2003-493157-norma-pl.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Altera a Lei nº 9.394, de 20 de dezembro de 1996, que estabelece as diretrizes e bases da educação nacional,")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Brazilians still earn less and are more likely to be poor",text:"IBGE data show white workers earned 61.4% more per hour than Black or brown workers in 2022. In 2021 the poverty rate was 34.5% for Black people against 18.6% for white people, and Black and brown people held only 29.5% of management jobs.",figure:"61.4% pay gap",year:"2021–2022",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are IBGE, so this rests on one (authoritative) publisher. The same IBGE report gives a 2020 homicide rate of 34.1 per 100,000 for pardo people against 11.5 for white people.",src:[SRCQ("IBGE News Agency: hourly earnings by colour or race, 2022","https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-news/2184-news-agency/news/38572-in-2022-hourly-earnings-of-white-workers-r-20-0-was-61-4-above-that-of-black-or-brown-ones-r-12-4","Supports the Black communities around the world page","hourly earnings of R$ 20.0, which was 61.4% above that of the black or brown population (R$12.4)"),SRCQ("IBGE: Desigualdades sociais por cor ou raça no Brasil (2nd edition)","https://biblioteca.ibge.gov.br/visualizacao/livros/liv101972_informativo.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","a taxa de pobreza das pessoas brancas era 18,6%, ao passo que entre as pessoas pretas o percentual foi de 34,5%")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"andes-pacific",h:"Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Venezuela",intro:"Along the Pacific coast, in the Andes and on the Caribbean, African-descended peoples have built distinct cultures and won legal recognition, often while being under-counted.",points:[
  {h:"Colombia's census count of Black people is disputed",text:"Colombia's 2018 census recorded 2,982,224 Black, Afro-Colombian, Raizal and Palenquero people (6.8%), well below 2005. Statistics office DANE admitted an omission and adopted an official figure of 4,671,160 (9.34%) from a survey.",figure:"4.67 million (9.34%)",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"Census count 2,982,224 (6.8%) versus DANE's adjusted official figure 4,671,160 (9.34%).",src:[SRCQ("DANE: Población NARP (presentation to the IACHR)","https://www.dane.gov.co/files/investigaciones/boletines/grupos-etnicos/CIDH_poblaci%C3%B3n_narp.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","hubo una omisión que afectó la completitud de los resultados en términos del volumen de la población negra"),SRCQ("Portafolio: Población afro, 4,6 millones","https://www.portafolio.co/economia/poblacion-afro-4-6-millones-535344","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Esta cifra representa al 9,34% de la población total nacional.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Law 70 of 1993 gave Black communities collective land",text:"Law 70 recognises Black communities' right to collective ownership of the lands they have occupied, mainly in the Pacific basin. Over 168 community councils have received collective titles totalling almost 6 million hectares, from the Panama border to Ecuador.",figure:"almost 6 million hectares",year:"1993",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ley 70 de 1993 (text published by Agencia Nacional de Minería)","https://www.anm.gov.co/sites/default/files/ley_70_de_1993.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","el derecho a la propiedad colectiva, de conformidad con lo dispuesto en los artículos siguientes."),SRCQ("Universidad de los Andes, School of Government: Nota de política 26","https://gobierno.uniandes.edu.co/wp-content/uploads/NP-26.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Estos títulos suman algo menos de 6 millones de hectáreas, principalmente en el litoral pacífico,")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"San Basilio de Palenque kept a unique African-rooted language",text:"Palenqueros are people of San Basilio de Palenque, founded by people who escaped slavery in the 17th century. Their language is the only creole in Latin America with Spanish vocabulary and Bantu grammar. UNESCO proclaimed the cultural space in 2005.",figure:"",year:"2005",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO: Cultural space of Palenque de San Basilio","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/cultural-space-of-palenque-de-san-basilio-00102","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the only creole language in Latin America with a lexical Spanish basis and grammatical characteristics of Bantu languages."),SRCQ("Colombian Ministry of Foreign Affairs: intangible heritage","https://www.cancilleria.gov.co/en/intangible-heritage","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Cultural Space of the Palenque de San Basilio (proclaimed in 2005 and inscribed in 2008)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Raizal are a distinct Afro-Caribbean people of San Andrés",text:"The Raizal are the native people of the San Andrés, Providencia and Santa Catalina islands. They speak an English-based Creole and are mainly Protestant. Colombia's Constitutional Court recognises them as a clearly defined ethnic group.",figure:"",year:"1999",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Corte Constitucional de Colombia: Sentencia C-053/99","https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/1999/c-053-99.htm","Supports the Black communities around the world page","La población \"raizal\" de San Andrés y Providencia es un grupo étnico perfectamente definido"),SRCQ("Unidad para las Víctimas (Colombian government): Black, Afro-Colombian, Raizal and Palenque communities","https://www.unidadvictimas.gov.co/?p=8096","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Is the native population of San Andres, Providencia and Santa Catalina Islands")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Pacific marimba music links Afro-Colombians and Afro-Ecuadorians",text:"Marimba music, chants and dances of people of African descent in Colombia's South Pacific and Ecuador's Esmeraldas province are on UNESCO's list, jointly since 2015. The whole community is seen as the bearer of the tradition.",figure:"",year:"2015",disputed:false,note:"First inscribed for Colombia alone in 2010, extended to Ecuador in 2015.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO: Marimba music, traditional chants and dances from the Colombia South Pacific region and Esmeraldas Province of Ecuador","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/01099","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the community as a whole is considered the bearer and practitioner, irrespective of age or gender"),SRCQ("El Comercio (Ecuador): La Bomba, símbolo musical de resistencia","https://www.elcomercio.com/tendencias/bomba-simbolo-musical-resistencia-minoria.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En la costera Esmeraldas, su ritmo melódico se tradujo en una más extendida marimba")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ecuador's Afro-Ecuadorian count fell sharply in 2022",text:"Afro-Ecuadorians live mainly in Esmeraldas, Guayas and the Andean Chota Valley. The 2022 census counted 814,495 (4.8%), down from 1,041,559 (7.2%) in 2010. Ecuador's 2008 Constitution recognises their collective rights.",figure:"4.8%",year:"2022",disputed:true,note:"No national statistics office page was read; MRG and Primicias both report INEC data. The fall may reflect question design rather than a real decline. MRG quote on the 2008 Constitution: 'The 2008 Constitution recognizes that the collective rights'.",src:[SRCQ("Minority Rights Group: Afro-Ecuadorians","https://minorityrights.org/minorities/afro-ecuadorians/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Afro-Ecuadorians were 814,495 or 4.8 per cent of the total population"),SRCQ("Primicias: Más mestizos, menos afros y pocos blancos","https://primicias.ec/noticias/sociedad/censo-ecuador/resultados-poblacion-mestizos-afro-indigena","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En 2010 fueron el 7,2%, más que los indígenas y un poco menos que los montubios.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Chota Valley's bomba is national heritage",text:"In the Chota Valley, between Imbabura and Carchi, people descended from Africans enslaved in mines and plantations from the 17th century created the bomba, a drum and music. Ecuador's heritage institute and culture ministry certified it as national intangible heritage in 2020.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Two press sources reporting a government (INPC and Ministry of Culture) decision; no official page was read.",src:[SRCQ("Primicias: La Bomba es parte del Patrimonio Cultural Inmaterial de Ecuador","https://www.primicias.ec/noticias/cultura/bomba-parte-patrimonio-cultural-inmaterial-ecuador","Supports the Black communities around the world page","la certificación a La Bomba, que la convierte en parte del Patrimonio Cultural Inmaterial del Ecuador."),SRCQ("El Comercio (Ecuador)","https://www.elcomercio.com/tendencias/bomba-simbolo-musical-resistencia-minoria.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","El ritmo bomba del Valle del Chota es un símbolo de resistencia de los afros que llegaron en condiciones de esclavitud.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-Peruvians: 828,000 people and a national culture day",text:"In Peru's 2017 census, about 828,000 people aged 12 and over (3.6%) identified as Afro-Peruvian. Since 2006 Law 28761 has marked 4 June as Afro-Peruvian Culture Day, the birthday of poet and musician Nicomedes Santa Cruz (born 1925).",figure:"3.6%",year:"2017",disputed:true,note:"INEI gives 828,894; El Comercio gives 828,841. The figure covers people aged 12 and over only.",src:[SRCQ("INEI: Censos Nacionales 2017, chapter 3","https://www.inei.gob.pe/media/MenuRecursivo/publicaciones_digitales/Est/Lib1642/cap03_03.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","alcanzó un total de 828 mil 894 personas"),SRCQ("El Comercio (Peru): Censos 2017","https://elcomercio.pe/peru/censos-nacionales-2017-autoidentifican-peruanos-esto-revelo-censo-2017-inei-noticia-555466-noticia","Supports the Black communities around the world page","y 3,6% (828 mil 841) como afrodescendiente."),SRCQ("Andina (Peruvian state news agency): Afro-Peruvian Culture Day","https://andina.pe/ingles/noticia-afroperuvian-culture-day-celebrating-a-legacy-that-shapes-perus-national-identity-1032189.aspx","Supports the Black communities around the world page","This commemorative date was established in 2006 by Law No. 28761, passed by the Congress of the Republic."),SRCQ("RPP: Día de la Cultura Afroperuana","https://rpp.pe/cultura/literatura/dia-de-la-cultura-afroperuana-recuerda-nacimiento-de-nicomedes-santa-cruz-noticia-372223","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En recuerdo al día de su nacimiento se promulga la ley 28761, que celebra el Día de la cultura Afroperuana.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Venezuela counted Afro-Venezuelans for the first time in 2011",text:"In the 2011 census, the first to let people self-identify this way, 2.9% of Venezuelans identified as Black (negra/negro) and 0.7% as Afro-descendant. Afro-Venezuelan drumming, dance and spirituality remain central to the culture.",figure:"3.6%",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("INE Venezuela: Censo Nacional 2011","https://ine.gob.ve/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Censo-Nacional-2011.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","2,9% se reconoce como Negra/Negro; 0,7% como"),SRCQ("Minority Rights Group (via Refworld): Afro-Venezuelans","https://www.refworld.org/reference/countryrep/mrgi/2017/en/64807","Supports the Black communities around the world page","0.7 per cent of the total population identified themselves as Afro-descendant and 2.9 per cent as black")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"central-north-south",h:"Central America, Mexico and the River Plate",intro:"From the Garifuna of the Caribbean coast to Afro-Mexicans and the candombe drummers of Montevideo, smaller communities are winning recognition after long invisibility.",points:[
  {h:"Garifuna language, dance and music are world heritage",text:"The Garifuna descend from African and Indigenous Caribbean people on Saint Vincent, deported by the British from 1797. They now live mainly in Honduras, Guatemala, Nicaragua and Belize and speak an Arawakan language. UNESCO proclaimed their language, dance and music in 2001.",figure:"",year:"2001",disputed:false,note:"Britannica gives 1797 in one entry and 1798 in its Belize entry.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO: Language, dance and music of the Garifuna","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/language-dance-and-music-of-the-garifuna-00001","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Today, Garifuna communities mainly live in Honduras, Guatemala, Nicaragua and Belize."),SRCQ("Britannica: Garifuna","https://www.Britannica.Com/topic/Garifuna","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the deportation of a group from Saint Vincent and Dominica by British forces starting in 1797")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Honduras has not yet returned Garifuna land as the court ordered",text:"In October 2015 the Inter-American Court of Human Rights ruled that Honduras violated the collective property rights of the Garifuna communities of Triunfo de la Cruz and Punta Piedra and ordered land restored within 2 years. Court monitoring in 2019 and 2021 found no substantial progress.",figure:"",year:"2015",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UCLA Promise Institute: Garifuna territorial reparation report","https://promiseinstitute.law.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ENGLISH-Garifuna-Territorial-Reparation.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","had violated the collective property rights of the community"),SRCQ("Mongabay: Garifuna land rights abuses persist despite court ruling","https://news.mongabay.com/2024/07/garifuna-land-rights-abuses-persist-in-honduras-despite-court-ruling/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","has not yet complied with sentences")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Almost a third of Panamanians identify as Afro-descendant",text:"In Panama's 2023 census, 31.7% of people, 1,286,857, identified as Afro-descendant, among the highest shares in the region.",figure:"31.7%",year:"2023",disputed:true,note:"EFE reported preliminary data (32.8%, 1,258,915); INEC's final commentary gives 31.7% (1,286,857). Use the INEC figure.",src:[SRCQ("INEC Panamá: Comentarios, Censo de Población 2023","https://www.inec.gob.pa/archivos/P053342420231009161532Comentarios_Poblacion%20RFB%202023%20VF.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Mientras que el 31.7% de la población, se autorreconocieron como afrodescendientes, 1,286,857 personas."),SRCQ("Swissinfo (EFE): El 32,8% de la población de Panamá se reconoce como afrodescendiente","https://www.swissinfo.ch/spa/el-32-8-de-la-población-de-panamá-se-reconoce-como-afrodescendiente/48328376","Supports the Black communities around the world page","El 32,8 % de la población de Panamá se reconoce a sí mismo como afrodescendiente")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Limón is the heart of Afro-Costa Rican life",text:"In Costa Rica's 2011 census 7.75% identified as Afro-descendant, up from 1.9% in 2000. Limón province on the Caribbean coast has the highest share at 15.75%, and many people there speak Limonese Creole.",figure:"7.75%",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"Total includes people identifying as negro/afrodescendiente and as mulato. The 2000 baseline (1.9%) rests on the INA document only.",src:[SRCQ("Instituto Nacional de Aprendizaje: Afrodescendientes y multiculturalismo según Censo 2011","https://www.inavirtual.ed.cr/pluginfile.php/2861689/mod_resource/content/2/Afrodescendientes__y__multiculturalismo__segun__Censo__2011.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Puntarenas es la segunda provincia que en términos relativos tiene más afrodescendientes 11,45% solo superada por Limón con un 15,75%."),SRCQ("Poder Judicial de Costa Rica: Acceso a la justicia de personas afrodescendientes","https://accesoalajusticia.poder-judicial.go.cr/images/Publicaciones/No_1_Acceso_justicia_Afro.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","esta población que representa el 7,8 % por ciento de la población, (Censo del 2011)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mexico recognised Afro-Mexicans in 2019 and counted them in 2020",text:"A 2019 amendment to Article 2 of Mexico's Constitution recognised Afro-Mexican peoples and communities as part of the nation's pluricultural make-up. The 2020 census counted 2,576,213 Afro-Mexicans, about 2% of the population, many on the Costa Chica of Guerrero and Oaxaca.",figure:"2,576,213 (2%)",year:"2019–2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diario Oficial (via Supreme Court of Mexico): decree adding section C to Article 2, 9 August 2019","https://www.scjn.gob.mx/sites/default/files/cpeum/decretos_reformas/2019-09/09082019_0.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","C. Esta Constitución reconoce a los pueblos y comunidades afromexicanas, cualquiera que sea su autodenominación,"),SRCQ("UNAM Global (National Autonomous University of Mexico)","https://unamglobal.unam.mx/global_revista/en-mexico-hay-una-deuda-historica-con-las-culturas-africanas-y-los-afrodescendientes/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En México, 2,576,213 personas se identifican como afrodescendientes o afromexicanas, de acuerdo con el Censo del INEGI 2020."),SRCQ("Minority Rights Group: Afro-Mexicans","https://minorityrights.org/minorities/afro-mexicans/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","there are 2,576,213 Afro-Mexicans in the country")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Argentina's Afro-descendant count nearly doubled",text:"Argentina's 2022 census counted 302,936 people who identified as Afro-descendant or of African ancestry, 0.7% of the population, up from 0.4% in 2010. The University of Buenos Aires notes these groups are often invisible in official statistics.",figure:"302,936",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("INDEC: Censo 2022, población afrodescendiente","https://www.indec.gob.ar/ftp/cuadros/poblacion/censo2022_poblacion_afrodescendiente.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Este grupo poblacional constituye 0,7% del total de las personas que habitan en viviendas particulares"),SRCQ("Universidad de Buenos Aires: UBA Noticias","https://www.uba.ar/ubanoticias/noticias/834","Supports the Black communities around the world page","frecuente invisibilización dentro de las estadísticas oficiales")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Candombe drumming is Uruguay's Afro-descendant heritage",text:"Candombe's drum calls belong to the Sur, Palermo and Cordón districts of Montevideo, home to people of African descent, and are passed down in families. Uruguay made 3 December the national day of candombe, Afro-Uruguayan culture and racial equity in 2006, and UNESCO inscribed candombe in 2009.",figure:"",year:"2009",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO: Candombe and its socio-cultural space","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/candombe-and-its-socio-cultural-space-a-community-practice-00182?RL=00182","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the Sur, Palermo and Cordón districts in southern Montevideo, Uruguay, home to a population of African descent."),SRCQ("IMPO (Uruguay official gazette): Ley 18.059","https://www.impo.com.uy/bases/leyes-originales/18059-2006","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Declárase el 3 de diciembre \"Día nacional del candombe, la cultura afrouruguaya y la equidad racial\".")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"france",h:"France and its overseas departments",intro:"France has large Black communities in mainland France and in its overseas departments of Guadeloupe, Martinique, French Guiana, Réunion and Mayotte. It was the first country to recognise slavery as a crime against humanity in law.",points:[
  {h:"France does not collect ethnic statistics",text:"France's statistics office says ethnic-based statistics are strictly controlled in law: a Constitutional Council decision of 15 November 2007 forbade processing data revealing racial or ethnic origin. Surveys can record country of birth and parents' country of birth, so there is no official count of Black people in France.",figure:"",year:"2007",disputed:false,note:"Any figure for France's Black population is an estimate, not an official statistic. Sabbagh notes the 2007 ruling struck the provision on procedural grounds; the Representative Council of Black Associations (CRAN) has argued for collecting such data.",src:[SRCQ("INSEE – Ethnic-based statistics","https://www.insee.fr/en/information/2388586","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The preparation of ethnic-based statistics is strictly controlled from a legal point of view."),SRCQ("French-American Foundation – The collection of ethnoracial statistics (Daniel Sabbagh, Sciences Po)","https://frenchamerican.org/wp-content/uploads/the-collection-of-ethnoracial-statistics.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","race is a presumptively illegal category")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The 2001 Taubira law: slavery a crime against humanity",text:"The law of 21 May 2001, named after Christiane Taubira, deputy for French Guiana, recognises the transatlantic slave trade and slavery as a crime against humanity. The Foundation for the Remembrance of Slavery says it made France the first country to do so.",figure:"",year:"2001",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Fondation pour la mémoire de l'esclavage – Kit mairies 2022","https://memoire-esclavage.org/sites/default/files/2022-04/Kit%20mairies%202022.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","a fait de la France le premier pays à reconnaître l'esclavage et la traite coloniale comme des crimes contre l'humanité"),SRCQ("Public Sénat – 20 ans après la loi Taubira","https://www.publicsenat.fr/article/societe/commemoration-de-l-abolition-de-l-esclavage-20-ans-apres-la-loi-taubira-la-date-du","Supports the Black communities around the world page","qui en 2001 avait déclaré la traite et l'esclavage comme crime contre l'humanité")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"10 May remembrance and local abolition days overseas",text:"10 May is the national day for the memories of the slave trade, slavery and their abolition, set in 2006 to mark the Senate's vote on the Taubira law. Each overseas territory also marks its own abolition day: Mayotte 27 April, Martinique 22 May, Guadeloupe 27 May, French Guiana 10 June and Réunion 20 December.",figure:"",year:"2006",disputed:false,note:"A separate national date, 23 May, commemorates the victims of slavery; Public Sénat reports the choice of dates is still debated.",src:[SRCQ("Fondation pour la mémoire de l'esclavage – Kit mairies 2022","https://memoire-esclavage.org/sites/default/files/2022-04/Kit%20mairies%202022.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","la journée du 10 mai comme journée nationale des mémoires de la traite, de l'esclavage et de leurs abolitions"),SRCQ("Public Sénat – 20 ans après la loi Taubira","https://www.publicsenat.fr/article/societe/commemoration-de-l-abolition-de-l-esclavage-20-ans-apres-la-loi-taubira-la-date-du","Supports the Black communities around the world page","à Mayotte, c'est le 27 avril que l'abolition est célébrée")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Négritude: a movement born of Caribbean and African writers",text:"In 1930s Paris, Aimé Césaire of Martinique, Léon-Gontran Damas of French Guiana and Léopold Sédar Senghor of Senegal shaped Négritude, a literary and political movement that reassessed African culture and challenged colonial values.",figure:"",year:"1930s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Britannica – Léon Damas","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Leon-Damas","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Damas, alongside Aimé Césaire and Léopold Sédar Senghor, critically examined Western values and reassessed African culture."),SRCQ("Store norske leksikon – Négritude","https://snl.no/n%C3%A9gritude","Supports the Black communities around the world page","I 1935 ble tidsskriftet L'Étudiant Noir (Den svarte student) grunnlagt.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"low-countries-germany",h:"The Netherlands, Belgium and Germany",intro:"These countries have made recent public reckonings with slavery and colonialism, and have long-established Surinamese, Antillean, Congolese and Afro-German communities. Germany's 2021 recognition of the Herero and Nama genocide is already covered on the hub — see that page rather than repeating it here.",points:[
  {h:"The Dutch government apologised for slavery in 2022",text:"On 19 December 2022 Prime Minister Mark Rutte apologised on behalf of the Dutch government for the state's past actions, speaking at the National Archives. He said that by 1814 more than 600,000 enslaved African people had been shipped to the Americas.",figure:"600,000+ enslaved Africans shipped by 1814",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Government of the Netherlands – Speech by Prime Minister Mark Rutte","https://www.government.nl/documents/speeches/2022/12/19/speech-by-prime-minister-mark-rutte-about-the-role-of-the-netherlands-in-the-history-of-slavery","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Today, on behalf of the Dutch government, I apologise for the past actions of the Dutch State"),SRCQ("Al Jazeera – Netherlands apologises for Dutch government's role in slavery","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/12/19/netherlands-apologises-for-dutch-governments-role-in-slavery","Supports the Black communities around the world page","For centuries, the Dutch state and its representatives have enabled and stimulated slavery and have profited from it.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The King's own apology on Keti Koti 2023",text:"On 1 July 2023, at the National Slavery Monument in Amsterdam's Oosterpark, King Willem-Alexander made his own apology and asked forgiveness for the failure to act against 'this crime against humanity'.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Royal House of the Netherlands – Speech by King Willem-Alexander","https://www.royal-house.nl/documents/speeches/2023/07/01/speech-by-king-willem-alexander-at-the-commemoration-of-the-role-of-the-netherlands-in-the-history-of-slavery","Supports the Black communities around the world page","I make this apology myself."),SRCQ("Open Research Amsterdam – King Willem-Alexander apologizes","https://openresearch.amsterdam/en/id/98232","Supports the Black communities around the world page","At the National Monument to the Past of Slavery in the Oosterpark in Amsterdam")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Keti Koti: 'broken chains', 1 July",text:"Keti Koti means 'broken chains' in Sranantongo. It marks 1 July 1863, when slavery was formally abolished in Suriname and the Dutch Antilles; enslaved people in Suriname were only fully freed in 1873 after a compulsory 10-year transition. It is a national holiday in Suriname and is marked across Dutch cities, with a national ceremony in Oosterpark.",figure:"",year:"1863",disputed:false,note:"The government source confirms the significance of 1 July; the meaning of the name and the 1873 date come from the two non-government sources. Campaigners have sought to make 1 July a public holiday in the Netherlands.",src:[SRCQ("DutchNews – Keti Koti: what you need to know","https://www.dutchnews.nl/news/2021/07/today-keti-koti-is-celebrated-in-the-netherlands-what-you-need-to-know/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Slavery was formally abolished in the former colonies of Suriname and the Dutch Antilles on July 1, 1863."),SRCQ("Leiden International Centre – Celebrating Keti Koti","https://leideninternationalcentre.nl/get-advice/blogs/celebrating-keti-koti-in-the-netherlands","Supports the Black communities around the world page","were not fully free until 1873"),SRCQ("Government of the Netherlands – Speech by Prime Minister Mark Rutte","https://www.government.nl/documents/speeches/2022/12/19/speech-by-prime-minister-mark-rutte-about-the-role-of-the-netherlands-in-the-history-of-slavery","Supports the Black communities around the world page","On the road to that important, symbolic date of 1 July 2023.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Surinamese Dutch: a community shaped by 50 years since independence",text:"Between 1975 and 2024, 256,000 people migrated from Suriname to the Netherlands. Today about 181,000 people born in Suriname live there, alongside 189,000 Dutch-born people with at least one Surinamese-born parent.",figure:"About 370,000 of Surinamese origin (181,000 + 189,000)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (CBS, authoritative). The combined total is our sum, not a CBS figure. Surinamese origin is not the same as Black: Suriname's population includes people of African, Indian, Javanese and other heritage.",src:[SRCQ("CBS (Statistics Netherlands) – Over 250,000 Surinamese have settled in the Netherlands","https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/news/2025/47/over-250-000-surinamese-have-settled-in-the-netherlands-since-independence","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Between 1975 and 2024, 256 thousand people migrated from Suriname to the Netherlands.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Belgium's King expressed 'deepest regrets' to Congo in 2020",text:"On the 60th anniversary of Congolese independence in June 2020, King Philippe wrote to President Félix Tshisekedi expressing his 'deepest regrets' for acts of violence and cruelty under colonial rule – a first for the Belgian monarchy. The letter did not name Leopold II and was not framed as an apology.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Neither source is in the authoritative category; we could not reach the Royal Palace's text. Treat wording as reported.",src:[SRCQ("NPR – Belgian King conveys 'deepest regrets'","https://www.npr.org/sections/live-updates-protests-for-racial-justice/2020/06/30/885178095/belgian-king-conveys-deepest-regrets-for-brutal-colonial-past-in-congo","Supports the Black communities around the world page","commemorated the 60th anniversary of the Central African state gaining its independence from Belgium"),SRCQ("Al Jazeera – Belgium's Congolese mark 60 years since DRC's independence","https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2020/6/30/belgiums-congolese-mark-60-years-since-drcs-independence","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The issues came to prominence when Black Lives Matter protesters")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Matonge: a Brussels quarter named after Kinshasa",text:"Matonge in Ixelles takes its name from a lively district of Kinshasa. Congolese students and elites began gathering there in the early 1960s near the Free University of Brussels; it became a hub for shops, bars and music. In 2018 Ixelles named a square after Patrice Lumumba.",figure:"",year:"1960s",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("University of Liège (Reflexions) – Matonge, a story of Congolese immigration","https://www.reflexions.uliege.be/cms/c_44052/en/matonge-a-story-of-congolese-immigration?part=2","Supports the Black communities around the world page","This name is in reality that of a lively neighbourhood in Kinshasa."),SRCQ("Al Jazeera – Belgium's Congolese mark 60 years","https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2020/6/30/belgiums-congolese-mark-60-years-since-drcs-independence","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The area was named after a district of the DRC's capital Kinshasa by the first wave of Congolese arrivals."),SRCQ("Brussels Times – Hidden Belgium: Place Lumumba","https://brusselstimes.com/353982/hidden-belgium-place-lumumba","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2018, the commune of Ixelles named a square Place Patrice Lumumba in honour of the African martyr.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The AfricaMuseum reopened with a 'decolonised' aim",text:"Belgium's AfricaMuseum at Tervuren reopened on 8 December 2018 after a five-year renovation, aiming to present 'a contemporary and decolonised vision of Africa' and working with artists from Africa or with African roots. Reviewers have said parts of the museum still fall short.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"The museum's own aims are contested; the peer-reviewed review criticises the Rotunda and the lack of Black staff and visitors.",src:[SRCQ("AfricaMuseum – History and renovation","https://www.africamuseum.be/en/about_us/history_renovation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The AfricaMuseum reopened on 8 December 2018, after a five-year renovation."),SRCQ("Science Museum Group Journal – 'Everything passes, except the past'","https://journal.sciencemuseum.ac.uk/article/everything-passes/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","not every area of the refurbished RMCA is successful in its efforts to decolonise")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-German self-organisation since the 1980s",text:"Black Germans founded the Initiative of Black People in Germany (ISD), one of the first Black German organisations, in the 1980s, with ADEFRA as a feminist sister organisation. The 1986 volume Farbe bekennen (Showing Our Colors), with the poet May Ayim (died 1996), was a landmark.",figure:"",year:"1986",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("University of Waterloo Centre for German Studies – interview with Tiffany Florvil","https://uwaterloo.ca/centre-for-german-studies/node/123","Supports the Black communities around the world page","ISD in particular is one of the first black German organizations that was created in Germany in the 1980s"),SRCQ("African American Intellectual History Society – Seeing May Ayim through Her Friends' Eyes","https://www.aaihs.org/?p=303644","Supports the Black communities around the world page","a pathbreaking Black German feminist volume that was published in 1986")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afrozensus: over a million people of African origin in Germany",text:"The 2020 Afrozensus, a community-led survey, estimates there are over one million people of African origin in Germany, noting there was hardly any other statistical data on the group. Respondents were born in 144 countries, and large majorities reported discrimination in housing and dealings with authorities.",figure:"1 million+",year:"2020",disputed:true,note:"Germany collects no official data on Black people; the figure is a community-survey estimate. Rests on one source (the Afrozensus report itself, not in the authoritative category) — treat as indicative.",src:[SRCQ("Afrozensus 2020 report","https://afrozensus.de/reports/2020/en/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In Germany there are over one million people of African origin.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"southern-northern-eu",h:"Portugal, Italy, Spain, Sweden, Ireland – and the EU picture",intro:"Africans have lived in Lisbon for over five centuries, and newer communities are growing across southern and northern Europe. The EU's own survey shows racial discrimination against people of African descent is rising.",points:[
  {h:"Africans made up about a tenth of 16th-century Lisbon",text:"By 1551 Africans – mostly enslaved – made up about 10% of Lisbon's population. In 1593 the Mocambo neighbourhood was created, named after an Angolan word for a place of refuge, and Black confraternities such as Nossa Senhora do Rosário dos Homens Pretos supported the community.",figure:"About 10% of Lisbon",year:"1551",disputed:false,note:"Sixteenth-century counts are estimates.",src:[SRCQ("Project MANIFEST – Aurora Almada e Santos (NOVA University Lisbon)","https://www.projectmanifest.eu/presence-of-african-people-in-portugal-at-the-time-of-the-trade-of-enslaved-people/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","by 1551 Africans represented 10% of the population of the city"),SRCQ("Lapham's Quarterly – Africans in Renaissance Lisbon","https://www.laphamsquarterly.org/node/8985","Supports the Black communities around the world page","That was equivalent to roughly 10 percent of the city's population.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Portugal still has no official ethnic data",text:"Portugal's statistics office left an ethnic-origin question out of the 2021 census, despite most of a government working group favouring it, and is testing a separate survey instead. UN experts who visited in 2021 noted the lack of data disaggregated by race, and that citizens of Cape Verde, Angola and other Portuguese-speaking African countries form most of the population of African descent.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The UN report also cites a 'Historical Road Map of an African Lisbon' and urges recognition of the contributions of people of African descent.",src:[SRCQ("UN Working Group of Experts on People of African Descent – Visit to Portugal (A/HRC/51/54/Add.1)","https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/2078619/G2246202.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Despite a lack of data disaggregated by race"),SRCQ("The Portugal News – Government backs omission of ethnic data in census","https://theportugalnews.com/news/government-backs-omission-of-ethnic-data-in-census/50187","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the census is not the best way to measure whether there is discrimination based on ethnic origin")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cécile Kyenge: Italy's first Black cabinet minister",text:"Cécile Kyenge, who came to Italy from Congo in 1983, became Minister for Integration in April 2013 – Italy's first Black cabinet minister. She prepared a bill to give citizenship to children born in Italy, and faced sustained racist abuse from political opponents.",figure:"",year:"2013",disputed:false,note:"Named in her public role as a former minister. Children born in Italy to foreign parents still cannot apply for citizenship until 18 (Al Jazeera, 2025).",src:[SRCQ("NPR – Italy's first Black minister","https://www.npr.org/transcripts/211336128","Supports the Black communities around the world page","She became Integration Minister back in April, an appointment held as a landmark for diversity."),SRCQ("TIME – Italy's First Black Minister Suffers Even More Racial Abuse","https://world.time.com/?p=94379","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Congolese immigrant who has lived in Italy since 1983")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Spain's first study of its African and Afro-descendant people",text:"In 2021 Spain's Ministry of Equality published its first study of the African and Afro-descendant population in Spain, covering identity and access to rights, by Mª Ángeles Cea D'Ancona and Miguel S. Valles.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source: both pages are Spanish government. We did not verify the study's findings, so none are quoted.",src:[SRCQ("La Moncloa – Igualdad celebra la Semana Antirracista","https://www.lamoncloa.gob.es/serviciosdeprensa/notasprensa/igualdad/Paginas/2021/170321-discriminacion_racial.aspx","Supports the Black communities around the world page","es la primera vez que se realiza un estudio sobre la población africana y afrodescendiente en España"),SRCQ("Spanish Government Publications Catalogue – Examination of the African and afro-descendant population in Spain","https://cpage.mpr.gob.es/publicacion/examination-of-the-african-and-afro-descendant-population-in-spain-2021-048210062-0001/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","identity and access to rigths. Executive summary")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-Swedes face the most hate crimes in Sweden",text:"Sweden's National Council for Crime Prevention found people of African descent are the group subjected to the most hate crimes. UN experts who visited in 2014 said authorities had failed to effectively investigate and prosecute Afrophobic hate crimes, while commending the government for prioritising Afrophobia.",figure:"",year:"2014",disputed:false,note:"Sweden does not count people by ethnicity, so there is no official figure for Afro-Swedes. Afro-Swede is the community's own term.",src:[SRCQ("UN News – UN experts urge Sweden to intensify fight against discrimination","https://news.un.org/en/node/485732","Supports the Black communities around the world page","travelled to Sweden from 1-5 December, visiting the cities of Stockholm, Malmö, and Lund"),SRCQ("Sveriges Radio – Afro-Swedes subjected to the most hate crimes in Sweden","https://www.sverigesradio.se/artikel/afro-swedes-subjected-to-the-most-hate-crimes-in-sweden","Supports the Black communities around the world page","People of African descent are the group subjected to the most hate crimes in Sweden")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Irish: over 76,000 in the 2022 census",text:"In Ireland's 2022 census, 67,546 people identified as Black or Black Irish – African, up 17% since 2016, and a further 8,699 as Black or Black Irish – any other Black background, up 28%.",figure:"76,245",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (Central Statistics Office, authoritative). Total is our sum of the two categories.",src:[SRCQ("CSO – Census 2022 Profile 5: Ethnic Group/Background","https://www.cso.ie/en/releasesandpublications/ep/p-cpp5/censusofpopulation2022profile5-diversitymigrationethnicityirishtravellersreligion/ethnicgroupbackground/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The number of people who identified as Black or Black Irish – African was 67,546, up 17% from 57,850 in 2016.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"EU survey: nearly half face racial discrimination",text:"The EU Fundamental Rights Agency's 'Being Black in the EU' (2023, latest edition, fieldwork 2022) surveyed almost 6,800 people of African descent in 13 EU countries. Al Jazeera reports 46% had experienced racial discrimination; FRA says 12-month prevalence rose from 24% to 34% since 2016. Rates were highest in Germany and Austria, at over three-quarters.",figure:"34% in past 12 months (up from 24%)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The 46% five-year figure is as reported by Al Jazeera; FRA's page gives the 12-month rise and a peak of 77% in one country. The 13 countries were Austria, Belgium, Denmark, Finland, France, Germany, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, Poland, Portugal, Spain and Sweden. The survey did not cover the Netherlands.",src:[SRCQ("EU Fundamental Rights Agency – Being Black in the EU","https://fra.europa.eu/en/publication/2023/being-black-eu","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Overall, the 12-month prevalence of racial discrimination has risen from 24% to 34%."),SRCQ("Al Jazeera – 'Wake-up call' for EU as report reveals spike in anti-Black racism","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/10/25/wakeup-call-for-eu-as-new-report-shows-spike-in-anti-black-racism","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Discrimination is particularly high in Germany and Austria.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"EU survey: housing discrimination rose sharply",text:"FRA found discrimination in the housing market rose from 6% in 2016 to 28% in 2022. Police stopped 26% of respondents in the previous five years, and 30% experienced racist harassment. FRA concluded the findings 'do not show any progress'.",figure:"Housing discrimination 6% → 28%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The second source confirms the overall survey, not the housing figure, which rests on FRA alone.",src:[SRCQ("EU Fundamental Rights Agency – Being Black in the EU","https://fra.europa.eu/en/publication/2023/being-black-eu","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the 12-month prevalence of discrimination in the housing market has risen from 6% in 2016 to 28% in 2022"),SRCQ("Al Jazeera – 'Wake-up call' for EU","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/10/25/wakeup-call-for-eu-as-new-report-shows-spike-in-anti-black-racism","Supports the Black communities around the world page","46 percent of first and second-generation Black immigrants in 13 EU countries have experienced discrimination")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"canada",h:"Black Canadians",intro:"Black Canadians include long-established communities descended from Black Loyalists and refugees from slavery, and newer Caribbean and African communities. Their history includes both segregation and a long record of resistance and achievement.",points:[
  {h:"1.5 million Black Canadians in the 2021 census",text:"The 2021 census counted 1,547,870 Black people in Canada, 4.3% of the population. Over 4 in 10 (41%) were born in Canada, and the Canadian-born Black population grew by 165.8% from 1996 to 2021.",figure:"1,547,870 (4.3%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Justice Canada projects the Black population could exceed 3 million by 2041.",src:[SRCQ("Statistics Canada – Black History Month 2025… By the numbers","https://www.statcan.gc.ca/o1/en/app/7728-black-history-month-2025-numbers-demography","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2021, the Black populations reached 1,547,870 people, representing 4.3% of the total population in Canada."),SRCQ("Department of Justice Canada – Demographics and Diversity of Black Populations","https://justice.canada.ca/eng/rp-pr/jr/obpccjsce-spnsjpcce/populations.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Black people numbered over 1.5 million individuals, representing 4.3 percent of the total Canadian population.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Loyalists founded Birchtown in 1783",text:"About 3,000 Black Loyalists left New York for Nova Scotia in 1783, many recorded in the 'Book of Negroes'. Birchtown became the largest community of free Black people outside Africa. In 1792 about 1,200 left Nova Scotia to help found Freetown in Sierra Leone.",figure:"About 3,000 arrived; about 1,200 left for Sierra Leone",year:"1783",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Canada's Historic Places – Birchtown","https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=3206","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Birchtown was the largest community of Free Blacks outside of Africa."),SRCQ("Royal Canadian Mint – A legacy of courage","https://mint.ca/en/blog/a-legacy-of-courage-the-story-of-black-loyalists-in-canada","Supports the Black communities around the world page","about 1,200 Black Loyalists left Nova Scotia in 1792"),SRCQ("Black Loyalist Heritage Centre – Exhibits","https://blackloyalist.com/exhibits/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","to found the colony of Freetown in Sierra Leone in 1792")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Africville: a community razed, then an apology",text:"Africville, a Black community on Halifax's edge settled by the 1840s, was denied running water and sewers and sited near a dump. Halifax razed and relocated it between 1964 and 1970. The city apologised on 24 February 2010, and a replica of its church opened in 2011.",figure:"",year:"2010",disputed:false,note:"CBC dates the founding to after the War of 1812, and oral history links some families to the 1700s.",src:[SRCQ("Halifax Regional Municipality – Recognizing the past, present, and future","https://www.halifax.ca/node/445581","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Africville was officially settled in the 1840s."),SRCQ("CBC – Africville: A Community Destroyed","https://amp.cbc.ca/lite/story/1.2919404","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The new Seaview African United Baptist Church opened in September 2011.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Viola Desmond: from wrongful conviction to the $10 note",text:"In 1946 the Halifax businesswoman Viola Desmond, who ran a beauty school drawing students from three provinces, was forcibly removed from a segregated New Glasgow cinema and convicted. Nova Scotia granted her a free pardon in 2010, and in 2018 she became the first Canadian woman on the face of a banknote.",figure:"",year:"1946",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are government bodies but different publishers.",src:[SRCQ("Bank of Canada – Backgrounder","https://www.bankofcanada.ca/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/backgrounder-canadian-woman.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Desmond received a posthumous free pardon from the Nova Scotia government on 15 April 2010."),SRCQ("Government of Nova Scotia – Viola Desmond, First Canadian Woman to Appear on a Banknote","https://news.novascotia.ca/en/2016/12/08/viola-desmond-first-canadian-woman-appear-banknote","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2010, the province granted her an official apology and free pardon.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Emancipation Day became national in 2021",text:"In March 2021 the House of Commons voted unanimously (335 to 0) to make 1 August Emancipation Day, marking the Slavery Abolition Act taking effect in 1834. Many Black Canadian communities had marked the day for decades; 2021 was the first national observance.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Canadian Heritage – Statement on first national Emancipation Day","https://www.canada.ca/en/canadian-heritage/news/2021/07/minister-chagger-makes-statement-on-first-national-emancipation-day.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","voted unanimously to designate August 1 of every year as Emancipation Day"),SRCQ("CBC – MPs vote to designate Aug. 1 as Emancipation Day","https://www.cbc.ca/1.5962960","Supports the Black communities around the world page","MPs in the House of Commons have voted unanimously to designate Aug. 1 as Emancipation Day across Canada")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"south-asia",h:"African-descended communities of South Asia and the Indian Ocean",intro:"People of East African descent have lived in India, Pakistan and Sri Lanka for centuries, arriving through Indian Ocean trade and enslavement. Some rose to rule kingdoms; their descendants keep distinct music and faith traditions today.",points:[
  {h:"Siddi communities live in four Indian states and in Sindh",text:"The Siddi (also spelled Sidi) descend from East Africans who came to India mainly through the Indian Ocean slave trade. Communities live in Gujarat, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Telangana, and in Sindh in Pakistan; one estimate puts their number in India at about 65,000.",figure:"about 65,000",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Population figure from one source (the Met); other sources give no count, so treat it as an estimate. 'Siddi' and 'Sidi' are both used; 'Sidi' derives from an Arabic honorific meaning 'my lord'.",src:[SRCQ("Habshi and Sidi – The Metropolitan Museum of Art","https://www.metmuseum.org/articles/habshi-and-sidi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Today there are about sixty-five thousand Africans living in Sidi communities in these regions of India."),SRCQ("Sidi – Encyclopedia of World Cultures (Helene Basu), via Encyclopedia.com","https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/sidi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Today small groups of Sidi live in the west Indian coastal states of Karnataka, Maharashtra, and Gujarat"),SRCQ("Who are the Siddis? – Sahapedia (Anisha Padma)","https://www.sahapedia.org/who-are-siddis-multiple-pasts-and-presents-african-community-india","Supports the Black communities around the world page","have argued that Siddis came to India as slaves via the Indian Ocean slave trade.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Siddi music and faith blend East African and Sufi traditions",text:"The central Siddi ritual is a drum-and-dance performance called goma or dhamal (dhamaal), honouring saints such as Bava Gor. In Karnataka Siddis are Hindu, Muslim and Christian; in Gujarat most are Muslim.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Sidi – Encyclopedia of World Cultures (Helene Basu), via Encyclopedia.com","https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/sidi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The central ritual activity of the Sidi consists of the performance of dancing and drumming called dammal or goma."),SRCQ("The incredible story of how East African culture shaped the music of a state in India – The Conversation","https://theconversation.com/the-incredible-story-of-how-east-african-culture-shaped-the-music-of-a-state-in-india-198412","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Dhamaal is a mix of Sufi and African (mostly East African) musical and dance traditions.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Malik Ambar: from enslaved boy to ruler of Ahmadnagar",text:"Malik Ambar was born Chapu in Ethiopia in 1548, enslaved and taken to India. He became the de facto ruler of the Ahmadnagar sultanate in the Deccan, resisted the Mughals for years with guerrilla tactics, and founded Khadki, later Aurangabad, around 1619.",figure:"",year:"1548–1626",disputed:true,note:"Year of death differs: the Met gives 1626, BlackPast gives 1627.",src:[SRCQ("Habshi and Sidi – The Metropolitan Museum of Art","https://www.metmuseum.org/articles/habshi-and-sidi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","became the de-facto king in Ahmadnagar"),SRCQ("Habshi – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Habshi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The most famous of them was Malik ʿAmbar of Ahmadnagar, who defied the Mughals for many years."),SRCQ("Malik Ambar (1548–1627) – BlackPast.org (Omar H. Ali)","https://blackpast.org/gah/ambar-malik-1548-1626","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In approximately 1619, Ambar founded the city of Khadki (the future site of Aurangabad)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Habshi sultans ruled Bengal and Siddi nawabs held Janjira",text:"'Habshi' comes from the Arabic for Abyssinian. Habshi rulers held the throne of Bengal from 1487 to the early 1490s, and the Sidis of Janjira on India's west coast commanded a fleet and kept their own state until 1948.",figure:"",year:"1487–1494",disputed:true,note:"End date of Habshi rule in Bengal differs: Banglapedia dates Shamsuddin Muzaffar Shah to 1491–1493; the Daily Star gives 1491–94.",src:[SRCQ("Eunuch – Banglapedia (Asiatic Society of Bangladesh)","https://en.banglapedia.org/index.php?title=Eunuch","Supports the Black communities around the world page","One of them, Shahzada alias Barbak, occupied the throne of Bengal in 1487 AD."),SRCQ("Habshi rule in Bengal (1487-94) – The Daily Star (Muhammad Ahmedullah)","https://www.thedailystar.net/views/in-focus/news/habshi-rule-bengal-1487-94-2123496","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Four rulers from an African background occupied the Sultanate of Bengal during 1487-94."),SRCQ("Habshi – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Habshi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","They accepted British supremacy and maintained their state until 1948.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Pakistan's Sheedi community and the Mangho Pir festival",text:"Pakistan has the largest Sheedi (Siddi) population in South Asia, mostly in southern Sindh. The Sheedi Mela at the Mangho Pir shrine near Karachi, with its dhamal dance procession, is the community's central celebration.",figure:"around 50,000",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Population estimate rests on one press source (Dawn/AFP); the encyclopedia confirms Sindh but gives no number. Sheedis also faced discrimination under British rule and after 1947, per Dawn.",src:[SRCQ("Crocodiles guard secrets of Pakistan's lost African past – Dawn (AFP)","https://www.dawn.com/news/1399778","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Pakistan holds the highest number of Sheedis on the subcontinent, upwards of around 50,000 people."),SRCQ("Sidi – Encyclopedia of World Cultures (Helene Basu), via Encyclopedia.com","https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/sidi","Supports the Black communities around the world page","as well as in Sindh in Pakistan")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-Sri Lankans: a Portuguese-era community and its own name",text:"Sri Lanka has a small community of African descent, brought from Mozambique by the Portuguese about 500 years ago and later by the Dutch and British. They are known locally and in official records as 'Kaffirs' (Lanka Kapiri), but the word comes from Arabic for 'unbeliever' and is a grave racial slur in South Africa, so this hub uses 'Afro-Sri Lankans'. Their manja/kaffringha songs in Sri Lanka Portuguese creole are endangered.",figure:"around 3,000",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Population figure (around 3,000) rests on one press source. Community members use the local name themselves; 'Afro-Sri Lankan' is the term used in this hub to avoid repeating a slur. The Cambridge project reports a 90-year-old last mother-tongue speaker of Sri Lanka Portuguese at the time of the project.",src:[SRCQ("Tracing the African roots of Sri Lanka Portuguese – UKRI Gateway to Research (University of Cambridge)","https://gtr.ukri.org/project/0C1E8CFE-7F32-4AAC-A0E3-934092D39385","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Manja is the only remaining African (possibly Mozambican) heritage of these communities."),SRCQ("Sunday Times (Sri Lanka), 24 April 2011","https://www.sundaytimes.lk/110424/Plus/plus_06.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the Kaffirs were brought to Ceylon by the Portuguese from Mozambique off Southern Africa, via Goa, around 500 years ago"),SRCQ("SARS v CCMA and Others [2016] ZACC 38 – Constitutional Court of South Africa (via SARS)","https://www.sars.gov.za/lapd-drj-cc-2016-01-sars-v-ccma-and-others-cct-19-zacc-38-8-november-2016","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The word 'kaffir' is derived from the Arabic word for non-believer or infidel"),SRCQ("Kaffir – Dictionary.com","https://dictionary.com/browse/kaffir","Supports the Black communities around the world page","in post-apartheid South Africa it is considered hate speech")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Chagossians: descendants of enslaved Africans, removed by Britain",text:"Chagossians are mostly descended from enslaved people from Madagascar and Mozambique and later indentured labourers. Britain removed the entire population, about 1,500 people, between 1967 and 1973 for a US military base; in 2019 the International Court of Justice found continued UK control unlawful.",figure:"",year:"1967–1973; 2019",disputed:false,note:"Many Chagossians now live in the UK. In October 2024 the UK announced it would cede sovereignty to Mauritius; the agreement's current status is not confirmed by the sources cited.",src:[SRCQ("UN News, February 2019","https://news.un.org/feed/view/en/story/2019/02/1033532","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The entire Chagossian population was forcibly removed from the territory between 1967 and 1973"),SRCQ("Newcastle University Press Office (Sue Farran)","https://www.ncl.ac.uk/press/articles/latest/2022/02/conversationchagosislandssovereignty/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Today many of those identifying as Chagossians are the descendants of these enslaved and indentured labourers."),SRCQ("Chagos 2025 – Minority Rights Group","https://minorityrights.org/chagos-2025/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","primarily enslaved people from Madagascar and Mozambique")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"middle-east",h:"African-descended communities of the Middle East and the Gulf",intro:"The Indian Ocean and Red Sea slave trades carried East Africans to Iraq, Iran, Arabia and the Ottoman lands over more than a thousand years. Their descendants have shaped music and culture across the region, and many still face discrimination.",points:[
  {h:"The Zanj rebellion in Basra, 869–883",text:"East Africans (Zanj) were brought to southern Iraq to drain the salt marshes east of Basra. Their revolt against the Abbasid caliphate lasted from 869 to 883, one of the largest uprisings of enslaved people in history.",figure:"",year:"869–883",disputed:false,note:"MRG reports that the rebellion 'reportedly involved half a million slaves'; Britannica describes 'several thousand' brought in. Numbers are uncertain.",src:[SRCQ("Zanj rebellion – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/event/Zanj-rebellion","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Zanj rebellion, (ad 869–883), a black-slave revolt against the ʿAbbāsid caliphal empire."),SRCQ("World Directory of Minorities and Indigenous Peoples – Iraq: Black Iraqis (Minority Rights Group, via UNHCR Refworld)","https://www.refworld.org/docid/5a53600d7.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","It was then that a robust slave trade developed between the East Coast of Africa and the port city of Basra.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Iraqis today: centred on Basra, without recognition",text:"Black Iraqis (Afro-Iraqis) live mostly in southern Iraq, with the largest community in Basra. Estimates range from 250,000 to 2 million. They are mainly Shi'a Muslims, keep African musical and spiritual traditions, and are sought-after wedding musicians, but face systematic discrimination and have no political representation.",figure:"250,000 to 2 million",year:"",disputed:true,note:"No official count exists; the range is informal. MRG is an NGO hosted on UNHCR's Refworld, not a statistics office. MRG reports that Jalal Thiyab, founder of the Free Iraqi Movement, was assassinated in Basra.",src:[SRCQ("World Directory of Minorities and Indigenous Peoples – Iraq: Black Iraqis (Minority Rights Group, via UNHCR Refworld)","https://www.refworld.org/docid/5a53600d7.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Black Iraqis continue to face systematic discrimination and marginalization."),SRCQ("Centuries-old black community still on the margins in Iraq – Africanews (AFP), 2022","https://www.africanews.com/amp/2022/06/02/centuries-old-black-community-still-on-the-margins-in-iraq/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The minority numbers 250,000 to two million people, according to a wide range of informal estimates.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-Iranians along the Persian Gulf coast",text:"Descendants of enslaved Africans live in rural and urban communities along Iran's Gulf coast, especially in Sistan and Baluchistan, Hormozgan and Khuzestan. They have blended African heritage with local religious traditions. Slavery in Iran is studied in the first full history of the subject, covering 1800 to emancipation in 1929.",figure:"",year:"1929",disputed:false,note:"The 1929 date comes from the book's title; the publisher's description says emancipation came 'in the mid-twentieth century'.",src:[SRCQ("Afro-Iranian Lives – Middle East Center, University of Pennsylvania","https://mec.sas.upenn.edu/node/18896","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the provinces of Sistan va Baluchistan, Hurmuzgan, and Khuzestan"),SRCQ("A History of Slavery and Emancipation in Iran, 1800–1929 – UBC Press (Behnaz A. Mirzai)","https://ubcpress.ca/a-history-of-slavery-and-emancipation-in-iran-1800-1929","Supports the Black communities around the world page","enslaved Africans and Iranians.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afro-Turks and the revival of Izmir's Calf Festival",text:"After the Ottoman Empire abolished the trade in enslaved Africans in 1857, Izmir had possibly the largest share of freed Africans of any Ottoman city. Their descendants now celebrate an Afro-Turkish identity, including the revived Dana Bayramı (Calf Festival), now supported by Izmir's municipality.",figure:"",year:"1857",disputed:false,note:"The late Mustafa Olpak, who founded the Afro-Turk Association, led much of the revival (Al-Monitor). No reliable count of Afro-Turks exists.",src:[SRCQ("The Making and Unmaking of the African Community of Ottoman Izmir – University of Arizona (Michael Ferguson, Concordia University)","https://sbs.arizona.edu/node/192","Supports the Black communities around the world page","By the 1880s Izmir arguably had the largest proportion of emancipated Africans of any Ottoman city"),SRCQ("Afro-Turks seek visibility in Turkey's cultural mosaic – Al-Monitor, September 2025","https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2025/09/afro-turks-seek-visibility-turkeys-cultural-mosaic","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The municipality of Izmir now supports it.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Slavery was abolished late on the Arabian Peninsula",text:"Enslaved Africans were carried across the Red Sea and sold throughout Arabia. Saudi Arabia was the last country in Arabia to abolish the legal status of slavery, by royal decree in November 1962, offering compensation to those who freed enslaved people.",figure:"",year:"1962",disputed:false,note:"The Antislavery in Domestic Legislation database is a university-led project (Rights Lab, University of Nottingham), though the page fetched did not name the university. The King Saud Library is a foundation linked to the royal family. Handled factually; dates for Yemen and Oman were not verified in this run.",src:[SRCQ("Saudi Arabia – Antislavery in Domestic Legislation database","https://antislaverylaw.ac.uk/country/saudi-arabia/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","This was done by a Royal Decree in November 1962."),SRCQ("Slavery During the Reign of King Saud – King Saud Library","https://kingsaud.org/en/archives/press/slavery-during-the-reign-of-king-saud/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In the presence of King Saud, on 9 Jumada II 1382 AH / 6 November 1962 CE, the Decree of Emancipation was issued.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Liwa: East African music in the Gulf",text:"Gulf genres such as liwa (lēwa) and tanbura have roots in East Africa and keep Swahili phrases and African rhythms. They are performed by African-descended communities in Oman and the United Arab Emirates, and many well-known Gulf singers of the 20th century were of African descent.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"The second source is a University of Georgia undergraduate journal and Fanack is an independent media site; the evidence is weaker than for other points.",src:[SRCQ("Khaliji music: a unique blend of Indian and African influences – Fanack","https://fanack.com/culture/khaliji-music-a-unique-blend-of-indian-and-african-influences/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Liwa has retained two features of its African origin: the underlying rhythmic structure and certain phrases from Swahili."),SRCQ("Lēwa and the Misguided Perception of Cultural Appropriation – The Classic Journal, University of Georgia","https://theclassicjournal.uga.edu/index.php/2025/01/11/lewa-and-the-misguided-perception-of-cultural-appropriation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","a genre adopted by African communities in Oman and throughout the United Arab Emirates")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Yemen's Muhamasheen: discrimination clear, African origin contested",text:"The Muhamasheen ('the marginalised', replacing the slur 'akhdam') face descent-based discrimination in Yemen, including being barred from owning land and from schools. Some say they descend from African enslaved people or Ethiopian soldiers; others say they have Yemeni roots.",figure:"153,133 (2004 census) to 3.5 million",year:"",disputed:true,note:"Include as an example where African descent is claimed but not settled; do not describe the Muhamasheen as Afro-Yemeni without this caveat. Population estimates vary enormously (2004 census vs UN-cited 3.5 million).",src:[SRCQ("Summary of stakeholders' information, UPR of Yemen (A/HRC/WG.6/18/YEM/3) – UN Human Rights Council","https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/762130/files/A_HRC_WG.6_18_YEM_3-EN.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","suffer from persecution and a series of caste-based discrimination and violence"),SRCQ("Climate worsens the distress of Yemen's Muhammasheen – Carnegie Endowment, May 2026","https://carnegieendowment.org/middle-east/diwan/2026/05/climate-worsens-the-distress-of-yemens-muhammasheen","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Some say they descend from pre-Islamic African slaves or Ethiopian soldiers, while others say they have Yemeni roots.")],kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"caribbean-freedom",h:"Caribbean islands: revolution, emancipation and sovereignty",intro:"Beyond Jamaica, each Caribbean nation has its own story of resistance and self-government. Haiti's revolution created the first state founded by formerly enslaved people; others reached independence and, in Barbados, a republic.",points:[
  {h:"Haiti: the first country founded by formerly enslaved people",text:"A general uprising of enslaved people in Saint-Domingue began in 1791. Led by figures including Toussaint Louverture and Jean-Jacques Dessalines, it defeated French and British forces, and on 1 January 1804 the country declared independence as Haiti.",figure:"",year:"1791–1804",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Haitian Revolution – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Haitian-Revolution","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the first country to be founded by former slaves"),SRCQ("Haiti's independence debt – Bibliothèque nationale de France","https://heritage.bnf.fr/france-ameriques/en/haiti-independence-debt-article","Supports the Black communities around the world page","On 1 January 1804 the population of Saint-Domingue declared independence and renamed the island Haiti.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"France made Haiti pay 150 million francs for recognition",text:"In 1825 Charles X of France demanded 150 million francs from Haiti, to compensate former colonists, in exchange for recognising its independence. The sum was later cut to 90 million francs, and Haiti took decades to pay, borrowing to do so: the 'double debt'.",figure:"150 million francs (reduced to 90 million)",year:"1825",disputed:true,note:"Sources differ on the reduced total and end date: BnF says 90 million francs, paid off in 1878 via an 1875 loan; LSU says 112 million francs paid over five decades after 1838. Present as disputed accounting, not a single figure.",src:[SRCQ("Haiti's independence debt – Bibliothèque nationale de France","https://heritage.bnf.fr/france-ameriques/en/haiti-independence-debt-article","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The total indemnity to be settled amounted to 150 million francs, to be paid in five annual instalments."),SRCQ("1825–2025: France, Haiti, & the Question of Indebtedness – Louisiana State University","https://lsu.edu/cffs/france-haiti-indebtedness.php","Supports the Black communities around the world page","effectively burdening the nation with what many refer to as the “double debt” of independence.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Barbados became a republic in 2021",text:"Barbados, independent since 1966, became a parliamentary republic on 30 November 2021, when Dame Sandra Mason took office as its first President and the Queen ceased to be head of state. It chose to stay in the Commonwealth.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The republic date and details rest on one authoritative source (House of Commons Library); the Commonwealth Secretariat source confirms only 1966 independence.",src:[SRCQ("Barbados becomes a republic – House of Commons Library","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/barbados-becomes-a-republic/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The first President of Barbados takes office on 30 November"),SRCQ("Barbados – Commonwealth Secretariat","https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/barbados","Supports the Black communities around the world page","1966, following independence from Britain")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Suriname: Maroons are a fifth of the population",text:"Maroons, descendants of Africans who escaped slavery and built free societies in the interior, make up about one-fifth of Suriname's population; Creoles of mainly African descent are another tenth to a fifth. Slavery was abolished on 1 July 1863, and in 2022 the Dutch government apologised for the Netherlands' role.",figure:"about one-fifth",year:"1863; 2022",disputed:false,note:"1 July is marked as Keti Koti ('the chains are broken'), but neither source fetched uses that name. Freed people were kept under 10 years of state supervision after 1863 (Britannica). UNESCO's listed Maroon heritage (Moore Town, 2008) is in Jamaica, not Suriname.",src:[SRCQ("Suriname: People – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Suriname/People","Supports the Black communities around the world page","(descendants of escaped enslaved people of African origin)"),SRCQ("Suriname: History – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Suriname/History","Supports the Black communities around the world page","On July 1, 1863, slavery was abolished in Suriname."),SRCQ("Government apologises for the Netherlands' role in the history of slavery – Government of the Netherlands","https://www.government.nl/latest/news/2022/12/19/government-apologises-for-the-netherlands-role-in-the-history-of-slavery","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Prime Minister Mark Rutte apologised for the past actions of the Dutch State")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Guyana: freed people bought land and built villages",text:"The Dutch and then the British brought enslaved West Africans to grow sugar in Berbice, Demerara and Essequibo; about 100,000 people were enslaved there in 1807. After full emancipation in 1838, Black freedmen left the plantations to set up their own settlements.",figure:"about 100,000",year:"1838",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (authoritative). The 1763 Berbice uprising and the 1823 Demerara rebellion could not be verified in this run.",src:[SRCQ("Guyana: History – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Guyana/History","Supports the Black communities around the world page","After full emancipation in 1838, black freedmen left the plantations to establish their own settlements")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"St Kitts and Antigua: World Heritage built by enslaved Africans",text:"Two UNESCO World Heritage Sites were built by enslaved Africans: Brimstone Hill Fortress on St Kitts (inscribed 1999) and the Antigua Naval Dockyard (inscribed 2016). UNESCO credits their 'labour and skills'.",figure:"",year:"1999; 2016",disputed:false,note:"Both entries come from one publisher (UNESCO), so this counts as one authoritative source.",src:[SRCQ("Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park – UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/910","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Designed by the British and built by African slave labour."),SRCQ("Antigua Naval Dockyard and Related Archaeological Sites – UNESCO World Heritage Centre","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1499","Supports the Black communities around the world page","made possible through the labour and skills of enslaved Africans")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Bermuda: enslaved Africans in a British Overseas Territory",text:"Africans transported in the slave trade were brought to Bermuda from the early colonial period. Slavery was outlawed there along with the rest of the British Empire under the 1833 Act; Bermuda remains a British Overseas Territory.",figure:"",year:"1833",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (authoritative). The Abolition Act was passed in 1833 and took effect on 1 August 1834. Mary Prince, born in Bermuda, could not be verified in this run (see rejected).",src:[SRCQ("Bermuda: History – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Bermuda/History","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Slavery was outlawed in Bermuda and the rest of the British Empire in 1833.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"caribbean-culture",h:"Caribbean culture and achievement, island by island",intro:"African-descended Caribbean people created music, religions and art forms now known worldwide, and the region has produced Nobel laureates. These are achievements to honour, not only histories of hardship.",points:[
  {h:"Trinidad and Tobago: calypso, Carnival and the steelpan",text:"Calypso grew from the early 19th century, when enslaved people in Trinidad sang veiled, improvised lyrics in the streets at Carnival. The steelpan, made from oil barrels, grew from early 20th-century Carnival percussion groups; the UN now marks 11 August as World Steelpan Day.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Britannica's text uses the word 'slaves'; this hub uses 'enslaved people'. Canboulay and the colonial bans on drumming were not verified in this run.",src:[SRCQ("Calypso – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/calypso-music","Supports the Black communities around the world page","dates to the early 19th century and was originally called caïso or cariso"),SRCQ("Steel band – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/steel-band","Supports the Black communities around the world page","made from the bottoms of 55-gallon oil barrels"),SRCQ("World Steelpan Day – United Nations","https://www.un.org/en/observances/steelpan-day","Supports the Black communities around the world page","This grew out of Trinidad and Tobago's early 20th-century Carnival percussion groups.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Saint Lucia: two Nobel laureates from one small island",text:"Saint Lucia has produced two Nobel laureates, both born in Castries on 23 January: the economist Sir Arthur Lewis (1915–1991, Economic Sciences 1979), for research on economic development, and the poet Derek Walcott (1930–2017, Literature 1992). Lewis was also Vice Chancellor of the University of the West Indies.",figure:"",year:"1979; 1992",disputed:false,note:"Both laureates have died. The two NobelPrize.org pages are from one publisher; UWI is the independent second source.",src:[SRCQ("Derek Walcott – Facts – NobelPrize.org","https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/literature/1992/walcott/facts/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","for a poetic oeuvre of great luminosity, sustained by a historical vision, the outcome of a multicultural commitment."),SRCQ("Arthur Lewis – Facts – NobelPrize.org","https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/1979/lewis/facts/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","for their pioneering research into economic development research with particular consideration of the problems"),SRCQ("Nobel Laureates – University of the West Indies","https://uwi.edu/alumnionline/node/117","Supports the Black communities around the world page","He received the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1992.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Garifuna: an Afro-Indigenous people from St Vincent",text:"The Garifuna formed on St Vincent from Africans, many of whom had escaped slavery, and Indigenous Caribs (Kalinago). After they resisted British colonisation in the Carib Wars, Britain deported them in 1797 to Roatán off Honduras. Their language, dance and music are UNESCO-listed.",figure:"",year:"1797; 2008",disputed:true,note:"Accounts of African origins differ: some describe escapees from Barbados plantations (Britannica), others a shipwrecked slave ship (International Encyclopedia of the Social Sciences). Garifuna communities today live mainly in Honduras, Guatemala, Nicaragua and Belize, with descendants still on St Vincent. UNESCO inscribed the element in 2008. The plural self-name is Garinagu.",src:[SRCQ("Language, dance and music of the Garifuna – UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/language-dance-and-music-of-the-garifuna-00001","Supports the Black communities around the world page","A population of mixed origin incorporating cultural elements of indigenous Caribbean and African groups"),SRCQ("Saint Vincent and the Grenadines: History – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/place/Saint-Vincent-and-the-Grenadines/History","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The Africans were mainly slaves who had escaped from plantations in Barbados"),SRCQ("Garifuna – Worldmark Encyclopedia of Cultures and Daily Life, via Encyclopedia.com","https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/garifuna","Supports the Black communities around the world page","were deported to the island of Roatan off the coast of Honduras in 1797.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cuba: Afro-Cuban rumba and Santería",text:"Rumba, mainly associated with African culture and popular where enslaved African communities lived, was inscribed by UNESCO in 2016. Santería, also called Regla de Ocha or the Lukumí religion, was brought by Yoruba people enslaved in great numbers in the early 19th century, who linked their orishas with Catholic saints.",figure:"",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"The Santería sentence rests on one source (Britannica, authoritative).",src:[SRCQ("Rumba in Cuba – UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/rumba-in-cuba-a-festive-combination-of-music-and-dances-and-all-the-practices-associated-01185?RL=01185","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The music and movements of rumba in Cuba are mainly associated with African culture"),SRCQ("Rumba – Encyclopaedia Britannica","https://www.britannica.com/art/rumba-dance","Supports the Black communities around the world page","rumba, ballroom dance of Afro-Cuban folk-dance origin that became internationally popular in the early 20th century."),SRCQ("Santería – Encyclopaedia Britannica (Joseph M. Murphy)","https://www.britannica.com/topic/Santeria","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Santería was brought to Cuba by the people of the Yoruban nations of West Africa")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"kongo-ndongo-loango",h:"Kongo, Ndongo, Matamba and Loango",intro:"West-central Africa (today's Angola and the two Congos) was home to powerful kingdoms whose rulers dealt with Portugal as equals and, at times, resisted it. Many people taken to the Americas, and many Afro-Brazilians and Afro-Caribbean people today, trace roots to this region.",points:[
  {h:"Mbanza Kongo: capital of a great central African state",text:"The Kingdom of Kongo was one of the largest states in the region from the 14th to the 19th century. Its capital, Mbanza Kongo in northern Angola, was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017.",figure:"",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are UNESCO pages and count as one publisher; the inscription is a UNESCO decision, so it rests on the authoritative record itself.",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO World Heritage Centre: three cultural sites inscribed (8 July 2017)","https://whc.unesco.org/en/news/1687","Supports the Black communities around the world page","one of the largest constituted states in Southern Africa from the 14th to 19thcenturies"),SRCQ("UNESCO: Mbanza Kongo, Vestiges of the Capital of the former Kingdom of Kongo","https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1511","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The historical area grew around the royal residence, the customary court and the holy tree")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Kongo's Christianisation from 1491",text:"Kongo envoys returned from Portugal in 1491 with Catholic missionaries, and Kongo's royal family was baptised. Kongo became a Catholic kingdom on its own terms, holding a distinctive place among African states.",figure:"",year:"1491",disputed:false,note:"Britannica confirms the baptisms but not the year; the 1491 date comes from the Bloomsbury textbook and an independent teaching site.",src:[SRCQ("Bloomsbury, Panorama: A World History vol. 2 (Dunn and Willoughby), Source 3.1","https://res.cloudinary.com/bloomsbury-online-resources/image/upload/v1786707560/Panorama%20-%20A%20World%20History/Volume%202/Primary%20Source%20Documents/Source_3.1_Volume_2.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","When they returned in 1491, they brought Catholic missionaries and European trade items, including firearms."),SRCQ("Britannica: João I (Nzinga a Nkuwu), king of Kongo","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Joao-I","Supports the Black communities around the world page","baptized and assumed Christian names—João I Nzinga a Nkuwu and Afonso I Mvemba a Nzinga, respectively."),SRCQ("Religion and Slavery: Afonso I to Portugal (independent teaching site)","https://religionandslavery.org/ps%3A-afonso-i-to-portugal","Supports the Black communities around the world page","since its official conversion to Catholicism in 1491, held a unique position among African polities")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afonso I's 1526 letters against the slave trade",text:"In 1526 King Afonso I of Kongo (Nzinga Mbemba) began a series of letters to King João III of Portugal protesting that Portuguese merchants were seizing his people, and stating that there should be no trade in enslaved people in his kingdoms. They are among the earliest African-written protests against the Atlantic slave trade.",figure:"",year:"1526",disputed:false,note:"Historians differ on emphasis: the Bloomsbury text frames the letters as seeking to limit and control the trade rather than end slavery outright. The quoted letter wording uses the historical term 'slaves'.",src:[SRCQ("World History Commons (Roy Rosenzweig Center, George Mason University): Afonso I letters","https://worldhistorycommons.org/node/2382","Supports the Black communities around the world page","began writing a series of twenty-four letters to the Portuguese King Joao III appealing for an end to the slave trade."),SRCQ("Bloomsbury, Panorama: A World History vol. 2, Source 3.1 (letter translated by John K. Thornton)","https://res.cloudinary.com/bloomsbury-online-resources/image/upload/v1786707560/Panorama%20-%20A%20World%20History/Volume%202/Primary%20Source%20Documents/Source_3.1_Volume_2.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Afonso I wrote many letters to the Portuguese king, João III, seeking to limit and control trade with Portuguese merchants."),SRCQ("Religion and Slavery: Afonso I to Portugal","https://religionandslavery.org/ps%3A-afonso-i-to-portugal","Supports the Black communities around the world page","it is our will that in these Kingdoms there should not be any trade of slaves nor outlet for them.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Njinga, queen of Ndongo and Matamba",text:"Njinga (about 1582–1663) took power in Ndongo after her brother's death in 1624, later conquered Matamba, and fought the Portuguese and their allies for decades. At her height in the 1640s she ruled about a quarter of today's northern Angola, and she is revered in Angola as a national heroine.",figure:"",year:"1624–1663",disputed:false,note:"Spellings vary (Njinga, Nzinga). The UNC page also records that she took part in slave trading after 1626; her legacy is complex. Birth year given as 1582 (UNC) or 1583 (other sources).",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Njinga","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Njinga","Supports the Black communities around the world page","After Ngola Mbande's death in 1624, Njinga took power in Ndongo—first as regent, then as queen."),SRCQ("University of North Carolina: Njinga of Angola: Africa's Warrior Queen","https://gwonline.unc.edu/node/7631","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Njinga is revered in Angola as a national heroine and honored in folk religions"),SRCQ("Thornton, 'Legitimacy and Political Power: Queen Njinga, 1624–1663', Journal of African History","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-african-history/article/abs/legitimacy-and-political-power-queen-njinga-162416631/0A8C82A9E8B34BBF94A7CA12D4A819D1","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the issue of legitimacy to rule in Ndongo was a complex one")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Loango coast and the Vili kingdom",text:"North of the Congo River, the Vili kingdom of Loango dominated a coast shared with Kakongo and Ngoyo from the 16th century. Its trade with Europeans shifted from cloth and other goods to the trade in enslaved people, which reshaped its politics until the old kingdom's demise in the 19th century.",figure:"",year:"1576–1870",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (a university repository record of Martin's doctoral study, later published by Clarendon Press).",src:[SRCQ("SOAS Research Online: Phyllis M. Martin, The External Trade of the Loango Coast and its Effects on the Vili, 1576–1870","https://soas-repository.worktribe.com/output/391453/the-external-trade-of-the-loango-coast-and-its-effects-on-the-vili-1576-1870","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In the sixteenth century, the region was divided into three kingdoms, Loango, Kakongo and Ngoyo."),SRCQ("SOAS Research Online: Martin, Loango Coast","https://soas-repository.worktribe.com/output/391453/the-external-trade-of-the-loango-coast-and-its-effects-on-the-vili-1576-1870","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the years from 1670 to 1793 have been called the Slave Trade Era")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"central-kingdoms-culture",h:"Central African kingdoms, art and music",intro:"Central Africa produced complex states, original writing systems and art and music that changed the world. These points honour that creativity across today's DRC, Cameroon, Gabon, Chad and the Central African Republic.",points:[
  {h:"Kuba cloth: raffia textiles of the Kuba kingdom",text:"In the Kuba kingdom of today's Democratic Republic of the Congo, men weave raffia-palm cloth and women decorate it, including the velvety cut-pile technique. Prestige panels served as currency, tribute and markers of status and were worn at ceremonies including funerals.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The Metropolitan Museum of Art: Double Prestige Panel (Kuba)","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/318398","Supports the Black communities around the world page","These panels were not worn but displayed, and served as currency and markers of prestige."),SRCQ("McClung Museum, University of Tennessee: Raffia cloth","https://mcclungmuseum.utk.edu/2020/07/22/ondisplay-raffia-cloth","Supports the Black communities around the world page","men typically do the weaving in Kuba culture, while women are responsible for the applique and embroidery of textiles."),SRCQ("University of Michigan Museum of Art: Raffia textile panel","https://umma.umich.edu/objects/raffia-textile-panel-1986-2-106","Supports the Black communities around the world page","used as a marker of prestige, as currency, to pay tribute")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Luba and Lunda states",text:"The Luba and Lunda states flourished from the late 15th to the late 19th century in the south of today's DRC and neighbouring areas. The Lunda empire reached its height by the 1850s, trading ivory and enslaved people for cloth and guns, before being eroded by Chokwe incursions and partitioned by colonial powers.",figure:"",year:"c.1490s–1890s",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (two Britannica pages count as one publisher). Britannica's wording uses 'slaves'.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Luba-Lunda states","https://www.Britannica.Com/place/Luba-Lunda-states","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The Luba state was situated east of the Kasai River around the headwaters of the Lualaba River."),SRCQ("Britannica: Lunda empire","https://www.britannica.com/place/Lunda-empire","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The empire reached the height of its power by the 1850s.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"King Njoya and the Bamum script of Cameroon",text:"From about 1896 King Ibrahim Njoya of the Bamum kingdom (western Cameroon) created an original writing system, revising it several times until around 1910. Njoya used it for records including a map of his kingdom; French colonial rule later removed him and suppressed its use.",figure:"",year:"c.1896–1910",disputed:false,note:"Sources differ on the final character count (70 in Riley; 80 in Endangered Alphabets). The suppression detail rests on the non-authoritative Endangered Alphabets page.",src:[SRCQ("Unicode Consortium document L2/07-023: Bamum script report (Charles Riley, Bamum Scripts and Archives Project)","https://www.unicode.org/L2/L2007/07023-bamum-report.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Njoya is believed to have begun working on the script shortly after the beginning of his reign, around 1896."),SRCQ("British Library Endangered Archives Programme EAP051: Bamum script and archives project","https://eap.bl.uk/index.php/project/EAP051/search?page=33","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Map of the boundaries of the Bamum Kingdom drawn by King Njoya himself, with localities noted in Akauku Mfemfe script"),SRCQ("Endangered Alphabets Project: Bamum","https://www.endangeredalphabets.net/?p=143","Supports the Black communities around the world page","they maneuvred Njoya out of power, smashed his printing press, burned his libraries and books")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Fang reliquary guardians of Gabon",text:"Fang communities of Gabon and Cameroon carved byeri guardian figures to watch over containers holding the relics of lineage ancestors and founders. Collected by European modernist artists such as André Derain and Jacob Epstein, they are now held by major museums.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Smarthistory: Female Figure from a Reliquary Ensemble (Fang peoples)","https://smarthistory.org/?p=16094","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Byeri was a Fang association devoted to the veneration of lineage ancestors and founders"),SRCQ("National Museums Scotland: bieri","https://www.nms.ac.uk/explore-our-collections/collection-search-results/bieri/333548","Supports the Black communities around the world page","female with rear projection to hook into reliquary box to contain ancestral bones"),SRCQ("The Menil Collection: Reliquary Guardian Figure (Eyima Byeri)","https://www.menil.org/collection/objects/5913-reliquary-guardian-figure-eyima-or-eyima-byeri","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Gabon or Cameroon")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Kanem-Bornu: a thousand-year state around Lake Chad",text:"Kanem-Bornu, founded around the 9th century, controlled the Lake Chad region until the 19th century, making it one of Africa's longest-lasting states. It became an Islamic state, and Idris Alawma (reigned about 1571–1603) is remembered as one of its great rulers.",figure:"",year:"9th–19th century",disputed:false,note:"Britannica gives the Sef dynasty's end as 1846; World History Encyclopedia says the empire lasted until French takeover in the late 19th century.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Kanem-Bornu","https://www.britannica.com/place/Kanem-Bornu","Supports the Black communities around the world page","controlled the area around Lake Chad from the 9th to the 19th century."),SRCQ("World History Encyclopedia: Kingdom of Kanem (Mark Cartwright, 2019)","https://www.worldhistory.org/Kingdom_of_Kanem/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The empire would endure until the late 19th century CE when it was taken over by the French")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Barthélemy Boganda and the Central African Republic",text:"Barthélemy Boganda (1910–1959), a former priest, founded the Social Evolution Movement of Black Africa (MESAN) in 1949 and led Ubangi-Shari towards independence. He died in a plane crash in 1959, before independence on 13 August 1960, and is revered as a national martyr.",figure:"",year:"1960",disputed:true,note:"The cause of the 1959 crash is unresolved; the AfricaMuseum says it 'remains unknown to this day'.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Barthélemy Boganda","https://www.britannica.com/print/article/71404","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Boganda remains revered by Central Africans as a national martyr."),SRCQ("AfricaMuseum (Royal Museum for Central Africa, Belgium): République centrafricaine","https://independance.africamuseum.be/en/node/179","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Barthélemy Boganda, a former priest, channels popular energy and hope with MESAN")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Congolese rumba: UNESCO heritage of the two Congos",text:"Congolese rumba, the music and dance of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and the Republic of the Congo, was inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in December 2021 after a joint bid. Its roots run back to the old Kongo kingdom, across the Atlantic to Cuba and back again, carried by artists such as Franco and TPOK Jazz and Tabu Ley Rochereau.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage: Congolese rumba","https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/congolese-rumba-01711?RL=01711","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Inscribed in 2021 (16.COM) on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity"),SRCQ("Al Jazeera: Rumba added to UNESCO heritage list (15 December 2021)","https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2021/12/15/congolese-rumba-added-unesco-heritage-list","Supports the Black communities around the world page","This cultural gem specific to the two Congos (Kinshasa & Brazzaville) is recognised for its universal value."),SRCQ("Africanews: Congolese rumba wins UNESCO heritage status","https://www.africanews.com/amp/2021/12/14/congolese-rumba-wins-unesco-world-heritage-status/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","comes from the Kikongo word for navel")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"colonial-uk",h:"Colonial violence and Central African communities in the UK",intro:"The Congo Free State was one of the worst colonial regimes, and campaigners in Britain and Ireland helped expose it. Today Congolese, Angolan and Cameroonian communities are part of British life.",points:[
  {h:"The Congo Free State: forced labour and mass death",text:"From 1885 to 1908 the Congo Free State was under King Leopold II of Belgium's personal control. Forced labour was used to gather wild rubber and ivory, people who failed to collect rubber were punished or killed and their wives taken hostage, and the population is said to have fallen sharply.",figure:"20 million to 8 million (estimate)",year:"1885–1908",disputed:true,note:"Population estimates are contested; Britannica itself hedges ('is said to have'). No reliable census existed.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Congo Free State","https://www.britannica.com/place/Congo-Free-State","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The population of the entire state is said to have declined from some 20 million to 8 million."),SRCQ("National Museum of Ireland: Casement in the Congo Free State (exhibition panel)","https://source.museum.ie/getmedia/bbc5ec22-153c-4051-8cef-9ec710f3b635/Panel-4-Casement-in-the-Congo-Free-State.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","They were punished viciously or killed and often their wives were taken hostage if they did not collect rubber.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Exposure by Casement and British campaigners",text:"Roger Casement's report, based on observation and interviews, was published in February 1904, and a campaign group founded by British citizens spread the truth about the regime. In 1908 Leopold was forced to hand the territory to the Belgian state, which ran it as the Belgian Congo.",figure:"",year:"1904–1908",disputed:false,note:"Congolese testimony was central to the evidence; the reform campaign is often told through European figures alone.",src:[SRCQ("National Museum of Ireland: Casement in the Congo Free State","https://source.museum.ie/getmedia/bbc5ec22-153c-4051-8cef-9ec710f3b635/Panel-4-Casement-in-the-Congo-Free-State.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","His report, based on observation and interviews, was published in February 1904."),SRCQ("Britannica: Congo Free State","https://www.britannica.com/place/Congo-Free-State","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 1908 the Congo Free State was abolished and replaced by the Belgian Congo.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Congolese, Angolan and Cameroonian communities in England and Wales",text:"The 2021 Census recorded 29,439 people in England and Wales born in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, 19,717 born in Angola and 14,614 born in Cameroon. Birmingham alone had 3,848 residents born in Central African countries.",figure:"DRC 29,439; Angola 19,717; Cameroon 14,614",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Rests on one source (a council profile quoting ONS Census 2021 country-of-birth data; the ONS dataset page could not be read as text). Country of birth counts first-generation migrants only, so UK-born Congolese, Angolan and Cameroonian people are not included.",src:[SRCQ("Birmingham City Council: Central African community health profile (Census 2021 data)","https://www.birmingham.gov.uk/info/50305/community_health_profiles/2916/central_african_community_health_profile","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Democratic Republic of the Congo: 29,439"),SRCQ("Birmingham City Council: Central African community health profile","https://www.birmingham.gov.uk/info/50305/community_health_profiles/2916/central_african_community_health_profile","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Overall, there were 3,848 people born in Central African countries living in Birmingham")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"caribbean-islands",h:"Caribbean islands: resistance, freedom and culture",intro:"Individual Caribbean nations have their own histories of resistance and creativity, and many families in the UK trace roots to them. These points look beyond the general word 'Caribbean' to specific places and people.",points:[
  {h:"Mary Prince: the first Black woman's narrative published in Britain",text:"Mary Prince, born to enslaved parents in Bermuda in 1788 and enslaved in Bermuda, Turks Island and Antigua, published The History of Mary Prince in London in 1831. It was the first narrative of a Black woman published in Britain and fed the campaign to end slavery.",figure:"",year:"1831",disputed:false,note:"The London Museum says only 'the West Indies' for her birthplace; Bermuda comes from the Women's History Network. That page also calls her petition the first anti-slavery petition by a Black woman to Parliament.",src:[SRCQ("London Museum: The life story of Mary Prince","https://londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/the-life-story-of-mary-prince","Supports the Black communities around the world page","It was the first narrative of a Black woman to be published in Britain."),SRCQ("University of Michigan Press: The History of Mary Prince","https://press.umich.edu/isbn/9780472084104","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Mary Prince was the first black British woman to escape from slavery and publish a record of her experiences."),SRCQ("Women's History Network: Witness against slavery, the story of Mary Prince","https://womenshistorynetwork.org/black-history-month-witness-against-slavery-the-story-of-mary-prince/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Mary Prince was born a slave in 1788 in Brackish Pond, Devonshire Parish, Bermuda.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Grenada's Fédon rebellion, 1795–96",text:"On 2 March 1795 Julien Fédon, a free man of colour, led a rising backed by the French Republic that aimed to end slavery and British rule in Grenada; some 7,000 enslaved people joined. It was put down in 1796 after more than a year of fighting and followed by mass executions.",figure:"about 7,000 enslaved people joined",year:"1795–1796",disputed:false,note:"UWI gives the official end as 19 June 1796; The National Archives cites the stronghold's capture reported by 4 July 1796. At least 77 men were sentenced to death.",src:[SRCQ("The National Archives (UK) blog: 'Excessive severity': Treason and the Grenadian Rebellion of 1795","https://blog.nationalarchives.gov.uk/excessive-severity-treason-and-the-grenadian-rebellion-of-1795","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Fédon too said he would emancipate the enslaved, and some 7,000 joined his forces."),SRCQ("University of the West Indies: Curtis Jacobs, The Fédons of Grenada, 1763–1814","https://global.uwi.edu/sites/default/files/bnccde/grenada/conference/papers/Jacobsc.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","they fought for the destruction of chattel slavery and British colonial rule.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Grenada Revolution, 1979–83",text:"In March 1979 the New Jewel Movement under Maurice Bishop took power in a bloodless coup and formed the People's Revolutionary Government. The University of the West Indies credits it with gains in education, housing and employment.",figure:"",year:"1979",disputed:true,note:"Its record is contested; see the counter-argument on detentions and the 1983 collapse.",src:[SRCQ("UWI Today (University of the West Indies), September 2014","https://sta.uwi.edu/UWIToday/archive/september_2014/article14.asp","Supports the Black communities around the world page","From 1979 to 1983, the People’s Revolutionary Government engaged Grenadians in a bold Caribbean experiment."),SRCQ("Global Voices: Grenada officially remembers the murders of Maurice Bishop and others (2023)","https://globalvoices.org/2023/10/20/forty-years-later-grenada-officially-remembers-the-murders-of-its-prime-minister-maurice-bishop-and-others/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","ousted Eric Gairy, the island's first prime minister, from office in a bloodless, popular coup while Gairy was abroad.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Detentions, Bishop's killing and the 1983 invasion",text:"The revolutionary government held people without trial; estimates range from 1,000 to 3,000. On 19 October 1983 Bishop and others were executed on orders from his deputy, and a United States-led invasion followed on 25 October. Grenada held its first public holiday to remember the killings in 2023.",figure:"1,000–3,000 detained (estimates)",year:"1983",disputed:true,note:"Detention figures are estimates. The deputy prime minister who gave the order is not named here. UWI Today gives 25 October for the landing.",src:[SRCQ("UWI Today, September 2014","https://sta.uwi.edu/UWIToday/archive/september_2014/article14.asp","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Some estimate there were 1000 imprisoned without reasons; others say the figure was probably 3000."),SRCQ("Global Voices (2023)","https://globalvoices.org/2023/10/20/forty-years-later-grenada-officially-remembers-the-murders-of-its-prime-minister-maurice-bishop-and-others/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Forty years later, Grenada has observed its first ever public holiday to commemorate this dark day in its history.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"The Bahamas: an African-descended majority and Junkanoo",text:"About 85% of Bahamians are estimated to be of African descent; Loyalists arriving after the American Revolution brought enslaved people, and slavery ended in 1834. Junkanoo, begun by enslaved people as a New Year celebration, has been promoted since majority rule in 1967 as an expression of Black Bahamian culture.",figure:"about 85%",year:"",disputed:true,note:"An estimate: the Bahamian government does not record ethnicity. The Junkanoo detail rests on one source (CultureGrams). Haitians are estimated at 10–25% of the population (MRG), a wide range.",src:[SRCQ("CultureGrams 2014: Bahamas (ProQuest and Brigham Young University)","https://www.gvsu.edu/cms4/asset/7D7DCFF8-C4AD-66A3-6344C7E690C4BFD9/bahamas_2014.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Some sources estimate that 85 percent of the population of the Bahamas is of African descent."),SRCQ("Minority Rights Group International: Bahamas","https://minorityrights.org/country/bahamas/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Some 85 per cent of the population of the Bahamas is of African descent.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Dominica: an African-descended nation alongside the Kalinago",text:"The great majority of Dominicans are of African descent, the island also being home to about 4,000 Indigenous Kalinago people. Slavery was abolished in 1833–34, and Dominica became independent from Britain on 3 November 1978.",figure:"",year:"1978",disputed:false,note:"The African-descent share rests on MRG (non-government); the High Commission page confirms the Kalinago and independence only.",src:[SRCQ("Minority Rights Group International: Dominica","https://minorityrights.org/country/dominica/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The overwhelming majority of the population of Dominica is of African descent"),SRCQ("High Commission for the Commonwealth of Dominica in the UK: History","https://dominicahighcommission.co.uk/history.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Dominica was already inhabited by the native Kalinago people."),SRCQ("Discover Dominica: History and culture","https://discoverdominica.com/history-culture","Supports the Black communities around the world page","On November 3, 1978, the island attained its independence from Britain.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Trinidad and Tobago: first national Emancipation Day, 1985",text:"On 1 August 1985 Trinidad and Tobago became the first country to declare a national holiday marking the abolition of slavery. The date recalls 1 August 1838, when full freedom came into effect; the holiday replaced Discovery Day, which marked Columbus's arrival.",figure:"",year:"1985",disputed:false,note:"The replacement of Discovery Day rests on the non-government guide only.",src:[SRCQ("Tobago House of Assembly: Free to Celebrate","https://www.tha.gov.tt/info-hub/free-to-celebrate/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Trinidad and Tobago became the first country in the world to declare a national holiday"),SRCQ("Discover Trinidad & Tobago (Media & Editorial Projects): Emancipation Day","https://www.discovertnt.com/?p=8971","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The holiday in Trinidad & Tobago replaced Columbus Discovery Day, which had marked Christopher Columbus' arrival in Trinidad on 31 July, 1498.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Canboulay: from burnt cane to the start of Carnival",text:"Canboulay, from the French cannes brûlées ('burnt cane'), began as a plantation work practice of the enslaved and became a procession celebrating the end of slavery. After the 1881 Canboulay Riots, when people defended it against colonial suppression, it survived as Carnival's opening procession before dawn on Carnival Monday.",figure:"",year:"1881",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Patrick Murphy, 'Canboulay and the Nègre Jardin', Journal of Festive Studies 6 (2024), University of Chicago Knowledge repository","https://knowledge.uchicago.edu/record/14266/files/Canboulay-and-the-Negre-Jardin.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Canboulay would persist as Carnival’s opening procession"),SRCQ("Trinidad Express: Canboulay, the origins of Carnival","https://vibes.trinidadexpress.com/arts/canboulay-the-origins-of-carnival","Supports the Black communities around the world page","commemorates the historic 1881 Canboulay Riots, when freed Africans courageously defended their cultural expressions against colonial suppression.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Berbice 1763 and Guyana's 23 February",text:"On 23 February 1763 enslaved people in the Dutch colony of Berbice rose up, led by Cuffy (Kofi), an Akan man; the rising was crushed in 1764. Guyana chose 23 February for Republic Day in 1970 to mark it, and Cuffy, now a national hero, stands on the 1763 Monument in Georgetown, unveiled in 1976.",figure:"more than 2,500 people rose up (DPI Guyana)",year:"1763",disputed:false,note:"The Republic Day link rests on a newspaper editorial; the Guyana government page confirms the rising and Cuffy's status. Spellings vary: Cuffy, Coffy, Kofi.",src:[SRCQ("The Black Archives (Amsterdam): Berbice Rebellion","https://www.theblackarchives.nl/berbice-rebellion.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","On February 23, 1763, the slave uprising in Berbice started."),SRCQ("Department of Public Information, Government of Guyana: Emancipation memorials","https://dpi.gov.gy/emancipation-memorials-honouring-our-freedom-fighters","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Despite his defeat, Cuffy became a national hero of Guyana, and a symbol for the fight against colonialism."),SRCQ("Stabroek News editorial: Dates (2020)","https://stabroeknews.com/2020/02/16/opinion/editorial/dates","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The date was chosen in 1970 to coincide with the outbreak of one of the great risings of enslaved in the region: the 1763 Uprising in Berbice."),SRCQ("Contemporary Monuments to the Slave Past (Renée Ater): 1763 Monument","https://slaverymonuments.org/items/show/1161","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The structure was unveiled by President Linden Forbes Sampson Burnham on May 23, 1976.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"census",h:"Who identifies as mixed? Census and survey categories",intro:"Shares below are what people told census-takers or survey interviewers about themselves, in each country's own categories. They measure self-identification, not ancestry, and are not directly comparable between countries.",points:[
  {h:"Brazil – pardo (and preto)",text:"In Brazil's 2022 census 45.3% (about 92.1 million) declared themselves pardo (brown, of mixed ancestry), the largest group for the first time since 1991; a further 10.2% declared preto (black). Pretos and pardos together are 55.5%. Pardo has historically denoted people of mixed ancestry, including African descent, but it can also include people of Indigenous–European descent.",figure:"pardo 45.3%; preto 10.2%; together 55.5%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"IBGE pages count as one publisher; Jornal do Brasil (with Agência Brasil) is the independent second source.",src:[SRCQ("IBGE News Agency: 2022 Census: self-reported brown population is the majority in Brazil for the first time","https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-news/2184-news-agency/news/38726-2022-census-self-reported-brown-population-is-the-majority-in-brazil-for-the-first-time","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2022, about 92.1 million persons reported being brown, which corresponds to 45.3% of the country’s population. ... Other 20.6 million persons reported being black (10.2%)"),SRCQ("IBGE Educa: Um Brasil preto e pardo","https://educa.ibge.gov.br/criancas/brasil/2848-nosso-povo/23072-um-brasil-preto-e-pardo.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","O Censo 2022 contou 92.083.286 pessoas que se declararam pardas, ou 45,3% da população total do país. ... Juntos, pretos e pardos somam 55,5% da população brasileira."),SRCQ("Jornal do Brasil (com Agência Brasil): Censo 2022: população parda supera a branca pela 1ª vez desde 1872","https://www.jb.com.br/brasil/2023/12/1047975-censo-2022-populacao-parda-supera-a-branca-pela-1-vez-desde-1872.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Por outro lado, os pardos aumentaram a participação de 43,1% para 45,3%. ... A população preta saltou de 7,6% para 10,2%."),SRCQ("Revista de Antropologia 67 (2024): Race and color in contemporary Brazil, political opportunism and historical trends","https://new.scielo.br/j/ra/a/6btsjxZXTRDhmYDDfmXZ69n/?lang=en","Supports the Black communities around the world page","while \"pardos\" were mixed-race individuals of African descent. ... Individuals with indigenous or black ancestry tended to identify as brown, even if dark-skinned")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cuba – mestizo / mulato",text:"Cuba's 2012 census recorded 26.6% of people as mestizo/mulato by skin colour (color de la piel), 9.3% negro and about 64% blanco.",figure:"mestizo/mulato 26.6%",year:"2012",disputed:true,note:"White share given as 64%, 64.1% and 64.3% by different secondary sources; 26.6% agrees across all. ONEI primary tables not cited here.",src:[SRCQ("Real Instituto Elcano, Documento de trabajo 5/2021: El rompecabezas de la desigualdad social en Cuba de 1980 a 2010","https://www.realinstitutoelcano.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/dt5-2021-fuente-bailey-el-rompecabezas-de-la-desigualdad-social-en-cuba-de-1980-a-2010.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","De acuerdo con el último censo disponible (2012), el 64% de los cubanos se reconoce dentro de esa categoría ... mientras que el 26,6% se autodefine como mulato o mestizo y el 9,3% como negro. [Figure source: Censos cubanos (ONEI, 2016)]"),SRCQ("OnCuba News: El racismo, herencias y vigencias","https://oncubanews.com/ecos/el-racismo-herencias-y-vigencias","Supports the Black communities around the world page","los blancos representan 64,3% de la población total, los negros 9,3% y los mulatos 26,6%"),SRCQ("Diario Las Américas: Negros en Cuba, más rehenes de su raza que nunca","https://www.diariolasamericas.com/america-latina/negros-cuba-mas-rehenes-su-raza-que-nunca-n4200743","Supports the Black communities around the world page","el 64.1% de la población es blanca, el 26.6% es mestiza y el 9.3% es negra.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Dominican Republic – indio, mulato, moreno",text:"The national census has not published a race result since 1960. A 2021 UNFPA national survey found 45% describing themselves with 'indio'-type terms, 16% moreno, 9% mulato, 8% negro, 18% blanco. 'Indio' here is a skin-colour term (e.g. indio claro, indio canela) for people of mixed African–European appearance, not a claim of Indigenous ancestry; the island's Taíno peoples were largely destroyed in the 16th century and the label was promoted in the Trujillo era.",figure:"indio 45%; mulato 9%; moreno 16% (survey)",year:"2021",disputed:true,note:"Survey, not census. ENHOGAR 2021 (per Acento) gives different figures: 48% indio/mestizo, 27% mulato. 2022 census race results not located.",src:[SRCQ("El Caribe: El 33% de los dominicanos se percibe moreno y negro","https://www.elcaribe.com.do/panorama/pais/el-33-de-los-dominicanos-se-percibe-moreno-y-negro/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","el 45% de las personas que participaron se autodescribe con algunas de las categorías vinculadas con la de “indio”. ... Tan solo el 9% hace referencia a “mulato”, el 8% a “negro”, mientras que “mestizo” fue mencionada tan solo por el 1%."),SRCQ("El Día: Solo el 8 % de los dominicanos se identifica como negro","https://eldia.com.do/solo-el-8-de-los-dominicanos-se-identifica-como-negro/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En cambio, el 45 % de los dominicanos se considera indio ... Solo el 8 % de los dominicanos se define como negro [article attributes 'indio' to Trujillo-era promotion and notes 'los pueblos nativos de la isla fueron exterminados en el siglo XVI']")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Venezuela – moreno",text:"In the 2011 census 51.6% identified as moreno/a (brown), 2.9% negro/a and 0.7% afrodescendiente; 43.6% blanco/a. 'Moreno' is a broad brown category that need not denote African ancestry.",figure:"moreno 51.6%; negro 2.9%; afrodescendiente 0.7%",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"MRG says the Afro-descendant shares are 'far lower than the likely actual proportion'.",src:[SRCQ("Minority Rights Group: Venezuela","https://minorityrights.org/country/venezuela/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","0.7 per cent of the total population identified themselves as Afro-descendant and 2.9 per cent as Black ... compared to 51.6 per cent as brown and 43.6 per cent as white"),SRCQ("Periferia (Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona) vol. 19 no. 2 (2014)","https://ddd.uab.cat/pub/periferia/periferia_a2014m12v19n2/periferia_a2014m12v19n2p50.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","AFRODESCENDIENTES SEGÚN CENSO DE POBLACIÓN 2011: Negra / Negro 755.626 2,87; Afrodescendiente 181.144 0,69; Morena / Moreno 13.594.603 51,64; Blanca / Blanco 11.490.347 43,65 (INE 2011)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Colombia – mulato within the NARP group",text:"Colombia's 2018 census asked one combined option 'Negro(a), mulato(a), afrodescendiente, afrocolombiano(a)' alongside raizal and palenquero; mulato cannot be separated out. The census counted 2,982,224 NARP people (DANE table; about 6.8% of those counted); the Ministry of Health cites 5.9%. DANE's official estimate after correcting census undercount is 9.34% (ECV 2018).",figure:"NARP 5.9–6.8% (census); 9.34% (DANE estimate)",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"Census undercount acknowledged by DANE; mulato not separately published.",src:[SRCQ("DANE/UNFPA: Análisis de la dinámica intercensal del autorreconocimiento NARP","https://www.dane.gov.co/files/censo2018/estudios-poscensales/06-analisis-dinamica-intercensal-autorreconocimiento-NARP-UNFPA.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","la población NARP a nivel nacional presentó una disminución del 30,8%, es decir, 1.329.533 personas. [Table 1: NARP 2005 4.311.757; 2018 2.982.224 — extractor computed 6,8%]"),SRCQ("Ministerio de Salud y Protección Social: Informe NARP (abril 2021)","https://www.minsalud.gov.co/sites/rid/Lists/BibliotecaDigital/RIDE/DE/PES/GP/informe-narp-abril-2021.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","De acuerdo con el Censo Nacional de Población y Vivienda de 2018- CNPV 2018 el 5,9% de la población Colombiana se autorreconoce como perteneciente a la población NARP.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Panama – afrodescendiente (no mixed category)",text:"Panama's 2023 census asked about Afro-descendant self-recognition, with sub-groups including moreno(a), negro(a), afrocolonial and afroantillano(a). 31.7% (1,286,857) identified as Afro-descendant, up from 9.2% in 2010. There is no separate 'mixed' category.",figure:"afrodescendiente 31.7%",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"A preliminary figure of 32.8% (swissinfo, March 2023) was superseded.",src:[SRCQ("TVN Noticias (Panamá): resultados del Censo","https://www.tvn-2.com/nacionales/educacion-analfabetismo-acceso-tecnologia-resultados_1_2065149.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","El porcentaje de la población que se autoreconoció como afrodescendiente ... tuvo un considerable aumento de 9.2% en 2010 a 31.7% en 2023."),SRCQ("Telemetro: El total de habitantes de Panamá es 4,202,572","https://www.telemetro.com/nacionales/el-total-habitantes-panama-es-4202572-n5900234","Supports the Black communities around the world page","1,286,857 personas son afrodescendientes ... comparado con el 2010, donde sólo el 9.2 se denominaba parte de este grupo étnico del país."),SRCQ("INEC Panamá, Censos 2023, Cuadro 41 (Población afrodescendiente de 5 a 39 años por grupo afrodescendiente)","https://www.inec.gob.pa/archivos/P053342420240206093153Cuadro%2041.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Afrodescendiente 111,013; Afropanameño(a) 85,231; Moreno(a) 112,650; Negro(a) 25,035; Afrocolonial 5,403; Afroantillano(a) 5,307; Otro grupo afrodescendiente (2) 344,593")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Puerto Rico – two or more races",text:"In the 2020 US census for Puerto Rico 49.8% chose two or more races (3.3% in 2010), while white alone fell to 17.1% and Black alone to 7.0%. The jump reflects changes to the question and coding, not a sudden change in ancestry.",figure:"two or more races 49.8%",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Center for Puerto Rican Studies (Hunter College, CUNY): Puerto Rico's 2020 Race/Ethnicity Decennial Analysis","https://centropr.hunter.cuny.edu/app/uploads/2021/10/022223-Puerto-Ricos-2020-Race_Ethnicity-Decennial-Analysis.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","from 3.3% of the total population in 2010 to 49.8% in 2020 ... dropped by 50.4% in 2020 from 12.4% from the total population in 2010 to 7.0% in 2020"),SRCQ("NBC News: Surprised by census results, many in Puerto Rico reconsider views on race","https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/surprised-census-results-many-puerto-rico-reconsider-views-race-rcna3101","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Nearly 50 percent of those represented in the 2020 census ... a jump from 3 percent — or some 122,200 of 3.72 million — who chose that option in the 2010 census.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cabo Verde – crioulo / mestiço",text:"Cabo Verde's census does not publish a race or ethnicity count. Reference works describe more than two-thirds of the population as mestiço or crioulo (mixed African and European, mainly Portuguese); the 71% figure commonly quoted is a 2006 CIA World Factbook estimate, not a census figure.",figure:"about 71% (estimate)",year:"2006 est.",disputed:false,note:"Not a census figure.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Cabo Verde summary","https://www.Britannica.Com/summary/Cabo-Verde","Supports the Black communities around the world page","More than two-thirds of its population are of mixed African and European origin (known as mestiço or Crioulo)"),SRCQ("Minority Rights Group: Cape Verde","https://minorityrights.org/country/cape-verde/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The population of Cape Verde today is 71 per cent Creole, 28 per cent African and 1 per cent European (CIA Factbook 2006)."),SRCQ("EBSCO Research Starters: Cape Verde (Cabo Verde)","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/social-sciences-and-humanities/cape-verde-cabo-verde","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Creole, or mixed African and Portuguese descent (71 percent); African (28 percent); and European (1 percent).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"South Africa – Coloured",text:"In Census 2022, 8.2% identified as Coloured (81.4% Black African, 7.3% White, 2.7% Indian/Asian). 'Coloured' was imposed as a legal category by the Population Registration Act 1950 but is today a self-identification with its own history and communities, rooted in Khoekhoe and San, enslaved people from Asia and East Africa, Bantu-speaking and European ancestors.",figure:"Coloured 8.2%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Stats SA, Mbalo Brief, October 2023 (Census 2022)","https://statssa.gov.za/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Mbalo-brief-October-2023-WEB-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Black Africans remain the dominant population group at 81,4%, followed by the coloured population at 8,2%."),SRCQ("SAnews (Government Communication and Information System): SA population grows from 51.7 million to 62 million","https://www.sanews.gov.za/index.ph%70/node/65820","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Black Africans remain the dominant population group at 81.4%, followed by the coloured population at 8.2%."),SRCQ("Human Molecular Genetics 19(3) (2010): Genetic structure of a unique admixed population: implications for medical research","https://doi.org/10.1093/hmg/ddp505","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Statistics South Africa continues to classify people by population group, in order to monitor progress in moving away from the apartheid-based discrimination of the past. However, membership of a population group is now based on self-perception and self-classification, not on a legal definition. [quoting the 2001 census]."),SRCQ("South African History Archive (SAHA), Sunday Times Heritage Project: An appalling 'science'","https://sthp.saha.org.za/memorial/articles/an_appalling_science.htm","Supports the Black communities around the world page","a person who is not a white person nor generally accepted as a member of aboriginal race or tribe of Africa ... When it was first passed in 1950, the Population Registration Act defined racial groups relatively loosely. ... As late as 1984 there were still re-classifications.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Namibia – no race question",text:"Namibia's censuses have not asked race since independence (it was not asked in 2001); the 2023 census recorded self-identified ethnic/language groups. Rehoboth Basters and Coloured communities exist but no census share is published here.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Census does not ask; no estimate given.",src:[SRCQ("Institutionalized Ethnicity project: Namibia country narrative","https://institutionalizedethnicity.net/country-narratives/namibia/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Race was counted on the census continuously throughout the 20th century but was not asked on the 2001 census ... We do not find substantial evidence of institutionalized race following Namibia's independence."),SRCQ("Namibian Sun: NSA admits 'oversight' in grouping white Namibians (2024)","https://namibiansun.com/local-news/nsa-admits-oversight-in-grouping-white-namibians2024-11-13","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the census allowed respondents to self-identify, which influenced the data categories. [Extractor: article does not say whether race was asked; refers to ethnicity/language groups]")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mauritius – no community question since 1972",text:"Mauritius has not produced community-disaggregated census statistics since 1972 (questions dropped from the 1983 census). Creoles of African and Malagasy descent fall within the constitutional 'General Population'.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Census does not ask.",src:[SRCQ("UN Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination, summary record CERD/C/SR.3147 (Mauritius)","https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4084064/files/CERD_C_SR.3147-EN.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","While community-disaggregated statistics had not been produced since 1972 ... including the Creoles, the Chagossians and the Ilois"),SRCQ("Le Mauricien: Historical overview – Tackling diversity through census exercises can prove very tricky","https://www.lemauricien.com/?p=115035","Supports the Black communities around the world page","As from the census of 1983 therefore, questions related to ethnic/communal belonging are not asked. ... Three census categories were recognized: General Population, Indo-Mauritians and Chinese.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Seychelles – no census race question located",text:"No census race/ethnicity count located. Reference works describe the population as largely of mixed European and African descent, with Indian, Chinese and Arab ancestry.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Descriptive only; no share.",src:[SRCQ("Library of Congress Country Studies (via countrystudies.us): Seychelles","https://countrystudies.us/seychelles/7.htm","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The population is a relatively homogeneous one of mixed European and African descent"),SRCQ("Global Geography (reproduces World Factbook-style entries): Seychelles People & Society","https://global-geography.org/af/Geography/Africa/Seychelles/People_Society","Supports the Black communities around the world page","mixed French, African, Indian, Chinese, and Arab")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Trinidad and Tobago – Mixed (incl. Dougla)",text:"In 2011, 22.8% identified as Mixed: 7.7% Mixed African/East Indian ('Dougla') and 15.1% Mixed Other; 34.2% African, 35.4% East Indian.",figure:"Mixed 22.8% (Dougla 7.7%)",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"EBSCO gives 15.3% for Mixed Other; CSO report says 15.1%.",src:[SRCQ("Central Statistical Office, Trinidad and Tobago 2011 Population and Housing Census Demographic Report (hosted by UNDP)","https://files.acquia.undp.org/public/migration/tt/TandT_Demographic_Report_2011.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The next largest group was described as ‘Mixed’ representing 22.8% of the population and disaggregated into the ‘African/East Indian’ 7.7% and ‘Mixed Other’ 15.1%. This group increased from 20.5% in 2000."),SRCQ("EBSCO Research Starters: Trinidad and Tobago","https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/social-sciences-and-humanities/trinidad-and-tobago","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Another 34.2 percent were of African descent, 7.7 percent were mixed African and East Indian descent, ... 15.3 percent were of another admixture"),SRCQ("Trinidad Express: All in the mix","https://trinidadexpress.com/news/local/all-in-the-mix/article_c814559d-eab7-58b3-a0ae-b50a5401c8c9.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","with the mixed population standing at 22.8 per cent and rising. ... 7.7 per cent are what we refer to as douglas")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Guyana – Mixed Heritage",text:"In 2012, 19.9% (148,532) were of Mixed Heritage, up from 16.7% in 2002; East Indian 39.8%, African 29.2%, Amerindian 10.5%.",figure:"Mixed 19.9%",year:"2012",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Bureau of Statistics Guyana: 2012 Population and Housing Census Compendium 2","https://statisticsguyana.gov.gy/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Final_2012_Census_Compendium2.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The third in rank are those of Mixed Heritage (19.9 percent) up from 16.7 percent in 2002 Census."),SRCQ("Stabroek News: report on 2012 census ethnic composition","https://www.stabroeknews.com/?p=385625","Supports the Black communities around the world page","who accounted for 148,532 or 19.9% of the total population")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Belize – Creole",text:"In 2022, 25.2% identified as Creole (of African and European, mostly British, descent) and 4.0% Garifuna (African–Indigenous Caribbean descent); Mestizo (51.7%) refers mainly to Spanish–Maya ancestry.",figure:"Creole 25.2%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistical Institute of Belize: Census 2022 Population","https://sib.org.bz/wp-content/uploads/Census2022_Population.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Mestizo: 51.7%; Creole: 25.2%; Maya: 11.2%; Garifuna: 4.0% (Distribution of major ethnic groups, 1980 - 2022)"),SRCQ("Amandala: SIB reveals results of 2022 Population and Housing Census","https://amandala.com.bz/news/sib-reveals-results-of-2022-population-and-housing-census/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Other significant ethnic groups include the Creoles (25.2%), Maya (11.2%), and Garifuna (4.0%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Jamaica – Mixed",text:"In 2011, 6.06% identified as Mixed and 92.11% Black. STATIN notes that answers reflect self-perception rather than ancestry.",figure:"Mixed 6.06%",year:"2011",disputed:true,note:"Only one authoritative source retrieved (STATIN). World Factbook archive pages now redirect. Needs second source before publication.",src:[SRCQ("STATIN: Population and Housing Census 2011 Jamaica, General Report Vol. 1","https://census.statinja.gov.jm/wp-content/themes/futurio-child/Census2011Reports/Population%20and%20Housing%20Census%202011%20Jamaica%20General%20Report%20Vol%201.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Table (xii) Percentage Distribution of the Population by Ethnic Origin: 2001 and 2011 ... Black 92.11 91.61 ... Mixed 6.06 6.21 ... For Jamaica the responses from the census are considered to reflect more of people’s perception of themselves rather than ascription to a particular group on the basis of physical appearance.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Bahamas – Black and White",text:"In 2010, about 2% (7,394 people) identified as mixed Black and White; 91% Black and 5% White.",figure:"Black and White 2.1%",year:"2010",disputed:false,note:"Second source is a reproduction of World Factbook data; treat as weak.",src:[SRCQ("Department of Statistics, The Bahamas: 2010 Census of Population and Housing, first release (hosted by CARICOM)","https://statistics.caricom.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Bahamas_2010_Census_First_release.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","91% of the population identified themselves as being black, five percent (5%) white and two percent (2%) of a mixed race (black and white) ... BLACK AND WHITE 7,394"),SRCQ("Global Geography (reproduces World Factbook-style entries): Bahamas People & Society","https://global-geography.org/af/Geography/America/Bahamas%2C_The/People_Society","Supports the Black communities around the world page","black 90.6%, white 4.7%, black and white 2.1%, other 1.9%, unspecified 0.7% (2010 est.)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"United States – Two or More Races; Black alone or in combination",text:"In 2020, 10.2% (33.8 million) reported Two or More Races, up from 2.9% (9 million) in 2010, partly due to improved question design and coding. Black or African American alone or in combination was 46.9 million (14.2%). 'Two or More Races' covers every combination, not only those with African ancestry.",figure:"Two or more races 10.2%; Black alone or in combination 14.2%",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("US Census Bureau: Improved Race and Ethnicity Measures Reveal U.S. Population Is Much More Multiracial (2021)","https://census.gov/library/stories/2021/08/improved-race-ethnicity-measures-reveal-united-states-population-much-more-multiracial.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","increasing from 2.9% of the population (9 million people) in 2010 to 10.2% of the population (33.8 million people) in 2020 ... 46.9 million people (14.2% of the total population) in 2020"),SRCQ("US Department of Commerce blog: 2020 Census statistics highlight local population changes and nation's racial and ethnic diversity","https://www.commerce.gov/news/blog/2021/08/2020-census-statistics-highlight-local-population-changes-and-nations-racial-and","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The Multiracial population was measured at 9 million people in 2010 and is now 33.8 million people in 2020, a 276% increase. ... surpassing the Black or African American population (46.9 million) as the second-largest race alone or in combination group."),SRCQ("Journal of Blacks in Higher Education: 2020 Census data on race and ethnicity","https://jbhe.com/2021/08/2020-census-data-on-race-and-ethnicity-of-the-population-of-the-united-states","Supports the Black communities around the world page","was measured at 9 million people in 2010 and is now 33.8 million people in 2020")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Canada – Black (incl. Black and White)",text:"In 2021, 1,547,870 people (4.3%) were in the Black population group, which includes people reporting 'Black' and 'White'; 10.2% of the Black group gave multiple responses. Canada has no single 'mixed' category.",figure:"Black 4.3%; 10.2% of these multiple responses",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Statistics Canada: Black History Month 2025… By the numbers: Demography","https://www.statcan.gc.ca/o1/en/app/7728-black-history-month-2025-numbers-demography","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2021, the Black populations reached 1,547,870 people, representing 4.3% of the total population in Canada. ... The “Black” category includes people who reported “Black” and “Black” and “White”. ... In the 2021 Census, 89.8% of the category “Black” was single-response answers and 10.2% was multiple-response answers"),SRCQ("Department of Justice Canada: Demographics and Diversity of Black Populations","https://justice.gc.ca/eng/rp-pr/jr/obpccjsce-spnsjpcce/populations.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2021 (the year of the most recent national Census), Black people numbered over 1.5 million individuals ... Over 25 percent of Black respondents to the 2021 Census reported more than one ethnic or cultural origin")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"United Kingdom (England and Wales) – Mixed or Multiple",text:"In 2021, 2.9% (1,717,977) of England and Wales identified as Mixed or Multiple: White and Black Caribbean 0.9% (513,040), White and Black African 0.4% (249,593), White and Asian 0.8%, Other Mixed 0.8%. England 3.0%, Wales 1.6%. Scotland and Northern Ireland run separate censuses.",figure:"Mixed 2.9%; mixed with Black ancestry 1.3%",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"1.3% is the sum of the two White-and-Black categories; some Other Mixed people also have African ancestry.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office Equality Hub): Population of England and Wales","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","[Table as returned:] Mixed overall 2.9% 1,717,977; Mixed White/Black Caribbean 0.9% 513,040; Mixed White/Black African 0.4% 249,593; Mixed White/Asian 0.8% 488,228; Mixed other 0.8% 467,116"),SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Black communities around the world page","[England] “Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups” was 3.0% (1.7 million) ... [Wales] “Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups” was 1.6% (49,000)"),SRCQ("ONS Census 2021 dictionary: Ethnic group","https://www.ons.gov.uk/census/census2021dictionary/variablesbytopic/ethnicgroupnationalidentitylanguageandreligionvariablescensus2021/ethnicgroup","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups: White and Asian ... White and Black African ... White and Black Caribbean ... Other Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups"),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion in Wales (Census 2021)","https://www.gov.wales/ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion-wales-census-2021-html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","49,000 usual residents in Wales (1.6%) identified with the high-level ethnic group category ... A smaller proportion of people in Wales in 2021 identified with \"Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups\" than in England (3.0%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"France – no ethnic statistics",text:"The French census does not ask about race or ethnicity, and public statistics generally do not collect ethnic affiliation; a 2007 Conseil constitutionnel decision (2007-557 DC) held that studies may use objective data but may not rest on ethnic origin or race. No share can be given.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Census does not ask.",src:[SRCQ("INSEE, Colloque 70 ans (presentation on 'statistiques ethniques')","https://www.insee.fr/fr/statistiques/fichier/2387407/Colloque-70-ans-Insee-Jugnot-presentation.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En France, la statistique publique ne collecte généralement pas l'appartenance ethnique. [Extractor notes the 15 Nov 2007 Conseil constitutionnel decision n° 2007-557 DC: studies may use objective data but may not 'reposer sur l'origine ethnique ou la race']"),SRCQ("French-American Foundation: The collection of ethnoracial statistics","https://frenchamerican.org/wp-content/uploads/the-collection-of-ethnoracial-statistics.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","race and ethnicity are absent from the census form ... the ban on collecting data revealing a person's race or ethnicity admits of several exceptions.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Portugal – grupo de origem ou pertença mista (survey)",text:"The 2021 census did not ask. INE's 2023 survey of residents aged 18–74 found 262,300 identifying with a mixed group, 169,200 Black and 6.4 million White (base about 7.6 million), i.e. roughly 3.4% mixed. The mixed group is not limited to African ancestry.",figure:"mixed about 3.4% (18–74, survey)",year:"2023",disputed:true,note:"3.4% computed by us from INE counts; Observador gives 263.2k vs INE 262.3k.",src:[SRCQ("INE Portugal: Inquérito às Condições de Vida, Origens e Trajetórias da População Residente 2023 (summary, hosted by DGE)","https://cidadania.dge.mec.pt/sites/default/files/pdfs/inquerito-ine-2023.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","6,4 milhões com o grupo étnico branco; 169,2 mil com o grupo negro; ... 262,3 mil com o grupo de origem ou pertença mista. [base: 7.6 million residents aged 18–74; 87.2% identified with one group only]"),SRCQ("Observador: INE – 6,4 milhões de portugueses identificam-se como brancos","https://observador.pt/2023/12/22/ine-64-milhoes-de-portugueses-identificam-se-como-brancos-e-mais-de-meio-milhao-como-de-outras-etnias/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","havendo mais de meio milhão que se dizem pertencentes ao grupo misto (263,2 mil), negros (169,2 mil)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Netherlands – origin, not race",text:"Statistics Netherlands (CBS) classifies people by country of birth of the person and parents (e.g. Surinamese, Antillean origin), not by race or mixed heritage. No share of mixed heritage can be given.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Census/register does not ask.",src:[SRCQ("CBS (Statistics Netherlands): The new classification by origin in brief (2022)","https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/statistische-trends/2022/new-classification-of-population-by-origin/2-the-new-classification-by-origin-in-brief","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In classification by origin, the key element from now on will be whether a person was born in the Netherlands or abroad, and only secondarily whether a person’s parents were born here or elsewhere."),SRCQ("DutchNews.nl: Dutch statistics office drops 'Western/non Western' migrant categories","https://dutchnews.nl/2022/02/dutch-statistics-office-drops-western-non-western-migrant-categories","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Ethnic origins will be measured in terms of country names rather than being divided into 'Western' or 'non-Western'")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"genetics",h:"What genetic studies say about ancestry",intro:"Genetic studies estimate the proportions of a person's ancestry that trace to broad regions. Ancestry is not race: the categories people choose depend on history, language and social context, and studies using different samples and methods give different averages.",points:[
  {h:"African Americans: average about three-quarters African ancestry",text:"A 23andMe-based study of 5,269 self-described African Americans found average genome-wide ancestry of 73.2% African, 24.0% European and 0.8% Native American. A more representative 2016 study of 3,726 African Americans estimated 82.1% African, 16.7% European and 1.2% American ancestry.",figure:"73.2% African / 24.0% European (2015); 82.1% / 16.7% (2016)",year:"2015; 2016",disputed:true,note:"Studies differ; the 2015 authors note their estimate is lower than earlier ones (77–93%).",src:[SRCQ("American Journal of Human Genetics 96 (2015): The genetic ancestry of African Americans, Latinos, and European Americans across the United States","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajhg.2014.11.010","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Genome-wide ancestry estimates of African Americans show average proportions of 73.2% African, 24.0% European, and 0.8% Native American ancestry (Table 1). ... We studied the genetic ancestry of 5,269 self-described African Americans ... who are 23andMe customers"),SRCQ("PLoS Genetics 12(5) (2016): The Great Migration and African-American Genomic Diversity","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1006059","Supports the Black communities around the world page","An estimated 82.1% of ancestors to African-Americans lived in Africa prior to the advent of transatlantic travel, 16.7% in Europe, and 1.2% in the Americas, with increased African ancestry in the southern United States compared to the North and West.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Brazil: colour is a poor guide to ancestry",text:"Across four regions, European ancestry was predominant on average (60.6% in the Northeast to 77.7% in the South). Self-declared colour categories were unreliable indicators of genomic ancestry; the authors link this partly to European immigration intended to 'whiten' Brazil.",figure:"European ancestry 60.6–77.7% by region",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("PLoS ONE 6(2) (2011): The genomic ancestry of individuals from different geographical regions of Brazil is more uniform than expected","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0017063","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In all regions studied, the European ancestry was predominant, with proportions ranging from 60.6% in the Northeast to 77.7% in the South. We propose that the immigration of six million Europeans to Brazil in the 19th and 20th centuries--a phenomenon described and intended as the \"whitening of Brazil\"--is in large part responsible"),SRCQ("Pesquisa FAPESP: Many colors, one people","https://revistapesquisa.fapesp.br/en/many-colors-one-people/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","there is no necessarily direct correspondence between skin color and ancestry ... the self-declared racial categories are unreliable indicators of genomic ancestry in Brazil")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cabo Verde: highly admixed, sex-biased",text:"845 people from six islands showed extensive African–European admixture (mean West African ancestry 0.57), varying by island; X- and Y-chromosome markers show mainly European male and African female ancestors.",figure:"mean West African ancestry 57%",year:"2012",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("PLoS ONE 7(11) (2012): The admixture structure and genetic variation of the archipelago of Cape Verde","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0051103","Supports the Black communities around the world page","We find extensive genetic admixture between European and African ancestral populations (mean West African ancestry = 0.57, sd = 0.08), with individual African ancestry proportions varying considerably among the islands. African ancestry proportions calculated with X and Y-chromosome markers confirm that the pattern of admixture has been sex-biased."),SRCQ("Britannica: Cabo Verde summary","https://www.Britannica.Com/summary/Cabo-Verde","Supports the Black communities around the world page","More than two-thirds of its population are of mixed African and European origin (known as mestiço or Crioulo)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Caribbean: high and variable African ancestry",text:"Caribbean island populations have higher African ancestry than mainland Latin American populations. Cubans ranged from 2% to 78% West African ancestry; Haitians averaged 84%; Dominicans cluster between African and European components.",figure:"Cuba 2–78% West African; Haiti 84%",year:"2013",disputed:false,note:"Second independent source for these specific figures not cited here.",src:[SRCQ("PLoS Genetics 9(11) (2013): Reconstructing the population genetic history of the Caribbean","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1003925","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Cubans, which show extreme variation in ancestry proportions ranging from 2% to 78% West African ancestry, and (3) Haitians, which showed the largest average proportion of West African ancestry (84%). ... Dominicans principally cluster between the African and European components.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"South African Coloured: among the most admixed populations in the world",text:"Studies find four or more major ancestral sources: Khoe-San (32–43%), Bantu-speaking African (20–36%), European (21–28%) and Asian (9–11%) in one Western Cape sample, with large regional variation; Khoe-San ancestry predominates in 14 of 22 locations sampled.",figure:"Khoesan 32–43%; Bantu 20–36%; European 21–28%; Asian 9–11%",year:"2010; 2025",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Human Genetics 128 (2010): Genome-wide analysis of the structure of the South African Coloured Population in the Western Cape","https://doi.org/10.1007/s00439-010-0836-1","Supports the Black communities around the world page","the major ancestral components of this population are predominantly Khoesan (32-43%), Bantu-speaking Africans (20-36%), European (21-28%) and a smaller Asian contribution (9-11%), depending on the model used."),SRCQ("BMC Biology (2025): Wide-scale geographical analysis of genetic ancestry in the South African Coloured population","https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-025-02317-5","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The South African Coloured (SAC) population is among the most admixed populations in the world ... Khoe-San ancestry predominates in 14 locations ... sex-biased admixture ratios show male-biased admixture from East Africans and Europeans, and female-biased admixture from Khoe-San populations"),SRCQ("Human Molecular Genetics 19(3) (2010): Genetic structure of a unique admixed population: implications for medical research","https://doi.org/10.1093/hmg/ddp505","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Statistics South Africa continues to classify people by population group, in order to monitor progress in moving away from the apartheid-based discrimination of the past. However, membership of a population group is now based on self-perception and self-classification, not on a legal definition. [quoting the 2001 census]."),SRCQ("Discover Magazine (blog): To be Coloured in South Africa means being all of the above","https://www.discovermagazine.com/health/to-be-coloured-in-south-africa-means-being-all-of-the-above","Supports the Black communities around the world page","We show that there is substantial genetic contribution from at least four distinct population groups [quoting a 2009 Human Molecular Genetics paper]")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ancestry is not race",text:"Researchers stress that self-identity and genetic ancestry overlap but are not the same; the 2015 US study found self-identification as African American follows a probability curve with African ancestry rather than a 'one-drop' rule.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("American Journal of Human Genetics 96 (2015): The genetic ancestry of African Americans, Latinos, and European Americans across the United States","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajhg.2014.11.010","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Genome-wide ancestry estimates of African Americans show average proportions of 73.2% African, 24.0% European, and 0.8% Native American ancestry (Table 1). ... We studied the genetic ancestry of 5,269 self-described African Americans ... who are 23andMe customers"),SRCQ("Pesquisa FAPESP: Many colors, one people","https://revistapesquisa.fapesp.br/en/many-colors-one-people/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","there is no necessarily direct correspondence between skin color and ancestry ... the self-declared racial categories are unreliable indicators of genomic ancestry in Brazil")],kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"word",h:"The word 'miscegenation'",intro:"The hub explains this word because people meet it in historical documents, but does not use it in its own voice.",points:[
  {h:"Coined in a political hoax, 1863",text:"'Miscegenation' (Latin miscere, to mix, + genus, race) was coined in an anonymous 72-page pamphlet sold in New York shortly before Christmas 1863 (title page dated 1864). It was written by Democratic journalists on the New York World to make voters believe that Republicans and abolitionists favoured 'race mixing'.",figure:"1863",year:"1863",disputed:true,note:"Publication straddles December 1863 / early 1864; Merriam-Webster gives first use 1863.",src:[SRCQ("Museum of Hoaxes: The Miscegenation Hoax","https://hoaxes.org/archive/permalink/the_miscegenation_hoax","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Shortly before Christmas, 1863, a 72-page pamphlet appeared for sale on newsstands in New York City. ... 'Miscegenation' was a word that the author of the pamphlet had coined."),SRCQ("Journal of the Civil War Era (Muster blog, 2021): Fake News, Dirty Tricks, and Civil War Politics","https://www.journalofthecivilwarera.org/2021/10/fake-news-dirty-tricks-and-civil-war-politics","Supports the Black communities around the world page","two journalists for the Democratic New York World ... the clever journalists – who even coined the word “miscegenation” for posterity ... Miscegenation constituted pure political trickery, engineered around an assumption about northern voters."),SRCQ("Merriam-Webster: miscegenation","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/miscegenation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","irregular from Latin miscēre to mix + genus race ... First known use: 1863 ... The word miscegenation is associated especially with historical laws against interracial marriage. In the United States, such laws were declared unconstitutional in 1967.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"US anti-'miscegenation' laws ended in 1967",text:"On 12 June 1967 the US Supreme Court in Loving v. Virginia unanimously held bans on interracial marriage unconstitutional, striking down laws still in force in 16 states. Alabama removed its unenforceable ban from its constitution only in 2000.",figure:"16 states",year:"1967",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("HISTORY.com: Loving v. Virginia","https://www.history.com/articles/loving-v-virginia","Supports the Black communities around the world page","The Supreme Court announced its ruling in Loving v. Virginia on June 12, 1967. ... struck down existing laws against interracial marriage in 16 U.S. states including Virginia"),SRCQ("Equal Justice Initiative: Loving v. Virginia","https://eji.org/news/history-racial-injustice-loving-v-virginia/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","in 1967, the U.S. Supreme Court held that anti-miscegenation laws [violated the Fourteenth Amendment] ... struck down laws in 16 Southern states ... the last state to remove its ban — in 2000"),SRCQ("Britannica: miscegenation","https://www.Britannica.Com/topic/miscegenation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","many states had laws against interracial marriage until the Supreme Court declared them unconstitutional in 1967 ... In South Africa the official policy of apartheid for many years included legal prohibitions on miscegenation."),SRCQ("Merriam-Webster: miscegenation","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/miscegenation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","irregular from Latin miscēre to mix + genus race ... First known use: 1863 ... The word miscegenation is associated especially with historical laws against interracial marriage. In the United States, such laws were declared unconstitutional in 1967.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Apartheid South Africa: Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act 1949",text:"One of the first apartheid laws, the 1949 Act banned marriages between White people and people of other groups; it was repealed by the Immorality and Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Amendment Act 1985.",figure:"1949–1985",year:"1949; 1985",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("South African History Online: 8 July 1949 – The Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act commences","https://sahistory.org.za/node/87611","Supports the Black communities around the world page","prohibited marriage or a sexual relationship between White people and people of other race groups in South Africa ... It was eventually repealed in 1985 by the Immorality and Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Amendment Act"),SRCQ("ESAT (Stellenbosch University): Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act","https://esat.sun.ac.za/index.php/Prohibition_of_Mixed_Marriages_Act","Supports the Black communities around the world page","one of the first pieces of apartheid legislation enacted after the National Party came to power ... It also made it a criminal offense for a marriage officer to perform an interracial marriage ceremony. ... The act was repealed in 1985 by the Immorality and Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Amendment Act"),SRCQ("Britannica: miscegenation","https://www.Britannica.Com/topic/miscegenation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","many states had laws against interracial marriage until the Supreme Court declared them unconstitutional in 1967 ... In South Africa the official policy of apartheid for many years included legal prohibitions on miscegenation.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Why the hub does not use it",text:"The word was invented to provoke fear of 'race mixing' and became the legal language of marriage bans in the US and South Africa; dictionaries associate it with those laws. It assumes separate biological races, which genetics does not support. The hub therefore says 'mixed heritage' or 'mixed ancestry', and quotes 'miscegenation' only in historical context.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Merriam-Webster: miscegenation","https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/miscegenation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","irregular from Latin miscēre to mix + genus race ... First known use: 1863 ... The word miscegenation is associated especially with historical laws against interracial marriage. In the United States, such laws were declared unconstitutional in 1967."),SRCQ("Journal of the Civil War Era (Muster blog, 2021): Fake News, Dirty Tricks, and Civil War Politics","https://www.journalofthecivilwarera.org/2021/10/fake-news-dirty-tricks-and-civil-war-politics","Supports the Black communities around the world page","two journalists for the Democratic New York World ... the clever journalists – who even coined the word “miscegenation” for posterity ... Miscegenation constituted pure political trickery, engineered around an assumption about northern voters."),SRCQ("Britannica: miscegenation","https://www.Britannica.Com/topic/miscegenation","Supports the Black communities around the world page","many states had laws against interracial marriage until the Supreme Court declared them unconstitutional in 1967 ... In South Africa the official policy of apartheid for many years included legal prohibitions on miscegenation.")],kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"caveats",h:"Caveats: reading these numbers",intro:"Why the shares should not be ranked as if they measured the same thing.",points:[
  {h:"Self-identification is not ancestry",text:"Census answers record how people see themselves (or how the question was asked), not genetic ancestry. STATIN says Jamaica's results reflect self-perception; Stats SA says population group is self-classified; Brazilian studies show colour categories poorly predict ancestry.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("STATIN: Population and Housing Census 2011 Jamaica, General Report Vol. 1","https://census.statinja.gov.jm/wp-content/themes/futurio-child/Census2011Reports/Population%20and%20Housing%20Census%202011%20Jamaica%20General%20Report%20Vol%201.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Table (xii) Percentage Distribution of the Population by Ethnic Origin: 2001 and 2011 ... Black 92.11 91.61 ... Mixed 6.06 6.21 ... For Jamaica the responses from the census are considered to reflect more of people’s perception of themselves rather than ascription to a particular group on the basis of physical appearance."),SRCQ("Human Molecular Genetics 19(3) (2010): Genetic structure of a unique admixed population: implications for medical research","https://doi.org/10.1093/hmg/ddp505","Supports the Black communities around the world page","Statistics South Africa continues to classify people by population group, in order to monitor progress in moving away from the apartheid-based discrimination of the past. However, membership of a population group is now based on self-perception and self-classification, not on a legal definition. [quoting the 2001 census]."),SRCQ("Pesquisa FAPESP: Many colors, one people","https://revistapesquisa.fapesp.br/en/many-colors-one-people/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","there is no necessarily direct correspondence between skin color and ancestry ... the self-declared racial categories are unreliable indicators of genomic ancestry in Brazil")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Categories differ, so shares are not comparable",text:"Some censuses offer 'mixed' categories (UK, Trinidad, Guyana), some colour terms (Brazil, Cuba, Venezuela), some multi-tick race (USA, Canada), some Afro-descendant identity (Colombia, Panama), and some ask nothing (France, the Netherlands, Mauritius, Namibia). Question changes alone can transform results: Puerto Rico's 'two or more races' rose from 3.3% to 49.8% between 2010 and 2020.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Center for Puerto Rican Studies (Hunter College, CUNY): Puerto Rico's 2020 Race/Ethnicity Decennial Analysis","https://centropr.hunter.cuny.edu/app/uploads/2021/10/022223-Puerto-Ricos-2020-Race_Ethnicity-Decennial-Analysis.pdf","Supports the Black communities around the world page","from 3.3% of the total population in 2010 to 49.8% in 2020 ... dropped by 50.4% in 2020 from 12.4% from the total population in 2010 to 7.0% in 2020"),SRCQ("US Census Bureau: Improved Race and Ethnicity Measures Reveal U.S. Population Is Much More Multiracial (2021)","https://census.gov/library/stories/2021/08/improved-race-ethnicity-measures-reveal-united-states-population-much-more-multiracial.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","increasing from 2.9% of the population (9 million people) in 2010 to 10.2% of the population (33.8 million people) in 2020 ... 46.9 million people (14.2% of the total population) in 2020")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Whitening and colourism shape answers",text:"Brazil's branqueamento ideology promoted subsidised European immigration and mixing to 'whiten' the population; research finds interviewers and respondents shifted lighter-skinned people towards 'white'. In the Dominican Republic 'indio' became a preferred label distancing people from Blackness.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("PLoS ONE 6(2) (2011): The genomic ancestry of individuals from different geographical regions of Brazil is more uniform than expected","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0017063","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In all regions studied, the European ancestry was predominant, with proportions ranging from 60.6% in the Northeast to 77.7% in the South. We propose that the immigration of six million Europeans to Brazil in the 19th and 20th centuries--a phenomenon described and intended as the \"whitening of Brazil\"--is in large part responsible"),SRCQ("Revista de Antropologia 67 (2024): Race and color in contemporary Brazil, political opportunism and historical trends","https://new.scielo.br/j/ra/a/6btsjxZXTRDhmYDDfmXZ69n/?lang=en","Supports the Black communities around the world page","while \"pardos\" were mixed-race individuals of African descent. ... Individuals with indigenous or black ancestry tended to identify as brown, even if dark-skinned"),SRCQ("El Día: Solo el 8 % de los dominicanos se identifica como negro","https://eldia.com.do/solo-el-8-de-los-dominicanos-se-identifica-como-negro/","Supports the Black communities around the world page","En cambio, el 45 % de los dominicanos se considera indio ... Solo el 8 % de los dominicanos se define como negro [article attributes 'indio' to Trujillo-era promotion and notes 'los pueblos nativos de la isla fueron exterminados en el siglo XVI']")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Counter-trend: more people now claim Black or mixed identity",text:"Brazil's pardo and preto shares rose between 2010 and 2022 and Panama's Afro-descendant share rose from 9.2% to 31.7%, partly reflecting campaigns for self-recognition rather than demographic change.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("IBGE News Agency: 2022 Census: self-reported brown population is the majority in Brazil for the first time","https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-news/2184-news-agency/news/38726-2022-census-self-reported-brown-population-is-the-majority-in-brazil-for-the-first-time","Supports the Black communities around the world page","In 2022, about 92.1 million persons reported being brown, which corresponds to 45.3% of the country’s population. ... Other 20.6 million persons reported being black (10.2%)"),SRCQ("TVN Noticias (Panamá): resultados del Censo","https://www.tvn-2.com/nacionales/educacion-analfabetismo-acceso-tecnologia-resultados_1_2065149.html","Supports the Black communities around the world page","El porcentaje de la población que se autoreconoció como afrodescendiente ... tuvo un considerable aumento de 9.2% en 2010 a 31.7% en 2023.")],kind:"counter-argument"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black and Black-Mixed communities around the world name themselves in their own ways, from Afro-Brazilian and Raizal to Afropean and Siddi; use their names.",refs:["gl-brazil-more-than-half-of-brazilians-identify-as", "gl-andes-pacific-the-raizal-are-a-distinct-afro-caribbean"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Latin America is home to the largest African-descended populations outside Africa, yet they are rarely taught in the UK.",refs:["gl-latam-overview-about-1-in-5-to-1-in-4-latin-americans-i"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"When colleagues have roots in Europe, Latin America or Asia as well as Africa and the Caribbean, recognise those journeys too.",refs:["gl-canada-1-5-million-black-canadians-in-the-2021-", "gl-latam-overview-about-1-in-5-to-1-in-4-latin-americans-i"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Several countries collect no ethnic data, and others count differently; compare with care and say how each country counts.",refs:["gl-france-france-does-not-collect-ethnic-statistic", "gl-andes-pacific-colombia-s-census-count-of-black-people-"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Census categories differ: shares of 'Black', 'brown' or 'Mixed' people are not comparable between countries.",refs:["gl-brazil-more-than-half-of-brazilians-identify-as", "gl-andes-pacific-ecuador-s-afro-ecuadorian-count-fell-sha"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"About 133 to 134 million Latin Americans are of African descent.",refs:["gl-latam-overview-about-1-in-5-to-1-in-4-latin-americans-i"]},{t:"In Brazil's 2022 census, 55.5% identified as Black or brown.",refs:["gl-brazil-more-than-half-of-brazilians-identify-as"]},{t:"France collects no ethnic statistics, so its Black population cannot be counted officially.",refs:["gl-france-france-does-not-collect-ethnic-statistic"]},{t:"Canada counted 1,547,870 Black Canadians in 2021.",refs:["gl-canada-1-5-million-black-canadians-in-the-2021-"]}]}]};
LP["honour"]={name:"Honouring our communities",pfx:"ho",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"places",h:"Plaques, statues, monuments and renamings",intro:"Marking places is one of the most visible ways the UK honours Black, African, Caribbean and Mixed communities. Several of these schemes take public nominations, so anyone can start the process.",points:[
  {h:"English Heritage blue plaques: under 4% honour Black and Asian figures",text:"In 2016 English Heritage said fewer than 4% of London's 900-plus blue plaques honoured Black and Asian figures. It set up a working group to propose Black and Asian nominees, held to the same criteria as public nominations. On the same day it unveiled a plaque to the footballer Laurie Cunningham at his childhood home in Stroud Green, north London.",figure:"under 4% of 900+ London plaques",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"Both pages come from English Heritage, so they count as one publisher. English Heritage is a national heritage body (authoritative). The point rests on one source. The share may have changed since 2016.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity and blue plaques – English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/about-us/search-news/diversity-blue-plaques/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","less than 4% of them are dedicated to Black and Asian figures from history"),SRCQ("Blue plaque for Laurie Cunningham – English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/about/search-news/blue-plaque-laurie-cunningham-diversity/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Currently less than 4% of the 900 plus Blue Plaques across the capital are dedicated to Black and Asian figures")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Anyone can nominate a London blue plaque",text:"English Heritage's London scheme is driven mainly by public suggestions. The person must have died at least 20 years ago, and a building linked to them must survive in Greater London. You email the completed form to A shortlisted plaque usually takes two to three years to go up.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Both pages are English Heritage's (one publisher, authoritative), so the point rests on one source. How to apply: the Propose a plaque page gives the form and email address.",src:[SRCQ("Propose a plaque – English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/propose-a-plaque","Supports the Honouring our communities page","At least 20 years must have passed since a candidate's death."),SRCQ("London's Black history – Blue plaque stories, English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/blue-plaque-stories/londons-black-history/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Public nominations are at the heart of the scheme, so if you know of someone eligible, nominate them")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Nubian Jak Community Trust: Black-led plaques across the UK",text:"Since 2006 the Nubian Jak Community Trust has installed more than 100 blue and black plaques and three memorials across the UK. Its plaques include a bronze plaque to Mary Prince on Senate House, University of London, in Camden, and the first commemorative plaque in Whitehall, on the Foreign Office.",figure:"100+ plaques, 3 memorials",year:"2006",disputed:false,note:"The Camden Council planning record (local government) is the authoritative source. The Trust's own page is the source for the count. Neither page sets out how to nominate.",src:[SRCQ("Plaques and memorials – Nubian Jak Community Trust","https://nubianjak.org/our-plaques","Supports the Honouring our communities page","install over 100 blue plaques and black plaques and three memorials across the UK since 2006"),SRCQ("Camden Council planning document","https://camdocs.camden.gov.uk/CMWebDrawer/Record/2801774/file/document","Supports the Honouring our communities page","the Mary Prince bronze plaque on Senate House, University of London, Russell Square, Camden (grade 2 building)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"National Windrush Monument at Waterloo station (2022)",text:"The National Windrush Monument by the Jamaican sculptor Basil Watson was unveiled at London Waterloo station on Windrush Day, 22 June 2022. It shows a man, woman and child in their Sunday best standing on suitcases. GOV.UK says it pays tribute to the dreams, ambition and resilience of the Windrush pioneers.",figure:"£1 million",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The GOV.UK page confirms the commission and purpose but not the date or location. The date, location and description come from ITV and Londonist, and the £1 million figure from Londonist alone.",src:[SRCQ("The National Windrush Monument – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-national-windrush-monument","Supports the Honouring our communities page","pays tribute to the dreams, ambition and resilience of the Windrush pioneers"),SRCQ("Windrush generation makes our culture richer, says William as monument unveiled – ITV News","https://www.itv.com/news/london/2022-06-22/windrush-generation-makes-our-culture-richer-says-william-as-monument-unveiled","Supports the Honouring our communities page","a man, woman and child in their Sunday best standing on top of suitcases"),SRCQ("National Windrush Monument unveiled at Waterloo station – Londonist","https://londonist.com/london/art-and-photography/national-windrush-monument-unveiled-waterloo-station-basil-watson","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The £1 million funding has come from the government's Department for Levelling Up")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mary Seacole statue at St Thomas' Hospital (2016)",text:"On 30 June 2016 a statue of the Jamaican-born nurse Mary Seacole by Martin Jennings was unveiled in the gardens of St Thomas' Hospital, London. It is believed to be the first statue in the UK of a named Black woman. A public appeal raised over £500,000, and £240,000 of LIBOR fines paid for installation.",figure:"£500,000+ raised",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"The NHS trust's own news release is a public-body source. The Mary Seacole Trust maintains the statue and runs education and diversity-in-leadership programmes.",src:[SRCQ("Mary Seacole statue unveiled – Guy's and St Thomas' NHS Foundation Trust","https://guysandstthomas.nhs.uk/news/mary-seacole-statue-unveiled","Supports the Honouring our communities page","is believed to be the first statue in the UK dedicated to a named black woman"),SRCQ("About – The Mary Seacole Trust","https://maryseacoletrust.org.uk/about","Supports the Honouring our communities page","donations from thousands of individual supporters")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Colston Hall renamed Bristol Beacon (2020)",text:"On 23 September 2020 Bristol Music Trust renamed its concert hall Bristol Beacon. The old name, Colston Hall, had acted as a memorial to Edward Colston, a merchant in the trade in enslaved Africans Edward Colston. The new name followed a public consultation with over 4,000 people.",figure:"4,000+ people consulted",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The venue's own statement is a primary source but is not on the brief's authoritative list. The Voice is an independent Black British newspaper. The consultation is a model others can copy.",src:[SRCQ("Our new name – Bristol Beacon","https://bristolbeacon.org/about-us/our-new-name/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Our former name, Colston Hall, acted as a memorial to the slave trader Edward Colston"),SRCQ("Bristol's Colston Hall renamed – The Voice","https://www.voice-online.co.uk/news/uk-news/2020/09/23/bristols-colston-hall-renamed/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The name was chosen following a public consultation, which followed the decision to change the name three-years ago.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Colston statue: from plinth to museum display",text:"The statue of Edward Colston was pulled down during a Black Lives Matter protest in Bristol in June 2020, and the council recovered it from the harbour. The We Are Bristol History Commission then ran a public survey that drew almost 14,000 responses. Four in five respondents wanted the statue shown in a city museum. A temporary display at M Shed let people see the statue and learn its history.",figure:"almost 14,000 responses; ~80% for museum",year:"2020–2022",disputed:true,note:"The removal of the statue was contested and led to a criminal trial, which is not covered here because it was not sourced. The point is limited to the public consultation process.",src:[SRCQ("The Colston Statue: What Next? 'We are Bristol' History Commission – Full Report (UWE repository)","https://uwe-repository.worktribe.com/output/9004543","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Almost 14,000 people filled out the survey."),SRCQ("Colston statue should be displayed in a museum, say Bristol residents – Museums Association","https://www.museumsassociation.org/museums-journal/news/2022/02/colston-statue-should-be-displayed-in-a-museum-say-bristol-residents/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Four out of five respondents told the survey they thought the statue"),SRCQ("Edward Colston statue on display at Bristol museum – Artnet News","https://news.artnet.com/art-world/edward-colston-statue-bristol-museum-1976541","Supports the Honouring our communities page","fished out by the local council a few days later")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"NHS trust plaques honour the Windrush generation",text:"In October 2024 University Hospitals of Derby and Burton unveiled new plaques at all its sites honouring the Windrush generation and their descendants. It organised the unveilings with its EMBRACE Staff Network, starting at Florence Nightingale Community Hospitals on 1 October.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"This point rests on one source, the NHS trust's own news release (a public body).",src:[SRCQ("UHDB honours the Windrush generation with commemorative plaques – UHDB NHS","https://uhdb.nhs.uk/latest-news/uhdb-honours-the-windrush-generation-with-commemorative-plaques-unveiled-across-all-trust-sites-18371","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The unveiling ceremonies have been organised alongside our EMBRACE Staff Network")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"days",h:"Days and months of remembrance",intro:"Fixed dates give communities and institutions a shared calendar for honouring: Black History Month in October, Windrush Day on 22 June, Emancipation Day on 1 August and Slavery Remembrance Day on 23 August.",points:[
  {h:"Black History Month began in London in 1987",text:"Black History Month in the UK began with an event in London on 1 October 1987. It was coordinated by Akyaaba Addai-Sebo, a special projects officer at the Greater London Council. It is now marked every October.",figure:"",year:"1987",disputed:false,note:"The Senedd (Welsh Parliament) is the authoritative source. The two pages spell the name differently: the Senedd uses 'Sebbo' and UCU uses 'Addai-Sebo'.",src:[SRCQ("Black History Month: This October marks a special anniversary – Senedd Cymru","https://senedd.wales/senedd-now/senedd-blog/black-history-month-this-october-marks-a-special-anniversary","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The first ever BHM event was held in London in 1987."),SRCQ("The History of Black History Month – UCU","https://www.ucu.org.uk/article/8966/The-History-of-Black-History-Month","Supports the Honouring our communities page","coordinated the first official Black History Month event on 1 October 1987")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"2026 theme: Honouring Our Communities",text:"The theme of Black History Month 2026 is 'Honouring Our Communities'. Black History Month UK says the theme is about people, and about recognising individuals whose everyday contributions too often go unseen. Organisations from town councils to the Royal College of Nursing have built their October programmes around it.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Lowestoft Town Council (local government) is the authoritative source. None of the sources says who chose the theme.",src:[SRCQ("Black History Month UK","https://www.blackhistorymonth.org.uk/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","This year's theme, 'Honouring Our Communities', is not simply about place or postcode."),SRCQ("Black History Month 2026 – Inclusive Employers","https://www.inclusiveemployers.co.uk/event/black-history-month-2026/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Black History Month 2026’s theme, “Honouring Our Communities,” invites organisations to recognise the individuals"),SRCQ("Black History Month 2026 – Lowestoft Town Council","https://lowestofttowncouncil.gov.uk/black-history-month-2026/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Honouring Our Communities")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Windrush Day, 22 June, and its government grant scheme",text:"22 June is National Windrush Day, marking the arrival of the MV Empire Windrush in 1948. For 2026 the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government offered £500,000 for events in England, with grants of £5,000 to £25,000. Councils, charities and community organisations could apply; individuals could not. Applications closed on 10 February 2026.",figure:"£500,000; £5,000–£25,000 per grant",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The GOV.UK guidance gives the arrival as both 21 June (at Tilbury) and 22 June 1948. Action Together also shows a £10,000 minimum in one panel. The first grant scheme ran in 2019 (GOV.UK National Windrush Monument page). How to take part: watch GOV.UK for the next round, which normally opens in January.",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Day Grant Scheme 2026: guidance for applicants – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026-guidance-for-applicants","Supports the Honouring our communities page","MHCLG is providing a total of £500,000 to fund activities across England."),SRCQ("Windrush Day Grant Scheme 2026 – Action Together","https://www.actiontogether.org.uk/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Grants of between £5,000 and £25,000 are available.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"33 Windrush Day projects funded for 2026",text:"GOV.UK lists 33 projects funded in 2026. They include a British Carnivals tapestry unveiled at Bath Abbey, talking benches in Pymmes Park in Enfield, a Windrush Festival in Coventry with an archive planned at the University of Warwick, and a Windrush Monument unveiling in Wolverhampton.",figure:"33 projects (2026); 100+ groups (Windrush 75, 2023)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The second source is a separate National Lottery funding round (2023, Windrush 75). It shows the same kind of community activity, including carnivals, exhibitions and school workshops.",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Day Grant Scheme 2026: projects to be funded – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026-projects-to-be-funded","Supports the Honouring our communities page","A list of the 33 projects to be funded under the 2026 Windrush Day Grant Scheme."),SRCQ("£1 million National Lottery funding boost for UK communities to commemorate Windrush 75 – The National Lottery Community Fund","https://www.tnlcommunityfund.org.uk/news/press-releases/2023-06-22/1-million-national-lottery-funding-boost-windrush-75","Supports the Honouring our communities page","More than 100 community groups across the UK have received a share of almost £1 million")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Slavery Remembrance Day, 23 August, in Liverpool",text:"Liverpool has marked Slavery Remembrance Day every 23 August since 1999. Liverpool City Council and National Museums Liverpool run it together, with a Walk of Remembrance and a libation ceremony. The date marks the 1791 uprising of enslaved Africans in Saint-Domingue (now Haiti).",figure:"",year:"1999",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are authoritative: a national museum and the city council's news service. How to take part: the walk is public and free, starting at noon on 23 August.",src:[SRCQ("10 years of International Slavery Museum – National Museums Liverpool","https://www.liverpoolmuseums.org.uk/news/press-releases/10-years-of-international-slavery-museum","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Slavery Remembrance Day has taken place annually in Liverpool on 23 August since 1999."),SRCQ("Slavery Remembrance Day: 25 years of commemoration in Liverpool – Liverpool Express (Liverpool City Council)","https://liverpoolexpress.co.uk/slavery-remembrance-day-25-years-of-commemoration-in-liverpool/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The walk will culminate with a libation ceremony at 2.30pm.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The international day behind 23 August",text:"The UN marks 23 August as the International Day for the Remembrance of the Slave Trade and its Abolition. It recalls the uprising that began in Saint-Domingue on the night of 22 to 23 August 1791. It was first marked in Haiti in 1998 and on Gorée Island, Senegal, in 1999.",figure:"",year:"1791 / 1998",disputed:false,note:"The UN source is authoritative.",src:[SRCQ("International Day for the Remembrance of the Slave Trade and its Abolition – United Nations","https://indonesia.un.org/en/242956-international-day-remembrance-slave-trade-and-its-abolition-23-august","Supports the Honouring our communities page","This International Day is intended to inscribe the tragedy of the slave trade in the memory of all peoples."),SRCQ("Slavery Remembrance Day – Liverpool Express","https://liverpoolexpress.co.uk/slavery-remembrance-day-25-years-of-commemoration-in-liverpool/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","an uprising of enslaved Africans on the island of Saint Domingue (modern Haiti) in 1791")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Emancipation Day, 1 August",text:"The Slavery Abolition Act 1833 took effect on 1 August 1834 across the British Empire. It did not bring immediate freedom: enslaved people over the age of six became 'apprentices' until 1838, and the compensation records cover estate owners and investors, not the people freed. Communities mark 1 August as Emancipation Day.",figure:"",year:"1834",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are authoritative: The National Archives and Parks Canada (a national government agency).",src:[SRCQ("1833 Abolition of Slavery Act and compensation claims – The National Archives","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/explore-by-time-period/georgians/1833-abolition-of-slavery-act-and-compensation-claims/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","When the Act came into effect on 1 August 1834, owners had to complete forms"),SRCQ("Celebrations of Emancipation Day – Parks Canada","https://www.parks.canada.ca/culture/designation/evenement-event/celebrations-emancipation","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Emancipation Day celebrations began on 1 August 1834,")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Afrikan Emancipation Day march in Brixton",text:"Every 1 August, campaigners hold the Afrikan Emancipation Day Reparations March from Windrush Square in Brixton. It ends in a rally calling for reparations and for wider recognition of Britain's role in the slave trade. Lambeth Council has publicly backed its aims.",figure:"",year:"annual",disputed:true,note:"Reparations are a contested political demand. The point records the event, not a position on reparations. Lambeth Council is the authoritative source.",src:[SRCQ("Brixton: March and rally set to go ahead – Lambeth Council","https://love.lambeth.gov.uk/brixton-march-and-rally-set-to-go-ahead","Supports the Honouring our communities page","has traditionally featured a march followed by a rally in Windrush Square and Max Roach Park."),SRCQ("Emancipation Day Reparations March kicks off in London – CARICOM Reparations Commission","https://caricomreparations.org/emancipation-day-reparations-march-kicks-off-london/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Campaigners departed from Windrush square at noon today.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"learning",h:"Museums, archives, schools and scholarships",intro:"Permanent galleries, community archives, the school curriculum and named scholarships keep Black British history in public view all year, not just in October.",points:[
  {h:"International Slavery Museum, Liverpool",text:"The International Slavery Museum opened on 23 August 2007 on the third floor of the Merseyside Maritime Museum at the Albert Dock. It had over four million visitors in its first ten years. It also hosts free events such as the Dorothy Kuya lecture.",figure:"4 million+ visitors",year:"2007",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("10 years of International Slavery Museum – National Museums Liverpool","https://www.liverpoolmuseums.org.uk/news/press-releases/10-years-of-international-slavery-museum","Supports the Honouring our communities page","first opening its doors on 23 August 2007"),SRCQ("Slavery Remembrance Day – Liverpool Express","https://liverpoolexpress.co.uk/slavery-remembrance-day-25-years-of-commemoration-in-liverpool/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","extremely proud of our partnership with National Museums Liverpool and International Slavery Museum")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"London, Sugar & Slavery gallery, London Museum Docklands",text:"The London, Sugar & Slavery gallery at London Museum Docklands opened in 2007 in a former sugar warehouse completed in 1802. The museum calls it one of the only permanent UK displays dedicated to the British trade in enslaved Africans. It is on the third floor, open 10am to 5pm daily, and listed among the free galleries.",figure:"",year:"2007",disputed:false,note:"The Museum of London Docklands is now called London Museum Docklands.",src:[SRCQ("London, Sugar & Slavery – London Museum","https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/docklands/free-galleries/london-sugar-slavery/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","one of the only permanent UK displays dedicated to the British trade in enslaved Africans"),SRCQ("The Wake: London's docks and the transatlantic trafficking of enslaved Africans – Greater London Authority","https://www.london.gov.uk/who-we-are/city-hall-blog/wake-londons-docks-and-transatlantic-trafficking-enslaved-africans","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The London Sugar & Slavery gallery is therefore housed in an old sugar warehouse, completed in 1802.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Cultural Archives, Brixton, funded by National Lottery players",text:"Black Cultural Archives on Windrush Square, Brixton, describes itself as the oldest national repository of Black history and culture in the UK. The National Lottery Heritage Fund awarded it £99,000 in 2020 for 'Securing the Future of Black British History'. A later £250,000 grant, 'Sankofa Resilience', is for clearing backlogs and widening access.",figure:"£99,000 (2020); £250,000",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The two sources describe two different grants. How to take part: the reading room opens Wednesday to Friday, by appointment.",src:[SRCQ("Securing the Future of Black British History – National Lottery Heritage Fund","https://www.heritagefund.org.uk/cy/node/124667","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Securing the Future of Black British History"),SRCQ("Sankofa Resilience: Strengthening Our Archive – Black Cultural Archives","https://blackculturalarchives.org/news/sankofa-resilience-strengthening-our-archive","Supports the Honouring our communities page","the oldest national repository of Black history and culture in the UK")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Heritage Fund grants: open to community groups year-round",text:"The National Lottery Heritage Fund offers grants of £10,000 to £250,000 with no deadline and a decision target of eight weeks. Charities, community groups and councils can apply. The heritage it funds includes 'cultures, traditions and people's memories'. Larger grants of £250,000 to £10 million have set deadlines; the next is 6 August 2026.",figure:"£10,000–£250,000",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Programme details checked in October 2026. Connected Voice is a community support body and covers the £250,000–£10 million strand. One of the fund's investment principles is 'inclusion, access and participation'.",src:[SRCQ("National Lottery Grants for Heritage £10,000 to £250,000 – National Lottery Heritage Fund","https://www.heritagefund.org.uk/node/143931","Supports the Honouring our communities page","There is no deadline so you can apply whenever you are ready."),SRCQ("The National Lottery: Heritage Fund – Connected Voice","https://connectedvoice.org.uk/services/support-and-development/funding/national-lottery-heritage-fund","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Not-for-profit organisations across the UK can now apply.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Portsmouth records its African and Caribbean stories",text:"The Portsmouth Black History Group received £68,645 from the National Lottery Heritage Fund. The money pays for recording the memories of African and Caribbean people who came to Portsmouth, mainly from after the Second World War to the early 1980s. The interviews will go to Portsmouth History Centre, with pop-up exhibitions and work with schools.",figure:"£68,645",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This point rests on one source, a university (authoritative).",src:[SRCQ("University supports project to share Portsmouth's post-WW2 Black stories – University of Portsmouth","https://www.port.ac.uk/news-events-and-blogs/news/university-supports-project-to-share-portsmouths-post-ww2-black-stories","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The £68,645 will be spent on recording the memories")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wales: diverse histories mandatory in the curriculum",text:"Wales has made the stories of Black, Asian and minority ethnic people a mandatory part of learning about Welsh history in the new curriculum. Teaching began in all primary schools and some secondary schools from September 2022. This followed a 2021 working group, chaired by Professor Charlotte Williams OBE, which made 51 recommendations; the minister accepted all of them.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Both pages are Welsh Government's (one publisher), so the point rests on one authoritative source. The official term 'Black, Asian and minority ethnic' is kept because it is in the quotation.",src:[SRCQ("Six things you may not know about new Curriculum for Wales – Welsh Government","https://www.gov.wales/six-things-you-may-not-know-about-new-curriculum-wales","Supports the Honouring our communities page","will be mandatory parts of the new curriculum"),SRCQ("Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic education report welcomed by Minister – Welsh Government","https://gov.wales/black-asian-and-minority-ethnic-education-report-welcomed-minister","Supports the Honouring our communities page","I am delighted to accept all of the report’s recommendations")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Stormzy Scholarship at Cambridge",text:"The Stormzy Scholarship, launched in 2018, pays full tuition fees and a maintenance grant for Black UK students on any undergraduate course at the University of Cambridge. At least 10 awards were available for students starting in 2026. Applications open in August.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"The University of Cambridge is the authoritative source. ITV (2021) says applicants must be a UK student of Black or mixed-race heritage.",src:[SRCQ("Stormzy Scholarship – University of Cambridge","https://www.undergraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/fees-funding/scholarships-bursaries/stormzy-scholarship","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The Stormzy Scholarship supports Black UK students studying any undergraduate course at Cambridge."),SRCQ("Stormzy will fund 30 more scholarships for Black students at Cambridge – ITV News","https://www.itv.com/news/anglia/2021-07-29/stormzy-will-fund-30-more-scholarships-for-black-students-at-cambridge","Supports the Honouring our communities page","began providing scholarships for black students at the university in 2018")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"recognition",h:"Honours, awards and workplace celebrations",intro:"Recognition schemes, from the national honours system to Black-led awards and NHS and council programmes, honour named individuals. Many are open to public nomination.",points:[
  {h:"Anyone can nominate someone for a national honour",text:"Anyone can nominate someone for an honour, such as an MBE, through GOV.UK. Honours go to people who have made life better for others or are outstanding at what they do, and nominees must still be active in that work. You need two supporting letters from people who know the nominee. It can take 12 to 18 months to hear back, and the lists are published at New Year and on the King's official birthday.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are authoritative: GOV.UK and The Gazette (the official public record). How to take part: use the nomination form on GOV.UK.",src:[SRCQ("Honours – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/honours","Supports the Honouring our communities page","They'll usually have made life better for other people or be outstanding at what they do."),SRCQ("Everything you need to know about nominating someone for a UK honour – The Gazette","https://www.thegazette.co.uk/awards-and-accreditation/content/103437","Supports the Honouring our communities page","two supporting letters to back up your nomination")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"NHS Windrush 70 Awards (2018)",text:"To mark 70 years of both Windrush and the NHS in 2018, NHS England ran the Windrush 70 Awards to celebrate the contribution of Black and minority ethnic staff. More than 11,000 patients, members of the public and staff made nominations across 11 categories.",figure:"11,000+ nominations; 11 categories",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"This was a one-off scheme. The Oxford Health page uses the abbreviation ‘BME’, which the hub style avoids; it is not used in our own text.",src:[SRCQ("Black and ethnic minority NHS staff honoured in NHS Windrush 70 awards – NHS England","https://www.england.nhs.uk/2018/06/black-and-ethnic-minority-nhs-staff-honoured-in-nhs-windrush-70-awards/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","More than 11,000 members of the public, patients and staff nominated NHS staff."),SRCQ("Nominations open for Windrush 70 NHS awards – Oxford Health NHS Foundation Trust","https://www.oxfordhealth.nhs.uk/news/nominations-open-for-windrush-70-nhs-awards","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Anyone working in the NHS, patients, service users and the public can nominate.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"MOBO Awards: Music Of Black Origin since 1996",text:"Kanya King founded the MOBO (Music Of Black Origin) Awards in 1996 to celebrate music rooted in Black British culture that mainstream awards had overlooked. MOBO now runs programmes for emerging artists, such as MOBO Unsung. Kanya King CBE died on 3 June 2026.",figure:"",year:"1996",disputed:false,note:"Neither source is on the brief's authoritative list. MOBO's own page is used as the official source for its own scheme, with an independent news source.",src:[SRCQ("MOBO – about Kanya King","https://www.mobo.com/node/3461","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Kanya founded the MOBO Awards in 1996, when a broadcast slot with Carlton Television arose."),SRCQ("Tributes paid to MOBO Awards founder Kanya King – Euronews","https://www.euronews.com/culture/2026/06/05/tributes-paid-to-mobo-awards-founder-and-cultural-pioneer-kanya-king-dead-at-57","Supports the Honouring our communities page","King founded the MOBOs in 1996.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Powerlist: published annually since 2007",text:"Powerful Media has published the Powerlist every year since 2007. It recognises the UK's most influential people of African, African Caribbean and African American heritage, chosen by an independent judging panel. It is sent to schools, sixth-form colleges, universities and corporate affinity groups.",figure:"",year:"2007",disputed:false,note:"Neither source is on the brief's authoritative list. The publisher's own page is used as the official source for its scheme.",src:[SRCQ("Powerful Media","https://powerful-media.com/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","the UK’s most influential people of African, African Caribbean and African American heritage"),SRCQ("Segun Osuntokun named in UK Black Powerlist – Bryan Cave Leighton Paisley","https://www.bclplaw.com/en-US/events-insights-news/segun-osuntokun-named-in-uk-black-powerlist-for-sixth-consecutive-year.html","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Launched in 2007, the Powerlist is an annual publication")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black British Business Awards: public nominations",text:"Since 2014 the Black British Business Awards have celebrated Black British professionals and entrepreneurs. There are Rising Star and Senior Leader awards across six sectors, from Arts and Media to STEM. The public can nominate themselves or someone else.",figure:"",year:"2014",disputed:false,note:"Neither source is authoritative, and the official BBBA page was not available when checked. Treat the details as indicative and check the BBBA website before linking.",src:[SRCQ("Black British Business Awards nominations – Startups.co.uk","https://startups.co.uk/news/black-british-business-awards-nominations/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","celebrate the achievements of Black British business professionals and entrepreneurs"),SRCQ("Nominations are now open for 2021 Black British Business Awards – WeAreTheCity","https://wearethecity.com/nominations-are-now-open-for-2021-black-british-business-awards/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Since 2014, the BBBAwards has been a driving force promoting diversity and inclusivity in the workplace.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Town council small grants and youth competitions",text:"For Black History Month 2026, Lowestoft Town Council offered grants of up to £200 to local groups holding an event. It also ran creative writing and visual arts competitions for children from nursery to sixth form, with entries closing on 23 October.",figure:"up to £200 per event",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This point rests on one source, a local council (authoritative).",src:[SRCQ("Black History Month 2026 – Lowestoft Town Council","https://lowestofttowncouncil.gov.uk/black-history-month-2026/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Nursery age through to Sixth Form")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Professional bodies honour communities in October",text:"The Royal College of Nursing's Eastern region held a free Black History Month 2026 day, 'Honouring Our Communities', at Kneesworth House Hospital in Royston on 23 October 2026. It focused on 'all the people and everyday local networks that shape and sustain British life' and counted for 4 CPD hours.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The second source confirms the theme, not the RCN event. The RCN page alone is the source for the event details. The RCN is a professional body and trade union.",src:[SRCQ("RCN Eastern Black History Month – Honouring Our Communities – Royal College of Nursing","https://www.rcn.org.uk/news-and-events/events/E-Black-History-Month-Event-Eastern-231026","Supports the Honouring our communities page","23 Oct 2026, 09:30 - 15:30"),SRCQ("Black History Month 2026 – Inclusive Employers","https://www.inclusiveemployers.co.uk/event/black-history-month-2026/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Black History Month 2026’s theme, “Honouring Our Communities,” invites organisations to recognise the individuals")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"proposals",h:"How you can honour communities: proposals by who acts",intro:"These proposals build on the sourced examples above. Each says who acts, what they do, where, when and for whom. They are suggestions, not facts.",points:[
  {h:"Individuals: nominate a plaque or an honour",text:"Hub proposal: Individuals can nominate someone. For a person who died 20 or more years ago, propose a London blue plaque to English Heritage or a plaque to Nubian Jak. For a living person in your community, use the GOV.UK honours form. Gather the two supporting letters in October so the nomination is in by the end of Black History Month.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Propose a plaque – English Heritage","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/propose-a-plaque","Supports the Honouring our communities page","At least 20 years must have passed since a candidate's death."),SRCQ("Nominating someone for a UK honour – The Gazette","https://www.thegazette.co.uk/awards-and-accreditation/content/103437","Supports the Honouring our communities page","two supporting letters to back up your nomination")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Workplaces and staff networks: plaques and named awards",text:"Hub proposal: Staff networks can work with leadership to unveil plaques or name awards after the people who built their organisation. University Hospitals of Derby and Burton's EMBRACE network did this with Windrush plaques at every site in October 2024. NHS England's Windrush 70 Awards show that colleagues, patients and the public will nominate in large numbers.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("UHDB honours the Windrush generation – UHDB NHS","https://uhdb.nhs.uk/latest-news/uhdb-honours-the-windrush-generation-with-commemorative-plaques-unveiled-across-all-trust-sites-18371","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The unveiling ceremonies have been organised alongside our EMBRACE Staff Network"),SRCQ("NHS Windrush 70 awards – NHS England","https://www.england.nhs.uk/2018/06/black-and-ethnic-minority-nhs-staff-honoured-in-nhs-windrush-70-awards/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","More than 11,000 members of the public, patients and staff nominated NHS staff.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Schools: record local stories and teach local history",text:"Hub proposal: Schools can partner with local Black history groups to record grandparents' memories and teach local Black history all year. Portsmouth's project and the Curriculum for Wales are models. A good time to start is Windrush Day (22 June), so the work can be shown the following October.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Portsmouth's post-WW2 Black stories – University of Portsmouth","https://www.port.ac.uk/news-events-and-blogs/news/university-supports-project-to-share-portsmouths-post-ww2-black-stories","Supports the Honouring our communities page","The £68,645 will be spent on recording the memories"),SRCQ("New Curriculum for Wales – Welsh Government","https://www.gov.wales/six-things-you-may-not-know-about-new-curriculum-wales","Supports the Honouring our communities page","will be mandatory parts of the new curriculum")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Councils and public bodies: small grants, consultations and calendars",text:"Hub proposal: Councils and public bodies can offer small Black History Month event grants, as Lowestoft does. They can apply each January for the Windrush Day grant, which is open to councils. Before renaming or reinterpreting a contested site, they can run an open consultation, as Bristol did. They can also put 22 June, 1 August, 23 August and October in the civic calendar.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Black History Month 2026 – Lowestoft Town Council","https://lowestofttowncouncil.gov.uk/black-history-month-2026/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Nursery age through to Sixth Form"),SRCQ("Windrush Day Grant Scheme 2026 guidance – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026-guidance-for-applicants","Supports the Honouring our communities page","MHCLG is providing a total of £500,000 to fund activities across England."),SRCQ("We are Bristol History Commission – Full Report","https://uwe-repository.worktribe.com/output/9004543","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Almost 14,000 people filled out the survey.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Faith and community groups: archives and remembrance",text:"Hub proposal: Faith and community groups can apply to the Heritage Fund at any time for £10,000 to £250,000 to collect members' memories and records. They can deposit what they gather with a local archive or Black Cultural Archives. They can also hold a public act of remembrance on 23 August, following Liverpool's walk and libation.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("National Lottery Grants for Heritage – Heritage Fund","https://www.heritagefund.org.uk/node/143931","Supports the Honouring our communities page","There is no deadline so you can apply whenever you are ready."),SRCQ("10 years of International Slavery Museum – National Museums Liverpool","https://www.liverpoolmuseums.org.uk/news/press-releases/10-years-of-international-slavery-museum","Supports the Honouring our communities page","Slavery Remembrance Day has taken place annually in Liverpool on 23 August since 1999.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Media and cultural institutions: permanent spaces and named honours",text:"Hub proposal: Media and cultural institutions can give Black history permanent space, not just an October slot. London Museum Docklands and the International Slavery Museum have permanent galleries. Black-led awards such as MOBO and the Powerlist show how naming and ranking achievement builds visibility for young people.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("London, Sugar & Slavery – London Museum","https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/docklands/free-galleries/london-sugar-slavery/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","one of the only permanent UK displays dedicated to the British trade in enslaved Africans"),SRCQ("Powerful Media","https://powerful-media.com/","Supports the Honouring our communities page","the UK’s most influential people of African, African Caribbean and African American heritage")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Government: keep national monuments and grants, publish what is funded",text:"Hub proposal: Central government can keep funding the Windrush Day grant and publish each year's funded projects, as GOV.UK did for 2026. That lets communities in every region find and copy local models. It can also look after the National Windrush Monument as a place to gather on 22 June.",figure:"",year:"",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Windrush Day Grant Scheme 2026: projects to be funded – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/windrush-day-grant-scheme-2026-projects-to-be-funded","Supports the Honouring our communities page","A list of the 33 projects to be funded under the 2026 Windrush Day Grant Scheme."),SRCQ("The National Windrush Monument – GOV.UK","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-national-windrush-monument","Supports the Honouring our communities page","pays tribute to the dreams, ambition and resilience of the Windrush pioneers")],kind:"argument"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"You can nominate a plaque or an honour for someone who served your community.",refs:["ho-places-anyone-can-nominate-a-london-blue-plaque", "ho-recognition-anyone-can-nominate-someone-for-a-nation"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Mark the days that matter to colleagues, from Windrush Day to Emancipation Day and Slavery Remembrance Day.",refs:["ho-days-windrush-day-22-june-and-its-government-", "ho-days-emancipation-day-1-august", "ho-days-slavery-remembrance-day-23-august-in-liv"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Give staff networks time and a small budget to record and celebrate local stories.",refs:["ho-learning-heritage-fund-grants-open-to-community-g", "ho-proposals-workplaces-and-staff-networks-plaques-an"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Consult openly before naming or renaming places, as Bristol did.",refs:["ho-places-colston-hall-renamed-bristol-beacon-2020", "ho-places-colston-statue-from-plinth-to-museum-dis"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Under 4% of London's blue plaques honoured Black and Asian figures in 2016; track who your own honours recognise.",refs:["ho-places-english-heritage-blue-plaques-under-4-ho"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Anyone can nominate someone for a national honour or a London blue plaque.",refs:["ho-recognition-anyone-can-nominate-someone-for-a-nation", "ho-places-anyone-can-nominate-a-london-blue-plaque"]},{t:"Windrush Day is marked on 22 June, with a government grant scheme for community events.",refs:["ho-days-windrush-day-22-june-and-its-government-"]},{t:"Slavery Remembrance Day is marked on 23 August, and Emancipation Day on 1 August.",refs:["ho-days-slavery-remembrance-day-23-august-in-liv", "ho-days-emancipation-day-1-august"]},{t:"Community groups can apply to the Heritage Fund for grants to record their history.",refs:["ho-learning-heritage-fund-grants-open-to-community-g"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["awards"]={name:"Recognition and representation",pfx:"aw",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"ukhonours",h:"UK honours lists",intro:"The Cabinet Office publishes the ethnicity of honours recipients whose ethnicity is known. It publishes nothing on nominations, so success rates cannot be calculated.",points:[
  {h:"New Year Honours 2025: Black recipients 3.7%",text:"In the New Year Honours List 2025, 43 of 1,177 recipients whose ethnicity was known were Black (3.7%) and 22 were Mixed (1.9%); 87.5% were White. Counted by the Cabinet Office, published on Ethnicity facts and figures in July 2025.",figure:"43 of 1,177 (3.7%)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities. The Secretariat's own page rounds the combined minority share for this list to 12%.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (Ethnicity facts and figures, edition 11.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/latest","Supports the Recognition and representation page","5.6% of honours recipients were Asian, 3.7% were Black, 1.9% had Mixed ethnicity, and 1.4% were from the Other ethnic group"),SRCQ("Honours and Appointments Secretariat: Diversity","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the source's per-list figures for recipients from a 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background' include 'New Year 2025': '12%' and 'King’s Birthday 2025': '11%'")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"King's Birthday Honours 2024: Black recipients 3%",text:"In the King's Birthday Honours List 2024, 33 of 1,062 recipients with known ethnicity were Black (3%) and 18 were Mixed (2%); 90% were White. Counted by the Cabinet Office.",figure:"33 of 1,062 (3%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities. Denominator is the sum of the published group counts; the page rounds to whole percentages.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 10.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/10.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","in the King's Birthday Honours List 2024, 90% of honours recipients were white (out of those whose ethnicity was known)"),SRCQ("Honours and Appointments Secretariat: Diversity","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","'King’s Birthday 2024': '9.9%' (the source's 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic' grouping)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"New Year Honours 2024: Black recipients 4.8%",text:"In the New Year Honours List 2024, 59 of 1,217 recipients with known ethnicity were Black (4.8%) and 19 were Mixed (1.6%); 87.2% were White. Counted by the Cabinet Office.",figure:"59 of 1,217 (4.8%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 9.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/9.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","in the New Year Honours List 2024, 87.2% of honours recipients were white (out of those whose ethnicity was known)"),SRCQ("Honours and Appointments Secretariat: Diversity","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","'New Year 2024': '12.8%' (the source's 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic' grouping)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"New Year Honours 2023: highest Black share published, 6.1%",text:"In the New Year Honours List 2023, 64 of 1,047 recipients with known ethnicity were Black (6.1%) and 24 were Mixed (2.3%). The combined ethnic minority share, 16.4%, is the highest the Cabinet Office has published.",figure:"64 of 1,047 (6.1%)",year:"2023",disputed:true,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities. 60 recipients had unknown ethnicity. The Three Year Report's table gives 168 minority recipients for NY23, while the Ethnicity facts and figures group counts sum to 172; both report 16.4%.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 8.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/8.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","in the New Year Honours List 2023, 83.6% of honours recipients were white (out of those whose ethnicity was known)"),SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","At New Year 2023, 16.4% of recipients were from ethnic minority backgrounds, with 12.4% at the senior levels.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Queen's Birthday Honours 2021: Black recipients 4.2%",text:"In the Queen's Birthday Honours List 2021, 46 of 1,107 recipients with known ethnicity were Black (4.2%) and 37 were Mixed (3.3%); 85.0% were White. Counted by the Cabinet Office.",figure:"46 of 1,107 (4.2%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 5.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/5.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","in the Queen’s Birthday Honours List 2021, 85.0% of honours recipients were White (out of those whose ethnicity was known)"),SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","BD2021: \"15% recipients from ethnic minority background\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Queen's Birthday Honours 2019: Black recipients 3.2%",text:"In the June 2019 Birthday Honours, 30 of 939 recipients with known ethnicity were Black (3.2%) and 11 were Mixed (1.2%); 89.6% were White. This was the first list published on Ethnicity facts and figures.",figure:"30 of 939 (3.2%)",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities. Denominator is the sum of published group counts.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 1.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/1.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","in June 2019, 89.6% of all honours recipients were from the White ethnic group"),SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","BD2019: \"10% recipients from ethnic minority background\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnic minority share of honours roughly doubled since 2014–15",text:"The Cabinet Office reports that the share of honours recipients from ethnic minorities (excluding White minorities) rose from 7.0% in June 2015 to 12.5% in December 2024, and from about 6% in 2014 to over 15% by 2023.",figure:"7.0% to 12.5% (2015–2024)",year:"2015–2024",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities. Shares fluctuate between lists (9.1% at NY2020 to 16.4% at NY2023).",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 11.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/latest","Supports the Recognition and representation page","went up from 7.0% to 12.5%"),SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the percentage of honours recipients from ethnic minorities went up from around 6% to over 15%.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ten lists, 2021 to 2025: minority share between 9.9% and 16.4%",text:"The Honours and Appointments Secretariat publishes one combined figure per list: from 14.2% (New Year 2021) to 16.4% (New Year 2023) down to 9.9% (Birthday 2024), then 12% (New Year 2025) and 11% (Birthday 2025). No separate Black figure is given there.",figure:"9.9%–16.4% per list",year:"2021–2025",disputed:false,note:"Uses the source's 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic' grouping; excludes undisclosed ethnicity. All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities.",src:[SRCQ("Honours and Appointments Secretariat: Diversity","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the source's per-list figures for recipients from a 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background' include 'New Year 2025': '12%' and 'King’s Birthday 2025': '11%'"),SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","NY21: \"14.2% recipients from ethnic minority background\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Higher honours: White share ranged from 73% to 97%",text:"The Cabinet Office reports White people received 97.0% of higher honours (damehoods, knighthoods and CBE-level) in Birthday 2021, 73.3% in New Year 2023, 85.0% in New Year 2024 and 95% in Birthday 2024.",figure:"White share 73.3%–97.0%",year:"2021–2024",disputed:false,note:"All figures come from the Cabinet Office family of publishers (Ethnicity facts and figures, the Honours and Appointments Secretariat, Cabinet Office reports). No independent recount was found. Percentages exclude recipients whose ethnicity is unknown (the Secretariat says roughly 10% do not provide it). 'Ethnic minority' excludes White minorities. Higher-honour numbers are small, so shares swing widely. No Black-specific figure for higher honours is published.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 5.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/5.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","White people received 97.0% of higher honours, including damehoods and knighthoods"),SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 10.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/10.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","white people received 95% of higher honours, including damehoods and knighthoods")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnic minority damehoods and knighthoods: 1 to 8 per list",text:"The Cabinet Office's Three Year Report counts ethnic minority recipients of DBE/Kt by list: 1 at Birthday 2019, 5 at New Year 2020, 1 at Birthday 2020, 1 at New Year 2021, 1 at Birthday 2021, 6 at New Year 2022, 3 at Birthday 2022 and 8 at New Year 2023.",figure:"26 DBE/Kt across 8 lists (BD19–NY23)",year:"2019–2023",disputed:false,note:"Total DBE/Kt awarded per list is not given in the same table, so no share can be computed. No Black-specific count. The 26 is a sum of published per-list counts.",src:[SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","NY23 8 15 38 79 28 168 16.4"),SRCQ("Honours recipients (edition 8.0)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/8.0","Supports the Recognition and representation page","white people received 73.3% of higher honours, including damehoods and knighthoods")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"New Year 2026: minority share of higher awards doubled to 14%",text:"The Cabinet Office said ethnic minority representation at higher awards rose from 7% in the King's Birthday 2025 list to 14% in the New Year 2026 list, which had 1,157 recipients.",figure:"7% to 14% (higher awards)",year:"2025–2026",disputed:false,note:"Government press release; all-levels ethnicity figures for 2026 lists not yet on Ethnicity facts and figures when checked (edition 11.0 runs to December 2024).",src:[SRCQ("UK celebrates community champions in HM The King's New Year's Honours List (Cabinet Office, 29 Dec 2025)","https://gov.uk/government/news/uk-celebrates-community-champions-in-hm-the-kings-new-years-honours-list","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the ethnic minority representation on the list at higher awards has doubled from 7% in BD25 to 14% at NY26"),SRCQ("Honours and Appointments Secretariat: Diversity","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the source's per-list figures for recipients from a 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background' include 'New Year 2025': '12%' and 'King’s Birthday 2025': '11%'")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Order of the Garter: first Black member appointed in 2022",text:"Baroness Valerie Amos became the first Black person appointed to the Order of the Garter, founded in 1348, invested at Windsor on 13 June 2022.",figure:"1st Black member since 1348",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Milestone, not a count. The Garter is in the Sovereign's personal gift, not the Honours Committee process.",src:[SRCQ("Order of the Garter Investiture (University College Oxford, 14 June 2022)","https://www.univ.ox.ac.uk/?p=20448","Supports the Recognition and representation page","she is the first black person to be appointed to the Order."),SRCQ("Tony Blair joins prestigious Order of the Garter (Rayo, 13 June 2022)","https://www.hellorayo.co.uk/downtown/uk/news/order-garter-tony-blair","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Baroness Amos becomes the first black member of the order since it was founded in 1348.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Learie Constantine: first Black life peer",text:"Sir Learie Constantine, the Trinidad-born cricketer and campaigner (died 1971), was the first Black life peer; he took his seat in the House of Lords on 26 March 1969.",figure:"Milestone",year:"1969",disputed:false,note:"Milestone, not a count. Parliament source is served from a parliament.uk test host (webtest.parliament.uk); the live page should be used when linking.",src:[SRCQ("And now some firsts in the UK (Gloucestershire County Council)","https://gloucestershire.gov.uk/your-community/black-history-month/black-history-month-2020/celebrating-bame-community-successes/and-now-some-firsts-in-the-uk","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first black life peer"),SRCQ("50th anniversary of Lord Constantine, the first black life peer, taking his seat (House of Lords, March 2019)","https://webtest.parliament.uk/business/lords/media-centre/house-of-lords-media-notices/2019/march-2019/50th-anniversary-of-lord-constantine-the-first-black-life-peer--taking-his-seat-in-the-house-of-lords-","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first black Life Peer who took his seat in the House of Lords 50 years ago today on 26 March 1969")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Walter Tull was not a VC holder: his Military Cross was never awarded",text:"Walter Tull (killed 25 March 1918), one of the first Black officers in the British Army, did not receive the Victoria Cross. Sources say he was recommended for the Military Cross, which was never awarded; MPs and campaigners have sought a posthumous award.",figure:"Not awarded",year:"1918",disputed:false,note:"Corrects a common error. Evidence of the recommendation is reported, not documented in an official citation; no Ministry of Defence position was quoted in either source.",src:[SRCQ("The campaign to award Walter Tull a posthumous Military Cross (History UK)","https://www.history.co.uk/articles/lets-see-if-we-can-adjust-history-the-campaign-to-award-walter-tull-a-posthumous-military","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He was recommended for a posthumous Military Cross but the honour was never bestowed."),SRCQ("MPs call for medal to be awarded to first black army officer (Forces News)","https://www.forcesnews.com/news/mps-call-medal-be-awarded-first-black-army-officer","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Walter's race was clearly a factor in explaining why he was never awarded the Military Cross that he was recommended for.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"CWGC 2021: up to 54,000 unequally commemorated",text:"The Commonwealth War Graves Commission's 2021 special committee found 45,000 to 54,000 mainly Asian and African First World War dead were commemorated unequally and at least 116,000 not named, citing 'pervasive racism'. Already on the hub: link only.",figure:"45,000–54,000 unequal; 116,000+ unnamed",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Link only; full treatment is elsewhere on the hub. Al Jazeera reports up to 350,000 possibly not commemorated by name.",src:[SRCQ("Pervasive racism behind failure to honour Black and Asian troops (Forces News, 22 April 2021)","https://www.forcesnews.com/news/pervasive-racism-behind-failure-honour-black-and-asian-troops","Supports the Recognition and representation page","between 45,000 and 54,000"),SRCQ("Britain's unequal troop commemorations due to 'pervasive racism' (Al Jazeera, 22 April 2021)","https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2021/4/22/britains-unequal-troop-commemorations-due-to-pervasive-racism","Supports the Recognition and representation page","entrenched prejudices, preconceptions and pervasive racism of contemporary imperial attitudes")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black share of honours is close to the Black population share",text:"Black recipients made up 3.0% to 6.1% of honours lists published from 2019 to 2025, against a Black population of 4.0% in England and Wales in 2021. Mixed recipients (1.2% to 3.3%) were mostly below the 2.9% Mixed population.",figure:"3.0%–6.1% vs 4.0%",year:"2019–2025",disputed:false,note:"Honours cover the whole UK and overseas, not only England and Wales; population includes children, who are rarely honoured, so the baseline is approximate.",src:[SRCQ("Population of England and Wales (Ethnicity facts and figures)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","followed by black (4.0%)"),SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Recognition and representation page","81.7% (48.7 million) of usual residents in England and Wales identified their ethnic group within the high-level")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Overall minority share remains below the 18.3% minority population",text:"Every published list from 2019 to 2025 had a lower ethnic minority share (9.1% to 16.4%) than the 18.3% of the England and Wales population outside the White groups in 2021 (100% minus 81.7% White).",figure:"9.1%–16.4% vs 18.3%",year:"2019–2025",disputed:false,note:"18.3% is computed from the published 81.7% White figure and includes Asian (9.3%), the largest group. Honours shares exclude White minorities.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Recognition and representation page","81.7% (48.7 million) of usual residents in England and Wales identified their ethnic group within the high-level"),SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","At New Year 2023, 16.4% of recipients were from ethnic minority backgrounds, with 12.4% at the senior levels.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"No published ethnicity data on honours nominations",text:"Neither the Honours and Appointments Secretariat nor the Three Year Report to Parliament publishes the ethnicity of nominees, so success rates by ethnic group cannot be calculated. The Cabinet Office says it is still challenging to increase ethnic minority recipients.",figure:"No published count",year:"2023–2025",disputed:false,note:"Gap in the evidence: under-nomination and lower success rates cannot be told apart.",src:[SRCQ("2023 Three Year Report to Parliament on the Operation of the Honours System (Cabinet Office)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/646e3922ab40bf00101969f2/2023-Three-Year-Report-to-Parliament-on-the-Operation-of-the-Honours-System.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","There remain challenges consistently to increase the number of people from ethnic minority backgrounds"),SRCQ("Honours and Appointments Secretariat: Diversity","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the source's per-list figures for recipients from a 'Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background' include 'New Year 2025': '12%' and 'King’s Birthday 2025': '11%'")],kind:"argument"}]},
 {k:"military",h:"Military medals and national honours",intro:"Firsts are documented, but no awarding body in the UK publishes a count of Black recipients of gallantry awards. The US publishes one for the Medal of Honor.",points:[
  {h:"William Hall: first Black recipient of the Victoria Cross, 1857",text:"William Hall, a Nova Scotian sailor in the Royal Navy, won the Victoria Cross for action at Lucknow on 16 November 1857 and received it on 28 October 1859. Veterans Affairs Canada and the Canadian Encyclopedia both describe him as the first Black recipient.",figure:"First Black VC recipient (1857 action)",year:"1857",disputed:false,note:"No awarding body publishes a count of Black VC recipients. 'First' is as documented by the two sources.",src:[SRCQ("William Neilson Edward Hall – Veterans Affairs Canada","https://www.veterans.gc.ca/en/node/449356","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black person in the British empire, to receive the Victoria Cross"),SRCQ("William Neilson Hall – The Canadian Encyclopedia","https://thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/william-neilson-hall","Supports the Recognition and representation page","William Hall was the first Black person, the first Nova Scotian and the first Canadian naval recipient of the Victoria Cross.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Samuel Hodge: first Black soldier awarded the VC, 1866",text:"Lance Corporal Samuel Hodge of the 4th West India Regiment, from Tortola, won the Victoria Cross for action in the Gambia on 30 June 1866. Two independent sources describe him as the first Black soldier, or the first soldier of African-Caribbean descent, to receive the VC.",figure:"First Black soldier awarded the VC (1866 action)",year:"1866",disputed:false,note:"Hall (a sailor) came first. Hodge is the first soldier. The sources word this differently.",src:[SRCQ("Samuel Hodge, the West India Regiments & Britain's Forgotten African-Caribbean Soldiers – History of War","https://www.historyanswers.co.uk/history-of-war/samuel-hodge-the-west-india-regiments-britains-forgotten-african-caribbean-soldiers/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first soldier of African-Caribbean descent to receive the Victoria Cross"),SRCQ("Caribbean Service Personnel in the British Armed Forces – West India Committee","https://westindiacommittee.org/historyheritageculture/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Caribbean-Service-Personnel-in-the-British-Armed-Forces.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Samuel Hodge of Tortola (the first Black soldier to be awarded the VC)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"William Gordon VC, West India Regiment, 1892",text:"Lance Corporal William James Gordon of the West India Regiment, from Jamaica, won the Victoria Cross for shielding his commanding officer at Toniataba in the Gambia on 13 March 1892. The London Gazette published the award on 9 December 1892.",figure:"VC gazetted 9 December 1892",year:"1892",disputed:false,note:"The award comes from the primary record (London Gazette). The sources do not say whether Gordon was Black. He is presented here only as a West India Regiment recipient documented in Caribbean military heritage sources.",src:[SRCQ("The London Gazette, issue 26352, 9 December 1892","https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/26352/data.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Lance-Corporal Gordon threw himself between. Major Madden and the muskets"),SRCQ("Samuel Hodge, the West India Regiments & Britain's Forgotten African-Caribbean Soldiers – History of War","https://www.historyanswers.co.uk/history-of-war/samuel-hodge-the-west-india-regiments-britains-forgotten-african-caribbean-soldiers/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","William James Gordon, another West India Regiment soldier, was awarded the VC in 1892.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Johnson Beharry VC: first living VC recipient since 1965",text:"Johnson Beharry, born in Grenada in 1979, was awarded the Victoria Cross in 2005 for two actions in Iraq in 2004. The National Portrait Gallery and History of War describe him as the first living recipient since 1965.",figure:"First living VC since 1965",year:"2005",disputed:false,note:"Neither source calls him the 'first Black' recipient of anything. Do not add that label. He is a living public recipient, named here in that role only.",src:[SRCQ("Johnson Gideon Beharry – National Portrait Gallery","https://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/portraitLarge/mw119769/Johnson-Gideon-Beharry","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He is the first person in the British army since 1965 to be awarded the Victoria Cross while still living."),SRCQ("Private Johnson Gideon Beharry VC – History of War","https://www.historyofwar.org/articles/people_beharry_johnson.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first living recipient since 1965")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Caribbean volunteers in the Second World War: about 10,000",text:"The West India Committee puts the number of Black West Indians who joined the armed forces in the Second World War at over 10,000. This includes over 5,500 RAF ground crew, over 400 airmen and over 600 women in the Auxiliary Territorial Service. A National Archives talk gives similar RAF figures: about 500 aircrew from the Caribbean and West Africa, and 6,000 Caribbean ground crew.",figure:"10,000+ (West India Committee); ~500 aircrew, 6,000 ground crew (National Archives talk)",year:"1939–1945",disputed:false,note:"These are service numbers, not awards. Neither source publishes a count of decorations to Caribbean or African personnel. The National Archives transcript has a typo ('495 five').",src:[SRCQ("Caribbean Service Personnel in the British Armed Forces – West India Committee","https://westindiacommittee.org/historyheritageculture/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Caribbean-Service-Personnel-in-the-British-Armed-Forces.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In total, over 10,000 black West Indians joined the armed forces."),SRCQ("The untold story of the RAF's Black Second World War fliers – The National Archives","https://media.nationalarchives.gov.uk/index.php/untold-story-raf-black-second-world-war-fliers-europe/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In addition to the airmen, we had 6,000 Caribbean ground crew personnel.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ulric Cross DFC DSO: most decorated Caribbean airman of WW2",text:"Squadron Leader Ulric Cross of Trinidad, a Pathfinder navigator, received the Distinguished Flying Cross in June 1944 and the Distinguished Service Order in January 1945. The RAF calls him 'the most decorated Caribbean airman of the Second World War'.",figure:"DFC 1944; DSO 1945",year:"1944–1945",disputed:true,note:"Disputed only on wording: the RAF says 'the most decorated' and the RAF Benevolent Fund says 'one of the most decorated'. No source counts decorations to Caribbean aircrew overall.",src:[SRCQ("Black History Month: Royal Air Force heroes – Royal Air Force","https://www.raf.mod.uk/news/articles/black-history-month-royal-air-force-heroes/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the most decorated Caribbean airman of the Second World War"),SRCQ("The remarkable legacy of Squadron Leader Ulric Cross – RAF Benevolent Fund","https://www.rafbf.org/news-and-stories/raf-history/black-history-month-remarkable-legacy-squadron-leader-ulric-cross","Supports the Recognition and representation page","in June 1944, he was awarded the Distinguished Flying Cross (DFC)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"US Medal of Honor: 7 Black WW2 soldiers recognised in 1997",text:"In 1993 the US Army commissioned a Shaw University study to find out whether race had affected Medal of Honor decisions. The study found that no Black soldier had received the medal for the Second World War. On 13 January 1997 President Clinton awarded it to seven Black veterans, and Vernon Baker was the only one still living.",figure:"7 awarded in 1997; 0 Black WW2 recipients before",year:"1997",disputed:false,note:"A 2002 review that Congress requested led to 24 further awards in 2014 to Hispanic, Black and Jewish veterans (Military Times). The sources do not give a Black-only count for 2014.",src:[SRCQ("Remarks by the President at Medals of Honor presentation, 13 January 1997 – Clinton White House archive","https://clintonwhitehouse6.archives.gov/1997/01/1997-01-13-president-remarks-at-medals-of-honor-presentation.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","No African American who deserved the Medal of Honor for his service in World War II received it."),SRCQ("Will more minority veterans receive the Medal of Honor? – Military Times, 6 February 2023","https://www.militarytimes.com/news/your-army/2023/02/06/will-more-minority-veterans-receive-the-medal-of-honor/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the medal was not given to Black soldiers who served during World War II")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Medal of Honor recipients: 92–93 of about 3,500 (2.6%)",text:"Military Times (2023) cites the Congressional Medal of Honor Society: 93 of the 3,515 recipients are Black, which is 2.6%. In 2020 the VFW, citing the same Society, gave 92 Black men of 'more than 3,500'. For comparison, 12.4% of the US population was Black alone in the 2020 Census.",figure:"93 of 3,515 (2.6%)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The two figures come from one counter (the Society) at different dates, so they are not in conflict. The medal dates from the Civil War, so a share of today's population is only a rough benchmark.",src:[SRCQ("Will more minority veterans receive the Medal of Honor? – Military Times","https://www.militarytimes.com/news/your-army/2023/02/06/will-more-minority-veterans-receive-the-medal-of-honor/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","of the 3,515 recipients, 93 are Black"),SRCQ("VFW honors African-American Medal of Honor recipients – VFW, February 2020","https://vfw.org/media-and-events/latest-releases/archives/2020/2/vfw-honors-african-american-medal-of-honor-recipients?p=1","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only 92 have been black men, according to the Congressional Medal of Honor Society")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Josephine Baker: first Black woman in the Panthéon, 2021",text:"Josephine Baker, performer and Resistance agent, was honoured in the Panthéon on 30 November 2021. She was the sixth woman, and the first Black woman, to be honoured there. She held the Légion d'honneur and the Croix de guerre.",figure:"First Black woman of 6 women in the Panthéon",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"France does not publish Légion d'honneur awards by ethnicity, so there is no published count. Public Sénat confirms the Panthéon milestone. Aberystwyth University confirms her decorations.",src:[SRCQ("Joséphine Baker: artist, activist, resistance fighter and now honoured in France's Panthéon – Aberystwyth University","https://aber.ac.uk/en/news/archive/2021/08/title-247242-en.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Baker will be the sixth woman, first entertainer and first Black woman to be honoured at the Parisian mausoleum."),SRCQ("Joséphine Baker devient la première femme noire à entrer au Panthéon – Public Sénat","https://www.publicsenat.fr/actualites/societe/josephine-baker-190194","Supports the Recognition and representation page","la première femme noire à entrer au Panthéon")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Jamaica's Order of National Hero: 7 holders since 1969",text:"Jamaica's Order of National Hero is the country's highest honour, set up under the National Honours and Awards Act 1969. It has 7 holders: Nanny of the Maroons, Samuel Sharpe, George William Gordon, Paul Bogle, Marcus Garvey, Sir Alexander Bustamante and Norman Manley.",figure:"7 holders",year:"1969–2026",disputed:false,note:"This is recognition by a Black-majority state. The sources do not record the holders' ethnicity, and we do not classify them.",src:[SRCQ("Order of National Hero – Jamaica Information Service","https://jis.gov.jm/information/awards/order-of-national-hero/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","rendered to Jamaica service of a most distinguished nature"),SRCQ("Jamaica's Highest Honour: The Order of National Hero – African Caribbean Institute of Jamaica","https://acij-ioj.org.jm/jamaicas-highest-honour-the-order-of-national-hero/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only seven individuals have received the award of the Order of National Hero")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Barbados: 11 National Heroes, the 11th named in 2021",text:"When Barbados became a republic on 30 November 2021, it named Rihanna as its 11th National Hero. National Heroes have been named since 1998, and she is the second living holder after Sir Garfield Sobers.",figure:"11 since 1998",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The government said in 2021 that the list would grow, so check the current number. Living holders are named only in their public role.",src:[SRCQ("Rihanna named National Hero – Barbados Government Information Service","https://gisbarbados.gov.bb/?p=566079","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Robyn Rihanna Fenty, has been named the 11th National Hero of Barbados."),SRCQ("New republic Barbados names Rihanna as a national hero – Al Jazeera, 30 November 2021","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/11/30/new-republic-barbados-names-rihanna-national-hero","Supports the Recognition and representation page","makes her the 11th Barbadian to have ever been honoured as a national hero")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"US Presidential Medal of Freedom: 18% of Obama's awards were Black",text:"In 2015 the Washington Post's The Fix found that African Americans made up 18% of President Obama's Medal of Freedom recipients and 17% of President George W. Bush's. Both shares are above the Black-alone share of the US population, 12.4% in the 2020 Census.",figure:"18% (Obama), 17% (Bush)",year:"2015",disputed:false,note:"This is one analysis, read through Political Wire's repost because the Washington Post original was not available when checked. It gives no denominators. The Conversation (2018) gives only a 'people of colour' share (39% for Obama), not a Black share.",src:[SRCQ("A different view on America's highest honor – Political Wire (reporting the Washington Post's The Fix)","https://politicalwire.com/2015/11/25/different-view-on-americas-highest-honor/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","18 percent of Obama's, compared to 17 percent for Bush"),SRCQ("What Trump's picks for the Presidential Medal of Freedom say about him – The Conversation","https://theconversation.com/what-trumps-picks-for-the-presidential-medal-of-freedom-say-about-him-107081","Supports the Recognition and representation page","39 percent people of color")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Order of Canada: no Black-specific count published",text:"Rideau Hall does not analyse appointments by ethnicity. Andrew Griffith's analyses for Policy Options covered 723 appointments in 2013 to 2016 and more than 2,000 in 2013 to 2023. Both found 'visible' or 'racialized' minorities under-represented, but neither gives a figure for Black appointees.",figure:"No published Black count",year:"2017; 2024",disputed:false,note:"Griffith identified minorities from names, citations and web searches, which is inference rather than self-declaration. No published counts were found for the Netherlands' orders either.",src:[SRCQ("The Order of Canada and diversity – Policy Options, March 2017","https://policyoptions.irpp.org/2017/03/the-order-of-canada-and-diversity/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Rideau Hall has not analyzed appointment diversity with respect to visible minorities."),SRCQ("How diverse are Order of Canada appointments? – Policy Options, June 2024","https://policyoptions.irpp.org/2024/06/diversity-order-canada/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Racialized minorities, however, are still notably underrepresented relative to their population share.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"science",h:"Nobel and science prizes, and learned academies",intro:"Published counts of Black laureates differ, and most science prizes publish no ethnicity data at all. Several academy figures come from the academies' own diversity reports.",points:[
  {h:"Nobel Prizes: 1,012 laureates to 2024",text:"The Nobel Prizes and the economics prize were awarded 627 times to 1,012 people and organisations between 1901 and 2024, according to the Nobel Foundation. This is the denominator used in later counts.",figure:"1,012 laureates (1901–2024)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Includes organisations. 2025 and 2026 awards not included in this figure. WorldAtlas gives an earlier individual-only total (916 to 2019).",src:[SRCQ("All Nobel Prizes – NobelPrize.org","https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/lists/all-nobel-prizes/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Between 1901 and 2024 ... were awarded 627 times to 1,012 people and organisations"),SRCQ("Black Nobel Prize Winners – WorldAtlas, 2019","https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/black-nobel-prize-winners.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","As of 2019, the Nobel Prize had been won by 916 individuals")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black Nobel laureates: published counts differ, 14 to 17",text:"Winston Morgan (University of East London) counted 14 Black laureates of more than 900 (1.5%) in 2018; WorldAtlas counted 16 of 916 individuals (1.7%) in 2019; a Scientific American analysis counts 17 Black laureates across peace, literature and economics.",figure:"14 of 900+ (1.5%, 2018); 16 of 916 (1.7%, 2019); 17 (undated, c. 2023)",year:"2018–2023",disputed:true,note:"No count after 2023 found. Counts differ by date and by how authors identify Black laureates; we have not compiled our own. Scientific American piece is undated. Contrast: no agreed world baseline; US 2020 Census Black alone 12.4%.",src:[SRCQ("A black scientist has never won a Nobel Prize for science – Winston Morgan (University of East London), The Conversation via ScienceAlert, 9 Oct 2018","https://www.sciencealert.com/a-black-scientist-has-never-won-a-nobel-prize-for-science","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only 14 (1.5 percent) have been black and none in science"),SRCQ("Black Nobel Prize Winners – WorldAtlas, 10 Dec 2019","https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/black-nobel-prize-winners.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","As of 2019, the Nobel Prize had been won by 916 individuals, and this includes 16 black recipients."),SRCQ("A Quiet Bias Is Keeping Black Scientists from Winning Nobel Prizes – Boyce, Crittenden and Allsop, Scientific American (opinion)","https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/nobel-prizes-overlook-black-scientists-because-of-this-quiet-bias","Supports the Recognition and representation page","While there have been 17 Black Nobel laureates in peace, literature and economics")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"No Black laureate in physics, chemistry or medicine",text:"Marc Zimmer counted zero Black laureates among 617 science laureates (to 2019). Winston Morgan and Popular Science report the same; Scientific American repeats it in a later piece.",figure:"0 of 617 science laureates (0%)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Morgan's and Zimmer's pieces both first appeared in The Conversation (different authors, different years). Not checked against 2024–2026 prizes in a published source.",src:[SRCQ("Nobel Prizes have a diversity problem even worse than the scientific fields they honor – Marc Zimmer, Nature Index (from The Conversation), 8 Oct 2020","https://www.nature.com/nature-index/news/nobel-prizes-have-a-diversity-problem-even-worse-than-the-scientific-fields-they-honor","Supports the Recognition and representation page","zero of the 617 science laureates have been Black."),SRCQ("A black scientist has never won a Nobel Prize for science – Winston Morgan (University of East London), The Conversation via ScienceAlert, 9 Oct 2018","https://www.sciencealert.com/a-black-scientist-has-never-won-a-nobel-prize-for-science","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only 14 (1.5 percent) have been black and none in science"),SRCQ("Why are there no black Nobel laureates in science? – Grace Wade, Popular Science, 25 Oct 2019","https://www.popsci.com/racial-inequality-nobel-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","which have seen no black laureates in their 61-year history"),SRCQ("A Quiet Bias Is Keeping Black Scientists from Winning Nobel Prizes – Scientific American","https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/nobel-prizes-overlook-black-scientists-because-of-this-quiet-bias","Supports the Recognition and representation page","a Black scientist still has never won a Nobel in physiology/medicine, physics or chemistry.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Most Black laureates won Peace or Literature",text:"Of the 14 Black laureates Morgan counted to 2018, 10 won the Peace Prize and 3 the Literature Prize, leaving 1 in economics.",figure:"10 Peace, 3 Literature, of 14 (2018)",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"Breakdown is Morgan's; the economics figure (1) is inferred from his 14 total less 13, and matches Global Voices' 'only' Black economics laureate as of 2020.",src:[SRCQ("No black scientist has ever won a Nobel – Winston Morgan, Big Think (republished from The Conversation)","https://bigthink.com/politics-current-affairs/no-black-scientist-has-ever-won-a-nobel-thats-bad-for-science-and-bad-for-society/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Almost all black laureates have been awarded for work in the fields of peace (ten) and literature (three)."),SRCQ("Google Doodle of economist and Nobel laureate Sir Arthur Lewis – Global Voices, Dec 2020","https://globalvoices.org/2020/12/10/google-doodle-of-economist-and-nobel-laureate-sir-arthur-lewis-makes-the-caribbean-proud/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He was the first (and thus far, only) Black person to win the coveted prize in the field of economic sciences.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ralph Bunche: first Black Nobel laureate, Peace 1950",text:"Ralph Bunche, a UN diplomat, became the first Black person and first person of colour to win the Nobel Peace Prize, in 1950.",figure:"1st Black laureate",year:"1950",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Ralph J. Bunche 1st Black To Win Nobel Prize – NewsOne","https://newsone.com/2807838/ralph-j-bunche-nobel-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Becoming the first Black person to win the coveted award"),SRCQ("Dec. 10, 1950 – Mississippi Today","https://mississippitoday.org/2023/12/10/1950-ralph-bunche-nobel-peace-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Ralph J. Bunche became the first Black American and the first person of color to win the Nobel Peace Prize.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Arthur Lewis: only Black economics laureate (to 2020)",text:"Sir Arthur Lewis, from St Lucia, received the 1979 economics prize. Princeton and Global Voices describe him as the only Black winner in economics or the science subjects.",figure:"1 Black laureate in economics (1979)",year:"1979",disputed:false,note:"'Only' is as of the source dates (2020). Princeton counts economics as a 'science subject'; Zimmer's 617 'science laureates' excludes it.",src:[SRCQ("Legacy of Nobel Laureate Sir W. Arthur Lewis – Princeton SPIA","https://spia.princeton.edu/news/legacy-nobel-laureate-sir-w-arthur-lewis-commemorated-robertson-hall","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He remains the only black individual to receive a Nobel Prize in the science subjects."),SRCQ("Global Voices, Dec 2020","https://globalvoices.org/2020/12/10/google-doodle-of-economist-and-nobel-laureate-sir-arthur-lewis-makes-the-caribbean-proud/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He was the first (and thus far, only) Black person to win the coveted prize in the field of economic sciences.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wole Soyinka: first African Literature laureate, 1986",text:"Wole Soyinka of Nigeria received the 1986 Nobel Prize in Literature, the first African writer to do so.",figure:"1st African Literature laureate",year:"1986",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Wole Soyinka – PEN America","https://pen.org/?p=16619","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 1986 Soyinka was awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature; the first African writer to win the prize."),SRCQ("Wole Soyinka – Biography.com","https://www.biography.com/writer/wole-soyinka","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 1986, the playwright and political activist became the first African to receive the Nobel Prize for Literature.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Toni Morrison: first Black woman Nobel laureate, 1993",text:"Toni Morrison won the 1993 Nobel Prize in Literature, the first Black woman to receive a Nobel Prize.",figure:"1st Black woman laureate",year:"1993",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Toni Morrison dead at 88 – Global News, 6 Aug 2019","https://globalnews.ca/news/5730547/toni-morrison-dead-author-beloved/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","She was the first black woman to receive the Nobel literature prize, which she was awarded in 1993."),SRCQ("Toni Morrison Nobel Prize in Literature – Essence","https://www.essence.com/news/toni-morrison-nobel-prize-in-literature","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Morrison was the first Black woman to receive a Nobel Prize in any category.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Abdulrazak Gurnah: first Black Literature laureate since 1993",text:"Gurnah's 2021 prize was the first Literature prize to a Black writer since Morrison in 1993, a gap of 28 years. NPR notes he is the fifth writer from Africa to win.",figure:"28-year gap (1993–2021)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nobel Prize in Literature 2021 – NPR, 7 Oct 2021","https://www.npr.org/2021/10/07/1043436576/nobel-prize-literature-2021","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He is the first Black person awarded the prize since Toni Morrison in 1993."),SRCQ("Black writer wins Nobel Prize in literature for first time since 1993 – Black Enterprise, 7 Oct 2021","https://blackenterprise.com/black-writer-wins-nobel-prize-in-literature-for-first-time-since-1993/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black writer to win the Nobel Prize in literature since 1993")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wangari Maathai: first African woman Peace laureate, 2004",text:"The Norwegian Nobel Committee named Wangari Maathai of Kenya the first woman from Africa to receive the Nobel Peace Prize, in 2004.",figure:"1st African woman Peace laureate",year:"2004",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Nobel Peace Prize 2004 press release – NobelPrize.org","https://nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/2004/press-release","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first woman from Africa to be honoured with the Nobel Peace Prize"),SRCQ("Kenyan wins Nobel Peace Prize – Al Jazeera, 8 Oct 2004","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2004/10/8/kenyan-wins-nobel-peace-prize","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Maathai is the first African woman to win the peace prize")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Argument: the science prizes lag even their own fields",text:"Zimmer argues that the Nobel science prizes are less diverse than the fields they honour; Morgan argues that the absence of Black science laureates harms science and society.",figure:"0 of 617 (0%)",year:"2018–2020",disputed:false,note:"Opinion pieces by academics; the Conversation and Nature Index versions of Zimmer are one source.",src:[SRCQ("Nobel Prizes have a diversity problem even worse than the scientific fields they honor – The Conversation","https://theconversation.com/nobel-prizes-have-a-diversity-problem-even-worse-than-the-scientific-fields-they-honor-145799","Supports the Recognition and representation page","zero of the 617 science laureates have been Black."),SRCQ("No black scientist has ever won a Nobel – Big Think","https://bigthink.com/politics-current-affairs/no-black-scientist-has-ever-won-a-nobel-thats-bad-for-science-and-bad-for-society/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Yet in over 100 years, we have never seen a black scientist become a Nobel laureate.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Counter-argument: the count depends on what counts as science",text:"Princeton describes Arthur Lewis as a Black Nobel laureate 'in the science subjects', counting economics; Zimmer's and Popular Science's zero covers physics, chemistry and medicine only.",figure:"1 (with economics) vs 0 (without)",year:"2020",disputed:true,note:"Use 'physics, chemistry or physiology/medicine' wording on the hub to avoid ambiguity.",src:[SRCQ("Princeton SPIA","https://spia.princeton.edu/news/legacy-nobel-laureate-sir-w-arthur-lewis-commemorated-robertson-hall","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He remains the only black individual to receive a Nobel Prize in the science subjects."),SRCQ("Why are there no black Nobel laureates in science? – Grace Wade, Popular Science, 25 Oct 2019","https://www.popsci.com/racial-inequality-nobel-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","which have seen no black laureates in their 61-year history")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Lasker Awards: 17 of 397 winners ‘non-white’, 1946–2022",text:"A BMJ study by Jacobs and colleagues classified 380 of 397 Lasker Award recipients (95.7%) as white and 17 (4.3%) as ‘non-white’, 1946–2022. It does not give a separate Black count.",figure:"17 of 397 ‘non-white’ (4.3%)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Authors classified recipients themselves; 'non-white' is not a Black count. Same publisher group for both sources; study is peer reviewed. No published Black-only count.",src:[SRCQ("Jacobs et al., The BMJ 2023;381:e074968","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10189906","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Most award recipients were categorized as white (95.7%, 380/397) and 4.3% (17/397) were categorized as non-white."),SRCQ("BMJ Group press release, 17 May 2023","https://bmjgroup.com/women-and-non-white-groups-still-missing-out-on-top-us-research-prize","Supports the Recognition and representation page","4.3% (17 of 397) were categorised as non-white")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Pritzker Prize: Francis Kéré first Black and African winner",text:"Diébédo Francis Kéré of Burkina Faso won the 2022 Pritzker Architecture Prize, the first Black and first African laureate; AllAfrica records him as the 51st laureate.",figure:"1 of 51 laureates (2022)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"51 is the count to 2022 quoted by AllAfrica; later laureates not added.",src:[SRCQ("NPR, 15 Mar 2022","https://www.npr.org/2022/03/15/1085457169/pritzker-architecture-prize-2022-diebedo-francis-kere","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The first Black winner of the Pritzker Architecture Prize had already received numerous accolades in his field"),SRCQ("AllAfrica, 16 Mar 2022","https://allafrica.com/stories/202203160081.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","has become the first African to win the Pritzker Prize"),SRCQ("Al Jazeera, 13 Aug 2022","https://www.aljazeera.com/video/talk-to-al-jazeera/2022/8/13/francis-kere-world-class-architecture-mudbrick-by-mudbrick","Supports the Recognition and representation page","became the first African and first Black person to win the most prestigious award in architecture")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Templeton Prize: Desmond Tutu, 2013",text:"Archbishop Desmond Tutu received the 2013 Templeton Prize, worth about US$1.7 million, for lifelong work advancing spiritual principles.",figure:"Winner 2013",year:"2013",disputed:false,note:"Neither source makes a 'first' claim; no published count of Black Templeton laureates.",src:[SRCQ("Tutu wins Templeton Prize – Anglican Journal","https://anglicanjournal.com/tutu-wins-templeton-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","has been awarded this year’s 2013 Templeton Prize, the world’s largest annual prize"),SRCQ("Desmond Tutu wins $1.7M Templeton Prize – theGrio, 4 Apr 2013","https://thegrio.com/2013/04/04/desmond-tutu-wins-1-7m-templeton-prize","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The honor comes with a 1.1 million-pound ($1.7 million) award")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Turing Award: no published ethnicity count",text:"No published count of Black Turing Award recipients was found. An ACM member's published letter criticised the 2020 award as going to 'two more white guys'; Slate/The Wire counted 3 women among 70 winners to 2020.",figure:"No published count",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Opinion and gender data only. Do not state a Black count.",src:[SRCQ("Tales of Two Turings – Communications of the ACM (letters)","https://cacm.acm.org/opinion/tales-of-two-turings","Supports the Recognition and representation page","it is yet another disappointment to see two more white guys get selected for this recognition."),SRCQ("Susan D'Agostino, The Wire Science (from Slate), Jan 2020","https://science.thewire.in/?p=426082","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Since 1966, 70 computer scientists have won the Turing Award. Only three have been women.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Royal Society Fellows: 0 Black respondents of 671 in 2021",text:"Of 671 Fellows and Foreign Members who gave their ethnicity in 2021, none were Black, Black British, Caribbean or African; 626 (93%) were White, 29 (4%) Asian and 6 (1%) Mixed. 689 of 1,711 completed the survey.",figure:"0 of 671 (0%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Survey-based: 671 of 1,711 Fellows gave ethnicity. Second source is the Society's own next-year report (same publisher).",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society 2021 diversity data report","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/topics/diversity-in-science/2021-diversity-data-report.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black, Black British, Caribbean or African 0% (0) of 671 Fellows and Foreign Members giving ethnicity"),SRCQ("Royal Society diversity data 2022–23","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2022-23.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","0% (0) of the 663 respondents ... Black, Black British, Caribbean or African")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society Fellows: 2 Black respondents of 721 in 2024",text:"At 31 March 2024, 2 of 721 Fellows and Foreign Members giving ethnicity were Black, Black British, Caribbean or African (0.3%, shown as 0%), against 684 White. In 2022 the figure was 0 of 663.",figure:"2 of 721 (0.3%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"The 0.3% is our calculation from the Society's numbers. Report chart labels White as 90% though 684/721 is 95%. Compare 4.0% Black in England and Wales (2021 Census).",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society diversity data 2023–24","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2023-24.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black, Black British, Caribbean or African: 2 respondents (0%) of 721"),SRCQ("Royal Society diversity data 2022–23","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2022-23.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","0% (0) of the 663 respondents ... Black, Black British, Caribbean or African")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Academy of Medical Sciences: six Fellows Black or Latin",text:"The Academy's 2020–24 diversity report says 8% of Fellows giving data are from ethnic minority groups and 'only six Fellows' are Black or Latin; race data is missing for 38% of Fellows.",figure:"6 Fellows Black or Latin (2024)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Combined Black-or-Latin figure, not Black only; the source's 'BAME' grouping used for other figures. Second source is the same publisher; no independent coverage of the six-Fellow figure found.",src:[SRCQ("Academy of Medical Sciences diversity report 2020–24","https://acmedsci.ac.uk/file-download/academy-of-medical-sciences-diversity-report-2020-24-final","Supports the Recognition and representation page","there are only six Fellows who are Black or Latin."),SRCQ("Progress revealed in Academy's seventh annual diversity report, 21 Jul 2025","https://acmedsci.ac.uk/news-and-insights/news/progress-revealed-academys-seventh-annual-diversity-report","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Among Council members who provided data, a quarter (25%) are from Black, Asian and/or Minority Ethnic backgrounds.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Academy of Medical Sciences: 29% of new Fellows ethnic minority",text:"Of candidates giving data in 2024, 23% were in the source's 'Black, Asian and/or Minority Ethnic' grouping; 25% of shortlisted candidates and 29% of new Fellows were. No Black-only breakdown is published.",figure:"29% of new Fellows (combined grouping)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Report says differences in shortlisting and election rates are not statistically significant. No Black-only data.",src:[SRCQ("Academy of Medical Sciences diversity report 2020–24","https://acmedsci.ac.uk/file-download/academy-of-medical-sciences-diversity-report-2020-24-final","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Of those candidates who have provided information, 23% are Black, Asian and/or Minority Ethnic."),SRCQ("Research Professional News","https://www.researchprofessionalnews.com/?p=439760","Supports the Recognition and representation page","has welcomed the highest proportion of experts from Black, Asian and minority ethnic backgrounds")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Academy of Engineering: 6.5% ethnic minority Fellows",text:"In 2020 the Academy reported that 6.5% of its Fellowship were from Black, Asian and minority ethnic backgrounds, against 9% of UK engineers, and set a target for half of new Fellows to come from under-represented groups.",figure:"6.5% (combined grouping, 2020)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Source's 'BAME' grouping; no Black-only figure published in these sources. More recent data not found.",src:[SRCQ("Racial parity in engineering – Dr Hayaatun Sillem, RAEng, 20 Oct 2020","https://raeng.org.uk/blogs/racial-parity-in-engineering-looking-beyond-black-history-month/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the Academy Fellowship comprises 6.5% Black, Asian and minority ethnic engineers"),SRCQ("RAEng working to deliver more representative Fellowship – The Chemical Engineer, 10 Aug 2020","https://www.thechemicalengineer.com/news/raeng-working-to-deliver-more-representative-fellowship/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","6.5% black, Asian and minority ethnic (BAME) engineers")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"US National Academy of Sciences does not publish race data",text:"The Journal of Blacks in Higher Education reports that the NAS does not publish its members' race; JBHE identified 4 African Americans among 100 new US members in 2019, of 2,347 active members.",figure:"4 of 100 elected (4%), 2019 (JBHE count)",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"Single publisher (JBHE); its own wording is 'it appears'. Compare US 2020 Census Black alone 12.4%. Treat as indicative. Only one source: the NAS does not publish data to corroborate.",src:[SRCQ("Four Black Scholars Elected to the National Academy of Sciences – JBHE, May 2019","https://jbhe.com/2019/05/four-black-scholars-elected-to-the-national-academy-of-sciences","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The National Academy of Sciences does not publish data on the race or ethnicity of its members.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"funding",h:"Research grants and fellowships",intro:"Funders that publish applications as well as awards let success rates be compared. Where they do, Black applicants succeed less often than White applicants.",points:[
  {h:"No Black British applicants to two Royal Society schemes, 2018–20",text:"Between 2018 and 2020 there were no Black British applicants to the University Research or Dorothy Hodgkin Fellowships; 1% of URF applicants of all nationalities were Black. Black researchers were 2% of 13,405 eligible postdocs.",figure:"0 Black British applicants (2018–20)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("No black applicants for some Royal Society grants for three years – Times Higher Education, 2021","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/711777","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Between 2018 and 2020, there were no black British applicants to the URF or DHF schemes."),SRCQ("Ethnicity in STEM – Royal Society, 25 Mar 2021","https://royalsociety.org/news-resources/projects/trends-ethnic-minorities-stem/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","There is no or very low participation of Black postdoctoral researchers to these schemes.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society 2022–23: 5 offers to 72 Black applicants",text:"Across five schemes (Jan 2022 to Mar 2023), Black or Black British applicants were 4% of applicants (72) and 2% of offers (5), a success rate of 6.9%.",figure:"5 of 72 (6.9%)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Success rate is our calculation from the Society's numbers. Same publisher for both extracts; independent coverage not found.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society diversity data 2022–23","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2022-23.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","0% (0) of the 663 respondents ... Black, Black British, Caribbean or African"),SRCQ("Royal Society diversity data 2022–23 (Newton International Fellowship)","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2022-23.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Newton International Fellowship: Black or Black British applicants 11% (51), offers 4% (2)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society 2023–24: 0 offers to 45 Black applicants",text:"In 2023–24 Research Fellowship rounds, 45 Black or Black British applicants (4%) received no offers, against 77 offers to 630 White applicants (12.2%), 31 to 489 Asian (6.3%) and 14 to 76 Mixed (18.4%).",figure:"0 of 45 (0%) vs White 77 of 630 (12.2%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Rates are our calculations from published numbers. Small numbers; one year. Chemistry World gives pipeline context, not the 2023–24 figures.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society diversity data 2023–24","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2023-24.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black, Black British, Caribbean or African: 2 respondents (0%) of 721"),SRCQ("New Royal Society fellowship to support Black researchers – Chemistry World","https://chemistryworld.com/news/new-royal-society-fellowship-to-support-black-researchers/4018115.article","Supports the Recognition and representation page","4% of Stem students at PhD level were from Black backgrounds")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society 2021: ethnic minority success rate 4.9% vs White 11.5%",text:"In 2021, applicants in the source's 'BME' grouping made 631 applications to six fellowship schemes and won 31 offers (4.9%); White applicants made 866 and won 100 (11.5%). No Black-only figure was published.",figure:"31 of 631 (4.9%) vs 100 of 866 (11.5%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Source's 'BME' grouping. Rates calculated by us from the Society's counts.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society 2021 diversity data report","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/topics/diversity-in-science/2021-diversity-data-report.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black, Black British, Caribbean or African 0% (0) of 671 Fellows and Foreign Members giving ethnicity"),SRCQ("Times Higher Education","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/711777","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Ninety per cent of British applicants to the URF scheme and 87.7 per cent to the DHF scheme were white.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society 2025: Black researchers 1% of URF applicants",text:"A 2025 Royal Society and CRAC benchmarking exercise found Black postdocs were 1% of University Research Fellowship applicants, against under 2% of the UK-national eligible pool and 3% of all nationalities.",figure:"1% of applicants vs 2–3% of eligible pool",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Page gives shares only, no counts or awards; the THE source is the 2021 baseline for comparison.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of applications for Royal Society early-career fellowship programmes – 2025, published 27 Feb 2026","https://www.royalsociety.org/news-resources/publications/2026/early-career-fellowship-applications-diversity/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","(Black researchers) under 2% of UK nationals, and 3% of all nationalities"),SRCQ("Times Higher Education, 2021 (earlier baseline)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/711777","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only 2 per cent of those were from a black background")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society Career Development Fellowship for Black researchers",text:"In 2023 the Royal Society piloted a Career Development Fellowship for researchers of Black heritage: up to 5 a year, each up to £690,000 over 4 years. HESA data show Black people were 4% of STEM PhD students but 2.5% of academic staff (2022).",figure:"Up to 5 awards a year",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Positive-action scheme; outcomes not yet published in sources reviewed.",src:[SRCQ("Chemistry World","https://chemistryworld.com/news/new-royal-society-fellowship-to-support-black-researchers/4018115.article","Supports the Recognition and representation page","falling to 2.5% among academic staff"),SRCQ("The Chemical Engineer, 3 Oct 2023","https://www.thechemicalengineer.com/news/royal-society-launches-fellowship-to-support-underrepresented-black-researchers/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","five scientists a year will get up to £690,000 (US$832,000) spread over four years.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"UKRI: Black lead applicants won 13% of bids, White 34% (2023–24)",text:"UK Research and Innovation's own equalities monitoring for 2023–24 reports an award rate of 13% for Black principal investigators against 34% for White and 25% for Asian; UKRI says the White–Black gap of 21 points is statistically significant. Over five years (2019–20 to 2023–24) the cumulative rate was 14% for Black and 31% for White applicants.",figure:"13% v 34% award rate",year:"2023–24",disputed:false,note:"UKRI counts are rounded to the nearest 5 and small cells suppressed. Times Higher Education reported a 21% Black award rate in 2021–22 on a very small base (80 Black applicants), so single-year rates swing widely. The 2020–21 report is a second, earlier UKRI publication showing the same 13% figure for that year (White 29%).",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Applicants from the White ethnic group had a significantly higher award rate (34%) compared to those from Asian (25%) and Black (13%) ethnic groups."),SRCQ("UKRI diversity data for funding applicants and awardees 2020 to 2021 update","https://www.ukri.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/UKRI-151222-Main-report-UKRI-diversity-data-for-funding-applicants-and-awardees-2020-to-2021-update.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The award rate for PIs was lowest for the black and other ethnic groups at 13% and 12% respectively."),SRCQ("Black academics 'give up' on UKRI research funding (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/731904","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black PIs: 8%, 12%, and 12% in the three years before 2021-22 ... White PIs: 29%, 30%, and 29%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"UKRI: 90 Black lead awardees against 10,510 White over five years",text:"UKRI's 2023–24 monitoring gives cumulative five-year counts of principal-investigator awardees: 90 Black and 10,510 White (2019–20 to 2023–24). Black principal investigators are under 1% of UKRI principal investigators, which UKRI says is below the HESA academic and labour-market share.",figure:"90 Black v 10,510 White awardees",year:"2019–20 to 2023–24",disputed:false,note:"Table extract via WebFetch; rounded to nearest 5. Denominator of all awardees not extracted, so no share is computed here. England and Wales population baseline: Black 4.0% (Census 2021).",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Five-year cumulative awardees (table 12): Black 90, White 10,510 (UKRI overall)."),SRCQ("UKRI publishes ethnicity analysis of funding applicants and awardees","https://www.ukri.org/news/ukri-publishes-ethnicity-analysis-of-funding-applicants-and-awardees","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black PIs account for less than 1% of PIs, which is below the HESA estimate of academic as well as the labour market share.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"UKRI: Black lead researchers' median award £161,000 v £367,000 White",text:"UKRI's median award to Black principal investigators was £161,000 against £367,000 for White in 2019–20; by 2023–24 the gap had nearly closed (£439,000 v £448,000). Times Higher Education reported that in 2020–21 Black principal investigators were awarded £5 million of £85 million requested (6%), against £1.4 billion of £4.4 billion (31%) for White.",figure:"£161k v £367k median (2019–20)",year:"2019–20 to 2023–24",disputed:false,note:"Medians on small Black counts are volatile year to year.",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","White principal investigators had the highest median award amount in all years except 2022 to 2023."),SRCQ("Black academics 'give up' on UKRI research funding (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/731904","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 2020-21, 6% of the funds Black PIs applied for were awarded (£5 million of £85 million). For white PIs, 31% was awarded (£1.4 billion of £4.4 billion).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black lead applicants to UKRI halved to 80 in 2021–22",text:"Times Higher Education, using UKRI data, reported that Black principal-investigator applicants fell from 160 in 2020–21 to 80 in 2021–22, the lowest for at least six years; at the Medical Research Council they fell from 85 to 20. Researchers quoted argued Black academics were 'giving up' on applying.",figure:"160 to 80 applicants",year:"2020–21 to 2021–22",disputed:false,note:"The 'giving up' explanation is the interviewees' argument, not a UKRI finding. Counts are rounded to the nearest 5.",src:[SRCQ("Black academics 'give up' on UKRI research funding (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/731904","Supports the Recognition and representation page","80 Black principal investigators (PIs) applied in 2021-22, down from 160 the year before ... the lowest figure for at least six years."),SRCQ("UKRI diversity data for funding applicants and awardees 2020 to 2021 update","https://www.ukri.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/UKRI-151222-Main-report-UKRI-diversity-data-for-funding-applicants-and-awardees-2020-to-2021-update.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The percentage of PI grant holders from the black, mixed, and other ethnic groups was lower, at 1%")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Broken Pipeline: 245 of 19,868 UKRI PhD studentships (1.2%) to Black",text:"Leading Routes' 'The Broken Pipeline' (September 2019), using a freedom of information response from UKRI, found that 245 of 19,868 research council PhD studentships over 2016–17 to 2018–19 went to Black or Black Mixed students (1.2%), of whom 30 were Black Caribbean.",figure:"245 of 19,868 (1.2%)",year:"2016–17 to 2018–19",disputed:false,note:"Many studentship records lacked ethnicity, so the share of known-ethnicity starts would be somewhat higher. 'Black Mixed' is Leading Routes' grouping. The original report PDF was not fetched; figures come via three secondary write-ups.",src:[SRCQ("Race Update 1 – Highlights from 2019 Reports (Royal Historical Society)","https://blog.royalhistsoc.org/?p=1195","Supports the Recognition and representation page","of the total 19,868 PhD funded studentships awarded by UKRI research councils collectively ... 245 (1.2%) were awarded to Black or Black Mixed students ... with just 30 of those being from Black Caribbean backgrounds"),SRCQ("Black students aren't getting funded PhDs – who's responsible for fixing that? (Wonkhe)","https://wonkhe.com/blogs/black-students-arent-getting-funded-phds-whos-responsible-for-fixing-that/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","245, or 1.2 per cent were awarded to Black students or Black mixed students"),SRCQ("Barriers to Black PhD students (YCEDE)","https://ycede.ac.uk/barriers-to-black-phd-students-a-reflection-on-progress-and-the-work-that-still-needs-to-be-done-by-ayo-barley-chair-ycede-external-advisory-board/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","just 1.2 per cent of the nearly 20,000 funded PhD studentship")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"UKRI studentship starts: about 2% Black in 2023–24",text:"Of 5,165 new UKRI-funded studentship starts in 2023–24, UKRI reports 2% as Black and 46% as White; ethnicity was undisclosed for 26% and unknown for a further 9%. Council shares ranged from 1% (AHRC) to 5% (ESRC).",figure:"2% of 5,165 starts",year:"2023–24",disputed:false,note:"Single publisher (UKRI); the 2019 Broken Pipeline figure (1.2%) is the earlier independent comparator. Percentages are of all starts including unknowns, so not directly comparable with Leading Routes. No Black count published; 2% of 5,165 is roughly 100 (our arithmetic).",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In the 2023 to 2024 financial year 5,165 new studentship starts were reported with UKRI funding."),SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 2023 to 2024 ethnicity was not disclosed for 26% of studentship starts, and unknown for another 9%, so our understanding of ethnicity for studentships is partial.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wellcome: UK minority-ethnic success rate 8% v White 14%",text:"Wellcome's grant funding data report for 2019–20 found that, over four years, UK-based applicants from Black, Asian and minority ethnic groups (Wellcome's grouping) had an 8% success rate against 14% for UK-based White applicants. Wellcome says it gave no awards to UK applicants who identified as Black or Black British in 2019–20.",figure:"0 UK Black awards (2019–20); 8% v 14%",year:"2016–17 to 2019–20",disputed:false,note:"The 'no awards' figure is Wellcome's data as reported by Chemistry World and Pesquisa; the Wellcome page fetched did not show that sentence.",src:[SRCQ("Grant funding data 2019 to 2020 (Wellcome)","https://wellcome.org/insights/reports/grant-funding-data-2019-2020","Supports the Recognition and representation page","differ by ethnicity (8% for UK-based Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic applicants, 14% for UK-based White applicants)"),SRCQ("Chemistry World","https://www.chemistryworld.com/4016089.article","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 2019/20, no awards were given by Wellcome to UK applicants who identified as Black or Black British"),SRCQ("Battle against institutional racism in science (Revista Pesquisa FAPESP)","https://revistapesquisa.fapesp.br/en/battle-against-institutional-racism-in-science/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 2019/2020, no awards were given by Wellcome to UK applicants who identified as Black")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wellcome 2024–25: Black or Black British 9.7% of UK awards",text:"Wellcome's 2024–25 funding data says 9.7% of UK awards went to Black or Black British lead applicants (14% globally). UK success rates on competitive schemes were 15.4% for White lead applicants and 12.2% for all other groups combined; Wellcome does not publish a Black-only rate.",figure:"9.7% of UK awards",year:"2024–25",disputed:true,note:"Single publisher (Wellcome; two pages). The 9.7% UK figure is far above UKRI's under 1% and should be checked against Wellcome's chart before quoting: it may reflect targeted schemes or small numbers. Ethnicity known for 87.3% of lead applicants. 'Globally' includes Africa-based applicants, so the 14% is not a UK diversity measure.",src:[SRCQ("Funding data 2024 (Wellcome)","https://wellcome.org/research-funding/funding-portfolio/funding-data-2024","Supports the Recognition and representation page","9.7% were to Black or Black British"),SRCQ("Funding data 2023 (Wellcome)","https://wellcome.org/research-funding/funding-portfolio/funding-data-2023","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Award rates for competitive schemes remain lower for applicants of Black, Asian, Mixed and Other ethnicities")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society: no Black UK applicants to URF or DHF, 2018–2020",text:"The Royal Society's own application data found no applications from Black UK nationals to its University Research Fellowship (URF) and no Black applicants to the Dorothy Hodgkin Fellowship in biological sciences, 2018–2020. Times Higher Education reported that 1% of URF applicants of all nationalities were Black, from an eligible pool that was 2% Black.",figure:"0 Black UK URF applicants",year:"2018–2020",disputed:false,note:"THE's wording ('URF or DHF') is broader than the Royal Society's (URF; DHF biological sciences). Award counts not published.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity application data for UK early career fellowship programmes (Royal Society, 2021)","https://royalsociety.org:443/-/media/policy/publications/2021/trends-ethnic-minorities-stem/diversity-application-data-for-uk-early-career-fellowship-programmes.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","There were no applications from Black UK nationals to the URF scheme"),SRCQ("No black applicants for some Royal Society grants for three years (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/711777","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Between 2018 and 2020, there were no black British applicants to the URF or DHF schemes.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society: Black about 1% of URF applicants; under 5 awards",text:"The Royal Society's 2025 report (application years 2020–21 to 2022–23) says Black researchers were around 1% of URF applicants and under 2% of the UK-national eligible pool; there have now been URF awards (fewer than five) to Black applicants, and no Black UK applicants to the Dorothy Hodgkin scheme.",figure:"c.1% of applicants; <5 awards",year:"2020–21 to 2022–23",disputed:false,note:"Single publisher (Royal Society: report and its web page). No Black-specific success rate published; counts suppressed below 5.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity of applications for Royal Society early-career fellowship programmes – 2025 (report)","https://www.royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/publications/2026/diversity-applications-royals-society-early-career-fellowship-programmes-2025.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","There have now been URF awards (fewer than five) to Black applicants."),SRCQ("Diversity of applications ... 2025 (Royal Society web page)","https://www.royalsociety.org/news-resources/publications/2026/early-career-fellowship-applications-diversity/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black postdoctoral researchers, in particular, comprised a very small number of applicants to the URF scheme (1%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"British Academy: no Black-only fellowship data found",text:"No published Black-specific application or award figures were found for British Academy Postdoctoral Fellowships. For its Small Research Grants partial-randomisation trial, the Academy reported that Black, Asian and other minority ethnic candidates (combined) were 27% of successful applicants, up from 18% under the old process.",figure:"no published count (Black only)",year:"2022–23",disputed:false,note:"The British Academy page was found by search but not fetched; the 27% is from THE. Combined minority grouping only; not about postdoctoral fellowships.",src:[SRCQ("More minority academics win funding in UK random grant trial (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/728190","Supports the Recognition and representation page","27% of successful applicants, versus 18% under the old scheme"),SRCQ("Promising results from first year of innovative grant awarding trial (British Academy)","https://www.thebritishacademy.ac.uk/news/promising-results-from-first-year-of-innovative-grant-awarding-trial/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","[page listed in search; not fetched]")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Leverhulme, BHF, ERC: no published Black-specific counts",text:"No published Black-specific applicant or award data were found for the Leverhulme Trust or the European Research Council (which publishes gender, not ethnicity, monitoring). The British Heart Foundation says individual ethnic groups were too small to analyse and its funding committees had no members from a Black background (2020–2023).",figure:"no published count",year:"2020–2023",disputed:false,note:"Absence of evidence from searches on 25 October 2026; the ERC point rests on its equality plan covering gender only and should be treated as 'not found' rather than confirmed.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity data in research funding (British Heart Foundation)","https://www.bhf.org.uk/what-we-do/equality-diversity-and-inclusion/diversity-data-in-research-funding","Supports the Recognition and representation page","We could not look at other individual ethnic groups due to the numbers being too small to study."),SRCQ("ERC adopts new Gender Equality Plan (ERC)","https://erc.europa.eu/news-events/magazine/erc-adopts-new-gender-equality-plan","Supports the Recognition and representation page","[page listed in search; not fetched]")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"NIH: Black applicants 10 points less likely to win R01s (2000–06)",text:"Ginther and colleagues (Science, 2011), analysing NIH R01 applications from fiscal years 2000–2006, found Black applicants were 10 percentage points less likely than White applicants to be funded after controlling for education, employer, experience and publications. NIH's own FAQ on the study confirms the finding.",figure:"10 percentage-point gap",year:"2000–2006",disputed:false,note:"US baseline: Black alone 12.4% (2020 Census).",src:[SRCQ("FAQ on NIH-commissioned study on race, ethnicity and NIH research awards (NIH)","https://www.nih.gov/node/26636","Supports the Recognition and representation page","black applicants from 2000-2006 were 10 percentage points less likely than white applicants"),SRCQ("Black scientists less likely than white counterparts to receive NIH funding (University of Kansas)","https://news.ku.edu/news/article/2011/08/18/black-scientists-less-likely-white-counterparts-receive-nih-funding-study-shows","Supports the Recognition and representation page","a 10 percentage point gap in research funding")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"NIH R01: Black applications 1.4% of total, funded 17.1% v 29.3%",text:"In the 2011 Ginther data, applications from Black principal investigators were 1.4% of new R01 applications and were funded at 17.1%, against 29.3% for White applicants (69.9% of applications) — a 1.7-fold White advantage, as summarised in eLife (2021).",figure:"17.1% v 29.3%; 1.4% of applications",year:"2000–2006",disputed:true,note:"Sources differ slightly (16% v 17.1%), probably raw v adjusted or different denominators. Black share of applications (1.4%) against a US Black population of 12.4%.",src:[SRCQ("Racial inequity in grant funding from the US National Institutes of Health (eLife, 2021)","https://elifesciences.org/articles/65697","Supports the Recognition and representation page","funded at a rate of 17.1% ... funded at a rate of 29.3%"),SRCQ("Racial disparities in funding at NIH (Center for Genetics and Society)","https://www.geneticsandsociety.org/biopolitical-times/racial-disparities-funding-nih","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only 16% of those from black scientists ... 29% of applications from whites were funded")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"NIH follow-up: Black 10.7% v White 17.7% R01 award rate (2011–15)",text:"Hoppe and colleagues (Science Advances, 2019), an NIH study of 157,549 R01 applications from 2011–2015, found award rates of 10.7% for African American/Black applicants against 17.7% for White applicants. Black applicants submitted about 1.5% of applications, and topic choice explained part of the gap.",figure:"10.7% v 17.7%",year:"FY2011–2015",disputed:false,note:"Paper PDF hosted on a university course page.",src:[SRCQ("Topic choice contributes to the lower rate of NIH awards to African-American/black scientists (Science Advances, 2019)","https://www.stat.cmu.edu/~brian/valerie/617-2022/617-2021/0%20-%20NIH%20Peer%20review/Topic%20Choice%20-%20eaaw7238.full.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","10.7% AA/B versus 17.7% WH in FY 2011–2015"),SRCQ("What's behind the research funding gap for Black scientists (NPR Illinois)","https://www.nprillinois.org/health-harvest/2019-10-18/whats-behind-the-research-funding-gap-for-black-scientists","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black applicants submitted only 1.5% of the total applications for these R01s")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Topic choice explains part of the NIH gap: a contested reading",text:"The 2019 NIH study argued that Black applicants cluster in topics (for example community and population health) with lower award rates, explaining part of the gap. Taffe and Gilpin (eLife, 2021) countered that the 1.7-fold White advantage persisted and estimated about 119 White applications scored in the 35th–59th percentile were funded against none from Black applicants.",figure:"1.7-fold advantage persists",year:"2011–2015",disputed:true,note:"The 119 v 0 estimate is eLife authors' own, derived from Hoppe et al. Table 1 via a blog post; treat as an estimate.",src:[SRCQ("Racial inequity in grant funding from the US NIH (eLife, 2021)","https://elifesciences.org/articles/65697","Supports the Recognition and representation page","~119 applications from white PIs with scores in the 35th–59th percentile range were funded ... zero applications from Black PIs"),SRCQ("NPR Illinois","https://www.nprillinois.org/health-harvest/2019-10-18/whats-behind-the-research-funding-gap-for-black-scientists","Supports the Recognition and representation page","White applicants continue to receive funding at nearly twice the rate of black applicants.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Black professors: 250 of 25,670 UK professors (just under 1%)",text:"Times Higher Education, reporting HESA staff data, says 250 of 25,670 UK professors were Black in 2023–24 (just under 1%), up from 210 the year before and 165 in 2021–22. HESA's 2021–22 release put Black professors at 165, under 1%, and 88% White.",figure:"250 of 25,670 (0.97%)",year:"2023–24",disputed:false,note:"Denominator may include undeclared ethnicity. England and Wales Black population 4.0%; ratio about 1:4. Context for fellowship pipelines.",src:[SRCQ("Number of non-EU academics in UK rises again (Times Higher Education)","https://www.timeshighereducation.com/news/number-non-eu-academics-uk-rises-again","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Of the 25,670 professors in the UK, 250 were black in 2023-24"),SRCQ("Higher Education Staff Statistics: UK 2021/22 (HESA)","https://hesa.ac.uk/node/140761","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Among professors who declared their ethnicity, 165 were black."),SRCQ("Higher Education Staff Statistics: UK 2022/23 (HESA)","https://www.hesa.ac.uk/node/142008","Supports the Recognition and representation page","represented only 1% of all professors")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Society Black-heritage fellowship: 8 fellows a year, 2024–25",text:"The Royal Society's Career Development Fellowship, piloted for researchers of Black and Mixed Black heritage, named eight fellows in its first cohort (2024) and eight in its second (2025), each with up to £690,000 over four years. Applicant numbers are not published.",figure:"8 + 8 fellows",year:"2024–2025",disputed:false,note:"Counts from the Royal Society only; Research Professional confirms scheme and value, not cohort size.",src:[SRCQ("First cohort of Royal Society Career Development Fellowship pilots","https://royalsociety.org/news/2024/09/career-development-fellowship-cohort-1","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Eight outstanding researchers have been announced as the first cohort"),SRCQ("Second cohort of Royal Society Career Development Fellowships","https://royalsociety.org/news/2025/10/career-development-fellowship-cohort-2","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Eight exceptional early career researchers have been announced as the second cohort."),SRCQ("Royal Society launches new fellowships for Black researchers (Research Professional News)","https://researchprofessionalnews.com/rr-news-uk-charities-and-societies-2023-9-royal-society-launches-new-fellowships-for-black-researchers","Supports the Recognition and representation page","starting with those from Black heritage backgrounds")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Stormzy Scholarship: 81 students funded by 2026",text:"The University of Cambridge and HSBC UK say 81 students will have received a Stormzy Scholarship by 2026; Cambridge's UK Black undergraduate admissions rose from 61 in 2018 to 141 in 2022, which both credit partly to the scheme.",figure:"81 scholars; 61 to 141 admissions",year:"2018–2026",disputed:false,note:"81 is a projection including funded future places. HSBC's own release says both 30 and 36 new scholarships; the causal link to admissions is the sponsors' claim.",src:[SRCQ("Stormzy Scholarships (University of Cambridge)","https://cam.ac.uk/node/241081","Supports the Recognition and representation page","a total of 81 students will have received a Stormzy Scholarship by 2026"),SRCQ("HSBC UK doubles support for Stormzy Scholars","https://www.about.hsbc.co.uk/news-and-media/hsbc-uk-doubles-support-for-stormzy-scholars-at-the-university-of-cambridge","Supports the Recognition and representation page","141 Black students admitted in 2022 (131% increase compared to 2018)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"10,000 Black Interns: over 4,000 placed by 2022",text:"Raconteur reported in September 2022 that the 10,000 Black Interns programme, founded in 2020, had given more than 4,000 young people internships and that almost a third of the 2021 cohort got full-time jobs with hosts. An earlier report cited almost 500 firms signed up and a target of 2,000 placements a year for five years.",figure:"4,000+ interns (2022)",year:"2020–2022",disputed:false,note:"No foundation-published outcome report fetched. A University of Surrey student blog says 'over 10,000 paid internship opportunities' in five years, but the foundation now also runs schemes for other groups, so not comparable.",src:[SRCQ("10,000 Black Interns and diversity hiring (Raconteur)","https://www.raconteur.net/hr/diversity-inclusion/10000-black-interns-diversity-hiring/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","given more than 4,000 young people a leg up on the career ladder"),SRCQ("Almost 500 firms have joined 10,000 Black Interns campaign (Insurance Business)","https://www.insurancebusinessmag.com/uk/news/breaking-news/almost-500-firms-have-joined-10000-black-interns-campaign-241599.aspx","Supports the Recognition and representation page","2,000 placements each year for five years to meet the 10,000 goal")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Amos Bursary: no published cumulative total found",text:"The Amos Bursary's 2020 impact report gives cohort figures (15 students joined in 2020; 23 in 2019) and outcomes (80% reached first- or second-choice universities; seven Oxbridge places), but no cumulative total of students supported.",figure:"15 joined (2020)",year:"2019–2020",disputed:false,note:"Single source (the charity's own report); no independent corroboration found. Link only, per brief.",src:[SRCQ("Amos Bursary Impact Report 2020","https://amosbursary.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/AMOS-Impact-Report-2020_revised_3.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Fifteen students joined us in 2020."),SRCQ("Amos Bursary Impact Report 2020","https://amosbursary.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/AMOS-Impact-Report-2020_revised_3.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","80% of the students achieved their grades to secure their first or second choice university places.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"scholarships",h:"Scholarships and bursaries",intro:"Most international scholarships publish recipients by country, not ethnicity. The figures below are the ones published.",points:[
  {h:"Rhodes Trust: Black scholars about 20% of recent cohorts",text:"The Rhodes Trust states that Black scholars of African descent from Africa, the Caribbean and North America make up 20% of recent cohorts, from over 100 scholarships a year; six African constituencies fund 19 scholarships annually.",figure:"c.20% of recent cohorts",year:"recent cohorts (to 2026)",disputed:false,note:"Single publisher (Rhodes Trust); 'recent cohorts' not defined, no numerator published. Caribbean constituency numbers not extracted. World baseline: no agreed figure.",src:[SRCQ("Race and the Rhodes Scholarship (Rhodes Trust)","https://www.rhodeshouse.ox.ac.uk/impact-legacy/legacy-equity-inclusion/race-and-the-rhodes-scholarship/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black Scholars of African descent – from Africa, the Caribbean, and North America – comprise 20% of recent cohorts."),SRCQ("Key facts about the Rhodes Scholarship (Rhodes Trust)","https://www.rhodeshouse.ox.ac.uk/about/key-facts/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","over 100 Scholarships a year")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"US Rhodes: 6 of 32 Black in 2022, 5 of 32 in 2026 class",text:"Independent counts of the US constituency: Diverse: Issues in Higher Education reported six Black students in the 32-member 2022 American class; the Journal of Blacks in Higher Education said 'it appears' five of 32 were Black in the class announced in November 2025.",figure:"6 of 32 (2022); 5 of 32 (2026)",year:"2022–2026",disputed:false,note:"The Trust does not publish race by US class; these are media counts, so treat as approximate. Shares: 18.8% and 15.6% against US Black population 12.4%.",src:[SRCQ("2022 American Rhodes class features six Black students (Diverse)","https://www.diverseeducation.com/news-roundup/article/15281943/2022-american-rhodes-class-feature-six-black-students-and-most-women-ever-selected","Supports the Recognition and representation page","includes six Black students and 22 women"),SRCQ("Five Blacks among the latest cohort of 32 Rhodes Scholars (JBHE)","https://jbhe.com/2025/11/five-blacks-among-the-latest-cohort-of-32-rhodes-scholars-from-the-united-states/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","it appears that five are Black")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Rhodes: first Black scholar Alain Locke, 1907; next US Black 1963",text:"Alain Locke, selected from the United States in 1907, was the first Black Rhodes Scholar; the Trust itself attributes a 56-year gap before the next African-American scholars in 1963 to 'entrenched racism'. Scholarships in South Africa were reserved for White applicants until protests from 1970.",figure:"1907; gap to 1963",year:"1907–1963",disputed:false,note:"Milestone, two independent sources.",src:[SRCQ("Race and the Rhodes Scholarship (Rhodes Trust)","https://www.rhodeshouse.ox.ac.uk/impact-legacy/legacy-equity-inclusion/race-and-the-rhodes-scholarship/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black Rhodes Scholar, Alain Locke, was selected out of the United States in 1907"),SRCQ("Alain Locke (Smithsonian NMAAHC)","https://nmaahc.si.edu/alain-locke","Supports the Recognition and representation page","became the first African American to be selected as a Rhodes Scholar")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Marshall: Black scholar numbers not published",text:"The UK government does not publish the ethnicity of Marshall Scholars. The Journal of Blacks in Higher Education estimated one African American among 40 scholars in the class announced in 2017, and four in a later class.",figure:"1 of 40 (JBHE estimate)",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"Both counts from one publisher (JBHE) and inferred, so no published count in the brief's sense; shown as context only.",src:[SRCQ("It appears there is only one African American among this year's 40 Marshall Scholars (JBHE)","https://jbhe.com/?p=69948","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the British government does not publicize the race or ethnicity of Marshall Scholars"),SRCQ("Four African Americans win Marshall Scholarships (JBHE)","https://jbhe.com/2017/12/four-african-americans-win-marshall-scholarships-2017","Supports the Recognition and representation page","[headline as listed in search]")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Fulbright US Students: Black recipients tripled to 99 (5.2%)",text:"The PIE News reported that Black recipients of the US Fulbright Student Program rose from 33 in 2005–06 to 99 in 2015–16, 5.2% of the cohort; 7% of US Scholar Program recipients were Black in 2015–16. The Chronicle of Higher Education had reported in 2008 that only 10.6% of US student recipients were Black or Hispanic combined.",figure:"99 (5.2%) in 2015–16",year:"2005–06 to 2015–16",disputed:false,note:"Figures attributed to the US State Department's programme data by PIE; denominator for 2015–16 implied about 1,900. US Black population 12.4%.",src:[SRCQ("US Fulbright doubles down on diversity aims (The PIE News)","https://thepienews.com/us-fulbright-doubles-down-on-diversity-aims/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the number has tripled to 99, or 5.2% of the cohort"),SRCQ("Fulbright emphasizes diversity among its fellows (Chronicle of Higher Education, via UW–Madison)","https://international.wisc.edu/fulbright-emphasizes-diversity-among-its-fellows-the-chronicle-of-higher-education/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only 10.6 percent of American students who received Fulbrights are black or Hispanic")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Chevening and Commonwealth: published by country, not ethnicity",text:"Chevening and Commonwealth Scholarships report by nationality, not ethnicity. The FCDO told Parliament in February 2026 that Nigeria, Egypt and South Africa were among the top ten Chevening countries; the Commonwealth Scholarship Commission welcomed 647 new scholars from 35 countries in 2024–25 but its regional chart did not extract cleanly.",figure:"no published ethnicity count",year:"2024–2026",disputed:false,note:"Nationality is not ethnicity. The CSC report has a 'new awards by region' chart (Southern, West, East Africa, Caribbean) — check the PDF chart before quoting regional numbers. The Cable (Nigeria) says about 1,500 Nigerians have held Chevening since 1983, unattributed.",src:[SRCQ("Chevening Scholarships Programme: Finance (parliamentary answer, 24 Feb 2026)","https://westminsterbrief.co.uk/archive/pq/113409","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The top 10 countries in terms of scholar numbers were Indonesia, Brazil, Pakistan ... Nigeria, Egypt, South Africa and Malaysia"),SRCQ("Commonwealth Scholarship Commission Annual Report 2025","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69402965cfacd5e888491ffc/Commonwealth-Scholarship-Commission-Annual-Report-2025.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","We were delighted to welcome 647 new Scholars and Fellows from 35 Commonwealth countries in the 2024-2025 academic year.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Gates Cambridge and MacArthur: no published Black counts",text:"Gates Cambridge publishes nationalities (95 scholars from 35 countries in the class of 2025) but not ethnicity. No authoritative published count of Black MacArthur Fellows was found; a 2026 opinion piece's own tally is rejected.",figure:"no published count",year:"2025–2026",disputed:false,note:"The MacArthur book (Kaba) claims Black men are over-represented relative to their adult share but its numbers were not available; not counted.",src:[SRCQ("Gates Cambridge class of 2025 (University of Cambridge)","https://www.cam.ac.uk/stories/gates-cambridge-class-2025","Supports the Recognition and representation page","95 new scholars have been selected and will form the Class of 2025"),SRCQ("Bridging the race and gender gaps: MacArthur Fellows 1981 to 2018 (Adonis & Abbey)","https://www.adonis-abbey.com/book_detail.php?bookid=259","Supports the Recognition and representation page","with Black men, Asian men, and Native American women having higher proportions than their adult proportions")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"screen",h:"Film, television and stage",intro:"Long-run counts of Oscar and BAFTA nominations come from a single analyst and are labelled. Totals the hub added up from a source's category counts say so in their notes.",points:[
  {h:"Black actors: 69 of 1,021 Oscar acting nominations, 1970 to 2020",text:"Pearn Kandola counted Black nominees across the four Oscar acting categories over 50 ceremonies (1970 to 2020): 20 of 255 lead actor, 11 of 255 lead actress, 19 of 256 supporting actor and 19 of 255 supporting actress nominations. Taken together that is 69 of 1,021 (6.8%), against a US Black population share of 12.4% to 14.2%.",figure:"69 of 1,021 (6.8%)",year:"1970–2020",disputed:true,note:"The 69 and 1,021 totals are our sum of Pearn Kandola's four published category counts. Pearn Kandola does not explain how ethnicity was assigned. The Dallas Morning News count covers a different period and found Black performers had 10% of acting nominations and 15% of wins, so the two counts disagree. Treat both as indicative.",src:[SRCQ("The Dallas Morning News, Race for the Oscars (2016)","https://interactives.dallasnews.com/2016/oscars-diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","these actors and actresses account for 10 percent of best acting nominations and 15 percent of the wins")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"17 Black acting winners at the Oscars, 1970 to 2020",text:"Pearn Kandola counted 17 Black winners in the four acting categories from 1970 to 2020: 3 lead actor, 1 lead actress, 6 supporting actor and 7 supporting actress. With four awards a year over 50 ceremonies, that is about 17 of 200 (8.5%).",figure:"17 of about 200 (8.5%)",year:"1970–2020",disputed:false,note:"The denominator of 200 is the number of acting awards (4 categories × 50 ceremonies), not a figure Pearn Kandola published. AMC is used only to corroborate individual winners, not the total.",src:[SRCQ("AMC Theatres, Historic moments for Black actors at the Academy Awards","https://www.amctheatres.com/amc-scene/historic-moments-for-black-actors-at-the-academy-awards","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black actor to do so")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Halle Berry is still the only Black winner of Best Actress",text:"Halle Berry won Best Actress in 2002 and is still the only Black woman to have won it. Pearn Kandola counted 11 Black lead actress nominations and 1 Black winner from 1970 to 2020. The 2025 and 2026 Best Actress winners did not change this (PBS winners list, 2026).",figure:"1 Black winner (11 nominations, 1970–2020)",year:"2002–2026",disputed:false,note:"Halle Berry is named as a public prize winner in her public role.",src:[SRCQ("ABC7, Halle Berry says she's 'heartbroken'","https://abc7.com/11535287","Supports the Recognition and representation page","remains the only Black woman to have won the award"),SRCQ("PBS NewsHour, full list of 2026 Academy Awards winners","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/amp/arts/heres-a-full-list-of-2026-academy-awards-winners","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Best Actress: Jessie Buckley, \"Hamnet\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"First Black winners: Hattie McDaniel (1940) and Sidney Poitier (1964)",text:"Hattie McDaniel was the first Black person to win an Oscar, for Best Supporting Actress at the 1940 ceremony (a 1939 film). Sidney Poitier was the first Black actor to win Best Actor, at the 1964 ceremony for Lilies of the Field.",figure:"Firsts: 1940 and 1964",year:"1940; 1964",disputed:false,note:"Both have died. Sources give the film year (1939; 1963) or the ceremony year (1940; 1964), which explains any one-year difference.",src:[SRCQ("AMC Theatres, Historic moments for Black actors at the Academy Awards","https://www.amctheatres.com/amc-scene/historic-moments-for-black-actors-at-the-academy-awards","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black person to win an Oscar"),SRCQ("Gold Radio, When Sidney Poitier made history","https://www.goldradio.com/news/tv-film/sidney-poitier-first-black-best-actor-oscars/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black star to win Best Actor at the Academy Awards")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"#OscarsSoWhite: all 20 acting nominees were white in 2015 and 2016",text:"For two ceremonies in a row, 2015 and 2016, all 20 Oscar acting nominees were white. In 2017, 7 of the 20 were not white.",figure:"0 of 20 nominees of colour (2016)",year:"2015–2017",disputed:false,note:"Neither source gives a Black-only count for 2017.",src:[SRCQ("CBS News, #OscarsSoWhite: Academy Awards slammed","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/oscars-2016-oscarssowhite-academy-awards-slammed-for-lack-of-diversity/?ftag=","Supports the Recognition and representation page","all 20 Academy Awards nominees for acting were white"),SRCQ("Al Jazeera, Oscar nominations more diverse after #OscarsSoWhite row","https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2017/1/24/oscar-nominations-more-diverse-after-oscarssowhite-row","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Seven of the 20 actors announced as nominees on Tuesday are not white.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"USC Annenberg: nominees of colour rose from 8% to 17% after 2015",text:"The USC Annenberg Inclusion Initiative found that nominees from underrepresented racial or ethnic groups made up 8% of nominees in 2008 to 2015 and 17% in 2016 to 2023. Across all 95 ceremonies and 19 categories, 6% of nominees were people of colour and fewer than 2% were women of colour.",figure:"8% → 17% of nominees",year:"2008–2023",disputed:false,note:"Neither source gives a separate Black figure or raw denominators. The finding that 20% of all Black nominees were in Original Song comes from Rolling Stone's report of the study only.",src:[SRCQ("USC School of Dramatic Arts, USC programs trace Oscars past","https://dramaticarts.usc.edu/usc-programs-trace-oscars-past-and-work-to-change-oscars-future/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","17% of nominees were from underrepresented racial/ethnic groups"),SRCQ("Rolling Stone, Oscars still #SoWhite","https://www.rollingstone.com/tv-movies/tv-movie-news/oscars-still-so-white-diversity-improvements-study-1234689140/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Twenty percent of all Black nominees have been in the Best Original Song category.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Best Director: 7 Black nominees and no Black winner in 98 ceremonies",text:"In 2026 Ryan Coogler became the seventh Black filmmaker nominated for Best Director. No Black filmmaker has won the award, and no Black woman has been nominated. Paul Thomas Anderson won in 2026.",figure:"0 winners; 7 nominees",year:"1929–2026",disputed:false,note:"theGrio counts the six men nominated before Coogler, so the two counts agree. Neither source gives the total number of Best Director nominees.",src:[SRCQ("TheWrap, Oscars 2026 Diversity Report","https://www.thewrap.com/industry-news/awards/oscars-2026-diversity-report-sinners-chloe-zhao-wagner-moura/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","just the seventh Black filmmaker to be nominated in this category"),SRCQ("theGrio, Ryan Coogler honours the 6 Black director nominees","https://thegrio.com/2026/02/26/ryan-coogler-oscars-best-director-black-nominees","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Six Black men have been nominated for Best Director Oscars; none have won."),SRCQ("Global News, 2026 Oscars winners list","https://globalnews.ca/news/11729892/2026-oscars-winners-list/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Best Director: Paul Thomas Anderson")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"2026: 4 of 20 acting nominees were Black and one Black actor won",text:"At the 2026 Oscars, 4 of the 20 acting nominees were Black, and 6 of the 20 came from underrepresented groups. Michael B. Jordan won Best Actor. TheWrap reported that 10 nominations for Sinners went to Black artists, equalling the record set by Judas and the Black Messiah in 2021.",figure:"4 of 20 (20%)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The 4 Black acting nominees are those named by TheWrap and the Atlanta Tribune (Jordan, Mosaku, Lindo, Taylor); we did not classify anyone. 4 of 20 is a single year, not a trend.",src:[SRCQ("TheWrap, Oscars 2026 Diversity Report","https://www.thewrap.com/industry-news/awards/oscars-2026-diversity-report-sinners-chloe-zhao-wagner-moura/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Ten of those nominations went to Black artists"),SRCQ("Atlanta Tribune, 2026 Oscars: the Black nominees","https://atlantatribune.com/2026/01/27/2026-oscars-the-black-nominees/?amp=1","Supports the Recognition and representation page","several Black stars earned nods"),SRCQ("PBS NewsHour, 2026 winners","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/amp/arts/heres-a-full-list-of-2026-academy-awards-winners","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Best Actor: Michael B. Jordan, \"Sinners\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"2026: first Black winner of the Oscar for cinematography",text:"In 2026 Autumn Durald Arkapaw (Sinners) became the first woman, and the first Black person, to win the Oscar for cinematography. Only three women had been nominated in the category before her.",figure:"First in the category's history",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Variety describes her as 'the first woman of color to win' and does not use the word 'Black'. We record her heritage as the sources state it.",src:[SRCQ("AP via WDIV ClickOnDetroit","https://www.clickondetroit.com/entertainment/2026/03/16/autumn-durald-arkapaw-becomes-first-woman-to-win-an-oscar-for-cinematography/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","She is also the first Black person to win the category."),SRCQ("Global News, 2026 Oscars winners list","https://globalnews.ca/news/11729892/2026-oscars-winners-list/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","first Black and Asian woman"),SRCQ("Variety Australia","https://au.variety.com/2026/film/news/autumn-arkapaw-cinematography-oscar-sinners-first-woman-34297","Supports the Recognition and representation page","of Filipino and African American Creole descent")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Best Picture: 1 Black-led winner and 12 of 592 nominees, 1980 to 2020",text:"Pearn Kandola counted 12 Black nominees for Best Picture among 592 nominations from 1980 to 2020, and one winner: Steve McQueen for 12 Years a Slave (2014). The BFI describes McQueen as the first Black producer of a Best Picture winner and the third Black Best Director nominee.",figure:"1 winner; 12 of 592 nominees (2.0%)",year:"1980–2020",disputed:false,note:"Best Picture nominations go to producers, and one film can have several credited producers. McQueen was one of seven producers credited on the film.",src:[SRCQ("BFI, Steve McQueen: making history","https://www.bfi.org.uk/features/steve-mcqueen-making-history","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first ever black producer on a best picture winner")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"News-desk counts: Black actors had 9% to 10% of acting nominations",text:"Mother Jones counted 18 Black acting nominees in the decade to 2016, 9% of all acting nominees. The Dallas Morning News found that Black performers held 10% of acting nominations and 15% of acting wins up to 2016.",figure:"18 nominees (9%) in one decade",year:"to 2016",disputed:true,note:"The two counts cover different periods and do not state their methods fully. The Dallas Morning News based its classification on parentage and how people publicly identify. Use with caution.",src:[SRCQ("Mother Jones, Raw Data: How #White Are the Oscars, Anyway?","https://fullsite.motherjones.com/?p=294681","Supports the Recognition and representation page","9 percent of all acting nominees during that period"),SRCQ("The Dallas Morning News, Race for the Oscars","https://interactives.dallasnews.com/2016/oscars-diversity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","10 percent of best acting nominations and 15 percent of the wins")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"2012: Academy voters were 94% white and 2% Black",text:"In 2012 a Los Angeles Times study identified about 5,100 of the Academy's 5,765 voting members (89%). It found voters were 94% white, 2% Black and 2% Hispanic.",figure:"2% Black of 5,765 voters",year:"2012",disputed:false,note:"Both sources report the same LA Times study; we did not fetch the original.",src:[SRCQ("theGrio, Study: Oscar voters mainly white, male","https://thegrio.com/2012/02/20/study-oscar-voters-mainly-white-male","Supports the Recognition and representation page","94 percent of the 5,765 voting members"),SRCQ("ComingSoon.net, Oscar Voters are 94% White","https://www.comingsoon.net/movies/news/562906-oscar-voters-are-94-white-and-77-are-males-with-a-median-age-of-62","Supports the Recognition and representation page","nearly 94% Caucasian")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Academy after 2025: 22% from underrepresented racial or ethnic groups",text:"In June 2025 the Academy invited 534 new members, 45% from underrepresented racial or ethnic communities. If all accept, membership will reach 11,120, of whom 22% are from underrepresented communities.",figure:"22% of 11,120 members",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The Academy does not publish a Black-only share. In 2021 The Hollywood Reporter put the Academy at 81% white (2021 class: about 37% ‘non-white’). AP reported that after the 2026 class of 529 the Academy will be 75% white, with 56% of the new class from 'underrepresented communities' (single publisher).",src:[SRCQ("TheWrap, 534 invited to join the Academy","https://www.thewrap.com/new-members-academy-motion-pictures-arts-sciences-2025/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","45% are from underrepresented ethnic and racial communities"),SRCQ("AOL, New Oscar voters","https://www.aol.com/ariana-grande-fernanda-torres-kieran-183032089.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","45% belong to underrepresented racial or ethnic communities")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Inclusion standards: Best Picture films must meet 2 of 4 from 2024",text:"On 8 September 2020 the Academy announced representation and inclusion standards. From the 96th Oscars (2024), a film must meet two of four standards (on screen; creative leadership and crew; industry access; audience development) to be eligible for Best Picture. Black/African American is one of the listed underrepresented groups.",figure:"2 of 4 standards",year:"2020; in force 2024",disputed:false,note:"Critics have noted that standards C and D can be met through studio-level programmes. Neither source gives data on how many films met each standard. CBS gives the announcement date as 9 September; ET gives Tuesday 8 September 2020, which was the Tuesday.",src:[SRCQ("CBS News, Academy announces new Oscar eligibility standards","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/academy-announces-new-oscar-eligibility-standards-diversity-inclusion-requirements","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Starting in 2024, films will have to meet two out of four newly created standards to be eligible to win Best Picture."),SRCQ("ET Online, Oscars set inclusion requirements","https://www.etonline.com/oscars-set-inclusion-requirements-for-best-picture-category-that-will-take-full-effect-in-2024","Supports the Recognition and representation page","must meet two of the four standards shown below")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"2021: the Golden Globes' voting body had no Black members",text:"In February 2021 the Los Angeles Times reported that none of the 87 members of the Hollywood Foreign Press Association, which then voted on the Golden Globes, was Black.",figure:"0 of 87 (0%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Both sources report the Los Angeles Times investigation. The HFPA was later dissolved and the Globes moved to a new voting body.",src:[SRCQ("theGrio, HFPA has zero Black members","https://thegrio.com/2021/02/22/hollywood-foreign-press-black-journalists","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The HFPA is comprised of 87 members."),SRCQ("LA Magazine, How the Golden Globes addressed the total lack of Black HFPA members","https://lamag.com/theindustry/how-the-golden-globes-addressed-the-total-lack-of-black-hfpa-members/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","there is not a single Black member")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"2023: new Golden Globes voting body about 10% to 11% Black",text:"In April 2023 the expanded Golden Globes voting body was reported as 310 members, 10% of whom identified as Black. In October 2023 it was reported as 300 journalists, 11% of them Black, with 60% described as racially and ethnically diverse.",figure:"10% of 310 (Apr 2023); 11% of 300 (Oct 2023)",year:"2023",disputed:true,note:"The two figures come from different announcements six months apart, with different denominators. Both are the Globes' own self-reported data.",src:[SRCQ("The A.V. Club, Golden Globes voter base","https://avclub.com/golden-globes-voter-base-diversity-2024-1850323446","Supports the Recognition and representation page","10% identify as Black"),SRCQ("GMA Network, Golden Globes expands to 300 members","https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/showbiz/showbizabroad/883964/golden-globes-expands-to-300-members-highlighting-diversity-gains/story/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","11% Black")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"BAFTA: 12.2% of members from minority ethnic groups in 2021",text:"In February 2021, 12.2% of BAFTA members were from minority ethnic groups, as were 33.1% of its 635 new members that year. The target for 2025 was 20%. By December 2024 BAFTA reported 17% overall and 28% of members joining since 2020, out of 13,500 members.",figure:"12.2% (2021) → 17% (2024)",year:"2021–2024",disputed:false,note:"BAFTA does not publish a Black-only figure. Its 'minority ethnic' grouping should be compared with the England and Wales baseline of all ‘non-white’ groups (about 18%), not with Black 4.0% or Mixed 2.9% (Census 2021). The 17% target fell short of 20%.",src:[SRCQ("BAFTA, Membership survey results and progress towards targets","https://www.bafta.org/media-centre/press-releases/membership-survey-results-commitment-targets-2025","Supports the Recognition and representation page","12.2% are from minority ethnic groups"),SRCQ("Screen Daily, Bafta makes progress in diversifying membership","https://screendaily.com/news/bafta-makes-progress-in-diversifying-membership-figures-reveal/5199825.article","Supports the Recognition and representation page","28% of new members since 2020 are from underrepresented ethnic groups, versus 17% in total")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"BAFTA Film 1970 to 2020: 31 of 885 acting nominations to Black actors",text:"Pearn Kandola counted Black BAFTA Film acting nominees from 1970 to 2020: 4 of 227 lead actor, 6 of 224 lead actress, 9 of 217 supporting actor and 12 of 217 supporting actress, a total of 31 of 885 (3.5%). There was no Black winner of Leading Actress, and Black actors won 5 of 15 Rising Star awards (2006 to 2020).",figure:"31 of 885 (3.5%)",year:"1970–2020",disputed:false,note:"The 31 and 885 totals are our sum of Pearn Kandola's published category counts. Their earlier 2018 analysis, reported by the WEF, uses the 'BAME' grouping rather than Black. The Rising Star award is voted for by the public. This is a single analyst; its classification method is not stated.",src:[SRCQ("World Economic Forum (from Business Insider), 94% of all BAFTA film award nominees have been white","https://weforum.org/agenda/2018/02/94-of-all-bafta-film-award-nominees-have-been-white-according-to-a-new-report","Supports the Recognition and representation page","94% of all BAFTA film award nominees have been white")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"BAFTA 2020: all 20 acting nominees were white, then 120 changes",text:"All 20 acting nominees at the 2020 BAFTA Film Awards were white, and no woman was nominated for Best Director. After a review that consulted more than 400 people, BAFTA announced 120 changes in September 2020, including longlists, compulsory viewing and 1,000 new members from under-represented groups.",figure:"0 of 20 nominees of colour",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Al Jazeera, Anger as BAFTA awards recognise only white actors","https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2020/1/7/laughable-anger-as-bafta-awards-recognise-only-white-actors","Supports the Recognition and representation page","All 20 acting nominees for the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA) awards"),SRCQ("CBC, BAFTA vows change","https://amp.cbc.ca/news/entertainment/bafta-vows-change-lack-diversity-1.5737840","Supports the Recognition and representation page","all 20 nominees in the lead and supporting performer categories were white"),SRCQ("Geo News, Bafta organisers announce changes","https://www.geo.tv/latest/309775-bafta-film-awards-organisers-announce-changes-after-diversity-row","Supports the Recognition and representation page","120 changes")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"BAFTA 2021: 16 of 24 acting nominees were from ethnic minority groups",text:"The year after the review, 16 of the 24 acting nominees at the BAFTA Film Awards came from ethnic minority groups. That is 67%, up from 0 of 20 in 2020.",figure:"16 of 24 (67%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Neither source gives a Black-only figure. KVIA says only 'more than half' of the 24.",src:[SRCQ("TechRadar, BAFTA 2021 nominations","https://www.techradar.com/news/bafta-2021-nominations-list-reveals-a-diverse-selection-of-film-nominees","Supports the Recognition and representation page","16 of the 24 nominated actors and actresses coming from ethnic minority groups"),SRCQ("CNN via KVIA, BAFTA 2021 nominees","https://kvia.com/entertainment/2021/03/09/bafta-film-awards-2021-nominees-announced-with-huge-rise-in-diverse-talent-featured/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Every person shortlisted for an acting award in 2020 was White.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"BAFTA TV 2023: all six leading actress nominees were white",text:"In 2023 all six BAFTA TV Leading Actress nominees were white. Deadline, reported via Yahoo, said nominees of colour fell to 24%, after more than 40% in each of the two previous years.",figure:"0 of 6; 24% of nominees of colour",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The 24% figure appears in one publisher only, with no headcount. No Black-only count was found.",src:[SRCQ("Deadline via Yahoo, BAFTA defends all-white best TV actress shortlist","https://uk.news.yahoo.com/bafta-says-older-white-women-110516485.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","All six nominees in the Leading Actress category are white"),SRCQ("The Daily Beast, BAFTAs celebrate diversity of all-white best TV actress nominees","https://thedailybeast.com/baftas-celebrate-diversity-of-all-white-best-tv-actress-nominees","Supports the Recognition and representation page","There is representation in that category")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Oliviers: Karla Burns was the first Black performer to win, in 1991",text:"Karla Burns won the 1991 Olivier Award for best supporting performance in a musical, as Queenie in Show Boat. She is believed to be the first Black performer to win an Olivier. No published count of Black Olivier nominees or winners was found.",figure:"First Black winner: 1991",year:"1991",disputed:false,note:"Karla Burns died in 2021. The Society of London Theatre hedges with 'it is believed'. The Society does not publish nominee ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("Boston Globe obituary","https://www.bostonglobe.com/2021/06/15/metro/karla-burns-who-broke-british-tonys-color-barrier-dies-66","Supports the Recognition and representation page","it is believed that Ms. Burns was the first Black performer to win that honor"),SRCQ("Official London Theatre (SOLT), Karla Burns dies at 66","https://officiallondontheatre.com/news/karla-burns-dies-at-66/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","became the first Black performer to receive a Laurence Olivier Award"),SRCQ("Legacy.com obituary","https://www.legacy.com/news/karla-burns-1954-2021-tony-nominated-singer-and-actress","Supports the Recognition and representation page","became the first Black person to win England's prestigious Laurence Olivier Award")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"BFI Diversity Standards had 'not yet responded' to racial inequality",text:"An LSE study by Clive Nwonka, made with the BFI's cooperation, examined 235 films (2016 to 2019) that met the BFI Diversity Standards. Of these, 40% cited race or ethnicity in off-screen employment, compared with 71% that cited gender. BAFTA's British categories and BIFA use these standards for eligibility.",figure:"40% of 235 projects",year:"2016–2019 (published 2020)",disputed:false,note:"This is about eligibility rules, not winners. BIFA does not publish the ethnicity of its nominees; we found only a 2017 BIFA analysis of gender.",src:[SRCQ("The Hollywood Reporter, BFI diversity standards have not tackled racial inequality","https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/bfi-diversity-standards-have-not-tackled-racial-inequality-report-1303275","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The Diversity Standards currently has not yet responded to the challenge of racial inequality in the film industry"),SRCQ("Screen Daily, BFI to support Bafta and Bifa on diversity standards","https://www.screendaily.com/news/bfi-to-support-bafta-and-bifa-to-ensure-british-entries-meet-specific-diversity-standards/5146358.article","Supports the Recognition and representation page","These findings form the basis of our review in 2020.")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"UK TV: Black share of off-screen work fell to 2.7% in 2025",text:"The Creative Diversity Network's Diamond report found Black people made 2.7% of off-screen contributions to UK TV in 2025, down from 4% in 2022 and below a workforce benchmark of 5.2%. Black writers' share peaked at nearly 9% in 2024 and was 6.1% in 2025.",figure:"2.7% off screen (benchmark 5.2%)",year:"2022–2025",disputed:false,note:"These figures are contributions (credits), not people. No Black-only on-screen figure was given: the source's 'BAME' grouping was 23% on screen.",src:[SRCQ("C21Media, Black creatives off screen stalled","https://www.c21media.net/news/efforts-to-ensure-greater-representation-of-black-creatives-off-screen-in-uk-have-stalled/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","national workforce benchmark (5.2%)"),SRCQ("Televisual, Number of Black creatives in TV stalled","https://www.televisual.com/news/report-number-of-black-creatives-in-tv-stalled/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","has decreased from 4% in 2022 to 2.7% in 2025"),SRCQ("theGrio, UK TV diversity report","https://thegrio.com/2026/07/28/uk-tv-diversity-report-black-lives-matter-pledges/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black people accounted for 2.7% of off-screen contributions in 2025")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ofcom 2020: 1% of senior TV managers were Black",text:"Ofcom found that 1% of senior managers in UK TV were Black, while Black people made up 3% of the national working population. Minority ethnic staff held 8% of senior TV roles and made up 14% of TV and radio employees.",figure:"1% vs 3% workforce",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Ofcom's benchmark is the UK working population, not Census 2021. These data were published in 2020.",src:[SRCQ("Ofcom, Broadcasters must look beyond London","https://www.ofcom.org.uk/news-centre/2020/broadcasters-must-look-beyond-london","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Only 1% of senior managers working in TV are Black"),SRCQ("C21Media, Ofcom: industry diversity slow to improve","https://www.c21media.net/news/ofcom-industry-diversity-slow-to-improve/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Just 8% of senior management roles in TV are occupied by minority ethnic colleagues")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Emmys 2020: a record 35 of 102 acting nominees were Black",text:"In 2020, 35 of the 102 Primetime Emmy acting nominees were Black (34.3%), a record. The share was 19.8% in 2019 and 27.7% in 2018 (Variety's count).",figure:"35 of 102 (34.3%)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Both sources cite Variety's count. TheWrap counted 49 performers of colour out of 118 acting nominations, a wider set including voice-over and short form, so its denominator differs.",src:[SRCQ("LA Magazine, Emmy nominations 2020","https://lamag.com/tv/emmy-nominations-2020/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","35 of the 102 acting nominees are Black"),SRCQ("Harper's Bazaar, A record number of Black actors landed Emmy nominations","https://www.harpersbazaar.com.sg/life/a-record-number-of-black-actors-landed-emmy-nominations/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","34.3 percent of the 102 acting nominations")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Emmys 2025: 24 performers of colour among 92 or 94 acting nominees",text:"In 2025, 24 acting nominees were people of colour, the lowest number in five years. TheWrap put the total at 92 and another count put it at 94. Neither published a Black-only total.",figure:"24 of 92 (26%) or 24 of 94 (26%)",year:"2025",disputed:true,note:"The denominators differ (92 or 94). There is no Black-only count, so we have no figure to compare with the 2020 Black share.",src:[SRCQ("TheWrap, Emmys Diversity Report 2025","https://www.thewrap.com/emmys-diversity-report-2025/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Of the 92 total performers nominated in the acting categories, 24 of them are people of color."),SRCQ("AOL, Emmys 2025 diversity report","https://www.aol.com/entertainment/emmys-2025-diversity-report-worst-171152999.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","A total of 94 actors were recognized in the main acting categories")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Tonys 2022: about half of performance nominees Black (counts differ)",text:"AP counted 16 Black performance nominations out of 33 (48%) at the 2022 Tony Awards. Broadway News's category-by-category figures add up to 21 of 45. Both report that several Black artists were nominated in every performance category.",figure:"16 of 33 (48%) or 21 of 45 (47%)",year:"2022",disputed:true,note:"The 21 of 45 is our sum of Broadway News's per-category counts. The denominators differ (33 and 45), probably because the sources defined the performance categories differently. AP's 2016 comparison (35% actors of colour) is not Black-only.",src:[SRCQ("AP via VOA, On Broadway, more visibility","https://www.voanews.com/a/on-broadway-more-visibility-but-also-an-unseen-threat-/6606255.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","There are 16 Black performance nods out of 33 slots"),SRCQ("Broadway News, The 2022 Tony nominations mark a historic shift","https://www.broadwaynews.com/the-2022-tony-nominations-mark-a-historic-shift-on-broadway/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","multiple Black artists nominated in every single performance category")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Viola Davis: first Black actor to win an acting Oscar, Emmy and Tony",text:"With her 2017 Oscar, Viola Davis became the first Black actor to win competitive acting awards at the Oscars, Emmys and Tonys (the acting 'triple crown').",figure:"First: 2017",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"The sources word this slightly differently: 'first African-American actor' and 'first black woman'.",src:[SRCQ("Christian Science Monitor","https://www.csmonitor.com/Arts-Culture/Movies/2017/0227/Following-Oscar-win-Viola-Davis-is-first-black-winner-of-acting-triple-crown","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first African-American actor to have received an Academy Award, an Emmy Award, and a Tony Award for acting roles"),SRCQ("Marie Claire UK","https://www.marieclaire.co.uk/entertainment/tv-and-film/viola-davis-first-black-woman-oscar-emmy-tony-acting-479526","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first black woman to win an Oscar, Emmy, and Tony for her craft as an actor")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cannes: Mati Diop, first Black woman director in competition (2019)",text:"In 2019 Mati Diop became the first Black woman director to compete for the Palme d'Or. She won the Grand Prix for Atlantics, and Scripps reported she was one of three Black directors ever to win the Grand Prix. Only one woman, Jane Campion in 1993, had won the Palme d'Or by then.",figure:"First: 2019 (72nd festival)",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"We found no published count of Black Palme d'Or winners. Scripps's figure of three Black Grand Prix winners comes from that source only.",src:[SRCQ("Essence","https://www.essence.com/entertainment/mati-diop-first-black-woman-cannes-film-festival-competition","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black woman to compete for the coveted Palme d'Or"),SRCQ("Scripps News","https://scrippsnews.com/stories/mati-diop-is-first-black-woman-to-win-cannes-grand-prix/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","one of just three black directors and four women directors ever to win the Grand Prix"),SRCQ("The Voice","https://www.voice-online.co.uk/entertainment/film/2019/06/27/mati-diop-1st-black-woman-to-win-award-at-cannes-film-fest/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first black female director to win an award at the Cannes Film Festival")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cannes: Spike Lee named first Black jury president (for 2020)",text:"In January 2020 Cannes named Spike Lee as the first Black president of its competition jury.",figure:"First: named 2020",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The 2020 festival did not take place in its usual form because of the pandemic. Neither source fetched confirms when he served.",src:[SRCQ("NBC News","https://www.nbcnews.com/pop-culture/pop-culture-news/spike-lee-first-black-person-lead-cannes-film-festival-jury-n1115246","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Spike Lee is first black person to lead Cannes Film Festival jury"),SRCQ("Scripps News","https://www.scrippsnews.com/entertainment/celebrity/spike-lee-named-first-black-jury-president-at-cannes","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Spike Lee will be the first black president of the jury at the Cannes Film Festival.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"arts",h:"Books, music, art and journalism",intro:"Only the Pulitzer Prizes have a published count of Black winners. For most UK prizes the record is a set of firsts.",points:[
  {h:"Booker Prize: first Black woman winner in 2019",text:"Bernardine Evaristo became the first Black woman to win the Booker Prize in 2019 (joint winner with Margaret Atwood), 50 years after the prize began in 1969. The South China Morning Post also reported her as the first Black British author to win.",figure:"1st Black woman winner, 50 years after 1969 launch",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"Milestone, not a count. The Booker Prize Foundation publishes no ethnicity breakdown of winners, shortlists or longlists that we found.",src:[SRCQ("The Booker Prizes: Bernardine Evaristo","https://thebookerprizes.com/the-booker-library/authors/bernadine-evaristo","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Bernardine Evaristo made history in 2019 as the first Black woman to win the Booker Prize."),SRCQ("SCMP: Authors Margaret Atwood and Bernardine Evaristo share 2019 Booker","https://scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3032917/authors-margaret-atwood-and-bernardine-evaristo-share-2019-booker","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Evaristo's win makes her the first black woman to win the Booker since it began in 1969 and the first black British author.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Booker: no published count of Black winners or nominees",text:"We found no count of Black winners, shortlisted or longlisted writers published by the Booker Prize Foundation. A 2013 Media Diversified analysis counted 14 nominations of ‘non-white’ women (about 5% of nominations, 1969 to 2013) and judging panels '99% white', but we found no second independent count to confirm it.",figure:"No published count",year:"2013",disputed:false,note:"Media Diversified uses 'non-white', not 'Black'. The Post45 academic dataset (9 UK prizes including Booker, Costa/Whitbread and Women's Prize, 1990 to 2022) records ethnicity but publishes no summary count on its page; we did not compile one. Its stated aim is 'to determine whether the shortlist and winners are representative of a multicultural Britain'.",src:[SRCQ("Media Diversified: The colour of the prize (2013)","https://mediadiversified.org/2013/09/12/the-colour-of-the-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","only fourteen nominations of non-white female authors for the Prize since its inception"),SRCQ("Post45 Data Collective: British literary prizes dataset (Binhammer, Batra, Gray, Jayasuriya)","https://data.post45.org/posts/british-literary-prizes/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","demographic information on 682 prize winners and shortlisted authors")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Carnegie Medal: no writer of colour won in its first 83 years",text:"Elizabeth Acevedo's 2019 Carnegie Medal made her the first writer of colour to win in the award's 83-year history. A peer-reviewed UCL study of UK young adult publishing had earlier noted that an author of colour had never won the medal, established 1935.",figure:"1st writer of colour in 83 years (0 before 2019)",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"Sources say 'writer of colour', not 'Black'. Acevedo is a US author of Dominican heritage; we do not classify her further. The Carnegie medals are now the Yoto Carnegies.",src:[SRCQ("The Writer: Elizabeth Acevedo becomes the first writer of color to win the Carnegie medal","https://www.writermag.com/?p=31553","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first writer of color to do so in the award's 83-year history"),SRCQ("University of Maryland English: Carnegie Medal goes to Elizabeth Acevedo","https://english.umd.edu/news/carnegie-medal-goes-elizabeth-acevedo-first-writer-color-medals-history","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Acevedo is the first writer of color to recieve the award in the Medal's history."),SRCQ("Ramdarshan Bold (UCL): The Eight Percent Problem (2006 to 2016)","https://discovery-pp.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10053872/1/Ramdarshan%20Bold_The%20Eight%20Percent%20Problem.%20Authors%20of%20Colour%20in%20the%20British%20Young%20Adult%20Market%20%282006%E2%80%932016%29_VoR.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","an author of colour has never won The Carnegie Medal for Children's Literature")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"CILIP diversity review followed an all-white longlist in 2017",text:"CILIP launched an independent diversity review of the Carnegie and Kate Greenaway medals in June 2017 after the 2017 Carnegie longlist included no Black, Asian or minority ethnic authors. The review consulted over 600 people and made ten recommendations.",figure:"0 Black, Asian or minority ethnic authors on the 2017 longlist",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"'BAME' is the sources' grouping. The longlist size is not given on these pages. The review page publishes no historic count of winners by ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("CILIP: Carnegie and Kate Greenaway Diversity Review","https://www.cilip.org.uk/CKGDiversityReviewFinal","Supports the Recognition and representation page","included no Black, Asian, Minority Ethnic (BAME) authors"),SRCQ("The Bookseller: CILIP to review Carnegie and Kate Greenaway medals","https://thebookseller.com/news/cilip-review-carnegie-and-kate-greenaway-medals-500231","Supports the Recognition and representation page","did not include a single black, Asian or minority ethnic (BAME) writer")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Authors of colour wrote 8% of UK young adult authors, 2006 to 2016",text:"A peer-reviewed UCL study found 217 authors of colour (8%) among all young adult authors published in the UK from 2006 to 2016; of 40 British authors of colour, 17 (42.5%) were Black British. This is the pipeline from which children's prizes draw.",figure:"17 Black British of 40 British authors of colour",year:"2006-2016",disputed:false,note:"The second source is a qualitative statement by Sunny Singh, not a count. The share of authors of colour fell from 8.7% (2006) to 4.79% (2016) in the same study. Context only: not award data.",src:[SRCQ("Ramdarshan Bold (UCL): The Eight Percent Problem","https://discovery-pp.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10053872/1/Ramdarshan%20Bold_The%20Eight%20Percent%20Problem.%20Authors%20of%20Colour%20in%20the%20British%20Young%20Adult%20Market%20%282006%E2%80%932016%29_VoR.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Overall, 8% (217 authors) of all of the YA authors were authors of colour"),SRCQ("London Met: First year of Jhalak Prize finds diversity is an overlooked issue","https://www.londonmet.ac.uk/news/articles/first-year-of-jhalak-prize-finds-diversity-is-an-overlooked-issue/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","a ridiculously small number of books by writers of colour are being published in the UK")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Jhalak Prize created in 2016 for British writers of colour",text:"The Jhalak Prize, founded by Sunny Singh and Nikesh Shukla and launched in February 2016, awards £1,000 to a British writer of colour. Its founder says it should close when it is no longer needed; it received 118 entries in its first year.",figure:"118 entries in first year",year:"2016-2017",disputed:false,note:"'BME' is the source's term. The prize covers all writers of colour; no published breakdown of Black winners found.",src:[SRCQ("London Met: First year of Jhalak Prize","https://www.londonmet.ac.uk/news/articles/first-year-of-jhalak-prize-finds-diversity-is-an-overlooked-issue/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","I started the Jhalak Prize with the intention of shutting it down when it is no longer needed."),SRCQ("The Asian Writer: The Jhalak Prize to celebrate the best book written by a BME writer","https://theasianwriter.co.uk/2016/02/23/the-jhalak-prize-to-celebrate-the-best-book-written-by-a-bme-writer/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first literary prize to open only to BME writers")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Pulitzer letters: first African American winner was in 1950",text:"Gwendolyn Brooks became the first African American to win a Pulitzer Prize, for poetry, in 1950, 33 years after the first prizes in 1917.",figure:"1st African American winner, 1950",year:"1950",disputed:false,note:"Britannica says 'first African American poet'; China Daily says first African American Pulitzer winner in literature. Brooks died in 2000.",src:[SRCQ("Britannica: Gwendolyn Brooks","https://britannica.com/biography/Gwendolyn-Brooks","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first African American poet to win the Pulitzer Prize (1950)."),SRCQ("China Daily: Gwendolyn Brooks, 1917-2000","https://language.chinadaily.com.cn/2012-06/05/content_15475648.htm","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 1950, Gwendolyn Brooks became the first African American to win the Pulitzer Prize for Literature.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Pulitzer drama: first African American winner in 1970",text:"Charles Gordone became the first African American to win the Pulitzer Prize for Drama in 1970, for No Place to Be Somebody.",figure:"1st African American drama winner, 1970",year:"1970",disputed:false,note:"Gordone died in 1995. No published count of Black drama winners found.",src:[SRCQ("Encyclopedia.com: Gordone, Charles","https://www.encyclopedia.com/african-american-focus/news-wires-white-papers-and-books/gordone-charles","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In 1970 Gordone became the first African American to receive the Pulitzer Prize for drama."),SRCQ("Indiana History Blog: Charles Gordone, Finding His Place to Be Somebody","https://blog.history.in.gov/author/jtaylor/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Gordone became known as the first African-American playwright to win the award.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Underrepresented artists were 44.6% of chart artists in 2024",text:"USC Annenberg Inclusion Initiative found 44.6% of artists on the Billboard Hot 100 year-end chart in 2024 were from underrepresented racial or ethnic groups, down from 61% in 2023 and up from 38.4% in 2012. The study covers 1,300 songs over 13 years.",figure:"44.6% (2024); 61% (2023); 38.4% (2012)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"'Underrepresented' covers all groups other than white, not Black alone; Annenberg publishes no Black-only share in these releases. US chart, so compare with the US 2020 Census (white non-Hispanic about 58%). Annenberg's 2026 release reports 57.8% for 2025 (one publisher only).",src:[SRCQ("USC Annenberg: Women in music hold on to gains, but have more room to grow","https://annenberg.usc.edu/node/75454","Supports the Recognition and representation page","44.6%"),SRCQ("Mix: USC Annenberg Inclusion Initiative releases 2025 report","https://www.mixonline.com/business/usc-annenberg-inclusion-initiative-releases-2025-report","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The percentage of underrepresented artists in 2024 (44.6%)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"2020: 59% of 173 chart artists were from underrepresented groups",text:"In 2020, 59% of the 173 artists on the Billboard Hot 100 year-end chart were from underrepresented racial or ethnic groups and 41% were white, the fourth annual rise, USC Annenberg found. Over 2012 to 2020 the share was 46.7%.",figure:"about 102 of 173 (59%)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Numerator (about 102) is derived from the published 59% of 173 and is approximate. Not a Black-only figure.",src:[SRCQ("MusicRow: Annenberg study reveals no meaningful change for women","https://musicrow.com/2021/03/annenberg-inclusion-initiative-study-reveals-no-meaningful-change-for-women-in-recorded-music/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Of 173 artists in 2020, 59% were underrepresented and 41% were white."),SRCQ("USC Annenberg: Inclusion in the Recording Studio 2021","https://annenberg.usc.edu/node/72558","Supports the Recognition and representation page","More than half (59%) of all artists in 2020 were underrepresented.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Grammy Album of the Year: four Black women winners by 2025",text:"Beyoncé's 2025 Album of the Year win made her the fourth Black woman to win the award, after Natalie Cole, Whitney Houston and Lauryn Hill (1999), and the first in the 21st century, AP and Khaleej Times reported.",figure:"4 Black women winners (to 2025)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"No denominator was published in these sources; Album of the Year has been given since 1959. Neither source counts all Black Album of the Year winners. Natalie Cole and Whitney Houston have died; living winners named in their public role.",src:[SRCQ("AP via Western Mass News: Beyoncé wins her first album of the year Grammy","https://www.westernmassnews.com/2025/02/03/beyonc-wins-her-first-album-year-grammy-award-cowboy-carter/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","That means Beyoncé is only the fourth Black woman to win album of the year at the Grammys."),SRCQ("Khaleej Times: Grammys 2025, Beyoncé wins first ever album of the year","https://www.khaleejtimes.com/entertainment/grammys-2025-beyonce-wins-first-ever-album-of-the-year-award-for-cowboy-carter?amp=1","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first Black woman in the 21st century to win the prize")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Recording Academy: voters of colour up from 24% to 38%",text:"After the Recording Academy changed its membership model in 2019, members who are people of colour rose from 24% to 38%, and 50% of the 2023 new member class were people of colour. The Academy reports 90% growth since 2019 in the share of Black or African American voting members.",figure:"24% to 38% people of colour; Black share +90% since 2019",year:"2019-2024",disputed:false,note:"The Academy publishes relative growth for Black members, not their share of voters; only the Academy's own release gives the Black figure (Black Enterprise does not). These are voters, not winners.",src:[SRCQ("Black Enterprise: Recording Academy people of color membership","https://www.blackenterprise.com/recording-academy-2400-people-of-color-membership/amp/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","membership among people of color has risen from 24% to 38%"),SRCQ("Recording Academy via Business Wire (2024)","https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20241003599230/en/Recording-Academy-Surpasses-2025-Membership-Goal-Adds-3000-Women-GRAMMY-Voters-Since-2019","Supports the Recognition and representation page","90% growth in the percentage of Black or African American+ voting members.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Brit Awards 2016: all-white nominees in 7 of 11 categories",text:"In 2016, all nominees were white in seven of the 11 Brit Award categories, prompting the #BritsSoWhite campaign. The Brits chairman admitted diversity 'was not adequately reflected' and promised change.",figure:"7 of 11 categories all-white",year:"2016",disputed:false,note:"The 7 of 11 count is New Statesman's alone; FACT confirms the criticism and the chairman's response but gives no count. Count is of categories, not individual nominees.",src:[SRCQ("New Statesman: #BritsSoWhite","https://www.newstatesman.com/culture/music/2016/02/britssowhite-audiences-criticise-awards-exclusion-black-british","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In seven of the 11 categories, all nominees are white."),SRCQ("FACT: Brits chairman addresses lack of diversity","https://factmag.com/2016/03/04/brits-chairman-addresses-lack-of-diversity-whiteness","Supports the Recognition and representation page","This was not adequately reflected at this year's Brits.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Brits voting academy: 17% from ethnic minorities after 2016 reform",text:"The Brits pledged 'at least 15% ‘BAME’ participation' in its voting academy. For 2017 it invited 718 new members, taking the source's 'BAME' share to 17% (from 15%) and women to 48% (from 30%).",figure:"17% of voting academy (2017)",year:"2016-2017",disputed:false,note:"'BAME' is the sources' grouping and includes all ethnic minorities. Compare with England and Wales 2021 Census: Black 4.0%, Mixed 2.9%. The 17% figure is i-D's; NME and FACT give the 15% target. No Black-only share published.",src:[SRCQ("i-D: Brit Awards diversify voting membership after #BritsSoWhite","https://i-d.co/article/brit-awards-diversify-voting-membership-after-britssowhite-campaign/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","a total of 718 new members"),SRCQ("NME: Brits promise to be more truly representative","https://www.nme.com/?p=1189733","Supports the Recognition and representation page","at least 15% BAME participation")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mercury Prize: first Black woman winner in 2002",text:"Ms Dynamite became the first Black woman to win the Mercury Music Prize in 2002 for A Little Deeper, beating David Bowie among others.",figure:"1st Black woman winner, 2002",year:"2002",disputed:true,note:"Sources differ: NPG says first Black woman; HELLO! says first solo Black artist (a wider claim we could not confirm). We found no published count of Black Mercury winners or shortlisted acts.",src:[SRCQ("National Portrait Gallery: Ms Dynamite","https://www.npg.org.uk/whatson/exhibitions/2007/devotional/ms-dynamite","Supports the Recognition and representation page","became the first black woman to win the Mercury Music Prize"),SRCQ("HELLO!: Miss Dynamite scoops Britain's top music accolade (2002)","https://www.hellomagazine.com/celebrities/469781/mercury-dynamite/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first solo black artist to scoop the accolade")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Pulitzer music: first jazz (1997) and first hip-hop (2018) winners",text:"Wynton Marsalis's Blood on the Fields (1997) was the first jazz work to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music, and Kendrick Lamar's DAMN. (2018) the first non-classical, non-jazz work.",figure:"1st jazz 1997; 1st non-classical non-jazz 2018",year:"1997, 2018",disputed:false,note:"These are genre firsts, not race firsts; both winners are living and named in their public role. The FOX and CBS text appears to share an AP origin. The Marsalis 'first jazz' claim has one fetched source (Nieman); pulitzer.org confirms only the 1997 award.",src:[SRCQ("Nieman Reports: The story behind the first Pulitzer for jazz","https://niemanreports.org/the-story-behind-the-first-pulitzer-for-jazz/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","the first jazz work to take the highest honor in American music"),SRCQ("FOX 9: Kendrick Lamar the 1st rapper to win Pulitzer","https://www.fox9.com/news/kendrick-lamar-the-1st-rapper-to-win-pulitzer","Supports the Recognition and representation page","It's the first non-classical or jazz work to win the award."),SRCQ("CBS Los Angeles: Kendrick Lamar wins Pulitzer for DAMN","https://www.cbsnews.com/losangeles/news/kendrick-lamar-pulitzer","Supports the Recognition and representation page","It's the first non-classical or jazz work to win the award.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"MOBO Awards founded in 1996 to recognise overlooked Black music",text:"Kanya King founded the MOBO (Music of Black Origin) Awards in 1996 to celebrate Black British musicians overlooked by other industry events. She died on 3 June 2026.",figure:"Founded 1996",year:"1996",disputed:false,note:"Context. MOBO is itself an award for music of Black origin, so its winners are not comparable with general awards.",src:[SRCQ("Music Week: Trailblazing MOBO founder Kanya King dies aged 57","https://www.musicweek.com/talent/read/trailblazing-mobo-founder-kanya-king-dies-aged-57/094246","Supports the Recognition and representation page","MOBO did not just celebrate Black music; it legitimised it, amplified it"),SRCQ("Irish News (PA): MOBO Awards founder Kanya King dies at 57","https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/mobo-awards-founder-kanya-king-dies-at-57-after-cancer-battle-LAXOOT4JNNMBZFC7J5ROE7N2EM","Supports the Recognition and representation page","established the awards ceremony in 1996")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Black music creators report racism: 63% in 2021 survey",text:"The Black Lives in Music survey of nearly 2,000 people (2021) found 63% of Black music creators and 73% of Black music professionals had experienced direct or indirect racism in the UK industry, and Black women earn 25% less on average than white women colleagues.",figure:"63% of creators; 73% of professionals",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Self-selected survey, not award data. DJ Mag extract is its headline from search results; percentages were fetched from Music Week only.",src:[SRCQ("Music Week: Black Lives In Music survey highlights systemic industry racism","https://musicweek.com/talent/read/black-lives-in-music-survey-highlights-systemic-and-institutionalised-industry-racism/084338","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Nearly 2,000 people responded to our survey"),SRCQ("DJ Mag: Majority of Black music artists and professionals have faced racism","https://djmag.com/news/majority-black-music-artists-and-professionals-have-faced-racism-industry-black-lives-music-survey-finds","Supports the Recognition and representation page","majority black music artists and professionals have faced racism")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Turner Prize: first Black winner in 1998",text:"Chris Ofili became the first Black artist to win the Turner Prize in 1998.",figure:"1st Black winner, 1998",year:"1998",disputed:false,note:"Britannica confirms the 1998 win but its extract did not state 'first'. Masterworks is a commercial art platform. No published count of Black Turner winners or shortlisted artists found.",src:[SRCQ("Studio Museum in Harlem: Chris Ofili","https://studiomuseum.org/artists/chris-ofili","Supports the Recognition and representation page","He received the prestigious Turner Prize—the first Black artist to do so—in 1998."),SRCQ("Masterworks Academy: Chris Ofili","https://www.masterworks.com/academy/artists/chris-ofili","Supports the Recognition and representation page","and the first Black artist to win the Turner Prize, in 1998.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Turner Prize: first Black woman winner in 2017",text:"Lubaina Himid became the first Black woman to win the Turner Prize in 2017; at 63 she was also its oldest winner.",figure:"1st Black woman winner, 2017",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"Artfix Daily described her as the 'first ‘non-white’ woman', a wider claim.",src:[SRCQ("Stylist: Turner Prize 2017 winner Lubaina Himid","https://stylist.co.uk/people/turner-prize-2017-winner-lubaina-himid-first-black-woman/177986","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Lubaina Himid has become the first black woman artist to win the prestigious Turner Prize."),SRCQ("ARTnews: Lubaina Himid wins 2017 Turner Prize","https://www.artnews.com/art-news/news/lubaina-himid-wins-2017-turner-prize-9433","Supports the Recognition and representation page","marking the first time a black woman has taken home the award")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"RIBA Royal Gold Medal: first Black recipient in 172 years",text:"David Adjaye was named the first Black recipient of the RIBA Royal Gold Medal, for 2021, in the award's 172-year history.",figure:"1st Black recipient in 172 years",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"No published count of medallists by ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("The Architect's Newspaper: Adjaye wins 2021 Royal Gold Medal","https://www.archpaper.com/?p=288288","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Adjaye is the first Black recipient of the Royal Gold Medal in the 172-year history of the award."),SRCQ("Dezeen: David Adjaye wins 2021 Royal Gold Medal","https://www.dezeen.com/2020/10/03/david-adjaye-riba-royal-gold-medal-stirling-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","was the first black architect to win the prize")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Stirling Prize: no published count of Black winners",text:"We found no published count of Black winners or shortlisted architects for the RIBA Stirling Prize (since 1996), from RIBA or an independent analyst.",figure:"No published count",year:"1996-2025",disputed:false,note:"Sources are about the Royal Gold Medal and are given only to show the absence of a comparable Stirling count. Gap to fill if RIBA publishes data.",src:[SRCQ("Dezeen: Adjaye Royal Gold Medal and Stirling Prize news","https://www.dezeen.com/2020/10/03/david-adjaye-riba-royal-gold-medal-stirling-prize/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","was the first black architect to win the prize"),SRCQ("The Architect's Newspaper","https://www.archpaper.com/?p=288288","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Adjaye is the first Black recipient of the Royal Gold Medal in the 172-year history of the award.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Pulitzer: 30 African American winners among 943 named winners",text:"Columbia Journalism Review counted 30 named African American Pulitzer winners among 943 named winners over 100 years (about 3.2%), and found 84% of winners were white. Nearly a third of African American winners won for commentary.",figure:"30 of 943 (3.2%)",year:"1917-2016",disputed:false,note:"One count (CJR), reported by Nieman Lab. Covers all categories but excludes team and newsroom prizes. Pre-1960 winners who could not be confirmed were 'assumed to be white'; CJR issued a correction saying key findings were unchanged. 3.2% computed from CJR's 30 and 943. Compare US 2020 Census Black alone 12.4% (but the period spans 100 years).",src:[SRCQ("Columbia Journalism Review: 100 years of data","https://cjr.org/analysis/100_years_of_data.php","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Only 30 named African Americans have won Pulitzers."),SRCQ("Nieman Lab: 100 years of Pulitzer Prize data reveal a race and gender disparity","https://niemanlab.org/reading/100-years-of-pulitzer-prize-data-reveal-a-race-and-gender-disparity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","84 percent of Pulitzer winners over the last 100 years have been white.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Pulitzer: 4 African American winners in International Reporting",text:"Statista's research department counted four African American winners of the Pulitzer for International Reporting from 1917 to 2016.",figure:"4 (to 2016)",year:"1917-2016",disputed:false,note:"Statista likely drew on CJR's data; treat as one count. No denominator for International Reporting given in the extract.",src:[SRCQ("Statista: African-American Pulitzer winners by category, 2016","https://www.statista.com/statistics/696349/pulitzer-prize-winner-ethnicity","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Since 1917, a total of four African-Americans have won the Pulitzer Prize for International Reporting."),SRCQ("Columbia Journalism Review: 100 years of data","https://cjr.org/analysis/100_years_of_data.php","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Only 30 named African Americans have won Pulitzers.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"British Journalism Awards: no ethnicity data on winners published",text:"Press Gazette, which runs the British Journalism Awards, has published gender analyses (740 male v 300 female finalists over eight years) but no count of winners or finalists by ethnicity. In 2020 it created the Barbara Blake-Hannah award for ethnic minority journalists, named after the UK's first Black on-screen TV news reporter.",figure:"No published count (ethnicity)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Press Gazette reported 'some 200 entries this year from ‘BAME’, female and disabled journalists' in 2020, a combined figure that cannot be split by ethnicity. Barbara Blake-Hannah is a living public figure named in her public role. We found no ethnicity data for the Orwell Prizes.",src:[SRCQ("Press Gazette: Barbara Blake-Hannah prize launched","https://pressgazette.co.uk/barbara-blake-hannah-prize-launched-as-press-gazette-bids-to-improve-british-journalism-awards-diversity/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","740 finalists have been male versus 300 female")],kind:"counter-argument"}]},
 {k:"professions",h:"Professions and industry",intro:"Professional recognition is measured through appointments and boards as well as awards.",points:[
  {h:"King's Counsel 2017–2023: 16 of 45 Black applicants appointed",text:"KC Appointments' ethnic minority statistics show that Black, African, Caribbean or Black British barristers made 45 applications across the 2017 to 2023 competitions. Of these, 16 were appointed, a success rate of 36%. The yearly number of Black applicants ranged from 3 to 9.",figure:"16 of 45 (36%)",year:"2017–2023",disputed:false,note:"The PDF table came through flattened, and the tool inferred the column order from the totals. Check against the PDF. The 36% is computed from KC Appointments' own numbers. No separate Mixed figure is given.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnic Minority statistics 2017–2023 – KC Appointments","https://kcappointments.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Ethnic-Minority-statistics-2017-2023.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","TOTAL 2023 29 7 48"),SRCQ("KC applications up as success rate continues to fall – Legal Futures, January 2025","https://www.legalfutures.co.uk/latest-news/kc-applications-up-as-succees-rate-continues-to-fall","Supports the Recognition and representation page","not one succeeded")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"King's Counsel 2024: 0 of 10 Black applicants appointed",text:"In the 2024 competition a record 10 Black barristers applied, and none was appointed. Overall, 105 of 326 applicants were appointed (32%), and the success rate for ethnic minority applicants was 30%.",figure:"0 of 10 (0%) vs 105 of 326 overall (32%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Legal Futures gives 327 applications and KC Appointments gives 326. The Selection Panel chair said the panel was 'concerned about the lack of success of black applicants'.",src:[SRCQ("KC applications up as success rate continues to fall – Legal Futures","https://www.legalfutures.co.uk/latest-news/kc-applications-up-as-succees-rate-continues-to-fall","Supports the Recognition and representation page","more Black barristers applied for silk than in any previous year (10)"),SRCQ("KC Appointments press release, 2024 competition","https://kcappointments.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/KCA-Press-Release-2024.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","we continue to be concerned about the lack of success of black applicants")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"King's Counsel 2025: 2 of 11 Black applicants appointed",text:"In the 2025 competition (announced January 2026), 2 of a record 11 Black applicants were appointed. Overall, 96 of 326 were appointed (29.5%), and 11 of 52 ethnic minority applicants were appointed (21%).",figure:"2 of 11 (18%) vs 96 of 326 (29.5%)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The Black figure comes from Legal Futures. KC Appointments' 2025 report gives only ethnic minority totals. The 18% is computed.",src:[SRCQ("Success rate falls again as 96 new KCs are named – Legal Futures, January 2026","https://www.legalfutures.co.uk/latest-news/success-rate-falls-again-as-96-new-kcs-are-named","Supports the Recognition and representation page","two of the record 11 who applied for 2025 did"),SRCQ("Report to the Lord Chancellor, 2025 competition – KC Appointments","https://kcappointments.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/FINAL-Report-to-the-LC-2025.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","we have recommended 11 applicants from an ethnic minority for appointment")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"FTSE 100 top roles: no Black chair, CEO or finance chief, 2021",text:"The Green Park Business Leaders Index 2021 found no Black chairs, chief executives or chief financial officers in the FTSE 100, the first time in six years of tracking. Only 10 of the 297 people in those three roles (3.4%) were from ethnic minority backgrounds.",figure:"0 of 297 Black; 10 of 297 (3.4%) ethnic minority",year:"2021",disputed:true,note:"Green Park's later update gives 11 of 295 (3.7%) ethnic minority leaders. Both figures are given here. Newer Green Park figures could not be found by fetch.",src:[SRCQ("Green Park Business Leaders Index: Britain's top firms failing Black leaders – Green Park, 3 February 2021","https://www.green-park.co.uk/insights/green-park-business-leaders-index-britain-s-top-firms-failing-black-leaders/s228945/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","there are no black Chairs, CEOs or CFOs, in the FTSE100"),SRCQ("No Black executives in top three roles of FTSE 100 firms – CNBC, 3 February 2021","https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/03/no-black-executives-in-top-three-roles-of-ftse-100-firms-report-shows.html","Supports the Recognition and representation page","just 10 of the 297 people, or just over 3%, in the top three roles")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Parker Review 2026: Black directors hold 2.3% of FTSE 100 seats",text:"The Parker Review (data at 31 December 2025, published 10 March 2026) found that ethnic minority directors held 208 of 1,063 FTSE 100 board seats (20%). Black directors held 2.3%, against 3.9% of the UK population aged 30 to 69. Black representation in senior management fell from 1.5% to 1.3%.",figure:"2.3% of 1,063 FTSE 100 directorships",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The review gives the Black share as a percentage only, not a number. It counted 14 ethnic minority chief executives in the FTSE 100 but gives no Black breakdown of them.",src:[SRCQ("Parker Review 2026 press release","https://parkerreview.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Parker-Review-2026-Press-Release.pdf","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Black directors now hold 2.3% of directorships, compared with 3.9% of the UK population aged 30-69"),SRCQ("Boardroom diversity at record high – Eastern Eye, 13 March 2026","https://www.easterneye.biz/boardroom-diversity-record-high-parker-review-uk/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","black directors hold 2.3 per cent of FTSE 100 board positions")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal College of Psychiatrists: first Black President, 2023",text:"Dr Lade Smith was elected President of the Royal College of Psychiatrists in January 2023 with 54.1% of the vote. King's College London and King's Health Partners both report that she is the College's first Black President and its fifth woman President.",figure:"1st Black President",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"No medical royal college publishes the ethnicity of its presidents or award winners across its history. King's College London and King's Health Partners are related bodies, so they count as close to one publisher. The College's own announcement (truncated in the fetch) did not show this wording.",src:[SRCQ("Dr Lade Smith CBE elected as the next President of the Royal College of Psychiatrists – King's College London","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/news/dr-lade-smith-cbe-elected-as-the-next-president-of-the-royal-college-of-psychiatrists","Supports the Recognition and representation page","She will be the College's first Black President and the fifth woman at the helm."),SRCQ("KHPeople: Dr Lade Smith CBE – King's Health Partners, 1 February 2023","https://www.kingshealthpartners.org/latest/khpeople-dr-lade-smith-cbe","Supports the Recognition and representation page","Dr Smith will be RCPsych's first Black President, and the fifth woman at the helm.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"ACCA: first African global president, 2022",text:"In November 2022 Joseph Owolabi became the first African, and the youngest person, elected global president of ACCA, the Association of Chartered Certified Accountants.",figure:"1st African president",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Both sources say 'African', not 'Black', and that wording is kept here. No accountancy body was found to publish its awards by ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("Joseph Owolabi first African elected as ACCA global president – Consultancy.com.au, 22 November 2022","https://www.consultancy.com.au/news/6169/joseph-owolabi-first-african-elected-as-acca-global-president","Supports the Recognition and representation page","has become the first African and youngest ever person elected as global president and chairman of the ACCA"),SRCQ("Owolabi: How I emerged first African president of global accountancy body ACCA at 39 – ThisDay","https://www.thisdaylive.com/2022/11/21/owolabi-how-i-emerged-first-african-president-of-global-accountancy-body-acca-at-39/","Supports the Recognition and representation page","not just the first African appointed in that role but also the youngest to attain such height")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"BAFTA TV Craft 2018: 1 Black crew member of 99 recognised",text:"Bectu's Black Members' Committee analysed the 2018 BAFTA TV Craft Awards and found that 98 of the 99 crew members recognised were white and 1 was Black. In his 2020 MacTaggart Lecture, David Olusoga said the 2020 Craft Awards had no Black or Asian winner.",figure:"1 of 99 (1.0%)",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"The count was made by a trade union committee, and its method is not described. The Media Reform Coalition post reproduces the same Bectu blog, so it is not independent. No published ethnicity data was found for the Royal Television Society awards.",src:[SRCQ("TV Craft Awards: lifting the lid on structural racism – Bectu, 27 August 2020","https://bectu.org.uk/news/tv-craft-awards-lifting-the-lid-on-structural-racism","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In total 99 crew members were recognised at the awards. Of these, 98 were white"),SRCQ("TV Craft Awards: lifting the lid on structural racism – Media Reform Coalition","https://www.mediareform.org.uk/blog/tv-craft-awards-lifting-the-lid-on-structural-racism","Supports the Recognition and representation page","In total 99 crew members were recognised at the awards. Of these, 98 were white (more on the sole black crew member later)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Football Black List: Black-led recognition since 2008",text:"Leon Mann and Rodney Hinds founded the Football Black List in 2008 to recognise leaders of African and Caribbean heritage across football. The Premier League has sponsored it for nine years running. It is recognition created by Black professionals themselves, alongside the mainstream awards.",figure:"Annual since 2008",year:"2008–",disputed:false,note:"The sources give no running total of people honoured. Sports Personality of the Year and Olympic medals have no published ethnicity analysis (see rejected).",src:[SRCQ("Football Black List celebrates diversity, says Premier League – Sky Sports","https://skysports.com/football/news/11095/11867270/football-black-list-celebrates-diversity-says-premier-league","Supports the Recognition and representation page","The initiative was established by Leon Mann and Rodney Hinds in 2008"),SRCQ("Football Black List – Premier League","https://www.premierleague.com/news/4298773","Supports the Recognition and representation page","to highlight leaders of African and Caribbean heritage working and volunteering across all areas of football")],kind:"argument"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Nominate people for honours: the Cabinet Office publishes no data on who is nominated, so low nominations stay invisible.",refs:["aw-ukhonours-no-published-ethnicity-data-on-honours-n", "aw-ukhonours-new-year-honours-2025-black-recipients-3"]},{t:"Black-led awards and schemes exist because mainstream recognition lagged; they are part of the record too.",refs:["aw-arts-mobo-awards-founded-in-1996-to-recognise", "aw-arts-jhalak-prize-created-in-2016-for-british", "aw-professions-football-black-list-black-led-recognitio"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Read 'first Black winner' stories with care: a first after 50 or 172 years is also a measure of who was missed.",refs:["aw-arts-booker-prize-first-black-woman-winner-in", "aw-arts-riba-royal-gold-medal-first-black-recipi"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"When you nominate staff for awards, check who you nominate as well as who wins.",refs:["aw-ukhonours-no-published-ethnicity-data-on-honours-n", "aw-professions-bafta-tv-craft-2018-1-black-crew-member-"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Funders and awarding bodies can publish applications and awards by ethnicity, as UKRI and the Royal Society do, so gaps can be seen.",refs:["aw-funding-ukri-black-lead-applicants-won-13-of-bid", "aw-funding-royal-society-2023-24-0-offers-to-45-bla"]},{t:"Who votes matters: academies widened membership after all-white nominee lists.",refs:["aw-screen-2012-academy-voters-were-94-white-and-2-", "aw-screen-academy-after-2025-22-from-underrepresen"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Compare like with like: say who counted, the period, and whether a figure covers Black people alone or a wider grouping.",refs:["aw-science-black-nobel-laureates-published-counts-d", "aw-screen-news-desk-counts-black-actors-had-9-to-1"]},{t:"Where an analyst's count is the only one, label it as such and look for a second.",refs:["aw-screen-black-actors-69-of-1-021-oscar-acting-no"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Black recipients were 43 of 1,177 (3.7%) in the 2025 New Year Honours.",refs:["aw-ukhonours-new-year-honours-2025-black-recipients-3"]},{t:"No Black laureate has won a Nobel Prize in physics, chemistry or medicine: 0 of 617 to 2019.",refs:["aw-science-no-black-laureate-in-physics-chemistry-o"]},{t:"Black actors had 69 of 1,021 Oscar acting nominations from 1970 to 2020.",refs:["aw-screen-black-actors-69-of-1-021-oscar-acting-no"]},{t:"UKRI's award rate was 13% for Black lead applicants and 34% for White in 2023–24.",refs:["aw-funding-ukri-black-lead-applicants-won-13-of-bid"]},{t:"Black recipients hold 93 of 3,515 US Medals of Honor (2.6%).",refs:["aw-military-black-medal-of-honor-recipients-92-93-of"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["ethdata"]={name:"Collecting ethnicity data",pfx:"ed",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"how",h:"How to capture ethnicity data",intro:"UK guidance is consistent: ask people to describe themselves, use the harmonised census-based question, always allow them to decline, and treat the answer as special category data with a clear lawful condition.",points:[
  {h:"Use the GSS harmonised question based on the 2021 Census",text:"The Race Disparity Unit's Standards for ethnicity data (April 2023) recommend the Government Statistical Service (GSS) harmonised standard, which follows the 2021 Census tick boxes for England and Wales. The government's 2026 pay gap proposals reuse the same list: 5 high-level groups and 19 options plus 'Prefer not to say'.",figure:"19 options + prefer not to say",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The GSS is redesigning the standard (research published June 2025); categories differ in Scotland and Northern Ireland. The count of 19 options is derived from the Annex C list.",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (Race Disparity Unit, April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","The RDU’s preferred approach is to use these standards to collect ethnicity data."),SRCQ("Review of the ethnicity harmonised standard: overview of redesign research (GSS, June 2025)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/review-of-the-ethnicity-harmonised-standard-overview-of-our-redesign-research-to-date","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Based on England and Wales Census 2021 tick boxes, informed by a prioritisation tool"),SRCQ("Annex C: GSS harmonised categories for ethnicity (pay gap reporting consultation, updated March 2026)","https://gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/annex-c-government-statistical-service-harmonised-categories-for-ethnicity","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","(Choose one option that best describes your ethnic group or background)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Let people describe themselves; proxy answers are lower quality",text:"The Race Disparity Unit says ethnicity reported by a third party is of lower quality than self-reported ethnicity. The GSS redesign keeps the question self-defined, and Home Office stop and search statistics treat self-defined ethnicity as the preferred measure.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (Race Disparity Unit, April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","will be of lower quality than when someone reports their own ethnicity"),SRCQ("Review of the ethnicity harmonised standard: overview of redesign research (GSS, June 2025)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/review-of-the-ethnicity-harmonised-standard-overview-of-our-redesign-research-to-date","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","The question will not define ethnicity, respecting its self-defined and personal nature."),SRCQ("Police powers and procedures: stop and search, year ending 31 March 2024 (Home Office)","https://gov.uk/government/statistics/stop-and-search-arrests-and-mental-health-detentions-march-2024/police-powers-and-procedures-stop-and-search-arrests-and-mental-health-detentions-england-and-wales-year-ending-31-march-2024","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","is the preferred measure of ethnicity")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Always offer 'Prefer not to say'",text:"The 2023 standards say every collection should include a 'prefer not to say' option. The Civil Service diversity form and the 2026 pay gap proposals both include it.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Separating 'prefer not to say' from 'not yet asked' matters for interpreting gaps (see Barriers).",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (Race Disparity Unit, April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Your data collection should also have a ‘prefer not to say’ option for people who do not want to give their ethnicity."),SRCQ("Annex C: GSS harmonised categories for ethnicity (pay gap reporting consultation, updated March 2026)","https://gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/annex-c-government-statistical-service-harmonised-categories-for-ethnicity","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Prefer not to say (for employees who do not wish to disclose their ethnicity)"),SRCQ("Annex A diversity form (Civil Service public appointment application)","https://apply-for-public-appointment.service.gov.uk/roles/6780/documents/2","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Prefer not to say")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Give write-in space for 'Any other' groups",text:"The 2021 Census used search-as-you-type write-in boxes for the 'Any other' options, and the ONS reported more people using the write-in options than in 2011. The GSS is testing a single write-in space for its revised standard.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The RDU warns that changing options can affect comparability over time. Write-ins depend on whether the collecting system can process them.",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (Race Disparity Unit, April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","‘search-as-you-type’ boxes were used for the ‘Any other…’ write-in option"),SRCQ("Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion quality information for Census 2021 (ONS)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/methodologies/ethnicgroupnationalidentitylanguageandreligionqualityinformationforcensus2021","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","There was an increase in the number of people using one of the write-in response options to the ethnic group question."),SRCQ("Review of the ethnicity harmonised standard: overview of redesign research (GSS, June 2025)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/review-of-the-ethnicity-harmonised-standard-overview-of-our-redesign-research-to-date","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","we are testing a single, clearly positioned space for write-in responses")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Treat ethnicity as special category data with a Schedule 1 condition",text:"Under UK GDPR, data revealing racial or ethnic origin needs both an Article 6 lawful basis and an Article 9 condition. Paragraph 8 of Schedule 1 to the Data Protection Act 2018 ('equality of opportunity or treatment') is the usual condition for monitoring, and requires an appropriate policy document. Public bodies such as the University of Cambridge rely on it and keep monitoring data separate.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The ICO says this guidance is under review following the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025.",src:[SRCQ("What is special category data? (ICO)","https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/special-category-data/what-is-special-category-data/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin"),SRCQ("Special category data (ICO guide to lawful basis)","https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/a-guide-to-lawful-basis/special-category-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Equality of opportunity or treatment"),SRCQ("What are the rules on special category data? (ICO)","https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/special-category-data/what-are-the-rules-on-special-category-data/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","must identify both a lawful basis under Article 6"),SRCQ("Appropriate policy document (University of Cambridge)","https://www.information-compliance.admin.cam.ac.uk/files/appropriate_policy_document.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","it is processed under this condition, is kept separate from other personal data,")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Do not infer ethnicity from names, photos or appearance",text:"The ICO says that if you intend to infer ethnicity, for example from a name or image, you are processing special category data however confident you are. ONS linkage shows records not based on self-report often disagree with the census: below 76% agreement for Mixed groups and below 72% for Other groups.",figure:"<76% agreement for Mixed groups",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The ONS agreement figures compare health records with Census 2021 and do not show how each record was captured. The two ICO extracts come from one page.",src:[SRCQ("What is special category data? (ICO)","https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/special-category-data/what-is-special-category-data/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","you may be able to infer a person's religion or ethnicity from their name or images of them"),SRCQ("What is special category data? (ICO)","https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/special-category-data/what-is-special-category-data/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","regardless of how confident you are that the inference is correct"),SRCQ("Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (ONS, November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/november2023","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Across all sources, agreement for Mixed categories was below 76%, and for Other categories below 72%.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Suppress small numbers and publish declaration rates",text:"The 2023 standards advise suppressing or rounding counts based on small numbers of people. The government's March 2026 pay gap response plans a minimum group size and requires employers to report declaration rates. In the 2018–19 consultation, 84.8% of respondents backed reporting declaration rates.",figure:"84.8% backed reporting declaration rates",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The minimum group size for pay gap reporting had not been set by March 2026.",src:[SRCQ("Standards for ethnicity data (Race Disparity Unit, April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","not disclosing data based on a small number of people, or by rounding numbers"),SRCQ("Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: consultation response (UK Government, March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69c14fded588c92c483e4bb1/2026-03-17_-_Pay_Gap_-_Main_Large_Print_ELAY_FINAL.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting will include requirements to report declaration rates."),SRCQ("Ethnicity pay reporting: government consultation response (2023)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/1165466/ethnicity_pay_reporting_consultation_response.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","It is not possible to draw reliable statistics when data is based on too few values.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"when",h:"When to capture it: stage of the process",intro:"Where collection happens affects both completeness and bias. The strongest pattern is to ask at the first formal contact, such as an application or registration, and to keep answers away from anyone making the decision.",points:[
  {h:"Recruitment: ask at application, hide it from the sift",text:"The Civil Service diversity form says answers will not be seen by anyone involved in selection. Since December 2015, name-blind recruitment has hidden names, addresses and nationality from sifting panels for most external roles below the Senior Civil Service.",figure:"Name-blind default since 2015",year:"2015",disputed:false,note:"The 2015 blog post does not say how monitoring forms are handled, and the form is undated.",src:[SRCQ("Annex A diversity form (Civil Service public appointment application)","https://apply-for-public-appointment.service.gov.uk/roles/6780/documents/2","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","will not be seen by anybody directly involved in the selection process"),SRCQ("Name blind recruitment: responding to your comments (Civil Service blog, December 2015)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2015/12/01/name-blind-recruitment-responding-to-your-comments/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","immediately")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Evidence of name bias supports separating identity from sifting",text:"A field experiment by the Centre for Social Investigation at Nuffield College sent applications to nearly 3,200 real jobs. Applicants from ethnic minority backgrounds had to send 60% more applications to get a positive response.",figure:"60% more applications",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"The study measured callbacks, not the effect of anonymising. The second source shows the policy response, not the evidence.",src:[SRCQ("New CSI report on ethnic minority job discrimination (Centre for Social Investigation, Nuffield College, January 2019)","https://www.nuffield.ox.ac.uk/news-events/news/new-csi-report-on-ethnic-minority-job-discrimination/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Applicants from minority ethnic backgrounds have to send 60% more applications to get a positive response from an employer."),SRCQ("Name blind recruitment: responding to your comments (Civil Service blog, December 2015)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2015/12/01/name-blind-recruitment-responding-to-your-comments/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","immediately")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Anonymised shortlisting alone has shown mixed results",text:"Canada's Public Service Commission pilot covered more than 2,200 candidates, 685 of them visible minorities. Removing identifying information had no effect on managers' screening decisions. An Australian Public Service trial with more than 2,100 staff found de-identification may hinder diversity efforts.",figure:"No effect for 685 candidates (Canada)",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"These are non-UK public service trials. Anonymisation addresses bias in decisions, not the completeness of monitoring data.",src:[SRCQ("Release of report on Name-Blind Recruitment Pilot Project (Public Service Commission of Canada, January 2018)","https://www.canada.ca/en/public-service-commission/news/2018/01/title.html","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","had no effect on the screening decisions of managers"),SRCQ("Going blind to see more clearly: unconscious bias in Australian Public Services shortlisting processes (APO record)","https://mcp.apo.org.au/node/97036","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","de-identification may frustrate efforts aimed at promoting diversity")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Grants and fellowships: collect at application, hidden from panels",text:"UKRI collects ethnicity through Je-S and its Funding Service, and its panels do not see applicants' demographic data. The Royal Society makes its Flexi-Grant diversity questions mandatory but includes 'prefer not to say'. Wellcome reports ethnicity for 87.3% of lead applicants.",figure:"Wellcome: 87.3% of lead applicants",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Wellcome's page does not say when or how it collects the data. The Royal Society does not say whether assessors see responses.",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024 (UKRI, June 2025)","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","panels making funding decisions do not have access to applicants’ demographic data"),SRCQ("Diversity data 2023-24 (Royal Society)","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2023-24.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Whilst the Society's online data collection methods make completion of the questions on the diversity form mandatory"),SRCQ("Funding data 2024 (Wellcome)","https://wellcome.org/research-funding/funding-portfolio/funding-data-2024","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","have not provided data, have responded 'prefer not to say'")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Honours and screen awards: collect after selection, kept confidential",text:"The Cabinet Office asks honours recipients to complete an anonymous monitoring form when they are offered their honour, not at nomination. The Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences collects a confidential inclusion form for Best Picture films, which has been a condition of eligibility since the 96th Oscars (2024).",figure:"Collected at offer (honours)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Collecting only at award stage cannot show whether nominations or shortlists are representative. This could not be confirmed for BAFTA or Arts Council grant practice from the pages read.",src:[SRCQ("Honours diversity (Cabinet Office Honours and Appointments Secretariat)","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Recipients are asked to anonymously complete a diversity monitoring form when they are offered their honour."),SRCQ("Academy establishes representation and inclusion standards for Oscars eligibility (Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences)","https://oscars.org/news/academy-establishes-representation-and-inclusion-standards-oscarsr-eligibility","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","confidential Academy Inclusion Standards form"),SRCQ("Oscars inclusion rules sparking debate (The Hollywood Reporter)","https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/oscars-inclusion-rules-sparking-debate-1235343128/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","The organization also has made it easier to add information anonymously, in response to concerns about privacy.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Data supplied later has bigger gaps than data given at application",text:"In UKRI's 2023–24 data, ethnicity was not disclosed for 26% of studentship starts and unknown for another 9%. Research organisations supply studentship data after award. For principal investigators, who answer at application, about 7% was not disclosed. In Arts Council England's 2021/22 data, ethnicity was 'not known' for 30% of contractual staff and 6% of permanent staff.",figure:"35% missing vs about 7%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The principal investigator rate is derived from 970 not disclosed out of about 13,070 awardees, 2019–20 to 2023–24. Other differences between the groups may also explain the gap.",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024 (UKRI, June 2025)","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","ethnicity was not disclosed for 26% of studentship starts"),SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: A Data Report 2021-2022 (Arts Council England)","https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/media/22476/download","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","unknown")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Public services: record it at registration or first contact",text:"Schools must return an ethnicity code for every pupil, recording 'NOBT' if not yet obtained and 'REFU' if a parent or pupil declines. In stop and search, officers ask people their self-defined ethnicity at the time of the search. NHS hospital guidance on recording ethnicity had not been updated since 2001 when the Nuffield Trust reviewed it in 2021.",figure:"NHS guidance from 2001",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"NHS England may have updated its guidance since 2021. This is not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("2025 to 2026 school census business and technical specification v1.4 (Department for Education)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/696774f897d42030a67b0d71/2025_to_2026_school_census_business_and_technical_specification_v1.4.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Where the ethnicity has not yet been collected this is recorded as 'NOBT' (information not yet obtained)."),SRCQ("Police powers and procedures: stop and search, year ending 31 March 2024 (Home Office)","https://gov.uk/government/statistics/stop-and-search-arrests-and-mental-health-detentions-march-2024/police-powers-and-procedures-stop-and-search-arrests-and-mental-health-detentions-england-and-wales-year-ending-31-march-2024","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","is the preferred measure of ethnicity"),SRCQ("Flawed NHS records distort our view of ethnic health gaps, study shows (Nuffield Trust with NHS Race and Health Observatory, June 2021)","https://nuffieldtrust.org.uk/news-item/flawed-nhs-records-distort-our-view-of-ethnic-health-gaps-study-shows","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","NHS guidance has not been updated since 2001")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"barriers",h:"What stops people and organisations disclosing",intro:"Individuals hold back mainly because they distrust how the data will be used or the categories do not fit them. Organisations cite legal uncertainty, low response, small numbers and cost.",points:[
  {h:"Unclear purpose and low trust",text:"In participatory research with 321 Black and South Asian people (2021–22), participants doubted that data was used as stated and asked what it was for. GSS research found people accept ethnicity questions asked for the 'right purposes' but can be annoyed when the reason is unclear.",figure:"321 participants",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Qualitative findings; they cannot be generalised to whole populations.",src:[SRCQ("Diverse voices on data: main report (ClearView Research for Understanding Patient Data, 2022)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-04/Diverse%20voices%20on%20Data%20-%20Main%20report_0.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","I am wary because I don't think the data is used for the purpose it is supposed to be used for."),SRCQ("Review of ethnicity harmonised standard: findings from phase 2 (GSS, July 2023)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/review-of-ethnicity-harmonised-standard-findings-from-phase-2-of-our-research/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","right purposes"),SRCQ("Employers unable to report on ethnicity pay due to missing data (CIPD, February 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/uk/about/news/employers-unable-to-report-ethnicity-pay-due-to-missing-data/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","danger of a psychological impact for people in trusting, particularly if their trust has been broken before")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Fear that disclosure leads to worse treatment",text:"Black African participants in the Understanding Patient Data research said they expected disclosing their ethnicity to affect the care they were offered. Field evidence of hiring discrimination, where minority applicants needed 60% more applications, shows this fear has some basis.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"No published UK survey quantified fear of tokenism in disclosure among the sources read.",src:[SRCQ("Diverse voices on data: main report (ClearView Research for Understanding Patient Data, 2022)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-04/Diverse%20voices%20on%20Data%20-%20Main%20report_0.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","I feel ethnicity will influence the kind of treatment I am offered, and I can guarantee you it won't be the best treatment."),SRCQ("New CSI report on ethnic minority job discrimination (Centre for Social Investigation, Nuffield College, January 2019)","https://www.nuffield.ox.ac.uk/news-events/news/new-csi-report-on-ethnic-minority-job-discrimination/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Applicants from minority ethnic backgrounds have to send 60% more applications to get a positive response from an employer.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Categories do not fit Mixed, African and Caribbean people well",text:"GSS research found the Mixed options all start with 'White and…', and that some people feel marginalised choosing 'Other'. In health records, agreement with the census was 70% to 86% for Black Caribbean and Black African, and below 20% for Other Black in some datasets.",figure:"Other Black below 20% agreement",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The ONS agreement figures are for Hospital Episode Statistics and Talking Therapies datasets.",src:[SRCQ("Review of ethnicity harmonised standard: findings from phase 2 (GSS, July 2023)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/review-of-ethnicity-harmonised-standard-findings-from-phase-2-of-our-research/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Not everyone that has a mixed heritage is mixed with white."),SRCQ("Diverse voices on data: main report (ClearView Research for Understanding Patient Data, 2022)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-04/Diverse%20voices%20on%20Data%20-%20Main%20report_0.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","I have parents from both backgrounds, and that's never really an option."),SRCQ("Diverse voices on data: main report (ClearView Research for Understanding Patient Data, 2022)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-04/Diverse%20voices%20on%20Data%20-%20Main%20report_0.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","I always put Black other and that just makes me feel silly."),SRCQ("Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (ONS, November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/november2023","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Other Black below 20%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Feeling compelled rather than asked",text:"Some participants felt they had no choice about giving personal details. Bodies that make the question mandatory, such as the Royal Society, add a 'prefer not to say' option, but not everyone answers.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Diverse voices on data: main report (ClearView Research for Understanding Patient Data, 2022)","https://understandingpatientdata.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-04/Diverse%20voices%20on%20Data%20-%20Main%20report_0.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","I feel like I am forced to provide my personal details as I am not given a choice whether or not to fill it in."),SRCQ("Diversity data 2023-24 (Royal Society)","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/about-us/who-we-are/diversity/diversity-data-2023-24.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","not all respondents choose to provide their diversity information")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Employers' legal uncertainty about UK GDPR",text:"In a PwC survey of more than 100 organisations, cited in the government's 2023 consultation response, 50% named legal and GDPR requirements as a barrier to collecting ethnicity data. The ICO's guidance sets out a specific condition for equality monitoring.",figure:"50% cited GDPR",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The PwC survey is reported second-hand in the government document. The ICO source shows a lawful route exists; it does not measure employers' views.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity pay reporting: government consultation response (2023)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/1165466/ethnicity_pay_reporting_consultation_response.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","legal and General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) requirements as a barrier"),SRCQ("Special category data (ICO guide to lawful basis)","https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/lawful-basis/a-guide-to-lawful-basis/special-category-data","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Equality of opportunity or treatment")],kind:"argument"},
  {h:"Low response and small numbers make data unusable",text:"The government's 2023 response cited PwC findings that about 47% of organisations collecting ethnicity data could not publish a pay gap because too few staff responded. Almost 35% collected none. Respondents to the 2025 consultation also warned of low declaration rates and risks to anonymity in small groups.",figure:"47% could not publish",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The 47% and 35% figures come from one PwC survey.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity pay reporting: government consultation response (2023)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/1165466/ethnicity_pay_reporting_consultation_response.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","It is not possible to draw reliable statistics when data is based on too few values."),SRCQ("The future of ethnicity pay gap reporting (Linklaters, July 2023)","https://www.linklaters.com/insights/blogs/employmentlinks/2023/july/the-future-of-ethnicity-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","poor or insufficient response rates"),SRCQ("Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: consultation response (UK Government, March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69c14fded588c92c483e4bb1/2026-03-17_-_Pay_Gap_-_Main_Large_Print_ELAY_FINAL.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","might lead to low declaration rates, which would affect the robustness of the pay gap data being reported")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cost, systems and missing records, more often than refusal",text:"Some employers told government that changing HR systems would be costly. In the Civil Service (March 2025), 76,190 staff had no ethnicity recorded and 20,760 had actively declined. In Arts Council England's workforce data, 21% were 'not known' and 4% 'prefer not to say'. Most gaps reflect people not being asked or not responding, rather than refusing.",figure:"76,190 unknown vs 20,760 declined",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The Civil Service counts are rounded to the nearest 5. The cost point is from the tool summary of the 2023 response, and the response gave few cost estimates. UKRI studentships run the other way: 26% not disclosed against 9% unknown.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Statistics data browser 2025: ethnicity (Cabinet Office)","https://www.civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2025/Ethnicity/index.html","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","actively declared that they do not want to disclose their ethnicity"),SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: A Data Report 2021-2022 (Arts Council England)","https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/media/22476/download","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","prefer not to say"),SRCQ("Ethnicity pay reporting: government consultation response (2023)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/1165466/ethnicity_pay_reporting_consultation_response.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","It is not possible to draw reliable statistics when data is based on too few values.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"attitudes",h:"Attitudes and declaration rates by sector",intro:"Where ethnicity is asked routinely and its purpose explained, declaration is high, but completeness varies widely between sectors. Published evidence on willingness by ethnic group is thin and mostly qualitative.",points:[
  {h:"Civil Service: about 4 in 5 staff have a recorded ethnicity",text:"At 31 March 2025, 452,710 of 549,660 civil servants (about 82%) had declared an ethnicity. Of those, 18.0% were from ethnic minority backgrounds, up from 16.6% in 2024. In 2024, 19% of civil servants did not report their ethnicity.",figure:"82.4% declared (2025)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The 82.4% rate is calculated from the data browser's rounded counts (undeclared 20,760; unknown 76,190) and is not a published headline.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Statistics data browser 2025: ethnicity (Cabinet Office)","https://www.civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2025/Ethnicity/index.html","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","actively declared that they do not want to disclose their ethnicity"),SRCQ("Civil Service workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/civil-service-workforce/latest","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","19% of civil servants did not report their ethnicity in 2024."),SRCQ("Civil Service Statistics 2025 (Civil Service World, July 2025)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/civil-service-statistics-2025-disability-ethnic-minority-record-high","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Of those with a known ethnicity, 18% are from an ethnic-minority background, up from 16.6% in 2024")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"NHS workforce: under 5% unknown",text:"NHS England's WRES 2024 report says 4.3% of trust staff did not disclose their ethnicity in March 2024. Using the source's 'BME' grouping, it put that group at 28.6% of the trust workforce (434,077 people). In 2019, ethnicity was unknown for 4.7% of NHS staff, who report their own ethnicity.",figure:"4.3% undisclosed (2024)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"The 4.3% figure is from the tool summary of the WRES report and is not directly quoted.",src:[SRCQ("Workforce Race Equality Standard 2024 data analysis report (NHS England, 2025)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Workforce-Race-Equality-Standard-2024-data-analysis-report.docx","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","28.6% of the workforce across NHS trusts came from a BME background."),SRCQ("NHS workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, March 2019 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/nhs-workforce/3.1","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Ethnicity wasn't known for 4.7% of NHS staff.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Legal profession: 4% of lawyers prefer not to say",text:"In the SRA's summer 2025 collection, 99.5% of law firms reported data on more than 225,000 people. 4% of lawyers preferred not to declare their ethnicity, the same share as in 2023.",figure:"4% prefer not to say",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Firms report aggregate figures, so 'not asked' cases may not be visible.",src:[SRCQ("Diversity in law firms' workforce (Solicitors Regulation Authority, 2025 data)","https://www.sra.org.uk/sra/research-publications/diversity-law-firms-workforce","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","preferred not to declare their ethnicity"),SRCQ("SRA: profession making progress on diversity (Legal Futures, 2023)","https://www.legalfutures.co.uk/latest-news/sra-profession-making-progress-on-diversity-especially-at-small-firms/print/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","with 4% preferring not to say")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Research funders: high disclosure, and the data shows award gaps",text:"About 7% of UKRI principal investigator awardees did not disclose their ethnicity (2019–20 to 2023–24). Five-year award rates were 31% for White, 22% for Asian and 14% for Black principal investigators, and 33% for those not disclosing. Wellcome reports 2024/25 UK success rates of 15.4% for White lead applicants and 12.2% for others.",figure:"UKRI award rate: Black 14%, White 31%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"UKRI award rates are from the tool summary of Table 11. People who did not disclose had the highest award rate, so non-disclosure may not be random.",src:[SRCQ("UKRI equalities monitoring 2023 to 2024 (UKRI, June 2025)","https://www.ukri.org/publications/ukri-equalities-monitoring/ukri-equalities-monitoring-2023-to-2024/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","‘Not disclosed’ refers to when respondents have consciously chosen to not disclose their personal information"),SRCQ("Funding data 2024 (Wellcome)","https://wellcome.org/research-funding/funding-portfolio/funding-data-2024","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","have not provided data, have responded 'prefer not to say'")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Honours: share with unknown ethnicity is disputed",text:"Ethnicity facts and figures says ethnicity was not known for 0.6% of recipients in December 2024 (7 of 1,177). The Honours and Appointments Secretariat says roughly 10% of recipients on any list do not provide it. 12.5% of December 2024 recipients were from Asian, Black, Mixed or Other groups.",figure:"0.6% vs roughly 10% unknown",year:"2024",disputed:true,note:"The two figures may use different denominators or counting rules. Neither page explains the difference.",src:[SRCQ("Honours recipients (Ethnicity facts and figures)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/honours-recipients/latest","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Ethnicity was not known for 0.6% of honours recipients in December 2024."),SRCQ("Honours diversity (Cabinet Office Honours and Appointments Secretariat)","https://honours.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/about/diversity","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","roughly 10% of recipients on any list do not provide this.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Arts and culture: large gaps outside permanent staff",text:"In Arts Council England's 2021/22 data from 806 organisations, ethnicity was 'not known' for 21% of the workforce and 'prefer not to say' for 4%. Gaps were higher for artists (29% not known) and volunteers (29%). In museums, the source's 'Black, Asian or ethnically diverse' share was 7% in 2022/23.",figure:"21% not known",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"'Unknown' can mean the organisation did not ask the question.",src:[SRCQ("Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: A Data Report 2021-2022 (Arts Council England)","https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/media/22476/download","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","not known"),SRCQ("National portfolio museums are becoming more diverse, ACE report finds (Museums Association, March 2025)","https://www.museumsassociation.org/museums-journal/news/2025/03/national-portfolio-museums-are-becoming-more-diverse-ace-report-finds/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Black, Asian or ethnically diverse")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Health records: mostly complete now, but less accurate for some",text:"A 2023 study found ethnicity recorded for 82.3% of currently registered patients in CPRD Aurum GP data. ONS found 89.9% agreement with Census 2021 in GP-derived data. In 2019/20, 13% of inpatients and 17% of outpatients had no known ethnicity recorded.",figure:"82.3% recorded (GP, 2021)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Completeness among all patients ever registered is much lower: 58.0% in Aurum and 36.5% in GOLD.",src:[SRCQ("Completeness, agreement, and representativeness of ethnicity recording in CPRD and linked HES (Population Health Metrics, March 2023)","https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12963-023-00302-0","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","ethnicity data recorded in CPRD-HES data is available for the majority of currently registered patients"),SRCQ("Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (ONS, November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/november2023","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","89.9%"),SRCQ("Flawed NHS records distort our view of ethnic health gaps, study shows (Nuffield Trust with NHS Race and Health Observatory, June 2021)","https://nuffieldtrust.org.uk/news-item/flawed-nhs-records-distort-our-view-of-ethnic-health-gaps-study-shows","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","13% of inpatients and 17% of outpatients")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Police: self-defined ethnicity missing in about 1 in 5 searches",text:"In the year ending March 2024, self-defined ethnicity was missing for 19.3% of stop and searches in England and Wales, up from 5.0% in 2014. It was 31.3% in the Metropolitan Police area. Suffolk reported 22% missing for local people searched in 2024–25.",figure:"19.3% not stated",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Using officer-observed ethnicity as a fallback cuts the unknown share to 1.6%, but that is an inferred measure.",src:[SRCQ("Police powers and procedures: stop and search, year ending 31 March 2024 (Home Office)","https://gov.uk/government/statistics/stop-and-search-arrests-and-mental-health-detentions-march-2024/police-powers-and-procedures-stop-and-search-arrests-and-mental-health-detentions-england-and-wales-year-ending-31-march-2024","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","the level of missing ethnicity data (self-defined) was 19.3%"),SRCQ("Use of Police Powers report, 16 January 2026 (Suffolk Police and Crime Commissioner)","https://suffolk-pcc.gov.uk/assets/documents/2026/01/c9cc786ff4/Accountability-and-Performance-Panel-Use-of-Police-Powers-Report-16-January-2026.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","22% of records for local subjects had no self-defined ethnicity recorded.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Employers: collection has been patchy",text:"In a CIPD survey of 250 HR practitioners (2019), 53% said their organisation collected some ethnicity data. In BITC's 2018 survey of 6,506 employees, 11% said their employer collected ethnicity pay gap data. By 2023, 44% of Race at Work Charter signatories published an ethnicity pay gap.",figure:"53% collect (CIPD 2019)",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"These are different samples. Charter signatories are likely to be more engaged than employers in general.",src:[SRCQ("Employers unable to report on ethnicity pay due to missing data (CIPD, February 2019)","https://www.cipd.org/uk/about/news/employers-unable-to-report-ethnicity-pay-due-to-missing-data/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","just over half (53%) reported that they collect some form of ethnicity data in their organisation"),SRCQ("Race at Work 2018: The Scorecard Report (Business in the Community)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/746970/BITC_Race_At_Work_Report.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","In 2018, just 11% of employees reported that their organisation collects data on the ethnicity pay gap ratio"),SRCQ("44% of employers voluntarily publish their ethnicity pay gap (HR Review, October 2023, reporting BITC)","https://hrreview.co.uk/?p=372959","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","from 30 percent in 2020 to 44 percent in 2023")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Stated willingness is higher than recorded disclosure",text:"In a 2022 OnePoll survey for INvolve of 1,500 employees, 95% said they were willing to disclose their ethnicity to their employer. In administrative data, about 18–19% of civil servants have no recorded ethnicity. Survey-based and qualitative evidence also differs: participants in the Understanding Patient Data research expressed mistrust.",figure:"95% willing vs ~82% recorded",year:"2022",disputed:true,note:"Commercial poll; no breakdown by ethnic group was published. No large published UK survey breaking down willingness to disclose by ethnic group was found.",src:[SRCQ("Majority of employees are happy to share ethnicity data, poll finds (People Management, May 2022)","https://www.peoplemanagement.co.uk/article/1787358/majority-employees-happy-share-ethnicity-data-poll-finds","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","Almost all employees are happy to share their ethnicity data"),SRCQ("Civil Service workforce (Ethnicity facts and figures, October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/civil-service-workforce/latest","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","19% of civil servants did not report their ethnicity in 2024.")],kind:"counter-argument"},
  {h:"Public trust and the pay gap reporting law",text:"In the government's 2024 tracker, 73% trusted the NHS to use data effectively. Adults of Asian or Black heritage were more likely to feel in control of who uses their data. The March 2026 response (857 replies) confirmed mandatory ethnicity pay gap reporting for employers with 250+ staff. The Equality (Race and Disability) Bill was not in the May 2026 King's Speech and had not been introduced.",figure:"73% trust NHS with data",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Bill status checked against sources dated 12 to 13 May 2026; later changes are not covered here. The earlier 2018–19 consultation had 321 responses, and the 2023 response decided against mandatory reporting at that stage.",src:[SRCQ("Public attitudes to data and AI: Tracker survey (Wave 4) report (UK government, Responsible Technology Adoption Unit, December 2024)","https://gov.uk/government/publications/public-attitudes-to-data-and-ai-tracker-survey-wave-4/public-attitudes-to-data-and-ai-tracker-survey-wave-4-report","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","is stronger among White adults compared with those of an ethnic minority background"),SRCQ("Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: consultation response (UK Government, March 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69c14fded588c92c483e4bb1/2026-03-17_-_Pay_Gap_-_Main_Large_Print_ELAY_FINAL.pdf","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","There were 857 responses to the consultation."),SRCQ("Is the government on course to introduce a new Race Equality Act? (Full Fact, updated 12 May 2026)","https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/race-equality-act/","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","legislation has not yet been formally introduced to Parliament"),SRCQ("Equality (Race and Disability) Bill absent from King's Speech (LexisNexis, 13 May 2026)","https://www.lexisnexis.co.uk/legal/news/equality-race-disability-bill-absent-from-kings-speech","Supports the Collecting ethnicity data page","The Government's Equality (Race and Disability) Bill was notably absent from today's King's Speech.")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"You can always choose 'prefer not to say'; organisations should tell you why they ask and who will see the answer.",refs:["ed-how-always-offer-prefer-not-to-say", "ed-barriers-unclear-purpose-and-low-trust"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Never guess a colleague's ethnicity from a name or photo; the data protection regulator treats a guess as sensitive data.",refs:["ed-how-do-not-infer-ethnicity-from-names-photos"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Ask at application or onboarding, keep answers away from whoever decides, and explain the purpose.",refs:["ed-when-recruitment-ask-at-application-hide-it-f", "ed-barriers-unclear-purpose-and-low-trust"]},{t:"Most gaps are people never asked, not refusals: ask again, and publish your declaration rate.",refs:["ed-barriers-cost-systems-and-missing-records-more-of", "ed-how-suppress-small-numbers-and-publish-decla"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Collect for monitoring only, never for decisions about an individual, under a stated lawful condition.",refs:["ed-how-treat-ethnicity-as-special-category-data"]},{t:"Data supplied after an award or appointment has bigger gaps than data given at application.",refs:["ed-when-data-supplied-later-has-bigger-gaps-than"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Use the harmonised census-based question, with write-in options, so data can be compared.",refs:["ed-how-use-the-gss-harmonised-question-based-on", "ed-how-give-write-in-space-for-any-other-groups"]},{t:"Suppress small counts, and check agreement for Mixed and Other Black groups, where records are least accurate.",refs:["ed-how-suppress-small-numbers-and-publish-decla", "ed-barriers-categories-do-not-fit-mixed-african-and-"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"UK guidance says people describe themselves, with 'prefer not to say' always offered.",refs:["ed-how-let-people-describe-themselves-proxy-ans", "ed-how-always-offer-prefer-not-to-say"]},{t:"Never infer ethnicity from a name or photo; that is still sensitive data.",refs:["ed-how-do-not-infer-ethnicity-from-names-photos"]},{t:"About 7% of UKRI lead researchers did not disclose, against 35% for studentships supplied after award.",refs:["ed-when-data-supplied-later-has-bigger-gaps-than"]},{t:"In the Civil Service, 76,190 staff had no ethnicity recorded and 20,760 declined.",refs:["ed-barriers-cost-systems-and-missing-records-more-of"]},{t:"Self-defined ethnicity was missing in 19.3% of stop and searches.",refs:["ed-attitudes-police-self-defined-ethnicity-missing-in"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["designing"]={name:"Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people",pfx:"dg",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"policy",h:"Policy design: the equality duty, impact assessment and evaluation",intro:"Public bodies in England, Scotland and Wales have a legal duty to think about equality before they decide. Good policy for Black and Mixed-heritage people starts with specific evidence, real involvement and evaluation that looks at different groups.",points:[
  {h:"The public sector equality duty applies to every function",text:"Section 149 of the Equality Act 2010 requires public authorities to have due regard to the need to eliminate discrimination, advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations. It also applies to other organisations, including contractors, when they carry out public functions, so it covers policy, services, content and data decisions that affect Black and Mixed-heritage people.",figure:"",year:"2010",disputed:false,note:"Race is one of the protected characteristics listed in section 149(7). The duty is about the decision process (due regard); it does not dictate a particular outcome. Applies in England, Scotland and Wales.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 149 Public sector equality duty","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","A public authority must, in the exercise of its functions, have due regard to the need to—"),SRCQ("Equality and Human Rights Commission: Public Sector Equality Duty (PSED) (28 June 2022)","https://www.equalityhumanrights.com/guidance/public-sector-equality-duty-psed","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Organisations carrying out public functions are private businesses or volunteer organisations that are contracted to work on behalf of public authorities.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Due regard means meeting different needs, not only avoiding harm",text:"The duty is not only about avoiding discrimination. Section 149(3) says advancing equality includes taking steps to meet needs that differ between groups and encouraging participation where it is disproportionately low. Service Standard point 5 reflects this: it reminds teams designing public services that public sector organisations have a legal duty to consider everyone's needs.",figure:"",year:"2010",disputed:false,note:"Use this to justify targeted work, such as outreach to groups with low take-up. Section 149(6) says compliance may involve treating some people more favourably than others, but only where the Act otherwise permits it; take legal advice before positive action.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 149 Public sector equality duty","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","take steps to meet the needs of persons who share a relevant protected characteristic that are different from the needs of persons who do not share it"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Service Manual: Service Standard point 5, Make sure everyone can use the service","https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/service-standard/point-5-make-sure-everyone-can-use-the-service","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Public sector organisations have a legal duty to consider everyone’s needs when they’re designing and delivering services.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Assess impact before you decide, and keep a record",text:"UK government guidance says the equality assessment must happen before and at the time a decision is considered, and that written records from that time are the easiest way to show compliance. In Scotland and Wales, regulations go further and require listed authorities to assess the impact of new or revised policies.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"In England there is no prescribed format for an equality impact assessment and no requirement to publish it. Scottish regulation 5 requires relevant evidence, including evidence from people who share the characteristic, and publication of the results; Welsh regulation 8 requires published reports where an assessment shows substantial impact. A form filled in after the decision does not show due regard.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Public sector equality duty: guidance for public authorities (18 December 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-sector-equality-duty-guidance-for-public-authorities/public-sector-equality-duty-guidance-for-public-authorities","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Your assessment must be before and at the time a particular decision is being considered, and not after the decision has been made."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties) (Scotland) Regulations 2012, regulation 5","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ssi/2012/162/regulation/5","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","assess the impact of applying a proposed new or revised policy or practice"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Equality Act 2010 (Statutory Duties) (Wales) Regulations 2011, regulation 8","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2011/1064/regulation/8","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","An authority must make such arrangements as it considers appropriate for—")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Involve the people affected, not only their data",text:"Welsh regulations require listed authorities to involve people who represent the interests of those who share protected characteristics when carrying out certain duties. UK government guidance calls it good practice to ask about equality impacts in every public consultation, and the Consultation Principles say to consider targeting specific groups.",figure:"",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"Wales regulation 5 is a legal requirement for listed Welsh authorities; the consultation question and targeting are good practice, not law, in England. Black and Mixed-heritage communities are not one group: involve Black African, Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean people and others specifically, and pay or support people for their time where possible.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Equality Act 2010 (Statutory Duties) (Wales) Regulations 2011, regulation 5 Engagement provisions","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2011/1064/regulation/5","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","represent the interests of persons who share one or more of the protected characteristics"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Public sector equality duty: guidance for public authorities (18 December 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-sector-equality-duty-guidance-for-public-authorities/public-sector-equality-duty-guidance-for-public-authorities","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","It is good practice to include a question about equality impacts in any public consultations."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Consultation principles 2018","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/consultation-principles-guidance/consultation-principles-2018","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Consider targeting specific groups if appropriate.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Start from published ethnicity data before commissioning new research",text:"The Race Disparity Unit publishes Ethnicity facts and figures, government data on how people from different ethnic groups fare across areas such as health, education, housing, work and crime. Policy teams can use it to build the evidence base for an impact assessment and to see where outcomes differ between specific groups.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Check the time period, geography and sample size of each measure: many are England-only or England and Wales, and some are several years old. Ethnicity facts and figures is an evidence source; it does not by itself explain the cause of a disparity.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Race Disparity Unit (organisation page)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/race-disparity-unit","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","We collect, analyse and publish government data on the experiences of people from different ethnic backgrounds."),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures: home page","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Government data about the UK's different ethnic groups.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Plan evaluation that can show different effects for different groups",text:"The Magenta Book, HM Treasury's guidance on evaluation, lists 'Have different groups been impacted in different ways, how and why?' as a typical impact evaluation question. It notes that experimental approaches are typically not strong at identifying different impacts on particular sub-groups. Separately, it says quota sampling can be used to over-sample minority groups that are not identified in the sampling frame.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Plan this at design stage: small sub-groups need enough sample size to detect differences. The Magenta Book was last updated in May 2026; it has no separate supplementary guide on equalities.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (HM Treasury): The Magenta Book, central government guidance on evaluation (updated 15 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/magenta-book-central-government-guidance-on-evaluation-html","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Have different groups been impacted in different ways, how and why?"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (HM Treasury): The Magenta Book, central government guidance on evaluation (updated 15 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/magenta-book-central-government-guidance-on-evaluation-html","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","or over-sampling of minority groups who are not necessarily identified in the sampling frame")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"service",h:"Service design: whole problems, inclusive research and real needs",intro:"The Service Standard asks teams to solve whole problems and make sure everyone can use a service. The evidence shows needs differ between and within Black and Mixed-heritage groups, so assumptions need testing with real users.",points:[
  {h:"The Service Standard: solve whole problems and exclude no group",text:"Service Standard point 5 says government services must work for everyone who needs them and teams should avoid excluding any group within their intended audience. Point 2 says users should not need to understand how government works to use a service, which matters for people facing several services at once.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Page accessed October 2026. These are assessment criteria for GOV.UK services in central government; other public bodies can use them as good practice. Include offline and assisted routes, not only the digital journey.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Service Manual: Service Standard point 5, Make sure everyone can use the service","https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/service-standard/point-5-make-sure-everyone-can-use-the-service","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","avoid excluding any groups within the audience they’re intended to serve"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Service Manual: Service Standard point 2, Solve a whole problem for users","https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/service-standard/point-2-solve-a-whole-problem","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","users should not have to understand how government works in order to use public services.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Recruit research participants who reflect your actual users",text:"The Service Manual tells teams to recruit research participants who represent the actual and likely users of a service, including a spread by ethnicity. Government Communication Service guidance warns that treating participants as homogeneous groups is limiting.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Recruit within groups as well as across them, for example Black African, Black Caribbean and Mixed White and Black Caribbean participants. Community groups and charities can help, but check that recruits still match your users.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Service Manual: Find user research participants (updated 22 September 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/find-user-research-participants","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","make sure that the members you recruit for research are representative of the actual and likely users of your service."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Service Manual: Service Standard point 5, Make sure everyone can use the service","https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/service-standard/point-5-make-sure-everyone-can-use-the-service","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","carry out research with participants who represent the potential audience for the service"),SRCQ("Government Communication Service (communications.gov.uk): 4 tips to make your campaign imagery more diverse (undated)","https://www.communications.gov.uk/blog/4-tips-to-make-your-campaign-imagery-more-diverse","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Classifying people into homogeneous groups when defining your research participants is limiting.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Recent internet use was not lower in Black or Mixed groups (UK, 2019)",text:"In January to March 2019, 90.8% of UK adults had used the internet in the previous 3 months. The figure was 92.8% for the Black group and 96.0% for the Mixed group. Do not assume Black or Mixed-heritage users are digitally excluded; test for skills, device, cost and confidence barriers directly.",figure:"Black 92.8%; Mixed 96.0%; all adults 90.8%",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"Base rate and limits: these are 2019 Labour Force Survey estimates for the UK and are now dated. Younger age profiles in the Black and Mixed groups may explain some of the difference. Recent use does not measure skills, data poverty or quality of access, and regional and age breakdowns are based on small samples.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures: Internet use (last updated 17 July 2020)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/digital/internet-use/latest","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","in 2019, 90.8% of people aged 16 and over had used the internet in the 3 months prior to being surveyed"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Service Manual: Service Standard point 5, Make sure everyone can use the service","https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/service-standard/point-5-make-sure-everyone-can-use-the-service","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","make sure that people are not excluded from being able to use the service because they lack digital skills")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Confidence in local police was lowest for some Black and Mixed groups",text:"In England and Wales in the year ending March 2023, 68% of adults had confidence in their local police. It was 49% for Black Caribbean people, 49% for Mixed White and Black Caribbean people and 48% for Black 'other' people, but 65% for Black African people. Lower confidence can affect whether people use, or trust, a service.",figure:"Black Caribbean 49%; Mixed White and Black Caribbean 49%; overall 68%",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"This measures confidence in local police only, from the Crime Survey for England and Wales. The ONS Trust in Government survey 2023 bulletin gives no ethnicity breakdown, so no official cross-service trust figure by ethnicity is cited here. Do not apply police figures to other services without your own research.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures: Confidence in the local police (last updated 17 October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/crime-justice-and-the-law/policing/confidence-in-the-local-police/latest/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","people with mixed ethnicity had the lowest rate of confidence in the local police out of all ethnic groups (57%)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Language needs vary widely within Black and Mixed groups",text:"In 2021, 1.5% of usual residents aged 3 and over in England and Wales could not speak English well and 0.3% could not speak it at all. In 2011, English (or Welsh in Wales) was the main language for 98.5% of Black Caribbean people but 71.9% of Black African people. GOV.UK publishing guidance says to look for evidence of user need, such as search terms, contact centre calls and requests for translations, when deciding whether to translate.",figure:"Black African 71.9%; Black Caribbean 98.5% (English main language, 2011)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The ethnic group breakdown is from the 2011 Census (England and Wales) and is dated; the overall 2021 figures are newer. Main language other than English does not mean poor English. Check contact centre data, search terms and requests for translations before translating.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Language, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/language/bulletins/languageenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","1.5% (880,000) could not speak English well"),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures: English language skills (last updated 26 March 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/demographics/english-language-skills/latest","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","people in the Bangladeshi ethnic group were the most likely to not speak English well, or at all"),SRCQ("GOV.UK publishing guidance: Consider translations (undated)","https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/plan-manage-content/consider-translations","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Look for evidence of a user need, such as:")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"content",h:"Content design: words, plain English, images and accessibility",intro:"Government guidance on writing about ethnicity is clear: be specific, avoid umbrella acronyms and use terms people recognise. Plain English, inclusive images and accessible formats help everyone, including Black and Mixed-heritage users.",points:[
  {h:"Do not use the acronyms that group everyone together",text:"GOV.UK style and the Race Disparity Unit both say not to use the umbrella acronyms that group ethnic minorities together (quoted below). They emphasise Asian and Black groups and leave out others, including Mixed groups. Where grouping is truly needed, use 'ethnic minorities' or 'people from ethnic minority backgrounds'.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"This is government style, not a legal rule. The NHS service manual gives the same advice.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (Race Disparity Unit): Writing about ethnicity (last updated October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","We do not use the terms BAME (Black, Asian and minority ethnic) and BME (Black and minority ethnic) because they emphasise certain ethnic minority groups (Asian and Black) and exclude others"),SRCQ("GOV.UK publishing guidance: A to Z style guide, entry 'ethnic minorities'","https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/style-guides/a-to-z-style-guide/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Do not use the terms BAME (Black, Asian and minority ethnic) and BME (Black and minority ethnic)."),SRCQ("NHS digital service manual: Inclusive content: ethnicity, religion and nationality (October 2023)","https://service-manual.nhs.uk/content/inclusive-content/ethnicity-religion-and-nationality","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Use \"ethnic minorities\", not \"BAME\" or \"BME\".")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Name specific groups, because broad groups hide differences",text:"Guidance says to refer to ethnic minority groups individually rather than as a single group, and to be as specific as you can. Broad terms can mask disparities between groups and lead to misleading readings of data.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"For example, Black Caribbean and Black African people often have different outcomes (see confidence in local police above). Name the groups your evidence covers, and say when you have had to combine groups.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK publishing guidance: A to Z style guide, entry 'ethnic minorities'","https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/style-guides/a-to-z-style-guide/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Refer to ethnic minority groups individually, rather than as a single group."),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (Race Disparity Unit): Writing about ethnicity (last updated October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","The terms can also mask disparities between different ethnic groups and create misleading interpretations of data."),SRCQ("NHS digital service manual: Inclusive content: ethnicity, religion and nationality (October 2023)","https://service-manual.nhs.uk/content/inclusive-content/ethnicity-religion-and-nationality","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Be as specific as you can.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Use phrases people accepted in research, with capital letters",text:"In Race Disparity Unit research, 'people from a Black Caribbean background', 'the Black ethnic group' and 'Black people' were acceptable, but 'Blacks' was not. The RDU writes 'people with a Mixed ethnic background' and avoids 'non-White'. The NHS service manual says to use a capital letter when writing about ethnicity.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"The GOV.UK A to Z style guide has no entry on capitalising Black; the capital-letter rule here comes from the NHS service manual and RDU practice. Describe people as they describe themselves where you can.",src:[SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (Race Disparity Unit): Writing about ethnicity (last updated October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","In research, ‘people from a Black Caribbean background’, ‘the Black ethnic group’ and ‘Black people’ were all acceptable phrases."),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (Race Disparity Unit): Writing about ethnicity (last updated October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","We do not use ‘non-White’ because defining groups in relation to the White majority was not well received in user research."),SRCQ("NHS digital service manual: Inclusive content: ethnicity, religion and nationality (October 2023)","https://service-manual.nhs.uk/content/inclusive-content/ethnicity-religion-and-nationality","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Use a capital letter when you're writing about ethnicity or when you're asking users for their ethnic group.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Plain English is mandatory on GOV.UK",text:"GOV.UK says plain English is mandatory because content must be understandable to everyone who needs it. It cites 1 in 6 adults in England with very poor literacy skills, and notes some users do not have English as their first language.",figure:"1 in 6 adults in England",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The literacy figure is quoted by GOV.UK without a date on the page; check the original survey before reusing it as a statistic. Plain English supports, but does not replace, translation where there is evidence of need.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK publishing guidance: Clear language","https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Plain English is mandatory for all of GOV.UK."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Consultation principles: guidance (19 March 2018)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/consultation-principles-guidance","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","we will try harder to use clear language and plain English in consultation documents")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Show a range of people in images, and avoid stereotypes",text:"The GOV.UK Design System warns that only showing people of a particular age, gender or ethnicity can exclude people and reinforce negative stereotypes. Government Communication Service guidance adds that diversity for its own sake should not be the goal and suggests testing images with the audience.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The Design System advises avoiding people in illustrations. Avoid tokenism: show Black and Mixed-heritage people in ordinary, relevant roles rather than only in content about race. The GCS page is undated.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Design System: Images","https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/images","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","only depicting people of a particular age, gender or ethnicity can exclude people and reinforce negative stereotypes"),SRCQ("Government Communication Service (communications.gov.uk): 4 tips to make your campaign imagery more diverse (undated)","https://www.communications.gov.uk/blog/4-tips-to-make-your-campaign-imagery-more-diverse","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Who better to give insight into how reflective of an audience an image is than the audience themselves?")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Accessibility is a legal requirement for public sector websites",text:"The 2018 accessibility regulations require most public sector websites and apps (there are exemptions and a disproportionate burden test) to meet accessibility standards and publish an accessibility statement. GOV.UK guidance says at least 1 in 5 people in the UK have a long term illness, impairment or disability.",figure:"At least 1 in 5 people in the UK",year:"2018",disputed:false,note:"GOV.UK guidance now points to WCAG 2.2 AA. Accessibility is not specific to ethnicity, but disabled Black and Mixed-heritage people can face both barriers, so test with them.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Understanding accessibility requirements for public sector bodies (updated 30 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/accessibility-requirements-for-public-sector-websites-and-apps","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","At least 1 in 5 people in the UK have a long term illness, impairment or disability."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2018/952/contents","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Obligation to make websites and mobile applications accessible")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"data",h:"Data design: collecting, modelling and using ethnicity data fairly",intro:"Good ethnicity data starts with self-identification and harmonised categories, and continues through to fair algorithms, transparency and safe publication. The hub's page on collecting ethnicity data covers the standard in more detail.",points:[
  {h:"Use the harmonised ethnicity standard so data can be compared",text:"The Government Statistical Service harmonised standard lists Black, African, Caribbean and Black British options and Mixed options such as White and Black Caribbean and White and Black African. In the 2021 Census, 4.2% of people in England (2.4 million) identified as Black and 3.0% (1.7 million) as Mixed or Multiple.",figure:"Black 4.2% (2.4 million); Mixed 3.0% (1.7 million), England, 2021",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The question wording differs slightly in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland. Using the standard lets you compare your data with Census and survey data. See the hub's separate page on collecting ethnicity data for detail.",src:[SRCQ("Government Analysis Function: Ethnicity harmonised standard","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/ethnicity-harmonised-standard/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Ethnic group, religion and national identity are self-identification measures reflecting how people define themselves."),SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","\"Black, Black British, Caribbean or African\" was 4.2% (2.4 million) \"Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups\" was 3.0% (1.7 million)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Design forms for self-identification, choice and write-in answers",text:"The Race Disparity Unit says the best way to collect ethnicity data is to ask people, with a 'prefer not to say' option and a clear explanation of why. Write-in options add detail: in the 2021 Census they showed 271,000 people identifying as Nigerian, 151,000 as Somali and 113,000 as Ghanaian.",figure:"Nigerian 271,000; Somali 151,000; Ghanaian 113,000 (England and Wales, 2021)",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Self-reported ethnicity is better than data recorded by a third party or proxy, which the RDU says is lower quality and may not reflect how people would describe themselves. Where possible, keep the detailed category and any write-in answer as well as the broad group (the hub's advice). Some people were offended when White was listed first, and others disliked inconsistent ordering, so use a consistent, tested order.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit): Standards for ethnicity data (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Your data collection should also have a 'prefer not to say' option for people who do not want to give their ethnicity."),SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","has allowed insights into specific African backgrounds"),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (Race Disparity Unit): Writing about ethnicity (last updated October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","In user research, some people were offended when White was placed first in a list of ethnic groups, while others did not like inconsistent ordering.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Leaving ethnicity out of a model does not stop bias",text:"The Centre for Data Ethics and Innovation review in 2020 found that 'fairness through unawareness' is often not enough, because other data can act as proxies. It said data is needed to monitor outcomes and identify bias, but protected characteristic data is often not available.",figure:"",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The review covered policing, local government, financial services and recruitment. Collect ethnicity data lawfully and for a stated purpose so you can test outcomes by group; seek legal advice on any bias mitigation technique. Its recommendation on transparency applies to algorithms that have an impact on significant decisions affecting individuals.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Centre for Data Ethics and Innovation): Review into bias in algorithmic decision-making, main report (27 November 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cdei-publishes-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making/main-report-cdei-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Fairness through unawareness is often not enough to prevent bias"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Centre for Data Ethics and Innovation): Review into bias in algorithmic decision-making, main report (27 November 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cdei-publishes-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making/main-report-cdei-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Data is needed to monitor outcomes and identify bias, but data on protected characteristics is not available often enough."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Centre for Data Ethics and Innovation): CDEI publishes review into bias in algorithmic decision-making (27 November 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cdei-publishes-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Organisations should be actively using data to identify and mitigate bias.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Facial recognition accuracy for Black people depends on settings",text:"note (last sentence replacement): The Home Office says a new algorithm has been procured and independently tested that can be used at settings with no significant demographic variation; it was due for operational testing in 2026.",figure:"False positive identification rate (FPIR) 5.5% Black, 9.9% Black female, 0.04% White (threshold 0.8, test database ~180,000 images, NPL 2025)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Two different algorithms and uses: the 2023 figures are for live facial recognition, the 2025 figures for retrospective searches. The 0.8 threshold was chosen by NPL for testing, not stated as the setting police use. The Home Office says a new algorithm has been procured and independently tested and can be used at settings with no significant variation between groups; it was still due for operational testing.",src:[SRCQ("National Physical Laboratory: Facial Recognition Technology in Law Enforcement Equitability Study, NPL Report MS 43 (March 2023)","https://science.police.uk/site/assets/files/3396/frt-equitability-study_mar2023.pdf","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","start to show a statistically significant imbalance between demographics with more Black subjects having a false positive than Asian or White subjects."),SRCQ("National Physical Laboratory for the Home Office: Accuracy and equitability evaluation of Cognitec FaceVACS-DBScan ID v5.5, Report 4153 (October 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/693002a4cdec734f4dff4149/1a_Cognitec_NPL_Equitability_Report_October_25.pdf","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","The FPIR for Black Male subjects (0.4 %) is lower than that for Black Female subjects (9.9 %)."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Home Office): Summary: accuracy and equitability evaluation of Cognitec FaceVACS-DBScan ID v5.5 (4 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/facial-recognition-technology-tests-national-physical-laboratory/summary-accuracy-and-equitability-evaluation-of-cognitec-facevacs-dbscan-id-v55","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","the algorithm is more likely to incorrectly include some demographic groups in its search results")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Publish how algorithmic tools work, including bias testing",text:"The Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard is mandatory for all government departments and some arm's-length bodies. Its guidance asks organisations to describe any bias and fairness evaluation they have done. The CDEI review recommended a mandatory transparency obligation for the public sector in 2020.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The standard is recommended, not mandatory, for the wider public sector. The ATRS guidance does not mention the equality duty by name; link your record to your equality impact assessment yourself.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard Hub (updated 8 May 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-hub","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","The ATRS is mandatory for all government departments"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard: guidance for public sector bodies (updated 8 May 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guidance-for-organisations-using-the-algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-guidance-for-public-sector-bodies","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","You should also describe any bias and fairness evaluation you have undertaken"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Centre for Data Ethics and Innovation): CDEI publishes review into bias in algorithmic decision-making (27 November 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cdei-publishes-review-into-bias-in-algorithmic-decision-making","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","Government should place a mandatory transparency obligation on all public sector organisations")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Protect small groups when you publish data",text:"Black and Mixed groups can be small in local or service data, which raises the risk of identifying people. Government Analysis Function guidance says disclosure risk is higher where table cells have low counts, and the RDU standards say not to disclose data based on small numbers of people.",figure:"Wales: Black 0.9% (28,000), Mixed 1.6% (49,000), 2021",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Small numbers also make estimates unreliable: report sample sizes and confidence intervals, and say when you have combined groups. Combining groups protects privacy but can hide differences, so explain the trade-off.",src:[SRCQ("Government Analysis Function: An introduction to Statistical Disclosure Control (SDC) (19 August 2026)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/an-introduction-to-sdc/","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","The risk is higher if there are cells with low counts, including zeros."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit): Standards for ethnicity data (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","not disclosing data based on a small number of people, or by rounding numbers."),SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Designing for Black and Mixed-heritage people page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" was 0.9% (28,000) \"Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups\" was 1.6% (49,000)")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Public bodies must think about your needs when they design policies and services, not only avoid harming you.",refs:["dg-policy-due-regard-means-meeting-different-needs", "dg-policy-the-public-sector-equality-duty-applies-"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Use the words people chose in research, and name specific groups rather than an acronym.",refs:["dg-content-do-not-use-the-acronyms-that-group-every", "dg-content-name-specific-groups-because-broad-group"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Ask for an equality impact assessment before a decision, and for evaluation that can show different effects for different groups.",refs:["dg-policy-assess-impact-before-you-decide-and-keep", "dg-policy-plan-evaluation-that-can-show-different-"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Recruit research participants who reflect your users, and involve the people affected, not only their data.",refs:["dg-service-recruit-research-participants-who-reflec", "dg-policy-involve-the-people-affected-not-only-the"]},{t:"Check assumptions against data: recent internet use was not lower in Black or Mixed groups in 2019.",refs:["dg-service-recent-internet-use-was-not-lower-in-bla"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Use the harmonised standard, design forms for self-identification and write-in answers, and protect small groups when you publish.",refs:["dg-data-use-the-harmonised-ethnicity-standard-so", "dg-data-design-forms-for-self-identification-cho", "dg-data-protect-small-groups-when-you-publish-da"]},{t:"Leaving ethnicity out of a model does not stop bias; test for it and publish how the tool works.",refs:["dg-data-leaving-ethnicity-out-of-a-model-does-no", "dg-data-publish-how-algorithmic-tools-work-inclu"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"The public sector equality duty applies to everything a public body does, including policy and service design.",refs:["dg-policy-the-public-sector-equality-duty-applies-"]},{t:"Assess impact before deciding, involve the people affected, and plan evaluation that can show different effects for different groups.",refs:["dg-policy-assess-impact-before-you-decide-and-keep", "dg-policy-involve-the-people-affected-not-only-the", "dg-policy-plan-evaluation-that-can-show-different-"]},{t:"Name specific groups and use the words people chose in research, not acronyms.",refs:["dg-content-do-not-use-the-acronyms-that-group-every", "dg-content-name-specific-groups-because-broad-group"]},{t:"Design forms for self-identification, and remember that leaving ethnicity out of a model does not remove bias.",refs:["dg-data-design-forms-for-self-identification-cho", "dg-data-leaving-ethnicity-out-of-a-model-does-no"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["mixed"]={name:"Mixed heritage",pfx:"mx",kpis:[{s:"numbers",h:"1.7 million people in England and Wales are in a Mixed ethnic group",n:"1,717,977",l:"people in a Mixed ethnic group in England and Wales, 2.9% (2021)",ic:"chart"},{s:"numbers",h:"Mixed groups are the youngest in England and Wales",n:"19",l:"median age in the Mixed groups, against 40 overall (2021)",ic:"chart"}],sections:[
 {k:"numbers",h:"How many people: Mixed ethnic groups across the UK",intro:"Census figures from each UK nation show a growing, young population of people who identify with a Mixed or Multiple ethnic group. Each nation uses different categories, so compare with care.",points:[
  {h:"1.7 million people in England and Wales are in a Mixed ethnic group",text:"At Census 2021, 1,717,977 people in England and Wales (2.9% of the population) identified with one of the 'Mixed or Multiple' ethnic groups, up from 2.2% in 2011 and 1.3% in 2001. The share was 3.0% in England and 1.6% in Wales, so local services in Wales should not assume the England picture.",figure:"1,717,977 (2.9%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Census figures are self-identified. ONS rounds the England (3.0%) and Wales (1.6%) shares separately; the combined 2.9% comes from GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures. 2011 and 2001 shares are taken from the same GOV.UK table.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Population of England and Wales (last updated 21 May 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","1.7 million people (2.9%) had mixed ethnicity"),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Mixed heritage page","\"Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups\" was 1.6% (49,000)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"White and Black Caribbean is the largest Mixed group",text:"In 2021, 513,040 people (0.9%) identified as Mixed White and Black Caribbean and 249,593 (0.4%) as Mixed White and Black African. The White and Black Caribbean share has risen from 0.5% in 2001 to 0.8% in 2011 and 0.9% in 2021, and White and Black African from 0.2% to 0.3% to 0.4%.",figure:"513,040 and 249,593",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"England and Wales only. Changes over time mix real population growth with people choosing different answers between censuses (see 'Words and identity').",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Population of England and Wales (last updated 21 May 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Mixed Mixed White/Asian 0.8 488,228 Mixed White/Black African 0.4 249,593 Mixed White/Black Caribbean 0.9 513,040"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Population of England and Wales (last updated 21 May 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/population-of-england-and-wales/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Mixed White/Black African 0.4 0.3 0.2 Mixed White/Black Caribbean 0.9 0.8 0.5")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mixed groups are the youngest in England and Wales",text:"People in Mixed groups had a median age of 19 in 2021, against 40 for the whole population of England and Wales; the White and Black African and White and Asian groups had the lowest median age of the 19 groups, 16. Nearly half (48.6%) of people identifying as White and Black African were aged 0 to 15, against 18.5% of the whole population.",figure:"Median age 19",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"A young age profile partly drives some outcome gaps, so compare like-for-like age groups where possible.",src:[SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Ethnic group by age and sex, England and Wales: Census 2021","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/articles/ethnicgroupbyageandsexenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Mixed heritage page","In 2021, nearly half (48.6%, 121,310) of the population who identified as \"White and Black African\" were aged 0 to 15 years."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Age groups (published 31 March 2023)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/demographics/age-groups/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","people from mixed ethnic groups had the lowest (19 years old)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Scotland: Mixed group tripled to about 61,000 people",text:"Scotland's Census 2022 counted 60,899 people (1.12%) in the 'Mixed or multiple ethnic group' category, against 19,815 (0.37%) in 2011, an increase NRS reports as 41,100 people. Scotland uses a single write-in Mixed category, with no separate White and Black Caribbean or White and Black African boxes.",figure:"60,899 (1.12%)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Counts are from the chart data published on the NRS page. Scotland's census was in 2022, a year after the rest of the UK, and its categories are not directly comparable with England and Wales.",src:[SRCQ("National Records of Scotland: Scotland's Census 2022 - Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion (21 May 2024)","https://www.scotlandscensus.gov.uk/2022-results/scotland-s-census-2022-ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","The ‘Mixed or multiple ethnic group’ category saw an increase of 41,100 people.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Northern Ireland: Mixed is the largest minority ethnic group",text:"In Northern Ireland's Census 2021, 14,400 people (0.8%) were in the 'Mixed Ethnicities' output category, the largest of the minority ethnic groups. The NI question offered a single 'Mixed ethnic group' option.",figure:"14,400 (0.8%)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"NISRA rounds figures for display. The NI classification differs from England, Wales and Scotland.",src:[SRCQ("NISRA: Census 2021 Main statistics for Northern Ireland, statistical bulletin: Ethnic group (22 September 2022)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/system/files/statistics/census-2021-main-statistics-for-northern-ireland-phase-1-statistical-bulletin-ethnic-group.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","the largest groups were Mixed Ethnicities (14,400), Black (11,000), Indian (9,900),"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: List of ethnic groups (style guide)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/ethnic-groups","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Black African Black Other Chinese Filipino Indian Irish Traveller Mixed ethnic group Roma White Any other ethnic group")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"One in ten households has members of different ethnic groups",text:"In 2021, 10.1% of households in England and Wales (2.5 million) had members who identified with two or more ethnic groups, up from 8.7% in 2011. In 2011, 88% of people identifying as White and Black Caribbean who lived as part of a couple were in an inter-ethnic relationship.",figure:"10.1% (2.5 million)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"The inter-ethnic relationships analysis uses 2011 data; ONS has not been found to publish the same analysis for 2021. Multi-ethnic households include, but are not limited to, Mixed families.",src:[SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Mixed heritage page","In England and Wales, 10.1% (2.5 million) of households consisted of members identifying with two or more different ethnic groups, an increase from 8.7% (2.0 million) in 2011."),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: What does the 2011 Census tell us about inter-ethnic relationships? (3 July 2014)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/marriagecohabitationandcivilpartnerships/articles/whatdoesthe2011censustellusaboutinterethnicrelationships/2014-07-03","Supports the Mixed heritage page","The groups most likely to contain people in an inter-ethnic relationship were White and Black Caribbean (88%)")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"words",h:"Words and identity",intro:"People describe mixed ancestry in many ways, and official categories differ from everyday language. Self-identification can change over a person's life.",points:[
  {h:"Government style: 'people with a Mixed ethnic background'",text:"The government's style guide refers to 'ethnicity' rather than 'race'. It avoids 'Mixed people' and 'Mixed race people' and usually says 'people with a Mixed ethnic background' or 'people from the Mixed ethnic group'.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"This is a style for official statistics, not a rule for how people describe themselves. Many people call themselves mixed race (see next point).",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Writing about ethnicity (last updated October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/writing-about-ethnicity","Supports the Mixed heritage page","However, we don’t say ‘Mixed people’ or ‘Mixed race people’. We usually say ‘people with a Mixed ethnic background’ or ‘people from the Mixed ethnic group’.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mixed race, mixed heritage, dual heritage: terms people use",text:"Academic research found 'mixed race' was the term most people in this group chose for themselves, although it is contested because it relies on the idea of 'race'. 'Mixed heritage' and 'dual heritage' are also used, including in official reports, and some young people dislike 'dual heritage' because it refers to only two backgrounds.",figure:"",year:"2009",disputed:false,note:"The research dates from 2009 and preferences change. Ask people which term they prefer and use it.",src:[SRCQ("University of Kent (Kent Academic Repository): Aspinall, Mixed race, mixed origins or what? Anthropology Today 25(2) (2009)","https://kar.kent.ac.uk/24454","Supports the Mixed heritage page","However, research indicates that the term of choice of most respondents in general population and student samples of this population group is 'mixed race', other terms - including 'mixed origins' - attracting little support."),SRCQ("Mixed Museum (Mix-d: Museum): What's in a name?","https://exhibition.mixedmuseum.org.uk/parenting/whats-in-a-name","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Dual Heritage: A modern term but some young people dislike the notion of only referring to two backgrounds"),SRCQ("HM Inspectorate of Probation: The experiences of black and mixed heritage boys in the youth justice system (October 2021)","https://cdn.websitebuilder.service.justice.gov.uk/uploads/sites/32/2025/03/The-experiences-of-black-and-mixed-heritage-boys-in-the-youth-justice-system-thematic-report-v1.0.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Black and mixed heritage boys were consistently over-represented in custodial cohorts.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Each UK nation asks the question differently",text:"England and Wales use 'Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups' with tick boxes for White and Black Caribbean, White and Black African, White and Asian, and a write-in for any other Mixed background. Scotland uses one 'Mixed or multiple ethnic group' option where people write in their ethnic group, and Northern Ireland a single 'Mixed ethnic group' option. Figures from the 4 nations cannot be added together group by group.",figure:"",year:"2021-2022",disputed:false,note:"Scotland figures come from its 2022 census; the others are from 2021.",src:[SRCQ("Government Analysis Function: Ethnicity harmonised standard","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/policy-store/ethnicity-harmonised-standard/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Mixed / Multiple ethnic groups White and Black Caribbean White and Black African White and Asian Any other Mixed / Multiple ethnic background, please describe"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: List of ethnic groups (style guide)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/ethnic-groups","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Mixed or multiple ethnic group Respondents write in their ethnic group")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Testing found some people uneasy with the word 'Mixed'",text:"ONS question testing for Census 2021 found most people were comfortable with the colour terms, but a small proportion were uncomfortable with terms including 'Mixed'. In 2011, people identifying as Mixed were more likely to tick several boxes, so ONS tested 2 write-in lines on the paper form.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Findings come from ONS focus groups and testing, not a representative survey.",src:[SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: National identity, ethnic group, language and religion question development for Census 2021","https://www.ons.gov.uk/census/censustransformationprogramme/questiondevelopment/nationalidentityethnicgrouplanguageandreligionquestiondevelopmentforcensus2021","Supports the Mixed heritage page","The majority of participants were comfortable with the colour terminology used for the high-level categories, but a small proportion of participants felt uncomfortable with some of the terminology, including “White”, “Black” and “Mixed”."),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: National identity, ethnic group, language and religion question development for Census 2021","https://www.ons.gov.uk/census/censustransformationprogramme/questiondevelopment/nationalidentityethnicgrouplanguageandreligionquestiondevelopmentforcensus2021","Supports the Mixed heritage page","When we reviewed the responses to the 2011 Census, we found that respondents who identified as Mixed ethnicity were more likely to respond across multiple categories and tick-boxes.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The same person may answer differently over time",text:"Linking the same people across the 2001 and 2011 censuses showed that Mixed categories were less stable than most others: 43% of people who chose White and Black African in 2001 chose another category in 2011. White and Black Caribbean was more stable (76.4% gave the same answer), and ONS notes people in Mixed groups are more likely to have more than one ethnicity in their records.",figure:"43% changed",year:"2001-2011",disputed:false,note:"Changes can reflect real changes in how people see themselves, who filled in the form, or errors and imputation. This is not evidence that anyone's answer is 'wrong'.",src:[SRCQ("University of Manchester, Centre on Dynamics of Ethnicity (CoDE): How have people's ethnic identities changed in England and Wales? (Census briefing, 2001-2011 data)","https://hummedia.manchester.ac.uk/institutes/code/briefingsupdated/how-have-people's-ethnic-identities-changed-in-england-and-wales.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","A larger proportion of those in mixed ethnic groups changed their recorded category (for example 43% of White and Black African in 2001 identified with another census category a decade later)."),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (16 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/2023-01-16","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Those from Mixed or Other ethnic categories are more likely to change over time and have more than one ethnicity listed in their records.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"outcomes",h:"Outcomes in official statistics",intro:"Some official statistics show distinct outcomes for Mixed groups, especially Mixed White and Black Caribbean. These figures describe differences; they do not on their own explain causes.",points:[
  {h:"White and Black Caribbean pupils: suspension rate almost twice average",text:"In England in the 2022 to 2023 school year, pupils in the Mixed White and Black Caribbean group had 1,736 suspensions for every 10,000 pupils, almost twice the national rate of 9.33 per 100. Only the Gypsy or Roma and Irish Traveller groups had higher rates.",figure:"17.36 per 100 pupils",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Rates count suspensions, not pupils, so one pupil can be counted more than once. These are 2022 to 2023 figures from Ethnicity facts and figures; DfE has since published newer years on Explore education statistics, which are not covered here here. The data cannot show causes.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Pupil suspensions (published 18 December 2024, 2022 to 2023 school year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/pupil-exclusions/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","pupils from the Mixed White and Black Caribbean ethnic group were almost 2 times more likely to be suspended than the national average, with 1,736 suspensions for every 10,000 pupils (a rate of 17.36)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Pupil suspensions (published 18 December 2024, 2022 to 2023 school year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/pupil-exclusions/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","All 9.33 787,000")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Higher permanent exclusion rates, even after other factors",text:"In 2022 to 2023 the permanent exclusion rate for Mixed White and Black Caribbean pupils in England was 0.24, against 0.11 for all pupils. The Timpson Review (2019) found these pupils were about 1.6 times as likely as White British pupils to be permanently excluded after controlling for factors such as poverty and special educational needs.",figure:"0.24 vs 0.11",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Small numbers (323 exclusions) make rates volatile. The Timpson analysis uses older data; it controls for recorded factors but cannot rule out unrecorded ones.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Permanent exclusions (published 11 December 2024, 2022 to 2023 school year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/permanent-exclusions/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Mixed White and Black Caribbean 0.24 323"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Permanent exclusions (published 11 December 2024, 2022 to 2023 school year)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/education-skills-and-training/absence-and-exclusions/permanent-exclusions/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","All 0.11 9,376"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): Edward Timpson publishes landmark exclusions review (7 May 2019)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/edward-timpson-publishes-landmark-exclusions-review","Supports the Mixed heritage page","After accounting for other factors, Black Caribbean were around 1.7 times more likely, and Mixed White and Black Caribbean children were around 1.6 times more likely, to be permanently excluded compared to White British children.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Children from Mixed groups are over-represented in care",text:"On 31 March 2025, 11% of children looked after by local authorities in England were from Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups. DfE says children from Mixed groups were over-represented compared with the overall child population.",figure:"11%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"DfE publishes this at the 5-group level only on the release page; it does not split out White and Black Caribbean or White and Black African there. Only one authoritative source verified.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Education: Children looked after in England including adoptions, reporting year 2025 (26 November 2025)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Children of White ethnicity account for 71% of CLA, 11% were Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups, 8% Black, African, Caribbean or Black British,"),SRCQ("Department for Education: Children looked after in England including adoptions, reporting year 2025 (26 November 2025)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/children-looked-after-in-england-including-adoptions","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Children from Mixed ethnic groups were over-represented and children from Asian ethnic groups were under- represented")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mixed children are over-represented in youth custody",text:"In the year ending March 2025, Mixed children were 18% of children in youth custody in England and Wales and 18% of those remanded in custody. An HM Inspectorate of Probation inspection (2021) found black and mixed heritage boys were consistently over-represented in custody and often received poorer services.",figure:"18% of children in custody",year:"2024/25",disputed:false,note:"Youth justice data uses 5 broad groups, so Mixed subgroups cannot be separated. Over-representation alone does not show discrimination or cause.",src:[SRCQ("Youth Justice Board and Ministry of Justice: Youth Justice Statistics 2024 to 2025 (29 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Despite decreases in the number of Black and Mixed children in custody compared to the previous year, Black and Mixed children remained overrepresented (accounting for 22% and 18% respectively)."),SRCQ("HM Inspectorate of Probation: The experiences of black and mixed heritage boys in the youth justice system (October 2021)","https://cdn.websitebuilder.service.justice.gov.uk/uploads/sites/32/2025/03/The-experiences-of-black-and-mixed-heritage-boys-in-the-youth-justice-system-thematic-report-v1.0.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Black and mixed heritage boys were consistently over-represented in custodial cohorts.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Employment: 69% of people from Mixed groups were in work",text:"In 2022, 69% of people aged 16 to 64 from Mixed ethnic groups in England, Scotland and Wales were employed, against 76% overall. The Mixed group is the only one where women had a higher employment rate (71%) than men (67%).",figure:"69%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Survey estimate (Annual Population Survey). Mixed groups are aggregated, so the figure cannot show White and Black Caribbean or White and Black African separately. The younger age profile may affect the rate.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Employment (last updated 8 December 2023, 2022 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/work-pay-and-benefits/employment/employment/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","the employment rate was higher for women (71%) than men (67%) in the mixed ethnic group – in all other ethnic groups, the rate was higher for men than women"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Employment (last updated 8 December 2023, 2022 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/work-pay-and-benefits/employment/employment/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Data is aggregated for the black, mixed and ‘other’ ethnic groups, which means estimates are shown for these groups as a whole")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Higher detention rates under the Mental Health Act",text:"In England in the year to March 2023, people from Mixed groups were detained under the Mental Health Act at a rate of 158 per 100,000, against 64 for White people. The rate for Mixed White and Black Caribbean was 102 and for Mixed White and Black African 139.",figure:"158 per 100,000",year:"2022/23",disputed:false,note:"Standardised rates from the Mental Health Services Data Set, as published on Ethnicity facts and figures, which gives 158 in its summary and 157 in one table. The Mixed figure is driven mainly by the Mixed Other group (288 per 100,000). Rates fell sharply in 2022/23 partly because 2021 census population estimates replaced older ones. Ethnicity recording in health data is less reliable for Mixed groups (see 'Data quality'). One source; NHS England's newer years are not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Detentions under the Mental Health Act (published 16 August 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/health/mental-health/detentions-under-the-mental-health-act/latest","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Mixed 214 222 228 158 Mixed White and Asian 94 101 104 69 Mixed White and Black African 198 269 210 139 Mixed White and Black Caribbean 195 172 194 102")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"history",h:"History of people of mixed African and European ancestry in Britain",intro:"Archives, museums and universities document people of mixed African and European ancestry in Britain over several centuries. This section names only people who have died.",points:[
  {h:"Dido Elizabeth Belle (1761 to 1804), raised at Kenwood",text:"Dido Elizabeth Belle was the daughter of Royal Navy officer Sir John Lindsay and an African woman, Maria Bell(e). She was raised by Lord Mansfield's family at Kenwood in north London, and her baptism in 1766 is recorded at St George's, Bloomsbury.",figure:"",year:"1761-1804",disputed:false,note:"Much of her early life is uncertain; English Heritage notes conflicting accounts of how her mother came to England.",src:[SRCQ("English Heritage: Dido Belle (Kenwood history)","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/kenwood/history/dido-belle/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Dido Elizabeth Belle was the illegitimate daughter of Sir John Lindsay (1737–88), an officer in the Royal Navy, and an African woman named Maria Bell(e)."),SRCQ("Scone Palace: Dido Elizabeth Belle","https://scone-palace.co.uk/dido-belle","Supports the Mixed heritage page","It has since come to light that John Lindsay remained in close contact with Dido’s mother Maria and eventually settled her in Pensacola, Florida, with her own house and small plot of land.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Samuel Coleridge-Taylor (1875 to 1912), composer",text:"Samuel Coleridge-Taylor was born in Holborn, London, in 1875. The National Archives records that his father was a medical student from Sierra Leone and his mother was English. He became famous for Hiawatha's Wedding Feast and died in 1912.",figure:"",year:"1875-1912",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("The National Archives: The story of Samuel Coleridge-Taylor: A trailblazing composer","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/samuel-coleridge-taylor/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","His father, Daniel Peter Hughes Taylor, was a medical student from Sierra Leone, and his mother, Alice Hare Martin, was English."),SRCQ("English Heritage: Samuel Coleridge-Taylor blue plaque","https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/samuel-coleridge-taylor/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Coleridge-Taylor was born in Holborn, the son of a Sierra Leonean student, and brought up in Croydon by his white English mother.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The 1930 Fletcher Report stigmatised mixed families in Liverpool",text:"In 1930 the “Liverpool Association for the Welfare of Half-Caste Children” published Muriel Fletcher's report on mixed families in Liverpool and other ports. The Mixed Museum describes it as biased and methodologically flawed but highly influential on how mixed race people and families were seen for many years.",figure:"",year:"1930",disputed:false,note:"The report uses offensive historic language; the University of Liverpool archive keeps the original terms only to describe the record accurately.",src:[SRCQ("Mixed Museum (Mix-d: Museum): Timeline, 1930 The Fletcher report","https://exhibition.mixedmuseum.org.uk/museum/timeline/the-fletcher-report","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Yet, despite local outrage about the methodologically flawed and biased study, the report was highly influential and would shape perceptions of mixed race people and families for many years to come."),SRCQ("University of Liverpool Special Collections and Archives: D7/5/5/5, Fletcher report record ([1930])","https://sca-archives.liverpool.ac.uk/Record/111438","Supports the Mixed heritage page","This record contains historic language that is offensive and/or outdated.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"About 2,000 'brown babies' born during the Second World War",text:"Around 2,000 babies were born in Britain to Black American servicemen and white British women during the Second World War. Nearly half were given up to local authorities or children's homes, and the British government blocked their adoption by Americans, including their fathers, despite interest from hundreds of African Americans.",figure:"About 2,000",year:"1942-1945",disputed:false,note:"The total is an estimate. Most evidence comes from oral histories and government records gathered by Professor Lucy Bland (Anglia Ruskin University).",src:[SRCQ("The National Archives blog: 'Brown Babies': The children born to black GI and white British women during the Second World War (Lucy Bland, Anglia Ruskin University)","https://blog.nationalarchives.gov.uk/brown-babies-second-world-war/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Nearly half of these babies were given up to local authorities or children’s homes"),SRCQ("National Museums Liverpool: 'Brown Babies' of the Second World War","https://www.liverpoolmuseums.org.uk/stories/brown-babies-of-second-world-war","Supports the Mixed heritage page","During the Second World War it is estimated that approximately 2,000 babies were born to Black American servicemen and white British women."),SRCQ("Anglia Ruskin University: Heartbreaking history of 'babies they left behind' (10 May 2019)","https://aru.ac.uk/news/heartbreaking-history-of-babies-they-left-behind","Supports the Mixed heritage page","The British government blocked the potential adoption of these children by Americans, including by their fathers, despite great interest from hundreds of African Americans.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mixed categories first appeared in the England and Wales census in 2001",text:"The census for England and Wales first asked about ethnicity in 1991, and 4 Mixed categories were added in 2001. ONS notes that Mixed groups largely reflect relationships formed since post-war migration. 660,000 people (1%) identified as Mixed in 2001, rising to 1.2 million (2%) in 2011.",figure:"660,000 (2001)",year:"2001",disputed:false,note:"Before 2001, people of mixed ancestry had to choose a single group or 'Other', so earlier counts are not comparable.",src:[SRCQ("University of Manchester, Centre on Dynamics of Ethnicity (CoDE): How have people's ethnic identities changed in England and Wales? (Census briefing, 2001-2011 data)","https://hummedia.manchester.ac.uk/institutes/code/briefingsupdated/how-have-people's-ethnic-identities-changed-in-england-and-wales.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Four Mixed categories were added in 2001, and the White category divided."),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: What does the 2011 Census tell us about inter-ethnic relationships? (3 July 2014)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/marriagecohabitationandcivilpartnerships/articles/whatdoesthe2011censustellusaboutinterethnicrelationships/2014-07-03","Supports the Mixed heritage page","In 2011, 1.2 million people (2% of the population) identified themselves with a mixed or multiple ethnicity, increasing from 660,000 (1%) in 2001.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"data",h:"Data quality and good practice",intro:"Ethnicity data for Mixed groups is less consistent than for most other groups. These points explain why and what to do about it.",points:[
  {h:"Health records agree least with census for Mixed groups",text:"When ONS linked 2011 Census answers to NHS records, agreement on ethnicity was below 67% for all Mixed groups, and below 21% for 'Other Mixed'. By comparison, White British and Bangladeshi had agreement of 92% or more.",figure:"Less than 67%",year:"2011-2021",disputed:false,note:"Linked data covers people found in both sources, which may introduce bias. Disagreement may reflect how records were collected, as well as changes in how people identify.",src:[SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (16 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/2023-01-16","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Across all three health administrative data sources, agreement was lower for all Mixed ethnic groups (less than 67%) and other ethnic groups,"),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Understanding consistency of ethnicity data recorded in health-related administrative datasets in England: 2011 to 2021 (16 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/healthinequalities/articles/understandingconsistencyofethnicitydatarecordedinhealthrelatedadministrativedatasetsinengland2011to2021/2023-01-16","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Agreement was generally lower for all Mixed or multiple ethnic groups and Other ethnic group categories.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Admin data can undercount Mixed young adults",text:"ONS's first admin-based ethnicity estimates for England under-represented the Asian, Black and Mixed groups among people aged 20 to 34, and the 'Other' group was much larger than expected compared with the 2011 Census. ONS has since revised its methods; it shows that admin records can give a different picture from the census for Mixed groups.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Research and experimental statistics; ONS has continued to change its methods.",src:[SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Producing admin-based ethnicity statistics for England: changes to data and methods (23 May 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/articles/producingadminbasedethnicitystatisticsforenglandchangestodataandmethods/2022-05-23","Supports the Mixed heritage page","there was under-representation of the Asian, Black and Mixed ethnic groups in those aged 20 to 24 years, those aged 25 to 29 years, and those aged 30 to 34 years")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Aggregated 'Mixed' figures hide differences",text:"Many surveys and administrative datasets publish only one 'Mixed' total, which can hide large differences between White and Black Caribbean, White and Black African, White and Asian and other Mixed groups. Government standards say to note this limit whenever groups are combined.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Some data on Ethnicity facts and figures still uses the older 2001 Census categories.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit): Standards for ethnicity data (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Mixed heritage page","For example, one limitation is that data for an aggregated ethnic group can hide differences between the constituent groups."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: List of ethnic groups (style guide)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/style-guide/ethnic-groups","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Some data on Ethnicity facts and figures still uses the ethnic groups from the 2001 Census when recording people's ethnicity")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"What designers and analysts should do",text:"Ask people to describe their own ethnicity rather than recording it for them, use the harmonised categories with a write-in option, and do not combine groups in non-standard ways. ONS advises using at least the 5 main ethnic groups, and analysts should flag small numbers and wide confidence intervals.",figure:"",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Self-reporting improves accuracy but cannot remove changes in how people identify over time.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit): Standards for ethnicity data (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Mixed heritage page","The best and most practical way of collecting data about people’s ethnicity is to ask them – for them to ‘self-report’ their ethnicity."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit): Standards for ethnicity data (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/standards-for-ethnicity-data/standards-for-ethnicity-data","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Avoid aggregating data for ethnic groups together in a non-harmonised way."),SRCQ("Office for National Statistics: Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion quality information for Census 2021","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/methodologies/ethnicgroupnationalidentitylanguageandreligionqualityinformationforcensus2021","Supports the Mixed heritage page","You should use the five main ethnic groups at a minimum for your research, which are:")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"world",h:"How other countries count and describe mixed African-descended identities",intro:"Countries in the Americas and Africa ask about colour, race, ethnicity or population group in very different ways, so their figures cannot be compared directly with UK census categories. Each term below is the country's own, translated where needed, and every figure comes from people describing themselves.",points:[
  {h:"Latin America: about 134 million Afro-descendants (ECLAC estimate)",text:"The UN Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean (ECLAC/CEPAL) estimates that Afro-descendants in Latin America number 134 million people, 21% of the region's population. This is a modelled regional estimate built from national censuses and surveys that ask the question differently, and ECLAC's 2020 presentation said more countries were expected to add an Afro-descendant question in the 2020 census round.",figure:"134 million (21% of the population)",year:"2020 (estimate)",disputed:false,note:"Modelled estimate by ECLAC, not a census count. The UNFPA page reports ECLAC's own launch of the figure, so the two sources are not independent. ECLAC's slide on census rounds lists 11 countries that asked an Afro-descendant question in the 2010 round; this count is taken from the list on the slide, not from a stated total.",src:[SRCQ("CEPAL: Síntesis del documento Afrodescendientes y la matriz de la desigualdad social en América Latina: retos para la inclusión (11 May 2021)","https://www.cepal.org/es/notas/sintesis-documento-afrodescendientes-la-matriz-la-desigualdad-social-america-latina-retos-la","Supports the Mixed heritage page","que asciende actualmente a 134 millones de personas, que representan un 21% de la población total"),SRCQ("UNFPA: Poner fin al racismo es tarea de todos y constituye un aspecto central en la construcción... (29 Oct 2020)","https://www.unfpa.org/es/updates/poner-fin-al-racismo-es-tarea-de-todos-y-constituye-un-aspecto-central-en-la-construcci%C3%B3n","Supports the Mixed heritage page","la población afrodescendiente asciende a 134 millones de personas, lo que representa el 21% de la población de la región."),SRCQ("CEPAL: Afrodescendientes y la matriz de la desigualdad social en América Latina, presentation (28 Oct 2020)","https://cepal.org/sites/default/files/presentation/files/201028_final_fiinal-28_de_octubre_alicia_barcena_afrodescendientes.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Se esperan 14 países más")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Brazil: 'pardo' (brown, mixed) became the largest group in 2022",text:"Brazil's statistics office IBGE asks people to declare their own colour or race from five options: branca (white), preta (Black), parda (brown), amarela (yellow) or indígena (Indigenous). IBGE says people may understand these categories in different ways, drawing on family origin, skin colour, physical features, ethnicity or community belonging, and that it treats race as a social category, not a biological one. In the 2022 Census 92.1 million people (45.3%) declared themselves pardo and 20.7 million (10.2%) preto, while 88.3 million (43.5%) declared themselves branco. It is the first census in IBGE's comparable series, which runs from 1991, in which more people declared themselves pardo than branco: in 1991, 2000 and 2010 the white share was larger.",figure:"Pardo 45.3% (92.1 million); preto 10.2% (20.6 million)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Figures are from IBGE's 2022 Census results on ethnic-racial identification (December 2023), with a newspaper report of the same release as a second source. 'Brown' is a direct translation of parda; it is often described in English as mixed, but that is not IBGE's definition. Some headlines say 'first time since 1872'; that compares with a nineteenth-century census whose categories are not comparable, so it is not used here.",src:[SRCQ("IBGE: Censo Demográfico 2022, identificação étnico-racial da população, por sexo e idade: resultados do universo (22 December 2023)","https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/media/com_mediaibge/arquivos/13ee0337cffc1de37bf0cd4da3988e1f.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","O pertencimento étnico-racial é investigado respeitando o critério de autoidentificação."),SRCQ("IBGE Educa: Cor ou raça (undated)","https://educa.ibge.gov.br/jovens/conheca-o-brasil/populacao/18319-cor-ou-raca.html","Supports the Mixed heritage page","as pessoas são perguntadas sobre sua cor de acordo com as seguintes opções: branca, preta, parda, indígena ou amarela."),SRCQ("Monitor Mercantil: Censo: maior parte da população do Brasil se declara parda (28 Dec 2023)","https://monitormercantil.com.br/censo-maior-parte-da-populacao-do-brasil-se-declara-parda/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Em 2022, cerca de 92,1 milhões de pessoas se declararam pardas, o equivalente a 45,3% da população do país.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Colombia: one census option joins 'negro', 'mulato' and Afro-Colombian",text:"Colombia's statistics office DANE asks people which group they recognise themselves as 'according to their culture, people or physical features' (de acuerdo con su cultura, pueblo o rasgos físicos). One answer option combines negro(a) (Black), mulato(a), afrodescendiente and afrocolombiano(a), with separate options for Raizal people of the San Andrés, Providencia and Santa Catalina archipelago and Palenquero people of San Basilio. In the 2018 census, answers in these groups fell 30.8% compared with 2005. DANE attributed this to incomplete answers, including the question not being asked, refusals and areas that could not be safely covered. Its official figure of 4,671,160 people (9.34%) therefore comes from a household survey estimate (the 2018 Quality of Life Survey) rather than the census count.",figure:"4,671,160 people (9.34%)",year:"2018",disputed:true,note:"The 2018 census count itself was 2,982,224 (6.8%), down from 4,311,757 (10.4%) in 2005; DANE adopted the survey estimate as the official total. The fall was publicly contested, including a 2020 legal challenge (tutela) reported by La Silla Vacía. 'Mulato' is usually translated as mixed, but DANE does not define the term.",src:[SRCQ("DANE: Población negra, afrocolombiana, raizal y palenquera, document for the IACHR hearing (June 2022)","https://www.dane.gov.co/files/investigaciones/boletines/grupos-etnicos/CIDH_poblaci%C3%B3n_narp.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","De acuerdo con su cultura, pueblo o rasgos físicos… es o se reconoce como:"),SRCQ("DANE: Comunicado de prensa, población NARP (25 Nov 2019)","https://www.dane.gov.co/files/investigaciones/boletines/grupos-etnicos/comunicado-grupos-etnicos-poblacion-NARP-2019-debate-ctrl-politico-camara-rep.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","es de 4.671.160 personas, que corresponde al 9,34% de la población total nacional."),SRCQ("La Silla Vacía: Detector: la cifra de afros en Colombia es debatible (22 Nov 2022)","https://www.lasillavacia.com/detector-de-mentiras/detector-la-cifra-de-afros-en-colombia-de-francia-marquez-es-debatible/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","la disminución en un 30.8 por ciento de la población afro del país respecto al censo de 2005.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mexico: 2.6 million people identified as Afro-Mexican in 2020",text:"Mexico's 2020 Census, run by INEGI, counted 2,576,213 people who identified as Afro-Mexican, Afro-descendant or Black (negra), about 2% of the population. Mexico's Constitution recognises Afro-Mexican peoples and communities 'whatever their self-designation', reflecting that people use several names for themselves.",figure:"2,576,213 people (2%)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"Count and share from INEGI's presentation of 2020 Census results, also reported by the Mexico City Human Rights Commission and the Wilson Center. People were counted if they recognised themselves as Afro-Mexican or Afro-descendant; the Wilson Center describes the group as including people who identify as Black (negra).",src:[SRCQ("INEGI: Censo de Población y Vivienda 2020, presentación de resultados (2021)","https://inegi.org.mx/contenidos/inegi/ccu/2021/primera_sesion/presentaciones/result_cues_cpv20.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","POBLACIÓN QUE SE AUTORRECONOCE AFROMEXICANA O AFRODESCENDIENTE 2 576 213"),SRCQ("Comisión de Derechos Humanos de la Ciudad de México: Boletín de prensa 11/2021 (25 Jan 2021)","https://cdhcm.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/Bol1121.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","2 millones 576 mil 213 personas afromexicanas y/o afrodescendientes en nuestro país"),SRCQ("Wilson Center: Infografía | Afrodescendientes en México (25 Jul 2022)","https://www.wilsoncenter.org/article/infografia-afrodescendientes-en-mexico","Supports the Mixed heritage page","incluye a la población que se identifica como afromexicana, afrodescendiente o negra"),SRCQ("Comisión de Derechos Humanos de la Ciudad de México: Folleto población afromexicana (undated)","https://cdhcm.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/Folleto-Afro-digital.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Esta Constitución reconoce a los pueblos y comunidades afromexicanas, cualquiera que sea su autodenominación,")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"United States: 'Two or More Races' rose to 33.8 million in 2020",text:"The US Census Bureau lets people choose more than one race. The 'Two or More Races' population was 33.8 million (10.2%) in 2020, up from 9 million in 2010, and 46.9 million people (14.2%) were Black or African American alone or in combination with another race. The Bureau says much of the rise reflects improved question design and coding, so 2010 to 2020 comparisons need caution.",figure:"33.8 million Two or More Races (10.2%); 46.9 million Black alone or in combination (14.2%)",year:"2020",disputed:false,note:"The 276% increase is partly a measurement change, as the Census Bureau states. Pew's figure of a 295% rise in non-Hispanic multiracial Black people compares 2000 with 2024 using Census Bureau data, including the 2024 American Community Survey, so it is a different source and period. In 2020, 3.1 million people reported both White and Black or African American, the third-largest multiracial combination.",src:[SRCQ("US Census Bureau: 2020 Census Illuminates Racial and Ethnic Composition of the Country (12 Aug 2021)","https://census.gov/library/stories/2021/08/improved-race-ethnicity-measures-reveal-united-states-population-much-more-multiracial.html","Supports the Mixed heritage page","It was measured at 9 million people in 2010 and is now 33.8 million people in 2020, a 276% increase."),SRCQ("Pew Research Center: Key facts about Black Americans (revised 5 Feb 2026; first published 10 Feb 2023)","https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/02/10/key-facts-about-black-americans/","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Notably, the number of Black people who are multiracial and not Hispanic has increased 295%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"South Africa: 'Coloured' is a population group used in statistics",text:"Statistics South Africa still publishes figures by population group, and in Census 2022 people in the 'Coloured' group made up 8.2% of the national population; the City of Cape Town's census profile puts the share in Cape Town at 35.0%. The categories come from apartheid-era legal race classification. Censuses have asked people to identify themselves since 1996, and Stats SA's Statistician-General explained that the question was kept so that progress in eradicating the consequences of apartheid could be measured and monitored. The figure records how people describe themselves in the census; it is a statistical category, not a judgement about anyone's ancestry.",figure:"8.2% Coloured (national); 35.0% (Cape Town)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Handle the term with care: it comes from apartheid race classification (a 2016 Stats SA presentation lists the pre-1994 legislated groups as including 'Coloured (Mixed)'). How far people today embrace or reject the label is not covered here. The Cape Town share is from the City of Cape Town, using Stats SA census data.",src:[SRCQ("Statistics South Africa: Media release: Census 2022 Population Count Results (10 Oct 2023)","https://statssa.gov.za/?p=16716","Supports the Mixed heritage page","Black Africans remain the dominant population group (81,4%), followed by coloureds at 8,2%,"),SRCQ("Statistics South Africa (UN Expert Group Meeting on Data Disaggregation): Disaggregation by Race within the Rainbow Nation (27-29 June 2016)","https://unstats.un.org/sdgs/files/meetings/egm-data-dissaggregation/PPT21-Booysen.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","progress in eradicating the consequences of apartheid could be measured and monitored"),SRCQ("City of Cape Town: 2022 Census Cape Town profile (18 Dec 2023)","https://resource.capetown.gov.za/documentcentre/Documents/City%20research%20reports%20and%20review/2022_Census_Cape_Town_profile.pdf","Supports the Mixed heritage page","The population is predominantly Black African (45.7%) and Coloured (35.0%).")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"How you describe your heritage is yours to choose, and it is normal for answers to change over time.",refs:["mx-words-mixed-race-mixed-heritage-dual-heritage-", "mx-words-the-same-person-may-answer-differently-o"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"'Mixed' covers many different heritages; ask people how they describe themselves rather than assuming.",refs:["mx-words-mixed-race-mixed-heritage-dual-heritage-", "mx-data-aggregated-mixed-figures-hide-difference"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Mixed groups are the youngest in England and Wales, so today's pupils and new recruits include many more Mixed-heritage people.",refs:["mx-numbers-mixed-groups-are-the-youngest-in-england"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Official statistics show White and Black Caribbean pupils suspended and excluded at higher rates, and Mixed children over-represented in care and custody.",refs:["mx-outcomes-white-and-black-caribbean-pupils-suspens", "mx-outcomes-children-from-mixed-groups-are-over-repr", "mx-outcomes-mixed-children-are-over-represented-in-y"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Do not report 'Mixed' as one group where numbers allow; records agree least with the census for Mixed groups.",refs:["mx-data-aggregated-mixed-figures-hide-difference", "mx-data-health-records-agree-least-with-census-f"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"About 1.7 million people in England and Wales are in a Mixed ethnic group; White and Black Caribbean is the largest.",refs:["mx-numbers-1-7-million-people-in-england-and-wales-", "mx-numbers-white-and-black-caribbean-is-the-largest"]},{t:"Mixed groups are the youngest ethnic groups in England and Wales.",refs:["mx-numbers-mixed-groups-are-the-youngest-in-england"]},{t:"Official statistics show higher suspension and exclusion rates for White and Black Caribbean pupils.",refs:["mx-outcomes-white-and-black-caribbean-pupils-suspens", "mx-outcomes-higher-permanent-exclusion-rates-even-af"]},{t:"Records agree least with the census for Mixed groups, so treat the data with care.",refs:["mx-data-health-records-agree-least-with-census-f"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["stem"]={name:"Science, engineering and the built environment",pfx:"st",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"stpicture",h:"Who works in science, engineering and the built environment",intro:"Published UK data show Black people are under-represented across engineering, architecture and planning, though the size of the gap varies by profession. Separate Mixed figures are published for some professions but not all.",points:[
  {h:"Black workers are under-represented in engineering and technology jobs",text:"EngineeringUK's analysis of the 2024 Labour Force Survey found 2.7% of people in UK engineering and technology occupations were Black/African/Caribbean/Black British, against 5.3% in all other occupations combined. Mixed/Multiple ethnic groups made up 1.3%, against 1.7% elsewhere. Overall, 13.7% of the engineering and technology workforce came from minority ethnic groups, compared with 17.5% in other occupations.",figure:"2.7% Black; 1.3% Mixed (other occupations: 5.3%; 1.7%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Labour Force Survey sample sizes limit the analysis to five broad ethnic groups. The April 2026 dashboard rounds the comparison (Black 3% vs 6%; Mixed 2% vs 2%; 15% minority ethnic) and does not state its reference year. Both sources come from the same publisher.",src:[SRCQ("EngineeringUK: The engineering and technology workforce (May 2025)","https://www.engineeringuk.com/media/vjzowjeq/the-engineering-and-technology-workforce-update-engineeringuk-may-2025.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","We found a smaller percentage of UK minority ethnic groups were working in engineering and technology occupations (13.7%) compared to all other occupations combined (17.5%) (table 3)."),SRCQ("EngineeringUK: Engineering and technology workforce dashboard (April 2026)","https://www.engineeringuk.com/media/mnuhargd/dashboard-engineering-tech-workforce-engineeringuk-april-26.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Black 3% 6%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black women are under-represented among women in engineering roles",text:"Among women working in UK engineering and technology occupations in 2024, 3.8% were Black/African/Caribbean/Black British, compared with 5.4% of women in all other occupations combined. Women from Mixed/Multiple ethnic groups were 2.0% of women in engineering and technology, against 1.7% elsewhere, a difference EngineeringUK describes as small or none.",figure:"3.8% Black women (vs 5.4%); 2.0% Mixed women (vs 1.7%)",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Single publisher; based on EngineeringUK analysis of 2024 Labour Force Survey data for the UK. Women from minority ethnic groups overall were better represented (20.7% vs 16.7%), driven by Asian women, so the aggregate hides the Black gap.",src:[SRCQ("EngineeringUK: The engineering and technology workforce (May 2025)","https://www.engineeringuk.com/media/vjzowjeq/the-engineering-and-technology-workforce-update-engineeringuk-may-2025.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","We saw the opposite effect, however, for Black/African/Caribbean and Black British women, who were underrepresented in engineering and technology occupations (3.8%) compared to all other occupations combined (5.4%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"About 1% of UK-registered architects are Black or Black British",text:"The Architects Registration Board's analysis of the Register in 2022 found Black or Black British people made up 1% of architects, against 4% of the UK population, and Mixed ethnic groups 2%, against 3%. In 2021, 2% of new registrants were Black or Black British. 70% of Black or Black British architects were based in London and the South East.",figure:"1% Black; 2% Mixed",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Ethnicity comes from ARB's voluntary EDI survey, completed by over 70% of architects; 7% preferred not to say their ethnicity. The trade press source reproduces ARB's release, so it is not independent evidence.",src:[SRCQ("Architects Registration Board: Architects today, analysis of the architects' profession in 2022 (April 2023)","https://arb.org.uk/architects-today/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Black or Black British people are underrepresented on the Register, accounting for 1% of architects but 4% of the UK population."),SRCQ("Architects Registration Board: Architects today (April 2023)","https://arb.org.uk/architects-today/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Mixed and Other ethnic groups account for 2% and 1% of the Register respectively, but 3% and 2% of the UK population."),SRCQ("Specification Online: New data highlights under-represented groups in the architects' profession (13 April 2023)","https://www.specificationonline.co.uk/articles/2023-04-13/new-data-highlights-under-represented-groups-in-the-architects-profession","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Representation is improving over time. For example, in 2021, 77% of new registrants were White and 2% were Black or Black British")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"About 5% of town planners are from racialised minorities",text:"The Royal Town Planning Institute's State of the Profession 2023 estimates, from a five-year average of Annual Population Survey data (2016-2020), that 95% of town planning officers belong to a White ethnic group and 5% to a racialised ethnicity. The RTPI says sample sizes are too small for more detailed breakdowns, so no separate Black or Mixed figures are published. 19.50% of UK student members identified with a racialised minority ethnicity, with no data for 43.41%.",figure:"5% racialised minority (no Black or Mixed breakdown)",year:"2016-2020 average (published November 2023)",disputed:false,note:"'Racialised minority' is the RTPI's term. The planner estimate is a UK five-year average of Annual Population Survey data for 2016-2020; the comparison population (81% White, 19% racialised minorities) is the 2021 Census for England and Wales, so the two do not cover the same geography or period. Single publisher.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Town Planning Institute: State of the Profession 2023 (November 2023)","https://www.rtpi.org.uk/media/16015/state-of-the-profession-2023-final.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Based on the 5-year average of APS estimates (2016-2020), 95% of Town Planning Officers belong to a White ethnic group, and 5% to a racialised ethnicity."),SRCQ("Royal Town Planning Institute: State of the Profession 2023 (November 2023)","https://www.rtpi.org.uk/media/16015/state-of-the-profession-2023-final.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Because of small sample sizes, more detailed breakdowns are not currently possible on the basis of this data.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black workers are 4% of the construction site workforce in CITB survey",text:"CITB's 2025 Construction Workforce Mobility Survey of 3,611 UK site workers found 4% were of Black origin, up from 2% in 2022, and 1% Mixed, against 5% and 2% of the UK population aged 16-64. Separately, Annual Population Survey data for 2021 published by the Government show 3.5% of Black workers and 5.6% of workers of Mixed ethnicity were employed in construction, compared with 6.5% of all workers.",figure:"4% Black; 1% Mixed (CITB site sample)",year:"2025 (CITB); 2021 (APS)",disputed:false,note:"The two measures differ: CITB gives the ethnic make-up of an on-site interview sample; the APS figures give the share of each ethnic group's workers employed in the construction sector (UK, 2021).",src:[SRCQ("CITB: Construction Workforce Mobility 2025, full report (2025)","https://www.citb.co.uk/media/dj0fmes3/workforce-mobility-2025-full-report.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","While still at low levels, significant increases have been seen in terms of those of Black origin (from 2% to 4%) and those of Asian origin (from 2% to 5%) since 2022."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Employment by sector (data for 2021)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/work-pay-and-benefits/employment/employment-by-sector/latest","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Construction 6.5 2.7 2.8 3.1 2.1 3.5 5.6 6.9 7.0 6.9 6.4 4.0")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"1.7% of UK STEM academic staff were Black in 2018/19",text:"Analysis of HESA records compiled by Jisc for the Royal Society found that in 2018/19, 18.7% of STEM academic staff in UK higher education were from ethnic minority groups, including 13.2% who were Asian and 1.7% who were Black. By comparison, 7.1% of STEM postgraduate entrants were Black. The report says there appears to be a drop-off of Black and Mixed ethnicity postgraduates entering STEM academic employment, and 3.5% of Black STEM academic staff held professor posts, against 11.9% of white staff.",figure:"1.7% Black (STEM academic staff)",year:"2018/19",disputed:false,note:"Data are now several years old. The report warns that broad categories hide variation by subject, sex, age, nationality and institution.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society: STEM sector must step up and end unacceptable disparities in Black staff and students academic progression and success (25 March 2021)","https://royalsociety.org/news/2021/03/stem-ethnicity-report","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","In 2018/19, 18.7% of academic staff in STEM were from ethnic minority groups, 13.2% were Asian compared to 1.7% who were Black."),SRCQ("Royal Society / Jisc: Ethnicity STEM data for students and academic staff in higher education 2007/08 to 2018/19 (March 2021)","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/Publications/2021/trends-ethnic-minorities-stem/Ethnicity-STEM-data-for-students-and-academic-staff-in-higher-education.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","There appears to be a drop-off of Black and mixed ethnicity STEM postgraduate students entering STEM academic employment at universities.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"stpath",h:"Pathways into science, engineering and construction",intro:"Black students enter engineering degrees broadly in line with all subjects, but published evidence shows gaps in degree results, early employment and progression. Learned-society reports describe cultural and structural barriers.",points:[
  {h:"8.1% of engineering and technology students were Black in 2020/21",text:"EngineeringUK's 'Engineering in higher education' report, using 2020/21 HESA data, found 8.1% of engineering and technology students were Black and 4.9% were Mixed, similar to all subjects (8.3% and 4.8%). Asian students were better represented in engineering and technology than across all subjects (18% vs 12.7%).",figure:"8.1% Black; 4.9% Mixed",year:"2020/21",disputed:false,note:"Data are for 2020/21 and cover UK higher education students. The 2.7% Black share of the engineering and technology workforce (EngineeringUK, 2024 Labour Force Survey) covers a different population and year, so the two figures cannot be compared directly. The second source reproduces the first.",src:[SRCQ("EngineeringUK: EngineeringUK releases new report on engineering in higher education (16 March 2023)","https://www.engineeringuk.com/latest-news/news-articles/engineeringuk-releases-new-report-on-engineering-in-higher-education/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","However, only 8.1% of engineering and technology students were Black and 4.9% were mixed – which is similar for all subjects (8.3% and 4.8 respectively)."),SRCQ("Electronic Specifier: EngineeringUK releases report on engineering in higher education (2023)","https://www.electronicspecifier.com/studentzone/studentzone-news/engineeringuk-releases-report-on-engineering-in-higher-education/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","However, only 8.1% of engineering and technology students were Black and 4.9% were mixed – which is similar for all subjects (8.3% and 4.8 respectively).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black STEM graduates half as likely as white peers to get a first",text:"In 2018/19, 17.9% of Black UK-domiciled STEM first-degree qualifiers achieved first-class honours, compared with 35.7% of white qualifiers, and 9.5% of Black students left with a third, against 3.2% of white students. The Jisc report for the Royal Society found the 17.8 percentage point first-class gap statistically significant. Non-completion among Black STEM first-degree students was 4.7% in 2017/18, compared with 2.7% for white students.",figure:"17.9% firsts (Black) vs 35.7% (white)",year:"2018/19",disputed:false,note:"Data are for 2018/19 (degree classes) and 2017/18 (non-completion). Raw gaps do not adjust for entry qualifications or age; the report shows older qualifiers in all groups get fewer good honours, and that after adjusting for POLAR4 participation area there is very little difference in first-degree non-completion between ethnic minority and white STEM students taken together. Mixed ethnicity STEM qualifiers are reported separately (45.8% achieved upper second class honours).",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society: STEM sector must step up and end unacceptable disparities in Black staff and students academic progression and success (25 March 2021)","https://royalsociety.org/news/2021/03/stem-ethnicity-report","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","White students in 2018/19 were twice as likely as Black students to graduate with first class honours – 35.7% compared to 17.9%."),SRCQ("Royal Society / Jisc: Ethnicity STEM data for students and academic staff in higher education 2007/08 to 2018/19 (March 2021)","https://royalsociety.org/-/media/policy/Publications/2021/trends-ethnic-minorities-stem/Ethnicity-STEM-data-for-students-and-academic-staff-in-higher-education.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","The largest variation by ethnic group is for those achieving first class honours, where the gap is 17.8 percentage points between Black and white STEM qualifiers.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnic minority share of construction apprenticeship starts: about 9%",text:"In England in 2024/25, apprentices from ethnic minorities (excluding white minorities) made up 18.0% of all apprenticeship starts. Calculated from the same Department for Education data, they made up about 9.3% of starts in Construction, Planning and the Built Environment (2,270 of 24,280 with known ethnicity) and 12.5% in Engineering and Manufacturing Technologies (5,670 of 45,330). Over the five years since 2019/20, starts grew 42.6% for Black learners and 52.0% for Mixed learners.",figure:"9.3% construction; 12.5% engineering (calculated)",year:"2024/25",disputed:false,note:"Sector percentages are calculated by this hub, not published by DfE, using DfE's own method for the 18.0% headline (62,880 of 348,530 starts with known ethnicity). Counts are rounded to the nearest 10. This data set does not split starts by sector for Black or Mixed learners separately; DfE also reports only 10.3% of 2023/24 leavers in Engineering and Manufacturing Technologies were from ethnic minorities.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Education: Apprenticeships, academic year 2024/25 (27 November 2025)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/apprenticeships/2024-25","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Starts by learners from ethnic minorities (excluding white minorities) as a proportion of all starts have increased year-on-year to 18.0% in 2024/25."),SRCQ("Department for Education: Apprenticeships, academic year 2024/25 (27 November 2025)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/find-statistics/apprenticeships/2024-25","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","52.0% for mixed / multiple ethnic groups and 42.6% for Black / African / Caribbean / Black British learners"),SRCQ("Department for Education: Subjects - Starts, Enrolments by Age, Sex, Ethnicity, LLDD, SSA, Detailed level, STEM (27 November 2025)","https://explore-education-statistics.service.gov.uk/data-catalogue/data-set/d88d482d-4235-4d83-b249-ab60110cf55f","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Ethnic minorities do not include white minorities.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black engineering graduates of 2009/10-2013/14 had lowest full-time work rate",text:"A Royal Academy of Engineering study published in November 2016, using HESA DLHE data for UK-domiciled full-time first-degree graduates from 2009/10 to 2013/14, found 71% of white engineering graduates were in full-time employment six months after graduating, against 51% of black and minority ethnic graduates and 46% of Black graduates. Six months after graduating, 14% of Black engineering graduates were unemployed, compared with 7% of white engineering graduates.",figure:"46% in full-time work (Black) vs 71% (white)",year:"Published November 2016 (DLHE cohorts to 2013/14)",disputed:false,note:"Single source, and the data predate the current Graduate Outcomes survey. The report says the differences were significantly greater for engineering graduates than across all subjects.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Academy of Engineering: Employment outcomes of engineering graduates: key factors and diversity characteristics (November 2016)","https://raeng.org.uk/media/u5mcn41w/employment-outcomes-of-engineering-graduates-report.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Six months after graduation, 14% of black engineering graduates were unemployed, compared with only 7% of white engineering graduates;"),SRCQ("Royal Academy of Engineering: Employment outcomes of engineering graduates (November 2016)","https://raeng.org.uk/media/u5mcn41w/employment-outcomes-of-engineering-graduates-report.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Black engineering graduates had the lowest proportion in full time work at 46%;")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black representation in chemistry falls from 4.9% of undergraduates",text:"The Royal Society of Chemistry's 2022 'Missing Elements' report found 4.9% of chemistry undergraduates identified as Black, but only 1.4% of PhD chemistry students and 0% of chemistry professors did. It also found Black and minoritised ethnic chemists were paid £6,000 less than White colleagues, and principal investigators from those groups were 7 percentage points less likely to win research funding.",figure:"4.9% undergraduates; 1.4% PhD; 0% professors (Black)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The 0% reflects rounding: IOM3 reports that values under 2.5% were rounded down to zero to protect anonymity, and that the 0% applies to professors identifying as Black or Mixed Race. The RSC release says 'PhD' students where IOM3 says 'postgraduate'.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Society of Chemistry: New £1.5m unit to tackle racial inequalities in science (March 2022)","https://www.rsc.org/news/2022/march/new-%C2%A31-5m-unit-to-tackle-racial-inequalities-in-science","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Yet this number rapidly drops along the academic career ladder, with only 1.4% of PhD chemistry students and 0% of chemistry professors identifying as Black."),SRCQ("IOM3: Royal Society of Chemistry reports racial inequalities within chemical sciences (16 March 2022)","https://www.iom3.org/resource/royal-society-of-chemistry-reports-racial-inequalities-within-chemical-sciences.html","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","The RSC found that while 4.9% of undergraduate students identify as Black, only 1.4% of postgraduate chemistry students do, while just 1% of non-professional academic chemistry staff do.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Engineers report race-related microaggressions and family barriers",text:"The Royal Academy of Engineering's 2023 study (1,507 engineers surveyed, plus 26 focus groups and 50 interviews) found engineering was often a second choice for people from South Asian or Black African backgrounds whose families may favour more 'prestigious' professions. Engineers who reported bullying or harassment mostly experienced microaggressions related to gender and race, which often went unchallenged. King's College London notes Black students have lower attainment in maths and physics and are less likely to achieve a 2:1 or first.",figure:"",year:"2022 fieldwork (published March 2023)",disputed:false,note:"Qualitative findings describe experiences, not prevalence by ethnic group. Seven in ten engineers in the same survey (69%) perceived the profession's culture as inclusive, so the evidence is mixed rather than one-directional. The King's College London statement comes from a 2022 funding announcement and gives no figures or reference year.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Academy of Engineering / DJS Research: Cultural inclusivity in engineering (March 2023)","https://raeng.org.uk/media/w5ndkndk/cultural-inclusivity-in-engineering-main-report-final.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Engineers who reported bullying or harassment mostly experienced microaggressions related to gender and race, which often go unchallenged and can have a more detrimental impact on mental health compared to explicit discrimination."),SRCQ("Royal Academy of Engineering / DJS Research: Cultural inclusivity in engineering (March 2023)","https://raeng.org.uk/media/w5ndkndk/cultural-inclusivity-in-engineering-main-report-final.pdf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","or Black African backgrounds (whose families may pressure them into more ‘prestigious’ professions e.g. Doctor.)"),SRCQ("King's College London: Equality programme, Success for Black Engineers awarded Royal Academy of Engineering funding (4 April 2022)","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/news/equality-programme-success-for-black-engineers-awarded-royal-academy-of-engineering-funding","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Black students have lower attainment in key engineering subjects of maths and physics compared to their white counterparts and are less likely to achieve a 2.1 or 1st class degree outcome.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"sthistory",h:"Documented British history",intro:"Museums, archives, universities and learned societies record Black and Mixed-heritage people in British science, engineering and architecture from the early nineteenth century. The records are often fragmentary, and institutions say more people remain to be found.",points:[
  {h:"John Edmonstone taught Charles Darwin taxidermy in Edinburgh",text:"The Natural History Museum describes John Edmonstone as 'a former enslaved Black man from Guyana' who, while living in Edinburgh, taught the young Charles Darwin taxidermy. The museum says he was born into slavery in Guyana in 1790 and by 1824 was working for the University of Edinburgh's zoological museum. The Linnean Society records that Darwin's Galápagos finches were preserved using techniques Edmonstone had taught him.",figure:"",year:"1820s",disputed:false,note:"Dates differ between sources: the Linnean Society says he moved to Edinburgh in 1823 and that Darwin met him in 1826. The Natural History Museum refers to his death but says his burial place is not known; neither source gives a date of death. The museum notes that the Galapagos mockingbirds, rather than the finches, did most to prompt Darwin's thinking on evolution.",src:[SRCQ("Natural History Museum: John Edmonstone, the man who taught Darwin taxidermy (n.d.)","https://nhm.ac.uk/discover/john-edmonstone-the-man-who-taught-darwin-taxidermy.html","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","A former enslaved Black man from Guyana, John was living in Edinburgh when he met a young Charles Darwin and taught him the skill of taxidermy."),SRCQ("Linnean Society: The man who taught Charles Darwin taxidermy (2 October 2018)","https://www.linnean.org/the-society/news/2018/10/02/the-man-who-taught-charles-darwin-taxidermy","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","The Galápagos finches, used to support his theory on the transmutation of species, were preserved using the techniques that Edmonstone had taught him.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Andrew Watson studied engineering at Glasgow before football fame",text:"University of Glasgow Archives record that Andrew Watson, born in Georgetown, Guyana, enrolled at the University in November 1875 to study philosophy, mathematics and engineering, leaving in 1876 for a football career. The Institution of Civil Engineers describes him as 'an engineer and the first Black British international footballer', who spent 20 years working on ships and became a qualified engineer.",figure:"",year:"1875",disputed:false,note:"The University gives his birth year as 1857; other accounts give 1856. He died in Kew, Surrey, on 8 March 1921 (Scottish Football Association). His engineering career is less documented than his football career.",src:[SRCQ("University of Glasgow Archives & Special Collections: Guyana (n.d.)","https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/archivespecialcollections/digitisedcollections/internationalarchives/southamerica/guyana","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","In November 1875, at the age of 19, he enrolled at the University of Glasgow to study philosophy, mathematics and engineering."),SRCQ("Institution of Civil Engineers: Black excellence isn't new: finding the UK's examples (31 October 2024)","https://www.ice.org.uk/news-views-insights/inside-infrastructure/black-excellence-isnt-new-finding-the-uks-examples","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Andrew Watson was an engineer and the first Black British international footballer."),SRCQ("Scottish Football Association: Remembering Andrew Watson","https://www.scottishfa.co.uk/news/remembering-andrew-watson","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Andrew died in Kew, Surrey on 8 March, 1921")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"George Bemand: UCL engineering student and artillery officer",text:"Dulwich College's First World War archive records that George Edward Kingsley Bemand, born in Jamaica in 1892 to 'a white father, George, and a black mother, Minnie', went on from the College to University College London, where he studied engineering. Commissioned in the Royal Field Artillery in May 1915, he was, the College says, 'very possibly the first person of colour, outside of India' to hold such a commission in the modern British Army. He was killed on 26 December 1916.",figure:"",year:"1915-1916",disputed:false,note:"Sources differ on his date of birth (19 March per Dulwich College; 9 March per the Western Front Association). The 'first' claim is hedged by its source.",src:[SRCQ("Dulwich College 1914-18: Bemand, GEK (n.d.)","https://dulwichcollege1914-18.co.uk/fallen/bemand-gek/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","From the College he went on to University College London, where he studied engineering."),SRCQ("Western Front Association: 26 December 1916, 2nd Lt George Edward Kingsley Bemand (n.d.)","https://www.westernfrontassociation.com/remembered-on-this-day/december/26-december-1916-2nd-lt-george-edward-kingsley-bemand/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","He subsequently went up to University College London as a student.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Lilian Bader qualified among the first WAAF instrument repairers",text:"The RAF Museum records that Lilian Bader, born in Liverpool in 1917 to a Barbadian father who had served in the Royal Navy and an Irish mother, joined the Women's Auxiliary Air Force in 1941 and trained as an Instrument Repairer. She became one of the first women in the air force to qualify in that trade, checked aircraft instruments for faults at RAF Shawbury and was promoted to Acting Corporal. She died on 14 March 2015.",figure:"",year:"1941",disputed:false,note:"Her trade was technical aircraft maintenance rather than professional engineering. The RAF Museum records that she was forced to leave a NAAFI canteen job in 1939 'because she was Black'.",src:[SRCQ("RAF Museum: Technician, Leading Aircraftwoman Lilian Bader (n.d.)","https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/online-exhibitions/pilots-of-the-caribbean/heroes-and-sheroes/technician-leading-aircraftwoman-lilian-bader/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","She nevertheless passed her course ‘First Class’, becoming one of the first women in the air force to qualify in that trade."),SRCQ("RAF Benevolent Fund: Black History Month, Lilian Bader, a trailblazer in the RAF (2 October 2025)","https://rafbf.org/news-and-stories/raf-history/black-history-month-lilian-bader-trailblazer-raf","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","There, she was one of the first women to become an instrument repairer, moving to Shropshire to work on Airspeed Oxford light bombers.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"RIBA archives: earliest Black member found so far elected in 1950",text:"RIBA's collections team identifies Reuben Johnson Oluwole Wright (1914-1990), an architect from Sierra Leone who trained in Edinburgh, as the earliest Black member it has found so far; he became an associate in 1950. Its search also surfaced Ransford Jarrett-Yaskey MBE (1924-2000), Chief Architect to the Sierra Leone Ministry of Works, who was elected an Associate Member in 1958.",figure:"",year:"1950",disputed:false,note:"RIBA stresses that nomination papers did not record ethnicity and that earlier Black members probably exist. Single institutional source.",src:[SRCQ("RIBA: Who was the first Black RIBA Member? (n.d.)","https://www.riba.org/explore/riba-collections/inside-our-collections/the-first-black-riba-member/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","The date of his election to our membership makes him the earliest Black member we have come across so far"),SRCQ("RIBA: Who was the first Black RIBA Member? (n.d.)","https://www.riba.org/explore/riba-collections/inside-our-collections/the-first-black-riba-member/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","In our membership lists, we find that Wright became an associate in 1950.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"stgov",h:"Government, the public sector and learned bodies",intro:"Two Civil Service professions cover this field: Government Science and Engineering and the Government Planning Profession. Official workforce data show lower ethnic minority representation in science and engineering than across the Civil Service, and very small numbers in planning.",points:[
  {h:"Government Science and Engineering: over 10,000 civil servants",text:"The Government Science and Engineering (GSE) profession describes itself as over 10,000 civil servants with a background or interest in science and engineering, working in specialist, deep specialist, policy, analytical and operational roles. It is managed from within the Government Office for Science, and Crown and public servants are welcome to be members.",figure:"10,000+ members (undated, page sets out 2016-17 priorities)",year:"",disputed:false,note:"Membership of the GSE profession is open and is not the same as the 'Science and Engineering' profession recorded in Civil Service Statistics. The 10,000 figure comes from an undated GOV.UK page that sets out priorities for 2016 to 2017 and names Sir Patrick Vallance as Head of Profession, so it may be out of date.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: About us, Government Science & Engineering Profession (n.d.)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/civil-service-government-science-engineering/about","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","We are made up of over 10,000 civil servants with a background or interest in science and engineering, who work in a range of roles"),SRCQ("GOV.UK GSE blog: Black History Month, contributions to science and engineering (1 October 2020)","https://governmentscienceandengineering.blog.gov.uk/2020/10/01/black-history-month-contributions-to-science-and-engineering/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","The Government Science and Engineering profession, championed by Patrick Vallance, includes scientists and engineers and those with training in these areas from across the Civil Service")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black staff are 3.1% of Civil Service science and engineering staff",text:"Civil Service Statistics for 31 March 2025 record 395 Black and 320 Mixed civil servants in the Science and Engineering profession: about 3.1% and 2.5% of the 12,765 with known ethnicity, with 13.7% from ethnic minority groups overall, against 18.0% across the whole Civil Service. Median salary was £38,380 for Black staff in the profession and £45,290 for White staff. In 2020 the GSE profession's own blog put its ‘BAME’ share at around 4%, against around 10% of all civil servants.",figure:"3.1% Black; 2.5% Mixed (calculated)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Percentages calculated by this hub from rounded headcounts (nearest 5), excluding 2,845 staff with unknown or undeclared ethnicity, so they are approximate. The 2020 figure used a different survey basis and is not directly comparable. Salary gaps are not adjusted for grade.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser 2025, profession of post by ethnicity (generated 6 November 2025)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2025/Profession_of_post/Ethnicity/index.html","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","\"In post\",\"2025\",\"Black\",\"Science and Engineering\",\"395\",\"385\",\"42960\",\"38380\""),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin, Civil Service Statistics 2025","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2025/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2025","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Of those with a known ethnicity, the percentage who are from an ethnic minority background is at a record high of 18.0%."),SRCQ("GOV.UK GSE blog: Black History Month, contributions to science and engineering (1 October 2020)","https://governmentscienceandengineering.blog.gov.uk/2020/10/01/black-history-month-contributions-to-science-and-engineering/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","around 10% of Civil Servants report that they are from BAME heritage whereas for the GSE profession this is around 4%.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Government Planning Profession: numbers too small for reliable shares",text:"The Government Planning Profession supports planners and planning inspectors, most of whom work in the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government and the Planning Inspectorate. Civil Service Statistics for March 2025 record 10 Black and 5 Mixed civil servants in the Planning profession, against 330 White (headcounts rounded to the nearest 5). Black and Mixed counts for Planning Inspectors are suppressed because they are too small to publish.",figure:"10 Black; 5 Mixed (Planning profession headcount)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Ethnicity is unknown or undeclared for 90 of the Planning profession headcount, so percentages would be unreliable. Most UK planners work in local government and the private sector, outside the Civil Service.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: About us, Government Planning Profession (n.d.)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/civil-service-the-government-planning-profession/about","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","We support professional planners and planning inspectors, most of whom work in the Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government and the Planning Inspectorate."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser 2025, profession of post by ethnicity (generated 6 November 2025)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2025/Profession_of_post/Ethnicity/index.html","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","\"In post\",\"2025\",\"Black\",\"Planning\",\"10\",\"10\",\"[c]\",\"[c]\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Royal Academy of Engineering funds university diversity projects",text:"The Royal Academy of Engineering's Diversity Impact Programme, launched in October 2021, gives grants of up to £100,000 to university engineering departments to address unequal outcomes for students from under-represented groups; the Academy reports almost £2m awarded to 20 universities. A 2022 award went to King's College London's 'Success for Black Engineers' project, which aims to increase Black applicants and raise attainment and wellbeing for Black engineering students.",figure:"Almost £2m to 20 universities",year:"2021-",disputed:false,note:"The 'almost £2m to 20 universities' total is a cumulative figure from an undated programme page. The Academy refers to an interim evaluation of the 2022 cohort, but its published programme pages give no outcome measures by ethnicity, so impact is not yet evidenced.",src:[SRCQ("Royal Academy of Engineering: Diversity Impact Programme (n.d.)","https://raeng.org.uk/programmes-and-prizes/programmes/uk-grants-and-prizes/support-for-education/diversity-impact-programme/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","We provide grant funding of up to £100,000 for new projects in university engineering departments that address the unequal outcomes experienced by students from underrepresented groups."),SRCQ("Royal Academy of Engineering: Academy awards grants totalling £1 million for innovative interventions to increase diversity and inclusion in engineering (30 March 2022)","https://raeng.org.uk/news/academy-awards-grants-totalling-1-million-for-innovative-interventions-to-increase-diversity-and-inclusion-in-engineering/","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","Launched in October last year, the Diversity Impact Programme aims to inspire change in university engineering departments so that all students succeed"),SRCQ("King's College London: Equality programme, Success for Black Engineers awarded Royal Academy of Engineering funding (4 April 2022)","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/news/equality-programme-success-for-black-engineers-awarded-royal-academy-of-engineering-funding","Supports the Science, engineering and the built environment page","The intermediate outcomes of the project are to increase black applicants to King’s Engineering programmes, increase attainment and wellbeing for black engineering students")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Museums and learned societies record Black and Mixed-heritage people in British science and engineering from the early nineteenth century.",refs:["st-sthistory-john-edmonstone-taught-charles-darwin-ta", "st-sthistory-lilian-bader-qualified-among-the-first-w"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Engineers from Black and South Asian backgrounds report microaggressions and family pressures; notice and challenge them.",refs:["st-stpath-engineers-report-race-related-microaggre"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"A study published in 2016 found Black engineering graduates had the lowest full-time employment rate; check who your recruitment reaches.",refs:["st-stpath-black-engineering-graduates-of-2009-10-2"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Ethnic minority apprentices were about 9.3% of construction starts in England in 2024/25, against 18.0% of all starts.",refs:["st-stpath-ethnic-minority-share-of-construction-ap"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Planning numbers are too small for reliable shares; publish Black and Mixed figures separately wherever numbers allow.",refs:["st-stgov-government-planning-profession-numbers-t", "st-stpicture-about-5-of-town-planners-are-from-racial"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"2.7% of people in engineering and technology jobs are Black, against 5.3% in all other jobs.",refs:["st-stpicture-black-workers-are-under-represented-in-e"]},{t:"About 1% of registered architects are Black, against 4% of the UK population.",refs:["st-stpicture-about-1-of-uk-registered-architects-are-"]},{t:"Black students enter engineering degrees at rates like other subjects. But 17.9% of Black STEM graduates got a first, against 35.7% of white graduates.",refs:["st-stpath-8-1-of-engineering-and-technology-studen", "st-stpath-black-stem-graduates-half-as-likely-as-w"]},{t:"In the Civil Service, 3.1% of science and engineering staff are Black.",refs:["st-stgov-black-staff-are-3-1-of-civil-service-sci"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["sport"]={name:"Sport: taking part, leading and belonging",pfx:"sp",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"spplay",h:"Taking part: activity levels by ethnicity",intro:"How often Black and Mixed people take part in sport and physical activity, as measured by national survey bodies. All figures are survey estimates for the period and nation stated, and most reports use broad ethnic groups.",points:[
  {h:"Black adults' activity has not risen in nine years (England)",text:"In the year to November 2025, 57.2% of Black adults in England did at least 150 minutes of activity a week, below White adults. Sport England reports that activity among Black adults, Asian (excluding Chinese) adults and adults from other ethnic groups has not increased since November 2015-16, so the gap to White British and White other adults has widened.",figure:"57.2%",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"'Active' means 150 or more minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, adults aged 16 and over in England. Both sources are from Sport England, the official statistics producer for this survey.",src:[SRCQ("Sport England: Active Lives Adult Survey November 2024-25 release (23 April 2026)","https://www.sportengland.org/activelivesapril26","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","certain ethnicities, with Black adults (57.2%) and Asian adults (56.3%) less likely to be active than White adults."),SRCQ("Sport England: Active Lives Adult Survey November 2024-25 Report (April 2026)","https://sportengland-production-files.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/2026-04/Active%20Lives%20Adult%20Survey%20November%202024-25%20Report_.pdf?VersionId=eNbVPPXI6E4LS5ozt489Yr_FnGdH84zQ","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Asian (excluding Chinese) adults, Black adults and those from other ethnic groups have not seen an increase in activity levels over the last nine years (compared to November 15-16)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mixed-heritage adults were the most active group in England, 2016 to 2022",text:"Adults from Mixed ethnic backgrounds were the most likely of all ethnic groups in England to be physically active in each year from 2016 to 2022, at 70.8% in the year to November 2022, against 56.1% of Black adults. Sport England's 2016-18 sub-group analysis found 72.4% of White and Black African adults and 65.9% of White and Black Caribbean adults active, compared with 58.5% of African and 58.2% of Caribbean adults.",figure:"70.8%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The sub-group figures combine two survey years (November 2016-18) and are older than the headline series. In the November 2024-25 report, adults from Mixed (69%) and White other (69%) backgrounds are the joint highest groups shown, rounded to whole numbers.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Physical activity (9 January 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/health/diet-and-exercise/physical-activity/latest","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","people from mixed ethnic backgrounds were the most likely out of all ethnic groups to be physically active – this has remained consistent for the last 7 years"),SRCQ("Sport England: Sport for all? Why ethnicity and culture matters in sport and physical activity (February 2020)","https://sportengland-production-files.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/2020-02/Sportforallreport.pdf?VersionId=td0pMbTNOs7caOjvMZ0HCRPwsI3jGnFA","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Our data shows that adults from mixed or multiple ethnic backgrounds are most likely to be active, and least likely to be inactive.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black, Asian and 'other' children are the least likely to be active (England)",text:"In the 2024-25 school year, 41% of Black children and young people in England (school Years 1-11) averaged 60 minutes or more of activity a day, compared with 52% of those from Mixed backgrounds. Sport England reports that children and young people from Asian (43%), Black and other (42%) ethnic backgrounds remain the least likely to be active, that there has been no long-term change for Black children and young people since 2017-18, and that the gender gap between Black boys and girls is 10.6%.",figure:"41%",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"Sport England notes that Black children are 6% of the child population, so changes for this group have wider confidence intervals. The report does not rank the Asian, Black and other groups against each other.",src:[SRCQ("Sport England: Active Lives Children and Young People Survey, academic year 2024-25 (4 December 2025)","https://www.activenorfolk.org/app/uploads/2025/12/Active-Lives-Children-and-Young-People-Survey-academic-year-2024-25-report_.pdf","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","there was no long-term change for Black children and young people, and boys specifically. Children and young people from Asian, Black and other ethnic backgrounds remain the least likely to be active."),SRCQ("Sport England: Active Lives Children and Young People Survey, academic year 2024-25 (4 December 2025)","https://www.activenorfolk.org/app/uploads/2025/12/Active-Lives-Children-and-Young-People-Survey-academic-year-2024-25-report_.pdf","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","The gender gap is widest between boys and girls from Asian (11.2%), Black (10.6%) and other (12.5%) ethnic groups."),SRCQ("Sport England: Ethnicity research page (undated)","https://www.sportengland.org/research-and-data/research/ethnicity","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","girls from Black backgrounds are more likely to be less active from Years 5-6 (ages 9-11) upwards.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black adults and children are under-represented in swimming",text:"Sport England's 2020 analysis found Black adults, 3.1% of England's adult population, under-represented in swimming, cycling and walking for leisure relative to their population share. Black children and young people, 4.6% of 5 to 16-year-olds, were over-represented in football and dance but under-represented in swimming, cycling and walking.",figure:"3.1% / 4.6%",year:"2016-19",disputed:false,note:"This compares each group's share of participants with its share of the population (adults November 2016-18; children and young people 2017-19). It is not a measure of swimming ability. Sport England's 2024-25 children's report gives its 25-metre swimming measure without an ethnicity breakdown. Both figures come from a single Sport England report.",src:[SRCQ("Sport England: Sport for all? Why ethnicity and culture matters in sport and physical activity (February 2020)","https://sportengland-production-files.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/2020-02/Sportforallreport.pdf?VersionId=td0pMbTNOs7caOjvMZ0HCRPwsI3jGnFA","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","3.1% share of the adult population. Under-represented in walking for leisure, cycling for travel, cycling for leisure and swimming relative to their population share."),SRCQ("Sport England: Sport for all? Why ethnicity and culture matters in sport and physical activity (February 2020)","https://sportengland-production-files.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/2020-02/Sportforallreport.pdf?VersionId=td0pMbTNOs7caOjvMZ0HCRPwsI3jGnFA","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","4.6% share of the 5-16 year old population. Over-represented in football and dance, but under-represented in swimming, cycling and walking relative to their population share.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wales and Scotland: school sport by ethnicity, and limited data",text:"In Wales in 2022, 38% of pupils who identified as Black, Black Welsh, Black British, Caribbean or African took part in organised sport outside the curriculum three or more times a week, compared with 43% of pupils from Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups and 41% of White pupils. sportscotland states that there is limited data on sports participation among ethnic minority people in Scotland because the populations are small.",figure:"38%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Sport Wales estimates that about 2,000 Black pupils took part three or more times a week; this is a population estimate, not the number of pupils surveyed. Sport Wales' commentary says White and Mixed pupils were more likely to take part than Asian pupils or pupils from another ethnic group. It does not directly compare Black pupils' participation with White or Mixed pupils'. The sportscotland note is undated and relies on 2011 Census data.",src:[SRCQ("Sport Wales: School Sport Survey 2022 (2022)","https://sport.wales/research/school-sport-survey/2022","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Pupils who identified as white and pupils who identified as being part of a mixed or multiple ethnic group were more likely to participate in organised sport outside of the curriculum"),SRCQ("sportscotland: Race and sport learning note (undated, cites 2011 Census)","https://sportscotland.org.uk/media/ygieqn0z/learning-note-race-and-sport.pdf","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","There is limited data about sports participation among ethnic minority people in Scotland.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"splead",h:"Leading: coaches, officials, volunteers and boards",intro:"Who coaches, officiates, volunteers and governs in sport. Most published leadership data combine Black, Asian, Mixed and other ethnic minority people, so Black-specific figures are rare and each point states the category used.",points:[
  {h:"Sport boards: 15% ethnically diverse, few chairs or CEOs",text:"Board members from culturally or ethnically diverse backgrounds in sport bodies surveyed by Sport England and UK Sport rose from 7.9% in 2020 to 15.11% in 2024, against 18.3% of the England and Wales population at the 2021 Census. Senior roles were less diverse: 4 of 76 chairs and 8 of 97 chief executives who answered were from culturally or ethnically diverse backgrounds, and all 29 performance directors were from a White background.",figure:"15.11%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"'Culturally or ethnically diverse' groups all ethnic minority respondents together; no Black-specific figure is published. Figures come from 669 board responses across around 128 boards.",src:[SRCQ("Sport England and UK Sport: Diversity in Sport Governance, Annual Survey 2024 (November 2024)","https://www.uksport.gov.uk/-/media/files/our-work/equality-diversity-inclusion/diversity-in-sport-governance---sport-england-and-uk-sport---november-2024.ashx","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Performance Director – Out of the 29 that responded to this question, all 29 were from a White background."),SRCQ("UK Sport: Diversity in Sport Governance Report 2024 (19 December 2024)","https://www.uksport.gov.uk/news/2024/12/19/diversity-in-sport-governance-report-2024","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","There is notable progress reflected in the findings, particularly regarding the increased representation of disabled people and those from culturally or ethnically diverse backgrounds serving on Boards")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Football clubs: 13% of coaches Black, Asian or Mixed Heritage",text:"Across the clubs signed up to the Football Leadership Diversity Code in 2022-23, 13% of coaches, 11% of senior coaches and 7% of senior leaders were Black, Asian or Mixed Heritage. The FA said this falls very short of its aim of representing what is seen on the pitch.",figure:"13%",year:"2022-23",disputed:false,note:"Figures cover the Code's 53 club signatories and combine Black, Asian and Mixed Heritage staff. In November 2023 the FA said a new rule making workforce diversity reporting mandatory for professional clubs had been approved in principle and, after consultation, would be introduced ahead of the 2024-25 season.",src:[SRCQ("The FA: Football Leadership Diversity Code results 2022/23 (November 2023)","https://www.thefa.com/-/media/thefacom-new/files/rules-and-regulations/football-leadership-diversity-code/the-fa-fldc-support-report-2022-23.ashx","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Meanwhile, 13% of coaches and 11% of senior coaches are Black, Asian or Mixed Heritage, which falls very short of our aim of representing what we see on the pitch."),SRCQ("The FA: FLDC year three report published (22 November 2023)","https://www.thefa.com/news/2023/nov/22/fldc-year-3-report","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Across the coaching workforce, 13% of coaches and 11% of senior coaches are Black, Asian or Mixed Heritage."),SRCQ("Premier League: Football Leadership Diversity Code - 2022/23 update (22 November 2023)","https://www.premierleague.com/news/3791315","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","In team operations, 23 per cent of new hires were Black, Asian or of Mixed Heritage, exceeding the 15 per cent target")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"England coaching staff: 23% in the men's game, 0% in the women's game",text:"The FA reported that 23% of its England coaching staff in the men's game were from Black, Asian, Mixed or other ethnic backgrounds in October 2025, up from 19% in June 2024, against a 2028 target of 25%. For England coaching staff in the women's game the figure was 0%, down from 4%, against a 2028 target of 7%. Separately, the number of registered referees from Black, Asian and Mixed Heritage backgrounds rose by 17.5% in the 2024-25 season.",figure:"23%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Both sources are published by the FA and use a combined Black, Asian, Mixed or other ethnic background category. The referee figure is a percentage increase, not a share of all referees.",src:[SRCQ("The FA: EDI End of Year Report 2025 (December 2025)","https://www.thefa.com/-/media/thefacom-new/files/about-the-fa/2025/fa-equality-diversity-and-inclusion-progress-report-2025.ashx","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","While we are pleased with our progress in some areas, like the ethnic diversity of our men’s team’s coaching staff"),SRCQ("The FA: A review of our 2024-2028 EDI strategy, 'A Game Free From Discrimination' (2 December 2025)","https://www.thefa.com/news/2025/dec/02/review-of-2024-2028-edi-strategy","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","In the 2024-25 season, the number of registered referees from Black, Asian and Mixed Heritage backgrounds has increased by 17.5%.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black former players: fewer reach management, more are dismissed",text:"A working paper tracking 3,420 players active in England's top two divisions between 1990 and 2010 identified 544 (16%) as Black or of mixed Black heritage; around 4% of them became first-team managers, compared with 11% of non-Black former players. The researchers found lower recruitment into junior managerial roles, slower progression and higher dismissal rates for Black former players.",figure:"4% vs 11%",year:"2024",disputed:true,note:"This is a working paper by Peeters, Hoey and Szymanski, described by the University of Liverpool as commissioned by the Black Footballers Partnership. Erasmus gives the sample as 3,420 players; Liverpool says about 3,500. The two summaries describe the 4% differently. Erasmus gives it as the share of Black former players who became first-team managers. Liverpool gives it as Black former footballers' share of all Premier League and EFL managerial roles. Liverpool also reports that a Black manager is 41% more likely to be fired than a white manager when performance is equal.",src:[SRCQ("Erasmus University Rotterdam: Black former players get fewer chances at management (27 March 2024)","https://www.eur.nl/en/news/black-former-players-get-fewer-chances-management","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Around 4% of Black former players eventually obtain a role as first team manager, whereas 11% of the non-Black former players progress to this level."),SRCQ("University of Liverpool Management School: Why are there so few black football managers? (16 September 2024)","https://liverpool.ac.uk/management/blog/research/why-are-there-so-few-black-football-managers","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","A black manager is 41% more likely to be fired than a white manager when factors such as performance are equal.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Volunteering: Black adults had the highest rate shown (England)",text:"In the 12 months to November 2025, 24.0% of Black adults in England volunteered to support sport and physical activity, the highest of the ethnic groups shown, compared with 25.2% in November 2016-17. Volunteering among adults of Mixed ethnicity fell by 5.2% over the same period to 22.8%, and among children it fell by 9.2% for Black girls and 9.5% for Mixed girls since 2017-18.",figure:"24.0%",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"Volunteering covers roles such as coaching, officiating and organising. The Black adult figures appear in the report's chart without commentary, and the change for Black adults is not marked as statistically reportable.",src:[SRCQ("Sport England: Active Lives Adult Survey November 2024-25 Report (April 2026)","https://sportengland-production-files.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/2026-04/Active%20Lives%20Adult%20Survey%20November%202024-25%20Report_.pdf?VersionId=eNbVPPXI6E4LS5ozt489Yr_FnGdH84zQ","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","It is, however, White British adults and those with mixed ethnicity who are driving the long-term decreases, both down 5.2% compared to November 16-17."),SRCQ("Sport England: Active Lives Children and Young People Survey, academic year 2024-25 (4 December 2025)","https://www.activenorfolk.org/app/uploads/2025/12/Active-Lives-Children-and-Young-People-Survey-academic-year-2024-25-report_.pdf","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","long-term drops remain among most groups and are particularly notable among girls from Black and Mixed backgrounds, with drops of 9.2% and 9.5% respectively, compared to academic year 2017-18.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"spracism",h:"Racism and discrimination: what is recorded",intro:"What official and established bodies record about racism in sport. Each count measures something different (arrests, incidents at matches, reports to a charity, disciplinary allegations or survey experience), and none captures the full extent of abuse.",points:[
  {h:"Arrests and other police actions for racist and indecent chanting rose to 33 (2024-25)",text:"Police made 33 football-related arrests and other police actions for 'racist and indecent chanting' in the 2024-25 season, up from 9 in 2023-24, out of 2,037 in all, according to Home Office figures for England and Wales. The Home Office records racist and indecent chanting as a single offence category, so racist chanting cannot be counted on its own.",figure:"33",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"The July 2025 release gave 32 for the domestic season; the November 2025 update gives 33 for the full 2024-25 season. Since 2019-20 these figures have included other police actions such as Fixed Penalty Notices, which the Home Office now shows separately (293 of the 2,037 in 2024-25). The figures measure police action, not the number of incidents.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Football-related arrests and banning orders 2024 to 2025 season, data tables (13 November 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69119a9243f8a16323729936/football-related-arrests-banning-orders-2025-tables.ods","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Table 6: Football-related arrests and other police actions by offence type, England and Wales, from 2010/11 season to 2024/25 season"),SRCQ("Home Office: Football-related arrests and banning orders, England and Wales: 2024 to 2025 domestic football season (17 July 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/football-related-arrests-banning-orders-202425-domestic-season/football-related-arrests-and-banning-orders-england-and-wales-2024-to-2025-domestic-football-season","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Other includes throwing missiles, racist and indecent chanting, unauthorised ticket resale (commonly known as ‘ticket touting’), possession of an offensive weapon")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Race is the most common hate crime incident at matches",text:"Hate crime incidents were reported at 420 regulated football matches in the 2024-25 domestic season, and 287 of the incidents related to race, more than any other category. A further 212 online hate crimes connected to football were recorded in that season, a measure the Home Office classes as official statistics in development.",figure:"287",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"Incident figures come from post-match police reports for regulated matches. The online figure counts offences reported to bodies such as the FA, UEFA, Kick It Out or the police that meet threshold tests. It is designated official statistics in development and is not broken down by type in the summary.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Football-related arrests and banning orders, England and Wales: 2024 to 2025 domestic football season (17 July 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/football-related-arrests-banning-orders-202425-domestic-season/football-related-arrests-and-banning-orders-england-and-wales-2024-to-2025-domestic-football-season","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Hate crime incidents were reported in relation to 420 matches; 287 incidents related to race, 140 to sexual orientation, 20 to religion, 19 to disability and 3 to gender identity."),SRCQ("Home Office: Football-related arrests and banning orders, 2024 to 2025 football season international update (13 November 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/football-related-arrests-banning-orders-202425-football-season-update/football-related-arrests-and-banning-orders-england-and-wales-2024-to-2025-football-season-international-update","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","During the 2024 to 2025 domestic football season there were 212 online hate crimes recorded in connection with football")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Kick It Out: 792 reports of racism in 2025-26",text:"Kick It Out received 792 reports of racism across professional, grassroots and online football in the 2025-26 season, up 19% on the previous year and 45% of all reports it received. These are reports made to the charity rather than proven incidents; it says its figures track rises recorded by the UK Football Policing Unit and the FA.",figure:"792",year:"2025-26",disputed:false,note:"Kick It Out is an anti-discrimination charity, and these are reports made to it, not proven incidents. Its 2024-25 summary gives a racism count for online reports only (268), so no like-for-like total for 2024-25 is shown here.",src:[SRCQ("Kick It Out: Reporting statistics, 2025/26 season (2026)","https://www.kickitout.org/reporting-statistics","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Racism remains the most reported form of discrimination, with 792 reports, up 19% year-on-year and making up 45% of the total."),SRCQ("Kick It Out: Reporting statistics, 2024/25 season (2025)","https://www.kickitout.org/reporting-statistics","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Racism continues to dominate in this space, accounting for 268 reports")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Grassroots football: 1,147 race-related allegations (2025-26)",text:"The FA recorded 1,147 serious case allegations relating to ethnicity, race or colour in grassroots football in England in the 2025-26 season (0.92 per 1,000 fixtures), up from 1,021 in 2024-25. Of 235 charges, 157 were proven and 57 not proven when the report was published, and race-related cases remain the largest category of discrimination.",figure:"1,147",year:"2025-26",disputed:false,note:"Grassroots means Steps 5 and below of the men's pyramid and Tiers 5 and below of the women's pyramid, including youth football. Allegations are not findings, and some charges were unresolved at publication. The FA cites independent research estimating that, for every 100 serious incidents brought to its attention, a further 89 go unreported. This 'reporting gap' of 1.89 covers all serious incidents, not only race-related ones.",src:[SRCQ("The FA: Grassroots Football Behaviour and Discipline Review 2025-26 season (October 2026)","https://www.thefa.com/-/media/thefacom-new/files/rules-and-regulations/2026-27/300926/2025-26-grassroots-behaviour-and-discipline-report.ashx","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Incidents involving references to race, ethnicity or colour continue to account for the largest proportion of discrimination cases, consistent with recent seasons."),SRCQ("The FA: Annual Grassroots Discipline Review for the 2024-25 season published (9 December 2025)","https://www.thefa.com/news/2025/dec/09/annual-discipline-review","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Discrimination offences referencing ethnicity, race, or colour remain the most prevalent category, as they have for the past seven seasons.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cricket: 75% of Black respondents reported discrimination",text:"The Independent Commission for Equity in Cricket, reporting in June 2023, found that 50% of more than 4,000 survey respondents had experienced discrimination in cricket in the past five years, rising to 75% of all Black respondents. The Commission concluded that 'cricket has failed Black communities'. The England and Wales Cricket Board apologised unreservedly and said the report's conclusions highlight that 'for too long women and Black people were neglected'.",figure:"75%",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The survey drew responses from people involved in cricket who chose to take part, and 79% classified themselves as White British, so it is not a random sample. The number of Black respondents is not given in these sources. Figures come from the ECB, Wisden and CNN's reporting of the Commission's report.",src:[SRCQ("CNN (syndicated by KVIA): Racism, sexism and class-based discrimination widespread in English cricket, independent report finds (27 June 2023)","https://kvia.com/cnn-sports/2023/06/27/racism-sexism-and-class-based-discrimination-widespread-in-english-cricket-independent-report-finds/","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","87% of people with Pakistani and Bangladeshi heritage, 82% of people with Indian heritage and 75% of all Black respondents."),SRCQ("England and Wales Cricket Board newsroom: ECB responds to ICEC report on equity in cricket (27 June 2023)","https://www.mynewsdesk.com/uk/england-and-wales-cricket-board/pressreleases/ecb-responds-to-icec-report-on-equity-in-cricket-which-finds-evidence-of-discrimination-across-the-game-3261498","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Powerful conclusions within the report also highlight that for too long women and Black people were neglected."),SRCQ("Wisden: The ICEC report – a game for all, Wisden Cricketers' Almanack 2024 (2024)","https://www.wisden.com/wisden-cricketers-almanack/the-icec-report-a-game-for-all-wisden-almanack-2024","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","An astonishing 50 per cent – people who play and organise cricket in England and Wales – said they had experienced discrimination of some kind.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"sphistory",h:"History: pioneers on the field",intro:"Documented Black and Mixed-heritage pioneers in British sport, each described in the words of the museum, archive, governing body or public heritage body cited.",points:[
  {h:"Emma Clarke: the first recorded black woman footballer (1895)",text:"Emma Clarke, born in Bootle in 1876, played for the South team in the British Ladies' Football Club match at Crouch End, north London, on 23 March 1895, watched by 10,000 people. Haringey Council describes her as considered to be the first known Black women's footballer in Britain, and a Nubian Jak blue plaque honouring her, her sister Jane and the 1895 team was unveiled in Hornsey in December 2019.",figure:"",year:"1895",disputed:false,note:"Haringey Council gives her dates as 1876 to 'after 1903'; her date of death is not recorded in these sources. Haringey places the match in Crouch End near Nightingale Lane; the Hornsey Historical Society says the exact field has not been confirmed.",src:[SRCQ("Haringey Council: Black History Haringey 365 – Plaques and monuments (undated)","https://haringey.gov.uk/leisure-parks-culture/culture/black-history-haringey-365/timeline/plaques-monuments","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Emma Clarke was a pioneering British footballer, considered to be the first known black women's footballer in Britain."),SRCQ("Hornsey Historical Society: 1895 – Women's Football Makes its Official Debut in Hornsey (undated)","https://hornseyhistorical.org.uk/1895-womens-football-makes-its-official-debut-in-hornsey/","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","One of the South team, Emma Clarke, was the first recorded black woman football player.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Rugby pioneers: James Peters (1906) and Clive Sullivan (1972)",text:"In 1906 James 'Jimmy' Peters became, in the Rugby Football Union's words, the first black player in England's international rugby union history, winning five caps to 1908; the RFU notes it would be 80 years before another black player represented England. In 1972 Cardiff-born Clive Sullivan captained Great Britain to the Rugby League World Cup, which the Dictionary of Welsh Biography records as making him the first Black person to captain a Great Britain team in any sport.",figure:"",year:"1906; 1972",disputed:false,note:"Peters died in 1954 and Sullivan in 1985. The RFU's '80 years' and the RFL's 'until 1988' differ slightly. The RFL also records Roy Francis as the first black player to play for Great Britain, in 1947.",src:[SRCQ("Rugby Football Union: Jimmy Peters – England Rugby's Black Pioneer (1 October 2021)","https://englandrugby.com/follow/news-and-media/jimmy-peters-england-rugbys-black-pioneer","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","In 1906, Jimmy Peters became the first black player in England’s international rugby union history."),SRCQ("Dictionary of Welsh Biography: Sullivan, Clive (1943-1985), rugby league player (undated)","https://biography.wales/article/s12-SULL-CLI-1943","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","In 1972 he was selected as Captain of the Great Britain side, becoming the first Black person to captain a Great Britain team in any sport."),SRCQ("Rugby Football League: The Black Pioneers of Rugby League, by Tony Collins (27 August 2020)","https://www.rugby-league.com/article/23664/the-black-pioneers-of-rugby-league","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","In 1913 Barrow signed James Peters, a black dockworker from Plymouth, whose five England caps between 1906 and 1908 made him the sole black player to play rugby union for England until 1988.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Harry Edward: Britain's first Black Olympic medallist (1920)",text:"At the 1920 Antwerp Olympic Games Harry Edward won bronze medals in the 100m and 200m, and Team GB describes his as the tale of the first black athlete to compete and win medals in its history. At the 1922 AAA Championships he won the 100, 220 and 440-yard finals within one hour; he later emigrated to the United States and died in 1973.",figure:"2 bronze medals",year:"1920",disputed:true,note:"Sources differ on his birth year: Team GB gives Berlin, 1895; DCMS gives Berlin, 1898.",src:[SRCQ("Team GB: Black History Month – The story of Harry Edward (1 October 2020)","https://www.teamgb.com/article/black-history-month-the-story-of-harry-edward/5hpMtVOaZq5BA01zVGTCvL","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Edward’s is the tale of the first black athlete to compete and win medals in the 124-year history of Team GB."),SRCQ("Department for Culture, Media and Sport: Harry Edward – Britain's first Black Olympian (undated)","https://dcms.shorthandstories.com/harry-edward-britain-s-first-black-olympian/index.html","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","He took 100m bronze at the 1920 Antwerp Games - a historical moment in British Sport.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Jack Leslie: called up for England in 1925, then dropped",text:"Plymouth Argyle striker Jack Leslie (1901-1988) was called up as a reserve for England in October 1925, but the honour was rescinded shortly afterwards, which the FA says is thought to have been on the grounds of racial prejudice. His family received a posthumous England cap in 2022, and Historic England unveiled a national blue plaque at his former Plymouth home in May 2025.",figure:"",year:"1925",disputed:false,note:"The plaque, which gives his dates as 1901 to 1988, reads that he was 'the first Black footballer selected for England'. Viv Anderson was the first Black footballer to play for England at senior level, in 1978. The FA also describes Leslie as the first Black footballer to captain a Football League team, and gives 137 goals in 401 appearances for Plymouth Argyle.",src:[SRCQ("The FA: Jack Leslie honoured with blue plaque (21 May 2025)","https://www.thefa.com/news/2025/may/21/jack-leslie-blue-plaque-unveiled-20252105","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Though this honour was rescinded shortly afterwards, it is thought on the grounds of racial prejudice"),SRCQ("Plymouth Argyle: Blue Plaque unveiled to honour Argyle legend Jack Leslie (19 May 2025)","https://www.pafc.co.uk/news/blue-plaque-unveiled-honour-argyle-legend-jack-leslie","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Historic England recently unveiled a national blue plaque in Plymouth to honour the life and groundbreaking career of John 'Jack' Leslie")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Learie Constantine: cricketer and Britain's first black peer",text:"Trinidad-born cricketer Learie Constantine played as a professional for Nelson in the Lancashire League, which won the league eight times in his ten seasons. During the Second World War he won damages after a London hotel refused him and his family accommodation. English Heritage describes him as Britain's first black peer; his 1969 peerage is covered on the hub's recognition page.",figure:"",year:"1969",disputed:true,note:"Dates differ between sources: English Heritage gives 1929 for the Nelson contract and 1943 for the hotel refusal; the Inner Temple gives 1928 and 1944. He died in London on 1 July 1971.",src:[SRCQ("English Heritage: Blue plaques – Learie Constantine (undated)","https://english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/learie-constantine","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","Britain’s first black peer, Sir Learie Constantine, was a cricketer, statesman and advocate of racial equality."),SRCQ("The Inner Temple: Learie Constantine – Professional Cricketer, Activist, Britain's first Black Peer (undated)","https://www.innertemple.org.uk/?p=55384","Supports the Sport: taking part, leading and belonging page","The case Constantine v Imperial London Hotels ruled in favour of Constantine and was considered a milestone in British racial equality.")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black adults had the highest rate of volunteering in sport of the groups shown, at 24.0%.",refs:["sp-splead-volunteering-black-adults-had-the-highes"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Race is the most common kind of hate crime incident at football matches; report what you see.",refs:["sp-spracism-race-is-the-most-common-hate-crime-incid"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"About 4% of Black former players became first-team managers, against 11% of other former players.",refs:["sp-splead-black-former-players-fewer-reach-managem"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Black adults and children are under-represented in swimming; this measures who swims, not ability.",refs:["sp-spplay-black-adults-and-children-are-under-repr"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Most leadership data combine Black, Asian, Mixed and other groups, and Scotland has limited data; ask for the split.",refs:["sp-splead-football-clubs-13-of-coaches-black-asian", "sp-spplay-wales-and-scotland-school-sport-by-ethni"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"57.2% of Black adults in England are active, and the figure has not risen in nine years.",refs:["sp-spplay-black-adults-activity-has-not-risen-in-n"]},{t:"Board members from ethnically diverse backgrounds rose to 15.11% in 2024; few chairs or chief executives are.",refs:["sp-splead-sport-boards-15-ethnically-diverse-few-c"]},{t:"Kick It Out received 792 reports of racism in 2025-26, and the FA recorded 1,147 race-related allegations in grassroots football.",refs:["sp-spracism-kick-it-out-792-reports-of-racism-in-202", "sp-spracism-grassroots-football-1-147-race-related-a"]},{t:"In cricket, 75% of Black respondents to the equity commission's survey reported discrimination.",refs:["sp-spracism-cricket-75-of-black-respondents-reported"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["services"]={name:"Uniformed and frontline public services",pfx:"sv",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"svfire",h:"Fire and rescue services",intro:"Ethnic minority firefighters are a smaller share of the workforce than in policing, and inspectors have recorded racism inside some services. Figures are for England unless stated; Scotland and Wales do not publish a comparable national breakdown.",points:[
  {h:"Black firefighters: 1.4% of firefighters in England, 2024",text:"In March 2024, 1.4% of firefighters in England whose ethnicity was known were Black and 2.6% were from Mixed ethnic backgrounds, against 4.4% and 2.5% of the working-age population at the 2021 Census. Across all fire and rescue staff the Black share was 1.7%. By 31 March 2025, 5.6% of firefighters were from an ethnic minority overall, up from 3.5% in 2011.",figure:"1.4%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Shares exclude staff whose ethnicity is not stated (8.8% of all staff in 2024; 8.5% of firefighters in 2025). On-call firefighters with more than one contract are counted more than once.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Fire and rescue services workforce (4 June 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/fire-and-rescue-services-workforce/latest","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","from 2011 to 2024, the percentage of firefighters who were from Mixed ethnic backgrounds went up from 1.2% to 2.6%"),SRCQ("Home Office: Fire and rescue workforce and pensions statistics: England, year ending March 2025 (4 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/fire-workforce-and-pension-statistics-year-ending-march-2025/fire-and-rescue-workforce-and-pensions-statistics-england-year-ending-march-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","5.6% (1,793) of firefighters were from an ethnic minority [footnote 5] , compared to 5.4% (1,701) in the previous year, 4.4% (1,410) 5 years ago and 3.9% (1,321) 10 years ago")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wholetime crews more diverse than on-call crews, 2025",text:"On 31 March 2025, 7.4% of wholetime firefighters in England were from an ethnic minority, compared with 2.2% of on-call firefighters, 4.1% of fire control staff and 9.3% of support staff. The published headcount table records 423 wholetime and 36 on-call firefighters as Black or Black British. Ethnic minorities made up 7.9% of firefighters recruited in the year.",figure:"7.4% wholetime; 2.2% on-call",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Counts are headcounts of staff with a stated ethnicity, taken from table FIRE1104.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Fire and rescue workforce and pensions statistics: England, year ending March 2025 (4 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/fire-workforce-and-pension-statistics-year-ending-march-2025/fire-and-rescue-workforce-and-pensions-statistics-england-year-ending-march-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","in 2025 the percentages of staff who are from an ethnic minority (7.4% for wholetime firefighters, 2.2% for on-call firefighters, 4.1% for fire control and 9.3% for support staff)"),SRCQ("Home Office: FIRE1104 Staff headcount by ethnicity, fire and rescue authority and role (4 December 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/692980212a37784b16ecf6db/FIRE1104.xlsx","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Staff headcount by ethnicity, fire authority and role. Displayed by year.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Senior fire ranks remain less diverse than the frontline, 2025",text:"Firefighter, the most junior and most common rank, was the only rank in England where the ethnic minority share (6.7%) was above the all-firefighter figure of 5.6% in March 2025. Every rank above it, from crew manager to brigade manager, was lower. The rank table records no staff at brigade manager level as Black or Black British, and three at area manager level.",figure:"0 Black brigade managers",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Rank headcounts are from Home Office table FIRE1104 (FIRE1104c) at 31 March 2025. Senior ranks are small groups (129 staff at brigade manager rank, of whom 11 did not state an ethnicity; 174 at area manager rank), so shares move sharply from year to year.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Fire and rescue workforce and pensions statistics: England, year ending March 2025 (4 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/fire-workforce-and-pension-statistics-year-ending-march-2025/fire-and-rescue-workforce-and-pensions-statistics-england-year-ending-march-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Firefighter (the lowest and most common rank of staff) was the only rank to show a percentage of firefighters that are from an ethnic minority (6.7%) higher than the all firefighter figure of 5.6%."),SRCQ("Home Office: FIRE1104 Staff headcount by ethnicity, fire and rescue authority and role (4 December 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/692980212a37784b16ecf6db/FIRE1104.xlsx","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Staff headcount by ethnicity, rank and role. Displayed by rank.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Inspectorate found racism in at least 11 services, 2023",text:"His Majesty's Inspectorate of Constabulary and Fire & Rescue Services reported in March 2023 that it found evidence of racism, sexism and homophobia in at least 11 fire and rescue services in England. In its staff survey, 62% of ethnic minority respondents who had experienced bullying or harassment had not reported it, compared with 41% of White respondents. The government's ethnicity statistics service cites the report alongside its fire workforce data.",figure:"11 services",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("HMICFRS: Values and culture in fire and rescue services (30 March 2023)","https://hmicfrs.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/publication-html/values-and-culture-in-fire-and-rescue-services/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","In at least 11 services, we found evidence of racism, sexism and homophobia"),SRCQ("HMICFRS: Values and culture in fire and rescue services (30 March 2023)","https://hmicfrs.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/publication-html/values-and-culture-in-fire-and-rescue-services/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","62 percent of respondents from ethnic minority backgrounds didn't report it, compared to 41 percent of White respondents."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Fire and rescue services workforce (4 June 2025)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/fire-and-rescue-services-workforce/latest","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","The report found evidence of racism, sexism and homophobia and that the culture discouraged staff from addressing inappropriate behaviour.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"London Fire Brigade: 'institutionally misogynist and racist', 2022",text:"An independent culture review of London Fire Brigade, chaired by Nazir Afzal and published in November 2022, described the brigade as institutionally misogynist and racist. The brigade itself states that the review found Black, Asian and minority ethnic staff experience poor treatment and do less well in their careers. The review recorded a Black firefighter who had a noose put above his locker.",figure:"",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("London Fire Brigade: Independent Culture Review of London Fire Brigade (published November 2022)","https://www.london-fire.gov.uk/about-us/improving-our-workplace-culture/independent-culture-review/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","It highlights that women, Black, Asian and minority ethnic, LGBTQ+ and neurodiverse staff experience poor treatment and do less well in their careers with us."),SRCQ("Fire Protection Association: Damning workplace culture report reveals severe shortcomings at London Fire Brigade (November 2022)","https://www.thefpa.co.uk/news/damning-workplace-culture-report-reveals-severe-shortcomings-at-london-fire-brigade","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","In one case of horrendous racial abuse, we spoke to a black firefighter who had a noose put above his locker.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"svjustice",h:"Prisons, probation and youth justice",intro:"HM Prison and Probation Service covers England and Wales. Its own data and the inspectors record differences between ethnic groups in grades, discipline and experience at work, especially in management grades.",points:[
  {h:"7.9% of prison and probation staff are Black, 2025",text:"At 31 March 2025, 7.9% of HM Prison and Probation Service staff who reported their ethnicity identified as Black or Black British, up 1.7 percentage points in a year; 16.1% were from an ethnic minority overall. Ethnic minority representation was lowest among operational managers (bands 7 to 9), at 6.1%. By 30 June 2026 the ethnic minority share had risen to 18.6%.",figure:"7.9%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"86.9% of staff had reported their ethnicity. Covers England and Wales.",src:[SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HM Prison and Probation Service annual staff equalities report 2024 to 2025 (27 November 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-staff-equalities-report-2024-2025/hm-prison-and-probation-service-annual-staff-equalities-report-2024-to-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Within ethnic minority groups, 7.9% of staff identified as Black or Black British (an increase of 1.7 percentage points since 31 March 2024)"),SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HMPPS recruitment diversity statistics, June 2026 (20 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-workforce-quarterly-june-2026/recruitment-diversity-statistics-june-2026","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","As at 30 June 2026, 18.6% of HMPPS staff who self-reported their ethnicity were from an ethnic minority background.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black prison officers: from 2.6% to 4.4%, 2017 to 2023",text:"In March 2023, 4.4% of prison officers in public sector prisons in England and Wales whose ethnicity was known were Black, matching the 4.4% Black share of the working-age population, up from 2.6% in 2017. In 2024 to 2025, band 3 to 5 prison officers were the grade with the highest share of ethnic minority recruits, at 53.4%, and 25.2% of all HMPPS joiners identified as Black.",figure:"4.4%",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"Ethnicity was unknown for 16.6% of prison officers in 2023. Covers bands 3 to 5 in public sector prisons in England and Wales; excludes private prisons. The population comparison is the one published by the source; its working-age total is the same figure used on the England-only fire page, so treat the match as approximate.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Prison officer workforce (24 September 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/prison-officer-workforce/latest","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","between 2017 and 2023: the percentage of prison officers who were black went up from 2.6% to 4.4%"),SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HM Prison and Probation Service annual staff equalities report 2024 to 2025 (27 November 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-staff-equalities-report-2024-2025/hm-prison-and-probation-service-annual-staff-equalities-report-2024-to-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Within the ethnic minority groups, 25.2% of joiners identified as Black and 7.9% as Asian.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black applicants: 40.3% of applications, 18.4% of accepted offers",text:"Between July 2024 and June 2026, 40.3% of all applications to HMPPS recruitment campaigns came from Black applicants, but Black candidates made up 18.4% of formal offers accepted. For band 3 prison officer posts, the relative rate index for ethnic minority applicants was 0.72, meaning a higher proportion of White applicants progressed through the process.",figure:"40.3% vs 18.4%",year:"2024-2026",disputed:false,note:"Published as official statistics in development.",src:[SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HMPPS recruitment diversity statistics, June 2026 (20 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-workforce-quarterly-june-2026/recruitment-diversity-statistics-june-2026","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","there were 396,139 from ethnic minority applicants making up 56.2% (11.9% Asian, 40.3% Black, 2.8% mixed, 1.1% other)"),SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HMPPS recruitment diversity statistics, June 2026 (20 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-workforce-quarterly-june-2026/recruitment-diversity-statistics-june-2026","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","There were 6,670 applications from ethnic minority applicants that accepted a formal offer representing 28.9% of the total (6.8% Asian, 18.4% Black, 3.0% mixed, 0.7% other)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Youth custody staff: 30.5% from ethnic minorities, 2026",text:"At 30 June 2026, 30.5% of Youth Custody Service staff who reported their ethnicity were from an ethnic minority, the highest share in HMPPS, against 18.2% in public sector prisons and 19.8% in the Probation Service. The Youth Custody Service share has risen from 17.2% in March 2021.",figure:"30.5%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Youth Custody Service figures are based on a small group (376 ethnic minority staff at 30 June 2026), so the share can move sharply. Covers England and Wales.",src:[SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HMPPS recruitment diversity statistics, June 2026 (20 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-workforce-quarterly-june-2026/recruitment-diversity-statistics-june-2026","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","18.2% of all Public Sector Prisons staff, 30.5% of all Youth Custody Service staff, 14.2% of HQ and Frontline Support staff, and 19.8% of Probation Service staff were from an ethnic minority background"),SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HMPPS workforce statistics tables, June 2026, Table 5 (20 August 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a7f07803bd75b81e2329a1a/hmpps-workforce-statistics-tables-jun-2026_final.ods","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Table 5b: Youth Custody Estate staff in post, by protected characteristic")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Higher discipline rates, lower bonus rates for ethnic minority staff",text:"In 2024 to 2025, ethnic minority HMPPS staff faced conduct and discipline cases at 2.6 per 100 staff, compared with 1.6 per 100 for White staff. Black or Black British staff had the lowest recognition bonus award rate of any ethnic group, at 29.8%. Black staff were also the least likely to receive temporary cover allowances (3.3%).",figure:"2.6 vs 1.6 per 100",year:"2024-25",disputed:false,note:"The report advises caution over small subgroup sizes, and says rising case numbers may partly reflect staff having more confidence to speak out. Differences in rates do not by themselves show why they arise.",src:[SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HM Prison and Probation Service annual staff equalities report 2024 to 2025 (27 November 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-staff-equalities-report-2024-2025/hm-prison-and-probation-service-annual-staff-equalities-report-2024-to-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","staff from ethnic minority backgrounds experienced a higher rate (2.6 per 100) than white staff (1.6 per 100)."),SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: HM Prison and Probation Service annual staff equalities report 2024 to 2025 (27 November 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hm-prison-probation-service-staff-equalities-report-2024-2025/hm-prison-and-probation-service-annual-staff-equalities-report-2024-to-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","staff who self-reported themselves as Black or Black British had the lowest award rate (29.8%) but a slightly higher average value (£176).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Probation inspectors: 45% of minority ethnic staff do not feel safe raising race concerns, 2023",text:"HM Inspectorate of Probation's 2023 follow-up to its 2021 race equality inspection found that 45% of minority ethnic probation staff surveyed did not feel safe raising issues of racial discrimination, and 41% did not believe staff recruitment offered equal opportunity. The Chief Inspector said there was still some way to go to reassure Black, Asian and minority ethnic staff that they would be treated equally.",figure:"45%",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"The survey covered 97 minority ethnic probation staff.",src:[SRCQ("HM Inspectorate of Probation: Race equality in probation follow-up: a work in progress, summary (September 2023)","https://cdn.websitebuilder.service.justice.gov.uk/uploads/sites/32/2023/09/Race-Equality-in-Probation-A-work-in-progress.pdf","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","45 per cent do not feel safe raising issues of racial discrimination and only two out of 17 who raised grievances felt they were dealt with to their satisfaction."),SRCQ("HM Inspectorate of Probation: Race equality in probation remains a work in progress (7 September 2023)","https://hmiprobation.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/news/race-equality-in-probation-remains-a-work-in-progress","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","there is still some way to go to reassure Black, Asian and minority ethnic staff that they will be treated equally.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"svother",h:"Other frontline services",intro:"Beyond fire and prisons, published data cover ambulance staff, police staff and volunteers, the Home Office's border and migration workforce and the armed forces reserves. Police officers and the regular forces are covered on Representation across the state. Most series report ethnic minorities together rather than Black and Mixed groups separately.",points:[
  {h:"Ambulance staff: 4.2% from ethnic minorities, 2022",text:"In June 2022, staff from ethnic minority groups made up 4.2% of NHS ambulance staff in England, far below the share among nurses or hospital doctors. In the 2024 NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard report, operational ambulance staff reported the highest level of harassment, bullying or abuse from patients and the public (50.3%) and were the least likely profession to believe their trust acts fairly on career progression (48.1%; 43.1% among ‘BME’ men).",figure:"4.2%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"The 4.2% excludes White minority groups and comes from NHS staff records for England in June 2022, the latest year published on this measure. The survey figures come from a different source and year (the NHS Staff Survey, reported in the 2024 WRES report).",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: NHS workforce (13 April 2023)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/nhs-workforce/latest","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","staff from ethnic minority groups made up 4.2% of ambulance staff"),SRCQ("NHS England: NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard (WRES) 2024 data analysis report for NHS trusts (2025)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Workforce-Race-Equality-Standard-2024-data-analysis-report.docx","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Overall, ambulance (operational) staff (50.3%) experienced the highest level of harassment, bullying or abuse from patients, relatives or the public.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black NHS staff least likely to see fair progression",text:"In the 2024 NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard report, just 42.3% of staff from a Black background believed their trust provides equal opportunities for career progression or promotion, a measure that has tracked below other ethnic groups since at least 2015. Women from Black backgrounds reported high levels of discrimination from managers or colleagues (17.9%).",figure:"42.3%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Covers all NHS trusts in England, including ambulance trusts. Survey measures come from the 2023/24 NHS Staff Survey, as reported in the 2024 WRES report.",src:[SRCQ("NHS England: NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard (WRES) 2024 data analysis report for NHS trusts (2025)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Workforce-Race-Equality-Standard-2024-data-analysis-report.docx","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","With disaggregation, just 42.3% of staff from a black background believed their trust provides equal opportunities for career progression or promotion, with levels below those of other ethnic groups since at least 2015."),SRCQ("NHS England: NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard (WRES) 2024 data analysis report for NHS trusts, web version (PRN01915)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/nhs-workforce-race-equality-standard-wres-2024-data-analysis-report-for-nhs-trusts/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Only 42.3% of staff from a black background believed their trust provides equal opportunities")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"PCSOs and special constables more diverse than officers, 2026",text:"At 31 March 2026, 14.3% of police community support officers in England and Wales who stated their ethnicity were from an ethnic minority, against 18.3% of the resident population, making them the most ethnically diverse part of the police workforce. Special constables followed at 13.1%. A year earlier the figures were 14.0% and 12.9%.",figure:"14.3%; 13.1%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Shares exclude White minorities and staff who did not state an ethnicity, and are based on full-time equivalents. Covers the 43 territorial forces in England and Wales, excluding British Transport Police. Black-only shares for these groups are published in the open data tables, not the bulletin.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Police workforce, England and Wales: 31 March 2026 (22 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2026","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","PCSOs identifying as ethnic minorities accounted for 14.3% compared with 18.3% of the resident population."),SRCQ("Home Office: Police workforce, England and Wales: 31 March 2025, second edition (updated 9 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2025/police-workforce-england-and-wales-31-march-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","PCSOs were the most ethnically diverse part of the police workforce as at 31 March 2025 (14.0%) followed by special constables (12.9%)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Home Office, including Border Force: 6.2% Black staff, 2025",text:"At the end of March 2025, 6.2% of Home Office staff were Black or Black British and 24.7% were from ethnic minority backgrounds. Migration and borders operations, which includes Border Force, Immigration Enforcement, UK Visas and Immigration and HM Passport Office, employed 81% of Home Office staff in 2024. Ethnic minority representation was lower at senior grades: 9.6% in the Senior Civil Service, against 28.7% at the most junior grades (AA/AO).",figure:"6.2%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"No separate Border Force breakdown by ethnicity is published; 32% of staff records had unknown ethnicity in 2024.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Home Office workforce diversity statistics 2024 to 2025 (31 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/home-office-workforce-diversity-statistics-2024-to-2025/home-office-workforce-diversity-statistics-2024-to-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","At the end of March 2025, 24.7% of Home Office staff were from ethnic minority backgrounds"),SRCQ("Home Office: Home Office workforce diversity statistics 2023 to 2024 (27 February 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/home-office-workforce-diversity-statistics-2023-to-2024/home-office-workforce-diversity-statistics-2023-to-2024","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Migration and borders operations – staff in this area include those working in UK Visas and Immigration (UKVI), Border Force, Immigration Enforcement, and His Majesty’s Passport Office")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Armed forces reserves: 7.0% from ethnic minorities, 2026",text:"At 1 April 2026, 7.0% of the UK's Future Reserves 2020 volunteer reserve were from ethnic minorities (excluding White minorities), up from 6.8% a year earlier. The share was 4.9% among reserve officers and 7.7% among other ranks. Ethnic minorities made up 9.8% of reserve intake in the year to 31 March 2026.",figure:"7.0%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Black-only shares for reserves are not given in the bulletin text.",src:[SRCQ("Ministry of Defence: UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2026 (9 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2026","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","4.9 per cent of Officers and 7.7 per cent of Other Ranks in the Future Reserves 2020."),SRCQ("Ministry of Defence: UK armed forces biannual diversity statistics: April 2025 (12 June 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2025/uk-armed-forces-biannual-diversity-statistics-april-2025","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","4.8 per cent of Officers and 7.4 per cent of Other Ranks in the Future Reserves 2020.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"svhistory",h:"A history of Black service in uniform",intro:"Black and Mixed-heritage people have served in British uniformed services for more than two centuries, often under formal colour bars and unequal terms. These records come from national museums, archives and the services' own histories.",points:[
  {h:"West India Regiments: raised 1795, partly from enslaved men",text:"Eight West India Regiments were embodied in 1795 as a formal part of the British Army. The National Army Museum records that estimates suggest 13,400 enslaved people were purchased for the regiments between 1795 and 1807, and that serving soldiers who had been enslaved when recruited were freed under the Mutiny Act of 1807. The National Archives notes the regiment was later deployed in colonial conflicts, particularly in Africa.",figure:"13,400",year:"1795-1807",disputed:false,note:"The 13,400 figure is given by the museum as an estimate.",src:[SRCQ("National Army Museum: West India Regiments","https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/west-india-regiments","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Between 1795 and 1807, estimates suggest 13,400 slaves were purchased for the West India Regiments."),SRCQ("The National Archives: Caribbean ancestors, an overview (research guide)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/caribbean-ancestors-an-overview/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Troops from the Caribbean, including the West India Regiment were also deployed in other colonial conflicts, particularly in Africa.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"British West Indies Regiment: 15,600 men, 1915 to 1918",text:"The British West Indies Regiment was established by Royal Warrant on 3 November 1915 and grew to 11 battalions and 15,600 men, two thirds of them from Jamaica. Military regulations prevented Black West Indian soldiers from holding a rank higher than warrant officer. By the end of the war, 185 men from the regiment had been killed in action and 1,071 had died of sickness.",figure:"15,600",year:"1915-1918",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Imperial War Museums: The West Indies war effort in the First World War","https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/how-the-west-indies-helped-the-war-effort-in-the-first-world-war","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","The regiment ultimately consisted of 11 battalions - a total of 15,600 men."),SRCQ("Imperial War Museums: British West Indies Regiment","https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/first-world-war/participation/british-west-indies-regiment","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","By the end of the First World War, 185 men from the BWIR had been killed in action and 1,071 had died of sickness.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Taranto, December 1918: protest against unequal treatment",text:"British West Indies Regiment soldiers were denied a pay rise given to the rest of the British Army because they were classed as so-called 'native' troops. Gathered at Taranto in Italy for demobilisation in December 1918, they were badly mistreated. Imperial War Museums describes a riot suppressed by armed soldiers; historians refer to the mutiny of the regiment's battalions and have long regarded it as resistance to a racist military hierarchy and an early stirring of Caribbean nationalism, while recent research also points to the veterans' hopes for imperial reform.",figure:"",year:"1918",disputed:false,note:"The second source is the abstract of a paper presented at The National Archives' Voices of the Home Fronts conference, 19 October 2018.",src:[SRCQ("Imperial War Museums: The World War 1 Black British soldiers deliberately forgotten","https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/first-world-war/participation/black-british-soldiers","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Then in December they were concentrated into the port of Taranto in Italy for demobilisation, but were badly mistreated."),SRCQ("Goldsmiths Research Online: Smith, The British West Indies Regiment mutiny at Taranto and multicultural memories of the First World War (2018)","https://research.gold.ac.uk/25273","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","the event continues to be regarded as embodying resistance to a racist military hierarchy and as a manifestation of nascent nationalism.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Caribbean and African aircrew in the RAF: about 450 flew, over 150 killed",text:"Around 450 volunteers from the Caribbean and Africa served as RAF aircrew in the Second World War, flying with Bomber, Fighter, Coastal and Flying Training Commands; the RAF Museum records that over 150 were killed. About 100 volunteers were commissioned and 103 decorated. The National Archives notes that for the first half of the 20th century only white men were eligible for combatant roles or commissions, with notable wartime exceptions.",figure:"450 aircrew",year:"1939-1945",disputed:false,note:"The RAF Museum's own pages differ slightly: one gives 150 killed, the other over 150. Ethnic origin was not recorded on RAF personnel files, so all totals are estimates.",src:[SRCQ("RAF Museum: Pilots of the Caribbean, The Second World War, 1939 to 1945: the record","https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/online-exhibitions/pilots-of-the-caribbean/across-the-commands/the-second-world-war-1939-to-1945-the-record","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Some 450 volunteers flew with Bomber, Fighter, Coastal and Flying Training Commands and over 150 of these men were killed."),SRCQ("RAF Museum: The Empire Windrush, Peter Devitt (22 June 2018)","https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/blog/the-empire-windrush/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Around 5,500 were engaged by the RAF as ground staff and 450 as aircrew; while 80 women joined the Women’s Auxiliary Air Force (WAAF)."),SRCQ("The National Archives: Caribbean ancestors, an overview (research guide)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/caribbean-ancestors-an-overview/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","For the first half of the 20th century only white men (of ‘European descent’) were eligible to take combatant roles or become officers in the British armed forces.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Blitz firefighters: George Arthur Roberts and Black 'front liners'",text:"London Fire Brigade records George Arthur Roberts, born in Trinidad, who fought on the Western Front in the First World War, as the first Black man to join the London Auxiliary Fire Service, in 1938. He served at New Cross Fire Station during the Blitz and was awarded the British Empire Medal in 1944; he died in 1970. In December 1940, A. A. Thompson, general secretary of the League of Coloured Peoples, praised the number of 'coloured men' in London working as wardens, Auxiliary Fire Service men, stretcher parties and first aid units.",figure:"",year:"1938-1945",disputed:false,note:"Sources differ on his birth year: London Fire Brigade gives 1891, Southwark News gives 1890.",src:[SRCQ("London Fire Brigade Museum: George Arthur Roberts BEM","https://www.london-fire.gov.uk/museum/london-fire-brigade-history-and-stories/trailblazers/george-arthur-roberts-bem","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Too old to fight in the Second World War, in 1938 George was the first Black man to join the London Auxiliary Fire Service (AFS)."),SRCQ("Southwark News: Blue plaque for first Black man to serve in army and fire brigade (15 September 2016)","https://www.southwarknews.co.uk/news/blue-plaque-first-black-man-serve-army-fire-brigade-camberwell/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","In 1944 he was awarded the British Empire Medal"),SRCQ("London Fire Brigade Museum: Black 'front liners' and fire service personnel during the London Blitz, Stephen Bourne","https://www.london-fire.gov.uk/museum/london-fire-brigade-history-and-stories/black-front-liners-and-fire-service-personnel-during-the-london-blitz","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","are to be found working as Wardens, A.F.S. (Auxiliary Fire Service) men, members of Stretcher Parties, First Aid units, and Mobile Canteens.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Early Black and mixed-heritage police officers: 1835 to 1968",text:"John Kent, born in 1805, whose father had been enslaved, began policing at Maryport in 1835 and joined Carlisle City Police in 1837; the Police Federation and Cumbria Police describe him as Britain's first Black police officer. Robert Branford joined the Metropolitan Police in 1838 and became superintendent of Southwark's M Division in 1856; an 1893 memoir by a former Met chief inspector described him as of mixed race. He died in 1869. Sislin Fay Allen joined the Met in 1968, becoming the UK's first Black policewoman; she died in 2021.",figure:"",year:"1835-1968",disputed:false,note:"Branford's heritage is known from an 1893 memoir cited by historian Jeffrey Green. 'First' claims rest on surviving records and may change as research continues.",src:[SRCQ("Police Federation of England and Wales: Remembering Britain's first black police officer (21 October 2021)","https://polfed.org/news/latest-news/2021/remembering-britains-first-black-police-officer/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","John began his policing career in 1835 where he was a constable at Maryport"),SRCQ("The Voice: Britain's first black police officer honoured with blue plaque (28 October 2019)","https://www.voice-online.co.uk/news/uk-news/2019/10/28/britains-first-black-police-officer-honoured-with-blue-plaque/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","He began his duties as a police officer in Maryport in 1835 and later joined Carlisle Police in 1837."),SRCQ("Jeffrey Green: Robert Branford, 1817-1869, London police superintendent","https://jeffreygreen.co.uk/173-robert-branford-1817-1869-london-police-superintendent/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Branford was promoted to sergeant in 1846, became an inspector in 1851 and superintendent of M-Division (Southwark) in 1856."),SRCQ("BBC News: Sislin Fay Allen, Britain's first black policewoman dies aged 83 (July 2021)","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-london-57742700","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","The UK's first black policewoman, Sislin Fay Allen, has died aged 83."),SRCQ("Police Professional: UK's first black female police officer dies aged 83 (7 July 2021)","https://policeprofessional.com/news/uks-first-black-female-police-officer-dies-aged-83/","Supports the Uniformed and frontline public services page","Sislin Fay Allen, who joined the Metropolitan Police Service (MPS) in 1968 at the age of 29, making her the first black female police officer in both the force and the UK")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black staff are the least likely NHS group to believe their trust offers fair progression.",refs:["sv-svother-black-nhs-staff-least-likely-to-see-fair"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"45% of minority ethnic probation staff surveyed did not feel safe raising racial discrimination.",refs:["sv-svjustice-probation-inspectors-45-of-minority-ethn"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Ethnic minority prison and probation staff faced discipline cases at 2.6 per 100, against 1.6 for White staff. A difference in rates does not by itself show its cause.",refs:["sv-svjustice-higher-discipline-rates-lower-bonus-rate"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Senior fire ranks are less diverse than the frontline, so look at who progresses, not only who joins.",refs:["sv-svfire-senior-fire-ranks-remain-less-diverse-th"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Most frontline series report ethnic minorities together rather than Black and Mixed groups separately; ask for the split.",refs:["sv-svother-ambulance-staff-4-2-from-ethnic-minoriti", "sv-svother-home-office-including-border-force-6-2-b"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"1.4% of firefighters in England were Black in 2024, against 4.4% of the working-age population.",refs:["sv-svfire-black-firefighters-1-4-of-firefighters-i"]},{t:"7.9% of prison and probation staff are Black. Black applicants made 40.3% of applications but 18.4% of accepted offers.",refs:["sv-svjustice-7-9-of-prison-and-probation-staff-are-bl", "sv-svjustice-black-applicants-40-3-of-applications-18"]},{t:"Inspectors found evidence of racism in at least 11 fire and rescue services in 2023.",refs:["sv-svfire-inspectorate-found-racism-in-at-least-11"]},{t:"Black and Mixed-heritage people have served in British uniformed services since at least 1795.",refs:["sv-svhistory-west-india-regiments-raised-1795-partly-"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["profession"]={name:"Your profession",pfx:"pr",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"prdata",h:"Ethnicity by profession in the Civil Service",intro:"Cabinet Office Civil Service Statistics for 31 March 2026 show which professions are most and least ethnically diverse, and where Black civil servants work.",points:[
  {h:"Operational Delivery is over half the Civil Service",text:"Operational Delivery is the largest Civil Service profession, with 300,080 civil servants at 31 March 2026. It accounts for 54.9% of civil servants with a recorded profession; the next largest are Policy (6.8%) and Digital and Data (5.8%).",figure:"300,080 (54.9%)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"54.9% is of the 546,425 with a reported profession; of all 557,825 civil servants the share is 53.8%. Professions data reporting rate was 98.0%.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Your profession page","The majority of civil servants (54.9%) work in the Operational Delivery profession."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics: 2026, Table 8 (29 July 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Your profession page","Headcount of all civil servants working in the Operational Delivery profession")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Civil Service ethnic minority share at a record 19.2%",text:"Of civil servants with a known ethnicity, 19.2% were from an ethnic minority background in 2026, up from 18.0% in 2025. Black civil servants numbered 26,655, the second largest ethnic minority group after Asian civil servants (46,310).",figure:"19.2%; Black 26,655 (5.8% of known ethnicity)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The 5.8% Black share is 26,655 of 459,540 civil servants with a known ethnicity (data browser totals); it is not a figure stated in the bulletin. Percentages exclude unknown and undeclared ethnicity, which together cover 98,285 people.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Your profession page","Of those with a known ethnicity, the percentage who are from an ethnic minority background is at a record high of 19.2%."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Your profession page","On a headcount basis, Asian civil servants (46,310) make up the largest ethnic minority group in the Civil Service, followed by Black civil servants (26,655).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Highest ethnic minority shares: Tax, Debt, Legal, Economics",text:"Among professions with more than 1,000 staff, Tax has the highest ethnic minority share of those with known ethnicity (27.6%), followed by Debt (25.3%), Legal (23.3%), Economics (23.3%) and Counter Fraud (23.0%). Cyber (25.5%) and Corporate Finance (23.7%) are also high but rest on fewer than 250 people with known ethnicity.",figure:"Tax 27.6%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Derived by the hub from Cabinet Office Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026) headcounts, rounded to the nearest 5: share = group headcount divided by headcount with known ethnicity (excluding Unknown and Undeclared). Cabinet Office does not publish these percentages. Tax was 23.8% on the same basis in 2025.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,White,Tax,9765,9140,45640,39560"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,Asian,Tax,2300,2210,39970,37680")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Lowest ethnic minority shares: Planning Inspectors, Geography, Veterinarian",text:"The lowest ethnic minority shares are in Planning Inspectors (2.3% of known ethnicity), Geography (3.7%) and Veterinarian (5.0%). Among larger professions, Security (10.2%), Property (11.5%), Intelligence Analysis (11.6%) and Social Research (12.1%) are below the Civil Service average of 19.2%.",figure:"Planning Inspectors 2.3%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Derived by the hub from Cabinet Office Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026) headcounts, rounded to the nearest 5: share = group headcount divided by headcount with known ethnicity (excluding Unknown and Undeclared). Cabinet Office does not publish these percentages. Suppressed cells ([c], fewer than 5 people) are counted as zero, so shares for small professions are lower bounds. Geography has 31.4% unknown or undeclared ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,White,Planning Inspectors,420,380,66980,66960"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","These figures should be interpreted with caution when the total number of employees in a group is small")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black shares by profession range from about 1% to 7.9%",text:"Among professions with more than 1,000 staff, Black civil servants make up 7.9% of known ethnicity in Tax, 7.1% in Counter Fraud and 6.7% in Operational Delivery, all above the Civil Service figure of 5.8%, and the lowest shares are in Intelligence Analysis (1.9%), Social Research (2.2%), Security (2.3%) and Operational Research (2.8%). In smaller professions the share is 1.2% in Geography (about 5 people), and Black counts are suppressed (fewer than 5 people) in Actuary, Veterinarian, Planning Inspectors and Occupational Psychology.",figure:"Tax 7.9%; Intelligence Analysis 1.9%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Derived by the hub from Cabinet Office Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026) headcounts, rounded to the nearest 5: share = group headcount divided by headcount with known ethnicity (excluding Unknown and Undeclared). Cabinet Office does not publish these percentages. Black counts across all profession categories, including 'Other' and 'Unknown', sum to 26,655, matching the bulletin total.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,Black,Tax,1065,1045,36660,32230"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,Black,Counter Fraud,830,785,35830,35620")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnicity by profession is in the data browser, not the tables",text:"The 2026 statistical tables publish profession headcounts (Table 8) and salary by profession but no table of ethnicity by profession; that breakdown is available only in the Civil Service Statistics data browser. The 2025 ethnicity pay gap tables (Table G) are broken down by department, not profession.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Cabinet Office has announced the 2026 Civil Service ethnicity and disability pay gap statistics for 28 October 2026 (GOV.UK statistics release announcement); check whether they add a profession breakdown once published.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics: 2026, Contents (29 July 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Your profession page","Civil Service employment; profession by government department"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","Data preview: All civil servants / Ethnicity / Profession_of_post")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"prown",h:"What professions publish about their own members",intro:"A few professions and their lead departments publish diversity data on their members; much of it is dated and little separates out Black staff.",points:[
  {h:"Policy Profession: 18.1% ethnic minority in 2021",text:"Cabinet Office official statistics on the Policy Profession show 18.1% of policy staff with a known ethnicity were from an ethnic minority at 31 March 2021. The share fell with seniority, from 31.4% at AA/AO to 9% at Senior Civil Service level; 1,090 policy staff were Black.",figure:"18.1%; SCS 9%; Black 1,090",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"No later edition published. The 2021 data exclude the Department for Work and Pensions and several Scottish Government agencies, and Cabinet Office gave no profession data for 90% of its staff. The 2026 figure of 17.5% for Policy is derived by the hub from the Cabinet Office data browser on a different basis.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Summary of civil servants in the policy profession: 2021 (24 March 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/summary-of-civil-servants-in-the-policy-profession-2021","Supports the Your profession page","Ethnic minority civil servants working in a policy role as percentage of known ethnicity")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Government Legal Department: 21.7% ethnic minority staff",text:"Source title: Government Legal Department: Annual Report and Accounts 2025–26 (18 June 2026)",figure:"21.7%; SCS 13.9%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Covers all Government Legal Department staff, not the whole Government Legal Profession, which also includes lawyers in other departments. No Black breakdown published.",src:[SRCQ("Government Legal Department: Annual Report and Accounts 2025–26 (17 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-legal-department-annual-report-and-accounts-2025-26","Supports the Your profession page","The proportion of ethnic minority staff (based on those who have self-declared) at SCS is 13.9% (2024-25: 14.3%). The proportion of all staff is 21.7% (2024-25: 22.7%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Government Legal Service: 19% from ethnic minorities in 2017",text:"The Government Legal Service published diversity monitoring data showing 19% of respondents declared a Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic background as of October 2017, and 10% at Senior Civil Service level. It compared this with 18% of practising solicitors and 13% of practising barristers.",figure:"19%; SCS 10%",year:"2017",disputed:false,note:"GOV.UK marks the page as withdrawn on 27 April 2018 and says the Government Legal Service is now called the Government Legal Profession. Figures are of questionnaire respondents only.",src:[SRCQ("Government Legal Service: Statistics at GLS (data as of October 2017; page withdrawn)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/civil-service-government-legal-service/about/statistics","Supports the Your profession page","19% of the total population declared that they were from a Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) background")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Government Social Research: 9% ethnic minority in 2019",text:"The GSR Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2021-2025 reported that 9% of GSR members were from an ethnic minority in 2019, against 13.9% across analytical professions and 12.7% in the wider Civil Service. GSR made ethnic minority and socio-economic diversity its two priorities.",figure:"9%",year:"2019",disputed:false,note:"Annex sources mix the Cross-Government Analytical Functions Diversity Survey 2019 and ACSES 2016/2018. The 2026 data browser gives 12.1% for Social Research.",src:[SRCQ("Government Social Research: GSR Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2021-2025 (29 January 2021)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/gsr-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2021-2025","Supports the Your profession page","we will initially focus on two specific issues that need targeted and urgent attention: ethnic minority diversity and socio-economic diversity.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Analysis Function survey: 33% of Black respondents reported discrimination",text:"In the Analysis Function's 2021 Diversity and Inclusion Survey, 33% of Black respondents said they had personally experienced discrimination at work in the past 12 months, compared with 7% of White British respondents. In the 2020 Civil Service People Survey, 13% of Black analysts reported discrimination.",figure:"33% vs 7%",year:"2021",disputed:true,note:"Self-reported, from a survey with a 12% response rate (about 1,800 analysts); the page is archived. The 2020 Analysis Function survey gave a similar 34%, while 13% of Black analysts in the 2020 Civil Service People Survey reported discrimination.",src:[SRCQ("Government Analysis Function: Analysis Function Diversity and Inclusion Survey results (6 April 2022)","https://analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk/news/analysis-function-diversity-and-inclusion-survey-results/","Supports the Your profession page","33% of black respondents indicated that they had personally experienced discrimination at work in the past 12 months, in contrast with 7% of white British respondents in 2021")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Government Economic Service: diversity reports, no public figures",text:"The GES Strategy 2024-2027, reflecting on its 2021-2024 strategy, says the profession began producing annual membership statistical reports on its diversity profile. No published edition of these reports or their ethnicity figures was found on GOV.UK.",figure:"",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The 2026 figures of 23.3% ethnic minority and 3.2% Black for the Economics profession are derived by the hub from the Cabinet Office data browser.",src:[SRCQ("Government Economic Service: GES Strategy 2024-2027 (22 May 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ges-strategy-2024-2027","Supports the Your profession page","Producing annual membership statistical reports, providing detailed data about the diversity profile of the GES.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"prdo",h:"Operational delivery: the largest profession",intro:"Over half of all civil servants work in operational delivery, and so do most Black civil servants.",points:[
  {h:"Frontline services: benefits, employment, prisons, licences",text:"Operational Delivery staff provide frontline government services directly to citizens or businesses. The work includes paying benefits and pensions, employment services, staffing prisons and issuing driving licences, as well as passports, tax, courts, borders and consular roles.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Your profession page","Operational Delivery staff provide frontline government services directly to citizens or businesses, including paying benefits and pensions, providing employment services, staffing prisons, and issuing driving licences."),SRCQ("Modern Civil Service blog: Welcome to the Operational Delivery Profession (30 September 2025)","https://moderncivilservice.blog.gov.uk/2025/09/30/welcome-to-the-operational-delivery-profession-delivering-excellence-every-day/","Supports the Your profession page","These roles vary hugely, from processing passport applications and helping people find work as a jobcentre coach, to working as tax officers, court officials, and in consular roles.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"300,080 people: the largest Civil Service profession",text:"At 31 March 2026, 300,080 civil servants worked in Operational Delivery, 54.9% of those with a recorded profession and eight times the next largest profession, Policy (37,275). The profession's own page puts its membership at over 290,000.",figure:"300,080",year:"2026",disputed:true,note:"The profession's page calls it the largest of 28 Civil Service professions; Table 8 has 35 profession categories and the GOV.UK government professions list names 33.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics: 2026, Table 8 (29 July 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Your profession page","Headcount of all civil servants working in the Operational Delivery profession"),SRCQ("Operational Delivery Profession: About us (updated 1 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/civil-service-operational-delivery-profession/about","Supports the Your profession page","The Operational Delivery Profession totals over 290,000 people who are spread across England, Wales, Scotland, Northern Ireland and overseas.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Concentrated in Justice, DWP, Home Office and HMRC",text:"Ministry of Justice has 82,290 Operational Delivery staff (84.4% of its civil servants), DWP 71,505, the Home Office 37,595 and HMRC 34,510. Together these four departments hold three quarters of the profession.",figure:"MoJ 82,290",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Four-department total is 225,900 of 300,080 (75.3%). Department shares from Table 8 headcounts.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical tables - Civil Service Statistics: 2026, Table 8 (29 July 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a677302dbf59f1cdb3622ee/Statistical_tables_-_Civil_Service_Statistics_2026.ods","Supports the Your profession page","Table 8: Civil Service employment; profession by government department, as at 31 March 2026")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mostly junior grades and lowest median pay after Debt",text:"Operational Delivery has the second lowest median salary of any profession, £32,140, above only Debt (£27,840). 110,175 staff are at AA/AO and 101,535 at EO, together 70.5% of the profession; 950 are at SCS level.",figure:"Median £32,140",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"23,460 Operational Delivery staff have an unknown grade.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin - Civil Service Statistics: 2026 (29 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Your profession page","Those with the lowest median salary are Debt (£27,840) and Operational Delivery (£32,140)."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Profession_of_post by Responsibility_level_grouped (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Profession_of_post/Responsibility_level_grouped/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,Operational Delivery,AA/AO,110175,99370,29800,28020")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Home to 62% of all Black civil servants",text:"16,645 Black civil servants work in Operational Delivery, 62.4% of the 26,655 Black civil servants in 2026, and 6.7% of the profession's staff with known ethnicity. The profession's ethnic minority share is 20.1%, up from 18.6% in 2025.",figure:"16,645 (62.4%)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Derived by the hub from Cabinet Office Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026) headcounts, rounded to the nearest 5: share = group headcount divided by headcount with known ethnicity (excluding Unknown and Undeclared). Cabinet Office does not publish these percentages. Operational Delivery holds 54.9% of all civil servants with a recorded profession, so the 62.4% figure shows Black civil servants are somewhat more concentrated there than civil servants overall. 17.0% of Operational Delivery staff have unknown or undeclared ethnicity. Median salary of Black Operational Delivery staff is £33,750.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2026), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post (generated 16 July 2026)","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2026/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","In post,2026,Black,Operational Delivery,16645,15845,35330,33750"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Statistics data browser (2025), Ethnicity by Profession_of_post","https://civil-service-statistics.jdac.service.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/data_browser_2025/Ethnicity/Profession_of_post/index.html","Supports the Your profession page","\"In post\",\"2025\",\"Black\",\"Operational Delivery\",\"14710\",\"13985\",\"33890\",\"32450\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"A profession with its own skills framework and routes",text:"The Operational Delivery Profession offers a Professional Skills Framework, apprenticeships, qualifications, a Fast Stream scheme and development programmes from AA to Senior Civil Service. It describes itself as the public face of the Civil Service.",figure:"",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Operational Delivery Profession: We are the Operational Delivery Profession (17 May 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/we-are-the-operational-delivery-profession","Supports the Your profession page","We offer apprenticeships, qualifications, a Fast Stream scheme, and our own development programmes from AA to SCS level.")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black civil servants work across every profession, but 62% of them are in Operational Delivery, the largest and lowest-paid profession.",refs:["pr-prdo-home-to-62-of-all-black-civil-servants", "pr-prdo-mostly-junior-grades-and-lowest-median-p"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Ethnic minority shares differ widely between professions, from Tax and Debt at the top to Planning Inspectors and Geography at the bottom.",refs:["pr-prdata-highest-ethnic-minority-shares-tax-debt-", "pr-prdata-lowest-ethnic-minority-shares-planning-i"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"In the Analysis Function's own survey, a third of Black respondents reported discrimination at work in the past year.",refs:["pr-prown-analysis-function-survey-33-of-black-res"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Most frontline public services are delivered by Operational Delivery staff, concentrated in four departments.",refs:["pr-prdo-frontline-services-benefits-employment-p", "pr-prdo-concentrated-in-justice-dwp-home-office-"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Ethnicity by profession is published only in the data browser; few professions publish their own figures.",refs:["pr-prdata-ethnicity-by-profession-is-in-the-data-b", "pr-prown-government-economic-service-diversity-re"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Operational Delivery is more than half the Civil Service, and home to 62% of Black civil servants.",refs:["pr-prdata-operational-delivery-is-over-half-the-ci", "pr-prdo-home-to-62-of-all-black-civil-servants"]},{t:"The Civil Service ethnic minority share reached 19.2% in 2026, but shares vary widely by profession.",refs:["pr-prdata-civil-service-ethnic-minority-share-at-a", "pr-prdata-highest-ethnic-minority-shares-tax-debt-"]},{t:"Black shares by profession range from about 1% to 7.9% of staff with a known ethnicity.",refs:["pr-prdata-black-shares-by-profession-range-from-ab"]},{t:"Few professions publish their own ethnicity figures; the Policy Profession, Government Legal Department and Government Social Research have.",refs:["pr-prown-policy-profession-18-1-ethnic-minority-i", "pr-prown-government-legal-department-21-7-ethnic-", "pr-prown-government-social-research-9-ethnic-mino"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["beyond"]={name:"Beyond government",pfx:"bg",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"bgemploy",h:"Employers and supply chains",intro:"Public bodies shape private employers through the law, the rules for publishing pay data and the contracts they award. This section sets out where ethnicity pay reporting stands, what employers already collect, and how procurement and the equality duty reach suppliers.",points:[
  {h:"Ethnicity pay gap reporting is still voluntary",text:"No employer in Great Britain has to publish an ethnicity pay gap. Government guidance from April 2023 offers a voluntary, consistent method. In March 2026 the government published its consultation response and draft clauses for mandatory reporting by employers with 250 or more staff, but the Equality (Race and Disability) Bill had not been introduced to Parliament by October 2026.",figure:"250+ staff (proposed threshold)",year:"April 2023 to October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Status as at 26 October 2026. The bill was not in the 13 May 2026 King's Speech, and the government's background briefing notes for that speech list no such bill (GOV.UK). One law firm's working assumption is that any duty would start no earlier than 2027. Proposed scope: private and voluntary employers in Great Britain and public bodies in England with 250 or more staff. The government response and draft clauses are covered on the DEEIA lens page; this point covers what it means for employers now.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Office for Equality and Opportunity): Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: government response (25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Beyond government page","The government will introduce mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting for employers with 250 or more employees."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit and Department for Business and Trade): Ethnicity pay reporting: guidance for employers, introduction and overview (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ethnicity-pay-reporting-guidance-for-employers/introduction-and-overview","Supports the Beyond government page","While ethnicity pay reporting is voluntary, many employers already report on their ethnicity pay data."),SRCQ("Full Fact: Is the government on course to introduce a new Race Equality Act? (updated 12 May 2026)","https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/race-equality-act/","Supports the Beyond government page","this legislation was not introduced before the prorogation of Parliament at the end of April 2026 and is yet to be published"),SRCQ("LexisNexis (LNB News): Equality (Race and Disability) Bill absent from King's Speech (13 May 2026)","https://www.lexisnexis.co.uk/legal/news/equality-race-disability-bill-absent-from-kings-speech","Supports the Beyond government page","The Government’s Equality (Race and Disability) Bill was notably absent from today’s King’s Speech"),SRCQ("Linklaters: Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: government publishes its consultation response (March 2026)","https://www.linklaters.com/insights/blogs/employmentlinks/2026/march/mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the Beyond government page","As a working assumption, mandatory reporting is unlikely to be required before 2027 at the earliest, and is likely to be later (depending on the Parliamentary timetable).")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"A Black pay gap may not show in published figures",text:"The planned rules would require at least a two-group comparison (for example, White and all ethnic minority staff), with five broad groups 'where possible'. The 2023 voluntary guidance advises publishing a group only where it has at least 50 employees. Where Black staff are few, their figures may be merged into a wider group or left out.",figure:"Binary comparison minimum",year:"2023 guidance; 2026 response",disputed:false,note:"The minimum group size for the mandatory scheme was still being decided in March 2026; the consultation had proposed at least 10 employees per group. The last sentence of the text is a reading of the two rules, not a finding from data.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Office for Equality and Opportunity): Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting: government response (25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Beyond government page","Ethnicity pay gap reporting will require employers to report a binary comparison at minimum and also aggregate to 5 ethnic groups where possible."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Race Disparity Unit and Department for Business and Trade): Ethnicity pay reporting: understanding and reporting your data (17 April 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ethnicity-pay-reporting-guidance-for-employers/understanding-and-reporting-your-data","Supports the Beyond government page","It is recommended that each ethnic group in your analysis have a minimum of 50 employees, if you are publishing these findings")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Most large employers already hold staff ethnicity data",text:"The government's impact assessment estimates that 89% of employers with 250 or more staff already collect their employees' ethnicity, and 44% already publish an ethnicity pay gap. Both estimates rest mainly on a 2023 Business in the Community survey of employers that had signed its Race at Work Charter.",figure:"89% collect; 44% publish",year:"2023 survey; 2026 estimate",disputed:false,note:"Scope: employers with 250 or more staff in England, Scotland and Wales. The survey covered nearly 250 employers already committed to action on race, so it may overstate practice across all large employers. The impact assessment treats the sample as 'relatively representative'. Its own 2025 business survey found lower figures: 82% collect ethnicity data, 27% calculate an ethnicity pay gap and 9% publish one. The impact assessment calls 44% 'a fair mid-point'. A 2024 CIPD survey found 40% of large employers carrying out ethnicity pay gap analysis. The Race at Work Charter itself is covered elsewhere in the hub.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Office for Equality and Opportunity): Final stage impact assessment: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting (25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/final-stage-impact-assessment-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-including-workforce-reporting-and-action-plans","Supports the Beyond government page","Our best estimate for the proportion of employers with 250 or more employees already voluntarily reporting their ethnicity pay gaps is 44%"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Office for Equality and Opportunity): Final stage impact assessment: mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting (25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/final-stage-impact-assessment-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-including-workforce-reporting-and-action-plans","Supports the Beyond government page","Our best estimate for the proportion of employers with 250 or more employees already voluntarily collecting data on the ethnicity of their employees is 89%"),SRCQ("HRreview: 44% of employers voluntarily publish their ethnicity pay gap, new research finds (11 October 2023)","https://hrreview.co.uk/hr-news/diversity-news/44-of-employers-voluntarily-publish-their-ethnicity-pay-gap-new-research-finds/372959","Supports the Beyond government page","revealed a notable increase in the number of employers voluntarily publishing their ethnicity pay gap, rising from 30 percent in 2020 to 44 percent in 2023")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Social value in central government contracts",text:"Since October 2025, central government buyers must use the Social Value Model in Procurement Policy Note 002 for contracts above the threshold. Social value must normally be worth at least 10% of the total score, with limited exceptions where it is 10% of the quality score. Buyers choose outcomes relevant to the contract. One listed example of fair pay practice is monitoring and reporting gender and ethnicity pay gaps.",figure:"10% minimum weighting",year:"February 2025 (mandatory from October 2025)",disputed:false,note:"The model has no outcome specific to race; ethnicity appears as an example under fair pay, and another outcome covers jobs for groups under-represented in the workforce. It applies to central government, not to every public body. Contracts and outcomes vary, so suppliers are not asked about ethnicity pay gaps in every tender.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Procurement Policy Note 002: The Social Value Model (13 February 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ppn-002-taking-account-of-social-value-in-the-award-of-contracts/procurement-policy-note-002-the-social-value-model-html","Supports the Beyond government page","Monitoring and reporting of gender and ethnicity pay gaps and plans to address them where necessary"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): PPN 002 Guide to using the Social Value Model (PDF, February 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/67c8206b2ecc810ad1fc656b/2025-02-08_PPN_002_Guide_to_using_the_social_value_model.docx__3_.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","PPN 002 mandates a minimum weighting for social value of 10% of the total scores available (or equivalent)."),SRCQ("Government Commercial Agency: What does the new social value model mean for public sector procurement? (15 December 2025)","https://www.gca.gov.uk/news/what-does-the-new-social-value-model-mean-for-public-sector-procurement","Supports the Beyond government page","From October 2025, the new model must be applied to central government procurements that meet the threshold requirement.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The equality duty follows public money into contracts",text:"When a public body buys services, the equality duty stays with it: Cabinet Office guidance says the duty cannot be delegated and has no financial threshold. A contractor that exercises public functions must itself have due regard to the duty's aims when carrying them out. The duty itself is set out on The DEEIA lens page.",figure:"",year:"Equality Act 2010 (in force); guidance 2025",disputed:false,note:"Section 149 extends to England, Scotland and Wales, not Northern Ireland. A private member's bill to repeal it is before Parliament and is not law; see the DEEIA lens page.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 149 (public sector equality duty)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the Beyond government page","A person who is not a public authority but who exercises public functions must, in the exercise of those functions, have due regard to the matters mentioned in subsection (1)."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): PPN 002 Guide to using the Social Value Model (PDF, February 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/67c8206b2ecc810ad1fc656b/2025-02-08_PPN_002_Guide_to_using_the_social_value_model.docx__3_.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","Contracting authorities should note that the PSED is non-delegable and that its requirements are not subject to any financial threshold."),SRCQ("UK Parliament Bills API: Public Sector Equality Duty (Repeal) Bill, private member's presentation bill (record updated 23 June 2026)","https://bills-api.parliament.uk/api/v1/Bills/4189","Supports the Beyond government page","A Bill to repeal section 149 and schedule 18 of the Equality Act 2010; to make provision consequential on that repeal; and for connected purposes.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Supplier diversity: local commitments, little national data",text:"Some public bodies buy membership of supplier diversity networks to reach businesses owned by ethnic minority people. The City of London Corporation describes itself as the first governing body to join Minority Supplier Development UK. Westminster City Council and the Metropolitan Police joined later. Its own paper says data protection law limits how buyers can find diverse-owned suppliers. A 2021 report for that network called on government and public bodies to lead.",figure:"",year:"2021 to 2023",disputed:false,note:"The sources give no national figure for public spending with Black-owned suppliers. 'Ethnic minority businesses' in these sources covers all ethnic minority groups, not Black-owned firms alone. The 2021 report was commissioned by the supplier diversity network itself. For business support and finance, see the Black enterprise page.",src:[SRCQ("City of London Corporation, Policy and Resources Committee: Supplier Diversity Sponsorship (8 June 2023)","https://democracy.cityoflondon.gov.uk/documents/s186833/Policy%20and%20Resources%20-%20Supplier%20Diversity%20Sponsorship%20FINAL.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","The City Corporation made a public commitment to support supplier diversity as the first governing body to join MSDUK (Minority Supplier Development UK)"),SRCQ("City of London Corporation, Policy and Resources Committee: Supplier Diversity Sponsorship (8 June 2023)","https://democracy.cityoflondon.gov.uk/documents/s186833/Policy%20and%20Resources%20-%20Supplier%20Diversity%20Sponsorship%20FINAL.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","Due to data protection legislation, there are limited ways to access DOE suppliers."),SRCQ("OPEN for MSDUK: Minority Businesses Matter: the contribution and challenges of ethnic minority businesses in the UK (February 2021)","https://resources.msduk.org.uk/hubfs/Resources/5.Minority-Businesses-Matter-FINAL.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","Government and public-sector bodies should take the lead in integrating supplier diversity into their procurement strategies, including the tender and contracting processes.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"bgvol",h:"The voluntary, community and faith sector",intro:"Black-led charities, community groups and churches deliver services alongside, and often for, public bodies. This section looks at who governs and works in the sector, how small many Black-led groups are, and the role of Black majority churches.",points:[
  {h:"Black trustees: 3% overall, 7% among under-60s",text:"In the Charity Commission's 2024 survey, 3% of trustees were Black, compared with 4% of adults. Among trustees under 60 the picture reverses: 7% were Black, compared with 5% of the population of that age.",figure:"3% of trustees",year:"July to August 2024",disputed:false,note:"England and Wales, registered charities. Survey of 2,194 trustees, weighted by charity income, compared with Census 2021. The regulator's summary names Asian, mixed and other ethnic groups, not Black people, as less likely to be trustees; the report gives no significance test for the 3% against 4% gap. Mixed-ethnicity trustees were 1% against 2% of adults. One trade press report places a 1% Asian figure in the under-60 group; the regulator's report gives it for over-60s.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Charity Commission, with Pro Bono Economics): Trusteeship – a positive opportunity: understanding skills, experience and demographics in England and Wales (29 April 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/charity-commission-research-into-trusteeship-and-the-trustee-population/trusteeship-a-positive-opportunity-understanding-skills-experience-and-demographics-in-england-and-wales","Supports the Beyond government page","In the younger age group (under 60) there are proportionally more trustees who are Black (7% compared to 5% in the population)"),SRCQ("Civil Society News: Lack of diversity and legal expertise on charity boards, regulator's research finds (29 April 2025)","https://www.civilsociety.co.uk/news/lack-of-diversity-and-legal-expertise-on-charity-boards-regulators-research-finds.html","Supports the Beyond government page","there are proportionately more Black trustees aged below 60 compared to the general population (7% compared to 5%)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black staff are 3.8% of the voluntary sector workforce",text:"In December 2023, 13% of people working in the UK voluntary sector were from Global Majority backgrounds, against 15% in the public sector and 16% in the private sector. Black employees made up 3.8% of the voluntary sector workforce.",figure:"3.8%",year:"December 2023",disputed:false,note:"UK, December 2023. NCVO puts the Global Majority share of the population at 18%. An earlier Almanac (2022) found charities less ethnically diverse than other sectors; the 2024 edition says the sector 'still lags behind slightly', with the recent rise driven mainly by Asian workers. 'Global Majority' is NCVO's term. Labour Force Survey estimates are now official statistics in development (ONS).",src:[SRCQ("NCVO: UK Civil Society Almanac 2024: who works in the voluntary sector? (2024)","https://www.ncvo.org.uk/news-and-insights/news-index/uk-civil-society-almanac-2024/workforce/who-works-in-the-voluntary-sector/","Supports the Beyond government page","these are most likely to be Asian (5.5%) followed by Black (3.8%) employees"),SRCQ("Charity Times: Charities 'less ethnically diverse' than the private and public sectors, research finds (19 October 2022)","https://www.charitytimes.com/ct/Charities-less-ethnically-diverse-than-the-private-and-public-sectors-research-finds.php","Supports the Beyond government page","While 86% of the public and private sector workforce identify as White, this proportion increases to 90% among charity workers.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Small groups with thin reserves",text:"A spring 2020 survey by the Ubele Initiative found that most responding Black, Asian and minority ethnic-led groups were micro or small, with yearly income under £100,000, and most held no reserves. The authors estimated that 87% could stop operating if the crisis lasted beyond three months.",figure:"87%",year:"19 March to 4 April 2020",disputed:true,note:"Self-selecting sample: 182 responses from 165 organisations, of which 137 were led by Black, Asian and minority ethnic people, and 62% of those were in London. It is not representative of the sector. The 87% figure is used in different ways: in the report for the share that could close (an author projection) and the share that were micro or small (61 of 70 Wave 2 respondents); in press coverage for the share without three months' reserves. The report found 68% held no reserves. The groups are not broken down into Black-led and others. No register counts Black-led charities.",src:[SRCQ("The Ubele Initiative: Impact of COVID-19 on the BAME community and voluntary sector, final report (April 2020; copy hosted by Diversity UK)","https://diversityuk.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/Ubele-The-impact-of-Covid-19-on-the-BAME-Community.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","if the crisis continues beyond the notional 3 months, given the level of reserves being held, then 87% (or 9 out of 10 organisations) could conceivably cease to operate after three months"),SRCQ("Charity Times: Coronavirus: nine in ten small and micro BAME charities set to close if crisis continues (2020)","https://charitytimes.com/ct/coronavirus-nine-in-ten-bame-charities-set-to-close.php","Supports the Beyond government page","Of the respondents, 87% said they do not have sufficient reserves to last more than three months.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Funding and commissioning barriers named by Black and minoritised-led groups",text:"The 2021 Booska Paper, convened by the Ubele Initiative, gathered the views of Black and minoritised community leaders on funding and commissioning during the pandemic. It made nine calls to action for funders. Among the barriers reported were tenders weighted mostly on price.",figure:"70% price, 30% quality (example)",year:"April 2021",disputed:false,note:"Qualitative research: the price and quality split is an example from participants, not a national figure. The second source repeats Ubele's own summary.",src:[SRCQ("The Ubele Initiative: Booska Paper: exposing structural racism in the third sector (April 2021)","https://ubele.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/Booska-Paper-2021.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","Public procurement favours cost over quality: for example, in some procurement exercises, the weighting is 70% price to 30% quality."),SRCQ("Manchester Community Central: Booska paper: exposing structural racism in the third sector (29 April 2021)","https://manchestercommunitycentral.org/node/46513","Supports the Beyond government page","Booska Paper exposes structural racism in the voluntary, community and social enterprise (VCSE) sector and explored the impact of the pandemic on funding attitudes in the UK")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black majority churches and London church growth",text:"Sunday church attendance in London rose by 16% between 2005 and 2012, bucking a national downward trend, with growth especially among Black majority and immigrant churches. Two-thirds of new London churches were Pentecostal Black majority churches. A study of Southwark, reported in 2017, counted 240 Black majority churches in that borough alone.",figure:"240 (Black majority churches in Southwark)",year:"2005 to 2012; Southwark study 2017",disputed:false,note:"London only; the London-wide figures date from 2012 and come from one study, the London Church Census (commissioned by London City Mission). The share of Inner London worshippers who are Black is covered elsewhere in the hub.",src:[SRCQ("British Religion in Numbers (BRIN): London churchgoing and other news (2013)","https://www.brin.ac.uk/london-churchgoing-and-other-news/","Supports the Beyond government page","in Inner London 48% of worshippers were black"),SRCQ("Evangelical Alliance: Black and minority ethnic Christians lead London church growth (25 July 2013, archived page)","https://www.eauk.org/church/one-people-commission/stories/black-and-ethnic-christians-lead-london-church-growth.cfm","Supports the Beyond government page","two new London churches opened every week in the seven year period and two thirds of those were Pentecostal black majority churches (BMC)"),SRCQ("LSE Research Online: Rogers, A. (2017) How are black majority churches growing in the UK? A London borough case study","https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/69662","Supports the Beyond government page","Andrew Rogers’ Being Built Together project found 240 black majority churches in Southwark, south London")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Churches as partners in the vaccine rollout",text:"In March 2021, leaders of sixty Black majority churches issued a joint statement backing the NHS COVID-19 vaccine rollout. Some church leaders offered their buildings as pop-up vaccination sites.",figure:"60 churches",year:"March to June 2021",disputed:false,note:"The sources give no count of vaccination clinics held in church buildings and no measure of the effect on uptake. Coverage is UK-wide but examples are mainly from London.",src:[SRCQ("The Voice: UK's black majority churches want their congregations to consider taking the COVID-19 vaccine (2 March 2021)","https://www.voice-online.co.uk/faith/2021/03/02/uks-black-majority-churches-want-their-congregations-to-consider-taking-the-covid-19-vaccine/","Supports the Beyond government page","SIXTY BLACK majority churches are uniting, and backing the NHS vaccine rollout."),SRCQ("Churches Together in England: Black church leaders speak out in support of covid vaccine (22 June 2021)","https://cte.org.uk/black-church-leaders-speak-out-in-support-of-covid-vaccine/","Supports the Beyond government page","The church leaders, some of whom have volunteered the use of their churches as pop up vaccinations sites")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"bgfund",h:"Funders and investment",intro:"Grant-makers and social investors decide which community groups can grow. This section brings together what funders publish about money reaching groups led by Black and other minority ethnic communities, and where the data stops short.",points:[
  {h:"Funder audits: about 5% of funding reached minority-led groups",text:"Twenty funders that audited over £271 million of grants with the Funders for Race Equality Alliance tool found that 5% of the money went to organisations both led by and working for Black, Asian and minority ethnic communities. An earlier group of 13 funders found that 6% of funded organisations were led by the communities they served.",figure:"5%",year:"Audits up to November 2021",disputed:false,note:"UK. The two figures measure different things (share of money and share of funded organisations); the 13 funders were the first cohort of the 20. 'Led by' meant at least half of the board and senior team came from the communities served. The audit uses a combined Black, Asian and minority ethnic category with no Black-only figure. Funders chose to take part, so the results may not represent all grant-makers.",src:[SRCQ("Equally Ours (host of the Funders for Race Equality Alliance): Funding audit: current practice in racial justice funding (results to November 2021)","https://www.equallyours.org.uk/resources/funding-audit-current-practice-in-racial-justice-funding/","Supports the Beyond government page","5% of funding went to Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic sector organisations"),SRCQ("ACEVO (blog by Barrow Cadbury Trust for the Funders for Race Equality Alliance): Funding and racial justice: data driving change (20 May 2021)","https://www.acevo.org.uk/2021/05/funding-and-racial-justice-data-driving-change/","Supports the Beyond government page","an even lower proportion, 6%, of funded organisations were led by representatives from the communities being served")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Foundations now publish their own racial equity figures",text:"Some large foundations now report every year how much of their funding goes to groups led by people facing racial inequity. Paul Hamlyn Foundation put 17% of its audited 2024/25 UK funding into grants to organisations 'led by and for' those communities; Esmée Fairbairn Foundation reported 25.4% of its 2025 funding to organisations they lead.",figure:"17% and 25.4%",year:"2024/25 and 2025",disputed:false,note:"UK. Definitions differ: Paul Hamlyn's 17% uses a majority of board and senior staff; on the stricter DEI Data Standard definition (at least 75% of the board and 50% of senior staff) its figure is 13%. Esmée's figure relies on organisations' own answers on a monitoring form and covers 93% of the grants it awarded in 2025. Neither report gives a Black-only figure. Figures from two foundations do not describe the sector.",src:[SRCQ("Paul Hamlyn Foundation: Racial justice audit analysis of PHF's UK grant-making 2024/25 (9 December 2025)","https://www.phf.org.uk/assets/documents/PHF_Grant-making-review-FREA-audit-2025_AW.pdf?v=1764067777","Supports the Beyond government page","17% of funding went to grants to organisations ‘led by and for’ communities experiencing racial inequity using the majority definition of ‘led by’"),SRCQ("Esmée Fairbairn Foundation: Who our funding is reaching 2025 (undated web page)","https://esmeefairbairn.org.uk/latest-news/who-our-funding-is-reaching-2025/","Supports the Beyond government page","25.4% of the total funding by amount went to organisations led by communities experiencing racial inequity")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"National Lottery grants for projects serving Black communities",text:"The National Lottery Community Fund awarded £767 million in 2024/25. It reports grants by the communities a project will mostly benefit: 756 grants worth £73.9 million for African communities, 575 worth £61.0 million for Caribbean communities and 625 worth £67.2 million for Black British communities.",figure:"£73.9m (756 grants, African)",year:"April 2024 to March 2025",disputed:false,note:"UK. Applicants tick the communities a project will mostly benefit and can tick more than one, so the groups overlap and must not be added together. The figures describe who a project serves, not who leads the organisation. A further 631 grants (£69.2 million) were for 'any other Black, African or Caribbean background'.",src:[SRCQ("The National Lottery Community Fund: Annual report and accounts 2024-25 (2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6890721c486754ec2887839e/National_Lottery_Community_Fund_-_Annual_Report_and_Accounts_24-25_V3.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","Applicants are asked if their project will mostly benefit people from specific communities experiencing ethnic or racial inequity, discrimination or inequality."),SRCQ("The National Lottery Community Fund: Annual report and accounts 2024-25 (2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6890721c486754ec2887839e/National_Lottery_Community_Fund_-_Annual_Report_and_Accounts_24-25_V3.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","African 2,351 756 £259,194,643 £73,918,260 Caribbean 1,877 575 £240,492,127 £60,968,595 Black British 2,113 625 £284,557,447 £67,181,600"),SRCQ("Civil Society News: National Lottery Community Fund's grants up by over £80m last year, accounts show (14 August 2025)","https://www.civilsociety.co.uk/news/national-lottery-community-fund-s-grants-up-by-over-80m-last-year-accounts-show.html","Supports the Beyond government page","the community funder awarded £767m in 12,708 grants last year")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Phoenix Fund and Phoenix Way: community-led grant-making",text:"The Phoenix Fund, launched in 2020 and designed by Black and racially minoritised community leaders, distributed £2.4 million to 184 groups across England from around 1,400 applications. In 2021 the National Lottery Community Fund and the Ubele Initiative announced a £50 million, five-year partnership, The Phoenix Way, with community panels shaping awards.",figure:"£50m over five years",year:"2020 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Phoenix Fund covered England; The Phoenix Way's panels are drawn from across England, though its stated aims refer to groups across the UK. Announced figures; the sources do not report how much of the £50 million has been paid out. Charity Times reported that about 85% of the budget would go to activity directly supporting Black and minority-led organisations. The Learning, games and community page lists The Phoenix Way as a funding route.",src:[SRCQ("The Ubele Initiative: The Phoenix Way (undated web page)","https://www.ubele.org/our-work/the-phoenix-way","Supports the Beyond government page","£2.4 million was distributed (£0.4 million in infrastructure support and £2 million in grants to 184 community groups) across all regions of England."),SRCQ("Alliance magazine: £50m for Black, minority communities in new partnership (16 July 2021)","https://www.alliancemagazine.org/blog/50m-for-black-minority-communities-in-new-partnership/","Supports the Beyond government page","announced a new £50 million partnership aimed to boost funding and support to Black and minoritised Community charities and grassroots groups over the next five years"),SRCQ("Charity Times: National Lottery announces £50m partnership to boost funding for Black and minority led charities (2 July 2021)","https://www.charitytimes.com/ct/National-Lottery-announces-50m-partnership-to-boost-funding-for-Black-and-minority-led-charities.php","Supports the Beyond government page","Around 85% of the total budget will be allocated, either through grant funding or leadership and capacity development, to activities that directly support Black and minitory led organisations and the communities they help.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Social investment: blended loans and grants",text:"In October 2026 Social Investment Business, the Ubele Initiative and Create Equity opened the Flexible Finance Fund for Black and racially minoritised charities and social enterprises in England; Charity Times reports it is worth £4 million. It offers loans of £50,000 to £1.5 million with a grant of 50% to 100% of the loan, up to £200,000.",figure:"£4m fund",year:"October 2026",disputed:false,note:"England only. Applicants need at least two years' operation and a turnover of at least £200,000. Grant funding comes from Access – The Foundation for Social Investment, and the fund is supported by the government's Growth Guarantee Scheme. The £4 million total is reported by trade press, not by the fund's own page. It is new, so no results have been reported. Loans must be repaid; groups should take independent advice.",src:[SRCQ("Social Investment Business: Flexible Finance Fund (open October 2026)","https://www.sibgroup.org.uk/fund/flexible-finance/","Supports the Beyond government page","provides a package of loan and grant funding and support to UK Black and Racially Minoritised charities and social enterprises based in England"),SRCQ("Charity Times: Grants and loans fund worth £4m launches for global majority led charities (25 October 2026)","https://www.charitytimes.com/ct/Grants-and-loans-fund-worth-4m-launches-for-global-majority-led-charities.php","Supports the Beyond government page","loans of between £50,000 and £1.5m are available alongside grants worth between 50% and 100% of the loan value, capped at £200,000")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"bgunion",h:"Trade unions and professional bodies",intro:"Unions and professional regulators collect some of the most detailed workforce data outside government. This section covers union membership, insecure work, how unions organise Black members, and diversity data from two professional regulators.",points:[
  {h:"Black employees have the highest union membership rate",text:"In 2025, 26.9% of Black or Black British employees in the UK were trade union members, the highest of any ethnic group and above the average of 22.4%. Over half of Black employees worked in health and social work, education or public administration and defence; these industries account for over three-quarters of Black union members.",figure:"26.9%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"UK employees, 2025. Based on the Labour Force Survey, now official statistics in development. The 95% confidence interval for Black employees (24.2% to 29.8%) lies above the intervals for every other ethnic group, including White (22.5% to 23.7%). The industry pattern describes where Black union members work; the source does not say it explains the higher rate.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Business and Trade): Trade union membership, UK, 1995 to 2025: statistical bulletin (28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/trade-union-statistics-2025/trade-union-membership-uk-1995-to-2025-statistical-bulletin","Supports the Beyond government page","The proportion of employees who were trade union members was highest in the Black or Black British ethnic group (26.9%), followed by those with White ethnicity (23.1%)."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Business and Trade): Trade union membership 2025: confidence intervals, Table 1a (May 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a170add65bc5f798327f407/trade-union-membership-2025-confidence-intervals-may-2026.ods","Supports the Beyond government page","Ethnicity: Black or Black British,26.9,24.2,29.8")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Insecure work among Black and minority ethnic workers",text:"TUC analysis found that 16.3% of Black and minority ethnic workers were in insecure work in 2024, compared with 10.8% of White workers. Insecure work here means zero-hours contracts, agency, casual and seasonal work (not fixed-term contracts), and self-employment paid below two-thirds of median earnings.",figure:"16.3% vs 10.8%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"UK. The TUC groups all Black and minority ethnic workers together, so there is no Black-only figure. Its release a year earlier gave 16.9% for 2023; figures differ between releases as data are updated. The TUC is a campaigning body and the analysis is its own, from Labour Force Survey and Family Resources Survey data. The Myths and facts page covers the TUC's 2022 poll on racism at work.",src:[SRCQ("Workplace Journal: TUC research finds 4 million people in insecure work across the UK (7 September 2025)","https://workplacejournal.co.uk/2025/09/tuc-research-finds-4-million-people-in-insecure-work-across-the-uk/","Supports the Beyond government page","In 2024, 16.3% of BME workers were in insecure work compared with 10.8% of white workers."),SRCQ("Personnel Today: One in six ethnic minority workers in insecure jobs (12 August 2024)","https://www.personneltoday.com/hr/ethnic-minority-workers-insecure/","Supports the Beyond government page","one in six black and ethnic minority workers are in insecure jobs, according to the TUC"),SRCQ("TUC: The scale of insecure work in the UK","https://www.tuc.org.uk/cy/node/531535","Supports the Beyond government page","TUC research shows that in 2024 16.3 percent of Black and Minority Ethnic (BME) workers are likely to be in insecure work.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Unions organise Black members in their own structures",text:"Unions such as UNISON run groups for Black members, and the TUC holds a Black Workers' Conference, which in 2026 met in Bournemouth from 24 to 26 April. UNISON uses 'Black' in the political sense, as a shared term for people facing racism.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Union definitions of 'Black' are often wider than the Black or Black British group used in official statistics, so union figures and government figures may not count the same people. The page on what it means to be Black explains political blackness.",src:[SRCQ("UNISON: Black members group (undated web page)","https://www.unison.org.uk/about/what-we-do/fairness-equality/black-members/","Supports the Beyond government page","Using Black is about creating unity in our fight against deep-rooted racism"),SRCQ("POA (the professional trades union for prison, correctional and secure psychiatric workers): POA delegation at the TUC Black Workers' Conference 2026 (July 2026)","https://poauk.org.uk/news-events/news-room/posts/2026/july/poa-delegation-at-the-tuc-black-workers-conference-2026/","Supports the Beyond government page","held at Bournemouth International Conference Centre from Friday 24 April to Sunday 26 April")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Black barristers: 3.6% of the Bar, 1.2% of KCs",text:"In December 2025, 3.6% of the practising Bar (including pupils) were Black or Black British, compared with 5.5% of the UK working-age population. The gap widens with seniority: 1.2% of King's Counsel were Black, against 3.9% of other barristers.",figure:"3.6%",year:"December 2025",disputed:false,note:"Barristers in England and Wales, compared with the UK working-age population. Percentages exclude those who did not give their ethnicity. The regulator notes the gap between King's Counsel and other barristers is widest for those of Black African background (0.4% of KCs against 2.2% of other barristers). The Professions point elsewhere in the hub uses Ministry of Justice figures on a different basis. Solicitors are covered on the Collecting ethnicity data page.",src:[SRCQ("Bar Standards Board: Diversity at the Bar 2025 (March 2026)","https://www.barstandardsboard.org.uk/asset/DD916C89%2D8287%2D442B%2DB1748C91B54B619C/","Supports the Beyond government page","By contrast, there is a smaller proportion of those from Black/Black British backgrounds (3.6% compared to 5.5%)"),SRCQ("Legal Cheek: Six in ten pupil barristers are women, new figures show (29 April 2026)","https://www.legalcheek.com/2026/04/six-in-ten-pupil-barristers-are-women-new-figures-show/","Supports the Beyond government page","Black and Black British barristers make up 3.9% of non-KCs and 1.2% of KCs")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Nursing and midwifery: a third from ethnic minority backgrounds",text:"On 31 March 2026, 33.8% of the 867,935 nurses, midwives and nursing associates on the UK register were from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, up from 32.5% a year earlier. The regulator's 2025 survey found 40% of responding professionals had faced discrimination in the previous year, most often on grounds of ethnicity or age.",figure:"33.8%",year:"March 2026",disputed:false,note:"UK. The register report gives no Black-only figure. The discrimination figure comes from a survey of registrants who chose to respond.",src:[SRCQ("Nursing and Midwifery Council: The NMC Register, 1 April 2025 to 31 March 2026 (UK data report, 2026)","https://www.nmc.org.uk/globalassets/sitedocuments/data-reports/2026/0184ca-data-report-uk-web.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","For the first time, more than a third of registered professionals are from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds (33.8% on 31 March 2026 compared to 32.5% on 31 March 2025)."),SRCQ("National Health Executive: Diversity soars in UK nursing, yet international joiners drop (2025)","https://www.nationalhealthexecutive.com/articles/diversity-soars-uk-nursing-yet-international-joiners-drop-30-whats-going","Supports the Beyond government page","That is just under a third of everybody on the NMC register (32.5 percent, compared to 30.6 percent on 31 March 2024)."),SRCQ("Nursing and Midwifery Council: The NMC Register, 1 April 2025 to 31 March 2026 (UK data report, 2026)","https://www.nmc.org.uk/globalassets/sitedocuments/data-reports/2026/0184ca-data-report-uk-web.pdf","Supports the Beyond government page","40% of the professionals who responded to our annual survey said they had experienced discrimination in the previous year, most often on grounds of ethnicity or age")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black people are 7% of charity trustees under 60, against 5% of that age group.",refs:["bg-bgvol-black-trustees-3-overall-7-among-under-6"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Black barristers are 3.6% of the Bar but 1.2% of King's Counsel.",refs:["bg-bgunion-black-barristers-3-6-of-the-bar-1-2-of-k"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Where Black staff are few, a pay gap report may not show their figures; ask for the breakdown.",refs:["bg-bgemploy-a-black-pay-gap-may-not-show-in-publishe"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"The equality duty follows public money into contracts; build it into what you buy.",refs:["bg-bgemploy-the-equality-duty-follows-public-money-i"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"No register counts Black-led charities, and the foundations that publish figures give no Black-only share.",refs:["bg-bgvol-small-groups-with-thin-reserves", "bg-bgfund-foundations-now-publish-their-own-racial"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"No employer in Great Britain has to publish an ethnicity pay gap; reporting is still voluntary.",refs:["bg-bgemploy-ethnicity-pay-gap-reporting-is-still-vol"]},{t:"Since October 2025, social value must normally be worth at least 10% of the score in central government contracts.",refs:["bg-bgemploy-social-value-in-central-government-contr"]},{t:"Twenty funders found that 5% of the money they audited went to organisations led by and for Black, Asian and minority ethnic communities.",refs:["bg-bgfund-funder-audits-about-5-of-funding-reached"]},{t:"26.9% of Black employees were union members in 2025, the highest of any ethnic group.",refs:["bg-bgunion-black-employees-have-the-highest-union-m"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["society"]={name:"Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity",pfx:"so",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"soold",h:"Older people",intro:"Black Caribbean elders, many of them the Windrush generation, are among the oldest ethnic groups in England and Wales, while Mixed-heritage groups are among the youngest. This section covers later-life income, care given and received, and dementia, and adds to the older people figures above.",points:[
  {h:"Mixed-heritage groups are the youngest; few are aged 65 or over",text:"At Census 2021, people in Mixed or Multiple ethnic groups had a median age of 19, against 40 for England and Wales as a whole. In the Mixed White and Black African group, 48.6% were aged under 16 and only 2.5% were 65 or over. The older age profile of the Black Caribbean group is covered in the older people figures above.",figure:"Median age 19 (Mixed) v 40 (all)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Census Day 21 March 2021, England and Wales. Age profile shapes most comparisons in this section: groups with few older people show lower rates of age-related conditions and benefits. Both sources present ONS census data, so there is effectively one publisher.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group by age and sex, England and Wales: Census 2021 (23 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/articles/ethnicgroupbyageandsexenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Only 2.5% (6,180) of the population who identified as being \"White and Black African” were aged 65 years or over."),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Age groups (31 March 2023)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/demographics/age-groups/latest/","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","people from mixed ethnic groups had the lowest (19 years old)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Windrush generation is now in later life",text:"Many of the people who came to the UK from Commonwealth countries before 1973 are now in later life. By the end of July 2026, 1,717 of the 12,272 claims made to the Windrush Compensation Scheme (14%) had been made on behalf of the estate of someone who had died. In January 2024 a Home Office minister told Parliament that at least 53 claimants had died before receiving compensation, a figure Age UK highlighted in February 2024.",figure:"1,717 estate claims (14%) to July 2026",year:"2019 to 2024",disputed:false,note:"The estate-claim total is calculated by adding the monthly and quarterly counts in Home Office table WCS_02; one person can make several claims, and claims can change category. Estate claims count claims made by estates, not deaths while waiting. The 53 figure was given by a Home Office minister and counts claimants who died before receiving compensation; it does not say the scheme caused those deaths. The National Audit Office counted 118 estate claims (5%) by March 2021. The hub's Windrush pages cover the scandal and the scheme in full.",src:[SRCQ("National Audit Office: Investigation into the Windrush Compensation Scheme (May 2021)","https://www.nao.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Investigation-into-the-Windrush-compensation-scheme-.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","118 (5%) were on behalf of the estate of someone who had passed away"),SRCQ("Age UK: Justice Denied: Reforming the Windrush Compensation Scheme (February 2024)","https://www.ageuk.org.uk/siteassets/documents/reports-and-publications/reports-and-briefings/equality-and-human-rights/id205516-windrush-compensation-report_final.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","At least 53 people have already died waiting for their claims to be decided.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black pensioners: more than twice the White rate of low income",text:"In the three years from April 2022 to March 2025, 29% of UK pensioners in households headed by a Black adult were in relative low income after housing costs, against 12% of pensioners in White-headed households. In 2021, Age UK reported that 44% of Black older households received an occupational pension, against 61% of White older households. The hub's earlier 31% figure (April 2021 to March 2024) comes from DWP data published before DWP revised its method in March 2026. The two figures are not directly comparable, so the difference should not be read as a fall.",figure:"29% v 12%",year:"2022/23 to 2024/25 (3-year average)",disputed:false,note:"UK. Relative low income means below 60% of median household income after housing costs; ethnicity is that of the household head. DWP table 6.5db gives 28.9% and 12.4%. The comparison group is all White households, not White British as in the earlier figure. In March 2026 DWP revised its estimates for 2021/22 to 2023/24 using administrative benefit data, creating a break in the series. The Black pensioner group is small (about 139,000 people), so the estimate is uncertain. Not age-standardised. Age UK's pension figures were published in 2021.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions: Households below average income, FYE 1995 to FYE 2025, pensioners detailed breakdown tables (26 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/households-below-average-income-for-financial-years-ending-1995-to-2025","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Table 6.5db: Percentage of pensioners in relative low-income groups by various family and household characteristics, United Kingdom"),SRCQ("Age UK: Ethnicity and financial disadvantage in later life (May 2021)","https://www.ageuk.org.uk/globalassets/age-uk/documents/reports-and-publications/reports-and-briefings/money-matters/ethincity_and_financial_disadvantge_in_later_life_may_2021.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Older people from Black and Asian ethnic groups are around twice as likely to be living in poverty as White pensioners."),SRCQ("Age UK: Poverty and low income among Black older people (October 2021)","https://www.ageuk.org.uk/discover/2021/october/poverty-and-low-income-among-black-older-people/","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","44% of Black older households receive an occupational pension compared to 61% of White older households.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Unpaid care: similar rates for Black Caribbean and White British women",text:"Taking age into account, 10.3% of Black Caribbean women and 11.3% of Mixed White and Black Caribbean women in England gave unpaid care in 2021, close to 11.0% of White British women. Rates were lower for Black African women (7.6%) and men (5.0%). In a 2023 survey, ethnic minority carers were more likely than White carers to be struggling to make ends meet (34% against 29%).",figure:"10.3%, 11.3% and 7.6% (women, England)",year:"2021 (census); 2023 (survey)",disputed:false,note:"Census figures are age-standardised percentages of usual residents aged 5 and over in England; they count who gives care, not how many hours. The Carers UK figures come from its State of Caring 2023 survey of carers across the UK and group all ethnic minority carers together, not Black carers alone; the briefing does not define the group but quotes a White Irish carer within it.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Unpaid care and protected characteristics, England and Wales: Census 2021 (24 April 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/socialcare/articles/unpaidcareandprotectedcharacteristicsenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","The ethnicity of unpaid carers largely follows the ethnic-group distributions in the whole population."),SRCQ("Carers UK: Updated briefing on ethnic minority carers (February 2024)","https://www.carersuk.org/briefings/the-experiences-of-ethnic-minority-carers-updated-briefing/","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","34% of ethnic minority carers said they were struggling to make ends meet, compared with 29% of White carers.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black workers are 23% of the adult social care workforce",text:"In 2025/26, 23% of the adult social care workforce in England were from Black, African, Caribbean or Black British ethnicities, against 6% of the economically active population and 4% of the whole population. Of adults receiving long-term care and support, 4.5% were from Black ethnic groups in 2021, and they made up a larger share of people supported in community or prison settings (5.3%) than in residential care (2.2%).",figure:"23% of workforce v 6% of economically active",year:"2025/26 (workforce); 2021 (people supported)",disputed:false,note:"England. Comparators are from Skills for Care's Chart 65: population from Census 2021, economically active from the Labour Force Survey 2025/26. The people-supported figures come from NHS data via Ethnicity facts and figures and are not age-standardised; the Black population is younger than average. Skills for Care's 2026 race equality report found global majority staff as a whole 50% more likely to enter formal disciplinary processes; it does not give a Black-only figure. The hub's earlier workforce point uses an older 'about a third ethnic minority' figure.",src:[SRCQ("Skills for Care: The state of the adult social care sector and workforce in England (2026)","https://www.skillsforcare.org.uk/Adult-Social-Care-Workforce-Data/workforceintelligence/resources/Reports/National/The-state-of-the-adult-social-care-sector-and-workforce-in-England-2026.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","over one in five were people of Black, African, Caribbean or Black British ethnicities (23%)"),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures: Adult social care – long term support (published 7 February 2022)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/health/social-care/adult-social-care-long-term-support/latest","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","the percentage of adults receiving long term support who were from black ethnic groups went up from 3.8% to 4.5%"),SRCQ("Skills for Care: New report highlights inequity faced by global majority colleagues working in social care (21 May 2026)","https://skillsforcare-newsroom.prgloo.com/news/new-skills-for-care-report-highlights-inequity-faced-by-people-from-global-majority-colleagues-working-in-social-care","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Global majority employees were 50% more likely to enter formal disciplinary processes.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Dementia: higher diagnosis rates and younger deaths",text:"A study of UK GP records for 2007 to 2015 found the rate of dementia diagnosis was 25% higher in Black women and 28% higher in Black men than in White women and men, after adjusting for age and other factors. A second study, of people aged 65 and over in England, found Black people with dementia died on average 2.7 years younger than White people with dementia. Alzheimer's Society says support is typically accessed later by Black communities in the UK.",figure:"25% (women) and 28% (men) higher diagnosis rate",year:"2007 to 2015; 1997 to 2018",disputed:false,note:"Both studies measure recorded diagnoses, which depend on who is assessed; the first estimates that Black men developing dementia were less likely than White men to be diagnosed. The age-at-death gap is an average difference and may partly reflect the younger age profile of Black older people. The two studies share an author. The evidence is from research studies and a charity, not official statistics; the Alzheimer's Society page is undated. A higher rate is not evidence of cause.",src:[SRCQ("Clinical Epidemiology (Pham TM and others): Trends in dementia diagnosis rates in UK ethnic groups: analysis of UK primary care data (8 August 2018)","https://www.dovepress.com/trends-in-dementia-diagnosis-rates-in-uk-ethnic-groups-analysis-of-uk--peer-reviewed-article-CLEP","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Compared with white women, the incidence of dementia diagnosis was 18% lower among Asian women (adjusted incidence rate ratio (IRR) 0.82, 95% CI 0.72–0.95) and 25% higher among black women"),SRCQ("Alzheimer's & Dementia (Mukadam N and others): Incidence, age at diagnosis and survival with dementia across ethnic groups in England (September 2022)","https://doi.org/10.1002/alz.12774","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","South Asian and Black people with dementia had a younger age of death than White participants"),SRCQ("Alzheimer's Society: Improving dementia and Alzheimer's support in Black communities (30 September 2020)","https://www.alzheimers.org.uk/blog/improving-dementia-support-black-communities","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","For Black communities in the UK there is a greater risk of dementia, but support is typically accessed later in the journey.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"sodis",h:"Disabled people",intro:"Disability rates in Black and Mixed groups depend heavily on their younger age profiles, so this section uses age-specific figures where they exist. Mental Health Act detentions and sickle cell in equality law are covered elsewhere on the hub, so this section links to them rather than repeating them.",points:[
  {h:"Within each age group, Black African people report less disability",text:"In England in 2021, 6.6% of Black African people aged 16 to 49 reported a disability, against 15.0% of White British people of the same age, and 30.9% against 35.3% at 65 and over. Black Caribbean rates were close to White British rates up to age 64 and higher at 65 and over (39.6%), and Mixed White and Black Caribbean rates were higher in every adult age group.",figure:"6.6% v 15.0% (aged 16 to 49)",year:"2021 (census); 2024/25 (survey)",disputed:false,note:"Census disability is self-reported against the Equality Act definition; figures are age-specific, England. A UK survey for 2024/25 gives a similar picture for ages 16 to 64: Black 17.7%, Mixed 25.5%, White 26.0% (calculated as disabled divided by disabled plus non-disabled from DWP table LMS007; not age-standardised). Differences in reported disability are not evidence of cause. See also the age-standardised 22.4% figure for Mixed White and Black Caribbean people above.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Protected characteristics by disability status, England and Wales: Census 2021 (17 May 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/healthandsocialcare/disability/articles/protectedcharacteristicsbydisabilitystatusenglandandwalescensus2021/latest","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","disability prevalence in each ethnic group increased with age and was lowest for younger people."),SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions: The employment of disabled people 2025, supplementary tables (24 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/the-employment-of-disabled-people-2025","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Table LMS007: Number and proportion of people by disability and employment status, ethnicity and year")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Disabled Black adults: 52.8% in work",text:"In 2024/25, 52.8% of disabled Black adults aged 16 to 64 in the UK were in employment, against 74.5% of non-disabled Black adults, a gap of 21.7 percentage points. DWP notes the gap for White adults is wider (28.7 points), partly because non-disabled White adults have the highest employment rate (84.3%). For Mixed groups, 49.1% of disabled adults were in work.",figure:"52.8% v 74.5%",year:"April 2024 to March 2025",disputed:false,note:"Annual Population Survey, UK, ages 16 to 64; not age-standardised. The 'BME' combined figures from the TUC are covered by the hub's existing TUC point and are not Black-only.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions: The employment of disabled people 2025 (updated 24 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/the-employment-of-disabled-people-2025/the-employment-of-disabled-people-2025","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Disabled people of Black/African/Caribbean/Black British have the largest disability employment gap (21.7 percentage points) for any ethnic minority group.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Disability benefits by ethnicity; no ethnicity data for Access to Work",text:"In the three years to 2024/25, 7% of benefit units headed by a Black adult received the daily living part of Personal Independence Payment, against 9% of White-headed units and 8% overall. Access to Work statistics are published by age, gender, medical condition, region, employer size and employment status, but not by ethnicity.",figure:"7% v 9% (PIP daily living)",year:"2022/23 to 2024/25 (3-year average); 2024/25",disputed:false,note:"Family Resources Survey, UK, by ethnic group of the head of the benefit unit; not age-standardised, and White-headed units are older on average. Lower receipt is not evidence of lower need or of barriers to claiming. Without ethnicity in Access to Work data, it is not possible to say whether Black disabled workers use the scheme in proportion. Both sources are DWP; no other publisher produces these figures.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions: Family Resources Survey FYE 2025, income and state support tables (2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2024-to-2025","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Benefit units by state support receipt and ethnic group of head, average of 2022 to 2023, 2023 to 2024 and 2024 to 2025, United Kingdom"),SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions: Access to Work statistics, April 2007 to March 2025 (updated 29 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/access-to-work-statistics-april-2007-to-march-2025/access-to-work-statistics-april-2007-to-march-2025","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","For further statistics on Access to Work approval volumes including breakdowns by age, gender, primary medical condition, region, employer size and employment status")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"About 17,000 people in England live with sickle cell disease",text:"Government and NHS England estimate that about 17,000 people in England live with sickle cell disease, with about 250 new cases a year. The national patient safety investigator puts the UK figure at about 15,000, with around 300 babies born with the condition each year and about 1 in 79 babies carrying the trait. It mainly affects people of African and Caribbean heritage, but anyone can inherit it.",figure:"About 17,000 (England)",year:"2023 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"The estimates differ in date, geography and source (the 15,000 UK figure cites the Sickle Cell Society), so treat both as approximate. The hub's DNA and genomes page covers sickle cell trait and malaria.",src:[SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care and NHS England: Sickle cell patients to get better treatment after £9 million boost (13 June 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/sickle-cell-patients-to-get-better-treatment-after-9m-boost","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","In England, there are around 17,000 people living with sickle cell disease - an inherited blood disorder, with 250 new cases a year."),SRCQ("Healthcare Safety Investigation Branch (now HSSIB): Management of sickle cell crisis, investigation report (22 June 2023)","https://hssib.org.uk/patient-safety-investigations/management-of-sickle-cell-crisis/investigation-report","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","In the UK, approximately 1 in 79 babies carry the sickle cell trait, and around 300 babies are born with sickle cell disease each year.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The NHS response: training, funding and a new gene therapy",text:"In 2023 the national patient safety investigator recommended that NHS England set minimum training for staff caring for people in sickle cell crisis. In 2025 NICE made the gene-editing therapy exagamglogene autotemcel (Casgevy) available for severe sickle cell disease through a managed access agreement. NHS England also funded £9 million to expand apheresis (blood exchange) services.",figure:"£9 million",year:"2023 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"Casgevy is for people aged 12 and over with severe sickle cell disease and is available while more evidence is collected. These are commitments and decisions; their effect on patients' experience of care is not shown by these sources.",src:[SRCQ("Healthcare Safety Investigation Branch (now HSSIB): Management of sickle cell crisis, investigation report (22 June 2023)","https://hssib.org.uk/patient-safety-investigations/management-of-sickle-cell-crisis/investigation-report","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","HSIB recommends that NHS England reviews the existing training and competence requirements within sickle cell care provision and specifies the minimum training requirements and content for staff."),SRCQ("NICE: Exagamglogene autotemcel for treating severe sickle cell disease in people 12 years and over, TA1044 (26 February 2025)","https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta1044","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Evidence-based recommendations on exagamglogene autotemcel (Casgevy) for treating severe sickle cell disease in people 12 years and over."),SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care and NHS England: Sickle cell patients to get better treatment after £9 million boost (13 June 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/sickle-cell-patients-to-get-better-treatment-after-9m-boost","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Backed by a £9 million investment from NHS England, the government is set to transform apheresis services")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"solgbt",h:"LGBT+ people",intro:"Black and Mixed-heritage LGBT+ people can meet racism, homophobia and transphobia at the same time. National data on this overlap are thin, so this section sets out what the census, government surveys and community organisations show, and where the gaps are.",points:[
  {h:"Sexual orientation by ethnic group at Census 2021",text:"In England and Wales in 2021, about 1.8% of Black African and 2.1% of Black Caribbean people aged 16 and over identified as gay, lesbian, bisexual or another minority sexual orientation, against about 3.1% of the whole population on the same basis (ONS's published overall figure is 3.2%). The figure was about 5.8% in the Mixed White and Black Caribbean group and 5.7% in the Mixed White and Black African group. ONS says the higher Mixed figures are likely to be related to these groups' younger age profiles.",figure:"About 1.8% (Black African) v 3.1% (all, same method)",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Group figures are the sum of ONS's three rounded percentages (gay or lesbian, bisexual, other), so each may differ from the unrounded total by up to 0.15 points. The difference between the Black African and Black Caribbean figures is within that margin. Not age-standardised. Percentages are of all usual residents aged 16 and over, including the 7.5% who did not answer the question. Only ONS publishes census figures on sexual orientation by ethnic group.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Sexual orientation, further personal characteristics, England and Wales: Census 2021 (1 November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/sexuality/articles/sexualorientationfurtherpersonalcharacteristicsenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","1.5 million (3.2%) identified as lesbian, gay, bisexual or another sexual orientation (LGB+)"),SRCQ("ONS: Figure 6 data, LGB+ sexual orientation by ethnic group, Census 2021 (1 November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/visualisations/dvc2745/Fig06/datadownload.xlsx","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Percentage of people aged 16 years and over who identified with each ethnic group, by LGB+ sexual orientation, England and Wales, Census 2021")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Gender identity by ethnicity: figures to treat with great caution",text:"Census 2021 recorded 1.6% of people in Black ethnic groups as trans, against 0.3% of White British people. ONS later found patterns suggesting some people with lower English proficiency may not have understood the question as intended, and in 2024 the estimates became official statistics in development. ONS says it is less confident in breakdowns by characteristics linked to English proficiency, such as ethnicity.",figure:"1.6% v 0.3% (unreliable)",year:"2021 (census); 2023 to 2025 (reviews)",disputed:true,note:"ONS cannot say whether the census overestimates or underestimates the trans population. These ethnic breakdowns should not be used as estimates of the number of Black trans people. Sexual orientation estimates were not affected and remain accredited official statistics.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Quality of Census 2021 gender identity data (13 November 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/genderidentity/articles/qualityofcensus2021genderidentitydata/2023-11-13","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","These patterns reflect those respondents with lower proficiency in English and characteristics likely associated with English language proficiency such as ethnicity and country of birth."),SRCQ("Office for Statistics Regulation: Conclusions of OSR's review of gender identity estimates from Census 2021 (letter, September 2024)","https://osr.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Ed_Humperson_Emma_Rourke_Conclusions_of_OSRs_review_of_gender_identity-_estimates_for_England_and_Wales_from_C.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","OSR supports your proposal to publish the Census 2021 gender identity statistics for England and Wales as official statistics in development rather than accredited official statistics."),SRCQ("ONS: Sexual orientation and gender identity quality information for Census 2021 (last revised 8 April 2025)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/sexuality/methodologies/sexualorientationandgenderidentityqualityinformationforcensus2021","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","we are less confident in the reliability of breakdowns by other variables.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Racism in LGBT spaces and pressure to change",text:"In a Stonewall survey of LGBT people in Britain carried out in 2017 and published in 2018, three in five Black LGBT respondents (61%) said they had faced discrimination or poor treatment in their local LGBT community because of their ethnicity. In the government's 2017 National LGBT Survey, 13% of Black cisgender respondents had undergone or been offered conversion therapy, against 7% of White respondents; among Black trans respondents the figure was 28%.",figure:"61%; 13% v 7%",year:"2017 to 2018",disputed:false,note:"Both are online surveys, not representative samples. The National LGBT Survey had 108,100 valid responses, of whom 1% were Black, so the Black trans figure rests on a small number of people. The hub's existing 51% figure covers all Black, Asian and minority ethnic LGBT people.",src:[SRCQ("Stonewall (with YouGov): LGBT in Britain – Home and Communities (2018)","https://www.stonewall.org.uk/resources/lgbt-britain-home-and-communities-2018","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","This number rises to three in five black LGBT people (61 per cent)."),SRCQ("Government Equalities Office: National LGBT Survey research report (July 2018)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5b3b2d1eed915d33e245fbe3/LGBT-survey-research-report.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Black/African/Caribbean/Black British (13%) and Asian/Asian British (14%) respondents, and respondents belonging to an ‘other’ ethnic group (15%), for example, were much more likely than White (7%) respondents")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Hate crime: no official count where race and sexuality meet",text:"Police in England and Wales recorded 21,407 sexual orientation and 3,955 transgender hate crimes in the year to March 2026. The Home Office reports victim ethnicity only for racially or religiously aggravated offences, so official figures cannot show how many victims were Black LGBT+ people. In Stonewall's 2017 survey, a third of Black, Asian and minority ethnic LGBT people had experienced a hate crime or incident in the previous year, against one in five White LGBT people.",figure:"21,407 and 3,955",year:"April 2025 to March 2026; 2017",disputed:false,note:"Police recorded crime reflects reporting and recording practice as well as offending; a change to the Metropolitan Police's recording system in 2024 limits comparisons over time. The Stonewall figure combines all ethnic minority LGBT people, from a YouGov survey of more than 5,000 LGBT people in Britain carried out in 2017.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Hate crime, England and Wales, year ending March 2026 (8 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hate-crime-england-and-wales-year-ending-march-2026/hate-crime-england-and-wales-year-ending-march-2026","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","There were 21,407 sexual orientation hate crimes recorded by the police in the year ending March 2026, a 1% fall compared with the previous year (21,527 offences)."),SRCQ("Stonewall (with YouGov): LGBT in Britain – Hate Crime and Discrimination (2018)","https://www.stonewall.org.uk/uploads/files/lgbt_in_britain_hate_crime.pdf","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Black, Asian and minority ethnic LGBT people are also disproportionately affected, with a third having experienced a hate crime or incident in the last year, compared to one in five white LGBT people.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"UK Black Pride at 20",text:"UK Black Pride marked 20 years in 2025 with an exhibition at Queer Britain, produced with the organisation and its co-founder, Phyll Opoku-Gyimah (Lady Phyll). London Museum describes her as 'a pioneering LGBTQ+ rights and anti-racism campaigner'.",figure:"20 years (2005 to 2025)",year:"2005 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"The hub's existing points cover its start in 2005, the first event in Southend-on-Sea and its 'Europe's largest' self-description; UK Black Pride's own history also says it was officially launched in 2006. London Museum calls it the world's largest, a wider claim the organisation does not make. There is no official statistical source on the organisation.",src:[SRCQ("London Museum: Lady Phyll: Activist and co-founder of UK Black Pride (undated)","https://londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/lady-phyll-activist-co-founder-of-uk-black-pride","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","She co-founded UK Black Pride, now the world’s largest celebration for LGBTQ+ people of African, Asian, Caribbean, Middle Eastern and Latin American descent."),SRCQ("Queer Britain: 20 Years of UK Black Pride exhibition (4 June to 31 August 2025)","https://queerbritain.org.uk/history-2025-uk-black-pride","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Produced in collaboration with UK Black Pride and their co-founder, Lady Phyll, this exhibition celebrated 20 years of what is now the biggest LGBTQ+ event for Black and global majority people."),SRCQ("UK Black Pride: Our history (undated)","https://www.ukblackpride.org.uk/history","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","UK Black Pride, established in 2005, began as a small gathering organised by Phyll Opoku-Gyimah (Lady Phyll) and other Black Lesbian activists.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Health: poorer mental health and gaps in HIV prevention",text:"In a Stonewall survey carried out in 2017 and published in 2018, 62% of Black, Asian and minority ethnic LGBT respondents in Britain said they had experienced depression in the previous year, against 52% of all LGBT respondents. In 2024, 0.6% of gay and bisexual men of Black ethnicity tested for HIV in sexual health services in England tested positive, against 0.2% of all gay and bisexual men tested. Among those with a need for HIV prevention medicine (PrEP), uptake was similar for ethnic minority and White gay and bisexual men (77.8% and 79.4%).",figure:"62%; 0.6% v 0.2%",year:"2018; 2024",disputed:false,note:"Stonewall's YouGov survey is not a random sample, combines all ethnic minority LGBT people and records self-reported depression. HIV test positivity reflects who comes forward for testing, not how common HIV is in the population. The PrEP figure combines all ethnic minority gay and bisexual men. Government's PrEP roadmap (2024) sets out actions to widen fair access. Disparities are not evidence of cause.",src:[SRCQ("Stonewall (with YouGov): LGBT in Britain – Health (2018)","https://www.stonewall.org.uk/resources/lgbt-britain-health-2018","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","LGBT people aged 18-24 (68 per cent), and Black, Asian and minority ethnic LGBT people (62 per cent) are also more likely to experience depression in the last year."),SRCQ("UK Health Security Agency: HIV testing, PrEP, new HIV diagnoses and care outcomes for people accessing HIV services: 2025 report (updated 15 April 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/hiv-annual-data/hiv-testing-prep-new-hiv-diagnoses-and-care-outcomes-for-people-accessing-hiv-services-2025-report","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","Among gay and bisexual men of any Black ethnicity, the number of men tested increased by 4% from 8,381 in 2023 to 8,694 in 2024, while proportion testing positive remained stable at 0.6%"),SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care: Roadmap for meeting the PrEP needs of those at significant risk of HIV (updated 21 February 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/roadmap-for-meeting-the-prep-needs-of-those-at-significant-risk-of-hiv/roadmap-for-meeting-the-prep-needs-of-those-at-significant-risk-of-hiv","Supports the Intersections: age, disability, sexual orientation and gender identity page","there is potential to improve access, uptake and use of HIV PrEP for all population groups at high risk of HIV")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"UK Black Pride marked 20 years in 2025.",refs:["so-solgbt-uk-black-pride-at-20"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"61% of Black LGBT respondents faced discrimination in their local LGBT community because of their ethnicity.",refs:["so-solgbt-racism-in-lgbt-spaces-and-pressure-to-ch"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Many of the Windrush generation are now in later life; 14% of compensation claims were made for people who had died.",refs:["so-soold-the-windrush-generation-is-now-in-later-"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Within each age group, Black African people report less disability, so compare like with like.",refs:["so-sodis-within-each-age-group-black-african-peop"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Official hate crime figures cannot show how many victims were Black LGBT+ people.",refs:["so-solgbt-hate-crime-no-official-count-where-race-"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"29% of Black pensioners were in low income after housing costs, more than twice the White rate of 12%.",refs:["so-soold-black-pensioners-more-than-twice-the-whi"]},{t:"Black workers are 23% of the adult social care workforce in England.",refs:["so-soold-black-workers-are-23-of-the-adult-social"]},{t:"52.8% of disabled Black adults are in work, against 74.5% of non-disabled Black adults.",refs:["so-sodis-disabled-black-adults-52-8-in-work"]},{t:"About 17,000 people in England live with sickle cell disease.",refs:["so-sodis-about-17-000-people-in-england-live-with"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["place"]={name:"Place: the four nations and the regions",pfx:"pl",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"plengland",h:"England's regions and places",intro:"Black communities live in every region of England, but in very different numbers and with different histories. This section looks at where people live today, how that is changing, and some of the places with the longest-established Black communities.",points:[
  {h:"Outside London and the West Midlands, regions are under 3% Black",text:"At Census 2021, 4.2% of people in England (2.4 million) identified with a Black ethnic group. Outside London and the West Midlands, the share was 2.9% in the East of England, 2.7% in the East Midlands, 2.4% in the South East, 2.3% in the North West, 2.1% in Yorkshire and The Humber, 1.2% in the South West and 1.0% in the North East.",figure:"4.2% of England (2.4 million)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), England and its regions",disputed:false,note:"Regional shares are of all usual residents in each region. The GOV.UK table is built from ONS census data, so the two sources share one underlying count.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Black, Black British, Caribbean or African\" was 4.2% (2.4 million)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Regional ethnic diversity (2021 Census data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","London (13.5%) and the West Midlands (4.5%) had the highest percentage of people who identified as black – the North East (1.0%) and Wales (0.9%) had the lowest"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Areas of England and Wales by ethnicity, table (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/areas-of-england-and-wales-by-ethnicity-table.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"East of England\",\"6.4\",\"2.9\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Lewisham, Southwark and Lambeth have the highest Black shares",text:"At Census 2021, Black residents made up 26.8% of the population in Lewisham, 25.1% in Southwark, 24.0% in Lambeth and 22.6% in Croydon. These were the four highest shares of any local authority in England and Wales. Croydon had the largest Black population of any London borough, at about 88,400 people.",figure:"26.8% (Lewisham)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), local authorities in England and Wales",disputed:false,note:"Calculated from the GOV.UK Census 2021 local authority file (331 local authorities in England and Wales, ranked by Black percentage); the Croydon count is rounded from 88,441. The file labels each local authority percentage as '% of region'.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Population by ethnicity and local authority, Census 2021 (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/population-by-ethnicity-and-local-authority-2021.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Lewisham\",\"E09000023\",\"Black\",\"ONS 2021 5+1\",\"80473\",\"300553\",\"2409283\",\"26.8\""),SRCQ("Southwark Council: Census 2021 profile – ethnicity, identity, language and religion (January 2023)","https://www.southwark.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2025-02/census_2021_profile_-_ethnicity_identity_language_and_religion.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Around 77,300 or one-quarter (25%) of Southwark’s residents identified their ethnicity to be Black, Black British, Caribbean or African."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Population by ethnicity and local authority, Census 2021 (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/population-by-ethnicity-and-local-authority-2021.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Croydon\",\"E09000008\",\"Black\",\"ONS 2021 5+1\",\"88441\",\"390724\",\"2409283\",\"22.6\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black shares grew in Birmingham, Manchester, Leicester and Newcastle",text:"Between 2011 and 2021, the Black share of residents rose from 9.0% to 11.0% in Birmingham, from 8.6% to 11.9% in Manchester, from 6.2% to 7.8% in Leicester and from 1.8% to 3.3% in Newcastle upon Tyne. Birmingham had about 125,800 Black residents, the largest number of any local authority in England and Wales. Across Greater Manchester, the Black population grew by 81.0% over the decade.",figure:"11.9% (Manchester, 2021)",year:"Census 2011 and Census 2021, local authorities in England",disputed:false,note:"Calculated: Birmingham's rank by number is from the GOV.UK Census 2021 local authority file. The Greater Manchester growth figure (2011 to 2021) is the combined authority's analysis of census data. The North East had the lowest Black share of any English region in 2021 (1.0%).",src:[SRCQ("ONS: How life has changed in Birmingham: Census 2021 (first published 8 December 2022, updated 19 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/visualisations/censusareachanges/E08000025/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","while 11.0% identified their ethnic group within the \"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" category (compared with 9.0% the previous decade)"),SRCQ("ONS: How life has changed in Manchester: Census 2021 (first published 8 December 2022, updated 19 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/visualisations/censusareachanges/E08000003/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","while 11.9% identified their ethnic group within the \"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" category (compared with 8.6% the previous decade)"),SRCQ("ONS: How life has changed in Leicester: Census 2021 (first published 8 December 2022, updated 19 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/visualisations/censusareachanges/E06000016/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","while 7.8% identified their ethnic group within the \"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" category (compared with 6.2% the previous decade)"),SRCQ("ONS: How life has changed in Newcastle upon Tyne: Census 2021 (first published 8 December 2022, updated 19 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/visualisations/censusareachanges/E08000021/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","while 3.3% identified their ethnic group within the \"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" category (compared with 1.8% the previous decade)"),SRCQ("Greater Manchester Combined Authority: Census 2021 briefing – ethnicity (2023)","https://www.greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/media/8179/census-2021-briefing_ethnicity_final-v6.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Other ethnic group saw the highest growth (up 144.3%) between 2011 and 2021, followed by the Black population (up 81.0%)."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Population by ethnicity and local authority, Census 2021 (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/population-by-ethnicity-and-local-authority-2021.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Birmingham\",\"E08000025\",\"Black\",\"ONS 2021 5+1\",\"125760\",\"1144922\",\"2409283\",\"11.0\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Fewer than 1 in 100 people in rural England are Black",text:"At Census 2021, about 52,400 people living in rural settlements in England identified with a Black ethnic group. That is 0.6% of the rural population and 2.2% of all Black people in England. Allerdale, Mid Devon and Torridge had Black shares of 0.1%, the lowest of any local authority in England and Wales.",figure:"52,400 (0.6% of rural residents)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), England",disputed:false,note:"Rural settlements are defined by the 2021 output area rural-urban classification. Defra's text rounds the shares to 1% and 2%; its Table E-3 gives 0.6% and 2.2%. Calculated: the lowest-share council areas are from the GOV.UK Census 2021 local authority file. Council area figures cover whole local authorities, including any towns within them, and use 2021 boundaries (Allerdale has since been replaced by Cumberland).",src:[SRCQ("Defra: Statistical Digest of Rural England – 1 Population (January 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6966372399fbdc498faecd7c/1_Population_14_01_26.odt","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","52,400 people living in rural settlements identified as “Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African” at Census 2021; this was equivalent to 1% of the rural population"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Population by ethnicity and local authority, Census 2021 (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/population-by-ethnicity-and-local-authority-2021.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Torridge\",\"E07000046\",\"Black\",\"ONS 2021 5+1\",\"75\",\"68114\",\"2409283\",\"0.1\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Liverpool's Black community dates back to the 18th century",text:"National Museums Liverpool describes Liverpool's Black community as the oldest in Europe, with settlers in the 1750s including sailors, freed enslaved people and sons of African rulers studying in the city. The National Lottery Heritage Fund describes it as Britain's oldest Black community, dating to the 1730s. At Census 2021, 3.5% of Liverpool's residents identified with a Black ethnic group.",figure:"3.5% (Liverpool, 2021)",year:"1730s to 2021",disputed:true,note:"Claims to be the 'oldest' Black community are contested: the two sources differ on Europe or Britain and on the 1730s or 1750s, and neither sets out evidence comparing Liverpool with other places. National Museums Liverpool uses 'Black' in its trail to mean people with African heritage.",src:[SRCQ("National Museums Liverpool: Liverpool Black community trail, Museum of Liverpool (PDF uploaded January 2020)","https://images.liverpoolmuseums.org.uk/2020-01/Liverpool-Black-community-trail.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Liverpool Black community is the oldest in Europe. In the 1750s Black settlers included sailors, freed slaves and student sons of African rulers."),SRCQ("National Lottery Heritage Fund: Discovering Black heritage in Toxteth, Liverpool (L8 Untold project)","https://www.heritagefund.org.uk/projects/l8-untold","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Liverpool is home to Britain's oldest black community, dating to the 1730's"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Population by ethnicity and local authority, Census 2021 (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/population-by-ethnicity-and-local-authority-2021.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Liverpool\",\"E08000012\",\"Black\",\"ONS 2021 5+1\",\"16964\",\"486088\",\"2409283\",\"3.5\"")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Bristol's African Caribbean community and St Pauls",text:"According to PortCities Bristol, most of the city's African-Caribbean community came to Bristol in the 1950s. Members of the African Caribbean community in St Pauls were essential to the 1963 Bristol Bus Boycott, and activists from the boycott went on to found St Pauls Carnival. At Census 2021, 5.9% of Bristol's residents identified with a Black ethnic group, compared with 6.0% in 2011.",figure:"5.9% (Bristol, 2021)",year:"1950s to 2021",disputed:false,note:"The PortCities Bristol page is undated, and its population estimate predates Census 2021.",src:[SRCQ("ONS: How life has changed in Bristol: Census 2021 (first published 8 December 2022, updated 19 January 2023)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/visualisations/censusareachanges/E06000023/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","while 5.9% identified their ethnic group within the \"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" category (compared with 6.0% the previous decade)"),SRCQ("University of Bristol: Old Market and St Pauls (Explore Bristol)","https://bristol.ac.uk/city/explore-bristol/old-market-and-st-pauls","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Members of St Pauls' African Caribbean community were essential to the Bristol Bus Boycott in 1963."),SRCQ("PortCities Bristol (Discovering Bristol): African-Caribbean people in Bristol – the 19th century until the present day (undated)","https://www.discoveringbristol.org.uk/slavery/after-slavery/bristol-in-black-and-white/african-caribbean-bristol/19th-century-to-date/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Most came to the city in the 1950s.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"plwales",h:"Wales",intro:"Wales has a smaller Black population than England, concentrated in its cities, and one of the oldest multi-ethnic communities in Britain. The Welsh Government has set itself the aim of making Wales an anti-racist nation by 2030.",points:[
  {h:"28,000 people in Wales identified as Black in 2021",text:"At Census 2021, 28,000 usual residents of Wales (0.9%) identified with the Black, Black Welsh, Black British, Caribbean or African group, up from 0.6% in 2011. Wales and the North East of England had the lowest Black shares of any region or nation in England and Wales.",figure:"28,000 (0.9%)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), Wales",disputed:false,note:"Figures are rounded by the publishers. The Welsh Government lists the category as 'Black, Black Welsh, Black British, Caribbean or African'; ONS lists the same group as 'Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African'.",src:[SRCQ("Welsh Government: Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion in Wales (Census 2021) (29 November 2022)","https://www.gov.wales/ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion-wales-census-2021-html","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","28,000 usual residents in Wales identified within the high-level category “Black, Black Welsh, Black British, Caribbean or African” (0.9% of the population, compared to 0.6% in 2011)."),SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African\" was 0.9% (28,000)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Regional ethnic diversity (2021 Census data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","the North East (1.0%) and Wales (0.9%) had the lowest")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Cardiff has the highest Black share in Wales, at 3.8%",text:"Cardiff had the highest share of residents in Black ethnic groups of any Welsh local authority in 2021, at 3.8%, or about 13,900 people. Outside Cardiff, Newport had the largest shares for the Black ethnic groups.",figure:"3.8% (Cardiff)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), local authorities in Wales",disputed:false,note:"The Cardiff count is rounded from 13,896 in the GOV.UK local authority file.",src:[SRCQ("Welsh Government: Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion in Wales (Census 2021) (29 November 2022)","https://www.gov.wales/ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion-wales-census-2021-html","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Cardiff had the highest proportions of people identifying within the high-level categories “Asian, Asian British or Asian Welsh” (9.7%), “Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African” (3.8%)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures (Cabinet Office): Population by ethnicity and local authority, Census 2021 (CSV download)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/uk-population-by-ethnicity/national-and-regional-populations/regional-ethnic-diversity/latest/downloads/population-by-ethnicity-and-local-authority-2021.csv","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Cardiff\",\"W06000015\",\"Black\",\"ONS 2021 5+1\",\"13896\",\"362310\",\"2409283\",\"3.8\""),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion in Wales (Census 2021) (29 November 2022)","https://www.gov.wales/ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion-wales-census-2021-html","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The largest proportions for the remaining ethnic groups within the high-level categories “Asian, Asian Welsh or Asian British”, “Black, Black Welsh, Black British, Caribbean or African”")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"'Black Welsh' was a new census option in Wales in 2021",text:"The 2021 census in Wales offered 'Black Welsh' and 'Asian Welsh' within the ethnic group question for the first time. These options appeared on the questionnaire in Wales only.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Welsh Government: Ethnic group, national identity, language and religion in Wales (Census 2021) (29 November 2022)","https://www.gov.wales/ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion-wales-census-2021-html","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Asian Welsh\" and \"Black Welsh\" ethnic groups were included on the census questionnaire in Wales only, these categories were new for 2021."),SRCQ("ONS: Ethnic group, England and Wales: Census 2021 (29 November 2022)","https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/culturalidentity/ethnicity/bulletins/ethnicgroupenglandandwales/census2021","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Asian Welsh\" and \"Black Welsh\" ethnic groups were included on the census questionnaire in Wales only, these categories were new for 2021.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Anti-racist Wales Action Plan sets a goal for 2030",text:"The Welsh Government published the Anti-racist Wales Action Plan in 2022, with a vision for 2030, and refreshed it in November 2024. In its culture, heritage and sport chapter, the 2024 update sets actions to require funded organisations to meet anti-racism performance requirements, which may be set through remit letters, award letters and other conditions of grant, and to require public bodies to establish highly inclusive, anti-racist working environments with anti-racism training. Its health chapter reports that NHS Wales health boards, trusts and special health authorities have each formulated anti-racism action plans.",figure:"",year:"2022, updated November 2024",disputed:false,note:"The plan is government policy, not legislation, and sits alongside the public sector equality duty in the Equality Act 2010. The plan's own page gives 20 July 2022 as its first publication date, while later documents say June 2022.",src:[SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan – introduction (first published 20 July 2022)","https://www.gov.wales/introduction-anti-racist-wales-html","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Plan sets a vision for 2030."),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan: 2024 update (5 November 2024)","https://www.gov.wales/anti-racist-wales-action-plan-2024-update-html","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Require public bodies to establish working environments which are highly inclusive and anti-racist, including anti-racism training as a core component of professional learning and development."),SRCQ("Bridgend County Borough Council: Anti Racist Wales Action Plan update, Cabinet Committee Equalities (6 November 2024)","https://democratic.bridgend.gov.uk/documents/s33772/ARWAP%20report%20Nov%2024.pdf?LLL=0","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The vision is for Wales to be an anti-racist nation by 2030.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"A Senedd committee found gaps in how the plan is delivered",text:"A Senedd committee inquiry, held from March 2023 to February 2024, welcomed the plan's scale and vision. Several councils and other bodies said they were not clear about their role in delivering it, and the plan does not include key measurable targets. The committee concluded that the 2030 aim would be undermined if the plan did not bring together those who can drive change, provide the resources needed and set targets to measure progress.",figure:"",year:"2023 to 2024",disputed:false,note:"Responding to the committee's recommendation to publish a yearly breakdown of funding, the Welsh Government said this was 'impossible' because the plan cuts across many policy areas.",src:[SRCQ("Senedd Research: The path to an anti-racist Wales: closing the implementation gap (2024, ahead of the 12 June plenary debate)","https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/the-path-to-an-anti-racist-wales-closing-the-implementation-gap/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","the Welsh Government’s efforts to achieve an anti-racist Wales by 2030 will be undermined if the Plan:"),SRCQ("Bridgend County Borough Council: Anti Racist Wales Action Plan update, Cabinet Committee Equalities (6 November 2024)","https://democratic.bridgend.gov.uk/documents/s33772/ARWAP%20report%20Nov%2024.pdf?LLL=0","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Several Councils and the Welsh Local Government Association (WLGA) said they were not clear in their role and responsibilities in delivering the actions in the ArWAP.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Tiger Bay in Cardiff is Wales' oldest multi-ethnic community",text:"Cardiff's former docklands, once known as Tiger Bay, are described by the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales as Wales' oldest multi-ethnic community, with sailors and workers from over 50 countries having settled there. A People's Collection Wales item about the Butetown History and Arts Centre states that research shows over 57 nationalities were living in Tiger Bay in the 1940s. During the race riots of 1919, violence occurred in the docks areas of Cardiff, Barry and Swansea.",figure:"Over 50 countries",year:"19th century to 1940s; riots in 1919",disputed:false,note:"Counts vary between sources: over 50 countries (Coflein) and over 57 nationalities in the 1940s (People's Collection Wales).",src:[SRCQ("Coflein (Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales): Cardiff Bay, site record 420177 (18 June 2014)","https://coflein.gov.uk/en/site/420177","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Bay is Wales' oldest multi-ethnic community, with sailors and workers from over 50 countries having settled here."),SRCQ("People's Collection Wales: The Voice of the Tiger, newsletter of the Butetown History and Arts Centre, number 1 (September 1994)","https://www.peoplescollection.wales/items/728376","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Research shows that in the 1940s over 57 different nationalities were living in Tiger Bay."),SRCQ("People's Collection Wales: 1919 Race Riots Collection","https://peoplescollection.wales/collections/1543631","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","In Wales, violence occurred in the docks areas of Cardiff, Barry and Swansea.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"plscot",h:"Scotland",intro:"Scotland's census was held in 2022, a year after the rest of the UK, and it records African and Caribbean or Black people under separate headings. Most Black people in Scotland live in its cities, and Scotland is also facing its own role in empire and slavery.",points:[
  {h:"About 65,400 people in Scotland were African, Caribbean or Black",text:"Scotland's Census 2022 counted 58,636 people in African ethnic groups (1.08% of the population) and 6,778 in Caribbean or Black groups (0.12%). Together that is about 65,400 people, or 1.2%. In 2011 the figures were 29,638 (0.56%) and 6,540 (0.12%), so the African groups roughly doubled.",figure:"65,414 (1.2%)",year:"Scotland's Census 2022 (20 March 2022), compared with 2011",disputed:false,note:"Calculated: the combined total adds the two published counts, and 'roughly doubled' compares the 2011 and 2022 African counts. Scotland's census was a year later than the 2021 censuses elsewhere in the UK, so the dates differ.",src:[SRCQ("National Records of Scotland: Scotland's Census 2022 – ethnic group, national identity, language and religion (21 May 2024)","https://www.scotlandscensus.gov.uk/2022-results/scotland-s-census-2022-ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Year\": 2022, \"Ethnic Group\": \"African, Scottish African or British African\", \"Count\": 58636, \"Percent\": 1.08,"),SRCQ("National Records of Scotland: Scotland's Census 2022 – ethnic group, national identity, language and religion (21 May 2024)","https://www.scotlandscensus.gov.uk/2022-results/scotland-s-census-2022-ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","\"Year\": 2022, \"Ethnic Group\": \"Caribbean or Black\", \"Count\": 6778, \"Percent\": 0.12,"),SRCQ("Coalition for Racial Equality and Rights: Scotland's Census 2022 – what do the latest statistics tell us about minority ethnic groups in Scotland?","https://www.crer.org.uk/blog/census-2022-bme-population","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","This was followed by Black groups, who made up 1.2%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Scotland's census uses different Black ethnic group names",text:"In Scotland's 2022 census, 'African, African Scottish or African British' and 'Caribbean or Black' were separate headings, unlike the single Black heading used in England and Wales. Most people in the African groups (52,729) were recorded as 'Other African', and 546 people recorded 'Black, Black Scottish or Black British'. Figures are therefore not directly comparable with the 2021 censuses elsewhere in the UK.",figure:"546 'Black, Black Scottish or Black British'",year:"Scotland's Census 2022",disputed:false,note:"NRS labels its combined African total 'African, Scottish African or British African' in its chart data, while the census heading reads 'African, African Scottish or African British'.",src:[SRCQ("National Records of Scotland: Scotland's Census 2022 – ethnic group, national identity, language and religion (21 May 2024)","https://www.scotlandscensus.gov.uk/2022-results/scotland-s-census-2022-ethnic-group-national-identity-language-and-religion/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Tick boxes were grouped under broad headings: ‘White’, 'Mixed or multiple’, ‘Asian, Asian Scottish or Asian British’, ‘African, African Scottish or African British’, ‘Caribbean or Black’ and ‘Other’."),SRCQ("ScotPHO (Public Health Scotland): Ethnic minorities – population composition (last updated 30 September 2025)","https://scotpho.org.uk/population-groups/ethnic-minorities/data/population-composition","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","African: Other African 52,729 0.97"),SRCQ("ScotPHO (Public Health Scotland): Ethnic minorities – population composition (last updated 30 September 2025)","https://scotpho.org.uk/population-groups/ethnic-minorities/data/population-composition","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Caribbean or Black: Black, Black Scottish or Black British 546 0.01")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"African people are the largest ‘non-white’ group in Aberdeen",text:"In 2022, African people were the largest ‘non-white’ ethnic group in Aberdeen, at about 4% of residents. In Glasgow, the number of people recording an African ethnic group rose from 1,257 in 2001 to 12,440 in 2011, while Caribbean or Black was the only group to fall between 2011 and 2022. Public Health Scotland lists Glasgow, Edinburgh, Aberdeen, East Renfrewshire and Dundee as having large ‘non-white’ populations.",figure:"About 4% (Aberdeen)",year:"Censuses of 2001, 2011 and 2022",disputed:false,note:"The Aberdeen and Glasgow figures are from the Glasgow Centre for Population Health's Understanding Glasgow site, which compares selected Scottish cities, and are rounded by that source.",src:[SRCQ("Understanding Glasgow (Glasgow Centre for Population Health): Ethnicity – Scottish cities, 2022 census data (last updated October 2025)","https://www.understandingglasgow.com/glasgow-indicators/population/ethnicity/scottish-cities","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","the largest is Pakistani in Glasgow (5%) and Dundee (2%), African in Aberdeen (4%), and Chinese in Edinburgh (3%)."),SRCQ("Understanding Glasgow (Glasgow Centre for Population Health): Ethnicity – Glasgow trends (last updated October 2025)","https://www.understandingglasgow.com/glasgow-indicators/population/ethnicity/trends","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The number of people who recorded their ethnic group as African increased by 890% (from 1,257 to 12,440) from 2001 to 2011."),SRCQ("ScotPHO (Public Health Scotland): Ethnic minorities – population composition (last updated 30 September 2025)","https://scotpho.org.uk/population-groups/ethnic-minorities/data/population-composition","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","At a local authority level, there were large non-White populations in Glasgow City, City of Edinburgh, Aberdeen City, East Renfrewshire and Dundee City.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Race Equality Framework sets Scotland's race equality aims to 2030",text:"The Scottish Government's Race Equality Framework for Scotland 2016-2030 sets out six themed visions for race equality, shaped through engagement with grassroots community organisations, public sector practitioners and individuals. In 2020 a Scottish Parliament committee found that progress on race equality in employment had been slow or stalled, and that the ethnicity employment gap had gone backwards. Public bodies in Scotland are also bound by the Equality Act 2010, which forms part of Scots law.",figure:"",year:"2016 to 2030; committee report 2020",disputed:false,note:"The framework is government policy, not legislation. The framework is cited from an archived copy of the Scottish Government PDF (March 2016).",src:[SRCQ("Scottish Government: Race Equality Framework for Scotland 2016-2030 (March 2016), archived copy","https://dera.ioe.ac.uk/id/eprint/25928/1/00497601.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Framework outlines how we will work in partnership with our agencies and other key stakeholders to address a wide range of opportunities for progress in six themed Visions."),SRCQ("Scottish Parliament Equalities and Human Rights Committee: Race equality, employment and skills: making progress? (3rd Report, 2020, Session 5)","https://www.parliament.scot/chamber-and-committees/committees/committee-reports/ehric/2020/11/15/ehrics052020r3/introduction","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","the Committee’s evidence shows that progress has either been slow, stalled, or in the case of the ethnicity employment gap gone backwards."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 217 (Extent)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/217","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","(2)This Act, apart from section 190 (improvements to let dwelling houses) and Part 15 (family property), forms part of the law of Scotland.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"An Anti-Racism Observatory for Scotland is being set up",text:"The Scottish Government is funding an Anti-Racism Observatory for Scotland (AROS), a new national organisation to work with government, public bodies and communities. In May 2025 the University of Strathclyde and the Coalition for Racial Equality and Rights were announced as its hosts during set-up, and it is anticipated that AROS will eventually become fully independent.",figure:"",year:"2024 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"The Scottish Government opened a tender on 11 July 2024 for a host organisation to incubate the observatory for two years.",src:[SRCQ("Scottish Government Public Procurement and Property blog: Anti-Racism Observatory for Scotland (11 July 2024)","https://blogs.gov.scot/public-procurement/2024/07/11/anti-racism-observatory-for-scotland/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The host organisation will be an independent organisation funded by the Scottish Government for improvement of public Scottish institutions"),SRCQ("University of Strathclyde: University of Strathclyde and CRER to host new body to tackle systemic racism in the public sector (13 May 2025)","https://www.strath.ac.uk/whystrathclyde/news/2025/universityofstrathclydeandcrertohostnewbodytotacklesystemicracisminthepublicsector/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Funded by the Scottish Government, it is anticipated AROS will eventually become fully independent."),SRCQ("Coalition for Racial Equality and Rights: Anti-Racism Observatory for Scotland","https://www.crer.org.uk/anti-racism-observatory-for-scotland","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","AROS is a new national organisation, funded by Scottish Government to work with government, public bodies and communities.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Scotland's economy was tied to empire and slavery for two centuries",text:"An independent steering group, part of a project sponsored by the Scottish Government, reported in 2022 that for more than two centuries Scotland's economy was closely tied to imperial trade and conquest, including the enslavement of human beings. The Scottish Government accepted its recommendations, including a dedicated space to address Scotland's role in empire, colonialism and historic slavery, in January 2024. In 1778 the Court of Session decided the case of Joseph Knight, who claimed that, despite being purchased by Wedderburn in Jamaica, the act of landing in Scotland had freed him; he was partially successful in arguing that Scots law could not support the status of slavery.",figure:"",year:"18th century to 2024",disputed:false,note:"The account of the Joseph Knight case is from National Records of Scotland, which holds the court papers.",src:[SRCQ("Empire, Slavery & Scotland's Museums Steering Group: Recommendations (June 2022), published by Museums Galleries Scotland","https://www.museumsgalleriesscotland.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/empire-slavery-and-scotlands-museums-recommendations-1.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Scotland is facing up to the reality that for more than two centuries, its economy was closely tied to imperial trade and conquest, including the enslavement of human beings."),SRCQ("Museums Galleries Scotland: Empire, Slavery & Scotland's Museums (project page)","https://www.museumsgalleriesscotland.org.uk/projects/empire-slavery-scotlands-museums/","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Scottish Government accepted these recommendations in January 2024 and work to deliver them is underway."),SRCQ("National Records of Scotland: The Joseph Knight case","https://nrscotland.gov.uk/learning-and-events/slavery-and-scotland/the-joseph-knight-case","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Finally Joseph Knight was partially successful in arguing that Scots law could not support the status of slavery.")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"plni",h:"Northern Ireland",intro:"Northern Ireland has a small but growing Black population, most of it in Belfast and Mid Ulster. Its equality law differs from Great Britain's, and recorded race hate has risen sharply since 2024.",points:[
  {h:"About 11,000 people in Northern Ireland were Black in 2021",text:"Census 2021 counted about 11,000 Black people in Northern Ireland, 0.6% of the population: 8,069 recorded as Black African and 2,963 as Black Other. Black people were the second largest minority ethnic group in Northern Ireland.",figure:"11,000 (0.6%)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), Northern Ireland",disputed:false,note:"NISRA rounds the published total; the Black African and Black Other counts come from its MS-B01 table. Northern Ireland uses its own ethnic group classification, so groups are not directly comparable with Great Britain.",src:[SRCQ("NISRA: Census 2021 main statistics for Northern Ireland, phase 1 – statistical bulletin: ethnic group (22 September 2022)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/system/files/statistics/census-2021-main-statistics-for-northern-ireland-phase-1-statistical-bulletin-ethnic-group.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Black 11,000 0.6%"),SRCQ("NISRA: Census 2021 main statistics table MS-B01, ethnic group (spreadsheet)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/system/files/statistics/census-2021-ms-b01.xlsx","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Northern Ireland,N92000002,1903179"),SRCQ("Draft equality impact assessment appendices: exceptions to proposed longer notice to quit periods in Northern Ireland (published in Belfast City Council committee papers)","https://minutes.belfastcity.gov.uk/mgConvert2PDF.aspx?ID=125884","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","with 1.71% of the population classified as Asian, 0.58% as Black,")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Belfast and Mid Ulster have the highest Black shares",text:"In 2021, 1.34% of Belfast's residents (4,640 people) were Black African or Black Other, as were 1.24% in Mid Ulster (1,864 people). In Mid Ulster more people recorded Black Other (1,155) than Black African (709). In every other council area the share was under 1%.",figure:"1.34% (Belfast)",year:"Census 2021 (21 March 2021), local government districts",disputed:false,note:"Calculated: totals and percentages add the Black African and Black Other counts and divide by all usual residents in each district.",src:[SRCQ("NISRA: Census 2021 main statistics table MS-B01, ethnic group (spreadsheet)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/system/files/statistics/census-2021-ms-b01.xlsx","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Belfast,N09000003,345419"),SRCQ("NISRA: Census 2021 main statistics table MS-B01, ethnic group (spreadsheet)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/system/files/statistics/census-2021-ms-b01.xlsx","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Mid Ulster,N09000009,150292,144294,550,81,206,326,111,30,103,1132,709,1155"),SRCQ("Draft equality impact assessment appendices: exceptions to proposed longer notice to quit periods in Northern Ireland (published in Belfast City Council committee papers)","https://minutes.belfastcity.gov.uk/mgConvert2PDF.aspx?ID=125884","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Mid Ulster 150,292 96.01% 0.37% 0.05% 0.14% 0.22% 0.07% 0.02% 0.07% 0.75% 0.47% 0.77%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Racial Equality Strategy 2015-2025 set seven shared aims",text:"The Executive Office's Racial Equality Strategy 2015-2025 set a framework for government departments to tackle racial inequalities, eradicate racism and hate crime, and promote good race relations. It set seven shared aims, with actions underscored by the Section 75 equality and good relations duties.",figure:"7 shared aims",year:"2015 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"The strategy period ended in 2025. Both sources are published by the Executive Office, the only publisher of the strategy itself.",src:[SRCQ("The Executive Office (NI): Racial Equality Strategy 2015-2025 (December 2015)","https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/publications/ofmdfm/racial-equality-strategy-2015-2025.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","In recognition of these issues, we have developed 7 shared aims to address specific areas of concern."),SRCQ("The Executive Office (NI): Independent review of progress on implementation of the Racial Equality Strategy 2015-25 – response (20 December 2024)","https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2024-12/teo-response-to-the-final-report-of-the-independent-review-of-progress-on-implementation-of-the-racial-equality-strategy.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The Racial Equality Strategy 2015-2025 (‘Strategy’) established a framework for action by Government Departments (and others):")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"A 2024 review found the strategy lacked an action plan",text:"An independent review commissioned by the Executive Office, published in December 2024, found that the strategy's aims had been undermined by the lack of an action plan, of involvement of people with lived experience, and of a process for ethnic monitoring. It called for an action plan with a budget. The Executive Office said most of the strategy's actions had been completed and that it would work with the minority ethnic sector on any new approach beyond 2025.",figure:"",year:"2023 to 2024",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Independent review of the progress on the implementation of the Racial Equality Strategy 2015-25: final report (published by The Executive Office, December 2024)","https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2024-12/independent-review-of-the-progress-on-the-implementation-of-the-racial.PDF","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","undermined by the lack of: an action plan; involvement of people with lived experience; and a process for ethnic monitoring;"),SRCQ("The Executive Office (NI): Independent review of progress on implementation of the Racial Equality Strategy 2015-25 – response (20 December 2024)","https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2024-12/teo-response-to-the-final-report-of-the-independent-review-of-progress-on-implementation-of-the-racial-equality-strategy.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Of the proposed actions in the Strategy, the majority of actions have been completed,"),SRCQ("The Executive Office (NI): Independent review of progress on implementation of the Racial Equality Strategy 2015-25 – response (20 December 2024)","https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2024-12/teo-response-to-the-final-report-of-the-independent-review-of-progress-on-implementation-of-the-racial-equality-strategy.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","there will be further engagement and collaboration by TEO with the wider minority ethnic sector to inform any new strategic approach beyond 2025.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Section 75 differs from the public sector equality duty",text:"In Northern Ireland, Section 75 of the Northern Ireland Act 1998 requires public authorities to have due regard to the need to promote equality of opportunity between people of different racial groups, among other groups, and regard to promoting good relations. The public sector equality duty in section 149 of the Equality Act 2010 does not extend to Northern Ireland. The main race discrimination law there is the Race Relations (Northern Ireland) Order 1997.",figure:"",year:"1997, 1998 and 2010",disputed:false,note:"Derived from the Act's extent section: section 149 is not among the provisions that section 217(3) extends to Northern Ireland. Section 75 also covers religious belief, political opinion, age, marital status, sexual orientation, sex, disability and people with dependants.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Northern Ireland Act 1998, section 75","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/47/section/75","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","(1)A public authority shall in carrying out its functions relating to Northern Ireland have due regard to the need to promote equality of opportunity—"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 217 (Extent)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/217","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","(3)Each of the following also forms part of the law of Northern Ireland—"),SRCQ("The Executive Office (NI): Racial Equality Strategy 2015-2025 (December 2015)","https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/publications/ofmdfm/racial-equality-strategy-2015-2025.pdf","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The most significant piece of legislation specific to race is the Race Relations (Northern Ireland) Order 1997.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Recorded race hate in Northern Ireland reached record levels",text:"In the 12 months to 31 March 2026, police in Northern Ireland recorded 2,367 race hate incidents and 1,507 race hate crimes, the highest since records began in 2004/05. NISRA links the rise to periods of civil unrest in August 2024 and June 2025. Figures for the 12 months to 30 June 2026 were higher again, at 2,561 incidents and 1,615 crimes.",figure:"2,367 incidents and 1,507 crimes",year:"12 months to 31 March 2026 (and to 30 June 2026)",disputed:false,note:"The figures cover all race-motivated incidents and crimes, not only those against Black people, and do not show victims' ethnicity. They include only incidents and crimes reported to and recorded by the police. Both sets of figures are official statistics published by NISRA (14 May 2026 and 27 August 2026).",src:[SRCQ("NISRA: Increased levels of race hate incidents recorded by police (14 May 2026)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/news/increased-levels-race-hate-incidents-recorded-police","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","There were 2,367 race incidents recorded, up 561 (31%) on the previous year and 1,507 race crimes, an increase of 320 (27%)."),SRCQ("NISRA: Increased levels of race hate incidents recorded by police (14 May 2026)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/news/increased-levels-race-hate-incidents-recorded-police","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","The rise in race incidents and crimes is linked to periods of civil unrest in August 2024 and June 2025."),SRCQ("NISRA: Police recorded over 2,500 race hate incidents (27 August 2026)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/news/police-recorded-over-2500-race-hate-incidents","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","Police recorded 2,561 race hate incidents, 503 more than in the previous 12 months."),SRCQ("NISRA: Police recorded over 2,500 race hate incidents (27 August 2026)","https://www.nisra.gov.uk/news/police-recorded-over-2500-race-hate-incidents","Supports the Place: the four nations and the regions page","There were also 1,615 race hate crimes recorded, an increase of 268.")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Liverpool's Black community dates back to the 18th century.",refs:["pl-plengland-liverpool-s-black-community-dates-back-t"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Fewer than 1 in 100 people in rural England are Black; rural services should still plan for them.",refs:["pl-plengland-fewer-than-1-in-100-people-in-rural-engl"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Each nation has its own race equality plan: the Anti-racist Wales Action Plan, Scotland's Race Equality Framework and Northern Ireland's Racial Equality Strategy.",refs:["pl-plwales-the-anti-racist-wales-action-plan-sets-a", "pl-plscot-the-race-equality-framework-sets-scotlan", "pl-plni-the-racial-equality-strategy-2015-2025-s"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"In Northern Ireland, Section 75 sets the equality duty, not the public sector equality duty.",refs:["pl-plni-section-75-differs-from-the-public-secto"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Census years and categories differ: Scotland counted in 2022 and uses separate African and Caribbean or Black headings.",refs:["pl-plscot-scotland-s-census-uses-different-black-e"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"4.2% of people in England are Black; outside London and the West Midlands every region is under 3%.",refs:["pl-plengland-outside-london-and-the-west-midlands-reg"]},{t:"Lewisham, Southwark and Lambeth have the highest Black shares, at 26.8%, 25.1% and 24.0%.",refs:["pl-plengland-lewisham-southwark-and-lambeth-have-the-"]},{t:"Scotland's 2022 census counted about 65,400 African, Caribbean or Black people; Wales counted 28,000 in 2021.",refs:["pl-plscot-about-65-400-people-in-scotland-were-afr", "pl-plwales-28-000-people-in-wales-identified-as-bla"]},{t:"About 11,000 people in Northern Ireland were Black in 2021.",refs:["pl-plni-about-11-000-people-in-northern-ireland-"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["worldpolicy"]={name:"What other governments do",pfx:"wp",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"wplatam",h:"Latin America: laws, quotas and data rules",intro:"Several Latin American governments have written African descent into law, through equality statutes, quotas, reserved seats and race-crime laws. Census counts, land rights and Mexico's 2019 recognition are on the hub's world page, so this section looks at the laws and data rules behind them.",points:[
  {h:"Brazil's 2010 racial equality statute defines who counts as Black",text:"A 2023 amendment (Law 14.553) requires the administrative records of public bodies, private employers and their workers, such as hiring and dismissal forms, to include self-classified ethnic and racial fields, and requires IBGE to survey the ethnic and racial make-up of the public sector every five years.",figure:"",year:"2010; amended April 2023",disputed:false,note:"Applies to Brazil only. The hub's Latin America material covers the preto and pardo census categories and their counts. The results of the five-yearly public-sector survey are not reported here.",src:[SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 12.288, Estatuto da Igualdade Racial (20 July 2010, consolidated text)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2007-2010/2010/lei/l12288.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","população negra: o conjunto de pessoas que se autodeclaram pretas e pardas, conforme o quesito cor ou raça usado pela Fundação Instituto Brasileiro de Geografia e Estatística (IBGE)"),SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 14.553 (20 April 2023)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2023-2026/2023/lei/L14553.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","realizará, a cada 5 (cinco) anos, pesquisa destinada a identificar o percentual de ocupação por parte de segmentos étnicos e raciais no âmbito do setor público"),SRCQ("Agência Senado: Lei determina inclusão de dados sobre raça em documentos trabalhistas (24 April 2023)","https://www12.senado.leg.br/noticias/materias/2023/04/24/lei-determina-inclusao-de-dados-sobre-raca-em-documentos-trabalhistas","Supports the What other governments do page","O presidente Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva sancionou a Lei 14.553, de 2023, que determina a inclusão de informações sobre raça em registros de trabalhadores."),SRCQ("Universidade Federal do Espírito Santo (ABEPSS proceedings): Estatuto da Igualdade Racial: conquistas de direitos e políticas (Correia and Moura)","https://periodicos.ufes.br/abepss/article/view/22688/15170","Supports the What other governments do page","Foi aprovado no dia 16 de junho de 2010 pelo Senado, e trinta e quatro dias depois transformado na Lei n. 12.288 de 20 de julho de 2010")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Brazil reserves half of federal university places, with racial shares",text:"Law 12.711 of 2012 requires federal universities to reserve at least 50% of places on each course for students who did all their secondary schooling in state schools. Within those places, the share for self-declared preto, pardo and Indigenous students must be at least their share of the state's population at the latest census. Law 14.723 of November 2023 added quilombola students (from communities descended from settlements of people who escaped slavery), lowered the income threshold and set an evaluation every ten years with an annual report.",figure:"At least 50% of places",year:"2012; revised November 2023",disputed:false,note:"Places are also reserved for disabled students. In April 2012 Brazil's Supreme Court unanimously upheld the University of Brasília's earlier race-based admissions policy (ADPF 186), in a case brought by a political party.",src:[SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 12.711 (29 August 2012, consolidated text)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2011-2014/2012/lei/l12711.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","no mínimo 50% (cinquenta por cento) de suas vagas para estudantes que tenham cursado integralmente o ensino médio em escolas públicas"),SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 14.723 (13 November 2023)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2023-2026/2023/lei/l14723.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","A cada 10 (dez) anos a contar da data de publicação desta Lei, será promovida a avaliação do programa especial"),SRCQ("Agência Senado: Sancionada ampliação da Lei de Cotas (14 November 2023)","https://www12.senado.leg.br/noticias/materias/2023/11/14/sancionada-ampliacao-da-lei-de-cotas","Supports the What other governments do page","Quilombolas passam a ser beneficiários pelo sistema."),SRCQ("Supremo Tribunal Federal: STF julga constitucional política de cotas na UnB (26 April 2012)","https://noticias.stf.jus.br/postsnoticias/stf-julga-constitucional-politica-de-cotas-na-unb/","Supports the What other governments do page","O Plenário do STF considerou constitucional a política de cotas étnico-raciais para seleção de estudantes da UnB.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Brazil: 30% of federal recruitment places reserved by quota law",text:"From 2014, Law 12.990 reserved 20% of places in federal civil service recruitment competitions for Black (negro) candidates, for a term of ten years. In 2024 the Supreme Court extended that law until Congress passed a replacement. Law 15.142 of 3 June 2025 replaced it, reserving 30% of places in federal competitions and temporary recruitment for people who self-declare as preto or pardo, Indigenous people and quilombolas, wherever two or more places are offered. Self-declarations as preto or pardo go through a confirmation procedure that must involve specialists in ethnic and racial relations.",figure:"30% of posts (20% before 2025)",year:"2014–2025",disputed:false,note:"In Great Britain, government guidance for employers says positive discrimination is unlawful and that setting recruitment quotas, as opposed to targets, would probably be unlawful discrimination; sections 158 and 159 of the Equality Act 2010 allow only limited positive action, and the hub's positive action guidance explains what is allowed. Brazil's Supreme Court kept the 2014 quota in force in ADI 7654: a judge's injunction of 26 May 2024 was confirmed unanimously by the full court on 17 June 2024.",src:[SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 15.142 (3 June 2025)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2023-2026/2025/lei/l15142.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","É reservado às pessoas pretas e pardas, indígenas e quilombolas o percentual de 30% (trinta por cento) das vagas oferecidas"),SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 12.990 (9 June 2014, repealed 2025)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2011-2014/2014/lei/l12990.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","Ficam reservadas aos negros 20% (vinte por cento) das vagas oferecidas nos concursos públicos"),SRCQ("Rádio Senado: Sancionada lei que retoma e amplia cotas raciais em concursos públicos federais (6 June 2025)","https://www12.senado.leg.br/radio/1/noticia/2025/06/06/sancionada-lei-que-retoma-e-amplia-cotas-raciais-em-concursos-publicos-federais","Supports the What other governments do page","Os candidatos deverão fazer a autodeclaração, que será avaliada por uma comissão de especialistas em relações étnico-raciais."),SRCQ("Supremo Tribunal Federal: STF mantém decisão que prorrogou lei de cotas raciais em concursos públicos (June 2024)","https://noticias.stf.jus.br/postsnoticias/stf-mantem-decisao-que-prorrogou-lei-de-cotas-raciais-em-concursos-publicos/","Supports the What other governments do page","Por unanimidade, Plenário confirmou liminar do ministro Flávio Dino e manteve em vigor a ação afirmativa até que seja sancionada nova lei sobre o tema."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Positive action in the workplace: guidance for employers","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/positive-action-in-the-workplace-guidance-for-employers/positive-action-in-the-workplace","Supports the What other governments do page","Positive discrimination is unlawful in Great Britain")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Brazil treats racism as a crime with no time limit on prosecution",text:"Brazil's 1988 Constitution makes the practice of racism a crime with no bail and no limitation period, punishable by imprisonment. In October 2021 the Supreme Court held by a majority that racial insult (injúria racial) is a form of racism and so has no limitation period. Law 14.532 of 11 January 2023 then moved racial insult into the racism law, with two to five years' imprisonment and a fine.",figure:"2 to 5 years' imprisonment",year:"1988; 2021; January 2023",disputed:false,note:"The 2021 ruling was debated among lawyers on the grounds that only Congress may define crimes and their limitation periods (the principle of legality) and on the separation of powers, according to an article by Douglas Strachicini in the Brazilian Senate's legal journal; the 2023 law put the point into statute.",src:[SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Constituição da República Federativa do Brasil de 1988, art. 5, XLII","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/constituicao/constituicao.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","a prática do racismo constitui crime inafiançável e imprescritível, sujeito à pena de reclusão, nos termos da lei"),SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Lei nº 14.532 (11 January 2023)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2023-2026/2023/lei/l14532.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","Injuriar alguém, ofendendo-lhe a dignidade ou o decoro, em razão de raça, cor, etnia ou procedência nacional. Pena: reclusão, de 2 (dois) a 5 (cinco) anos, e multa."),SRCQ("Senado Federal, Revista de Informação Legislativa: Imprescritibilidade da injúria racial (Strachicini, January–March 2025)","https://www12.senado.leg.br/ril/edicoes/62/245/ril_v62_n245_p181.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","Em 28/10/2021 o plenário do STF decidiu, por maioria de votos, que o crime de injúria racial configurava uma forma de racismo e, portanto, seria imprescritível."),SRCQ("Supremo Tribunal Federal: injúria racial é crime imprescritível (28 October 2021)","https://portal.stf.jus.br/noticias/verNoticiaDetalhe.asp?idConteudo=475646","Supports the What other governments do page","O Plenário do Supremo Tribunal Federal (STF) decidiu, nesta quinta-feira (28), que o crime de injúria racial configura uma forma de racismo e é imprescritível.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Colombia reserves two seats in its lower house for Afro-descendants",text:"Article 176 of Colombia's Constitution creates special constituencies in the House of Representatives, two of whose seats are elected by the constituency of Afro-descendant communities. The seats were first contested in 1994; in 1996 the Constitutional Court struck down the law behind them, and they stayed empty until Law 649 of 2001 regulated them again. Under that law, candidates must be members of the community and endorsed by an organisation registered with the Ministry of the Interior.",figure:"2 seats",year:"First elected 1994; current wording from 2015",disputed:true,note:"An article by Ana Carolina Osorio in the journal of Colombia's national civil registry (Registraduría) records long-standing questions about how representative the elected members are, citing low votes and 'paper' organisations. Collective land rights under Law 70 of 1993 are covered on the hub's Latin America page.",src:[SRCQ("Secretaría del Senado (Colombia): Constitución Política de 1991, artículo 176 (consolidated text)","https://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/constitucion_politica_1991_pr005.html","Supports the What other governments do page","dos (2) por la circunscripción de las comunidades afrodescendientes"),SRCQ("Registraduría Nacional del Estado Civil, Revista Democracia Actual: En busca de la comunidad afrocolombiana (Osorio, vol. 5, 2020)","https://revistas.registraduria.gov.co/index.php/democracia/article/download/51/38/44","Supports the What other governments do page","Desde la primera elección en 1994, esta acción afirmativa ha sido objeto de cuestionamientos en cuanto a la verdadera representatividad de los congresistas elegidos"),SRCQ("Registraduría Nacional del Estado Civil, Democracia Actual: Ana Carolina Osorio (2020)","https://revistas.registraduria.gov.co/index.php/democracia/article/download/51/38/44","Supports the What other governments do page","ser miembros de la respectiva comunidad y avalados previamente por una organización inscrita ante la Dirección de Asuntos de Comunidades Negras del Ministerio del Interior")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Colombia's equality ministry lost its legal basis in June 2026",text:"Law 2281 of January 2023 created a Ministry of Equality and Equity whose priority groups included Afro-descendant, Black, Raizal and Palenquero peoples. In May 2024 the Constitutional Court struck the law down because Congress passed it without assessing its fiscal impact, but delayed the effect for two legislative years. A bill to re-create the ministry was not fully passed before 20 June 2026. Decrees of 19 June 2026 put the ministry into liquidation (Decree 0626) and returned the three agencies attached to it, including the family welfare institute ICBF, to their former sectors (Decree 0627).",figure:"",year:"January 2023 – June 2026",disputed:false,note:"The ministry served many groups, not only Afro-Colombians. The court's objection was to the law-making procedure, not to the ministry's aims.",src:[SRCQ("Secretaría del Senado (Colombia): Ley 2281 de 2023 (4 January 2023, annotated text)","https://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/ley_2281_2023.html","Supports the What other governments do page","3. Pueblos afrodescendientes, negros, raizales, palanqueros, indígenas y Rrom."),SRCQ("Corte Constitucional de Colombia: Sentencia C-161 de 2024 (8 May 2024)","https://sidn.ramajudicial.gov.co/SIDN/NORMATIVA/TEXTOS_COMPLETOS/97_JURISPRUDENCIA_RELACIONADA/C-161-24.RTF","Supports the What other governments do page","Una vez culmine la legislatura 2025-2026, la Ley 2281 de 2023 dejará de producir efectos definitivamente y no formará parte del ordenamiento jurídico."),SRCQ("Presidencia de la República (Colombia), Diario Oficial 53.534: Decreto 0627 de 2026 (19 June 2026)","https://sidn.ramajudicial.gov.co/SIDN//NORMATIVA/TEXTOS_COMPLETOS/5_DECRETOS/DECRETOS%202026/Decreto%200627%20de%202026.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","ante la imposibilidad de aprobar la totalidad del articulado antes del 20 de junio de 2026, el proyecto no cumpliría con el requisito previsto en el artículo 162 de la Constitución Política"),SRCQ("Gobierno de Colombia: Decreto 0626 de 2026 (19 June 2026)","https://sidn.ramajudicial.gov.co/SIDN//NORMATIVA/TEXTOS_COMPLETOS/5_DECRETOS/DECRETOS%202026/Decreto%200626%20de%202026.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","Liquídese el Ministerio de Igualdad y Equidad creado mediante la Ley 2281 de 2023")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Uruguay sets an 8% public-job quota for Afro-descendants",text:"Uruguay's Law 19.122 of 2013 recognises that Afro-descendants have historically suffered racism and discrimination, and requires state bodies to give 8% of the posts they fill each year to Afro-descendant candidates. The National Civil Service Office reports on this every year. In 2024, 415 people were recruited under the rule, 1.72% of recruitment in the bodies covered.",figure:"1.72% of 2024 recruitment against an 8% target",year:"Law 2013; data for 1 January – 31 December 2024",disputed:false,note:"The 1.72% leaves out bodies whose intake was too small for 8% to make up one post. Seven bodies met the quota in 2024. The law also sets an 8% share in training programmes and shares in study grants. A 2024 report by Uruguay's national human rights institution and the Civil Service Office reviews its first ten years.",src:[SRCQ("IMPO, Uruguay's official legal database: Ley Nº 19.122 (promulgated 21 August 2013, current text)","https://www.impo.com.uy/bases/leyes/19122-2013","Supports the What other governments do page","están obligados a destinar el 8% (ocho por ciento) de los puestos de trabajo a ser llenados en el año, para ser ocupados por personas afrodescendientes"),SRCQ("Oficina Nacional del Servicio Civil (Uruguay): Ingreso de personas afrodescendientes en el Estado 2024 (signed 30 May 2025)","https://www.gub.uy/ministerio-economia-finanzas/sites/ministerio-economia-finanzas/files/documentos/publicaciones/Ingreso%20de%20personas%20Afro_2024.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","ha sido de 415 (197 hombres y 218 mujeres), lo que representa el 1,72% de los ingresos en estos organismos."),SRCQ("Oficina Nacional del Servicio Civil (Uruguay): Informe de los 10 años de cumplimiento de la ley 19.122 (26 July 2024)","https://www.gub.uy/oficina-nacional-servicio-civil/comunicacion/publicaciones/informe-10-anos-cumplimiento-ley-19122-desafios-su-implementacion","Supports the What other governments do page","Informe realizado en conjunto entre la Institución Nacional de Derechos Humanos y Defensoría del Pueblo (INDDHH) y la Oficina Nacional del Servicio Civil (ONSC)")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"The Americas have a regional anti-racism treaty, in force since 2017",text:"The Inter-American Convention against Racism, Racial Discrimination and Related Forms of Intolerance was agreed at the Organization of American States in Antigua, Guatemala, on 5 June 2013 and came into force on 11 November 2017. Costa Rica and Uruguay were the first states to ratify. Brazil's Congress approved it by the procedure that gives human rights treaties the same standing as constitutional amendments, and it took effect for Brazil on 27 June 2021.",figure:"",year:"Adopted 2013; in force 2017",disputed:false,note:"The OAS treaty register listed six states as having ratified as of 26 October 2026: Antigua and Barbuda, Brazil, Costa Rica, Ecuador, Mexico and Uruguay. The UK is not a member state of the OAS.",src:[SRCQ("United Nations Treaty Collection: registration No. 54915, Inter-American Convention against racism (registered 29 January 2018)","https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/No%20Volume/54915/Part/I-54915-08000002804ec1cd.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","Antigua, 5 June 2013 Entry into force: 11 November 2017, in accordance with article 20"),SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Decreto nº 10.932 (10 January 2022)","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2019-2022/2022/decreto/D10932.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","o instrumento de ratificação à Convenção e que esta entrou em vigor para a República Federativa do Brasil, no plano jurídico externo, em 27 de junho de 2021"),SRCQ("Presidência da República (Brazil): Constituição de 1988, art. 5, § 3","https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/constituicao/constituicao.htm","Supports the What other governments do page","por três quintos dos votos dos respectivos membros, serão equivalentes às emendas constitucionais"),SRCQ("Organization of American States: A-68 signatories and ratifications (table, accessed 26 October 2026)","https://www.oas.org/en/sla/dil/inter_american_treaties_A-68_racism_signatories.asp","Supports the What other governments do page","Mexico | --/--/-- | 11/19/19 RA | 1/21/20 | Yes")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"wpasia",h:"Asia and the Middle East: listing, review and redress",intro:"Few governments in Asia and the Middle East name African-descended communities in law, so legal listings such as India's Scheduled Tribes and UN treaty reviews do much of the work. The hub's world page covers the history of Siddi, Sheedi, Afro-Iraqi, Afro-Turkish and Afro-Iranian communities; this section looks at what governments have done or been asked to do.",points:[
  {h:"India lists Siddi communities as Scheduled Tribes in named districts",text:"India's Parliament sets lists of Scheduled Tribes state by state, sometimes limited to named districts. Siddi communities are listed in parts of Gujarat. An Act passed in 2002 (in force January 2003) added 'Siddi (in Uttar Kannada district)' to Karnataka's list, and an Act of March 2020 extended the Karnataka listing to Siddis in Belagavi and Dharwad districts.",figure:"",year:"2003 Act; 2019 bill",disputed:false,note:"Status depends on the district as well as the state. A 2019 press report cited estimates of 17,000 to 18,000 Siddis in Karnataka; this is not a census count. The hub's world page covers Siddi history.",src:[SRCQ("Gazette of India (Ministry of Law and Justice): Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes Orders (Amendment) Act, 2002, No. 10 of 2003 (8 January 2003)","https://socialjustice.gov.in/public/ckeditor/upload/56951762485904.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","after entry 49, insert \"50. Siddi (in Uttar Kannada district)\""),SRCQ("Gazette of India (Ministry of Law and Justice): Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes Orders (Amendment) Act, 2002 – Gujarat entry","https://socialjustice.gov.in/public/ckeditor/upload/56951762485904.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","in entry 26, after \"Siddi\" and before the brackets, insert \", Siddi- Badshan\""),SRCQ("Rajya Sabha Bill No. IV of 2019: Constitution (Scheduled Tribes) Order (Second Amendment) Bill, 2019 (statement dated 8 January 2019; copy hosted by PRS Legislative Research)","https://prsindia.org/files/bills_acts/bills_parliament/2019/ST%20order%20(Second%20Amendment)%20Bill,%202019.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","‘‘Siddi’’ community of Dharwad and Belagavi districts alongwith existing ‘Siddi’ Community of Uttar Kannada district at entry 50"),SRCQ("Down To Earth: Activists favour grant of Scheduled Tribe status to Siddis across Karnataka (8 February 2019)","https://www.downtoearth.org.in/amp/story/environment/activists-favour-grant-of-scheduled-tribe-status-to-siddis-across-karnataka-63183","Supports the What other governments do page","The state has the largest concentration of the Afro-Indian community but ST status is granted to them in only 1 district"),SRCQ("Gazette of India: The Constitution (Scheduled Tribes) Order (Amendment) Act, 2020 (Act No. 4 of 2020, 20 March 2020)","https://prsindia.org/files/bills_acts/bills_parliament/2019/The%20Constitution%20(Scheduled%20Tribes)%20Order%20(Amendment)%20Act,%202020.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","\"(in Belagavi, Dharwad and Uttar Kannada districts)\"")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Pakistan's Sheedi community in UN race-equality reviews",text:"In 2016 the UN Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination (CERD) regretted that Pakistan had given no information on the Sheedi, descendants of East Africans, and asked it to ensure they could take part in cultural life, including the Sheedi Mela festival. Pakistan's reply, received in 2021, said the Sheedi enjoy all rights as citizens and that no rights complaint about them had reached the Sindh Human Rights Commission. In its September 2024 findings the committee was concerned about high poverty and unemployment among ethnic minority groups including the Sheedi, and recommended better access to jobs, training and support for small businesses.",figure:"",year:"2016–2024",disputed:true,note:"The government and the committee describe the situation differently. Pakistan's report also says the first Sheedi woman was elected to the Sindh Assembly in 2018. At the 2024 review a committee member asked whether Pakistan's census allows people of African descent, including the Siddi community, to identify themselves.",src:[SRCQ("UN CERD: Concluding observations on Pakistan, CERD/C/PAK/CO/21-23 (3 October 2016)","https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/1311154/files/CERD_C_PAK_CO_21-23-EN.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","The Committee regrets the absence of information on the situation of the Sheedi community, the descendants of East Africans"),SRCQ("Government of Pakistan, report to UN CERD: CERD/C/PAK/24-26 (issued 10 February 2022; received 28 October 2021)","https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/2068717/G2225425.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","The Sheedi community primarily resides in the province of Sindh and enjoys all rights as citizens of Pakistan."),SRCQ("UN CERD: List of themes for Pakistan, CERD/C/PAK/Q/24-26 (28 June 2024)","https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4053541/files/CERD_C_PAK_Q_24-26-EN.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","information on measures taken at the national, provincial and district levels to guarantee the rights and protection of the Sheedi people"),SRCQ("Nations Unies Genève: Examen du Pakistan au CERD, compte rendu de séance (August 2024)","https://www.ungeneva.org/fr/news-media/meeting-summary/2024/08/examen-du-pakistan-au-cerd-des-experts-font-notamment-part-de","Supports the What other governments do page","l’auto-identification des Afro-descendants et des personnes à peaux brunes ou foncées (la communauté siddi, d’ascendance africaine)"),SRCQ("UN Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination: concluding observations on Pakistan, CERD/C/PAK/CO/24-26 (24 September 2024)","https://tbinternet.ohchr.org/_layouts/15/treatybodyexternal/Download.aspx?symbolno=CERD%2FC%2FPAK%2FCO%2F24-26&Lang=En","Supports the What other governments do page","High levels of poverty and unemployment among ethnic minority groups, including the Sheedi and Baloch communities")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"UN reviews press Iraq on discrimination against Black Iraqis",text:"In 2014 CERD noted that Black Iraqi and Roma communities were affected by poverty and social exclusion, and recommended special measures. In 2019 it was still concerned about structural discrimination against people of African descent. Iraq's 2023 report to the committee says its High Commission for Human Rights referred a complaint about people of African descent in Basra to prosecutors. In September 2024 the committee said Iraq had taken too few measures against structural discrimination targeting people of African descent and again recommended special measures on poverty, living conditions, education, health care and jobs.",figure:"",year:"2014–2023",disputed:true,note:"Iraq's report says its laws, plans and policies are free from racial discrimination; the committee's findings rest partly on civil society reports, including an unofficial estimate that unemployment among Black Iraqis could be as high as 80%. The hub's world page covers population estimates and history.",src:[SRCQ("University of Minnesota Human Rights Library: CERD concluding observations on Iraq, CERD/C/IRQ/CO/15-21 (22 September 2014)","https://hrlibrary.umn.edu/country/iraq2014.html","Supports the What other governments do page","The Committee notes that the Black Iraqi and Roma communities are negatively affected by poverty and social exclusion."),SRCQ("UN CERD: Concluding observations on Iraq, CERD/C/IRQ/CO/22-25 (11 January 2019)","https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/1458448/1930_1551364867_g1900512.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","the Committee is still concerned about the persistence of structural racial discrimination, marginalization and stigmatization against people of African descent"),SRCQ("Government of Iraq, report to UN CERD: CERD/C/IRQ/26-27 (25 July 2023)","https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/2104306/G2315196.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","The Iraqi High Commission for Human Rights received a complaint relating to people of African descent in Basra which it recorded and referred to the Public Prosecution Service"),SRCQ("UN Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination: concluding observations on Iraq, CERD/C/IRQ/CO/26-27 (18 September 2024)","https://tbinternet.ohchr.org/_layouts/15/treatybodyexternal/Download.aspx?symbolno=CERD%2FC%2FIRQ%2FCO%2F26-27&Lang=En","Supports the What other governments do page","The Committee is concerned about the lack of measures taken to address and combat structural discrimination, marginalization and stigmatization targeting people of African descent")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Israel set up a government anti-racism unit after a 2016 review",text:"In 2016 Israel's government set up an inter-ministerial team, led by the Ministry of Justice's director general, to tackle racism against Israelis of Ethiopian origin. The government adopted its recommendations in July 2016, including a Government Unit to Coordinate the Fight Against Racism in the Ministry of Justice, which was set up in 2017. The unit's remit later widened to all groups facing institutional racism.",figure:"",year:"2016–2017",disputed:true,note:"A 2023 assessment by the Institute for National Security Studies says the unit's authority was not set in law and its budget was inadequate. The same assessment cites a 2023 survey reporting that 90% of Israelis of Ethiopian origin had encountered institutional racism.",src:[SRCQ("aChord Center, Hebrew University of Jerusalem: Policy to Eradicate Racism (undated web page)","https://en.achord.huji.ac.il/programs/research-projects-and-reports/policy-eradicate-racism","Supports the What other governments do page","an inter-ministerial team was established in 2016 to eradicate racism towards Ethiopian immigrants."),SRCQ("Institute for National Security Studies: An Effective State Mechanism to Combat Racism, INSS Insight No. 1756 (10 September 2023)","https://www.inss.org.il/publication/racism/","Supports the What other governments do page","Originally created to fight institutional racism against Israelis of Ethiopian descent, it now works with all groups that have experienced institutional racism."),SRCQ("Institute for National Security Studies: An Effective State Mechanism to Combat Racism, INSS Insight No. 1756 (10 September 2023)","https://www.inss.org.il/publication/racism/","Supports the What other governments do page","the failure to codify its authority and responsibility in law and the lack of a proper budget for its operation have impeded its work")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The UN standard behind these reviews: General Recommendation 34",text:"In 2011 CERD adopted General Recommendation 34 on racial discrimination against people of African descent. It asks states to identify communities of African descent, especially by collecting disaggregated data, and to run regular surveys of discrimination. The committee cites it when reviewing states in the region, for example Iraq in 2019.",figure:"",year:"2011",disputed:false,note:"The UK is reviewed by the same committee; the hub's law material covers the 2024 UK review.",src:[SRCQ("United Nations Office at Geneva: CERD General recommendation No. 34, CERD/C/GC/34 (79th session, 2011), English–Chinese text","https://ls-cts.unog.ch/sites/default/files/2019-05/CERD_GC_34_ENG-CHI_BT.html","Supports the What other governments do page","Take steps to identify communities of people of African descent living in their territories, especially through the collection of disaggregated data on the population"),SRCQ("UN CERD: Concluding observations on Iraq, CERD/C/IRQ/CO/22-25 (11 January 2019)","https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/1458448/1930_1551364867_g1900512.pdf","Supports the What other governments do page","No. 34 (2011) on racial discrimination against people of African descent, the Committee recommends that the State party redouble its efforts")],kind:"guidance"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Brazil's Supreme Court treats racial insult as a form of racism, with no time limit on prosecution.",refs:["wp-wplatam-brazil-treats-racism-as-a-crime-with-no-"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Uruguay's 8% public-job quota is far from met, which shows the value of publishing results.",refs:["wp-wplatam-uruguay-sets-an-8-public-job-quota-for-a"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"A UN standard asks states to identify communities of African descent and collect data about them.",refs:["wp-wpasia-the-un-standard-behind-these-reviews-gen"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"India lists Siddi communities as Scheduled Tribes only in named districts, so status depends on where people live.",refs:["wp-wpasia-india-lists-siddi-communities-as-schedul"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Colombia's equality ministry was struck down because its law skipped a fiscal assessment, not because of its aims.",refs:["wp-wplatam-colombia-s-equality-ministry-lost-its-le"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Brazil's 2010 statute defines who counts as Black, and a 2023 law requires race fields in employment records.",refs:["wp-wplatam-brazil-s-2010-racial-equality-statute-de"]},{t:"Brazil reserves 30% of federal recruitment places under a 2025 law; in Great Britain, quotas would probably be unlawful.",refs:["wp-wplatam-brazil-30-of-federal-recruitment-places-"]},{t:"Colombia reserves two seats in its lower house for Afro-descendant communities.",refs:["wp-wplatam-colombia-reserves-two-seats-in-its-lower"]},{t:"UN reviews in 2024 pressed Pakistan and Iraq on discrimination against African-descended communities.",refs:["wp-wpasia-pakistan-s-sheedi-community-in-un-race-e", "wp-wpasia-un-reviews-press-iraq-on-discrimination-"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["joining"]={name:"Joining government",pfx:"jn",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"jnroutes",h:"Ways in",intro:"There is no single door into government: you can apply for a job directly, join an early careers scheme or come in on secondment from another employer. These are the main routes, with the details that matter when you are deciding where to start.",points:[
  {h:"Civil Service Jobs is where you find and apply for roles",text:"Civil Service Jobs is the Civil Service's own recruitment website, and you can apply there at any time without joining a talent scheme. Set up job alerts so new roles that match your skills come to you, and check whether each advert is open to everyone or only to existing civil servants.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Some vacancies are advertised only across government or inside one department, so not every role is visible to external candidates. Some organisations take applications on their own websites, linked from the advert.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Civil Service Jobs is the recruitment site for the Civil Service."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Early careers (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-early-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","However, you don’t need to participate in a talent development programme to start your journey. You can browse and apply for current vacancies anytime through the Civil Service jobs portal.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Fast Stream: the graduate leadership programme, closing 5 November",text:"The Fast Stream is a leadership development programme with 18 specialist schemes, including digital, cyber security, statistics and economics. Most schemes need a 2:2 degree or above and many are open to degree apprentices; you start on £31,554 and move between postings in different departments.",figure:"£31,554 starting salary",year:"2027 entry; applications close midday 5 November 2026",disputed:false,note:"The 2027 window closes at midday on 5 November 2026, so dates and pay will change for the next round. Competition is intense: about 1% of applicants were recommended for appointment in the 2024-25 cycle. The hub's Schemes and programmes section profiles the Fast Stream in more detail, including its diversity funnel.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: The Civil Service Fast Stream (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/","Supports the Joining government page","Most schemes are open to graduates with a 2:2 degree or above, and many are also open to degree apprentices."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream, understanding the application process (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","The application window for 2027 entry is open between midday 8 October 2026 and midday 5 November 2026."),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Civil service fast stream explainer (first published 3 June 2022; page shows last updated 9 October 2024, with 2025-26 pay figures)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/civil-service-fast-stream","Supports the Joining government page","In 2025-26, fast streamers in their first year on the programme earn £31,554, while those in their fourth year on the scheme are paid £40,098."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service Fast Stream Annual Report 2025, Table 1","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68cd421fa1e4472207995dc1/Fast_Stream_Annual_Report_2025_V5.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","All schemes")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Apprenticeships: earn while you learn from age 16",text:"Civil Service apprenticeships pay you while you study for a recognised qualification, at levels 2 (GCSE equivalent) to 7 (master's equivalent), in professions including digital, cyber security, finance and science and engineering. They are open from age 16 to anyone who meets the nationality rules, and mid-career apprenticeships are offered to people changing career.",figure:"Levels 2 to 7",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"The old Civil Service Fast Track Apprenticeship has closed and is not a current route; look on Civil Service Jobs for departmental apprenticeships instead. The Career Launch apprenticeship for school leavers launched in 2025. The hub's Recruitment and career routes section covers apprenticeships as an entry route.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Apprenticeships (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/apprenticeships/","Supports the Joining government page","Apprenticeships can be undertaken in England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, with opportunities available at levels 2 to 7, and each level corresponding to a different educational qualification:"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Internship scheme to get more working class students into Civil Service (1 August 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/internship-scheme-to-get-more-working-class-students-into-civil-service","Supports the Joining government page","Earlier this year the Government launched a new Career Launch apprenticeship scheme targeted at young people and school leavers."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Fast Track Apprenticeship): No Fast Track application window in 2019 (22 February 2019; withdrawn 27 November 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/no-fast-track-application-window-in-2019","Supports the Joining government page","The Fast Track Apprenticeship programme is now closed."),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Cabinet Office halts Fast Track apprenticeship applications for 2019 (25 February 2019)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/cabinet-office-halts-fast-track-apprenticeship-applications-for-2019","Supports the Joining government page","The government’s flagship Fast Track civil service apprenticeship scheme will not open for applications in 2019 due to a review of the programme, the Cabinet Office has announced."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Experienced careers (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-experienced-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","We offer mid-career apprenticeships and professional qualifications in areas like business administration, commercial, digital, and finance.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Summer Internship Programme: paid placements for students",text:"The Summer Internship Programme (SIP) gives undergraduates from lower socio-economic backgrounds eight weeks of paid work in a department, at £475 a week. You need to be in your final two years of a degree, and a good appraisal can earn you a Fast Pass through the early Fast Stream stages.",figure:"£475 a week for 8 weeks",year:"2027 programme; applications close midday 5 November 2026",disputed:false,note:"SIP replaced the Summer Diversity Internship Programme (SDIP), which was open to ethnic minority and lower socio-economic background undergraduates and later also to disabled students. Since August 2025 SIP is for people from lower socio-economic backgrounds only, assessed using Office for National Statistics measures. You cannot apply for SIP and the Fast Stream in the same year. The hub's Schemes and programmes section also covers SIP; a separate Care Leavers Internship Scheme exists too.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Summer Internship Programme (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-summer-internship-programme/","Supports the Joining government page","The Fast Stream Summer Internship Programme (SIP) gives people from lower socio-economic backgrounds the opportunity to see what a career in the Civil Service is like."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Internship scheme to get more working class students into Civil Service (1 August 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/internship-scheme-to-get-more-working-class-students-into-civil-service","Supports the Joining government page","The programme will give roughly 200 undergraduates the opportunity to work in a Civil Service department for two months"),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Civil service internship scheme now targets only 'working class' applicants (1 August 2025)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/news/article/civil-service-internship-scheme-now-targets-only-working-class-applicants","Supports the Joining government page","For a long time there was the Civil Service Summer Diversity Internship Programme, which was initially for undergraduates from ethnic minority and lower socioeconomic backgrounds only")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Supported routes for people facing barriers to work",text:"Going Forward into Employment (GFiE) recruits people who face barriers to work, such as care leavers, carers, veterans and people with convictions, into real, ring-fenced roles with simpler applications. It runs under an exception to open competition that the Civil Service Commission allows for employment programmes.",figure:"",year:"2018 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"The hub's circumstances section covers this in more depth. The GFiE page gives two different totals for people recruited since 2018 (over 3,050 and over 3,600), so no headline figure is used here.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Going Forward into Employment (undated page, read 29 October 2026; map covers April 2022 to December 2025)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/about-gfie/","Supports the Joining government page","GFiE is a network of pathways across the Civil Service that civil servants can use to recruit people into the service who face barriers to employment."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Exceptions (last updated August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/exceptions/","Supports the Joining government page","The Going Forward into Employment (GFiE) Team, in the Cabinet Office, has delegated authority from the Commission to accredit new schemes for an agreed list of cohorts under Exception 2 (Support for government employment programmes).")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Join from outside government, or come in on a secondment",text:"You do not need government experience to apply, and roles at every level up to the Senior Civil Service are advertised to external candidates, though not every vacancy is open to them. If you want to keep your current job, a secondment lets you work in government for a fixed period, usually up to two years, while staying employed by your own organisation.",figure:"Secondments up to 2 years",year:"2018 rules; in force 2026",disputed:false,note:"Secondees go through the same pre-employment and security checks as new joiners. Schemes include GovXchange (local and central government) and a digital secondment programme.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Experienced careers (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-experienced-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","You do not need prior experience in government to join us."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service secondments (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/secondments/","Supports the Joining government page","A secondment allows you to gain valuable experience working within the Civil Service while maintaining your permanent employment with your organisation."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles 2018 (in force; PDF re-issued in new branding 17 March 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","Departments may make inward secondments from outside the Civil Service of up to two years.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Grades run from administrative assistant to Senior Civil Service",text:"There are seven broad grades: AA/AO, EO, HEO, SEO, Grade 7, Grade 6 and the Senior Civil Service. Civil Service Careers gives average starting salaries from about £23,000 at AA/AO to about £76,000 at SCS level, but pay depends on department, location and role.",figure:"£23,000 to £76,000 average starting salary",year:"Undated averages; 2025 medians",disputed:false,note:"Treat these as indicative only: the careers page does not date its figures, and the Institute for Government reports 2025 medians of around £24,500 (AO/AA) to almost £89,000 (SCS). Some departments use their own grade names, and job titles such as 'lead' mean different grades in different places.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service grades and salaries (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-grades-and-pay/","Supports the Joining government page","Average starting salary: £23,000"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service grades and salaries (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-grades-and-pay/","Supports the Joining government page","Average starting salary: £76,000"),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Civil service grades explainer (15 April 2025, updated 21 October 2025)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/civil-service-grades","Supports the Joining government page","Civil service pay varies from a median of around £24,500 at the AO/AA grade to almost £89,000 at the SCS level.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Digital, data and technology roles, including at GDS",text:"Government employs product managers, developers, designers, data scientists, architects and user researchers, and the capability framework sets out each role and the skills needed at every level. The Government Digital Service (GDS) builds GOV.UK and GOV.UK One Login and recruits across these professions.",figure:"",year:"Framework updated 30 September 2026",disputed:false,note:"The framework was previously called the Digital, Data and Technology (DDaT) Profession Capability Framework and now sits on a new web address under a new name. GDS has moved into the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport; some GDS pages still name its former department.",src:[SRCQ("UK government: Government Digital and Data Profession Capability Framework (last updated 30 September 2026)","https://understand-digital-data-roles-skills.service.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","Learn about the digital, data and technology roles in government, and the skills you need to do them."),SRCQ("UK government (GOV.UK): Working for GDS (updated 21 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/government-digital-service/about/recruitment","Supports the Joining government page","We are a team of more than 1,000 product managers, software engineers, designers, researchers, technical architects and other specialists working with colleagues across government to advance users’ experience of digital government."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Home (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","Are you curious about working in digital? You don’t need a traditional tech background to make an impact."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Government Digital Service (read 29 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/government-digital-service","Supports the Joining government page","GOV.UK One Login")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"jnapply",h:"Applying",intro:"Civil Service recruitment follows published rules, so you can find out exactly what will be assessed before you write a word. Read the advert closely: it tells you which elements of the Success Profile you need to show and how you will be tested.",points:[
  {h:"Success Profiles: the five things you may be assessed on",text:"Roles are assessed against up to five elements: behaviours, strengths, ability, experience and technical skills. Not every role uses all five; the job advert tells you which ones apply and how each will be tested.",figure:"5 elements",year:"Updated January 2025",disputed:false,note:"The hub's Recognition section maps contributions to Success Profiles behaviours. The GOV.UK candidate overview gives the same wording for 'technical' as for behaviours; Civil Service Careers defines technical as the demonstration of specific professional skills, knowledge or qualifications.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles, candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","Not all of the elements are relevant to every role. They will vary depending on the profession, level and type of role. You should read the job description carefully."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: About the application process (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","The demonstration of specific professional skills, knowledge or qualifications.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Write behaviour examples with the STAR method",text:"When an advert asks for behaviour examples, describe a real situation, what you did and how, and the result: official guidance suggests the STAR method (situation, task, action, result). Examples can come from work, volunteering or a hobby, and the online form cuts off anything over 250 words per example.",figure:"250 words per example",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Do not assume the panel knows your context: spell out how the example shows the behaviour being assessed.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles, candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","Consider using the STAR method which allows you to set the scene, show what and how you did and the overall outcome."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","The online application form will automatically delete any words which exceed the 250 allowed for each example."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A candidate's guide to artificial intelligence (AI) in recruitment (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/artificial-intelligence-and-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","ask AI to help you structure them using the STAR format or other ways to strengthen your examples")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"CVs and personal statements: match the essential criteria",text:"Your CV and personal statement are scored against the essential criteria in the advert, so only include what is relevant. Use specific examples with facts and figures, stay within the word limit, and you may be asked to remove details such as your name, age or gender so the sift is anonymous.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Anonymised (name-blind) CVs are used by some recruiters, not all; the advert or form will say. Work history can include volunteering and work experience.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: How to write your CV (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-write-your-cv/","Supports the Joining government page","Your skills and experience will be scored against the essential criteria in the job advert, so make sure your information is relevant."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: How to write your personal statement (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-write-your-ps/","Supports the Joining government page","try to include specific facts and figures that demonstrate the tangible results of your work"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles, candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","We may ask you to provide your CV in an anonymised format. This will omit information that could identify you, such as your name, age or gender.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The sift: aim for at least 4 out of 7 on each example",text:"A sift panel reads and scores your written evidence before deciding who to interview. Many organisations use a seven-point scale, and you generally need at least 4, 'acceptable demonstration', for each example to go further.",figure:"4 out of 7",year:"Updated January 2026",disputed:false,note:"Feedback is not usually available at sift stage. The same example can pass one sift and fail another, because each vacancy is a separate competition.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Generally to progress to the next stage a candidate should score at least 4 for each example."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: About the application process (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","We’ll assess your application against the requirements in the job advert.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Online tests: practise first with the free official versions",text:"You may be asked to take one or more online tests, such as verbal, numerical or judgement tests; the advert says which. Free practice tests are available on GOV.UK, no specialist knowledge is needed, and your answers must be your own.",figure:"8 commonly used tests",year:"Updated January 2026",disputed:false,note:"GOV.UK lists eight tests after adding the Civil Service Styles Assessment in January 2026; the Civil Service Careers page still lists seven.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Civil Service online tests (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/civil-service-online-tests","Supports the Joining government page","No specialist knowledge or experience is needed for our tests."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Assessments and interviews (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/assessments-and-interviews/","Supports the Joining government page","When you apply for a job in the Civil Service, we may assess you using a psychometric test.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Use AI to prepare, never to answer for you",text:"You can use AI tools to research a department, check spelling or practise interview questions. You must not use them to invent experience or to complete tests and live assessments, and applications can be rejected or withdrawn if you do.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A candidate's guide to artificial intelligence (AI) in recruitment (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/artificial-intelligence-and-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","Do not use AI to: inflate or invent your skills and experiences"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream guidance on the use of generative AI (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-how-to-apply/fs-gen-ai/","Supports the Joining government page","If we suspect that you have used Gen AI tools during your tests or assessments, your application may be withdrawn.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"By law, appointments are on merit after fair and open competition",text:"The Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010 requires Civil Service appointments to be made on merit after fair and open competition, and the independent Civil Service Commission sets the rules departments must follow. If you think a department has broken them, you can complain first to the department and then to the Commission.",figure:"",year:"2010 Act; 2018 Principles",disputed:false,note:"The Commission cannot re-score a competition or rule on feedback, security vetting or internal promotions.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles 2018 (in force; PDF re-issued in new branding 17 March 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","Merit means the appointment of the best available person judged against the published criteria for the role."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles 2018 (in force; PDF re-issued in new branding 17 March 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","Open competition means that appointment opportunities must be advertised publicly."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (last updated August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the Joining government page","We can hear a complaint from anyone who believes a department has breached the requirements of the Recruitment Principles.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"When roles can be filled without open competition",text:"The Recruitment Principles allow ten exceptions, including short temporary appointments, secondments, highly specialist skills and government employment programmes. Exceptions are meant to be rare, and senior or higher-paid appointments need the Commission's prior approval.",figure:"10 exception categories",year:"Reviewed November 2024 and December 2025",disputed:false,note:"Temporary or programme appointees at junior grades can sometimes be made permanent after 12 months.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Exceptions (last updated August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/exceptions/","Supports the Joining government page","The Recruitment Principles outline ten exception categories designed by us to meet the needs of the Civil Service for use where appropriate by departments."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles 2018 (in force; PDF re-issued in new branding 17 March 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","describe the circumstances in which the Commission may except appointments from the requirement to select on merit on the basis of fair and open competition.")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"jninterview",h:"Interview preparation",intro:"Civil Service interviews are structured: the panel scores you against the criteria in the advert, not on how polished you seem. Prepare evidence for behaviour and technical questions, and answer strength questions honestly and on the spot.",points:[
  {h:"Prepare real examples for behaviour questions",text:"Before the interview, reread the job description and plan examples showing each behaviour listed. You can reuse examples from your application or choose stronger ones, and think about how you would handle similar situations in future. Interviews usually last 30 to 60 minutes.",figure:"Interviews usually 30 to 60 minutes",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Guidance gives slightly different lengths: GOV.UK says 30 to 60 minutes and Civil Service Careers says 45 to 60 minutes.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","You can use the same examples you wrote about in your application or new ones if they are a better answer to the question asked."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Assessments and interviews (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/assessments-and-interviews/","Supports the Joining government page","Before you attend an interview, look carefully at the job description and think about examples you can give of times when you have previously demonstrated the behaviours outlined.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Strength questions: answer genuinely, do not rehearse",text:"Strength questions ask what you enjoy and do well, and there are no right or wrong answers. The panel wants your first, honest response, so the best preparation is to reflect on your strengths and how you like to work rather than learning answers.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Strengths are not tied to grade, and you will never be expected to show every strength in the Civil Service Strengths Dictionary.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A guide to Civil Service Success Profiles (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/a-guide-to-civil-service-success-profiles/","Supports the Joining government page","Do not rehearse your answers – because we are looking for your initial response."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles, candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","Before your interview it would be helpful to reflect on what you feel are your personal strengths and preferred ways of working.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Technical interviews, presentations and exercises",text:"For specialist roles, such as digital and data jobs, you may face technical questions, a presentation, a test or a work sample. The topic may be sent in advance or given on the day, and assessors usually ask follow-up questions about it.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Hiring managers can use the digital and data capability framework to write adverts and assess candidates at interview, so reading your role's page helps you see what level is expected.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles, candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","It is likely that the assessors will ask you questions about your presentation."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A guide to Civil Service Success Profiles (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/a-guide-to-civil-service-success-profiles/","Supports the Joining government page","If the role you are applying for is within a profession, additional information about the technical requirements for that role will be included within the job description."),SRCQ("UK government: Government Digital and Data Profession Capability Framework (last updated 30 September 2026)","https://understand-digital-data-roles-skills.service.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","assess the suitability of a candidate during interview")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Video and pre-recorded interviews",text:"Some interviews happen by video, either live or pre-recorded, where you record answers to questions shown on screen. Check your set-up beforehand and treat it with the same preparation as a face-to-face interview.",figure:"2 types of video interview",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"AI tools must not be used to produce answers during live or recorded interviews.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Assessments and interviews (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/assessments-and-interviews/","Supports the Joining government page","a pre-recorded interview where you will be asked to pre-record answers in response to interview questions presented to you via an online platform."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles, candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","This may be face to face but sometimes we use telephone, video or recorded interviews.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Assessment centres combine several exercises",text:"Some roles and schemes use an assessment centre with a mix of interview, presentation, written exercise or role play, lasting half a day or a full day. Your invitation explains what will happen; the Fast Stream's virtual centre includes a written advice exercise and a stakeholder exercise.",figure:"",year:"2026 to 2027 Fast Stream cycle",disputed:false,note:"Ask for reasonable adjustments early if you need them, for example extra time or materials in another format.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Assessments and interviews (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/assessments-and-interviews/","Supports the Joining government page","An assessment centre will usually consist of a combination of different selection methods, such as an interview, a presentation, a written exercise and/or a role-play exercise."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream, understanding the application process (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","The assessment centre will take place over half a day – it will consist of a written advice exercise, a stakeholder communication exercise, and a personal development conversation.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Bring your own questions: the interview works both ways",text:"Use the interview to find out whether the team and organisation suit you, for example about the work, ways of working or development. Before applying, you can also contact the person named on the advert with questions about the job.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","This is also your chance to see if the role and the recruiting organisation are right for you."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: About the application process (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","Contact details are provided, in case you have any questions about the job.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Always ask for feedback, whatever the outcome",text:"After the final stage you will be told the outcome, and you are encouraged to ask for feedback even if you are successful. Test feedback is available in your Civil Service Jobs account.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Feedback is generally not available at the sift stage.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Assessments and interviews (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/assessments-and-interviews/","Supports the Joining government page","At this final stage, we encourage you to ask for feedback, regardless of the outcome of your application, as this can help you in the future."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Civil Service online tests (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/civil-service-online-tests","Supports the Joining government page","Once you have completed the test, you can access test feedback by going to your account on Civil Service Jobs.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Reserve lists: a near miss can still lead to a job",text:"Panels rank appointable candidates in order of merit. If there are more good candidates than jobs, you can be placed on a reserve list and offered the same or a similar role for up to 12 months without being tested again.",figure:"Up to 12 months",year:"2018 Principles, in force 2026",disputed:false,note:"Being on a reserve list is not a job offer; whether you are contacted depends on vacancies arising.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles 2018 (in force; PDF re-issued in new branding 17 March 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","This may be used for up to 12 months to fill the same role or other similar roles with closely matching essential criteria without further testing of merit."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A guide to Civil Service Success Profiles (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/a-guide-to-civil-service-success-profiles/","Supports the Joining government page","Ultimately using Success Profiles gives us a merit list that means we have the best possible chance of selecting the right person for the right role.")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"trwhat",h:"What to record for each application",intro:"Civil Service recruitment runs in stages, often over several months, and you may have several campaigns open at once. A short, consistent record for each one stops you missing a step and gives you material for the next application.",points:[
  {h:"Start with the vacancy's own identifiers: reference, department, grade",text:"Record the vacancy reference number, the job title, the recruiting organisation, the grade and the closing date for every application. Grades are not identical across departments, so note the grade exactly as the advert words it. You can only apply once for each vacancy, so a clear record also stops you starting a duplicate application.",figure:"",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Grade names such as AO, EO, HEO, SEO, Grade 7 and Grade 6 are a guide only; each organisation may use its own structure.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","The grading structure within the Civil Service varies as each organisation may have its own grading structure."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","You are not allowed to apply for a job more than once, so you should make sure your application is correct before submission."),SRCQ("Lancaster University Management School Careers: Managing your job search (12 February 2024)","https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/lums/careers/blogs/managing-your-job-search","Supports the Joining government page","Name the job descriptions with the job reference number, position, and organisation that you're applying for.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Save the advert and the exact version you submitted",text:"Adverts are often taken down after they close, and you cannot change an application once it is submitted. Save a copy of the advert, the job description and the exact text, CV or statement you sent, and note which version went where. You will need them to prepare for interview and to reuse good material later.",figure:"",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","You cannot make changes to a submitted application, so you should make sure your application is correct before submission."),SRCQ("National Careers Service: Application forms (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://nationalcareers.service.gov.uk/careers-advice/application-forms","Supports the Joining government page","You should keep a copy of your answers so you can use it for future applications and refer back to it if you're invited to an interview."),SRCQ("Careers Wales: Write applications (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://careerswales.gov.wales/getting-a-job/write-applications","Supports the Joining government page","Save a copy of the job application."),SRCQ("Lancaster University Management School Careers: Managing your job search (12 February 2024)","https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/lums/careers/blogs/managing-your-job-search","Supports the Joining government page","it's good practice to save the advertisements for jobs that you've applied for so you can reference or reflect on the organisation's request once the job posting has been taken down.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Note which Success Profiles elements each stage assesses",text:"The Civil Service recruits using Success Profiles, which has five elements: Behaviours, Strengths, Ability, Experience and Technical. Each advert chooses which elements it assesses and at which stage, and behaviours are pitched at the grade of the role. Write down the elements and behaviours named in the advert so you can match your examples and your feedback to them.",figure:"5 elements",year:"2018 framework, current 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: How to apply (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","Our Success Profile is made up of five elements:"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles: candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","Civil Service behaviours are tailored to the grade level of the job role."),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide, Our application process (last updated 31 August 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/our-application-process/","Supports the Joining government page","Each stage is designed to assess candidates against the Success Profiles framework")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Log every stage, its date and its result",text:"A campaign can include a sift, online tests, an interview, a presentation or an assessment centre, and not every campaign uses all of them. For each stage record the date you were invited, the date it took place, any score you were given and the outcome. A significant number of organisations use the Civil Service rating scale of 1 to 7, and generally a score of at least 4 is needed to progress.",figure:"Scale 1 to 7; 4 usually needed",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Rating scales can vary between recruiting organisations; check the advert or your results email.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: How to apply (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","Some roles only require an interview, a presentation, an online test or an assessment centre but for others, you might need to complete all of these elements."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Generally to progress to the next stage a candidate should score at least 4 for each example."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles: candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","Part of the assessment process will usually involve an interview."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","A significant number of organisations use this rating scale for recruitment.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"If you are put on a reserve list, record the date and when it ends",text:"When a panel finds more appointable candidates than jobs, it can hold the others on a reserve list and offer them the same or a similar role later without testing them again. Reserve lists last up to 12 months, so note the date you were told and the date the list will expire. A reserve list place is not a job offer.",figure:"Up to 12 months",year:"2018 Principles, in force 2026",disputed:false,note:"Whether you are contacted depends on vacancies arising during the life of the list.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles (April 2018 edition, the version the Commission links as current in August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","This may be used for up to 12 months to fill the same role or other similar roles with closely matching essential criteria without further testing of merit."),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide, Use of reserve lists (last updated 31 August 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/use-of-reserve-lists/","Supports the Joining government page","Reserve lists typically operate for a maximum of 1 year, after which they are no longer used.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Track the offer, each pre-employment check and the start date",text:"A successful candidate first receives a provisional offer, and a formal offer only follows once pre-employment checks are complete. Record the date of the provisional offer, the documents you sent and when, each check and its outcome, the date of the formal offer and the agreed start date. The checks can take several weeks.",figure:"Several weeks (indicative)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Higher security clearances add time; see the deadlines section.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","If you are successful you'll receive a provisional offer of employment, pending satisfactory completion of pre-employment checks."),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide, Pre-employment checks (last updated 31 August 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/pre-employment-checks/","Supports the Joining government page","Candidates will be advised of the outcome of the pre-employment checks process and advised of next steps.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"trtools",h:"Ways to keep track",intro:"No single method suits everyone; most people combine the Civil Service Jobs account with a simple record of their own. The hub describes types of tool only and endorses no product or service.",points:[
  {h:"Use the Applications page in your Civil Service Jobs account",text:"If you applied on the Civil Service Jobs site itself, you can follow the application from the Applications page of your account and open each job for more detail. Online test feedback is also held in your account. If your contact email changes after you apply, update it once in Account details and it carries through to your submitted applications.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","You can track your application through Civil Service Jobs if it was completed on the site."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","The address will automatically update in all applications you have submitted through the site."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Civil Service online tests (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/civil-service-online-tests","Supports the Joining government page","Once you have completed the test, you can access test feedback by going to your account on Civil Service Jobs.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Keep your own record for anything applied for elsewhere",text:"Some organisations take applications on their own websites or by email, and those applications do not appear in your Civil Service Jobs account. Formal offers from those organisations are not shown there either. Your own record is the only place where every application sits together.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Any applications you made using the \"Apply at advertiser's site\" process or emailed directly to an address specified in the advert are not included."),SRCQ("Lancaster University Management School Careers: Managing your job search (12 February 2024)","https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/lums/careers/blogs/managing-your-job-search","Supports the Joining government page","Tracking what you're putting out is very important so that you know what to follow up on and to track any communication sent to you.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Let job alerts bring vacancies to you, and pause them when needed",text:"Civil Service Jobs can email you new vacancies that match saved search criteria, as often as you choose. You can pause, restart, edit or delete alerts, or run a search from an alert. Closing your account withdraws applications in progress and stops alerts, so save copies of past applications first.",figure:"First alert may arrive within 24 hours (daily setting)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Civil Service Jobs will email you with any new matches to your search criteria."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","You may want to keep a copy of your previous applications, as once your account is closed you'll no longer be able to access this information."),SRCQ("Careers Wales: Job websites (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://careerswales.gov.wales/getting-a-job/job-websites","Supports the Joining government page","Jobs in the Civil Service and central government organisations")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"A spreadsheet tracker: one row per application",text:"A spreadsheet with one row per application is a simple way to see everything at once: role, organisation, location, link, date applied, closing date, stage and status. University careers blogs suggest adding follow-up reminders and protecting the file with a password, because it holds personal information. Any spreadsheet program works; the hub endorses none.",figure:"",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Lancaster University Management School Careers: Managing your job search (12 February 2024)","https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/lums/careers/blogs/managing-your-job-search","Supports the Joining government page","Create a spreadsheet to track your applications."),SRCQ("Lancaster University Management School Careers: Managing your job search (12 February 2024)","https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/lums/careers/blogs/managing-your-job-search","Supports the Joining government page","Protect this file with a password as you'll be saving sensitive information in it."),SRCQ("University of Liverpool Careers and Employability: Step-by-step guide for students applying for jobs in the UK (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/careers/students/blog/hits-tip-advice/step-by-stepguideforstudentsapplyingforjobsintheuk/","Supports the Joining government page","Create a spreadsheet to log details such as company names, job roles, opening and closing dates, application stages, and follow-up reminders."),SRCQ("National Careers Service: Application forms (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://nationalcareers.service.gov.uk/careers-advice/application-forms","Supports the Joining government page","You should keep a copy of your answers so you can use it for future applications and refer back to it if you're invited to an interview.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Calendar reminders and a task list for the steps in between",text:"Put every closing date, test window, interview and document deadline in a calendar with a reminder. A short to-do list that breaks each application into smaller steps can help: the NHS says breaking tasks into manageable chunks and planning ahead can help relieve stress.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles: candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","You can usually take the tests at any time within a specific number of days."),SRCQ("NHS Every Mind Matters: Stress (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-health-issues/stress/","Supports the Joining government page","You might feel less stressed if you can take practical steps, such as breaking a task down into easier, more manageable chunks."),SRCQ("NHS Every Mind Matters: Stress (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-health-issues/stress/","Supports the Joining government page","Planning ahead for upcoming stressful days or events - creating a to-do list, planning your journey and listing things you need to take - can really help to relieve stress.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"One folder for adverts, applications and letters",text:"Keep one main folder with subfolders for job descriptions, application materials, unfinished applications, offers and rejection emails. Name each file with the vacancy reference, title and organisation so it matches your tracker. If you claim Jobseeker's Allowance, the same record helps you show your work coach your applications and interviews.",figure:"",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Lancaster University Management School Careers: Managing your job search (12 February 2024)","https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/lums/careers/blogs/managing-your-job-search","Supports the Joining government page","Save everything in one main folder and have subfolders for the smaller details like job descriptions, application materials, incomplete applications, and rejection emails."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: New Style Jobseeker's Allowance, Your JSA claim (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/jobseekers-allowance/your-jsa-claim","Supports the Joining government page","At these appointments, you must show your work coach what you've been doing to look for work, for example proof of job applications and interviews.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"trdates",h:"Key timelines and deadlines",intro:"Some timings in Civil Service recruitment are fixed by rules; many vary by campaign. Treat the figures below as the official position or as indicative, as labelled, and always follow the dates in your own advert and emails.",points:[
  {h:"Closing times: often 23:55 on the closing date",text:"Guidance for departments that publish adverts on Civil Service Jobs tells them to use 23:55 as the closing time, so many adverts close five minutes before midnight. Do not plan to submit in the last hour; check the closing date and time shown on the advert itself. If you need adjustments, contact the advert's named contact well before the closing date.",figure:"23:55 (indicative)",year:"2022 guidance, read 2026",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing), guidance for advert-only departments last updated in June 2022; the advert's own closing time always takes precedence.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Create and publish job adverts (vX), guidance for recruiters (updated 22 June 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/create-and-publish-job-adverts","Supports the Joining government page","Confirm that the Preferred vacancy closing date is correct - use 23:55 as the closing time."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","approach the point of contact in the job advert as soon as possible before the closing date to discuss your needs")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Sift and interview dates vary by campaign",text:"There is no standard time between the closing date and the sift result, or between sift and interview. The recruiting organisation tells you what to expect at each stage, and interviews usually last 30 to 60 minutes. Record the dates you are given so you can see when a stage is overdue and ask the named contact.",figure:"Interview 30 to 60 minutes",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Timescales for completing a sift can vary."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: How to apply (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","We'll let you know what to expect at this stage as it can be different for each job."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles: candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","It will usually last 30-60 minutes.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Online tests have their own completion window",text:"If you are invited to take an online test, you will be sent an email invitation with instructions, and it will usually say how many days you have to complete it. The test may be timed, so choose a time when you can give it your full attention. Put the window's last day in your calendar as soon as the email arrives.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles: candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","You can usually take the tests at any time within a specific number of days. The email sent to you will explain this."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Civil Service online tests (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/civil-service-online-tests","Supports the Joining government page","Meeting the minimum required percentile is no guarantee of an invitation to continue the selection process."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Civil Service online tests (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/civil-service-online-tests","Supports the Joining government page","If you apply for a job and need to complete a test, you will be sent an email invitation, with full instructions on how to proceed.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Reserve lists last up to 12 months",text:"The Recruitment Principles allow a reserve list to be used for up to 12 months. The Scottish Government says its reserve lists typically operate for a maximum of one year and that candidates are contacted if a suitable post arises. Note the expiry date in your tracker.",figure:"Up to 12 months",year:"2018 Principles, in force 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles (April 2018 edition, the version the Commission links as current in August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","This may be used for up to 12 months to fill the same role or other similar roles with closely matching essential criteria without further testing of merit."),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide, Use of reserve lists (last updated 31 August 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/use-of-reserve-lists/","Supports the Joining government page","Candidates placed on a reserve list will be notified by the recruitment team and contacted if a suitable position becomes available.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Pre-employment checks: several weeks, longer with vetting",text:"Baseline checks can take several weeks, and roles needing national security vetting can take longer. United Kingdom Security Vetting says every clearance application is unique, its checks involve several agencies, and it cannot give exact timescales. Both GOV.UK and the vetting guidance say not to hand in your notice until you have a formal offer or a decision.",figure:"Several weeks (indicative)",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"No official source publishes a standard number of days for checks or clearance; any figure you see elsewhere should be read as indicative.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","These checks can take several weeks. If you are in employment do not hand in your notice until a formal job offer is made."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (United Kingdom Security Vetting): United Kingdom Security Vetting, applicant guidance (updated 26 July 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/united-kingdom-security-vetting-applicant","Supports the Joining government page","Every security clearance application is unique and can take varying amounts of time to complete."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (United Kingdom Security Vetting): SC guidance pack for applicants (updated 23 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/united-kingdom-security-vetting-clearance-levels/sc-guidance-pack-for-applicants","Supports the Joining government page","If you are currently employed, please don't hand in your notice until you have been contacted with a decision.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Notice periods: check your contract before you resign",text:"Your contract or written statement sets the notice you must give your current employer. By law the minimum is one week once you have worked there for at least a month, but many contracts require more. Record your notice period so you can agree a realistic start date. This is general information, not legal advice.",figure:"Statutory minimum 1 week",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Acas can advise on exceptions to the statutory minimum.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Handing in your notice, Giving notice (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/handing-in-your-notice/giving-notice","Supports the Joining government page","You must give at least a week's notice if you've been in your job for more than a month."),SRCQ("Acas: Notice when resigning (last updated 24 November 2025)","https://www.acas.org.uk/notice-periods/notice-when-resigning","Supports the Joining government page","If they have worked for at least 1 month, they must give a minimum of 1 weeks' notice."),SRCQ("Acas: Notice when resigning (last updated 24 November 2025)","https://www.acas.org.uk/notice-periods/notice-when-resigning","Supports the Joining government page","The employee's contract, written statement of employment particulars or a staff policy might say an employee needs to give more notice than the statutory minimum to resign.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Complaints about fair recruitment: within 12 months of closing",text:"If you believe a department broke the Recruitment Principles, complain to the department first, following the steps in the advert. You can then take it to the Civil Service Commission, which expects complaints within 12 months of the closing date for applications. Keeping dated records makes this possible. This is general information, not legal advice.",figure:"12 months from closing date",year:"2018 Principles, in force 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Commission may consider a complaint lodged after 12 months in exceptional circumstances. It does not re-score competitions and does not consider feedback, pre-employment checks or vetting, or internal promotions.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles (April 2018 edition, the version the Commission links as current in August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","Complaints should be lodged with the Commission within 12 months of the closing date for applications"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (last updated August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the Joining government page","You can refer your complaint to us if you have taken the prescribed steps as outlined in the job advert and you remain dissatisfied with the outcome."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment Principles 2018","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/document/02a-recruitment-principles-april-2018-final/","Supports the Joining government page","Complaints should be lodged with the Commission within 12 months of the closing date for applications, but the Commission will consider complaints lodged out of time in exceptional circumstances.")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"trfeedback",h:"Getting and using feedback, and looking after yourself",intro:"Feedback turns each application into practice for the next. A long search can also be tiring, so build in support for yourself as well as for your applications.",points:[
  {h:"Ask for feedback whatever the outcome",text:"Civil Service Careers encourages every candidate to ask for feedback after the final stage, whether or not they were successful. Careers services say the same for any interview. Note in your tracker when you asked, who you asked and when it arrived.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: How to apply (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/how-to-apply/","Supports the Joining government page","We encourage you to ask for feedback, regardless of the outcome, as this can really help you in the future."),SRCQ("National Careers Service: Interview advice (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://nationalcareers.service.gov.uk/careers-advice/interview-advice","Supports the Joining government page","You can also ask the interviewer for feedback on how it went and where you could have done better."),SRCQ("Careers Wales: Interview techniques (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://careerswales.gov.wales/getting-a-job/interview-techniques","Supports the Joining government page","Ask the employer for feedback on how your interview went and how you could improve.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Know what feedback to expect at each stage",text:"Feedback is generally not available after the sift, though some organisations give comments against the rating scale. Online test feedback appears in your Civil Service Jobs account, with a report for the Judgement Test and Styles Assessment. The guidance sets no deadline for asking, so ask promptly.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The Civil Service Commission does not consider complaints about the provision of feedback.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Feedback is generally not available at sift stage, but recruiting organisations may provide comments in line with the Civil Service rating scale."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service Resourcing): Civil Service online tests (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/civil-service-online-tests","Supports the Joining government page","For the Judgment Test and Styles Assessment you will receive a feedback report to help you understand your test performance."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (last updated August 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the Joining government page","Provision of feedback;")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Record feedback against the scale and the Success Profiles element",text:"Write feedback down against the element or behaviour it refers to and, where given, the score on the 1 to 7 scale. Over several campaigns this shows which behaviours you score well on and which need stronger examples. Success Profiles is designed in part to give candidates standardised feedback to support their development.",figure:"Scale 1 to 7",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A guide to Civil Service Success Profiles (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/a-guide-to-civil-service-success-profiles/","Supports the Joining government page","Lastly it allows us to provide standardized feedback to candidates to help them to develop their own personal success profile and development plan."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Generally to progress to the next stage a candidate should score at least 4 for each example.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Build a bank of STAR examples and reuse the best",text:"Keep your examples in one document, each written in the STAR shape: situation, task, action and result, with the behaviour it shows and where you used it. You can use the same example at interview as in your application, or a better one. Update the bank after each round of feedback.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Success Profiles: candidate overview (updated 29 January 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles/success-profiles-candidate-overview","Supports the Joining government page","Consider using the STAR method which allows you to set the scene, show what and how you did and the overall outcome."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","You can use the same examples you wrote about in your application or new ones if they are a better answer to the question asked."),SRCQ("Careers Wales: Interview techniques (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://careerswales.gov.wales/getting-a-job/interview-techniques","Supports the Joining government page","STAR stands for Situation, Task, Action, Result.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Treat setbacks as information, and set small goals",text:"Being unsuccessful is a normal part of a search, and it is normal to apply for several roles before getting an interview. Reflect on what happened, use the feedback, and set yourself small goals so you can see progress, celebrating your achievements as you go.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("National Careers Service: Application forms (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://nationalcareers.service.gov.uk/careers-advice/application-forms","Supports the Joining government page","It's normal to apply for several different roles before getting an interview."),SRCQ("National Careers Service: How to bounce back in a tough jobs market (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://nationalcareers.service.gov.uk/careers-advice/how-to-bounce-back-in-a-tough-jobs-market","Supports the Joining government page","set yourself small goals and celebrate your achievements as you go"),SRCQ("University of Liverpool Careers and Employability: Step-by-step guide for students applying for jobs in the UK (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/careers/students/blog/hits-tip-advice/step-by-stepguideforstudentsapplyingforjobsintheuk/","Supports the Joining government page","Reflect on your performance and identify areas for improvement for your next interview.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Look after your wellbeing during a long search",text:"Job problems are among the possible causes of stress. Talk to people you trust, keep active, and try slow breathing before an interview. A low mood that lasts longer than about two weeks may be a sign of depression; if low mood or stress is affecting your daily life, call NHS 111 or talk to a GP. In England you can refer yourself to NHS talking therapies.",figure:"About 2 weeks",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"If you feel unable to cope or keep yourself safe, get urgent help straight away.",src:[SRCQ("NHS Every Mind Matters: Stress (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-health-issues/stress/","Supports the Joining government page","money worries , housing issues or job problems"),SRCQ("NHS Every Mind Matters: Low mood (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/mental-health-issues/low-mood/","Supports the Joining government page","A low mood should lift after a few days, but if it lasts longer than about 2 weeks, it may be a sign of depression."),SRCQ("NHS Every Mind Matters: Work-related stress (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/work-related-stress/","Supports the Joining government page","You can also use it before situations that might make you feel anxious or nervous, like a meeting or an interview.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"You do not have to do it alone",text:"Careers advisers, mentors, a manager or HR, or a Jobcentre Plus work coach if you are not in work, can help after a setback. Careers services such as the National Careers Service in England and Careers Wales offer advice on applications and interviews.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("National Careers Service: How to bounce back in a tough jobs market (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://nationalcareers.service.gov.uk/careers-advice/how-to-bounce-back-in-a-tough-jobs-market","Supports the Joining government page","If you're not in work, you could speak to a Jobcentre Plus work coach ."),SRCQ("Careers Wales: Write applications (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://careerswales.gov.wales/getting-a-job/write-applications","Supports the Joining government page","Contact us for advice and support with filling in job applications.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"jnday",h:"A typical day and life at work",intro:"Civil servants describe days that vary with the job, from code reviews and stand-ups in a digital team to briefing ministers on policy. Alongside the work come shared rules on office attendance, a defined benefit pension, leave and guaranteed time for learning.",points:[
  {h:"No typical day: what digital and early careers staff describe",text:"One GDS developer described a day that starts with a stand-up, then coding, code reviews, planning and meetings, some of them for staff clubs or networks. Fast streamers describe moving between departments and roles and taking on responsibility early.",figure:"",year:"2021 and 2026",disputed:false,note:"The GDS developer accounts date from 2021, during pandemic remote working, so office patterns have since changed. These are individual experiences, not a standard day.",src:[SRCQ("Government Digital Service blog: Working at GDS as a developer (15 September 2021)","https://gds.blog.gov.uk/2021/09/15/working-at-gds-as-a-developer/","Supports the Joining government page","My day starts with a “stand-up”, where we share what we plan to work on and see if anybody needs help."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: The Civil Service Fast Stream (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/","Supports the Joining government page","You get a lot of responsibility from the get-go, so you’ll be managing a lot of research projects, maybe, or a lot of contracts.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Hybrid working: at least 60% of your time in the office",text:"Most civil servants are expected to spend at least 60% of their time in a government building or on official business, with senior managers expected to do more. Within that, many roles offer flexible patterns such as part-time, compressed hours and job sharing.",figure:"60% minimum",year:"Confirmed October 2024; reaffirmed February 2026",disputed:false,note:"Not every department can meet the rule because of office space, and the FDA union says most of its surveyed members think the mandate has not worked. Check each advert, as some roles differ.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Civil Service continues commitment to minimum 60% office attendance (24 October 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/civil-service-continues-commitment-to-minimum-60-office-attendance","Supports the Joining government page","Guidance on office attendance will remain in place, with most civil servants expected to spend at least 60% of their time at a Government building or on official business, such as visiting stakeholders."),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Hybrid working, GPA helping departments to meet 60% mandate (27 February 2026)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/hybrid-working-departments-working-with-gpa-to-ensure-all-organisations-can-meet-60-mandate","Supports the Joining government page","In its response to the report, the government said the 60% approach “offers the best balance for most employees”"),SRCQ("FDA (union): 78% of civil servants believe office attendance mandate has failed (undated news page, report published February 2025)","https://www.fda.org.uk/news/future-office-work/","Supports the Joining government page","78% of respondents believe the 60% mandate overall has not been beneficial")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Pension: alpha, a career average defined benefit scheme",text:"New joiners usually join alpha, which adds 2.32% of your pensionable pay to your pension each year, increased in line with inflation. Your own contributions depend on your pay band, starting at 4.6% on earnings up to £36,199 in 2026-27.",figure:"2.32% of pay a year",year:"Rates for April 2026 to March 2027",disputed:false,note:"Contribution rates are reviewed each year, so check the current rates. A defined contribution option called Partnership is also available.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Pension Scheme: alpha contributions explained factsheet (contribution rates for 1 April 2025 to 31 March 2026)","https://www.civilservicepensionscheme.org.uk/alpha-contributions-explained-factsheet","Supports the Joining government page","You build up alpha pension by adding 2.32% of your actual Pensionable Earnings from each scheme year to your alpha pension."),SRCQ("British Medical Association: Civil service pension scheme, Alpha scheme (updated 19 April 2024)","https://www.bma.org.uk/pay-and-contracts/pensions/additional-pensions-advice/civil-service-pension-scheme-alpha-scheme","Supports the Joining government page","The Alpha scheme has an accrual rate of 2.32% of each year's actual pensionable pay going towards a pension."),SRCQ("Civil Service Pension Scheme: Contribution rates, member contribution rates 2026/2027 (read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civilservicepensionscheme.org.uk/memberhub/joining-the-pension-scheme/contribution-rates/","Supports the Joining government page","Member contribution rates 2026/2027")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Annual leave usually starts at 25 days and rises to 30",text:"Many departments give 25 days' annual leave on joining, rising to 30 with length of service, plus public and privilege holidays. How quickly it rises differs between departments.",figure:"25 rising to 30 days",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"There is no single Civil Service rule here: GDS adds a day each year, while the Valuation Office Agency reaches 30 days after five years.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: What it's like to work at GDS (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/gds-what-its-like-to-work-at-gds/","Supports the Joining government page","25 days annual leave – increasing by a day each year up to 30 days – plus an extra day off for the King’s birthday"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Food Standards Agency): Working for FSA (updated 26 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/food-standards-agency/about/recruitment","Supports the Joining government page","We offer a very generous annual leave package, starting at 25 days, plus nine days’ public and privilege holidays, rising to 30 days with length of service."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Valuation Office Agency): Working for VOA (updated 31 July 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/valuation-office-agency/about/recruitment","Supports the Joining government page","Annual Leave – 25 days of annual leave, rising to 30 days after five years")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Learning: at least 5 days a year, plus Civil Service Learning",text:"Every civil servant is guaranteed five days a year for learning and development, with courses on Civil Service Learning, coaching, job shadowing and professional qualifications. Some organisations offer more, such as 10 days and a personal training budget at GDS.",figure:"5 days a year",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Working for the Civil Service (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/workingforthecivilservice/","Supports the Joining government page","access to Civil Service Learning courses and a guaranteed 5 days dedicated to your learning and development per year"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: What it's like to work at GDS (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/gds-what-its-like-to-work-at-gds/","Supports the Joining government page","A minimum of 10 paid working days a year for you to focus on your learning and development")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"One Big Thing: a yearly learning push for every civil servant",text:"One Big Thing is a shared learning activity that every civil servant is asked to join each year. The 2025 edition, 'AI for All', ran from October 2025 to February 2026 and covered using AI safely and responsibly at work.",figure:"",year:"13 October 2025 to 13 February 2026",disputed:false,note:"Earlier themes were data (2023) and innovation (2024). No 2026 edition had been announced by late October 2026.",src:[SRCQ("UK government (Modern Civil Service campaign): One Big Thing (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://moderncivilservice.campaign.gov.uk/one-big-thing/","Supports the Joining government page","Launched on 13 October 2025 and running until 13 February 2026, One Big Thing 2025 invites all civil servants to:"),SRCQ("Civil Service World: All civil servants to get AI training (9 June 2025)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/civil-servants-ai-training-one-big-thing-2025","Supports the Joining government page","Last year's focus was innovation, while the 2023 One Big Thing was focused on data.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Staff networks and the Civil Service Race Champion",text:"Departments have volunteer staff networks for wellbeing, equality and professions, and there are more than 20 cross-government networks for people from under-represented groups. The Civil Service also has a Race Champion, who marked Black History Month 2025 by setting out progress on representation.",figure:"Over 20 cross-government networks",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Race Champion role was held by the Civil Service Chief Operating Officer, Cat Little, in October 2025.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Home (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","We have over 20 cross-government staff networks. They help people from under-represented groups to feel a sense of community and find support."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service staff networks (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-networks/","Supports the Joining government page","There are also a number of cross Civil Service staff networks."),SRCQ("Civil Service blog: Black History Month 2025, standing firm in power and pride (2 October 2025)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2025/10/02/black-history-month-2025-standing-firm-in-power-and-pride/","Supports the Joining government page","the percentage of civil servants from ethnically diverse backgrounds has risen from 10.1% in 2014 to 18% today")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"jnvet",h:"Checks before you start",intro:"Every offer is provisional until your pre-employment checks are complete, and some roles also need national security vetting. Knowing what is checked, and how long it can take, helps you plan your notice and start date.",points:[
  {h:"Nationality rules decide which jobs you can apply for",text:"UK nationals can apply for any Civil Service job, and about three quarters of posts are also open to Commonwealth and European Economic Area nationals. The rest, which need special allegiance to the state, are reserved for UK nationals; each advert states who is eligible.",figure:"About 75% of posts",year:"Rules updated August 2023",disputed:false,note:"The hub's circumstances section covers this in detail. Rules for European Economic Area nationals changed from 1 January 2021, so check the full rules and the advert for your own case.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Civil Service): Civil Service recruitment, nationality rules (updated 8 August 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules","Supports the Joining government page","You can apply for any job in the Civil Service as long as you are a UK national or have dual nationality with one part being British."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Apprenticeships (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/apprenticeships/","Supports the Joining government page","to anyone that meets CS nationality rules (further restrictions may apply)")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Baseline Personnel Security Standard (BPSS): four checks",text:"Everyone joining government goes through the Baseline Personnel Security Standard: identity, right to work, employment history and unspent criminal convictions. Your employment, study or other activity is usually checked for at least the last three years.",figure:"4 checks; 3 years of history",year:"Version 7.0, June 2024",disputed:false,note:"Gaps of six months or more in the last three years need references or evidence, so keep records of study, caring or time abroad.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): UK Government Baseline Personnel Security Standard, version 7.0 (June 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-baseline-personnel-security-standard/uk-government-baseline-personnel-security-standard-version-70-june-2024-html","Supports the Joining government page","BPSS comprises four main checks for an individual."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): UK Government Baseline Personnel Security Standard, version 7.0 (June 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-baseline-personnel-security-standard/uk-government-baseline-personnel-security-standard-version-70-june-2024-html","Supports the Joining government page","Verify an individual’s disclosed employment, academic history and/or qualifications (where applicable), for a minimum of three years prior to BPSS checks being conducted."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (United Kingdom Security Vetting): National security vetting, clearance levels (collection updated 23 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/united-kingdom-security-vetting-clearance-levels/national-security-vetting-clearance-levels","Supports the Joining government page","Individuals with any access to government assets require BPSS. This means all civil servants, members of the armed forces, temporary staff in departments, and government contractors.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Some roles need security clearance: CTC, SC or DV",text:"Roles with regular access to sensitive information need national security vetting on top of BPSS. Security Check (SC) covers regular access to SECRET material and Developed Vetting (DV) covers TOP SECRET; both include criminal record, credit and Security Service checks, and DV adds an interview.",figure:"5 main vetting levels",year:"Updated September 2024",disputed:false,note:"Clearance can add weeks or months before you start; the Summer Internship Programme warns SC can take up to 3 months.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (United Kingdom Security Vetting): United Kingdom Security Vetting, clearance levels (updated 23 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/united-kingdom-security-vetting-clearance-levels","Supports the Joining government page","There are 5 main levels of national security vetting;"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (United Kingdom Security Vetting): National security vetting, clearance levels (collection updated 23 September 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/united-kingdom-security-vetting-clearance-levels/national-security-vetting-clearance-levels","Supports the Joining government page","require them to have long-term, frequent and uncontrolled access to SECRET assets and/or occasional, supervised access to TOP SECRET assets"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Summer Internship Programme (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-summer-internship-programme/","Supports the Joining government page","Please note some roles require SC clearance which can take up to 3 months.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Clearance usually needs years of UK residence",text:"Vetting normally expects a minimum period living in the UK: 3 years for CTC, 5 years for SC and 10 years for DV. If you have lived abroad, it may still be possible, but check with the recruiter before you apply.",figure:"3, 5 or 10 years",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"These are 'normal' expectations set out in departmental candidate guidance, not fixed legal bars: the Home Office says a lack of UK residency is not necessarily a bar to clearance. UK Security Vetting says only that UK residency may affect the checks carried out.",src:[SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide, pre-employment checks (last updated 31 August 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/pre-employment-checks/","Supports the Joining government page","This type of check normally includes meeting a minimum UK residency period as determined by the level of vetting being undertaken, which for SC level is 5 years."),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide, pre-employment checks (last updated 31 August 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/pre-employment-checks/","Supports the Joining government page","This type of check normally includes meeting a minimum UK residency period as determined by the level of vetting being undertaken, which for DV level is 10 years."),SRCQ("Home Office Careers: Security checks (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://careers.homeoffice.gov.uk/security-checks","Supports the Joining government page","5 years for SC clearance and 10 years for DV.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Prove your right to work before your first day",text:"You must prove your right to work in the UK before you start. British and Irish citizens can use a passport (current or expired); other people can use a share code or eligible immigration documents.",figure:"",year:"Updated October 2024",disputed:false,note:"If you are a Commonwealth citizen without documents, you may be able to get them through the Windrush Scheme.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Prove your right to work to an employer (updated 31 October 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/prove-right-to-work","Supports the Joining government page","You’ll need to prove your right to work in the UK to your employer before you start working for them."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): UK Government Baseline Personnel Security Standard, version 7.0 (June 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-baseline-personnel-security-standard/uk-government-baseline-personnel-security-standard-version-70-june-2024-html","Supports the Joining government page","Identity and RTW checks shall be completed prior to NSV applications being initiated.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Do not resign until you have a formal offer",text:"Successful candidates first get a provisional offer, which becomes formal only when all pre-employment checks are complete. These checks can take several weeks, so keep your current job until the formal offer arrives.",figure:"",year:"Updated January 2026",disputed:false,note:"Secondees go through the same checks as any new joiner.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","These checks can take several weeks. If you are in employment do not hand in your notice until a formal job offer is made."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service secondments (undated page, read 29 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/secondments/","Supports the Joining government page","As you will be working within the Civil Service, you must undergo pre-appointment checks in the same way as any new joiner.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Probation: a settling-in period for new joiners",text:"New joiners usually serve a probation period to show their conduct, performance and attendance are suitable. Length varies between organisations (six months at the Food Standards Agency, for example) and should not usually be more than two years.",figure:"Normally no more than 2 years",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Check your contract for the length that applies to you.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Government Recruitment Service and Civil Service Resourcing): Using the Civil Service Jobs website as a candidate (updated 27 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/using-the-civil-service-jobs-website","Supports the Joining government page","Any period of probation should not usually exceed 2 years."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Food Standards Agency): Working for FSA (updated 26 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/food-standards-agency/about/recruitment","Supports the Joining government page","All staff joining us who are new to the Civil Service, will undertake a probation period of six months.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"cvisa",h:"Nationality and visas",intro:"A common question is whether being on a visa rules you out. The answer depends on your nationality, your immigration status and the salary of the job, so here is what the rules say.",points:[
  {h:"Who can work in the Civil Service: the nationality rules",text:"The Civil Service Nationality Rules decide who can be employed. In general you are eligible if you are a UK, Irish or Commonwealth national with the right to work in the UK, and certain EU, EEA, Swiss and Turkish nationals are eligible too. Having a visa that lets you work is not enough on its own: your nationality must also fit the rules.",figure:"",year:"Rules last updated 8 August 2023",disputed:false,note:"Stateless people are not eligible under the rules. The rules apply across the whole Civil Service, not just the Fast Stream. This is general information, not immigration advice. Always check the job advert and the current Home Office rules on GOV.UK, and get advice from a solicitor or an adviser regulated by the Immigration Advice Authority if your situation is not straightforward.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office / Civil Service: Civil Service Nationality Rules – guidance on checking eligibility (last updated 8 Aug 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules/civil-service-nationality-rules-html","Supports the Joining government page","In general, only nationals from the following countries (and associations of countries) are eligible for employment in the Civil Service: the United Kingdom, the Republic of Ireland, and the Commonwealth."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream nationality requirements (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/nationality-requirements/","Supports the Joining government page","Nationals of Commonwealth countries who have the right to work in the UK"),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide – nationality requirements (updated 31 Aug 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/nationality-requirements/","Supports the Joining government page","To qualify for employment at the Scottish Government, candidates need to have the right to live and work in the UK.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Some posts are reserved for UK nationals only",text:"A small number of sensitive posts are 'reserved' and open only to UK nationals. All posts in the Security Service, the Secret Intelligence Service and GCHQ are reserved, and so are posts in the Diplomatic Service and the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office unless a minister decides otherwise. Some schemes are also open to British citizens only, so read each scheme's page.",figure:"",year:"Rules last updated 8 August 2023",disputed:false,note:"Other departments can reserve particular posts where a minister decides special allegiance to the Crown is needed. The job advert will say if a post is reserved.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office / Civil Service: Civil Service Nationality Rules – guidance on checking eligibility (last updated 8 Aug 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules/civil-service-nationality-rules-html","Supports the Joining government page","All posts within the security and intelligence services (that is, the Security Service, the Secret Intelligence Service, and the Government Communications Headquarters) are automatically reserved to UK nationals."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream nationality requirements (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/nationality-requirements/","Supports the Joining government page","Certain schemes are restricted to British Citizens only. We strongly advise checking the specific requirements on the individual scheme pages.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"EU, EEA and Swiss nationals and the EU Settlement Scheme",text:"Since free movement ended on 31 December 2020, most EU, EEA and Swiss nationals can work in non-reserved Civil Service posts if they have settled or pre-settled status, or a valid pending application, under the EU Settlement Scheme. Irish citizens do not need this status, and some people qualify in other ways, for example as Commonwealth citizens or with leave to remain if they were eligible for the scheme by 31 December 2020. You will be asked for evidence of your status before you start. Some people can still apply to the scheme late if they have reasonable grounds.",figure:"",year:"Since 1 January 2021",disputed:false,note:"The deadline for most people to apply to the EU Settlement Scheme was 30 June 2021. Family members' eligibility depends on their own status. This is general information, not immigration advice. Always check the job advert and the current Home Office rules on GOV.UK, and get advice from a solicitor or an adviser regulated by the Immigration Advice Authority if your situation is not straightforward.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office / Civil Service: Civil Service Nationality Rules – guidance on checking eligibility (last updated 8 Aug 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules/civil-service-nationality-rules-html","Supports the Joining government page","Following the end of free movement in the UK on 31 December 2020, only certain EEA nationals are eligible for employment in non-reserved posts in the Civil Service."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream nationality requirements (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/nationality-requirements/","Supports the Joining government page","Nationals of the EU, Switzerland, Norway, Iceland or Liechtenstein and family members of those nationalities with settled or pre-settled status under the European Union Settlement Scheme (EUSS)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Find an immigration adviser (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/find-an-immigration-adviser","Supports the Joining government page","You can apply if you have ‘reasonable grounds’ for not applying by the deadline - for example, you had an illness or were the victim of domestic abuse.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Visa sponsorship is possible but limited by salary thresholds",text:"Departments can sponsor Skilled Worker visas, but the job must be at an eligible skill level and usually pay at least £41,700 a year or the going rate for the role, whichever is higher. Entry-level salaries are often below this, and the Fast Stream starting salary does not meet the threshold for new sponsorship. Adverts should say if a role is not open to sponsorship, and sponsorship is only considered after a job offer.",figure:"£41,700",year:"Threshold shown on GOV.UK, 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Salary thresholds and eligible occupations change often; lower rates can apply in some cases, such as some extensions. A certificate of sponsorship does not guarantee a visa. Your nationality must still meet the Civil Service Nationality Rules. This is general information, not immigration advice. Always check the job advert and the current Home Office rules on GOV.UK, and get advice from a solicitor or an adviser regulated by the Immigration Advice Authority if your situation is not straightforward.",src:[SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration / GOV.UK: Skilled Worker visa – your job (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa/your-job","Supports the Joining government page","You’ll usually need to be paid the ‘standard’ salary rate of at least £41,700 per year, or the ‘going rate’ for your job, whichever is higher."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream nationality requirements (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/nationality-requirements/","Supports the Joining government page","The current starting salary for the Fast Stream does not meet the minimum threshold for new skilled worker visa sponsorship."),SRCQ("HM Revenue and Customs careers: answer to a candidate question on sponsorship (13 May 2026)","https://careers.hmrc.gov.uk/discussions/i-would-also-like-to-mention-that-my-visa-is-valid-until-september-2027-it-would-be-a-great-opportunity-for-me-if-i-could-secure-a-role-that-offers-sponsorship-or-at-least-a-position-that-allows-me-to-gain-valuable-work-experience-over-the-next-couple-o","Supports the Joining government page","our job adverts will advise if a role is not open to sponsorship due to the income threshold."),SRCQ("Scottish Government: Recruitment candidate guide – nationality requirements (updated 31 Aug 2026)","https://www.gov.scot/publications/recruitment-candidate-guide/pages/nationality-requirements/","Supports the Joining government page","To qualify for sponsorship through the Skilled Worker Visa scheme, you must meet a list of specific requirements including your job meeting the minimum salary of £41,700 per year.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Which departments hold a sponsor licence",text:"The Home Office register of licensed sponsors lists many government bodies, including the Cabinet Office, HM Treasury, HM Revenue and Customs, the Department for Business and Trade, the Department for Culture, Media and Sport, the Department for Education, the Department for Transport, the Department for Work and Pensions, the Department of Health and Social Care, the Ministry of Justice and the Office for National Statistics. The Scottish Government, Welsh Government and Northern Ireland Civil Service are listed too. A licence only means a body can sponsor: it does not mean any particular vacancy offers sponsorship.",figure:"",year:"Register dated 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The register is updated almost daily and lists each organisation under its own licence name, so a department not shown here may still hold a licence, possibly under a different name. Always go by the advert. This is general information, not immigration advice. Always check the job advert and the current Home Office rules on GOV.UK, and get advice from a solicitor or an adviser regulated by the Immigration Advice Authority if your situation is not straightforward.",src:[SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration: Register of licensed sponsors – workers (last updated 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/register-of-licensed-sponsors-workers","Supports the Joining government page","List of organisations licensed to sponsor workers on the Worker and Temporary Worker immigration routes."),SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration: Worker and Temporary Worker register CSV (29 Oct 2026) – Cabinet Office row","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae304e8976f73ef5cfb67aa/SP_-_Worker_and_Temporary_Worker_Web_Register_-_2026-10-29.csv","Supports the Joining government page","Cabinet Office,London,,Worker (A rating),Skilled Worker"),SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration: Worker and Temporary Worker register CSV (29 Oct 2026) – Department for Culture Media and Sport row","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae304e8976f73ef5cfb67aa/SP_-_Worker_and_Temporary_Worker_Web_Register_-_2026-10-29.csv","Supports the Joining government page","Department for Culture Media and Sport,London,Greater London,Worker (A rating),Skilled Worker"),SRCQ("Forest Research: Skilled Worker sponsorship – guidance for applicants and managers (Oct 2024)","https://cdn.forestresearch.gov.uk/2024/10/Skilled-Worker-sponsorship-Guidance-for-applicants-and-managers.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","Where an applicant (or their role) does not meet the Home Office requirements to be sponsored their application cannot proceed")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Graduate visas, dependants and proving your right to work",text:"A Graduate visa lets you work in most jobs, but for a Civil Service role your nationality must still meet the nationality rules. Graduate visas last 2 years if you apply on or before 31 December 2026 and 18 months if you apply from 1 January 2027 (3 years for a PhD). Every new starter must prove their right to work before starting, and it is one of the four baseline security checks.",figure:"18 months",year:"Graduate visa length from 1 January 2027",disputed:false,note:"The Graduate visa length is changing on 1 January 2027. Partners and children may be able to join or stay with a Skilled Worker as dependants, with stricter rules for some 'medium skilled' jobs. You cannot extend a Graduate visa, but you may be able to switch to another visa. This is general information, not immigration advice. Always check the job advert and the current Home Office rules on GOV.UK, and get advice from a solicitor or an adviser regulated by the Immigration Advice Authority if your situation is not straightforward.",src:[SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration / GOV.UK: Graduate visa (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/graduate-visa","Supports the Joining government page","18 months if you apply on or after 1 January 2027"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Prove your right to work to an employer (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/prove-right-to-work","Supports the Joining government page","You’ll need to prove your right to work in the UK to your employer before you start working for them."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Baseline Personnel Security Standard, version 7.0 (June 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-baseline-personnel-security-standard/uk-government-baseline-personnel-security-standard-version-70-june-2024-html","Supports the Joining government page","BPSS is a series of checks conducted once a provisional offer of employment is accepted by individuals."),SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration / GOV.UK: Skilled Worker visa – your partner and children (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa/your-partner-and-children","Supports the Joining government page","Your partner and children may also be able to apply to join you or stay in the UK as your ‘dependants’.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Where to get regulated immigration advice",text:"If you need help with your own immigration situation, use a solicitor or an adviser registered with the Immigration Advice Authority. It was previously called the Office of the Immigration Services Commissioner (OISC) and is sponsored by the Home Office. Check an adviser is registered, and whether they charge, before you use them.",figure:"",year:"Renamed January 2025",disputed:false,note:"You cannot get your money back if an adviser is not regulated. Solicitors who give immigration advice can be found through the Law Society in England and Wales, the Law Society of Scotland or the Law Society of Northern Ireland.",src:[SRCQ("Immigration Advice Authority: About us (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/immigration-advice-authority/about","Supports the Joining government page","Previously known as the Office of the Immigration Services Commissioner, we rebranded as the Immigration Advice Authority (IAA) in January 2025."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Find an immigration adviser (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/find-an-immigration-adviser","Supports the Joining government page","All immigration advisers must be registered with IAA or be a member of an approved professional body.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"cfamily",h:"Parents, carers and work–life balance",intro:"Can a Civil Service job fit around family life? These are the legal rights every employee has, and what the Civil Service offers on top.",points:[
  {h:"You can ask for flexible working from your first day",text:"Every employee can make a statutory request for flexible working from day one, covering hours, times, days and where you work. You can make up to two requests in 12 months, and your employer must consult you before refusing and normally decide within two months. An employer can still refuse for a good business reason.",figure:"Day one",year:"Since 6 April 2024",disputed:false,note:"These rights come from the Employment Relations (Flexible Working) Act 2023 and the Flexible Working (Amendment) Regulations 2023. The Employment Rights Act 2025 makes further changes to how employers must handle requests; check GOV.UK and Acas for when they apply.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Flexible working (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/flexible-working","Supports the Joining government page","Employees can make a request for flexible working from their first day in a job."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Flexible Working (Amendment) Regulations 2023 (SI 2023/1328)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1328/made","Supports the Joining government page","No conditions as to duration of employment need to be satisfied by an employee in order to be entitled to make a flexible working application on or after 6th April 2024."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Employment Relations (Flexible Working) Act 2023, as enacted","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/33/enacted","Supports the Joining government page","make more than two applications under this section to the same employer during any period of 12 months;")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Part-time work and job shares in the Civil Service",text:"The Civil Service says it promotes all forms of flexible working, including part-time work and job sharing, where two people share one full-time role. Job shares exist right up to Director General level. Adverts say which working patterns a role can offer, so check before you apply.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Flexible working is offered 'where we can', so what is possible depends on the role. Office attendance expectations differ between departments and are set out in adverts.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A guide to Civil Service job sharing (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/guide-to-job-sharing/","Supports the Joining government page","As an equal opportunities employer, the Civil Service positively promotes all forms of flexible working, including job sharing."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: quote from Ruth Hannant and Polly Payne, Directors General (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/guide-to-job-sharing/","Supports the Joining government page","We have been job-sharing together for 12 years and were incredibly proud to be the first Director General-level job-share in the Civil Service."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Disability Confident scheme page – benefits panel (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/disability-confident-scheme/","Supports the Joining government page","Our annual leave and pension schemes are very competitive and we offer flexible working wherever we can."),SRCQ("Civil Service blog: How job sharing is going mainstream, by two former Directors General (5 July 2022)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2022/07/05/how-job-sharing-is-going-mainstream/","Supports the Joining government page","so we see successful job-shares across the Civil Service and at all levels")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Maternity, adoption and shared parental leave",text:"By law you can take up to 52 weeks of maternity or adoption leave, and parents can share up to 50 weeks of leave and 37 weeks of pay through Shared Parental Leave. As a civil servant you may have statutory maternity pay topped up to full pay for the first 26 weeks, and more in some departments. Shared parental pay can also be topped up to full pay, subject to conditions.",figure:"Up to 26 weeks",year:"Civil Service guide, 2026",disputed:false,note:"Statutory maternity pay is not a day-one right, and full pay depends on meeting your department's conditions. Check your department's own policy.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Maternity pay and leave – leave (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/maternity-pay-leave/leave","Supports the Joining government page","Statutory Maternity Leave is 52 weeks."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Shared Parental Leave and Pay (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/shared-parental-leave-and-pay","Supports the Joining government page","You can share up to 50 weeks of leave and up to 37 weeks of pay between you."),SRCQ("Government People Group: Shared Parental Leave – a guide for civil servants having a baby and their managers (2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e187c92f65594cb0299bd/SPL_Having_a_baby_HMG_2026v0c.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","first 26 weeks to full pay (or more than 26 weeks in some departments).")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Partners can take paternity leave from day one",text:"Paternity leave is available from your first day of employment, although statutory paternity pay still needs 26 weeks' service. Most civil servants get full pay while on paternity leave. If your baby is born on or after 6 April 2026, you can take paternity leave before or after shared parental leave.",figure:"Day one",year:"Births from 6 April 2026",disputed:false,note:"Check your department's paternity leave policy for the exact pay you will get.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Paternity pay and leave – eligibility (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/paternity-pay-leave/eligibility","Supports the Joining government page","You’re eligible for Paternity Leave from the first day of employment."),SRCQ("Government People Group: Shared Parental Leave – a guide for civil servants having a baby and their managers (2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e187c92f65594cb0299bd/SPL_Having_a_baby_HMG_2026v0c.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","Most civil servants are entitled to full pay while on paternity leave."),SRCQ("Government People Group: Shared Parental Leave – a guide for civil servants having a baby and their managers (2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4e187c92f65594cb0299bd/SPL_Having_a_baby_HMG_2026v0c.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","If your baby is born on or after 6 April 2026, you can take paternity leave either before or after shared parental leave.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Carer's leave: one week a year from your first day",text:"If someone depends on you for long-term care, you can take up to one week of unpaid carer's leave every 12 months, from your first day in a job. You can take it as a whole week, single days or half days, and you do not need to give evidence of the person's care needs. The dependant does not have to be a family member.",figure:"1 week a year",year:"Since 6 April 2024",disputed:false,note:"Carer's leave is unpaid by law; some employers offer more. It is separate from parental leave.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Unpaid carer's leave (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/carers-leave","Supports the Joining government page","Employees are entitled to carer’s leave from their first day of work for their employer."),SRCQ("Acas: Carer's leave (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/carers-leave","Supports the Joining government page","The law is the Carer's Leave Act 2023. It came into effect on 6 April 2024."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Carer's Leave Regulations 2024 (SI 2024/251)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2024/251/made","Supports the Joining government page","(2) The Regulations come into force on 6th April 2024.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Tax-Free Childcare: £2 for every £8 you pay",text:"If you are working, Tax-Free Childcare adds £2 for every £8 you pay into a childcare account, up to £500 every 3 months (£2,000 a year) per child. It is up to £4,000 a year if your child is disabled. Your child must usually be 11 or under (16 or under if disabled) and your provider must be signed up.",figure:"£2,000 a year",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"You must reconfirm your details every 3 months. Other childcare support, such as free hours, has its own rules.",src:[SRCQ("HM Revenue and Customs / GOV.UK: Tax-Free Childcare (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/tax-free-childcare","Supports the Joining government page","The total top up you can get for each child is £500 every 3 months (up to £2,000 a year)."),SRCQ("Department for Education, Best Start in Life: How Tax-Free Childcare works (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://beststartinlife.gov.uk/?p=475","Supports the Joining government page","For every £8 you pay into this account, the government will pay in £2 to use to pay your childcare provider."),SRCQ("Rutland County Council: Tax-Free Childcare (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.rutland.gov.uk/education-learning/early-education-childcare/early-education-childcare-funding/tax-free-childcare","Supports the Joining government page","You could get up to £500 every three months (up to £2,000 a year) for each of your children to help with the costs of childcare.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"creturn",h:"Returning after a break, and changing career",intro:"If you have been out of work for a while, or want to move into government from another sector, a gap in your CV or a career change is not a barrier in itself.",points:[
  {h:"A returnship pilot for software developers",text:"In 2026 the government began piloting a paid 18-month returnship for senior software developers who had been away from work for 18 months or more. The Government Digital Service offered placements in the Home Office and the Ministry of Justice. Applications for the pilot closed on 13 May 2026, and the approach could be expanded if the pilot succeeds.",figure:"18 months",year:"Announced 12 March 2026",disputed:false,note:"This is a pilot, not a permanent scheme. The length, seniority and closing date come from one source, the advert as republished by Manchester Digital. Watch Civil Service Jobs for any future returner opportunities.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK press release: Government breaks down barriers to help more women and girls enter the tech sector (12 Mar 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-breaks-down-barriers-to-help-more-women-and-girls-enter-the-tech-sector","Supports the Joining government page","The returnship scheme will be piloted with Home Office and Ministry of Justice and will be open to any software developers who have been away from work for 18 months or more"),SRCQ("Manchester Digital: Senior Software Developer Returnship Programme (2026)","https://www.manchesterdigital.com/post/gds/senior-software-developer-returnship-programme","Supports the Joining government page","This is a paid 18‑month returnship where you’ll be placed in a real delivery team at either the UK Home Office or the Ministry of Justice."),SRCQ("Government Transformation: Returnship scheme to bring women back into Civil Service digital roles (2026)","https://www.government-transformation.com/transformation/returnship-scheme-to-bring-women-back-into-civil-service-digital-roles","Supports the Joining government page","The Civil Service returnship scheme will run initially as a pilot, with potential for the approach to be expanded if successful.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Experience from outside paid work counts",text:"The Civil Service recruits using Success Profiles, which look at behaviours, strengths, ability, experience and technical skills. Skills gained through voluntary work or hobbies, such as teamwork or communication, can show the experience a role needs, so time away from paid work can still provide good evidence. Only the elements relevant to the role are assessed.",figure:"5 elements",year:"Framework page updated 29 January 2025",disputed:false,note:"Each advert lists the behaviours and other elements it will assess; read it closely.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: A guide to Civil Service Success Profiles (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/a-guide-to-civil-service-success-profiles/","Supports the Joining government page","Sometimes experience can be transferable from a non-work context. Skills gained through voluntary work or a hobby, such as teamwork or communication, can demonstrate the experience required for the role."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office / GOV.UK: Success Profiles (last updated 29 Jan 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/success-profiles","Supports the Joining government page","Not all of the elements are relevant to every role, and will vary depending on the profession, level and type of role.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Age is not a barrier",text:"Age is a protected characteristic under the Equality Act 2010, and age discrimination can happen to people of any age. There is no default retirement age for most jobs, and the Civil Service says you can apply for the Fast Stream at any age.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"A small number of jobs can have a compulsory retirement age if the employer can justify it.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Age discrimination – what the law says (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/age-discrimination","Supports the Joining government page","Age is one of 9 'protected characteristics' covered by discrimination law (Equality Act 2010)."),SRCQ("Acas: Retirement (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/retirement","Supports the Joining government page","Workers in most jobs can choose when to retire. There is no default retirement age, except for a small number of jobs."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Experienced careers (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-experienced-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","You can apply for the Civil Service Fast Stream at any age.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Changing career: no government background needed",text:"You do not need to have worked in government before. Many roles need skills you may already have, such as project management, finance, data, digital and people management, and the Civil Service offers mid-career apprenticeships and professional qualifications in areas such as commercial, digital and finance. Apprenticeships run from level 2 to level 7.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Apprenticeships are paid jobs; in England at least 20% of normal working hours is spent on training.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Experienced careers (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-experienced-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","You do not need prior experience in government to join us."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Experienced careers – career changers (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-experienced-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","We offer mid-career apprenticeships and professional qualifications in areas like business administration, commercial, digital, and finance."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Become an apprentice (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/become-apprentice","Supports the Joining government page","get time for training and study related to your role (at least 20% of your normal working hours)")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"cvets",h:"Veterans and service leavers",intro:"If you have served in the armed forces, the Civil Service has dedicated routes in. Your service experience counts, and there is support to translate it.",points:[
  {h:"Great Place to Work for Veterans: get through to the next stage",text:"If you opt into the Great Place to Work for Veterans scheme and meet the minimum criteria, you progress to the next stage for most Civil Service vacancies, usually an interview or online test. You opt in on Civil Service Jobs by confirming you served in the armed forces. Appointments are still made on merit.",figure:"",year:"Guaranteed interviews first announced 6 February 2020",disputed:false,note:"The scheme began in 2020 as a guaranteed interview for veterans and now runs as Great Place to Work for Veterans. It covers most, not all, vacancies.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Great Place to Work for Veterans scheme (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/great-place-to-work-for-veterans/","Supports the Joining government page","When a veteran opts into the scheme and meets the minimum criteria, they’ll progress to the next stage for most vacancies – whether that’s an interview or an online test."),SRCQ("Ministry of Defence, VALOUR: Top routes into public sector careers for UK veterans and families (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://valour.campaign.gov.uk/public-sector-careers-after-service/","Supports the Joining government page","You can opt in during the application process, and if you meet the minimum criteria, you’ll automatically progress to the next stage for most Civil Service vacancies – usually an interview or online test."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office and Office for Veterans' Affairs press release: guaranteed interviews for government jobs (6 Feb 2020)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-delivers-on-promise-to-veterans-announcing-guaranteed-interviews-for-government-jobs","Supports the Joining government page","Military veterans are to be guaranteed interviews for civil service jobs, the government has announced.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Going Forward into Employment for veterans and military partners",text:"Going Forward into Employment runs a life chance pathway for veterans, service leavers and military spouses and partners, with simplified applications and support once you start. Over the last three years it has helped more than 180 veterans and military spouses or partners into Civil Service careers. Service leavers can see its vacancies through the Career Transition Partnership.",figure:"180+",year:"Three years to July 2026",disputed:false,note:"Spouses and partners of serving personnel, or of someone who left the forces in the last 12 months, can usually apply.",src:[SRCQ("Going Forward into Employment blog: Building the bridge from military excellence to Civil Service careers (1 Jul 2026)","https://gfie.blog.gov.uk/2026/07/01/building-the-bridge-from-military-excellence-to-civil-service-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","Over the last three years, the GFiE Life Chance pathway has achieved remarkable success, empowering over 180 veterans and military spouses/partners to begin rewarding careers within the Civil Service."),SRCQ("Ministry of Defence, VALOUR: Top routes into public sector careers for UK veterans and families (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://valour.campaign.gov.uk/public-sector-careers-after-service/","Supports the Joining government page","This scheme offers opportunities across government through its Life Chance Pathway for Veterans and Military Spouses/Partners programme.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"The Career Transition Partnership helps you plan the move",text:"The Career Transition Partnership is the Ministry of Defence's resettlement programme. It offers workshops, training, one-to-one guidance, employer events and a jobs portal, tailored to how long you served. In most cases you can use it from 2 years before discharge until 2 years after.",figure:"2 years",year:"Before and after discharge",disputed:true,note:"Official pages currently name different delivery partners for the programme, so check the Career Transition Partnership website for the latest details.",src:[SRCQ("Ministry of Defence, VALOUR: The Career Transition Partnership (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://valour.campaign.gov.uk/career-transition-partnership/","Supports the Joining government page","In most cases, you can access Career Transition Partnership support from 2 years before discharge until 2 years after."),SRCQ("Career Transition Partnership: home page (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.ctp.org.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","The Career Transition Partnership (CTP) is the Ministry of Defence working with Right Management."),SRCQ("Going Forward into Employment blog: Building the bridge from military excellence to Civil Service careers (1 Jul 2026)","https://gfie.blog.gov.uk/2026/07/01/building-the-bridge-from-military-excellence-to-civil-service-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","Veterans and service leavers are able to view any vacancies GFiE has to offer through the Career Transition Partnership (CTP) who are the official resettlement service providers for the Armed Forces.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The Office for Veterans' Affairs and the Armed Forces Covenant",text:"The Office for Veterans' Affairs leads government work to support veterans and is now part of the Ministry of Defence. The Armed Forces Covenant is a promise that people who serve or have served, and their families, are treated fairly. In law, certain public bodies must have due regard to the principles of the Covenant when carrying out some housing, education and healthcare functions.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Covenant's legal duty covers housing, education and healthcare functions; it does not create a right to a job. VALOUR is the UK-wide veteran support system for wider help.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Office for Veterans' Affairs (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/office-for-veterans-affairs","Supports the Joining government page","OVA is part of the Ministry of Defence."),SRCQ("Armed Forces Covenant (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.armedforcescovenant.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","The Armed Forces Covenant is a promise by the nation ensuring that those who serve or have served in the Armed Forces, and their families, including the bereaved, are treated fairly."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Armed Forces Act 2021, section 8","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2021/35/section/8","Supports the Joining government page","(a)a relevant housing function,")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"csecond",h:"Second chances: prison leavers and people with convictions",intro:"Having a criminal conviction is not, on its own, a barrier to joining the Civil Service. Here is how disclosure and checks work, and the routes designed for people leaving prison.",points:[
  {h:"Going Forward into Employment started with prison leavers",text:"Going Forward into Employment began in 2018 to help prison leavers into Civil Service jobs and now covers several groups facing barriers to work. Its roles are real, ring-fenced jobs with simplified applications and support for you and your manager, and most are outside London. The Civil Service welcomes applications from recent prison leavers and people with unspent convictions.",figure:"3,600+",year:"People appointed since 2018",disputed:true,note:"The same page also gives a figure of over 3,050, so treat the total as approximate. Some offences, including life sentences, arson, sexual offences and hate and terror offences, are currently exceptions, and departments may weigh an offence against the role.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Going Forward into Employment (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/about-gfie/","Supports the Joining government page","We have appointed over 1000 people in the last two years and over 3,600 since 2018, and we plan to do much more!"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Prison leaver recruitment (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/prison-leaver-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","We welcome applications from recent prison leavers or those with an unspent conviction."),SRCQ("Going Forward into Employment blog: All about Going Forward into Employment (24 May 2022)","https://gfie.blog.gov.uk/2022/05/24/all-about-going-forward-into-employment/","Supports the Joining government page","We work with government departments, charities and Prisons across the UK to support Prison Leavers into finding Civil Service jobs in support of the government’s Beating Crime plan.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"'Ban the Box': no conviction questions at the first stage",text:"Many Civil Service departments do not ask about criminal convictions on the application form. The Civil Service says it only asks about convictions once a job offer is made and accepted, so your application is judged alongside everyone else's. This approach dates from February 2016.",figure:"",year:"Since 8 February 2016",disputed:false,note:"The source says 'many' departments, not all. Roles with specific security requirements, such as prison officers, can be exempt, and roles working with children or vulnerable people still require certain disclosures. This is general information, not legal advice. Rules differ in Scotland and Northern Ireland. Use the GOV.UK checker or a specialist charity such as Unlock or Nacro to check whether your conviction is spent before you disclose.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Prison leaver recruitment (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/prison-leaver-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","Many Civil Service departments operate ‘Ban the Box’. This is a recognised statement of intent that they will not ask candidates about criminal convictions at application stage."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Prison leaver recruitment (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/prison-leaver-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","We only ask about criminal convictions once a job offer is made and accepted."),SRCQ("Civil Service blog: Ban the Box (8 Feb 2016)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2016/02/08/ban-the-box/","Supports the Joining government page","The Civil Service will still ask about criminal convictions during the recruitment process, but we will do this after the initial application form stage."),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Civil service to 'ban the box' to help rehabilitate ex-convicts (Feb 2016)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/articles/news/civil-service-ban-box-help-rehabilitate-ex-convicts-david-cameron-announces","Supports the Joining government page","Civil service job application forms regularly ask candidates to declare unspent convictions, and it is this stage of the process that is being eliminated")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Spent convictions under the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974",text:"Most convictions and cautions become 'spent' after a set time, which can be months, years or immediately. Once spent, you are treated as if the offence had never been committed and you usually do not have to disclose it unless the role needs a standard or enhanced DBS check. You only need to tell an employer about an unspent conviction if they ask.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"This is general information, not legal advice. Rules differ in Scotland and Northern Ireland. Use the GOV.UK checker or a specialist charity such as Unlock or Nacro to check whether your conviction is spent before you disclose.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Telling an employer about your criminal record – what information you need to give (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/tell-employer-or-college-about-criminal-record/what-information-you-need-to-give","Supports the Joining government page","You only need to tell an organisation about an unspent conviction or caution if they ask you to."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Baseline Personnel Security Standard, version 7.0 (June 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-baseline-personnel-security-standard/uk-government-baseline-personnel-security-standard-version-70-june-2024-html","Supports the Joining government page","Once spent an individual must be treated as if the offence had never been committed."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974, section 4","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/53/section/4","Supports the Joining government page","shall be treated for all purposes in law as a person who has not committed or been charged with or prosecuted for or convicted of or sentenced for the offence")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Security checks: unspent convictions are judged case by case",text:"After you accept a provisional offer, the Baseline Personnel Security Standard checks your identity, right to work, employment history and unspent convictions. Its policy is that a criminal conviction should not be an automatic barrier to employment. Departments look at factors such as how relevant the offence is to the role, how long ago it was and how serious it was.",figure:"4 checks",year:"BPSS version 7.0, June 2024",disputed:false,note:"A conviction for fraud, for example, may rule you out of a finance role. Some posts need higher national security vetting. This is general information, not legal advice. Rules differ in Scotland and Northern Ireland. Use the GOV.UK checker or a specialist charity such as Unlock or Nacro to check whether your conviction is spent before you disclose.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Baseline Personnel Security Standard, version 7.0 (June 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-baseline-personnel-security-standard/uk-government-baseline-personnel-security-standard-version-70-june-2024-html","Supports the Joining government page","Policy Principle: A criminal conviction should not be an automatic barrier to employment and shall be assessed on a case by case basis."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Prison leaver recruitment (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/prison-leaver-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","In most cases, security clearance will be dealt with by the hiring department, generally at Baseline Personnel Security Standard (BPSS) level.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"New Futures Network links prisons with employers",text:"The New Futures Network is a specialist part of HM Prison and Probation Service that brokers partnerships between prisons and employers in England and Wales. If you are still in prison or recently released, ask your prison employment lead or probation practitioner about the opportunities it brokers.",figure:"50,000",year:"People released from prison each year (approx.)",disputed:false,note:"The network covers England and Wales only.",src:[SRCQ("New Futures Network, HM Prison and Probation Service (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.newfuturesnetwork.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","We are a specialist part of HM Prison and Probation Service (HMPPS) which attracts and supports employers to work with prisons in England and Wales."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Prison leaver recruitment (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/prison-leaver-recruitment/","Supports the Joining government page","The New Futures Network is a specialist part of Her Majesty’s Prison and Probation Service. We broker partnerships between prisons and employers in England and Wales."),SRCQ("New Futures Network, HM Prison and Probation Service (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.newfuturesnetwork.gov.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","Around 50,000 people are released from prison each year.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"cother",h:"Other circumstances",intro:"Other circumstances people often ask about, with the support or dedicated routes that exist for each.",points:[
  {h:"Care Leavers Internship Scheme",text:"Care leavers aged 18 to 30 can apply for a paid 18-month internship in a government department or agency, run by the Department for Education. No qualifications are required and you are paid the same as other civil servants. After the first 12 months you may be made permanent, depending on your performance and the department's headcount and funding. More than 880 care leavers have been offered roles, and around 80% were made permanent.",figure:"880+",year:"2026 campaign closed",disputed:false,note:"The 2026 campaign has closed; in 2026 the advert ran from 8 to 31 May. You must be entitled to a leaving care package (including at least 13 weeks in care, some of it after your 16th birthday) and meet the nationality rules.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Care Leavers Internship Scheme (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-care-leavers-internship-scheme/","Supports the Joining government page","offering job roles to over 880 care leavers across government, around 80% of these interns have been made permanent"),SRCQ("Kent Virtual School: UK Civil Service Care Leavers Internship Scheme (17 Jul 2025)","https://kentcarestown.lea.kent.sch.uk/news/2025/07/17/uk-civil-service-care-leavers-internship-scheme/","Supports the Joining government page","No qualifications are required for this internship, however you will need to provide examples that clearly demonstrate the criteria listed.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Refugees: eligibility depends on your nationality",text:"Being a refugee does not automatically remove your nationality, so your eligibility depends on your nationality in the normal way. You will be asked to confirm in writing whether your nationality was taken away or renounced, and departments should not contact your country's embassy. People who are stateless are not eligible under the current rules.",figure:"",year:"Rules last updated 8 August 2023",disputed:false,note:"Asylum seekers are only eligible if they meet the nationality rules and have been given permission to work. This rests on one source, the Civil Service Nationality Rules. It is general information, not immigration advice: check the job advert and the current Home Office rules on GOV.UK, and get advice from a solicitor or an adviser regulated by the Immigration Advice Authority if your situation is not straightforward.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office / Civil Service: Civil Service Nationality Rules – guidance on checking eligibility (last updated 8 Aug 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules/civil-service-nationality-rules-html","Supports the Joining government page","A refugee does not automatically lose his or her nationality. It must either be taken away by the government of the country concerned, or the candidate must have voluntarily renounced it."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office / Civil Service: Civil Service Nationality Rules – guidance on checking eligibility (last updated 8 Aug 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules/civil-service-nationality-rules-html","Supports the Joining government page","Stateless persons are not eligible for appointment to the Civil Service even if they had past connections with the United Kingdom or the Commonwealth."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office / Civil Service: Annex A – Nationality Rules (last updated 8 Aug 2023)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nationality-rules/annex-a-nationality-rules-html","Supports the Joining government page","A refugee does not automatically lose his or her nationality.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Disability Confident: an interview if you meet the minimum criteria",text:"The Civil Service runs the Disability Confident scheme, offering an interview to a fair and proportionate number of disabled applicants who meet the minimum criteria for the job. To qualify you must have a condition with a substantial and long-term adverse effect on day-to-day activities, and show you meet the minimum criteria in the advert. It does not mean every disabled applicant gets an interview.",figure:"",year:"Scheme guidance updated 23 July 2026",disputed:false,note:"Employers may limit interview numbers in high-volume campaigns. You can ask for adjustments even if you do not use the scheme.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Disability Confident scheme (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/disability-confident-scheme/","Supports the Joining government page","The Civil Service runs a Disability Confident Scheme, offering an interview to a fair and proportionate number of disabled applicants that meet the minimum selection criteria for the job."),SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions: Disability Confident guidance – level 1 (updated 23 Jul 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disability-confident-guidance-for-levels-1-2-and-3/level-1-disability-confident-committed","Supports the Joining government page","This does not mean that all disabled people are entitled to an interview. They must meet the minimum criteria for a job as defined by the employer.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Adjustments at every stage, Access to Work and the passport",text:"You can ask for adjustments at any stage, such as extra time in tests, questions in writing or a sign language interpreter, and you can raise them later in the process if you need to. Once in work, Access to Work can fund practical support, and a Workplace Adjustment Passport records what you and your manager have agreed, which helps if you change manager. Employers must make reasonable adjustments by law.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Adjustments can cover physical and mental health conditions. Access to Work has its own eligibility rules.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Reasonable adjustments (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/reasonable-adjustments/","Supports the Joining government page","We want to support you if you need an adjustment in the recruitment process, even if you do not feel you qualify for the Disability Confident Scheme."),SRCQ("Department for Work and Pensions / GOV.UK: Access to Work (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/access-to-work","Supports the Joining government page","Access to Work can help you get or stay in work if you have a physical or mental health condition or disability."),SRCQ("Acas: Reasonable adjustments at work (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/reasonable-adjustments","Supports the Joining government page","Employers must make reasonable adjustments by law."),SRCQ("Government Science and Engineering blog: The importance of the Workplace Adjustment Passport (29 Nov 2021)","https://governmentscienceandengineering.blog.gov.uk/2021/11/29/the-importance-of-the-workplace-adjustment-passport-for-civil-service-employees","Supports the Joining government page","acting as a record of that conversation and of the adjustments agreed.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Qualifications from overseas: UK ENIC",text:"UK ENIC (previously UK NARIC) is the official organisation for comparing international qualifications with UK ones. A Statement of Comparability shows how your qualification compares, which can help when an advert asks for a particular level. It is a chargeable service, and only some roles ask for formal qualifications.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"UK ENIC is managed by Ecctis for the UK government; Home Office visa guidance also refers applicants to Ecctis. Check whether the advert actually needs a qualification before paying.",src:[SRCQ("UK ENIC: home page (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.enic.org.uk/","Supports the Joining government page","The UK National Information Centre (UK ENIC, previously UK NARIC) is the official organisation for the recognition and comparison of international qualifications and skills."),SRCQ("UK ENIC: Statement of Comparability (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.enic.org.uk/individuals/statement-of-comparability","Supports the Joining government page","A Statement of Comparability is a certificate that shows how international qualifications compare to the UK education systems."),SRCQ("UK Visas and Immigration / GOV.UK: Skilled Worker visa, printable guide (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa/print","Supports the Joining government page","If your degree was taught in English and was not awarded by a UK institution, you’ll need to apply for an assessment from Ecctis.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Faith and religious observance are protected",text:"Religion or belief, including having no religion, is a protected characteristic under the Equality Act 2010. Acas advises employers to be flexible about time for prayer and to consider requests for time off for religious festivals. You can raise your needs at offer stage or with your manager.",figure:"",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Employers do not have to provide a dedicated prayer room but should consider a suitable space. Agreed prayer time is usually made up, so total hours stay the same.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 10 (up to date to 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/10","Supports the Joining government page","(1)Religion means any religion and a reference to religion includes a reference to a lack of religion."),SRCQ("Acas: Religion or belief discrimination – what the law says (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/religion-or-belief-discrimination","Supports the Joining government page","Religion or belief is one of 9 'protected characteristics' covered by discrimination law (Equality Act 2010)."),SRCQ("Acas: Preventing religion or belief discrimination (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/religion-or-belief-discrimination/preventing-religion-or-belief-discrimination","Supports the Joining government page","If someone requests time for prayer during the day, consider how you can be flexible.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"More jobs outside London: Places for Growth",text:"Places for Growth is moving Civil Service roles out of London. The government aims for 50% of UK-based senior civil servants and 50% of Fast Stream roles to be outside London by 2030, and plans to cut London-based posts by 12,000 by 2030.",figure:"50%",year:"Target date 2030",disputed:false,note:"These are targets, not guarantees. The share of senior civil servants based in London fell from 68% in 2020 to 61% in 2024.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office / GOV.UK: Places for Growth 2030 (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/places-for-growth","Supports the Joining government page","ensuring 50% of UK-based Senior Civil Servants (SCS) are located outside London in the English regions, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland by 2030."),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Relocation of the civil service (explainer)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/relocation-civil-service","Supports the Joining government page","The government also plans to cut London-based civil servants by 12,000 by 2030 and close 11 London office buildings over the next five years."),SRCQ("Civil Service World: London-based civil service headcount to be cut by 12,000 (May 2025)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/londonbased-civil-service-headcount-to-be-cut-by-12000","Supports the Joining government page","Today's announcement also included plans to have 50% of Fast Stream roles based outside London")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Early careers and routes without a degree",text:"You can apply for a Civil Service apprenticeship from age 16, with levels from 2 to 7, and you can apply for vacancies on Civil Service Jobs at any time without joining a talent programme. The Summer Internship Programme is for university students from lower socio-economic backgrounds, and the Fast Stream is for graduates of any age. Some schemes, such as the Care Leavers Internship Scheme, ask for no qualifications at all.",figure:"From 16",year:"Accessed 29 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Each advert sets its own essential criteria, so check whether a qualification is needed.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Early careers (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-early-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","From the age of 16, you can apply for an apprenticeship."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Early careers (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-early-careers/","Supports the Joining government page","However, you don’t need to participate in a talent development programme to start your journey."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Apprenticeships (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/apprenticeships/","Supports the Joining government page","Apprenticeships can be undertaken in England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, with opportunities available at levels 2 to 7"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Become an apprentice (accessed 29 Oct 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/become-apprentice","Supports the Joining government page","Apprenticeships combine practical training in a job with study.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"jnbarriers",h:"What the numbers show",intro:"Published figures on who applies, who gets in and how people from ethnic minority and Black backgrounds experience work in government and technology. A difference in rates does not by itself show its cause.",points:[
  {h:"Black African Fast Stream applicants: 14% of applicants, 0.3% success",text:"In the 2026 Fast Stream competition, applicants from a Black or Black British – African background made up 14.0% of applications but 3.9% of those recommended for appointment, a success rate of 0.3% against 1.0% overall. The pattern has persisted: in 2025 the figures were 15.3% of applicants and 2.8% of appointments (0.2%).",figure:"0.3% success rate (26 of 9,143)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Fast Stream is a graduate leadership scheme with about 1% overall success; figures are by first-preference application. Black Caribbean and Other Black counts were suppressed (fewer than five).",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office (GOV.UK): Civil Service Fast Stream: recruitment data 2026 (24 September 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-fast-stream-recruitment-data-2026/civil-service-fast-stream-recruitment-data-2026-html","Supports the Joining government page","Black or Black British - African 9,143 14.0% 26 3.9% 0.3%"),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Fast Stream gets biggest ever number of applicants (26 September 2025)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/news/article/fast-stream-gets-biggest-ever-number-of-applicants","Supports the Joining government page","The success rate remains low for applicants from 'Black or Black British – Africa' background, who made up 15.3% of applicants")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnic minority Fast Stream success is just over half the white rate",text:"Ethnic minority applicants made up 43.0% of 2026 Fast Stream applications but 29.7% of those recommended for appointment: a success rate of 0.7%, against 1.3% for white applicants. The gap widened in 2024, when ethnic minority candidates were 36% of applicants but 22% of those recommended.",figure:"0.7% vs 1.3%",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Base rate: 703 of 69,046 applications (1.0%) were recommended in 2026. The data does not show what causes the gap; in 2024 an Institute for Government researcher, quoted by Civil Service World, said it was unclear what was driving it.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Fast Stream Annual Report 2026 tables, PDF (September 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6aaa4fcd086ce7c57b818545/Fast_Stream_Annual_Report_2026_V5.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","Ethnic minority 28,783 43.2% 28,151 43.0% 200 29.7% 0.7%"),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Fast Stream diversity gaps widen (2 October 2024)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/fast-stream-diversity-statistics-white-ethnic-minority-applicants-divide-widens","Supports the Joining government page","It's unclear what is driving the widening gap between application and success rates for ethnic minority applicants")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black women are 1.8% of the UK workforce but 0.7% of IT professionals",text:"BCS and Coding Black Females estimated that at least 20,000 more Black women would need to work in IT for them to be proportionately represented. In the accompanying survey, 67% of respondents from the Coding Black Females network said they faced more barriers to entering tech than women from other ethnicities. Asked what puts them off applying for IT roles, individual respondents mentioned degree requirements, more than three rounds of interviews and a lack of part-time or flexible options.",figure:"0.7% vs 1.8%; 67%",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"Workforce figures are BCS analysis of the ONS Labour Force Survey for 2021, published in October 2022. The survey heard from over 350 Black women working in tech, reached through the Coding Black Females network, so the 67% is indicative rather than representative. The comments on what puts people off applying are individual responses, not measured shares. This rests on one source, BCS.",src:[SRCQ("BCS: Thousands of black women 'missing' from the IT industry, report warns (27 October 2022)","https://www.bcs.org/articles-opinion-and-research/thousands-of-black-women-missing-from-the-it-industry-report-warns/","Supports the Joining government page","whilst black women make up 1.8% of the UK workforce, they only make up 0.7% of IT professionals.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Black people are 3% of IT specialists; ethnic minority IT unemployment is nearly three times the white rate",text:"BCS analysis of the Labour Force Survey shows that in 2023 Black, African, Caribbean and Black British people made up 3% of IT specialists, against 4% of all occupations. The unemployment rate for Black, Asian and minority ethnic IT specialists was 4.0%, against 1.4% for white IT specialists, which BCS describes as more than three times higher. Black, Asian and minority ethnic IT specialists were also more likely to hold a higher education qualification (88% against 71%).",figure:"3% of IT specialists; 4.0% vs 1.4% unemployment",year:"2023",disputed:false,note:"From the published rounded rates the ratio is about 2.9. Taken together, Black, Asian and minority ethnic groups are over-represented in IT (21% of IT specialists), largely because of the high share of people of Indian ethnicity (9%), so the combined figure hides lower Black representation. The unemployment rate is not published separately for Black IT specialists.",src:[SRCQ("BCS: Diversity report 2024 – Ethnicity and IT employment (2024; page undated)","https://bcs.org/policy-and-influence/equity-diversity-and-inclusion/bcs-diversity-report-2024-ethnicity/ethnicity-and-it-employment","Supports the Joining government page","Black/African/Caribbean/Black British 3% 4% 4%"),SRCQ("BCS: Diversity report 2024 – Ethnicity: key findings (2024; page undated)","https://www.bcs.org/policy-and-influence/equity-diversity-and-inclusion/bcs-diversity-report-2024-ethnicity/ethnicity-key-findings/","Supports the Joining government page","The associated unemployment rate for BAME IT specialists (4.0%) was more than three times higher than that of their ‘white’ counterparts (1.4%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"61% of Black graduates felt their ethnicity disadvantaged them in job applications",text:"In the Prospects Early Careers Survey 2024, 54% of graduates from ethnic minority backgrounds said they felt disadvantaged in the job application process because of their ethnicity, up from 43% in 2023. Among graduates from Black, African, Caribbean or Black British backgrounds the figure was 61%, up from 51%; among Asian or Asian British graduates it was 53%.",figure:"61%",year:"2024",disputed:false,note:"Perception data from a survey of 6,102 students and graduates in January and February 2024, published by Prospects at Jisc and reported by HRreview and ITV News. It measures how people felt, not measured discrimination. It is the most recent published breakdown by ethnicity found.",src:[SRCQ("HRreview: Male and ethnic minority graduates feel held back in jobs market – Prospects Early Careers Survey 2024 (30 May 2024)","https://hrreview.co.uk/hr-news/diversity-news/male-and-ethnic-minority-graduates-feel-held-back-in-jobs-market/375594","Supports the Joining government page","This sentiment was particularly pronounced among graduates from Black, African, Caribbean, or Black British backgrounds (61%)"),SRCQ("Prospects at Jisc, on FE News: More graduates feel held back in jobs market (30 May 2024)","https://www.fenews.co.uk/employability/more-graduates-feel-held-back-in-jobs-market/","Supports the Joining government page","Some 61% of respondents from a Black, African, Caribbean or Black British background said this")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnic minority civil servants report more discrimination, every year",text:"King's College London's analysis of People Survey data found ethnic minority civil servants reported higher bullying and discrimination than white peers in every year, with a consistent gap. In 2024, 11% of ethnic minority staff reported discrimination against 8% of white staff, and inclusion scores have diverged since 2018.",figure:"11% vs 8% discrimination (2024)",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"Rates for all groups have fallen since the pandemic; the KCL data only separates 'White' and 'Ethnic Minority', so Black staff cannot be isolated in that analysis.",src:[SRCQ("King's College London Policy Institute: Trends in bullying, discrimination and inclusion in the Civil Service, 2025 (December 2025)","https://www.kcl.ac.uk/policy-institute/assets/trends-in-bullying-discrimination-and-inclusion-in-the-civil-service-2025.pdf","Supports the Joining government page","Civil servants from ethnic minority backgrounds report higher levels of bullying and discrimination in every year in our data, than their white peers, and the size of this gap has been consistent over time."),SRCQ("Civil Service World: People Survey – a deep dive into bullying and discrimination trends (22 December 2025)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/in-depth/article/people-survey-a-deep-dive-into-bullying-and-discrimination-trends","Supports the Joining government page","civil servants from ethnic-minority backgrounds experience significantly more discrimination than their white colleagues: 11% in 2024, compared with 8%.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Hidden 'unwritten rules' and London tilt slow progression",text:"The Social Mobility Commission found Civil Service progression depends on mastering unwritten rules and access to senior 'organisational guides', often formed on cultural similarity. It also found senior roles concentrated in London, and cumulative barriers for working-class staff who are also from ethnic minority communities.",figure:"",year:"2021",disputed:false,note:"Based on around 300,000 survey responses and over 100 interviews; focused on socio-economic background, with race treated as an intersecting factor. This rests on one source, Social Mobility Commission.",src:[SRCQ("Social Mobility Commission: Navigating the labyrinth – socio-economic background and career progression within the Civil Service (20 May 2021)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/navigating-the-labyrinth/navigating-the-labyrinth-socio-economic-background-and-career-progression-within-the-civil-service","Supports the Joining government page","it is mastery of the unwritten rules that provide the most effective roadmap through the labyrinth.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Ethnic minority staff are least represented in the Senior Civil Service",text:"Ethnic minority representation in the Civil Service reached a record 19.2% at 31 March 2026, but was lowest in the Senior Civil Service, at 10.6%. There were 26,655 Black civil servants, the second-largest ethnic minority group after Asian civil servants.",figure:"SCS 10.6% ethnic minority (2026)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Percentages exclude people whose ethnicity is unknown or undeclared. The senior grade figure covers all ethnic minority groups combined, not Black staff alone. Source: Civil Service Statistics 2026, Cabinet Office, published 29 July 2026. This rests on one source, Cabinet Office (GOV.UK).",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office (GOV.UK): Civil Service Statistics 2026 statistical bulletin (29 July 2026, updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Joining government page","Of those with a known ethnicity, the percentage who are from an ethnic minority background is at a record high of 19.2%.")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Check the nationality rules and the advert first, then use the finder on this page to see the routes and support that fit you.",refs:["jn-cvisa-who-can-work-in-the-civil-service-the-na", "jn-jnroutes-civil-service-jobs-is-where-you-find-and"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Staff networks and the Race Champion are there to help new joiners settle; point candidates to them.",refs:["jn-jnday-staff-networks-and-the-civil-service-rac"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Ask for feedback for every candidate, keep reserve lists, and offer adjustments at every stage.",refs:["jn-jninterview-always-ask-for-feedback-whatever-the-out", "jn-cother-adjustments-at-every-stage-access-to-wor"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Entry-level pay can fall below the visa sponsorship threshold, so say clearly in adverts whether a role can be sponsored.",refs:["jn-cvisa-visa-sponsorship-is-possible-but-limited"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Publish Fast Stream and recruitment outcomes by ethnic group, as the Cabinet Office does, so gaps at each stage can be seen.",refs:["jn-jnbarriers-black-african-fast-stream-applicants-14-", "jn-jnbarriers-ethnic-minority-fast-stream-success-is-j"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"About 1% of Fast Stream applicants are recommended for appointment, and the current round closes at midday on 5 November.",refs:["jn-jnroutes-fast-stream-the-graduate-leadership-prog"]},{t:"Your nationality decides which Civil Service jobs you can do: a visa that lets you work is not enough on its own.",refs:["jn-cvisa-who-can-work-in-the-civil-service-the-na"]},{t:"Care leavers, carers, veterans and people with convictions have supported routes in, and most departments ask about convictions only after an offer.",refs:["jn-jnroutes-supported-routes-for-people-facing-barri", "jn-csecond-ban-the-box-no-conviction-questions-at-t"]},{t:"In 2026, 0.3% of Black African Fast Stream applicants were recommended for appointment, against 1.0% overall.",refs:["jn-jnbarriers-black-african-fast-stream-applicants-14-"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["fairtreat"]={name:"If you are treated unfairly",pfx:"ft",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"rdrecruit",h:"If it happens during recruitment",intro:"Job applicants are protected by equality law from the advert to the offer. Most routes start with the recruiting department, and the Civil Service Commission is an independent last stop for some, not all, recruitment complaints.",points:[
  {h:"Equality law protects applicants, not only employees",text:"In England, Scotland and Wales, section 39 of the Equality Act 2010 stops an employer discriminating against or victimising a job applicant in how it decides who to offer a job to, in the terms offered, or by not offering the job. Race includes colour, nationality and ethnic or national origin. A claim can be brought against a potential employer as well as a current one.",figure:"Equality Act 2010, s39(1) and (3)",year:"2010 to date",disputed:false,note:"Northern Ireland is covered by separate laws, including the Race Relations (Northern Ireland) Order 1997; see the Northern Ireland points. This is general information, not legal advice.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 39 Employees and applicants (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/39","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","in the arrangements A makes for deciding to whom to offer employment"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Make a claim to an employment tribunal, when you can claim (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/employment-tribunals","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","You can make a claim to an employment tribunal if you think someone has treated you unlawfully, such as your employer, a potential employer or a trade union."),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","race (including colour, nationality, ethnic and national origin)")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Ask the recruiting department first, using the steps in the advert",text:"Raise the concern with the department or agency that ran the campaign, using the complaints steps set out in the job advert, and ask for feedback and for the reasons for decisions. The Civil Service Commission will not look at a complaint until the department has had the chance to respond and has given a final answer. Asking specific questions, such as what the panel noted, can help either to resolve the matter or to decide on next steps.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Commission does not itself deal with the provision of feedback, so feedback requests go to the department.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (Recruitment Principles complaints) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","In the first instance, the matter should be raised with the department concerned."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The Commission will only consider your complaint if you have raised it first with the relevant department or agency"),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","why you did not get a promotion, including any notes from the interview panel on the decision made")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The Civil Service Commission: who can complain and when",text:"The Commission regulates the legal requirement that people are selected for the Civil Service on merit through fair and open competition, and it publishes the Recruitment Principles. Anyone, including a member of the public, can complain that a department breached those Principles once the department's own process is finished. Its guide says it considers complaints about competitions concluded in the past 12 months, and those concluded more than a year earlier only in exceptional circumstances.",figure:"12 months from the end of the competition",year:"Guide last updated 2017; web page current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The guide dates from January 2017; the web page and complaint form (March 2026) are newer, so check the current form before submitting. The Commission aims to complete investigations within 3 months.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010, section 10 Selections for appointments to the civil service (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/25/section/10","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","A person's selection must be on merit on the basis of fair and open competition."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (Recruitment Principles complaints) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","We can hear a complaint from anyone who believes a department has breached the requirements of the Recruitment Principles."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","your complaint relates to a recruitment exercise concluded in the past 12 months")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"What the Commission cannot look at",text:"The Commission does not deal with internal competitions and promotions, scores, feedback, pre-employment checks and vetting, or HR matters such as a withdrawn offer. It does not 'replay' competitions and will not usually act while the same matter is before an employment tribunal. It cannot hear complaints about most non-departmental public bodies or the wider public sector, whose staff are not civil servants.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Discrimination in any of these excluded areas can still be raised with the employer and, where the law allows, at an employment tribunal. A small number of Crown non-departmental public bodies employ civil servants and are covered.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (Recruitment Principles complaints) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Pre-employment checks and security and vetting issues;"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the Commission will not usually hear a complaint while it is subject to legal challenge at an Employment Tribunal or other court proceedings."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the Recruitment Principles do not formally apply to them as their employees are not members of the Civil Service.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Most complaints to the Commission are out of scope",text:"The Commission publishes the outcomes of recruitment complaints. Of 212 complaints in its 2024-25 table, 182 were recorded as out of scope, 13 were referred back to the department and 11 closed with no response from the complainant; of the few investigated, 3 found a breach and 3 found no breach. Complaints about scoring and reasonable adjustments were usually out of scope or sent back to the department.",figure:"182 of 212 out of scope; 3 breach, 3 no breach (2024-25)",year:"April 2024 to March 2025",disputed:false,note:"Figures are counted from the published table (converted from PDF), not stated as totals by the Commission, so treat them as approximate. The base rate is that very few complaints reach a finding, which is why the departmental and legal routes matter.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Outcome of Recruitment Complaints 2024-2025 (published 1 April 2025)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2025-04-01-Outcome-of-Recruitment-Complaints-202425.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Outcome of Recruitment Complaints 2024-2025"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Recruitment (Recruitment Principles complaints) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/recruitment-principles/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","We publish the outcomes of recruitment complaints."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The Commission will not consider appeals from people who simply want their application for a post to be re-considered.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"If a reasonable adjustment is refused",text:"Employers have a legal duty to make reasonable adjustments for disabled applicants, for example to application forms, tests and interviews. In Civil Service recruitment, ask for adjustments when you apply and, if a need appears later, contact the named person in the job advert. If an adjustment is refused, complain to the department first; a refusal can also be raised at an employment tribunal.",figure:"Equality Act 2010, s39(5)",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Commission's 2024-25 table shows reasonable adjustment complaints treated as out of scope or referred back to the department. Race and disability can overlap; both can be included in one complaint.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 39 Employees and applicants (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/39","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","A duty to make reasonable adjustments applies to an employer."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Discrimination: your rights, discrimination at work (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/discrimination-your-rights/discrimination-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","aptitude tests, for example giving extra time to complete the tests"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Reasonable Adjustments (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/reasonable-adjustments/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","please request help from the named contact in the job advert.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Northern Ireland: a separate Civil Service and separate laws",text:"The Northern Ireland Civil Service is outside the Great Britain Civil Service Commission's legal remit. The Civil Service Commissioners for Northern Ireland uphold the merit principle through their Recruitment Code and by auditing recruitment, and expect complaints to be handled promptly. Race discrimination in employment, including recruitment, is unlawful under Northern Ireland law and advice is available from the Equality Commission for Northern Ireland.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Northern Ireland Commissioners' published role covers the Recruitment Code, audit and Code of Ethics appeals; it does not describe an individual recruitment complaints route, so use the complaints steps in the job advert. Advice is available from the Equality Commission for Northern Ireland and Advice NI. This is general information, not legal advice.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010, section 1 Application of Chapter (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/25/section/1","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the Northern Ireland Civil Service;"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commissioners for Northern Ireland: What We Do (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nicscommissioners.org/what-we-do","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Auditing NICS recruitment policies and practices, using the Audit Framework and Guidance, to establish whether the Recruitment Code is being observed;"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commissioners for Northern Ireland: Recruitment Code, Version 16 (file dated May 2026)","https://www.nicscommissioners.org/files/nicscommissioners/2026-05/Recruitment%20Code%20V.16%20%281%29.DOCX","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Enquiries and/or complaints are dealt with in an efficient, appropriate and timely manner."),SRCQ("nidirect (Northern Ireland government): Racial discrimination (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/racial-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","in recruitment and selection - this includes arrangements for deciding who should be offered employment")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Scotland and Wales: same Commission, own codes",text:"The merit requirement and the Commission's complaints route cover the civil service of the State, which includes the Scottish Government and Welsh Government; only listed parts such as the security and intelligence agencies and the Northern Ireland Civil Service are excluded. Each government has its own version of the Civil Service Code. Acas offers its services in Welsh on request, and the same employment tribunal time limits apply across Great Britain, except for breach of contract claims in Scotland until 9 November 2026.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Councils, NHS bodies and most other public bodies are not civil service employers, so the Commission's route does not apply to them; their own complaints procedures and the tribunal route do. Breach of contract claims in Scotland follow a different time-limit timetable (change from 9 November 2026).",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010, section 1 Application of Chapter (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/25/section/1","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","this Chapter applies to the civil service of the State."),SRCQ("UK government: The Civil Service Code (updated 16 March 2015)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-code/the-civil-service-code","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Civil servants working for the Scottish and Welsh Governments, and their agencies, have their own versions of the code."),SRCQ("Acas: Contact Acas (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/contact","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","We can provide our services in Welsh on request.")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"rdwork",h:"If it happens while you are working",intro:"Inside a job, the usual order is informal first, then a written grievance under the department's procedure, then appeal. Some routes, such as the Civil Service Code and whistleblowing, cover different problems and are not substitutes for a discrimination grievance.",points:[
  {h:"Start informally where it is safe to do so",text:"Acas and GOV.UK both advise trying to sort the problem out informally first, with your manager, HR or someone senior. Informal resolution is usually quicker, but it is not suitable for every situation, and you can go straight to a formal complaint if the matter is serious.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"If the person involved is your manager, raise it with someone else in the management chain or HR.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","It's usually best to raise the problem informally first. You can do this with your manager, HR or someone senior from work."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Discrimination: your rights, what you can do (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/discrimination-your-rights/what-you-can-do","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Employees should talk to their employer first to try and sort out the problem informally."),SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Not every situation will be suitable for informal resolution.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Use the department's grievance and anti-bullying procedures",text:"If informal steps do not work, make a formal grievance in writing. Every employer should have a written grievance procedure, and your department may also have a specific policy on bullying, harassment and discrimination, which may set its own time limits. Follow that policy where there is one.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Departmental policies differ; check the intranet or HR for the current version.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Raise a grievance at work (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/raise-grievance-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","you can make a formal grievance complaint in writing."),SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","If your employer has a specific policy for making discrimination complaints, you should follow that policy."),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","whether there are any time limits for raising a complaint")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The Acas Code sets the minimum standard",text:"The Acas Code of Practice on disciplinary and grievance procedures says grievances should be raised in writing without unreasonable delay, heard at a meeting, decided in writing and open to appeal. Workers have a legal right to be accompanied by a colleague or trade union representative. A tribunal can adjust an award by up to 25% if either side unreasonably fails to follow the Code.",figure:"Up to 25% adjustment",year:"Code in effect since 11 March 2015",disputed:false,note:"The Code applies in Great Britain. Northern Ireland has its own Labour Relations Agency Code of Practice.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Code of Practice on disciplinary and grievance procedures (in effect from 11 March 2015)","https://www.acas.org.uk/acas-code-of-practice-on-disciplinary-and-grievance-procedures/html","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","If it is not possible to resolve a grievance informally employees should raise the matter formally and without unreasonable delay with a manager who is not the subject of the grievance."),SRCQ("Acas: Code of Practice on disciplinary and grievance procedures (in effect from 11 March 2015)","https://www.acas.org.uk/acas-code-of-practice-on-disciplinary-and-grievance-procedures/html","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Where an employee feels that their grievance has not been satisfactorily resolved they should appeal."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Raise a grievance at work (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/raise-grievance-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","any compensation you might get could be adjusted by up to 25%.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Civil Service Code concerns: nominated officers, then the Commission",text:"If you are asked to act against the Civil Service Code's values (integrity, honesty, objectivity, impartiality), raise it with your line management chain or your department's nominated officers, then with the Civil Service Commission if the response is unreasonable. Only serving civil servants can use this route. The Code does not cover HR management issues, so a discrimination grievance normally goes through the grievance route instead.",figure:"",year:"Code updated 2015; current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"In Northern Ireland, the Civil Service Commissioners for Northern Ireland hear appeals under the NICS Code of Ethics.",src:[SRCQ("UK government: The Civil Service Code (updated 16 March 2015)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-code/the-civil-service-code","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","you should raise the matter with your department’s nominated officers who have been appointed to advise staff on the code."),SRCQ("UK government: The Civil Service Code (updated 16 March 2015)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-code/the-civil-service-code","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","This code does not cover HR management issues."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: Code (Raising a Code Complaint) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/code/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Only civil servants currently serving in UK government departments, agencies and other bodies (excluding the Diplomatic Service) can bring complaints under the Code."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commissioners for Northern Ireland: What We Do (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nicscommissioners.org/what-we-do","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Considering and determining appeals from current civil servants under the NICS Code of Ethics")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Whistleblowing covers public-interest wrongdoing, not grievances",text:"Whistleblowing law protects workers who report wrongdoing in the public interest, such as a crime or a breach of a legal obligation. A personal grievance, including about how you were treated, is normally not covered unless your case is in the public interest. Since 6 April 2026, reporting sexual harassment counts as a qualifying disclosure.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Victimisation for making a discrimination complaint is covered by equality law instead (see the victimisation point).",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Whistleblowing for employees (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/whistleblowing","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Personal grievances are not covered by whistleblowing law, unless your particular case is in the public interest."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Employment Rights Act 1996, section 43B Disclosures qualifying for protection (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/18/section/43B","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","that a person has failed, is failing or is likely to fail to comply with any legal obligation to which he is subject,"),SRCQ("Acas: Employment Rights Act 2025 (last reviewed 27 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/employment-rights-act-2025","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","On 6 April 2026, sexual harassment became a 'qualifying disclosure' under whistleblowing law.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Trade unions can advise, accompany and represent",text:"If you are a member, a trade union representative can advise you, help you write a complaint or questions, and go with you to grievance and appeal meetings.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"What a union can offer depends on its membership terms; check with the union.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Joining a trade union (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/join-trade-union","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","going with members to disciplinary and grievance meetings"),SRCQ("Acas: Grievance procedure step 2, raising a formal grievance (last reviewed 21 May 2024)","https://www.acas.org.uk/grievance-procedure-step-by-step/step-2-raising-a-formal-grievance","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Employees can also talk to their trade union, if they have one, for advice and support."),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","You can also ask a trade union representative, if you have one, to send it on your behalf.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Staff networks and employee assistance",text:"Departmental diversity and race networks, and cross-government networks such as the Civil Service Race Forum, can offer support and a safe place for discussion, and can help you find the right route. They are not formal complaint routes and cannot decide a grievance. Your employer may also offer counselling or mental health support through an employee assistance programme.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Civil Service Race Forum description comes from a 2019 Civil Service blog post; check current network and race champion arrangements in your department.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service Networks (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-networks","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","offer support and a safe place for discussion"),SRCQ("Civil Service blog (GOV.UK): Focus on staff networks: Civil Service Race Forum (28 May 2019)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2019/05/28/focus-on-staff-networks-civil-service-race-forum/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The Civil Service Race Forum (CSRF), is an umbrella group for the elected chairs of each departmental race network"),SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","counselling or mental health support through an employee assistance programme (EAP)")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Protection from victimisation and third-party harassment",text:"It is unlawful to treat someone badly because they made, supported or gave evidence about a discrimination complaint in good faith. From 30 October 2026, employers in Great Britain can also be liable for harassment of their staff by third parties, such as members of the public, unless they took all reasonable steps to prevent it.",figure:"In force 30 October 2026 (third-party harassment)",year:"2010; changed 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The third-party harassment duty was brought into force on 30 October 2026 by S.I. 2026/1065, which commences section 21 of the Employment Rights Act 2025. The revised Equality Act text on legislation.gov.uk may not yet show the new wording. It applies in Great Britain only. This is general information, not legal advice.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Victimisation (last reviewed 26 August 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/discrimination-and-the-law/victimisation","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Victimisation is when someone is treated unfairly as a result of being involved with a discrimination or harassment complaint."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 39 Employees and applicants (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/39","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","An employer (A) must not victimise an employee of A's (B)"),SRCQ("Acas: Harassment law changes (last reviewed 26 August 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/employment-rights-act-2025/harassment-law-changes","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","On 30 October 2026 the law will change so employers will be liable for harassment from third parties."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Employment Rights Act 2025, section 21 Harassment by third parties (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2025/36/section/21","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","An employer (A) must not permit a third party to harass a person (B) who is an employee of A."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Employment Rights Act 2025 (Commencement No. 6 and Transitional and Saving Provisions) Regulations 2026 (S.I. 2026/1065), as made","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/1065/made","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","(b) section 21 (harassment by third parties);")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"rdexternal",h:"Outside routes, advice and the tribunal",intro:"If internal routes fail, conciliation and the tribunal are the legal routes, with free advice services alongside. Time limits run from the date of the act, not the end of an internal complaint.",points:[
  {h:"Acas early conciliation comes before most tribunal claims",text:"In Great Britain you must notify Acas before making most employment tribunal claims. Acas offers voluntary early conciliation, which can last up to 12 weeks, and the tribunal time limit is put on hold while it runs. If it does not settle the matter, Acas issues a certificate whose number goes on the claim form; if you notified Acas within your time limit, you have at least 1 month from receiving the certificate to claim.",figure:"Up to 12 weeks; at least 1 month after the certificate",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Acas says early conciliation is very busy and can take around 9 weeks to start, so notify early. Interim relief claims after dismissal have a 7-day limit and may not need notification.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Early conciliation (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/early-conciliation","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","You must notify Acas if you want to make a claim to an employment tribunal."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Make a claim to an employment tribunal, when you can claim (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/employment-tribunals","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The time limit for making a claim is put on hold while Acas helps you resolve your dispute."),SRCQ("Acas: How early conciliation works (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/early-conciliation/how-early-conciliation-works","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Early conciliation can last up to 12 weeks."),SRCQ("Acas: How early conciliation works (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/early-conciliation/how-early-conciliation-works","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","you'll have at least 1 month from the date you received the certificate to make your claim to the employment tribunal."),SRCQ("Acas: Contact Acas (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/contact","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","It can take around 9 weeks for conciliation to start."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Employment Tribunals (Early Conciliation: Exemptions and Rules of Procedure) Regulations 2014, Schedule (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2014/254/schedule","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","F4 Word in Sch. rule 6(1) substituted (1.12.2025) by The Employment Tribunals (Early Conciliation: Exemptions and Rules of Procedure) (Amendment) Regulations 2025 (S.I. 2025/1153)")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Tribunal time limit: now 6 months minus 1 day in Great Britain",text:"For problems on or after 1 October 2026, the time limit for most employment tribunal claims, including discrimination, is 6 months minus 1 day. For problems before that date the old limit of 3 months minus 1 day still applies; where conduct continued over a period, what counts is the date it ended. For discrimination the clock starts from the most recent act in the claim, and a tribunal can allow a later claim if it thinks it just and equitable.",figure:"6 months minus 1 day (acts from 1 October 2026); 3 months minus 1 day (earlier acts)",year:"Changed 1 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The change was made by the Employment Rights Act 2025 and commenced by S.I. 2026/954, which keeps the old limit where the act, or the end of conduct over a period, was before 1 October 2026. Some older Acas pages still mention 3 months minus 1 day. Northern Ireland is not covered by this change. This is general information, not legal advice.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Employment tribunal time limits (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/employment-tribunal-time-limits","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The law on time limits changed on 1 October 2026. How long you have depends on when the problem happened."),SRCQ("Acas: Employment tribunal time limits (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/employment-tribunal-time-limits","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","You have 6 months minus 1 day for most claims."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Make a claim to an employment tribunal, when you can claim (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/employment-tribunals","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","6 months if the problem happened on or after 1 October 2026"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 123 Time limits (revised text, up to date to 29 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/123","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Word in s. 123(1)(a) substituted (1.10.2026) by Employment Rights Act 2025"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 123 Time limits (revised text, up to date to 29 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/123","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","such other period as the employment tribunal thinks just and equitable."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The Employment Rights Act 2025 (Commencement No. 5 and Transitional Provisions) (Amendment) Regulations 2026 (S.I. 2026/954), as made","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/954/made","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The amendments made by Schedule 12 to the 2025 Act do not apply in any case where the relevant date occurs before 1st October 2026.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Northern Ireland: Labour Relations Agency and a 3-month limit",text:"In Northern Ireland anyone wishing to claim to the Industrial Tribunal or Fair Employment Tribunal must first notify the Labour Relations Agency about early conciliation. Race discrimination claims to the Industrial Tribunal must usually be made within 3 months. Religious belief and political opinion claims go to the Fair Employment Tribunal, with a 3-month limit from knowledge of the act, capped at 6 months.",figure:"3 months (race, Industrial Tribunal)",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The 1 October 2026 extension applies to Great Britain only. Conciliation can pause the deadline for up to one calendar month in Northern Ireland.",src:[SRCQ("Labour Relations Agency (Northern Ireland): Early Conciliation (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.lra.org.uk/early-conciliation","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Since 27 January 2020 anyone who wishes to lodge a claim with the Industrial or Fair Employment Tribunal must first notify the Labour Relations Agency and discuss the option of Early Conciliation."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Race Relations (Northern Ireland) Order 1997, Article 65 (revised text, up to date to 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisi/1997/869/article/65","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the period of 3 months beginning when the act complained of was done; or"),SRCQ("nidirect (Northern Ireland government): Racial discrimination (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/racial-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Complaints must be made within three months of the date of the alleged discrimination."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Fair Employment and Treatment (Northern Ireland) Order 1998, Article 46 Period within which proceedings to be brought (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisi/1998/3162/article/46","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the end of the period of 6 months beginning with the day on which the act was done.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Equality Advisory and Support Service (EASS)",text:"EASS is a helpline giving information about discrimination and human rights. It can explain the law, help resolve problems informally, refer to conciliation or mediation and help check eligibility for legal aid, but it cannot give legal advice or help once a claim has started. For employment disputes, the legal aid service asks people to speak to Acas before calling EASS.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Opening hours are Monday to Friday 9am to 7pm and Saturday 10am to 2pm. EASS covers England, Scotland and Wales; in Northern Ireland use the Equality Commission for Northern Ireland, the Labour Relations Agency or Advice NI.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Equality Advisory Support Service (EASS) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/equality-advisory-support-service","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The EASS provides information about discrimination and your rights."),SRCQ("Citizens Advice: Equality Advisory Support Service discrimination helpline (England page, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.citizensadvice.org.uk/law-and-courts/discrimination/about-discrimination/equality-advisory-support-service-discrimination-helpline/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The EASS can’t give you legal advice."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Check if you can get legal aid: next steps (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://checklegalaid.service.gov.uk/public/next-steps","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","For employment discrimination problems, please speak to ACAS before you call EASS."),SRCQ("Equality Advisory and Support Service: home page (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.equalityadvisoryservice.com/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The EASS helpline advises and assists individuals on issues relating to equality and human rights, across England, Scotland and Wales.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"The equality commissions: Great Britain and Northern Ireland",text:"The Equality and Human Rights Commission is the equality regulator for Great Britain; it no longer runs a public helpline (EASS replaced it) but has legal power to assist individuals in some proceedings. In Northern Ireland, the Equality Commission must give advice to a prospective complainant under fair employment law unless the request is frivolous, and may grant wider assistance, including in race cases. nidirect lists the Equality Commission and Advice NI as sources of free and impartial advice.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"This rests on legislation and government websites rather than the commissions' own guidance, so how each commission chooses which cases to support is not described here. Commission assistance in individual cases is discretionary. This is general information, not legal advice.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Equality and Human Rights Commission (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/equality-and-human-rights-commission","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) monitors human rights, protecting equality across 9 grounds"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Equality Advisory Support Service (EASS) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/equality-advisory-support-service","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","It has replaced the helpline service previously provided by the Equality and Human Rights Commission."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2006, section 28 Legal assistance (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/3/section/28","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The Commission may assist an individual who is or may become party to legal proceedings if"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Fair Employment and Treatment (Northern Ireland) Order 1998, Article 45 (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisi/1998/3162/article/45","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the Commission shall give him such advice unless it considers that the request is frivolous."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Race Relations (Northern Ireland) Order 1997, Article 64 Assistance by Commission (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisi/1997/869/article/64","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","the Commission shall consider the application and may grant it if it thinks fit to do so"),SRCQ("nidirect (Northern Ireland government): Racial discrimination (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/racial-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Advice NI and The Equality Commission for Northern Ireland can provide free and impartial advice.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Free legal advice: Citizens Advice, Law Centres, unions and legal aid",text:"GOV.UK points to Acas, Citizens Advice and trade union representatives if a problem cannot be sorted out informally. Law Centres give free legal advice and can represent people. Legal aid can be available for discrimination cases, and Civil Legal Advice gives free advice to those who are eligible.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Availability of local Law Centres varies and some have closed; check the Law Centres Network list.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Discrimination: your rights, what you can do (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/discrimination-your-rights/what-you-can-do","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","If things cannot be sorted out informally, talk to Acas, Citizens Advice or a trade union representative."),SRCQ("Law Centres Network: What is a Law Centre? (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.lawcentres.org.uk/what-is-a-law-centre","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Law Centres help those in greatest need and do not charge money for their services."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Discrimination: your rights, what you can do (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/discrimination-your-rights/what-you-can-do","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Check if you can get legal aid to help with your legal costs if you think you’ve been discriminated against.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Ombudsmen do not handle appointments or personnel matters",text:"The Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman cannot investigate action about appointments, pay, discipline or other personnel matters in Crown employment, so it is not a route for recruitment or workplace discrimination complaints against government departments. The Scottish Public Services Ombudsman has the same exclusion.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"This is based on legislation. For Civil Service recruitment, the independent route is the Civil Service Commission. This is general information, not legal advice.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1967, Schedule 3 Matters not subject to investigation (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1967/13/schedule/3","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Action taken in respect of appointments or removals, pay, discipline, superannuation or other personnel matters"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Scottish Public Services Ombudsman Act 2002, Schedule 4 Excluded matters (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asp/2002/11/schedule/4","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Action taken in respect of appointments or removals, pay, discipline, superannuation or other personnel matters.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Asking questions: no statutory form in Great Britain since 2014",text:"The formal discrimination questionnaire in the Equality Act 2010 was abolished in Great Britain from 6 April 2014. You can still send your employer a statement and questions, but there is no law requiring an answer. In Northern Ireland the statutory questions procedure still exists in race discrimination law.",figure:"Abolished 6 April 2014 (Great Britain)",year:"2014 to date",disputed:false,note:"A data subject access request is a separate legal right to see your own personal data (see the evidence section).",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013, section 66 (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2013/24/section/66","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","In the Equality Act 2010, omit section 138 (obtaining information, etc)."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013, section 66 (revised text, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2013/24/section/66","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","S. 66 in force at 6.4.2014"),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","However there's no law that says they have to answer your questions."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Race Relations (Northern Ireland) Order 1997, Article 63 Help for aggrieved persons in obtaining information (revised text, up to date to 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/nisi/1997/869/article/63","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","forms by which the person aggrieved may question the respondent on his reasons for doing any relevant act")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"rdevidence",h:"Keeping records and looking after yourself",intro:"Good records made at the time make every route easier, and complaints can be stressful. Start a record early and keep copies somewhere safe outside work systems you may lose access to.",points:[
  {h:"Keep a dated record from the start",text:"Write down what happened, when, who was present and how it affected you, as soon as you can. Keep emails, messages and screenshots. Note the dates of key decisions, such as sift results, interview dates and offers.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","dates and times it happened"),SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","any evidence, for example emails or screenshots of social media posts"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","provide details of any significant dates when decisions were taken;")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Keep the recruitment paper trail",text:"Save the job advert, person specification, your application, test results, invitations, feedback and every message with the recruiting team. Departments only have to keep records of external competitions for two years, so act early.",figure:"Records kept for 2 years",year:"Guide last updated 2017",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","include copies of relevant supporting material, retaining the originals;"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Departments and agencies are only required to maintain records of external recruitment competitions for two years."),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Keep a copy of the information you've sent in a safe place.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Ask for your own data with a subject access request",text:"You have a legal right to ask for a copy of the personal information an organisation holds about you, which can show what was recorded about you during recruitment or at work. Organisations usually have one month to respond, and requests are usually free.",figure:"1 month (can be extended by 2 months)",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"Organisations can withhold some information, for example about judicial or ministerial appointments, and do not have to say why.",src:[SRCQ("Information Commissioner's Office: Getting copies of your information (subject access request) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://ico.org.uk/for-the-public/getting-copies-of-your-information-subject-access-request/","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Organisations usually have one month to respond to a SAR."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Data protection, find out what data an organisation has about you (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/data-protection/find-out-what-data-an-organisation-has-about-you","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","The organisation must give you a copy of the data they hold about you as soon as possible, and within 1 month at most.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"How to word a complaint",text:"Put it in writing and set out what happened, when, who was involved, why you think it was discrimination and which protected characteristic it relates to. Ask clear questions and say what outcome you want. Keep it factual and concise.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Code of Practice on disciplinary and grievance procedures (in effect from 11 March 2015)","https://www.acas.org.uk/acas-code-of-practice-on-disciplinary-and-grievance-procedures/html","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","This should be done in writing and should set out the nature of the grievance."),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","why you think what happened might be discrimination and against the law"),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","explain what resolution you would ideally like to see.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Name people in the complaint, use roles in public",text:"A complaint to the employer should name the people involved so it can be investigated, and your own name will usually be shared with the department. In anything public, such as a network discussion, describing roles rather than naming individuals helps protect fairness and your own position.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The advice to use roles in public is the hub's own editorial guidance, not a quoted rule from these sources.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Give the names of the people who you think may have discriminated against you."),SRCQ("Civil Service Commission: A guide to bringing a Recruitment Principles complaint to the Civil Service Commission (last updated 3 January 2017)","https://civilservicecommission.independent.gov.uk/wp-content/themes/csc/download-document.php?file=2017-01-01-A-Guide-to-bringing-a-Recruitment-Principles-complaint-to-the-Civil-Service-Commission.pdf","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","we will, as a general rule, need to disclose your name to the department or agency")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Do not let internal steps run down the clock",text:"Raising a grievance or complaint inside the organisation does not stop the tribunal time limit. When you set a date for your employer to reply, allow for the deadline, and notify Acas in time if you may need to claim.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Early conciliation (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/early-conciliation","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","If you raise the problem with your employer first, this does not change the time limits to make an employment tribunal claim."),SRCQ("Acas: Asking and answering questions about discrimination (last reviewed 24 October 2022)","https://www.acas.org.uk/asking-and-answering-questions-about-discrimination","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","There are strict time limits for making a claim to an employment tribunal so you should consider this when setting a date.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Look after your wellbeing during a complaint",text:"Race discrimination can be very distressing and, in some cases, seriously affect mental health. Support may be available through an employee assistance programme, mental health first aiders, health and wellbeing representatives, a trade union and helplines such as Acas and EASS. Keeping a record can also help if talking about the experience is distressing.",figure:"",year:"Current at 30 October 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acas: Making and handling race discrimination complaints (last reviewed 21 August 2023)","https://www.acas.org.uk/race-discrimination/making-and-handling-race-discrimination-complaints","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Race discrimination can be very distressing and in some cases have a severe impact on someone's mental health and wellbeing."),SRCQ("Acas: How to raise a problem at work (last reviewed 29 August 2025)","https://www.acas.org.uk/how-to-raise-a-problem-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","mental health first aiders"),SRCQ("Acas: If you've been discriminated against at work (last reviewed 1 October 2026)","https://www.acas.org.uk/if-youve-been-discriminated-against-at-work","Supports the If you are treated unfairly page","Keeping a record can be especially helpful if you find talking about the experience particularly distressing.")],kind:"guidance"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Write things down at the time and keep copies outside work systems; the escalation ladder on this page shows each step and its time limit.",refs:["ft-rdevidence-keep-a-dated-record-from-the-start"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"If a colleague tells you about unfair treatment, listen, point them to their union or network, and remind them that time limits run.",refs:["ft-rdwork-trade-unions-can-advise-accompany-and-re", "ft-rdevidence-do-not-let-internal-steps-run-down-the-c"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Deal with grievances without unreasonable delay and in writing, as the Acas Code expects.",refs:["ft-rdwork-the-acas-code-sets-the-minimum-standard"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Put the complaints route in every advert and make adjustments easy to ask for.",refs:["ft-rdrecruit-ask-the-recruiting-department-first-usin", "ft-rdrecruit-if-a-reasonable-adjustment-is-refused"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Most complaints to the Commission are out of its scope, so publish clear routes for what it cannot hear.",refs:["ft-rdrecruit-most-complaints-to-the-commission-are-ou", "ft-rdrecruit-what-the-commission-cannot-look-at"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"Equality law protects job applicants as well as employees.",refs:["ft-rdrecruit-equality-law-protects-applicants-not-onl"]},{t:"Complain to the recruiting department first; the Civil Service Commission hears complaints about fair and open competition after that.",refs:["ft-rdrecruit-ask-the-recruiting-department-first-usin", "ft-rdrecruit-the-civil-service-commission-who-can-com"]},{t:"In Great Britain, tribunal claims about problems from 1 October 2026 must be made within 6 months minus 1 day, after contacting Acas.",refs:["ft-rdexternal-tribunal-time-limit-now-6-months-minus-1", "ft-rdexternal-acas-early-conciliation-comes-before-mos"]},{t:"Internal complaints do not stop the tribunal clock, so keep a dated record from the start.",refs:["ft-rdevidence-do-not-let-internal-steps-run-down-the-c", "ft-rdevidence-keep-a-dated-record-from-the-start"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["leadtargets"]={name:"Targets, commitments and allyship",pfx:"tg",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"tgcs",h:"Civil Service-wide targets and commitments",intro:"What the Civil Service as a whole has committed to, and the baseline at 30 October 2026. There is no current central ethnicity target for senior grades.",points:[
  {h:"Ethnic minority civil servants at a record 19.2%, SCS level at 10.6%",text:"At 31 March 2026, 19.2% of civil servants with a known ethnicity were from an ethnic minority background, a record high and up from 18.0% in 2025. Representation falls with seniority: at SCS level it is 10.6%, up only slightly from 10.5% in 2025. Black civil servants numbered 26,655 by headcount, the second largest ethnic minority group. This is the baseline any 2026-27 or 2027-28 ambition will be measured against.",figure:"19.2% overall; 10.6% at SCS level (31 March 2026)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Percentages exclude people whose ethnicity is unknown or undeclared. The SCS level measure used here differs from the Cabinet Office SCS Database, which holds the official headline Senior Civil Service figures. Civil Service Statistics 2026 gives headcounts by ethnic group; the latest percentage split on Ethnicity facts and figures (March 2024 data) showed 4.5% Black and 2.4% Mixed among civil servants with known ethnicity.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin, Civil Service Statistics 2026 (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Of those with a known ethnicity, the percentage who are from an ethnic minority background is at a record high of 19.2%."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin, Civil Service Statistics 2026 (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%, which is slightly up from 10.5% in 2025"),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (UK government): Civil Service workforce (published 11 October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/civil-service-workforce/latest","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","8.9% of civil servants were Asian, 4.5% were black, 2.4% had mixed ethnicity"),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Diversity in the civil service, explainer (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/civil-service-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The SCS has more than doubled its proportion of minority ethnic staff from 5% in 2010 to 10.7% in 2024")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"No current Civil Service-wide SCS ethnicity target",text:"The Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy covered 2022 to 2025 and deliberately chose not to set standalone representation targets, embedding ambitions in other programmes instead. Its period has ended and no successor Civil Service-wide strategy or published Senior Civil Service ethnicity target is listed alongside it on GOV.UK at 30 October 2026. Organisations therefore have no central numeric target to report against in 2026-27; departmental objectives carry the weight.",figure:"",year:"2022 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"No successor strategy or Senior Civil Service ethnicity target was listed on GOV.UK at 30 October 2026. A target set internally by a department, or published elsewhere, would change this picture.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service: Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2022 to 2025 (24 February 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Rather than relying on standalone targets, our ambitions will be embedded in these key deliverables designed to improve"),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Diversity in the civil service, explainer (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/civil-service-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Publishing and implementing diversity strategies, including A Brilliant Civil Service 2017-2020"),SRCQ("Civil Service World: Civil service diversity strategy criticised for lack of targets and guidance (14 December 2022)","https://www.civilserviceworld.com/professions/article/civil-service-diversity-strategy-criticised-lack-targets-guidance-disability-gender-race","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The broad-brush approach to equality set out in the latest civil service diversity and inclusion strategy has left departments without targets or guidance to improve ethnicity, disability and gender representation")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Places for Growth 2030: half of UK-based SCS outside London",text:"The government aims to have 50% of UK-based Senior Civil Servants outside London by 2030, to cut London-based civil servants by 12,000 by 2030 and to offer half of Fast Stream places outside London. London has the highest share of ethnic minority civil servants of any region, so relocation can change who reaches senior roles. The 2022 to 2025 Civil Service strategy committed to monitor diversity data through the rollout, and staff networks can ask whether that monitoring continues.",figure:"50% of UK-based SCS outside London by 2030; London headcount down 12,000",year:"2025 to 2030",disputed:false,note:"A published government ambition, not a statutory duty. The 38.0% London figure for ethnic minority staff is from 2024 data. The Institute for Government notes the SCS relocation ambition is taking longer to achieve.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Places for Growth 2030 collection (last updated 29 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/places-for-growth","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","ensuring 50% of UK-based Senior Civil Servants (SCS) are located outside London in the English regions, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland by 2030."),SRCQ("UK government: Thousands of Civil Service roles moved out of London in latest reform to the state (press release, 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/thousands-of-civil-service-roles-moved-out-of-london-in-latest-reform-to-the-state","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","the number of London based civil servants will reduce by 12,000 by 2030"),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Relocation of the civil service, explainer (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/relocation-civil-service","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","which is taking longer to achieve"),SRCQ("Ethnicity facts and figures (UK government): Civil Service workforce (published 11 October 2024)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/workforce-and-business/workforce-diversity/civil-service-workforce/latest","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","out of all regions, London had the highest percentage of staff from ethnic minority groups (not including white minorities), at 38.0%"),SRCQ("Civil Service: Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2022 to 2025 (24 February 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Monitor diversity data throughout Places for Growth’s rollout to identify and address any unintended consequences.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Civil Service ethnicity pay gaps 2026: Black and Mixed groups",text:"The Cabinet Office publishes Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics each year. In 2026 the median hourly earnings gap for Black civil servants compared with White civil servants widened to 5.3%, while the median bonus gap narrowed to 40.3% from 42.9%. For the Mixed or Multiple group the median earnings gap was 0% and the median bonus gap 7.7%. Departmental figures, published in the same release, are the starting point for any departmental pay action plan.",figure:"Black: 5.3% earnings gap, 40.3% bonus gap; Mixed: 0% and 7.7% (2026)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, Cabinet Office. Earnings pay gaps vary widely by department, and part of the gap reflects the larger share of ethnic minority civil servants working in London, where pay is generally higher.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The median ethnicity earnings pay gap for Black civil servants compared to White civil servants is 5.3%, up from 4.5% in 2025."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","This means the median bonus of Black civil servants is 40.3% lower than that of White civil servants."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","This means the median hourly earnings of civil servants from the Mixed or Multiple ethnic group is the same as that of White civil servants."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service statistics 2026 (published 29 July 2026, updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Added Disability and Ethnicity 2026 pay gap bulletins and supporting documents."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Earnings pay gap statistics vary widely by department."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","This is because of the disproportionate number of ethnic minority civil servants employed in London, where pay levels are generally higher.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"People Survey: discrimination at 7%, ethnic background cited by 11%",text:"In the 2025 Civil Service People Survey, the Civil Service benchmark score for experiencing discrimination at work in the previous 12 months was 7%, unchanged from 2024. Among those reporting discrimination, the median benchmark for citing ethnic background was 11%, up 2 percentage points. The Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport says it will measure progress on its bullying, harassment and discrimination objective through People Survey data, so results by ethnic group matter for 2026-27 reporting.",figure:"7% discriminated against; 11% of those cited ethnic background (2025)",year:"2025 survey, published 2026",disputed:false,note:"The 2025 survey was shorter and some theme scores are not comparable with earlier years. The 11% figure is a median benchmark across organisations, read from Table 2 of the results highlights. Earlier analysis found Black (9.5%) and Mixed (11.4%) civil servants more likely than White colleagues (7.8%) to report bullying or harassment in 2023.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service People Survey 2025, results highlights (updated 28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results-highlights","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The Civil Service benchmark score for discrimination (those who answered ‘Yes’ to E01) was 7% (Figure 4), the same when compared with 2024."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service People Survey 2025, results highlights (updated 28 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results/civil-service-people-survey-2025-results-highlights","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Table 2: 2025 Civil Service median benchmark scores, grounds of discrimination (E02) and the percentage point (pp) change compared to 2024"),SRCQ("Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-digital-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Progress will be measured through People Survey data."),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Diversity in the civil service, explainer (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/civil-service-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Civil servants from all minority ethnic categories were more likely to say they were bullied or harassed in the past 12 months")],kind:"evidence"}]},
 {k:"tgdept",h:"Department and legal duties",intro:"What the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport, including GDS, has published, what the law already requires of public bodies, and what is only proposed.",points:[
  {h:"DCMS equality objectives 2025 to 2030 include a sponsorship pilot",text:"The Department for Culture, Media and Sport became the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport in July 2026, and GDS is now part of it. Its published equality objectives for 2025 to 2030 commit to eliminating bullying, harassment and discrimination, to improving senior representation for under-represented groups through career interventions and a sponsorship pilot, and to annual pay gap reports and equity audits. Progress is to be tracked through People Survey and workforce data.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2030",disputed:false,note:"The objectives do not name Black or Mixed-heritage staff and set no numeric targets. The page still lists the former department's documents; GDS has no separate equality page.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-digital-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Enhancing representation at senior levels for underrepresented groups by supporting junior staff through targeted career interventions and piloting a sponsorship programme."),SRCQ("Department for Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (withdrawn 16 September 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The Department for Culture, Media and Sport was replaced by the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport in July 2026."),SRCQ("Government Digital Service: organisation page on GOV.UK (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/government-digital-service","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Part of Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Departmental pay gap reporting: gender published, ethnicity not listed",text:"The department commits to annual pay gap reports and equity audits. Its equality page lists gender pay gap reports only, the latest listed being 2024, with a 2025 gender pay gap report published in December 2025. No departmental ethnicity pay gap report is listed. The Cabinet Office publishes ethnicity pay gap figures for each department alongside its Civil Service-wide statistics, and these are the published figures that cover the department's staff.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"A department may hold ethnicity pay analysis it has not published. Read 'not listed' as 'not found on the equality page at 30 October 2026'.",src:[SRCQ("Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-digital-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Remaining committed to annual pay gap reports and equity audits, continuously refining diverse recruitment and promotion practices"),SRCQ("Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-digital-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","We publish reports which summarise the difference between the average salary of women and men in DCMS."),SRCQ("Department for Culture, Media and Sport: DCMS gender pay gap report 2025 (16 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/dcms-gender-pay-gap-report-2025/dcms-gender-pay-gap-report-2025","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Published 16 December 2025"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","All departmental statistics are provided at Table G")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"PSED: publish information yearly and objectives every four years",text:"Public authorities listed in the 2017 specific duties regulations must publish information showing compliance with the public sector equality duty at intervals of not more than one year, including information on employees if they have 150 or more. They must publish one or more equality objectives at intervals of not more than four years, and each objective must be specific and measurable. The general duty requires due regard to eliminating discrimination, advancing equality of opportunity and fostering good relations.",figure:"Information: at least yearly; objectives: at least every 4 years",year:"In force since 2017",disputed:false,note:"General information, not legal advice. These regulations cover authorities listed in Schedule 2 (mainly England and some cross-border bodies); Scotland and Wales have their own specific duties. The Ministry of Justice page describes the earlier 2011 regulations, since replaced.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties and Public Authorities) Regulations 2017, regulation 4 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2017/353/regulation/4","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","subsequently at intervals of not greater than one year beginning with the date of last publication."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties and Public Authorities) Regulations 2017, regulation 5 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2017/353/regulation/5","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","An objective published by a public authority in compliance with paragraph (1) must be specific and measurable."),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010 (Specific Duties and Public Authorities) Regulations 2017, regulation 5 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2017/353/regulation/5","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","subsequently at intervals of not greater than four years beginning with the date of last publication."),SRCQ("Ministry of Justice: Public sector equality duty (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-sector-equality-duty","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","equality objectives, at least every four years (from 6th April 2012)"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 149 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/149","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","advance equality of opportunity between persons who share a relevant protected characteristic and persons who do not share it;")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Ethnicity pay gap law: proposed, not introduced at 30 October 2026",text:"The government confirmed in March 2026 that it will legislate for mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting for employers with 250 or more staff, and published draft clauses alongside its consultation response. The Equality (Race and Disability) Bill had not been introduced before Parliament was prorogued in April 2026 and was absent from the King's Speech of 13 May 2026. No date for reporting to start has been published, so there is no statutory ethnicity pay gap duty in 2026-27 and none is confirmed for 2027-28.",figure:"",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Status can change quickly if a bill is introduced. Linklaters' working assumption, published in March 2026, is that mandatory reporting is unlikely before 2027 at the earliest and likely to be later.",src:[SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The responses to this consultation have informed our work to continue developing legislation to introduce mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting for large employers"),SRCQ("Full Fact: Is the government on course to introduce a new Race Equality Act? (12 May 2026)","https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/race-equality-act/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","this legislation was not introduced before the prorogation of Parliament at the end of April 2026 and is yet to be published."),SRCQ("LexisNexis: Equality (Race and Disability) Bill absent from King's Speech (13 May 2026)","https://www.lexisnexis.co.uk/legal/news/equality-race-disability-bill-absent-from-kings-speech","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The Government’s Equality (Race and Disability) Bill was notably absent from today’s King’s Speech"),SRCQ("Linklaters: Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting (March 2026)","https://www.linklaters.com/insights/blogs/employmentlinks/2026/march/mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","mandatory reporting is unlikely to be required before 2027 at the earliest, and is likely to be later"),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Equality (Race and Disability) Bill, mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, consultation outcome (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","along with draft clauses of primary legislation to introduce mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Proposed reporting would show Black and Mixed groups separately",text:"Under the proposed model, employers would report a binary comparison of White (including White Other) with all other groups, and also report by five broad groups where numbers allow, including Black and Mixed or multiple groups. Public sector bodies in England, and certain public authorities working across Great Britain on non-devolved functions, would be in scope if they have 250 or more employees. The government intends that large employers also publish action plans to tackle any ethnicity pay gap.",figure:"250+ employees; 5 broad ethnic groups",year:"Proposed (2026)",disputed:false,note:"Proposed, not law. The consultation proposed a minimum of 10 employees in each group reported on, but the government says work to set the threshold is ongoing.",src:[SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Ethnicity pay gap reporting will require employers to report a binary comparison at minimum and also aggregate to 5 ethnic groups where possible."),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","the government intends to introduce a requirement for large employers to publish action plans to tackle any ethnicity and disability pay gaps"),SRCQ("Linklaters: Mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting (March 2026)","https://www.linklaters.com/insights/blogs/employmentlinks/2026/march/mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The government has confirmed that it will mandate action plans."),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Policy work is ongoing to determine the most appropriate threshold."),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","certain public authorities operating across Great Britain in relation to non-devolved functions")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Equality action plans: voluntary to 30 March 2027, mandatory planned",text:"Employers with 250 or more employees can publish a voluntary gender equality action plan in the 2026 to 2027 reporting year; for most public authority employers the deadline is 30 March 2027. Subject to legislation, action plans become mandatory from spring 2027. The government aims for a single equality action plan covering sex, race and disability once all reporting duties are in force, so building ethnicity into plans now is a practical step.",figure:"Deadline 30 March 2027 (most public authorities)",year:"2026-27 and 2027-28",disputed:false,note:"Action plans cover the gender pay gap and menopause, not ethnicity, though the guidance asks employers to consider how sex overlaps with characteristics such as ethnicity. Mandatory plans depend on legislation, and the single combined plan is an aim, not yet law.",src:[SRCQ("UK government: Creating an action plan, guidance for employers, overview (updated 13 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/creating-an-action-plan-guidance-for-employers/overview","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Subject to legislation, these will become mandatory from spring 2027."),SRCQ("UK government: Creating an action plan, guidance for employers, overview (updated 13 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/creating-an-action-plan-guidance-for-employers/overview","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","30 March 2027 – for most public authority employers"),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","We are aiming to harmonise the approaches so that employers can produce a single equality action plan on the same service covering sex, race (including ethnicity) and disability"),SRCQ("Lewis Silkin: New government guidance on equality action plans published (13 March 2026)","https://www.lewissilkin.com/en/insights/2026/03/13/new-government-guidance-on-equality-actions-plans-published","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Publishing action plans will be voluntary from April 2026 but mandatory from April 2027."),SRCQ("UK government: Creating an action plan, guidance for employers, overview (updated 13 May 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/creating-an-action-plan-guidance-for-employers/overview","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","You should consider how employees may be disadvantaged in the workplace due to the overlapping impact of their sex and other characteristics, such as:")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"tgwider",h:"Wider public sector targets and plans",intro:"Commitments in the NHS, policing, Windrush redress and Wales with dates in 2026-27 or 2027-28. Each point says whether it is statutory, a published commitment or voluntary.",points:[
  {h:"NHS WRES: tenth national report published April 2026",text:"NHS England published the 2025 Workforce Race Equality Standard report on 7 April 2026, the tenth since the standard was mandated, covering nine indicators for NHS trusts. Representation of Black and minority ethnic staff continues to rise, but staff remain under-represented at senior and very senior levels. Trusts are expected to keep collecting WRES data and acting on it in 2026-27.",figure:"10th annual WRES publication; 9 indicators",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The WRES has been required through the NHS Standard Contract since 2015/16. Arrangements for national NHS bodies are changing, so check NHS England for the 2026 collection timetable.",src:[SRCQ("NHS England: Workforce Race Equality Standard 2025 data analysis report for NHS trusts (7 April 2026)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/publication/workforce-race-equality-standard-2025-data-analysis-report-for-nhs-trusts/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Published 7 April 2026."),SRCQ("NHS England: Workforce Race Equality Standard 2025 data analysis report for NHS trusts (7 April 2026)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/publication/workforce-race-equality-standard-2025-data-analysis-report-for-nhs-trusts/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","10th publication since the WRES was mandated"),SRCQ("NHS Employers: Workforce Race Equality Standard report published (17 April 2026)","https://www.nhsemployers.org/news/workforce-race-equality-standard-report-published","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","While representation of Black and minority ethnic (BME) staff continues to increase, the report highlights ongoing disparities in career progression, with underrepresentation at senior and very senior levels."),SRCQ("NHS Employers: Workforce Race Equality Standard report published (17 April 2026)","https://www.nhsemployers.org/news/workforce-race-equality-standard-report-published","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The latest data marks the tenth annual publication and tracks progress across nine indicators used to measure workplace equality in NHS trusts."),SRCQ("NHS England: NHS Workforce Race Equality Standard (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/about/equality/equality-hub/workforce-equality-data-standards/equality-standard/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","through the NHS standard contract, starting in 2015/16")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Mann review on NHS racism: progress reports due 2026 and 2027",text:"In June 2026 the government accepted Lord Mann's review of antisemitism and other forms of racism in the NHS, including robust accountability for race equality metrics and better data and transparency. It committed to an initial update to Parliament by October 2026 and a detailed report within 12 months of the review's publication on 4 June 2026. NHS staff standards published in July 2026 set minimum requirements for employers, including on tackling racism.",figure:"Initial update by October 2026; detailed report within 12 months",year:"2026 to 2027",disputed:false,note:"No October 2026 update had been published on GOV.UK by 30 October 2026. Some recommendations need consultation or legislation. These commitments and the staff standards apply to the NHS in England.",src:[SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care: Government response to the Lord Mann review into antisemitism and other forms of racism in the NHS (4 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-response-to-the-lord-mann-review-on-antisemitism-and-other-forms-of-racism-in-the-nhs/government-response-to-the-lord-mann-review-into-antisemitism-and-other-forms-of-racism-in-the-nhs","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","providing an initial update by October 2026 and a detailed report within 12 months of publication of the review."),SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care: Government response to the Lord Mann review into antisemitism and other forms of racism in the NHS (4 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-response-to-the-lord-mann-review-on-antisemitism-and-other-forms-of-racism-in-the-nhs/government-response-to-the-lord-mann-review-into-antisemitism-and-other-forms-of-racism-in-the-nhs","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","set out expectations for employers to take sustained and meaningful action to prevent and root out racism and discrimination in the NHS workplace"),SRCQ("NHS Employers: Workforce Race Equality Standard report published (17 April 2026)","https://www.nhsemployers.org/news/workforce-race-equality-standard-report-published","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The government has accepted in full Lord John Mann’s review into antisemitism and wider racism in the NHS."),SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care: NHS staff standards (6 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-staff-standards","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The standards set out the actions employers must take to deliver them and what staff can expect at work as a result."),SRCQ("Department of Health and Social Care: Lord Mann review on antisemitism and other forms of racism in the NHS (4 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/lord-mann-review-on-antisemitism-and-other-forms-of-racism-in-the-nhs","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Published: 4 June 2026")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Police Race Action Plan continues in a new form after 2026 review",text:"The Police Race Action Plan was launched in May 2022 to address inequalities affecting Black people. Its Independent Scrutiny and Oversight Board published its final report in April 2026. The chair of the National Police Chiefs' Council said progress had been inconsistent and that the plan will continue in a new guise with an operational focus on anti-racism. The government has committed to include its data in future versions of the Police Performance Framework.",figure:"Ethnic minority officers 8.5% of FTE workforce",year:"2022 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Sector-led and voluntary, not statutory. The 8.5% figure excludes White minorities. No dated milestones for 2026-27 or 2027-28 were published in the sources used.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Race Equality Engagement Group, One year of action 2025 to 2026 (15 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action-2025-to-2026/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The government has also committed to include PRAP data in future iterations of its Police Performance Framework to ensure improvements can be further driven."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Race Equality Engagement Group, One year of action 2025 to 2026 (15 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action-2025-to-2026/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","making up 8.5% of the workforce, the same proportion as the previous year"),SRCQ("National Police Chiefs' Council: Response to Police Race Action Plan ISOB report (2 April 2026)","https://news.npcc.police.uk/releases/response-to-police-race-action-plan-isob-report-2","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","PRAP will continue in a new guise with a strong operational focus on anti-racism")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Windrush Compensation Scheme: monthly data, no closing target",text:"The Home Office publishes Windrush Compensation Scheme data every month; the August 2026 release was published on 29 October 2026. By the end of August 2026 the scheme had paid about £133.5 million across 4,221 claims. Under changes announced in October 2025, following recommendations from the independent Windrush Commissioner, claimants who request a review of their decision can be paid up to 75% of their award in advance.",figure:"£133.5m paid on 4,221 claims (end of August 2026)",year:"2019 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, Home Office. The £133.5 million and 4,221 figures are from table WCS_03 of the August 2026 data tables. No numeric target or end date for 2026-27 or 2027-28 is published.",src:[SRCQ("Home Office: Windrush Compensation Scheme data, August 2026 (29 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/windrush-compensation-scheme-data-august-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Data on claims and payments made under the Windrush Compensation Scheme."),SRCQ("Home Office: Windrush Compensation Scheme overhaul to deliver faster justice (24 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/windrush-compensation-scheme-overhaul-to-deliver-faster-justice","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Victims of the Home Office Windrush scandal will be paid up to 75% of their compensation award in advance as part of a major overhaul of the claims process."),SRCQ("Home Office: Windrush Compensation Scheme overhaul to deliver faster justice (24 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/windrush-compensation-scheme-overhaul-to-deliver-faster-justice","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","They follow recommendations by the independent Windrush Commissioner Reverend Clive Foster"),SRCQ("Home Office: Windrush Compensation Scheme overhaul to deliver faster justice (24 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/windrush-compensation-scheme-overhaul-to-deliver-faster-justice","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Advance payments will be made where a claimant is unhappy with their compensation decision and requests a review.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"No standalone UK race equality strategy; REEG and maternity pledge",text:"No standalone UK government race equality strategy had been published by 30 October 2026, and the government has said there will be no separate Race Equality Act; its race equality legislation is planned as the Equality (Race and Disability) Bill. The Race Equality Engagement Group, set up in March 2025, reported on its first year in July 2026 and feeds community voices into government plans on policing, finance, maternal health and education. The manifesto commitment to set an explicit target to close the Black and Asian maternal mortality gap has not yet produced a published numeric target.",figure:"",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Absence of a strategy is unconfirmed and could change. The maternal mortality target is a commitment awaiting a national action plan from the maternity and neonatal taskforce.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Race Equality Engagement Group, One year of action 2025 to 2026 (15 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action-2025-to-2026/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The Group helps to shape the government’s ambitious plan to tackle racial inequalities, channeling community voices and lived experiences directly into the policy process"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Race Equality Engagement Group, One year of action 2025 to 2026 (15 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action-2025-to-2026/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","set an explicit target to close the Black and Asian maternal mortality gap"),SRCQ("Full Fact: Is the government on course to introduce a new Race Equality Act? (12 May 2026)","https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/race-equality-act/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","A Cabinet Office spokesperson confirmed to Full Fact in August 2025 there was no plan to introduce a separate Race Equality Act."),SRCQ("Full Fact: Is the government on course to introduce a new Race Equality Act? (updated 12 May 2026)","https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/race-equality-act/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","It appears then that the Race Equality Act, as outlined in Labour’s manifesto, has been renamed as the Equality (Race and Disability) Bill")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Wales aims to be an anti-racist nation by 2030",text:"The Anti-racist Wales Action Plan, refreshed in 2024, commits the Welsh Government to making Wales an anti-racist nation by 2030. Senior leaders in the Welsh Government civil service will have clear anti-racism objectives and 360-degree feedback, and leaders in Welsh Government-funded bodies will have measurable anti-racism objectives. A maturity matrix for tracking progress was published by January 2026.",figure:"Anti-racist Wales by 2030",year:"2022 to 2030",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, Welsh Government. It is a government policy commitment, not a statutory duty, and applies to Wales only.",src:[SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan, 2024 update (13 November 2024)","https://www.gov.wales/anti-racist-wales-action-plan-2024-update-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","This refreshed plan emphasises the changes required to deliver on our commitment to make Wales an anti-racist nation by 2030."),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan, 2024 update (13 November 2024)","https://www.gov.wales/anti-racist-wales-action-plan-2024-update-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Leaders in Welsh Government-funded bodies will also have measurable anti-racism objectives."),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan progress update (28 January 2026)","https://www.gov.wales/anti-racist-wales-action-plan-arwap-progress-update","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","A new Maturity Matrix was published to monitor progress towards becoming an anti-racist organisation.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"tgadvocate",h:"What communities and networks could advocate for",intro:"Asks grounded in published recommendations that staff networks and communities can pursue during 2026-27 and 2027-28.",points:[
  {h:"Ask for data by Black and Mixed groups, not only 'ethnic minority'",text:"Aggregate 'ethnic minority' figures can hide different outcomes for Black and Mixed-heritage staff, as the Civil Service pay gap statistics show. The proposed national reporting model uses five broad groups that separate Black and Mixed groups. Networks can ask departments to publish workforce, progression and pay data in the same five groups now, where numbers are large enough to protect anonymity.",figure:"",year:"2026-27",disputed:false,note:"Small numbers in some grades mean figures may need to be suppressed or combined to protect privacy.",src:[SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","In practice, this means that individual ethnic groups would be aggregated into one of 5 broader ethnic groups:"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Civil Service ethnicity pay gap statistics 2026, statistical bulletin (28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gap-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","There are five different ethnic groups presented in these statistics : Asian, Black, Mixed and Multiple ethnic group, Other ethnic group and White"),SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Doing more to encourage employees to disclose their ethnicity")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Press for published pay gap action plans with targets",text:"The McGregor-Smith Review recommended that employers publish aspirational targets and report progress annually. The Race at Work Charter asks signatories to capture ethnicity data and publicise progress. The government intends to require large employers to publish action plans on ethnicity pay gaps, so networks can ask for a plan now rather than waiting for the law.",figure:"",year:"2017 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Targets recommended by McGregor-Smith are aspirational, not quotas. The legal duty for action plans on ethnicity does not yet exist.",src:[SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Employers must publish their aspirational targets, be transparent about their progress and be accountable for delivering them"),SRCQ("Business in the Community: Race at Work Charter (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/race-at-work-charter/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Capture ethnicity data and publicise progress"),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Consultation on mandatory ethnicity and disability pay gap reporting, government response (updated 25 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/equality-race-and-disability-bill-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting/outcome/consultation-on-mandatory-ethnicity-and-disability-pay-gap-reporting-government-response","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","the government intends to introduce a requirement for large employers to publish action plans to tackle any ethnicity and disability pay gaps")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Turn mentoring into sponsorship that changes careers",text:"Government guidance notes that women from ethnic minority backgrounds may be over-mentored but under-sponsored. Sponsorship means a senior person advocating for someone and opening up opportunities, not only giving advice. The department's own objectives include piloting a sponsorship programme, and earlier Civil Service schemes paired ethnic minority Grade 6 and 7 staff with senior sponsors to help them reach the Senior Civil Service.",figure:"",year:"2020 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"The 2026 guidance was written for gender pay gap action plans but its sponsorship advice applies more widely. The current status of the earlier Civil Service scheme described in 2020 is unconfirmed.",src:[SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Offer mentoring, sponsorship and other development programmes (4 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/offer-mentoring-sponsorship-and-other-development-programmes/offer-mentoring-sponsorship-and-other-development-programmes","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Women – especially those from ethnic minority backgrounds – may potentially be ‘over-mentored but under-sponsored’ compared with men."),SRCQ("Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-digital-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Enhancing representation at senior levels for underrepresented groups by supporting junior staff through targeted career interventions and piloting a sponsorship programme."),SRCQ("Civil Service blog: How sponsorship empowers (27 February 2020)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2020/02/27/how-sponsorship-empowers/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The programme aims to enable high-potential ethnic minority staff at Grade 6 or 7, who are not on any formal talent scheme, to reach the Senior Civil Service.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Ask for a stated ambition for progression to senior grades",text:"Ethnic minority representation at SCS level (10.6%) is about half the overall Civil Service rate (19.2%). McGregor-Smith said every employer with more than 50 staff should set aspirational targets throughout the organisation, not just at the bottom. Networks can ask for a published, time-bound ambition for senior grades and for Black and Mixed-heritage staff in particular.",figure:"SCS level 10.6% v 19.2% overall (2026)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"General information, not legal advice. Quotas or reserved posts are generally not lawful; aspirational targets and positive action within the Equality Act are.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Statistical bulletin, Civil Service Statistics 2026 (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/civil-service-statistics-2026/statistical-bulletin-civil-service-statistics-2026","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","those at SCS level having the lowest representation rate at 10.6%, which is slightly up from 10.5% in 2025"),SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","So all employers with more than 50 people must set aspirational targets to increase diversity and inclusion throughout their organisations – not just at the bottom"),SRCQ("Institute for Government: Diversity in the civil service, explainer (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/explainer/civil-service-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","although this is below the population benchmark of economically active adults")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Use lawful positive action in development and recruitment",text:"The Equality Act allows proportionate positive action where an employer reasonably thinks people who share a protected characteristic are disadvantaged or under-represented, for example targeted development schemes. In recruitment and promotion, an employer may treat a candidate more favourably only if they are as qualified as the other candidate, the employer has no policy of favouring that group, and the action is proportionate. The 2022 to 2025 Civil Service strategy committed to using positive action where needed.",figure:"",year:"In force since 2010",disputed:false,note:"General information, not legal advice. Specialist HR or legal advice is needed before designing any positive action scheme.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 158 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/158","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","enabling or encouraging persons who share the protected characteristic to overcome or minimise that disadvantage,"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 159 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/159","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","A is as qualified as B to be recruited or promoted,"),SRCQ("Civil Service: Civil Service Diversity and Inclusion Strategy 2022 to 2025 (24 February 2022)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025/civil-service-diversity-and-inclusion-strategy-2022-to-2025-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","We will use positive action where needed in relation to training, support, recruitment and promotion"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: Equality Act 2010, section 159 (read 30 October 2026)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/159","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","taking the action in question is a proportionate means of achieving the aim referred to in subsection (2).")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Hold executive sponsors to account with objectives and reports",text:"McGregor-Smith called for executive sponsorship of key targets and for diversity to be a key performance indicator. Business in the Community now builds executive sponsor leadership into every Race at Work Charter commitment. The Welsh Government gives its senior leaders clear anti-racism objectives with 360-degree feedback, a model networks can point to.",figure:"",year:"2017 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"These are recommendations and voluntary commitments, not legal duties.",src:[SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Ensuring executive sponsorship for key targets"),SRCQ("Business in the Community: Race at Work Charter (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/race-at-work-charter/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Executive sponsor leadership has been embedded into each charter commitment."),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan, 2024 update (13 November 2024)","https://www.gov.wales/anti-racist-wales-action-plan-2024-update-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The Welsh Government Civil Service will ensure senior leaders have clear anti-racism objectives, embed 360-feedback, and provide bespoke anti-racism training.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Give networks a formal advisory voice",text:"The Civil Service Race Forum brings together ethnic minority staff networks and aims to advise Civil Service leadership on racial diversity and equality. The Race Equality Engagement Group shows a similar model at national level, feeding lived experience directly into policy. Networks can ask for a standing place in workforce and equality objective decisions, with time and resources recognised.",figure:"",year:"2020 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Race Forum page describes leadership arrangements dated 2020; current arrangements may differ.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service blog: Civil Service Race Forum (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/civil-service-race-forum/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","act in an advisory role to the leadership of the Civil Service on issues and policies addressing racial diversity and equality"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Race Equality Engagement Group, One year of action 2025 to 2026 (15 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action-2025-to-2026/race-equality-engagement-group-one-year-of-action","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","channeling community voices and lived experiences directly into the policy process")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"tgally",h:"Visible sponsorship, allyship and leading by example",intro:"Concrete behaviours drawn from published guidance for sponsors, leaders and allies.",points:[
  {h:"Executive sponsors: own the race agenda visibly",text:"The Race at Work Charter's first original commitment was to appoint an executive sponsor for race. Senior sponsorship gives inclusion work momentum, and the McGregor-Smith Review said the sponsor should be held to account for delivering aspirational targets. Visible behaviours suggested in guidance include having race-inclusion objectives, discussing race at senior management meetings, sponsoring ethnic minority staff networks and taking part in events such as Black History Month.",figure:"",year:"2017 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Descriptions of behaviours are drawn from guidance, not from evaluation of outcomes.",src:[SRCQ("CIPD and Business in the Community: Meeting the BITC Race at Work Charter, an employer's guide (March 2021)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/bitc-core-meeting-the-bitc-race-at-work-charter-an-employers-guide-mar24.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Senior management sponsorship is key to getting real momentum behind inclusion and diversity (I&D) initiatives."),SRCQ("CIPD and Business in the Community: Meeting the BITC Race at Work Charter, an employer's guide (March 2021)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/bitc-core-meeting-the-bitc-race-at-work-charter-an-employers-guide-mar24.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Appoint an executive sponsor for race"),SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Ensuring executive sponsorship for key targets"),SRCQ("CIPD and Business in the Community: Meeting the BITC Race at Work Charter, an employer's guide (March 2021)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/bitc-core-meeting-the-bitc-race-at-work-charter-an-employers-guide-mar24.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","This could take many forms, including discussing race at senior management meetings, sponsoring ethnic minority staff networks, or actively participating in race inclusion events such as Black History Month."),SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","This individual should be held to account for the overall delivery of aspirational targets.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Take part in reverse mentoring and 360-degree feedback",text:"McGregor-Smith recommended that all senior managers consider reverse mentoring with someone more junior, to understand what it is like to be an ethnic minority employee in their organisation. The CIPD and Business in the Community suggest reverse mentoring and 360-degree feedback as ways to make leaders own inclusion. Leaders should listen, act on what they hear and report back.",figure:"",year:"2017 to 2021",disputed:false,note:"Reverse mentoring relies on junior colleagues' time and openness; it should be voluntary and recognised.",src:[SRCQ("UK government: Race in the workplace, The McGregor-Smith Review (28 February 2017)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7f81c6ed915d74e33f6dc4/race-in-workplace-mcgregor-smith-review.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","all senior management should consider undertaking reverse mentoring with someone more junior in the organisation"),SRCQ("CIPD and Business in the Community: Meeting the BITC Race at Work Charter, an employer's guide (March 2021)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/bitc-core-meeting-the-bitc-race-at-work-charter-an-employers-guide-mar24.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Building in reverse mentoring and 360-degree feedback processes can be one way to encourage senior leaders to take ownership of delivering inclusion and diversity.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Sponsor actively: advocate, open doors, find stretch work",text:"Sponsorship is advocacy and opportunity, while mentoring is guidance and listening. Good sponsors use their influence, for example by finding high-profile projects, giving references, and raising the profile of the person they sponsor with senior networks. Guidance suggests training sponsors and setting a clear timeframe, such as 6 to 12 months.",figure:"Suggested duration 6 to 12 months",year:"2020 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Offer mentoring, sponsorship and other development programmes (4 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/offer-mentoring-sponsorship-and-other-development-programmes/offer-mentoring-sponsorship-and-other-development-programmes","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Participants need to understand the difference between mentoring (guidance and listening) and sponsorship (advocacy and opportunity)."),SRCQ("Office for Equality and Opportunity: Offer mentoring, sponsorship and other development programmes (4 March 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/offer-mentoring-sponsorship-and-other-development-programmes/offer-mentoring-sponsorship-and-other-development-programmes","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Sponsors should understand how best to use their influence, such as finding them high-profile projects."),SRCQ("Civil Service blog: How sponsorship empowers (27 February 2020)","https://civilservice.blog.gov.uk/2020/02/27/how-sponsorship-empowers/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","They are sponsored by a senior leader, who helps them raise their profile and gives them exposure to opportunities and networks.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Be an active bystander and call out racism",text:"Allies challenge racist remarks and microaggressions when they happen and make room for ethnic minority colleagues to be heard in meetings. Acas points to the Business in the Community guide, which lists recognising privilege, being an active bystander and being ready to be vulnerable. Allyship is one part of a wider race equality strategy, not a replacement for it.",figure:"",year:"2021 to 2024",disputed:false,note:"The Acas source is a blog post by a named Acas official, not formal Acas guidance.",src:[SRCQ("Acas: What do we mean when we talk of allyship? (blog, 16 February 2021)","https://www.acas.org.uk/what-do-we-mean-when-we-talk-of-allyship","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","It contains simple tips like recognising the advantages of privilege, being an active bystander and being ready to be vulnerable."),SRCQ("Business in the Community: Anti-racism and allyship in the workplace, a guide (August 2024)","https://bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/bitc-guide-anti-racism-and-allyship-in-the-workplace-august24-v2.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Be an active bystander and anti-racist"),SRCQ("Acas: What do we mean when we talk of allyship? (blog, 16 February 2021)","https://www.acas.org.uk/what-do-we-mean-when-we-talk-of-allyship","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","allyship should be just one part of a broader strategy for race equality")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Lead by example on zero tolerance of harassment",text:"The Race at Work Charter asks boards to commit to zero tolerance of harassment and bullying. Leaders can introduce and enforce clear policies so people are held accountable for racism, and model inclusive behaviour in every interaction. The department's own objective gives leaders tools to tackle bullying, harassment and discrimination.",figure:"",year:"2021 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Business in the Community: Race at Work Charter (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/race-at-work-charter/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Commit at board level to zero tolerance of harassment and bullying"),SRCQ("CIPD and Business in the Community: Meeting the BITC Race at Work Charter, an employer's guide (March 2021)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/bitc-core-meeting-the-bitc-race-at-work-charter-an-employers-guide-mar24.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Champion race equality and lead by example"),SRCQ("Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport: Equality and diversity (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-digital-culture-media-and-sport/about/equality-and-diversity","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Leaders will be provided with the necessary tools to address these issues effectively.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Learn continuously and accept you will make mistakes",text:"Good allies keep themselves informed about race and racism and are comfortable talking about it at work. Business in the Community's guide says allies will make mistakes and should treat them as a chance to learn. The Race at Work Charter now asks employers to move people from allyship to solidarity.",figure:"",year:"2021 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("Acas: What do we mean when we talk of allyship? (blog, 16 February 2021)","https://www.acas.org.uk/what-do-we-mean-when-we-talk-of-allyship","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","A great ally is one who is informed and is continually updating their knowledge."),SRCQ("Business in the Community: Anti-racism and allyship in the workplace, a guide (August 2024)","https://bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/bitc-guide-anti-racism-and-allyship-in-the-workplace-august24-v2.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Mistakes are an opportunity to learn and change your approach to ensure your words and actions make a difference."),SRCQ("Business in the Community: Race at Work Charter (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/race-at-work-charter/","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","Progress employees from allyship to solidarity")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Put allyship into objectives and appraisal",text:"Inclusion behaviours can be part of performance objectives for everyone, with heavier expectations for senior leaders. The CIPD and Business in the Community suggest measures such as 360-degree feedback, taking part in allyship programmes and advocating for minority groups. Wales applies this to senior leaders through anti-racism objectives.",figure:"",year:"2021 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"",src:[SRCQ("CIPD and Business in the Community: Meeting the BITC Race at Work Charter, an employer's guide (March 2021)","https://www.bitc.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/bitc-core-meeting-the-bitc-race-at-work-charter-an-employers-guide-mar24.pdf","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","taking part in allyship programmes and supporting minority groups through advocacy and other activities"),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Anti-racist Wales Action Plan, 2024 update (13 November 2024)","https://www.gov.wales/anti-racist-wales-action-plan-2024-update-html","Supports the Targets, commitments and allyship page","The Welsh Government Civil Service will ensure senior leaders have clear anti-racism objectives, embed 360-feedback, and provide bespoke anti-racism training.")],kind:"guidance"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Ask your organisation for figures on Black and Mixed groups, not only 'ethnic minority' totals.",refs:["tg-tgadvocate-ask-for-data-by-black-and-mixed-groups-n"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Be an active bystander: challenge racist remarks when they happen.",refs:["tg-tgally-be-an-active-bystander-and-call-out-raci"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Sponsor, do not only mentor: advocate for people and open doors to stretch work.",refs:["tg-tgally-sponsor-actively-advocate-open-doors-fin", "tg-tgadvocate-turn-mentoring-into-sponsorship-that-cha"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Public bodies must publish equality information yearly and objectives at least every four years.",refs:["tg-tgdept-psed-publish-information-yearly-and-obje"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Proposed pay gap reporting would show Black and Mixed groups separately; prepare the data now.",refs:["tg-tgdept-proposed-reporting-would-show-black-and-"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"19.2% of civil servants are from an ethnic minority background, but 10.6% at Senior Civil Service level.",refs:["tg-tgcs-ethnic-minority-civil-servants-at-a-reco"]},{t:"There is no current Civil Service-wide target for ethnic minority representation in the Senior Civil Service.",refs:["tg-tgcs-no-current-civil-service-wide-scs-ethnic"]},{t:"Ethnicity pay gap reporting is proposed for employers with 250 or more staff, but no bill had been introduced by 30 October 2026.",refs:["tg-tgdept-ethnicity-pay-gap-law-proposed-not-intro"]},{t:"Black civil servants had a 5.3% median earnings gap and a 40.3% bonus gap in 2026.",refs:["tg-tgcs-civil-service-ethnicity-pay-gaps-2026-bl"]}]}],dx:false};
LP["participate"]={name:"Taking part in public life",pfx:"yp",kpis:[],sections:[
 {k:"pavoice",h:"Have your say: youth parliaments, councils and advisory groups",intro:"Every nation has an elected or selected body that carries young people's views to people in power. Most take members from about age 11 or 12, and most run two-year terms.",points:[
  {h:"UK Youth Parliament: elected members aged 11 to 18",text:"The UK Youth Parliament has around 300 elected Members of Youth Parliament aged 11 to 18 who carry young people's views to government and service providers. Elections are usually held every two years, often in schools. The National Youth Agency has run the programme since May 2024, after the British Youth Council closed in March 2024.",figure:"c. 300 members; ages 11 to 18; 2-year terms",year:"2024 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Elections are not held in every constituency: the government said in 2024 that they run in over 70% of constituencies. One council page counts 369 seats for elected members, so totals differ by source.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Evaluation of the UK Youth Parliament (20 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The UK Youth Parliament is made up of circa 300 Members of Youth Parliament (MYPs) aged between 11 and 18 years old."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Government secures future of UK Youth Parliament (press release, 21 May 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-secures-future-of-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Young people vote for their MYPs in elections every two years, which are held in over 70% of constituencies, and often in schools."),SRCQ("North Northamptonshire Council: UK Youth Parliament (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.northnorthants.gov.uk/local-democracy-and-civic-functions/uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","MYPs are usually elected in youth elections throughout the UK once every 2 years. Any young person aged 11-18 can stand or vote.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Make Your Mark and the House of Commons sitting",text:"Make Your Mark is the UK Youth Parliament's survey of young people's priorities, and members take the results into national debate. Members meet at an annual conference and hold a sitting in the House of Commons chamber. Awareness is still low: in the government's evaluation only about a quarter of young people who were not members had heard of the programme.",figure:"26% of non-members had heard of UKYP",year:"2024 to 2025",disputed:false,note:"Awareness figures come from an omnibus survey in year 2 of the evaluation (2024/25). Ballot dates for Make Your Mark are set each year by the National Youth Agency.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Government secures future of UK Youth Parliament (press release, 21 May 2024)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/government-secures-future-of-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Through the Make Your Mark survey and other activities undertaken regionally, the MYPs will continue to make a valuable contribution towards political debate on youth matters"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Evaluation of the UK Youth Parliament (20 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Key activities MYPs participate in include an Annual Conference and a House of Commons sitting."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Evaluation of the UK Youth Parliament (20 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","In Year 2, the young person’s omnibus survey found that around a quarter of young people had heard of the programme (26%); nearly two-thirds (64%) had not heard of it.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Scottish Youth Parliament: vote at 12 to 25, stand at 14 to 25",text:"Around 160 Members of the Scottish Youth Parliament, aged 14 to 25, are elected every two years to represent young people in every part of Scotland. Young people aged 12 to 25 can vote. The 2026 elections run from 2 to 15 November 2026, using the single transferable vote.",figure:"c. 160 members; vote 12 to 25",year:"November 2026",disputed:false,note:"The candidate deadline for 2026 has passed; the next chance to stand is expected in 2028. Voting methods (paper or online with a Young Scot card) vary by council area.",src:[SRCQ("Scottish Youth Parliament: SYP Elections 2026 (2026, read 30 October 2026)","https://syp.org.uk/?p=13237","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Every two years, around 160 members of the Scottish Youth Parliament – MSYPs – aged 14 – 25 are elected to represent young people in every part of Scotland"),SRCQ("Scottish Youth Parliament: SYP Elections 2026 (2026, read 30 October 2026)","https://syp.org.uk/?p=13237","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Voting will open on 2nd November and closes on 15th November 2026. Young people aged 12 – 25 will be able to vote"),SRCQ("Scottish Borders Council: Scottish Youth Parliament election process (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.scotborders.gov.uk/youth-participation/youth-parliament/2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You and all the other young people aged 12-25 will be able to cast your vote using one of two methods")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Welsh Youth Parliament (Senedd Ieuenctid): 60 members",text:"The Welsh Youth Parliament has 60 members. Forty are elected by young people voting in constituency elections and 20 are elected through partner organisations, so that more groups of young people are represented. A term lasts two years, and young people aged 11 up to 18 can register to vote.",figure:"60 members: 40 constituency, 20 partner",year:"2018 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"The current members' page gives their ages as 11 to 17. The voting age range comes from the 2018 launch announcement (updated 2024); check the Welsh Youth Parliament site for the next election timetable.",src:[SRCQ("Senedd Cymru, Welsh Youth Parliament: About (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://youthparliament.senedd.wales/about/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","60 young people aged 11 - 17 are your Welsh Youth Parliament Members."),SRCQ("Senedd Cymru, Welsh Youth Parliament: About (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://youthparliament.senedd.wales/about/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You elected 40 of them by voting in our election. The remaining 20 were elected by young people from partner organisations."),SRCQ("Senedd Cymru: Voter registration opens for new Welsh Youth Parliament (31 May 2018, last updated 16 December 2024)","https://senedd.wales/senedd-now/news/voter-registration-opens-for-new-welsh-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","A Welsh Youth Parliament term will last for two years")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Northern Ireland Youth Assembly: 90 members chosen at random",text:"The Northern Ireland Youth Assembly has 90 members who were in school years 9 to 12 (about ages 12 to 16) when recruited. Members are chosen by an anonymised random selection, 54 by constituency and 36 to reflect groups such as gender, religion, race, care experience and disability. Committees meet monthly online and plenary sittings are held twice a year, usually in the Assembly chamber.",figure:"90 members; school years 9 to 12",year:"2025 to 2027",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, the Northern Ireland Assembly Commission (two pages of its Youth Assembly site). The current members took their seats in November 2025; unsuccessful applicants can join a consultation forum list.",src:[SRCQ("Northern Ireland Assembly Commission, NI Youth Assembly: Learn more (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://niyouthassembly.org/learn-more/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The current 90 Youth Assembly Members took their seats in November 2025. At the time of recruitment, they were in school years 9-12 which is approximately age 12-16."),SRCQ("Northern Ireland Assembly Commission, NI Youth Assembly: Learn more (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://niyouthassembly.org/learn-more/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Recruitment is carried out using an enhanced random selection process."),SRCQ("Northern Ireland Assembly Commission, NI Youth Assembly: home page (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.niyouthassembly.org/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","It’s a group of 90 young people from Years 9 to 12, (this is roughly ages 12-16), ready to voice their thoughts and shake things up in Northern Ireland.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Children's Commissioners' young advisers",text:"Children's Commissioners recruit groups of young people to advise them. In England a new advisory board of up to 16 young people aged 16 to 18 meets every six weeks until December 2027 to advise on AI in education. In Scotland the Commissioner's Young Advisors are aged 12 to 17 and meet about once a month.",figure:"England: up to 16 members aged 16 to 18; Scotland: ages 12 to 17",year:"2022 to 2027",disputed:false,note:"Applications for the England board closed on 21 June 2026. England's first Children's Advisory Board (2022) was a pilot with 10 schools for ages 10 to 13. The Wales and Northern Ireland Commissioners also involve young people; check their sites for current panels.",src:[SRCQ("Children's Commissioner for England: A new youth advisory board to share experiences and views on AI in education (29 May 2026)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/blog/a-new-youth-advisory-board-to-share-experiences-and-views-on-ai-in-education/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Children’s Commissioner is looking for a group of up to 16 young people aged 16 to 18 from across England"),SRCQ("Children's Commissioner for England: A new youth advisory board to share experiences and views on AI in education (29 May 2026)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/blog/a-new-youth-advisory-board-to-share-experiences-and-views-on-ai-in-education/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Meet on a six-weekly basis until December 2027 with the other young people on the advisory group"),SRCQ("Children and Young People's Commissioner Scotland: Young Advisors (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.cypcs.org.uk/about-us/young-advisors/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Their ages range from 12 to 17."),SRCQ("Children's Commissioner for England: The Children's Commissioner launches her Children's Advisory Board (5 December 2022)","https://www.childrenscommissioner.gov.uk/blog/the-childrens-commissioner-launches-her-childrens-advisory-board/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","the board made up of 10-13 year-olds (up to 15-year-olds for children with SEND)")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Local youth councils and young mayors",text:"Some councils run a youth council, youth cabinet or young mayor scheme, and local youth councils are the second most common way members of the UK Youth Parliament first heard of it. Lewisham Council says it has the longest established young mayor programme, begun in 2004, and its young mayor has a £25,000 budget in 2026-27. The national youth strategy promises more local youth ambassador networks.",figure:"Lewisham young mayor budget: £25,000",year:"2026-27",disputed:false,note:"Schemes are set locally, so ages, election methods and budgets vary; contact your council's youth service. In the UKYP evaluation, 31% of members first heard of the programme through their local youth council.",src:[SRCQ("Lewisham Council: The Young Mayor (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://lewisham.gov.uk/mayorandcouncil/young-mayor","Supports the Taking part in public life page","We have the longest established young mayor programme in the country (it was started in 2004 by Sir Steve Bullock, former Mayor of Lewisham)."),SRCQ("Lewisham Council: The Young Mayor (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://lewisham.gov.uk/mayorandcouncil/young-mayor","Supports the Taking part in public life page","In 2026–27, the Young Mayor will have a budget of £25,000 to spend on improving the lives of young people in Lewisham."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Evaluation of the UK Youth Parliament (20 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The second most common source was their local youth council (31%)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Youth Matters: Your National Youth Strategy, summary report (10 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/youth-matters-your-national-youth-strategy-summary-report","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Whether it’s through local youth ambassador networks or co-designing programmes with us, your voice won’t just be heard – it’ll help shape the future.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"School and college councils",text:"In Wales the law requires the governing body of every maintained school, except nursery and infant schools, to set up a school council, and the head teacher must make sure it meets six times a school year. In England the Department for Education counts school and college democracy as part of civic engagement in its enrichment benchmarks.",figure:"Wales: 6 meetings a year by law",year:"2005; 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Welsh regulation is shown as originally made in 2005, and the six-meetings rule does not apply to community or foundation special schools. No statutory duty for school councils in England, Scotland or Northern Ireland is cited here; pupil councils there depend on each school.",src:[SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The School Councils (Wales) Regulations 2005, regulation 3 (as made, 2005)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2005/3200/regulation/3/made","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The governing body of a school must establish a school council"),SRCQ("legislation.gov.uk: The School Councils (Wales) Regulations 2005, regulation 3 (as made, 2005)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2005/3200/regulation/3/made","Supports the Taking part in public life page","ensure that meetings of the school council are convened on six occasions during the school year"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): The enrichment framework for schools and colleges: enrichment benchmarks (15 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-enrichment-framework-for-schools-and-colleges/enrichment-benchmarks","Supports the Taking part in public life page","civic engagement, for example volunteering, debating, school and college democracy, community engagement")],kind:"law"}]},
 {k:"padem",h:"Democracy and Parliament: vote, contact, petition, take part",intro:"Anyone can contact their representatives, sign petitions and answer consultations, whatever their age. Voting ages differ by nation and by type of election, and a UK-wide change to 16 is going through Parliament.",points:[
  {h:"Voting age now: 16 in Scotland and Wales, 18 elsewhere",text:"At 30 October 2026 you must be 18 to vote in UK Parliament elections and in local elections in England and Northern Ireland. In Scotland and Wales you can vote at 16 in Scottish Parliament, Senedd and local council elections. You can register from 14 in Scotland and Wales and from 16 in England and Northern Ireland.",figure:"16 (Scotland, Wales devolved/local); 18 (UK Parliament)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"These are the rules in force today. They will change if the Representation of the People Bill becomes law (next point).",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Vote in UK elections: check which elections you can vote in (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/elections-in-the-uk/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","be 18 or over on the day of the election (‘polling day’) (16 or over in Scotland and Wales)"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Register to vote (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote","Supports the Taking part in public life page","If you live in Scotland or Wales You must be aged 14 or over to register."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Register to vote (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote","Supports the Taking part in public life page","If you live in England or Northern Ireland You must be aged 16 or over to register.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Votes at 16 for all UK elections: a Bill, not yet law",text:"The Representation of the People Bill would extend the vote to 16 and 17 year olds in all UK elections and let people register from 14. At 30 October 2026 the Bill has passed the House of Commons and is at committee stage in the House of Lords, with sittings scheduled from 2 November to 14 December 2026. It is not yet law.",figure:"Lords committee stage from 2 November 2026",year:"October 2026",disputed:false,note:"The Bill can still be amended, and the date the new age takes effect will depend on Royal Assent and commencement. The government's youth strategy says votes at 16 will be in place by the next elections; treat that as a stated aim until the Act is passed.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Housing, Communities and Local Government): Representation of the People Bill policy summaries: Votes at 16 (updated 28 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/representation-of-the-people-bill-policy-summaries/votes-at-16","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Representation of the People Bill will extend the voting age to 16 for all UK elections."),SRCQ("UK Parliament Bills API: Representation of the People Bill, bill 4080 (last updated 29 October 2026)","https://bills-api.parliament.uk/api/v1/Bills/4080","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Make provision extending the right to vote to 16 and 17 year olds"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Youth Matters: Your National Youth Strategy, summary report (10 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/youth-matters-your-national-youth-strategy-summary-report","Supports the Taking part in public life page","By the next elections, you’ll be able to vote at 16.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Register to vote: about five minutes online",text:"You register once, not for every election, and again only if you change name, address or nationality. Online registration usually takes about five minutes and asks for your National Insurance number, though you can still register without one. You can register up to two years before you reach voting age.",figure:"about 5 minutes",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Some groups are less likely to be registered, including young people and Black and Mixed-heritage people; see the representation section. An anonymous registration route exists for people with safety concerns.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Register to vote (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote","Supports the Taking part in public life page","It usually takes about 5 minutes."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Register to vote (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You can register to vote up to 2 years before you reach voting age"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Elections in the UK overview (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/elections-in-the-uk","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You’ll need to be registered to vote to take part in elections and referendums.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Petitions: thresholds differ by parliament",text:"UK Parliament petitions stay open for six months; at 10,000 signatures the government responds and at 100,000 Parliament may debate it. Senedd petitions are reviewed by a committee at over 250 signatures and may be put forward for debate at over 10,000. Anyone of any age can submit a Scottish Parliament petition, and the committee looks at every published one.",figure:"UK: 10,000 / 100,000; Senedd: 250 / 10,000",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"To start or sign a UK Parliament petition you must be a British citizen or UK resident; to create a Senedd petition you need an address in Wales. Rules for Northern Ireland Assembly petitions are not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("UK Parliament Petitions: How petitions work (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://petition.parliament.uk/help","Supports the Taking part in public life page","If an e-petition receives 10,000 signatures, it will receive a response from the Government."),SRCQ("Senedd Cymru Petitions: Help (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://petitions.senedd.wales/help","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Petitions Committee reviews all petitions that collect over 250 signatures."),SRCQ("Scottish Parliament Petitions: Help (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://petitions.parliament.scot/help","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Any person or organisation can submit a petition. You do not have to be a certain age or live in Scotland")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Give evidence to committees and answer consultations",text:"Select committees in Parliament publish inquiries that invite views from the public. Government policy papers and consultations are listed together on GOV.UK. Taking part in a consultation about local services is one of the forms of civic engagement the government measures each year.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Consultations have closing dates; read the scope and how responses will be used before replying.",src:[SRCQ("UK Parliament: Get involved (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.parliament.uk/get-involved/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Find out how to get involved with the work of select committees and see which inquiries want to hear your views"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Policy papers and consultations search (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/search/policy-papers-and-consultations","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Policy papers and consultations"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26: civic engagement report (updated 14 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement-report--2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Civic consultation is defined as taking part in a consultation about local services or problems in the local area")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Free education services and visits at each parliament",text:"The UK Parliament education service offers free school visits, classroom workshops and teacher training, and organisations can sign up to run UK Parliament Week activities. The Northern Ireland Assembly and Scottish Parliament also run education services, tours and events.",figure:"UK Parliament education service: free",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"UK Parliament Week dates for 2026 are not confirmed here. The Senedd also runs an education service; check its website for current visits and workshops.",src:[SRCQ("UK Parliament: Education programmes (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.parliament.uk/get-involved/education-programmes/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators."),SRCQ("Northern Ireland Assembly: Education service (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://education.niassembly.gov.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Supporting young people’s understanding of the Northern Ireland Assembly and democracy."),SRCQ("Scottish Parliament: Members of the Scottish Parliament (MSPs) (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.parliament.scot/msps","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Find out about tours and events at the Scottish Parliament.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"paservice",h:"Serve and volunteer in public institutions",intro:"Many public institutions depend on volunteers and lay members, from courts and police to prisons, schools and public boards. Most roles start at 18, while police cadets take younger members.",points:[
  {h:"Magistrate: from 18, at least 13 days a year",text:"Magistrates in England and Wales are volunteers aged 18 to 74 who hear cases in panels of three. You must sit for at least 13 full days (or 26 half days) a year and are normally expected to serve for at least five years. By law, employers must allow reasonable time off.",figure:"15,259 magistrates (1 April 2026)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"These rules apply in England and Wales. Scotland uses Justices of the Peace and Northern Ireland lay magistrates, with different rules not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Become a magistrate (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/become-magistrate/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You have to be aged between 18 and 74. Magistrates must retire at 75."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Become a magistrate (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/become-magistrate/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You’ll need to be in court for at least 13 full days a year. You can ask to serve 26 half days instead."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Justice): Diversity of the judiciary: 2026 statistics (9 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Taking part in public life page","There were 15,259 magistrates in post across England and Wales as at 1 April 2026, up 4% from the previous year (14,636).")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Special constable: volunteer police officer from 18",text:"Special constables are volunteers with the same powers as police officers. You can apply at 17 but must be 18 to be offered the role. Specials usually commit to at least 16 hours a month after training.",figure:"minimum 16 hours a month",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Hours and recruitment rounds vary by force; Police Scotland runs its own special constabulary.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Police community support officers (PCSOs) and special constables (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/police-community-support-officers-what-they-are/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Special constables are volunteers but have the same powers as police officers."),SRCQ("Joining the Police (College of Policing service): Volunteering with the police (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.joiningthepolice.co.uk/career-progression/volunteering-with-the-police","Supports the Taking part in public life page","To apply for the special constabulary, the minimum age is 17 years old, but you must be 18 years old to be offered the role."),SRCQ("Joining the Police (College of Policing service): Volunteering with the police (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.joiningthepolice.co.uk/career-progression/volunteering-with-the-police","Supports the Taking part in public life page","work a minimum of 16 hours a month")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Volunteer Police Cadets: a uniformed youth group",text:"The Volunteer Police Cadets is the police uniformed youth group in England and Wales; in Scotland it is Police Scotland Youth Volunteers. Its aim is adventure and good citizenship, not recruiting future officers. Ages vary by force: Thames Valley Police, for example, takes 13 to 18 year olds, and some forces run junior units for 11 to 13 year olds.",figure:"typical ages 13 to 18; juniors 11 to 13",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Each police force runs its own units, so check your local force for ages, meeting nights and costs. The 13 to 18 range quoted is from Thames Valley Police.",src:[SRCQ("Volunteer Police Cadets: About us (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://vpc.police.uk/about-us/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Volunteer Police Cadets is the nationally recognised police uniformed youth group throughout England & Wales and in Scotland known as Police Scotland Youth Volunteers."),SRCQ("Volunteer Police Cadets: About us (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://vpc.police.uk/about-us/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The purpose of the VPC is not to recruit police officers of the future, but to encourage the spirit of adventure and good citizenship amongst its members."),SRCQ("Volunteer Police Cadets: Be a cadet (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://vpc.police.uk/Be-a-Cadet","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Junior VPC 11- 13 year olds"),SRCQ("Thames Valley Police careers: Police cadets (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://tvpcareers.co.uk/opportunities/cadets/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Police Cadets are a diverse group of young people aged 13-18 years")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"NHS youth voice and patient involvement",text:"NHS England's Youth Forum is a group of 25 young people who work with NHS England policy leads. More broadly, local groups that make decisions about health or education services are one of the ways people take part in civic activism.",figure:"NHS Youth Forum: 25 members",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The NHS Youth Forum page gives no age range; recruitment is run with Barnardo's. Local NHS volunteering ages and roles vary by trust or health board.",src:[SRCQ("NHS England: NHS Youth Forum (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/get-involved/get-involved/how/forums/nhs-youth-forum/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The NHS Youth Forum is made up of 25 young people from across the country."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26: civic engagement report (updated 14 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement-report--2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","groups making decisions about local health or education services")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"School or academy governor: from 18",text:"Governors and trustees oversee schools' strategy, finance and standards as volunteers. In England governors must be 18 or over when elected or appointed. Being a school governor is one of the local roles the government counts as civic activism.",figure:"minimum age 18",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Age rule quoted is for maintained schools in England. Wales also has associate pupil governors; Scotland uses parent councils and Northern Ireland boards of governors, not covered here.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): Maintained schools: governance guide (updated 15 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/maintained-schools-governance-guide/maintained-schools-governance-guide","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Governors must be 18 or over at the time of election or appointment."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26: civic engagement report (updated 14 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement-report--2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Civic activism is defined as involvement in local community roles such as being a local councillor, school governor, volunteer special constable or magistrate (for those aged 18 or over).")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Independent custody visitors and monitoring boards",text:"Independent custody visitors are volunteers who visit police custody suites to check the welfare and rights of people held in police custody. Independent Monitoring Boards check day-to-day standards in each prison and immigration detention centre. Monitoring board volunteering usually takes two or three days a month.",figure:"IMB: 2 to 3 days a month",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Minimum ages are not stated on these pages; ask the local scheme. Monitoring boards cover every prison in England and Wales and immigration detention across the UK.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Volunteer to check standards in prison (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/volunteer-to-check-standards-in-prison/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Independent Monitoring Boards check the day-to-day standards of prisons - each prison has one associated with it."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Volunteer to check standards in prison (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/volunteer-to-check-standards-in-prison/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Volunteering usually takes up about 2 or 3 days per month."),SRCQ("Independent Custody Visitors Association: home page (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://icva.org.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Every week, Independent Custody Visitors (ICVs) walk into police custody suites across the country to check on the welfare and rights of people being detained."),SRCQ("Independent Monitoring Boards: home page (read 30 October 2026)","https://imb.org.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Inside every prison, immigration removal centre and short term holding facility there is an Independent Monitoring Board (IMB).")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Jury service: a civic duty from 18",text:"Jury service is a legal duty, not a volunteer role. In England and Wales jurors are chosen at random from the electoral register, must be 18 to 75, and must reply to a summons within 7 days. Service usually lasts up to 10 working days.",figure:"ages 18 to 75; up to 10 working days",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Rules shown are for England and Wales. Scotland and Northern Ireland have their own jury rules and summons arrangements.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Jury service (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/jury-service/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","be aged between 18 and 75 on the day you start jury service"),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Jury service (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/jury-service/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Jury service usually lasts up to 10 working days."),SRCQ("Scottish Courts and Tribunals Service: Jurors (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.scotcourts.gov.uk/coming-to-court/jurors/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","It is important that you call this number as it will provide you with up to date information about your jury service.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Public appointments to boards of public bodies",text:"Public bodies and advisory committees recruit chairs and members through open public appointment campaigns. Vacancies are listed on the Cabinet Office's public appointments service, where you can set up alerts. Government says boards work best when they reflect the society they serve.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Each advert sets out the experience needed and the time commitment. Diversity data are in the representation section.",src:[SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Apply for a public appointment (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://publicappointments.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Boards of public bodies are most effective when they reflect the diversity of views of the society they serve."),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Apply for a public appointment (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://publicappointments.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Search and apply for an appointment on the board of a public body or advisory committee"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Public Appointments Data Report 2024/25 (2 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","We hold data on ethnicity for 51% of appointees.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"paeducation",h:"While at school, college or university",intro:"Education offers some of the best-supported routes into public life, from enrichment activities to paid government internships. Some long-running schemes have changed, so check current status before applying.",points:[
  {h:"Civil Service routes from age 16",text:"You can apply for a Civil Service apprenticeship from 16, an internship while at university, and the Fast Stream after graduating. Apprentices are paid employees who study for a recognised qualification. You can apply while still at school if you will be 16 by the end of the summer holidays.",figure:"apprenticeships from 16",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"See the hub's joining page for application steps. Apprenticeship systems differ in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service early careers (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/home/civil-service-early-careers/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","There are many routes into the Civil Service. From the age of 16, you can apply for an apprenticeship."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Become an apprentice (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/become-apprentice/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You can apply for an apprenticeship while you’re still at school but you’ll need to be 16 or over by the end of the summer holidays to start the apprenticeship.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Summer Internship Programme: apply by 5 November 2026",text:"The Fast Stream Summer Internship Programme gives undergraduates from lower socio-economic backgrounds a paid eight-week placement in a government department. You must be in your final two years at university. Applications for summer 2027 close at midday on 5 November 2026, and the placement runs July to August 2027.",figure:"8 weeks, paid; closes 5 November 2026",year:"2026-27",disputed:false,note:"Eligibility is assessed using ONS measures of socio-economic background, plus Civil Service nationality rules. See the hub's joining page for how to apply.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream Summer Internship Programme (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-summer-internship-programme/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Applications close at midday on 5 November 2026"),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream Summer Internship Programme (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-summer-internship-programme/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You need to be available for the internship in the summer holiday period from July 2027 to August 2027."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Internship scheme to get more working class students into Civil Service (1 August 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/internship-scheme-to-get-more-working-class-students-into-civil-service","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Students from lower-income backgrounds to get paid government placements.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Care Leavers Internship Scheme: 18 months, ages 18 to 30",text:"The Care Leavers Internship Scheme, run by the Department for Education, offers 18-month paid jobs in government departments and agencies. Applicants must be care leavers entitled to a leaving care package and aged 18 to 30. Interns can be made permanent after 12 months.",figure:"18 months; ages 18 to 30",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"You must have been in care for at least 13 weeks, some of it after your 16th birthday. See the hub's joining page for more.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service Care Leavers Internship Scheme (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-care-leavers-internship-scheme","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The duration of the internship scheme is 18-months."),SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Civil Service Care Leavers Internship Scheme (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/civil-service-care-leavers-internship-scheme","Supports the Taking part in public life page","aged 18-30 at the time of application"),SRCQ("GOV.UK blog, Children's social care: My path to the Civil Service: a care leaver's perspective (21 July 2025)","https://childrenssocialcare.blog.gov.uk/2025/07/21/civil-service-care-leavers-internship-scheme-2025-2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Civil Service Care Leavers Internship Scheme changed that.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Youth Select Committee: 12 members of the Youth Parliament",text:"The Youth Select Committee copies the work of Commons select committees. It has 12 members drawn from the UK Youth Parliament, and the National Youth Agency runs it with support from Parliament. Its 2025/26 inquiry began with a meeting at Parliament in September 2025.",figure:"12 members",year:"2025/26",disputed:false,note:"Sources give different upper ages: Parliament says 14 to 18 and a council news item says 14 to 19. The committee now continues under the National Youth Agency after the British Youth Council closed.",src:[SRCQ("UK Parliament: Youth Select Committee (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.parliament.uk/get-involved/education-programmes/youth-select-committee/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","is a National Youth Agency initiative, supported by UK Parliament and Department for Culture, Media and Sport."),SRCQ("Carmarthenshire County Council newsroom: Youth Council member went to Parliament as Youth Select Committee launches inquiry (September 2025)","https://newsroom.carmarthenshire.gov.wales/2025/09/carmarthenshire-youth-council-member-went-to-parliament-as-youth-select-committee-launches-inquiry/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Youth Select Committee is a unique parliamentary initiative, delivered by the National Youth Agency and supported by the House of Commons.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"DofE, debating and Model UN through school",text:"The Duke of Edinburgh's Award is open to anyone aged 14 to 24. The Department for Education's 2026 enrichment framework names the Award, debating, Model United Nations and mock magistrates' trials as ways schools build civic engagement. Ask your school or college what it offers.",figure:"DofE: ages 14 to 24",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Some enrichment activities have costs; the framework lists free resources alongside paid options. The hub endorses no provider.",src:[SRCQ("The Duke of Edinburgh's Award: Do your DofE (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.dofe.org/do/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","if you’re between 14-24, The Duke of Edinburgh’s Award (DofE) is for you."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): The enrichment framework for schools and colleges: enrichment benchmarks (15 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-enrichment-framework-for-schools-and-colleges/enrichment-benchmarks","Supports the Taking part in public life page","civic engagement is nurtured through activities such as Model United Nations, public speaking and debating, mock magistrates, and volunteering")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Cadet forces: over 137,000 young people",text:"The Ministry of Defence sponsors five cadet forces: Sea Cadets, the Volunteer Cadet Corps, Army Cadets, Air Cadets and the school-based Combined Cadet Force. At 1 April 2026 there were 88,870 community cadets. The 2025 Strategic Defence Review recommended growing cadet forces by 30% by 2030.",figure:"88,870 community cadets (1 April 2026)",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Minimum joining ages differ: Sea Cadets and the Volunteer Cadet Corps from 9, Army Cadets from 12, Air Cadets from 12 and in school year 8, and the school-based Combined Cadet Force from school year 8. Membership usually ends at 18 or on leaving school. The 137,000 total includes 48,240 Combined Cadet Force members.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Defence): MOD sponsored cadet forces: 1 April 2026 (11 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/mod-sponsored-cadet-forces-statistics-2026/mod-sponsored-cadet-forces-1-april-2026","Supports the Taking part in public life page","As at 1 April 2026, there were 88,870 cadets in the Community Cadets, made up of 13,720 Sea Cadets, 460 Volunteer Cadet Corps, 39,490 Army Cadets and 35,190 Air Cadets."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Defence): MOD sponsored cadet forces: 1 April 2026 (11 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/mod-sponsored-cadet-forces-statistics-2026/mod-sponsored-cadet-forces-1-april-2026","Supports the Taking part in public life page","There are over 137,000 young people and more than 28,000 adult volunteers in approximately 3,500 cadet units across the UK."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): The enrichment framework: resources to support schools and colleges with enrichment (15 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-enrichment-framework-for-schools-and-colleges/resources-to-support-schools-and-colleges-with-enrichment","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The Combined Cadet Force (CCF) is a partnership between schools and colleges and the Ministry of Defence (MOD)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"National Citizen Service has closed",text:"The National Citizen Service, which offered 16 and 17 year olds in England residential trips and social action projects, ended on 31 March 2025. The government is replacing it with a wider national youth strategy, including a Youth Guarantee and Young Futures Hubs. Older guides that still recommend NCS are out of date.",figure:"closed 31 March 2025",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"The NCS Trust website no longer operates. Programmes under the youth strategy are being rolled out over the next ten years.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: National Citizen Service organisation page (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/national-citizen-service","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The National Citizen Service programme concluded on 31 March 2025 and the National Citizen Service Trust will be closed in due course."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (NCS Trust Board and Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Joint statement (June 2025)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/686cf722a08d3a3ca3b6789d/Joint_Statement_NCS-DCMS_-_accessible.pdf","Supports the Taking part in public life page","set out her decision to wind down the National Citizen Service (NCS) programme by 31 March 2025"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Youth Matters: Your National Youth Strategy, summary report (10 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/youth-matters-your-national-youth-strategy-summary-report","Supports the Taking part in public life page","We’re delivering a Youth Guarantee and expanding the number of employment hubs")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"#iWill and youth social action",text:"The #iWill Movement supports young people to take part in social action such as campaigning, volunteering and fundraising. Its Festival of Social Action runs every November. The national youth strategy promises more chances to volunteer through schools, colleges and communities.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The #iWill site describes itself as for ages 10 to 25. The hub endorses no single organisation.",src:[SRCQ("#iWill Movement: home page (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.iwill.org.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The #iWill Festival of Social Action is the #iWill Movement's annual flagship campaign - three days of youth-led events, place-based celebration and national social action, taking place every November."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Youth Matters: Your National Youth Strategy, summary report (10 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/youth-matters-your-national-youth-strategy-summary-report","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You’ll see more chances to get involved in sport, art, music, outdoor adventures and volunteering – through your school, college and local community.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"paholiday",h:"Holidays, half-terms and gap years",intro:"School holidays and gap years are good times to try public service, from free holiday clubs to paid placements. Under-18s have extra legal protections at work and in activities.",points:[
  {h:"Holiday Activities and Food programme (England)",text:"The HAF programme gives children eligible for benefits-related free school meals free activities and a healthy meal in the Easter, summer and Christmas holidays. In 2026-27 councils may also run it in half-terms. From September 2026, eligibility stays at the targeted free school meals threshold, not the wider expanded free school meals group.",figure:"half-term spend expected to stay within 5% of allocation",year:"2026-27",disputed:false,note:"This applies to England only. Councils can use up to about 15% of funding for places for other children; ask your council. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland run their own holiday schemes.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): Holiday activities and food programme 2026 to 2027 (updated 8 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/holiday-activities-and-food-programme/holiday-activities-and-food-programme-2026-to-2027","Supports the Taking part in public life page","While the main focus of HAF remains Easter, summer and Christmas school holidays, local authorities have discretion to extend delivery of HAF across half-term holidays within the 2026 to 2027 fiscal year."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): Holiday activities and food programme 2026 to 2027 (updated 8 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/holiday-activities-and-food-programme/holiday-activities-and-food-programme-2026-to-2027","Supports the Taking part in public life page","covering pupils who are in households in receipt of Universal Credit with annual household earnings of £7,400 or less."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Youth Matters: Your National Youth Strategy, summary report (10 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/youth-matters-your-national-youth-strategy-summary-report","Supports the Taking part in public life page","free holiday activities for those who need them")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Council and community holiday schemes",text:"Councils publish holiday play schemes and clubs, which can be run by schools, charities or private providers. Some councils fund free or low-cost places beyond the HAF-eligible group.",figure:"up to 15% of HAF funding for wider places",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"The GOV.UK club finder covers England and Wales only. Quality and cost vary; check that providers follow safeguarding guidance (see the safeguarding point).",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Find before and after school and holiday clubs (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/after-school-holiday-club","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Search for before and after school clubs and holiday play schemes in your area. They can be run by a school, or a private or voluntary organisation."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): Holiday activities and food programme 2026 to 2027 (updated 8 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/holiday-activities-and-food-programme/holiday-activities-and-food-programme-2026-to-2027","Supports the Taking part in public life page","local authorities have discretion to use some funding – we expect up to 15% – to provide free or subsidised holiday club places for any school-age children regardless of their background")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Museums, archives and heritage: Takeover Day",text:"On Takeover Day, museums, galleries, archives and heritage sites invite young people to take over jobs usually done by adults. The 2026 national Takeover Day is on Friday 20 November and its theme is careers, though venues can hold one at any time of year.",figure:"20 November 2026",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, Kids in Museums. The national date falls in term time; ask your school or local museum. Black History Month programmes are set by each venue.",src:[SRCQ("Kids in Museums: Takeover Day (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://kidsinmuseums.org.uk/takeover-day/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Takeover Day is when museums, galleries, historic homes, archives and heritage sites invite young people in to take over jobs normally done by adults."),SRCQ("Kids in Museums: Takeover Day (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://kidsinmuseums.org.uk/takeover-day/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Friday 20 November 2026"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): The enrichment framework: resources to support schools and colleges with enrichment (15 June 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-enrichment-framework-for-schools-and-colleges/resources-to-support-schools-and-colleges-with-enrichment","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Historic England is a public body that works to champion and protect historic places, including through supporting the cultural and heritage education of children and young people.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Gap year: paid placements and apprenticeships",text:"A gap year can be paid. The Year in Industry places school and college leavers in paid STEM placements, typically for 12 months, and you apply in your final year of A levels, Highers or equivalents. Apprenticeships are paid jobs with training and are open from 16.",figure:"typically 12 months, paid",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"Year in Industry details rest on one source, the Engineering Development Trust. Placement numbers are limited and competitive. Apprentice pay follows age-based minimum wage rates.",src:[SRCQ("Engineering Development Trust: The Year in Industry (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.etrust.org.uk/the-year-in-industry","Supports the Taking part in public life page","These are paid placements and typically last for 12 months providing a full year in industry."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Become an apprentice (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/become-apprentice/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","paid and be entitled to the National Minimum Wage")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Summer placements in government",text:"The Civil Service Summer Internship Programme runs in the university summer holidays, July to August, for students from lower socio-economic backgrounds. A good report can fast-track you into Fast Stream selection if you apply within 12 months. Applications for 2027 close on 5 November 2026.",figure:"July to August 2027",year:"2026-27",disputed:false,note:"Parliament's own work experience and placement schemes are not covered here; check UK Parliament careers pages for current offers.",src:[SRCQ("Civil Service Careers: Fast Stream Summer Internship Programme (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.civil-service-careers.gov.uk/fast-stream/fs-summer-internship-programme/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","You need to be available for the internship in the summer holiday period from July 2027 to August 2027."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Internship scheme to get more working class students into Civil Service (1 August 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/internship-scheme-to-get-more-working-class-students-into-civil-service","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The scheme will give talented undergraduates from lower socio-economic backgrounds the opportunity to see what a career in the Civil Service is like.")],kind:"guidance"},
  {h:"Volunteering in the holidays and gap years",text:"Volunteers' Week runs across the UK from the first Monday in June, and the 2027 week is 7 to 13 June. In England, 18% of people aged 16 and over volunteered formally at least once a month in 2025/26. Local volunteer centres list roles that suit holidays and gap years.",figure:"18% formal monthly volunteering (2025/26)",year:"2019/20; 2027",disputed:false,note:"England only, from the Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26. Under-18s may need parental consent and some roles have minimum ages.",src:[SRCQ("Volunteers' Week (Volunteer Scotland, Volunteer Now, NCVO and WCVA): About (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.volunteersweek.org/about","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Volunteers’ Week is an annual UK-wide campaign held from the first Monday in June"),SRCQ("Volunteers' Week (Volunteer Scotland, Volunteer Now, NCVO and WCVA): About (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.volunteersweek.org/about","Supports the Taking part in public life page","The 2027 celebration will run from Monday 7th – Sunday 13th June."),SRCQ("GOV.UK Ethnicity facts and figures: Volunteering (Community Life Survey 2019/20 data)","https://www.ethnicity-facts-figures.service.gov.uk/culture-and-community/civic-participation/volunteering/latest","Supports the Taking part in public life page","23% of people aged 16 and over in England took part in formal volunteering at least once a month in the year ending March 2020"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26: volunteering and charitable giving report (16 September 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-volunteering/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-volunteering-and-charitable-giving-report","Supports the Taking part in public life page","18% of people aged 16+ and over in England engaged with formal volunteering at least once a month in the last 12 months, a 2 percentage point increase from 2024/25 (17%).")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Working in the holidays if you are under 16",text:"Children can work part-time from 14 (13 in some council areas). In school holidays, 14 year olds can work up to 25 hours a week and 15 to 16 year olds up to 35 hours. Every child must have a two-week break from work during the school holidays each year.",figure:"14: 25 hours; 15-16: 35 hours a week",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, GOV.UK. Local bylaws may add limits and many councils require a child employment permit.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK: Child employment (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/child-employment/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","During school holidays, 14-year-olds are only allowed to work a maximum of 25 hours a week."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Child employment (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/child-employment/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","During school holidays, 15 to 16-year-olds can only work a maximum of 35 hours a week.")],kind:"law"},
  {h:"Safeguarding for under-18s",text:"Holiday clubs, tuition and activity providers should follow the government's out-of-school settings safeguarding guidance, including DBS checks where needed. Children under school leaving age may need a licence for performances and some paid activities. Parents and young people can ask providers about their safeguarding policy before signing up.",figure:"",year:"2026",disputed:false,note:"This is non-statutory guidance written for England, but it sets out the legal requirements providers must follow, and its principles may help providers elsewhere in the UK.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): Out-of-school settings: safeguarding guidance for providers (updated 9 February 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/keeping-children-safe-in-out-of-school-settings-code-of-practice","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Safeguarding guidance, e-learning and resources for providers of after-school clubs, activities and tuition, and other out-of-school settings."),SRCQ("GOV.UK: Child employment (printable guide, undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/child-employment/print","Supports the Taking part in public life page","A child may need a licence if they’re under school leaving age and taking part in:"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Education): After-school clubs, community activities and tuition: safeguarding guidance for providers (PDF, updated February 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6986066acfe7ccf77efbc75b/After-school_clubs__community_activities__and_tuition_-_Safeguarding_guidance_for_providers.pdf","Supports the Taking part in public life page","This publication provides non-statutory guidance from the Department for Education.")],kind:"guidance"}]},
 {k:"parepresent",h:"Who takes part: representation and why it matters",intro:"Published data show Black and Mixed-heritage people are present in most public roles but under-registered to vote and under-represented in some. Figures below are given exactly as published, with their dates.",points:[
  {h:"UK Youth Parliament: Black members in line with population",text:"In 2025, 7% of Members of the UK Youth Parliament were Black, compared with 6% of 11 to 17 year olds in England and Wales. Mixed-heritage members were 5%, compared with 6% of 11 to 17 year olds. Asian members were over-represented (18% compared with 12%) and White British members under-represented.",figure:"Black 7% vs 6%; Mixed 5% vs 6%",year:"2025",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, the government's UKYP evaluation, using the National Youth Agency's 2025 member census. Members are UK-wide but the comparison population is England and Wales (2021 Census). Most decision makers interviewed felt members were mainly from middle-class backgrounds, though a few said this was an assumption rather than based on hard evidence.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Evaluation of the UK Youth Parliament (20 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Black, Black British, Black Welsh, Caribbean or African 4% 6% 6% 7%"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Evaluation of the UK Youth Parliament (20 October 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament/evaluation-of-the-uk-youth-parliament","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Mixed or multiple ethnic groups 3% 7% 6% 5%")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Magistrates: 5% Black, 2% Mixed in 2026",text:"At 1 April 2026, 14% of magistrates in England and Wales were from an ethnic minority: 5% Black, 2% Mixed and 7% Asian. The Black share rose from 4% a year earlier. In 2025-26, 17% of new magistrates were from an ethnic minority, compared with 9% of those who left.",figure:"Black 5%; Mixed 2% (1 April 2026)",year:"2025 to 2026",disputed:false,note:"Shares are of magistrates who declared their ethnicity. 81% of magistrates are 50 or over, so younger people of all backgrounds are under-represented.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Justice): Diversity of the judiciary: 2026 statistics (9 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Black individuals constituted 5% of magistrates."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Justice): Diversity of the judiciary: 2026 statistics (9 July 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2026-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2026-statistics","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Mixed ethnicity individuals constituted 2% of magistrates."),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Ministry of Justice): Diversity of the judiciary: 2025 statistics (23 July 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-2025-statistics/diversity-of-the-judiciary-legal-professions-new-appointments-and-current-post-holders-2025-statistics--2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Black individuals constituted 4% of magistrates.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Public appointments: 13% from ethnic minorities",text:"At 31 March 2025, 13% of public appointees with known ethnicity were from an ethnic minority, up from 9% in 2024 and 12% in 2023. In 2024/25 campaigns, 20% of appointments went to ethnic minority candidates. Only 8% of chairs were from an ethnic minority.",figure:"13% of appointees; 8% of chairs",year:"2024/25",disputed:false,note:"Ethnicity is known for only 51% of appointees, so figures may not reflect all boards. Separate Black and Mixed figures are not published in this report.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Public Appointments Data Report 2024/25 (2 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","13% (293) were from an ethnic minority background, compared to 9% in 2024 and 12% in 2023"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Cabinet Office): Public Appointments Data Report 2024/25 (2 December 2025)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/public-appointments-data-report-202425/public-appointments-data-report-202425-html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","20% of public appointments were made to candidates from an ethnic minority background"),SRCQ("Cabinet Office: Apply for a public appointment (undated page, read 30 October 2026)","https://publicappointments.cabinetoffice.gov.uk/","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Boards of public bodies are most effective when they reflect the diversity of views of the society they serve.")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Civic engagement: Black adults at the England average",text:"In 2025/26, 46% of adults in England took part in civic participation, consultation or activism. The rate for Black adults was also 46% (Black African 46%, Black Caribbean 46%), up from 37% in 2024/25. White and Black Caribbean adults were less likely to take part (41%), and 16 to 24 year olds were the least engaged age group (35%).",figure:"Black 46%; White and Black Caribbean 41%; ages 16-24 35%",year:"2025/26",disputed:false,note:"England only; survey estimates with confidence intervals (Black total 44% to 48%). Voting is excluded. White and Black African adults were 48%; the report does not list them as different from the average. Black and Mixed figures are read from data table A11.",src:[SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26: civic engagement report (updated 14 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement-report--2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","People from the Indian (41%), Pakistani (41%), Any other Asian background (41%) and White and Black Caribbean (41%) ethnic groups were less likely"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26: civic engagement report (updated 14 August 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement/community-and-engagement-survey-202526-civic-engagement-report--2","Supports the Taking part in public life page","people aged 16 to 24 (35%), 25 to 34 (44%) and 75 and over (43%) were less likely"),SRCQ("GOV.UK (Department for Culture, Media and Sport): Community and Engagement Survey 2025/26, civic engagement data tables, table A11 (August 2026)","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a70540d0c36759b5ccaa334/community_and_engagement_2025_26_civic_engagement_data_tables.ods","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Engagement in civic participation, activism or consultation activities in the last 12 months, by demographic characteristics")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Voter registration gaps for Black and Mixed-heritage people",text:"The Electoral Commission's study of the December 2018 registers found 84% of eligible White people were correctly registered, compared with 75% of Black people and 69% of people from Mixed backgrounds. Young people, students and recent movers were the least likely to be registered.",figure:"White 84%; Black 75%; Mixed 69% (2018)",year:"2018 registers; published 2019",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, the Electoral Commission (written evidence to Parliament). The figures are for Great Britain in 2018; the Commission published a later study in 2023 whose ethnicity figures are not reported here.",src:[SRCQ("UK Parliament, Joint Committee on Human Rights: Electoral Commission written evidence to the inquiry into Black people, racism and human rights (2020)","https://committees.parliament.uk/writtenevidence/11633/html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","For people from an Asian or black background, completeness levels were found to be similar (76% and 75% respectively)"),SRCQ("UK Parliament, Joint Committee on Human Rights: Electoral Commission written evidence to the inquiry into Black people, racism and human rights (2020)","https://committees.parliament.uk/writtenevidence/11633/html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","for people from mixed backgrounds it was 69%"),SRCQ("UK Parliament, Joint Committee on Human Rights: Electoral Commission written evidence to the inquiry into Black people, racism and human rights (2020)","https://committees.parliament.uk/writtenevidence/11633/html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","completeness was highest among those from white backgrounds (84%)")],kind:"evidence"},
  {h:"Council candidates in Wales: about 1% Black, 1% Mixed",text:"In the 2022 Welsh local elections survey, 96% of candidates who responded were White, about 1% were Black and 1% were Mixed. The survey is repeated at each ordinary election.",figure:"Black c. 1%; Mixed 1% (2022)",year:"2022",disputed:false,note:"This rests on one source, the Welsh Government. Response rates were low (15% of county and 11% of community candidates). Comparable councillor census data for England (Local Government Association, 2022) are not reported here.",src:[SRCQ("Welsh Government: Local Government Candidates Survey 2022 summary (14 March 2023)","https://www.gov.wales/local-government-candidates-survey-2022-summary-html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","around 1% reported they were from Black, Black Welsh, Black British, Caribbean or African ethnic groups"),SRCQ("Welsh Government: Local Government Candidates Survey 2022 summary (14 March 2023)","https://www.gov.wales/local-government-candidates-survey-2022-summary-html","Supports the Taking part in public life page","Overall, 96% of the 1,077 candidates reported they were from a White ethnic group.")],kind:"evidence"}]}],vant:[{who:"If you are Black, African, Caribbean or Mixed",ic:"heart",items:[{t:"Black members of the UK Youth Parliament are in line with the population of their age group; stand, vote and encourage others to.",refs:["yp-parepresent-uk-youth-parliament-black-members-in-lin"]}]},{who:"Colleagues and allies",ic:"hands",items:[{t:"Share opportunities with young people you know, especially those in care or out of school.",refs:["yp-paeducation-care-leavers-internship-scheme-18-months", "yp-pavoice-local-youth-councils-and-young-mayors"]}]},{who:"Line managers and leaders",ic:"flag",items:[{t:"Give staff time to serve as magistrates, governors or volunteers.",refs:["yp-paservice-magistrate-from-18-at-least-13-days-a-ye", "yp-paservice-school-or-academy-governor-from-18"]}]},{who:"Policy makers and service designers",ic:"layers",items:[{t:"Black and Mixed-heritage people were less likely to be registered to vote in the last published figures; target registration drives.",refs:["yp-parepresent-voter-registration-gaps-for-black-and-mi"]}]},{who:"Data and analysis professionals",ic:"chart",items:[{t:"Public appointment figures give no Black or Mixed breakdown; ask for one.",refs:["yp-parepresent-public-appointments-13-from-ethnic-minor"]}]}],short:[{who:"In short",ic:"info",items:[{t:"From 11 you can stand or vote in UK Youth Parliament elections; each nation has its own youth parliament or assembly.",refs:["yp-pavoice-uk-youth-parliament-elected-members-aged", "yp-pavoice-welsh-youth-parliament-senedd-ieuenctid-"]},{t:"In Scotland and Wales you can vote at 16 in devolved and council elections; votes at 16 UK-wide is not yet law.",refs:["yp-padem-voting-age-now-16-in-scotland-and-wales-", "yp-padem-votes-at-16-for-all-uk-elections-a-bill-"]},{t:"You can serve as a magistrate, special constable or school governor from 18.",refs:["yp-paservice-magistrate-from-18-at-least-13-days-a-ye", "yp-paservice-special-constable-volunteer-police-offic", "yp-paservice-school-or-academy-governor-from-18"]},{t:"National Citizen Service closed on 31 March 2025.",refs:["yp-paeducation-national-citizen-service-has-closed"]}]}],dx:false};
